{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrecords=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor e in records:\n    if e.get('category') == 'micro_opt' or e['id']=='hyp-20260906-002-r5s1':\n        print(json.dumps({k:e.get(k) for k in ['id','title','category','outcome','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','seeds']}))\nfor eid in ['hyp-20260906-001-r6s0','hyp-20260906-001-r9s0','hyp-20260906-002-r5s1']:\n    e=next(e for e in records if e['id']==eid)\n    print('\\\\n'+eid)\n    notes=e.get('implementation_notes','')\n    lines=notes.splitlines()\n    selected=set()\n    for i,line in enumerate(lines):\n        if any(k in line.lower() for k in ['critical','jalr','timing','carry','routed','seed','path','logic depth','cone','fmax']):\n            selected.update(range(max(0,i-1),min(len(lines),i+4)))\n    for i in sorted(selected):\n        print(f'{i+1}: {lines[i]}')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-e113d0a\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Eliminate load-use bubbles caused by immediate bitfields\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": 0.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.96, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [123.75, 127.96, 132.24]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -5.98, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.36, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [141.2, 164.83, 157.36]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Shorten divider carry chains with block carry-select subtraction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -35.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 108.23, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [108.23, 107.12, 112.61]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Carry-free conditional sign correction at divider completion\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -37.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 105.35, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [105.35, 130.29, 104.55]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Balanced one-hot register-file reads with integrated write bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -7.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 159.01, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [159.01, 143.86, 188.96]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"delta_pct\": 7.14, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [184.06, 191.13, 179.89]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Precompute JALR alignment before operand forwarding selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -8.89, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.7, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [182.25, 165.26, 167.7]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Align five-bit ALU control codes with instruction funct3\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -17.88, \"fmax_mhz\": 151.15, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [157.23, 151.15, 147.67]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Infer distributed register storage by resetting per-register validity\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -12.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.15, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [160.15, 161.52, 158.4]}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Flatten EX operand selection with direct one-hot forwarding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"delta_pct\": -4.16, \"fmax_mhz\": 176.4, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"seeds\": [173.07, 176.4, 187.97]}\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r6s0\n1: Implemented the JALR alignment precomputation hypothesis. All local correctness\n2: gates passed, but the measured fitness **regressed 8.89%, from 409.80 to 373.37**.\n3: Median reported Fmax fell from 184.06 to **167.70 MHz** with unchanged benchmark\n4: cycles. The estimated four percent improvement was not observed. Local gate\n5: success is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\n6: \n10: the existing forwarding encoding: 1 selects EX/MEM, 2 selects MEM/WB, and both\n11: 0 and 3 default to the ID/EX value. Both JALR target bit 1 and its misalignment\n12: predicate consume that selected bit directly. The original full-width\n13: `rs1 + in.imm` still supplies target bits [31:2], including the carry from the\n14: low bits and modulo-2^32 wraparound. Target bit 0 remains cleared. No full-width\n15: adder was replicated and no predicate is computed from already-forwarded rs1.\n16: \n28: come from an independent Python full-width addition. Further vectors cover\n29: carry into bit 2 and high bits, negative immediates, wraparound, PC+4 wrap,\n30: independent rs2 forwarding, held owners, invalid metadata, synchronous reset\n31: over hold, and unchanged PC-relative JAL/branch alignment. Checks cover target,\n32: redirect, trap/reg_write, PC+4, pc_next, complete captured operand values, and\n36: `test_pipeline.py` adds register-file, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, competing-writer, x0,\n37: load-use, and divider-result JALR cases with aligned/misaligned sums and negative\n38: immediates. Each runs with zero-wait and deterministic random bus stalls.\n39: Tests verify actual forwarding selections, freshest-writer priority, suppressed\n40: trap links, discarded stores, consecutive redirects, one-cycle ordinary/divide\n75: \n76: All three complete final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n77: \n78: | Seed | Fmax MHz | Source -> endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n79: | --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n80: | 1 | 182.25 | EX/MEM rd[3], through forwarding/control, to saved divide rs2 capture CE | 5.49 | 2.02 / 3.46 |\n81: | 2 | 165.26 | ID/EX alu_op[2], through ALU result selection, to normal result/address bit 26 | 6.05 | 2.61 / 3.45 |\n86: The synthesized design retains **one MULT36X36 and four DPX9B block RAMs**;\n87: all three packed/routed seeds retain one MULT36X36 and four DP blocks. Every\n88: memory-address bit still comes directly from its normal-result DFF.\n89: \n90: Connectivity inspection of the actual Gowin netlist found exactly three LUT4\n97: candidate predicate. The three outputs then enter forwarding-controlled LUT/MUX\n98: selection. Actual connectivity paths from each predicate to IF pc[1], captured\n99: EX pc_next[1], and the EX trap control contain LUT/MUX logic and no native ALU\n100: cells. Thus the intended arithmetic-before-forwarding order survives mapping,\n101: but different paths and placement dominate the reported result.\n102: \n103: A supplemental seed-1 timing/SDF export reproduced the gate's routed netlist\n104: byte-for-byte and the same Fmax. All four input timing arcs are present for\n105: each new predicate LUT4. However, several existing bank selectors and\n106: downstream LUT1/2/3 selection/qualification cells have no IOPATH entries. The\n107: detailed timing report includes IF pc[1] but omits the EX pc_next[1] and trap\n108: endpoints. These omissions are **not zero-delay evidence**: no complete physical\n109: timing margin is claimed for those paths. An initial attempt to reload the\n110: routed JSON for export aborted with nextpnr's array2d bounds assertion; rerunning\n111: the unchanged seed from synth.json with export options produced the identical\n112: route. No timing model, constraint, or gate was modified.\n113: \n114: The evaluator's recorded **aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ten-iteration fixture** remains\n115: distinct from the supplemental **aggregate 6 KB** validation. The latter ELF\n117: Fresh baseline and candidate simulations used identical ELFs and identical\n118: `--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed for each size.\n119: Their complete final markers and UART strings match exactly.\n120: \n121: | Workload | Start / stop markers, both versions | Baseline cycles | Candidate cycles | Baseline / candidate iter/sec |\n127: 167.70 MHz; baseline FPGA routing was not rerun. Every simulation reaches\n128: EBREAK, reports no OOB access, has valid timing brackets, reports ten iterations,\n129: and includes the literal `Correct operation validated.` Evaluator CRCs remain\n130: seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\n131: Supplemental 6 KB CRCs are **seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52,\n132: crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART compiler description says -O2;\n133: the actual recorded build commands use -O3. Neither contract was altered.\n134: \n135: Evidence directory:\n136: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r6s0-jalr-mivgf56j`\n137: \n138: It contains formal-output.log, unit-tests.log and all nine result XMLs,\n139: cosim-output.log, fpga-output.log, the three original nextpnr logs and routed\n140: netlists, jalr-premap.json, mapped-jalr-connectivity.json, timing-seed1.json/SDF,\n141: timing-arc-audit.json, workload-comparison.json, all four baseline/candidate\n142: benchmark logs, sixk/build.log, and verified-source-sha256.json. All launched\n143: validation commands have completed.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r9s0\n3: Implemented the requested combinational change. Local correctness gates pass,\n4: but the physical hypothesis regresses: median Fmax is 176.40 MHz and fitness\n5: 392.74, versus the reproduced baseline's 184.06 MHz and 409.80 (-4.16%).\n6: The predicted 3% improvement was not observed. The implementation is left\n7: for the orchestrator to evaluate; local correctness does not imply acceptance.\n26:   The load-address exclusion remains confined to divider launch.\n27: - Branch comparisons, full-width JALR addition, stores, RVFI and saved\n28:   divider operands still consume raw rs1/rs2. Arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas,\n29:   ALU operation/result cases, result-bank ownership, direct memory-address\n30:   register, reset/hold/flush priorities and all top-level nret=1 ports are\n41:   overrides, ADD/MUL in real and ALTOPS modes, every branch comparison,\n42:   full-width JALR carry/alignment cases, raw store/RVFI operands, and all\n43:   four DIV/REM operations with load readiness and saved forwarded operands.\n44:   Existing exact latency, held completion, result ownership, reset and\n45:   inactive-payload checks remain active.\n46: - Extended `test_pipeline.py` with producer distances, competing writers,\n47:   ALU/immediate/AUIPC/branch/JALR/store/DIV/REM consumers, x0, real load-use\n48:   dependencies, trap-suppressed writes, consecutive dependent divisions,\n49:   and 17-cycle dmem holds with overlapping imem stalls and retained WB data.\n50: - All 10 candidate pytest runners passed: 149 cocotb tests across real\n54:   baseline run; other modes compare. Baseline RTL was copied before edits\n55:   and selected through the existing test helper's RTL/build paths. Exact\n56:   comparison passed for 50 distinct bus schedules, 6,503 retirements,\n57:   1,599 accepted memory effects and 29,990 driven cycles. Every RVFI field,\n58:   retirement cycle, accepted memory cycle/data, final memory and driven\n73:   CoreMark ELF. No ISS/harness/evaluation files were edited.\n74: - Unchanged synthesis and FPGA evaluation scripts: all three routed seeds\n75:   completed successfully. Baseline seeds were independently rerun and\n76:   exactly reproduced the supplied winner.\n77: - `formal-deep` was not run: arithmetic is unchanged; real arithmetic was\n78:   exercised by unit tests, directed ISS checks and both CoreMark workloads.\n79: \n80: ## Routed results and mapped cones\n81: \n82: | Variant | Seed 1 MHz | Seed 2 MHz | Seed 3 MHz | Median MHz | Fitness | LUT4 | DFF | MULT36X36 | BSRAM |\n83: |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n84: | Original snapshot | 184.06 | 191.13 | 179.89 | 184.06 | 409.80 | 5490 | 2112 | 1 | 4 |\n85: | Direct enables | 173.07 | 176.40 | 187.97 | 176.40 | 392.74 | 5531 | 2091 | 1 | 4 |\n90: Both mapped designs still use 32 final LUT3 cells on each ALU operand,\n91: with mux INIT=0xB8; bit-0 cones retain a MUX2_LUT5 and LUT3 feeding that\n92: final mux. Direct RTL enables did not force a flat physical mux. The\n93: longest structural tag-to-ALU-input path remains five LUT/MUX cells for\n94: both operands. Graph traversal also retains the long ALU arithmetic and\n95: result-selection cone, and the seven-cell RF-to-ID read cone. Structural\n96: counts include primitive dependencies and are not timing measurements.\n97: \n98: Baseline seeds 1/3 are limited by EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding/JALR to\n99: IF PC[1], at 5.43/5.56 ns. Baseline seed 2 is limited by wrapper LFSR[20]\n100: through fetch/register-read selection into ID, at 5.23 ns.\n101: \n102: Candidate seed 1 is limited by RF x14[3] to ID at 5.78 ns (2.49 ns logic,\n103: 3.29 ns routing). Seeds 2/3 are limited by wrapper LFSR[20] through the\n104: fetch/register-read cone into ID at 5.67/5.32 ns (2.50/2.39 ns logic and\n105: 3.17/2.93 ns routing). Thus moving the original limiting cone did not\n106: improve the median timing result.\n107: \n108: Additional detailed-report routes reproduced the same three candidate\n109: frequencies. Inspected the normal ALU-result register endpoints as well:\n110: bits 2 and 11 have the largest native detailed-delay entries in seeds\n111: 1/2, and bit 5 in seed 3. Reports contain only 11 normal-result bits and\n112: one IF PC bit; unreported paths and missing technology arcs are unknown,\n113: not evidence of timing margin. Native detailed-delay fields are kept\n114: separate from the authoritative final Fmax/critical-path reports. Timing\n115: models and constraints were not modified. A readback-only attempt to\n116: analyze saved PNR JSON crashed in nextpnr's array2d assertion; generating\n117: reports by routing the original synth JSON succeeded reproducibly.\n123: **4,491,485 marker-window cycles** (start 15,801; stop 4,507,286). Its UART\n124: passed the existing validator: seedcrc=e9f5, list=e714, matrix=1fd7,\n125: state=8e3a, final=fcaf and `Correct operation validated.` No OOB access.\n126: This 2 KB result alone determines the fitness above.\n127: \n128: Separately built the requested TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, ITERATIONS=10, -O3\n129: binary in scratch and ran it on both variants with identical bus seeds.\n130: Both gave **32,836,671 marker-window cycles** (start 56,393; stop\n131: 32,893,064), seedcrc=8a02, list=d4b0, matrix=be52, state=5e47,\n132: final=5949, validation literal present, clean EBREAK and no OOB access.\n133: The 6 KB binary is not substituted into the evaluator or compared against\n134: the 2 KB binary. The UART's hardcoded compiler-flags string still says\n154: under `$TMPDIR/forwarding-r9s0/`; contract files were read or copied\n155: unchanged. Notes are at the explicitly requested worktree-root path.\n156: \n157: Evidence under that scratch directory includes `mapped-cones.json`,\n158: `physical-summary.json`, `routed-endpoint-review.json`,\n159: `coremark-full-trace-equivalence.json`, `pipeline-traces/`,\n160: `validation/{fpga-result,cosim-result,coremark-2k,coremark-6k}.json`,\n161: `validation/formal/last_run-30236.log`, the unit/baseline/candidate test\n162: XMLs, and each variant's `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/` reports.\n163: The full CoreMark streams were hashed while streaming, not retained.\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r5s1\n1: Implemented the pipeline-storage hypothesis. Local evaluation reports **409.80 fitness**, up **7.14%** from 382.48, with unchanged benchmark cycles. Median reported Fmax increases from 171.79 to **184.06 MHz**. Resources are **5,490 LUT4 and 2,112 DFF**, versus 5,513 LUT4 and 2,087 DFF. All local correctness gates passed. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\n2: \n3: Only `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv` and `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. Each retains its packed output representation but assigns disjoint control and payload fields in separate clocked processes. Every state bit has one procedural owner; Yosys `check` reports zero problems.\n4: \n8: \n9: There are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. Pipeline depth, dispatch timing, eight-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\n10: \n11: Tests retain all existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response-hold, ownership, ISS/RVFI, retirement-spacing, and accepted-memory-effect assertions. Invalid-cycle whole-bundle-zero comparisons in the EX tests were replaced with valid/control-zero checks; whole-bundle reset checks remain. Added coverage includes a direct ID-stage test and directed pipeline overlaps between memory holds and load-use, division, and redirects, plus unavailable fetches with distinct payloads. The EX test varies inactive normal data through computation and acceptance, then checks a prolonged owner hold and the first dependent consumer. To vary that bank during an active divide, the isolated EX test deliberately combines DIV with PC+4 selection; this is a synthetic interface stress vector. Integrated tests use legal decoded instructions and the unchanged ISS.\n12: \n18: - Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and evaluator CoreMark.\n19: - Additional C++ selftest cosimulation with both stall flags and seeds 1, 19, and 99: **PASS**, all 115 retirements compared against the unchanged ISS per run. Starting and final RTL also have identical retirement-cycle sequences for each seed.\n20: - Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: **PASS**, all three routed seeds and the UART, CRC, OOB, and marker gates.\n21: - Source verification confirms 154 staged source/contract files, all 53 formal PASS results, exact agreement between staged formal RTL and submitted RTL, and agreement between the starting RTL snapshot and HEAD. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\n22: \n23: The synthesized storage cones establish the intended structural change. In module-preserving Yosys `proc; opt` output, all ID payload flops changed from synchronous `reset OR flush` to reset alone, while retaining negative-polarity `stall` enables and direct payload data. EX's normal-result data cone previously included `div_accept`, `div_wait`, and incoming valid; its enable also included those decisions. The final data cone contains only ordinary ALU output, PC, and architectural result selection; its enable is only `!stall` and synchronous reset is only reset. Other EX payload fields likewise lose their bubble-zero data muxes. Saved-operand selection still uses `is_div`, as required for accepting metadata.\n24: \n25: The actual Gowin netlist has 160 unique surviving ID payload DFFRE cells, all with the common capture enable and `cpu.reset` reset. It has 190 unique surviving EX payload DFFR cells with `cpu.reset`; the FPGA wrapper ties memory ready high, so EX's memory-hold enable optimizes away. All 32 normal-result bits are directly registered, and the memory-address vector is identical to those register bits. Other output payload fields directly alias their stored fields; no replacement output-side bubble mux was added. The expected four BSRAMs and one MULT36X36 remain in every routed seed. Counts account for synthesis merging shared fields and pruning unused metadata.\n26: \n27: All three final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n28: \n29: | Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported worst path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n30: | --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n31: | 1 | 184.06 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.43 | 3.06 / 2.38 |\n32: | 2 | 191.13 | Wrapper instruction stimulus lfsr[20] to ID rs2_val[3] | 5.23 | 2.61 / 2.63 |\n33: | 3 | 179.89 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.56 | 3.06 / 2.50 |\n34: \n35: Figures are rounded independently by nextpnr. Tools are Yosys 0.64 (`d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61`) and nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`. The requested standalone lint used Verilator 5.047; the unit and cosimulation gates used the installed Verilator 5.046 with the Conda Python environment containing cocotb and YAML. The eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\n36: \n37: Timing remains a reported tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish complete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multiplier, or other forwarding paths have timing margin.\n38: \n39: The checked-in evaluator uses aggregate **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**, whereas the requested supplemental run uses aggregate **6 KB**. The evaluator and its expected CRCs remain unchanged. The separate 6 KB ELF was built from identical benchmark sources with the existing Makefile options `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`. Starting and final RTL were freshly simulated with the exact same ELF per size and identical `--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed.\n40: \n41: | Workload | Start / stop markers, both RTL versions | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Starting / final iter/sec |\n42: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n45: \n46: Rates use the recorded starting 171.79 MHz and measured final 184.06 MHz; starting FPGA routing was not rerun. Both versions reach EBREAK, report no OOB access, provide valid marker brackets, and produce identical UART output for each workload. Both include the literal `Correct operation validated.` and ten iterations. Evaluator CRCs are seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The separately verified **6 KB CRCs are seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART description says -O2; the actual compile commands use -O3.\n47: \n48: All build artifacts and scratch contract copies are under the configured TMPDIR:\n49: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r5s1-storage-78f91x4q`.\n50: Evidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, the nine result XMLs, `cosim-output.log`, `stalled-iss.json`, `fpga-output.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`, `storage-cones.json`, both premap JSONs, `workload-comparison.json`, individual baseline/candidate benchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. This notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5. All remaining changes are confined to bench RTL and test_*.py files.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
