{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(x) for x in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor r in rows:\n    if r.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n        print(r['id'], r.get('outcome'), r.get('delta_pct'), r['title'])\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-001-r1s0','hyp-20260906-001-r2s0','hyp-20260906-002-r7s1','hyp-20260906-001-r11s0']:\n    r=next(x for x in rows if x.get('id')==rid)\n    print('\\\\nRECORD',rid)\n    for k in ['hypothesis','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz','seeds']:\n        print(k, r.get(k))\n    notes=r.get('implementation_notes','')\n    if isinstance(notes,str):\n        print('notes:', notes[:14500] if rid.endswith('r2s0') else notes[-11000:] if rid.endswith('r11s0') else notes[:3500])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(x) for x in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor r in rows:\n    if r.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n        print(r['id'], r.get('outcome'), r.get('delta_pct'), r['title'])\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-001-r1s0','hyp-20260906-001-r2s0','hyp-20260906-002-r7s1','hyp-20260906-001-r11s0']:\n    r=next(x for x in rows if x.get('id')==rid)\n    print('\\\\nRECORD',rid)\n    for k in ['hypothesis','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz','seeds']:\n        print(k, r.get(k))\n    notes=r.get('implementation_notes','')\n    if isinstance(notes,str):\n        print('notes:', notes[:14500] if rid.endswith('r2s0') else notes[-11000:] if rid.endswith('r11s0') else notes[:3500])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-e113d0a improvement 0.0 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260906-001-r1s0 regression 0.73 Eliminate load-use bubbles caused by immediate bitfields\nhyp-20260906-001-r2s0 regression -5.98 Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\nhyp-20260906-001-r3s0 regression -35.87 Shorten divider carry chains with block carry-select subtraction\nhyp-20260906-001-r4s0 regression -37.58 Carry-free conditional sign correction at divider completion\nhyp-20260906-001-r5s0 regression -7.44 Balanced one-hot register-file reads with integrated write bypass\nhyp-20260906-001-r6s0 regression -8.89 Precompute JALR alignment before operand forwarding selection\nhyp-20260906-001-r7s0 regression -17.88 Align five-bit ALU control codes with instruction funct3\nhyp-20260906-001-r8s0 regression -12.99 Infer distributed register storage by resetting per-register validity\nhyp-20260906-001-r9s0 regression -4.16 Flatten EX operand selection with direct one-hot forwarding\nhyp-20260906-001-r10s0 regression -20.34 LUT-map JALR target bit one after operand forwarding\nhyp-20260906-001-r11s0 improvement 0.42 Remove launch qualification from idle divider operand storage\n\nRECORD hyp-20260906-001-r1s0\nhypothesis {'motivation': \"The baseline hazard unit compares a load destination with both raw instruction\\nfields [19:15] and [24:20], regardless of whether the following instruction\\nactually reads those registers. For example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by\\nADDI x6,x0,5 inserts a bubble because the immediate's low five bits look like\\nrs2=x5. Independent loads and instructions with no register operands can\\nsuffer the same false dependency. The bench history contains only the 282.82\\nbaseline and no prior attempt in this category; LESSONS.md is absent.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Add a small opcode-only source-use predecode to load-use detection. OP,\\nSTORE and BRANCH consume both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD and JALR consume rs1\\nonly; LUI, AUIPC, JAL, MISC-MEM and SYSTEM consume neither under this core's\\nexisting decoder contract. Keep conservative checks for unrecognized\\nopcodes. Stall only when a nonzero load destination matches a source that\\nthe following instruction consumes. In particular, all RV32M operations\\nstill consume both sources, and store-data dependencies still require\\nthe existing stall.\\n\\nThis removes one bubble per otherwise exposed false dependency without\\nchanging pipeline depth or adding a forwarding path. Use shallow opcode\\ncomparisons alongside the register comparisons, rather than routing the\\nfull validated decoder through the stall path. Preserve decoder legality,\\nreal load-use interlocks, redirect priority, dmem hold behavior, retained\\nMEM/WB forwarding data, and the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\\n\\nPredict a modest improvement in CoreMark iterations per cycle with nearly\\nunchanged Fmax. The estimated two-percent fitness gain is unmeasured;\\nfalse-hazard frequency and any extra delay on the stall path are the main\\nuncertainties. Validate with directed cycle-count and operand checks,\\nexisting formal and cosim gates, and the unchanged three-seed FPGA fitness\\nevaluation with canonical CoreMark CRCs and timing markers.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 2, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Pass if_id_w.instr[6:0] to a new opcode input on hazard_unit alongside\\nthe existing source fields. Preserve all top-level ports and existing\\npipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake connections.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Derive uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from the incoming opcode using the source\\nclasses in the hypothesis. Default unrecognized opcodes conservatively\\nto both sources used. Replace the unconditional two-field match with\\n(uses_rs1 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1) ||\\n(uses_rs2 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2), retaining id_ex_mem_read and the\\nnonzero-destination guard. Preserve every other stall, flush and hold\\nequation and its existing priority. Do not change instruction legality.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add directed zero-wait programs where a load destination aliases only\\nimmediate bits in ADDI, a shift-immediate, a following independent LOAD,\\nand LUI/AUIPC/JAL. Compare retirement spacing with equivalent nonaliasing\\ncases to prove removal of the spurious bubble, accounting for the\\nexisting JAL redirect behavior. Verify true rs1 and rs2 dependencies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert correct operands, memory effects, retirement PCs and consecutive\\nRVFI order values. Keep existing assertions and external cosim intact.\\n'}]}\nfitness 284.89\ncycles 4491485\nfmax_mhz 127.96\nseeds [123.75, 127.96, 132.24]\nnotes: # Implementation notes\n\nImplemented opcode-only source-use predecode for load-use detection in the\nbench core. OP (including all RV32M instructions), STORE, BRANCH, and unknown\nopcodes check both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR check rs1; LUI, AUIPC, JAL,\nMISC-MEM, and SYSTEM check neither. The nonzero load-destination guard remains.\nDecoder legality, pipeline depth, bus/redirect priorities, MEM/WB hold behavior,\nand the nret=1 top-level port set are unchanged.\n\n## Deviations required by validation\n\n- The first formal run passed 52 checks and failed `reg_ch0` at step 20.\n  Its counterexample retired a SYSTEM instruction with raw rs1=x4 immediately\n  after an LB to x4. Although SYSTEM consumes no registers, this core's RVFI\n  and external cosim report and check both raw source values. Eliminating the\n  bubble exposed the load address instead of its loaded value in RVFI.\n- `mem_stage.sv` now repairs these retirement metadata fields from the preceding\n  completed load's MEM/WB data. This path does not feed execution operands or\n  store data. Retained WB data remains eligible through memory holds.\n- EX/MEM forwarding excludes loads, whose result is unavailable at that stage.\n  Real consumers retain their interlock and existing MEM/WB forwarding. This\n  also preserves the old raw register value after a misaligned load that traps\n  without writing its destination; such loads are excluded from metadata repair.\n- A guarded `core_pkg.sv` include in `core.sv` fixes the baseline typedef-order\n  errors in the exact requested glob-based lint command. Bus-only hazard\n  equations were separated without changing their values, removing a Verilator\n  public-signal build's artificial combinational-cycle warning.\n\n## Directed tests\n\nThe existing pipeline assertions remain. The harness now supports deterministic\nimem/dmem waits, poisoned undelivered data, sparse jump targets, and stores\ncommitted only on a ready clock edge. Every program checks consecutive RVFI\norders, next-PC continuity, and both raw operand values against retired writes.\n\nAdded paired alias/nonalias timing checks for ADDI, all three immediate shifts,\nindependent LOAD, JALR immediate bits, LUI/AUIPC/JAL source-field aliases,\nFENCE/FENCE.I, illegal SYSTEM, EBREAK, and loads to x0. Independent consumers\nretire one cycle after the load, with the existing redirect bubble after jumps.\nTrue rs1/rs2 dependencies retain exactly one additional cycle, including store\naddress/data, taken/untaken branches, JALR, and both inputs of all eight RV32M\noperations. Unknown opcodes keep conservative stalls. Added misaligned-load and\ncombined-wait regressions, including retained MEM/WB forwarding while a branch\nredirect overlaps both bus stalls.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings.\n- Formal: PASS, **53 passed / 0 failed**, after **one fix attempt** (two complete\n  invocations). Used the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh`, `wrapper_si.sv`,\n  `checks_si.cfg`, and upstream checks, with ALTOPS and the original depths.\n- Cocotb: PASS, **85 tests** across all five existing runners, including\n  18 pipeline tests and the actual RV32M arithmetic vectors.\n- Negative control: the new ADDI and EBREAK timing tests both fail on the\n  original bench RTL, reporting a two-cycle load/consumer gap instead of one.\n- Unchanged `test/cosim/run_cosim.py` selftest: PASS, **115 retirements**.\n  Repeating the same field-by-field comparison with both `--istall --dstall`\n  also passes all 115 retirements.\n\nRECORD hyp-20260906-001-r2s0\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Move ALU result-select decoding across the existing ID/EX register,\\nwithout adding a pipeline stage. Add a 16-bit selector to id_ex_t:\\nbits 0 through 14 select the existing ALU_ADD through ALU_MULHSU results\\nusing their unchanged numeric operation constants, and bit 15 selects\\nthe shared DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU response. Generate these mutually exclusive\\nenables from dec_alu_op in ID and latch them with the instruction.\\nKeep the encoded operation for the divider's signedness, quotient versus\\nremainder choice, and distinct formal ALTOPS substitutions.\\n\\nIn EX, consume the registered enables directly. Implement ALU output\\nselection as masked result terms with a balanced OR reduction, avoiding\\na new priority chain or re-encoding followed by another binary decode.\\nUse selector bit 15, still qualified by in.valid and !is_illegal, for\\nthe existing is_div classification. The division result term retains\\nits existing div_resp_valid qualification. Keep every arithmetic\\nexpression, multiplier, divider recurrence, and ALTOPS formula intact.\\nThis is one control-path micro-optimization, not an arithmetic redesign.\\n\\nDerive selectors from the existing decoder output without changing its\\nlegality decisions. Preserve its default ADD selection even for illegal\\ninstructions; an unsupported internal ALU operation selects no result.\\nReset and flush clear the complete ID/EX bundle, while every hold retains\\nthe selectors with the instruction. Preserve the eight-compute-clock\\ndivider, terminal-edge acceptance, held response, operand snapshots,\\nload interlocks, forwarding, redirect priority, and nret=1 RVFI contract.\\nIn particular, add no completion cycle: the previous divider notes show\\nthat this would violate the unchanged bounded liveness check.\\n\\nExpect identical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and a modest Fmax gain\\nfrom removing binary operation decode from the measured EX paths. The\\nfive-percent fitness estimate is unmeasured. Extra selector fanout, OR\\nrouting, synthesis restructuring, and a newly dominant ID path could\\nerase the gain. Validate actual mapped timing and area with the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA evaluation against the current winner, and compare cycle\\ncounts using identical binaries and bus-stall settings. Run existing\\nlint, formal, arithmetic, pipeline, and cosim gates; retain the required\\n6 KB CRCs, UART validation, memory bounds, and timing markers. If saved\\nevaluator artifacts use a different working-set size, report those\\nmeasurements separately without altering the evaluation contract.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 5, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Define the 16 result-selector positions and add an alu_result_sel\\nfield only to id_ex_t. Keep the existing five-bit ALU operation\\nvalues, ctrl_t legality fields, downstream bundles, and RVFI widths.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Decode dec_alu_op into the specified result enables before the\\nID/EX register. Latch them with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold on stalls and clear on reset/flush. Include all four division\\noperations in the shared bit-15 class. Preserve decoder defaults\\nand avoid deriving selectors from unvalidated raw instruction bits.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Wire the registered selectors into alu and use their division-class\\nbit for is_div with the existing valid/legal guards. Preserve the\\nencoded divider operation, all request/response and stall equations,\\ncaptured operands, jump-result mux, traps, and EX/MEM acceptance timing.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': \"Accept the predecoded selector vector and replace the encoded-op\\noutput case with parallel masked result selection and a balanced OR\\ntree. Preserve all real arithmetic expressions and formal ALTOPS\\nformulas, including each multiply variant. Keep div_unit's encoded\\nop input and handshake unchanged; its result term still outputs zero\\nunless div_resp_valid. A zero selector vector produces zero output.\\n\"}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Update local stimulus helpers to drive the new selector input in\\nstep with every operation change, retaining every existing expected\\nvalue and timing assertion. Exercise all result classes, zero-hot\\nselection, rapid operation transitions, signed and unsigned multiply\\nhigh halves, and the existing division edge cases and held responses\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Keep shared test infrastructure intact.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add directed mixed-operation streams to verify that registered\\nselectors stay paired with operands across forwarding, load-use\\nbubbles, imem stalls, dmem holds, redirects, and blocking division.\\nCheck unchanged retirement spacing for ordinary ALU/multiply and\\nconsecutive divides, full RVFI operand/result and order consistency,\\nand absence of wrong-path effects. Retain all existing assertions.\\n'}]}\nfitness 350.35\ncycles 4491485\nfmax_mhz 157.36\nseeds [141.2, 164.83, 157.36]\nnotes: # hyp-20260906-001-r2s0: predecoded ALU result selection\n\nImplemented the proposed control change. All local correctness gates pass,\nbut the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation regresses median Fmax and\nthroughput by 5.98%. The measured result does not support the estimated gain.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`: added 16 named selector positions and a\n  16-bit `alu_result_sel` field only to `id_ex_t`. Positions 0 through 14\n  retain the numeric ADD through MULHSU operation values; position 15\n  selects the shared divider response. Encoded operations, `ctrl_t`,\n  downstream bundles, and RVFI widths are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv`: predecode comes exclusively from\n  `dec_alu_op`. Equality enables for operations 0 through 14 and a shared\n  DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU enable are latched with the existing ID/EX instruction\n  enable. Reset/flush clear the entire bundle and holds preserve it.\n  Illegal decoder outputs retain default ADD; unsupported internal operation\n  values match no selector. Decoder legality logic is untouched.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: passes the registered enables to the ALU\n  and uses bit 15 with the existing valid/legal guards for `is_div`.\n  Divider launch, acceptance, stall, forwarding, captured operands, jump\n  result selection, redirects, traps, and EX/MEM timing are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: replaced the encoded output case with 16 masked\n  result terms and explicit 8/4/2/1 balanced OR levels. Every arithmetic\n  expression, signed/unsigned multiplier expression, and ALTOPS formula is\n  preserved. The divider term remains qualified by `div_resp_valid`.\n  `div_unit.sv` is unchanged, including encoded operation selection and\n  terminal-edge response acceptance. A zero selector produces zero output.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: every operation drive now supplies its\n  selector. All original vectors and timing assertions remain. Added\n  randomized rapid transitions across all combinational classes, zero-hot\n  selection, unsupported operations, and switching result selection while\n  each type of divider response is held, in real and ALTOPS builds.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`: added dependent streams covering all\n  16 classes, exact one-clock ordinary/multiply and nine-clock divide\n  retirement spacing, and mixed streams with both bus stalls. The latter\n  compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and explicitly\n  require EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, selector/bundle\n  holds, redirects simultaneous with each bus stall, blocking division,\n  illegal-default ADD, and absence of wrong-path stores/divider requests.\n\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Four-bit selector-position\nconstants explicitly narrow the unchanged five-bit operation constants to\navoid Verilator array-index width warnings. No source outside the four RTL\nfiles and two allowed test files was changed, apart from this requested note.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- PASS: the exact requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` command, with no warnings.\n  The same lint also passes with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\n- PASS: unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, auto-selecting `nret: 1`,\n  `wrapper_si.sv`, and `checks_si.cfg`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, first formal\n  invocation, zero formal retries. Includes non-vacuous channel-0\n  retirement, liveness, uniqueness, register/PC routing, and M-ext ALTOPS.\n  No counterexample was produced. Full real-arithmetic formal-deep was\n  not run; multiplier/divider arithmetic was not redesigned.\n- PASS: all six pytest runners, **127 cocotb cases**: 32 real ALU, 32 ALTOPS\n  ALU, 30 decoder, 8 immediate, 20 pipeline, and 5 register-file tests.\n- PASS: unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` gate, two ELFs. Additional selftest\n  comparison against the unchanged Python ISS passed all 115 retirements\n  both zero-wait and with `--istall --dstall`.\n- PASS: both saved 2 KB and required 6 KB CoreMark binaries complete with\n  in-bounds memory, start/stop timing markers, 10 iterations, exact CRCs,\n  and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Both designs were run\n  locally against the same binary bytes and `--bench --istall --dstall`.\n\nAll generated files and copied gate infrastructure are under `$TMPDIR`,\nnot in protected worktree directories. The installed Miniconda Python was\nselected for cocotb, click, and YAML dependencies; the default Homebrew\nPython lacked cocotb/click. No dependency or gate files were edited.\n\n## Three-seed mapped comparison\n\nRan unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1` and\n`BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, using Yosys 0.64 and the existing nextpnr\nscript/constraints. Compared against the saved current divider winner,\nwhose formal-staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte equal to starting HEAD.\nThe winner's evaluator sources and FPGA constraints also match this tree.\n\n| Measurement | Current winner | This implementation |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Seed 1 routed Fmax, MHz | 165.32 | 141.20 |\n| Seed 2 routed Fmax, MHz | 174.03 | 164.83 |\n| Seed 3 routed Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\n| LUT4 | 5,438 | 5,509 |\n| DFF | 2,094 | 2,122 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 3 |\n| Saved evaluator 2 KB fitness, iter/s | 372.62 | 350.35 |\n\nAll three placements succeeded. Median frequency falls 5.98%; LUT4 rises\n1.31% and DFF rises 1.34%. Although the RTL multiplier expressions are\nidentical, the different selection network changes synthesis sharing and\nmaps three MULT36X36 cells instead of one. This is a material area concern.\n\nThe final candidate paths are 7.08, 6.07, and 6.36 ns for seeds 1, 2, and 3.\nThey start at `id_ex_w[46]` for seeds 1/3 and `[44]` for seed 2: registered\nMULHU and MUL selectors, respectively. All end at EX/MEM `alu_result[4]`\n(`ex_mem_w[235]`, `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_59`). Binary result decode was\nremoved, but the mapped result-selection paths remain critical and slower.\n\n## Working-set distinction and identical-binary cycle comparison\n\nThe repository's unchanged evaluator and default CoreMark Makefile use\n`TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`, despite the requested 6 KB contract. Its 350.35\niter/s result above is therefore a separate 2 KB measurement, not a 6 KB\nfitness claim. Neither evaluator constants nor benchmark sources were\nmodified to reconcile this discrepancy.\n\n| Binary / bus settings | Winner cycles | Candidate cycles | Start / stop cycles, both |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| Saved 2 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 15,801 / 4,507,286 |\n| Required 6 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56,393 / 32,893,064 |\n\nThe saved 6 KB build log confirms `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; its UART\ncompiler-flags string is a stale hardcoded `-O2` string. The reported\n`CoreMark Size: 2000` is per algorithm for this 6000-byte total working set.\nIts exact CRCs pass: seed `0x8a02`, list **`0xd4b0`**, matrix **`0xbe52`**,\nstate **`0x5e47`**, final `0x5949`. Combining its measured cycles with the\nthree-seed frequencies gives **50.97 -> 47.92 iter/s** for 6 KB, also -5.98%.\nThis separate calculation does not replace the unchanged evaluator result.\n\nBinary SHA-256 values used for both designs:\n\n- 2 KB: `54d433aa39f063a87e7ebc9697bb14dc3a6b855705812d18cbc5ead32cc155b3`\n- 6 KB: `7b52a15eb9a3de9a8fe8bf6e69abff8810cfe9753be9ee5ce94bbe509dba3955`\n\n`make fpga` rebuilt its own 2 KB ELF with different file metadata; every\nPT_LOAD address, size, and byte hash matches the saved 2 KB ELF, and its\nmeasured bracket is also exactly 4,491,485 cycles.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCandidate scratch root:\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-001-predecode-brw7_gc6/`\n\n- `formal-console.log` and `formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-33833/checks/`\n- `unit-tests.log` and `cores/bench/test/*_runner.result.xml`\n- `cosim-gate.log` and `selftest-comparison.json`\n- `fpga-eval.log`, `fpga-result.json`, `timing-paths.json`, and\n  `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`\n- `coremark-comparison.json` and `{winner,candidate}-{2k,6k}-coremark.log`\n\nSaved winner root: `$TMPDIR/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/`.\nThe five-percent gain remains unsupported; this implementation is correct\nunder the local gates but is a measured fitness regression.\n\n\nRECORD hyp-20260906-002-r7s1\nhypothesis {'motivation': \"Result selection is a recurring routed bottleneck in this core. The\\nr6s1 structural attempt removed live forwarding comparisons, but all\\nthree candidate seeds became limited by ID/EX alu_op to the normal\\nEX/MEM result, producing an 8.01% regression. In r2s0, multiply selector\\npaths remained critical and replacing the complete ALU case with\\none-hot masking increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. Meanwhile,\\nr2s1's separate divider completion bank and r5s1's independent payload\\nstorage improved fitness without changing CoreMark cycles. These results\\nmotivate separating multiply result ownership at the existing register\\nboundary. They do not establish that multiplier arithmetic limits the\\ncurrent 184.06 MHz winner, whose reported worst paths also include JALR\\nforwarding and register-file reads.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Add one parallel 32-bit multiply-result register at the existing EX/MEM\\nboundary. Retain the ordinary integer/link-result bank and the existing\\ndivider completion bank. Select among these registered owners after\\nEX/MEM using registered result-class metadata. This is the sole structural\\nchange: every RV32I and MUL instruction keeps its existing execution,\\nforwarding and retirement cycle, and DIV/REM keeps its current schedule.\\n\\nIn alu.sv, expose separate combinational integer and multiply results.\\nThe integer output case retains ADD through LUI and the current default\\nzero behavior, with no multiply term or multiply-result mux feeding it.\\nThe multiply output uses a compact encoded case for MUL, MULH, MULHU and\\nMULHSU, with zero as its default. Keep the existing signed/unsigned product\\nexpressions and exact real and ALTOPS results. Preserve synthesis sharing\\nwithin this multiply case; do not create individual per-operation product\\nregisters, replicate multipliers, or introduce the failed full-ALU one-hot\\nmasked-OR network. The core must not recombine these two outputs before\\ntheir EX/MEM registers.\\n\\nEX captures the integer result or PC+4 in its current normal bank and\\ncaptures the selected multiply result in the new bank on the same edge.\\nGive both banks synchronous reset and the existing !stall payload capture\\nenable. Capture inactive multiply data freely during bubbles, division\\ncomputation and other instructions; validity, multiply recognition and\\ndivide acceptance must not enter either bank's data-zero or enable cone.\\nPreserve the winner's separation of payload from bubble/flush controls.\\n\\nRegister a multiply-owner bit alongside the existing divider-owner bit.\\nReset and an accepted bubble clear ownership, a real memory hold retains\\nit, and an accepted legal multiply selects the new bank. Preserve PC+4\\npriority for a jump and the existing divide-accept priority. The bits\\nmust be mutually exclusive; ordinary instructions select the normal bank.\\nReconstruct out.alu_result with one small class selection using only\\nregistered selectors and registered data. Both existing EX/MEM forwarding\\nand MEM/WB capture consume that same selected value. There must be no\\nadditional completion edge, live opcode qualification, or extra consumer\\nbubble. Consecutive multiplies and immediate dependent ALU, branch, JALR,\\nload-address, store-data, multiply and divide consumers retain their\\nexisting schedules and exact post-forward RVFI operands.\\n\\nKeep mem_addr directly connected to the normal registered integer/link\\nbank. Loads and stores always own that bank; the multiply/divide output\\nselection must never enter the dmem address, lane-selection or alignment\\npath. A memory hold freezes all EX/MEM banks, selectors and metadata so\\nits request remains stable. Preserve the existing divider bank lifetime,\\neight compute clocks, nine-clock consecutive-divide dispatch spacing,\\nsaved divide operands and reset cancellation. MEM/WB must continue to\\nretain its forwarding payload and reg_write control when a memory hold\\nclears retirement valid. No hazard or retirement policy changes are needed.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is a smaller integer-result selection cone and\\nindependent placement of multiply capture, at a cost of about 32 data\\nflops and one ownership bit before synthesis. Predict four percent higher\\nfitness at unchanged marker-bracketed cycles, with low confidence. The\\nextra registered-bank selection can lengthen result-to-forwarding paths;\\nJALR, register-file timing or placement may still dominate. Verify the\\nmapped separation, one MULT36X36 and four block RAMs, then inspect all\\nthree routed seeds including bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB paths. Prior notes\\nidentify missing timing arcs on result-bank muxes: report any continuing\\ncoverage gap explicitly and never treat an omitted arc as zero delay or\\na frequency gain. Leave timing models, constraints and evaluation intact.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1, the complete external IO, decoder legality, all traps,\\nordered RVFI and every existing correctness gate. Run real and ALTOPS\\narithmetic and bank-ownership tests, unchanged formal including liveness,\\nstalled ISS cosimulation, and the three-seed FPGA fitness evaluation.\\nCompare retirement timing and marker cycles with starting RTL using\\nidentical binaries and stall schedules. Verify UART validation, bounds,\\nmarkers and canonical 6 KB CRCs. Prior implementation notes distinguish\\nan evaluator 2 KB workload from the supplemental 6 KB run; keep results\\nseparately labeled and do not modify benchmark or evaluator files.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 4, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Separate the ordinary integer output from a dedicated combinational\\nmultiply output. Preserve all arithmetic expressions, the compact\\nencoded multiply case and DSP sharing, every ALTOPS formula, and the\\nexisting divider interfaces. Remove multiplication from the output\\nconsumed by the normal EX/MEM bank.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': \"Add the parallel multiply-result payload register and registered\\nmultiply ownership. Capture it with synchronous reset and !stall,\\nindependent of validity or completion. Select registered integer,\\nmultiply or divider banks for out.alu_result at the existing cycle;\\npreserve jump-link priority, bubble/hold rules, divider ownership,\\nmetadata, and the unselected normal bank's direct mem_addr connection.\\n\"}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Route existing multiply checks to the new multiply output without\\nremoving any exact-result vector. Check all four real and ALTOPS\\nmultiply operations, signedness and high-half boundaries, random\\noperands and rapid operation transitions against independent expected\\narithmetic. Retain every integer and divider result/protocol assertion.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_ex_result_banks.py', 'description': 'Extend ownership tests to distinguish integer, multiply and divider\\nbanks. Cover distinct consecutive multiply results, all class\\ntransitions, inactive payload capture, synchronous reset, bubbles,\\nprolonged holds, jump-link priority and existing divide completion\\ntiming in real and ALTOPS modes. Verify the selected result and its\\nmetadata stay coherent while the direct memory-address bank remains\\nindependent of multiply/divide selection.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add mixed multiply producer/consumer streams across both forwarding\\nages, repeated destinations, x0, load-produced operands and dependent\\nALU, branch, JALR, memory and M-extension instructions. Exercise both\\nbus stalls, valid-cleared WB forwarding, redirects and reset. Retain\\nISS/RVFI comparisons and accepted-memory-effect checks; compare with\\nstarting RTL to assert unchanged instruction spacing and no added\\nmultiply latency or consumer bubble.\\n'}]}\nfitness 382.21\ncycles 4491485\nfmax_mhz 171.67\nseeds [175.25, 171.67, 167.56]\nnotes: # Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\n\nImplemented the proposed structural change. Correctness and cycle comparisons pass. The unchanged FPGA evaluator reports a regression: median 171.67 MHz and fitness 382.21, versus the starting core's recorded 184.06 MHz (6.73% lower at identical cycles). The predicted 4% improvement was not observed. The starting frequency is the recorded value in core.yaml, not a new starting-RTL P&R measurement.\n\nStarting HEAD: `53c327aef09bbb852fbebae9b62adc224456ce3e`.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: retained `out` as the integer-only combinational output, with ADD through LUI and default zero. Added `mul_out` with the compact encoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU case and default zero. Original signed/unsigned product expressions and all ALTOPS formulas are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: added one 32-bit `mul_result_q` and one `mul_result_sel_q`. Integer/link and multiply payloads capture independently on the existing `!stall` edge with synchronous reset, without validity, ownership, or divide-accept gating. Legal accepted multiplies acquire ownership; jump-link priority and divide-accept priority are preserved. Reset and accepted bubbles clear both selectors; holds retain them. One encoded selection of registered banks drives the existing forwarding and MEM/WB result consumers. `mem_addr` remains wired directly to `reg_q.alu_result`.\n- Extended the three requested test files. Other RTL, decoder legality, nret=1, IO, divider implementation, MEM/WB hold behavior, hazards, retirement, evaluation scripts, benchmark sources, timing models and constraints are unchanged. No extra multiply edge or consumer bubble was added.\n\n## Correctness and timing comparisons\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- Unmodified `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including liveness, cover, unique, illegal and every instruction check. First gate attempt passed; no RTL fix/retry was needed. Used `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`; nret=1 automatically selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/config. A private copy of the formal framework under the supplied TMPDIR kept generated checks away from the shared framework. The installed conda Python supplied click, YAML and cocotb missing from the default Python environment.\n- All nine pytest runners passed: **151 cocotb cases** across ALU real/ALTOPS (34 each), EX ownership real/ALTOPS (7 each), pipeline (25), decoder (30), ID payload (1), immediate generation (8), and register file (5). Two ownership tests were strengthened after the full run to require distinct consecutive products and synchronous reset of an asserted multiply-owner bit, then passed again in both arithmetic modes.\n- Arithmetic coverage retains every existing integer/divider vector and protocol assertion. Added cross-products of signedness/high-half boundaries, 256 additional random operand pairs, rapid opcode/class/default transitions, and zero on each inactive ALU output. Ownership coverage includes all integer/multiply/divider/bubble class transitions; inactive captures under invalid, illegal and jump stimuli; poisoned inactive multiply inputs during divide computation; prolonged holds; saved divide operands; terminal acceptance; reset; and link priority.\n- Mixed pipeline streams cover all four multiply operations, both forwarding ages, repeated destinations, x0, load operands, dependent ALU, branch, JALR, load/store address, \n\nRECORD hyp-20260906-001-r11s0\nhypothesis {'motivation': \"Divider launch qualification still controls wide operand storage in the\\ncurrent winner. In ex_stage.sv, div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q capture only on\\ndiv_req_valid && div_req_ready; in div_unit.sv, the same handshake gates\\ninitialization of quotient/remainder, divisor, dividend and sign metadata.\\nThis connects forwarding-tag comparisons and load-availability decisions\\nto many register enables and data-selection cones. The r7s0 implementation\\nnotes report forwarding-to-quotient_q critical paths in two seeds, and\\nr10s1 reports a forwarding/load-availability path to div_rs2_q CE. These\\nare observed bottlenecks in rejected candidates, not proof that they are\\nthe current winner's worst paths.\\n\\nThe accepted r5s1 change improved fitness 7.14% by separating pipeline\\npayload capture from transaction control. Apply that principle narrowly\\nto the remaining divider operand banks. This avoids repeating the failed\\ndivider arithmetic rewrites, ALU recoding, and JALR timing experiments.\\nNo instruction should gain or lose a cycle; any fitness gain must come\\nfrom a higher three-seed median Fmax.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Separate divider control ownership from idle operand capture, without\\nadding registers, pipeline stages, or an execution shortcut. The sole\\noptimization is to remove request qualification from operand-storage\\nenables and source selection. An unowned operand bank may change freely;\\nthe actual request edge must still capture that edge's exact operands.\\n\\nIn div_unit.sv, retain the existing equations and sequential transitions\\nfor req_ready, resp_valid, busy_q, count_q, held_q and result_q. Put the\\narithmetic storage in disjoint clocked processes with synchronous reset.\\nOn every edge whose pre-edge busy_q is zero, capture the existing magnitude\\nprefix into quotient_q/remainder_q and capture divisor_q, dividend_q and\\nall four operation/sign flags, regardless of req_valid or held_q. While\\nbusy_q is one, retain those operand/flag registers; update quotient_q and\\nremainder_q only on the existing seven !final_cycle recurrence edges.\\nHold Q/R on the eighth, sign-correction edge. In ALTOPS mode, capture the\\nunchanged selected subtraction/XOR formula into alt_result_q on each idle\\nedge and retain it while busy. Give every register exactly one owner.\\n\\nA request can only be accepted while busy_q is zero, so the same edge\\ninitializes its operands and sets busy_q. Idle captures never start an\\noperation or advance the count. A held response remains in result_q and\\ndoes not depend on the now-unowned arithmetic bank; consequently idle\\ncaptures are also safe while held_q is one. Preserve simultaneous held\\nresponse consumption and next-request acceptance, and leave result_q\\nunchanged except for reset and the existing terminal capture.\\n\\nIn ex_stage.sv, retain div_active_q's exact launch/accept state machine\\nbut split its RVFI operand snapshots into a separate process. After reset,\\ncapture forwarded rs1/rs2 on every edge with !div_active_q, without using\\nopcode, load availability, request/ready, stall, or valid as capture\\nqualifiers. Once active, retain both snapshots through computation,\\nblocked response, and the acceptance edge. EX/MEM samples the old snapshots\\non that edge before ownership clears. Later idle snapshot updates cannot\\nalter an already-captured EX/MEM owner or the divider's result bank.\\n\\nPreserve all real arithmetic, corner cases, eight-clock response timing,\\nnine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, request qualification, load-use\\nprotection, forwarding priority, zero-control bubbles, memory holds,\\ntraps, and nret=1 RVFI behavior. Keep the normal registered memory-address\\nbank and multiplier unchanged. Extra idle switching is a tradeoff, and\\nbusy_q fanout or unrelated routing may erase the predicted timing gain.\\n\\nValidate exact accepted operands, results and retirement cycles against\\nthe starting winner under identical programs and bus schedules. Retain\\nthe existing lint, real/ALTOPS unit, formal and stalled cosim gates and\\ncanonical 6 KB CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. Compare the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA fitness using identical binaries and unrounded\\niterations/cycle; keep any existing evaluator working-set discrepancy\\nexplicitly separate. Inspect mapped D/CE cones to confirm that request\\nqualification disappeared from these banks, and report all three routed\\ncritical paths without inferring timing margin from missing arcs.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 3, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/div_unit.sv', 'description': 'Split protocol/result state from arithmetic operand storage. Capture\\nprefix Q/R, normalized divisor, raw dividend and flags on !busy_q;\\nadvance Q/R on busy_q && !final_cycle and hold on final_cycle. Apply\\nthe same idle-capture ownership rule to alt_result_q. Preserve every\\narithmetic expression, reset value, request/response transition and\\nresult_q write condition, including held-response refill behavior.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Give div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q a separate synchronous-reset process with\\ncapture enable !div_active_q and the existing forwarded rs1/rs2 D\\ninputs. Leave div_active_q and all request/accept/wait equations\\nunchanged. Retain snapshots for the complete active transaction and\\npreserve EX/MEM metadata selection, stalls and result-bank ownership.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Retain all arithmetic, prefix-state, exact-latency, reset and held-bank\\nchecks. Add transactions preceded by changing idle operands/opcodes\\nwith req_valid low, followed by different operands on the actual\\nrequest edge. Exercise changed live inputs while busy, idle captures\\nbeneath an unconsumed response, consume/refill, and reset at request,\\nseventh-recurrence and terminal edges in real and ALTOPS modes. Check\\nthat no idle capture produces a response or changes result_q.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_ex_result_banks.py', 'description': 'Extend ownership tests with changing idle forwarded values, delayed\\nlaunch from load unavailability and memory holds, then distinct\\naccepted operands from each forwarding source. Check exact RVFI\\nsnapshots through busy, blocked completion and acceptance, including\\nan EX/MEM divide owner held after div_active_q clears while idle\\nsnapshots change. Preserve all existing result and direct-address\\nassertions in both arithmetic modes.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add directed mixed streams with load/divide producers, competing\\nforwarding writers, retained WB operands, back-to-back DIV/REM and\\nimmediate consumers. Exercise bus stalls, redirects and reset around\\nlaunch/completion. Compare full RVFI, accepted memory effects and\\nretirement-cycle sequences with the unchanged ISS and starting RTL;\\nrequire unchanged ordinary and divide spacing and no extra request\\nor retirement from idle operand capture. Keep all existing checks.\\n'}]}\nfitness 411.53\ncycles 4491485\nfmax_mhz 184.84\nseeds [190.33, 184.84, 168.69]\nnotes: # Idle divider operand storage\n\nImplemented the hypothesis against starting winner `8357476ebbe4f81ec39bae4793d68a4eef9d0eac` (`hyp-20260906-002-r5s1`). Local correctness gates pass. The unchanged three-seed evaluator improves median Fmax from 184.06 to 184.84 MHz, or **0.423774856%**, with identical execution cycles.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv`: separated protocol/result ownership from the arithmetic storage, with synchronous reset and exactly one clocked owner per register. On pre-edge `!busy_q`, Q/R capture the existing four-bit magnitude prefix, and divisor, dividend, and all four flags capture current inputs regardless of request validity or a held response. Q/R advance on the existing seven recurrence edges and hold on the eighth correction edge. ALTOPS captures the unchanged selected subtraction/XOR expression on every idle edge and holds while busy.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: retained the launch/accept state machine verbatim; moved RVFI snapshots to their own synchronous-reset process enabled only by `!div_active_q`. Snapshots retain the accepted operands through blocked completion and the acceptance edge, when EX/MEM samples their old values.\n- No arithmetic expressions, state declarations, pipeline stages, multiplier, normal memory-address bank, request qualification, forwarding priority, result-bank write conditions, or top-level ports changed. `nret` remains 1. Idle captures do not claim ownership, advance count, or produce responses.\n\nNo architectural deviation was required. The old ALU test assertion that arithmetic Q/R remained owned after completion was replaced with an assertion of the requested idle prefix capture. All response/result-bank stability assertions and all arithmetic, prefix, latency, reset, bubble, direct-address, and retirement checks remain.\n\n## Tests and equivalence\n\n- Requested Verilator lint passes with no warnings/errors, in real and ALTOPS modes.\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, using unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config and ALTOPS. Environment: `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`, with the installed conda Python on PATH. The first configured proof run passed; no RTL formal fixes or retries were needed. The initial system-Python setup could not run the gate, so validation used the existing conda installation. Formal was staged under `$TMPDIR` to leave contract files untouched. Real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; arithmetic expressions are unchanged, with real unit, ISS, and full-trace coverage below.\n- All nine pytest runners pass: real ALU 34 cocotb tests, ALTOPS ALU 34, real EX 5, ALTOPS EX 5, decoder 30, ID 1, immediate generator 8, pipeline 24, regfile 5; **146 cocotb tests total**.\n- Added changing idle operands/opcodes, exact different request-edge operands, busy input/request poisoning, captures beneath blocked responses, simultaneous consume/refill, and request/seventh-recurrence/terminal reset checks. EX tests cover all nine forwarding-source combinations, load-unavailable launch delays, memory holds, active snapshot retention, and a held EX/MEM divide owner surviving changing idle snapshots after ownership clears.\n- Added mixed pipeline streams with competing writers, all operand forwarding sources, retained WB load data, dependent and consecutive DIV/REM, immediate consumers, bus waits, redirects, and reset at request, recurrence, terminal, and completed-owner boundaries. Coverage assertions require the intended forwarding and overlap events. Initial test-only assertions were corrected on the starting RTL to include deliberate bus-hold cycles and avoid asking an aborted pre-launch scenario to consume a later hold schedule.\n- The identical protocol suite runs on the starting and candidate RTL. Optional `PIPELINE_TRACE_MODE=record` / `compare`, sharing `PIPELINE_TRACE_DIR`, records complete valid RVFI, bus schedules and accepted effects, accepted divide operands, dispatch results, resets, and cycle numbers. **34 scenarios, 28,706 sampled edges/events, 2,450 launches, and 5,814 retirements match exactly.** Each scenario also passes the unchanged Python ISS comparison. Existing one-clock ordinary/consumer and nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing checks pass; launch-to-accept remains eight clocks.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim.run_cosim` passes on both versions, including selftest and the evaluator's CoreMark configuration. Selftest additionally passes the unchanged ISS with both buses stalled using seeds 1, 2, 3, 19, and 97; all 115 retirements and their cycles match the starting RTL for each schedule.\n- The complete canonical 6 KB stalled CoreMark RVFI streams were compared byte for byte: **22,093,920 retirements and 6,463,986,468 bytes**, including every emitted retirement cycle and operand/result/memory field. No difference. Shared stream SHA256: `34ef03d244b986a66be40660efea1b01b6705ecbf1263bf493d109b7184ac4a0`.\n\n## FPGA evaluation and working-set discrepancy\n\nBoth versions used unchanged synthesis, wrapper, constraints, nextpnr script, seeds 1/2/3, and evaluator. All six routes completed. `make fpga TARGET=bench` used `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`. The starting RTL reproduces the winner's recorded median and resource counts.\n\n| Metric | Starting | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 190.33 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 191.13 | 184.84 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 179.89 | 168.69 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 184.84 |\n| Evaluator bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Exact evaluator iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,491,485 | 10 / 4,491,485 |\n| Evaluator fitness, iterations/s | 409.7976504430049 | 411.5342698461644 |\n| LUT4 | 5,490 | 5,485 |\n| Mapped FF | 2,112 | 2,119 |\n\nThe evaluator still builds **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000**, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and validates its existing 2 KB CRC table. This is a pre-existing discrepancy from the requested 6 KB contract; no evaluator or benchmark source was changed. Its unrounded iteration rate is `2.2264351322558128e-6`, not the rounded JSON field `2e-6`. Both versions ran the identical ELF, SHA256 `9b7d11c69a8a2b0db598fa2156474184488a3b2d22017f3fac05e6113ec74133`.\n\nSeparately built and validated the requested **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10** configuration from unchanged sources, using the same ELF for both versions (SHA256 `f224290c528bdbc3caa7badb285d6b6ae1fab75523bde60110cefe80de23a9e4`). Both runs have:\n\n- Start marker cycle 56,393; stop marker cycle 32,893,064; **32,836,671 bracketed cycles**; final EBREAK cycle 32,900,232; no OOB access.\n- Exact iteration rate `10 / 32836671 = 3.0453757020618807e-7`.\n- CRCs `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, `seedcrc=0x8a02`, `crcfinal=0x5949`, and the literal `Correct operation validated.` UART message. UART and completion records match exactly.\n- Using the same routed medians gives separate 6 KB fitness **56.053185172150975 -> 56.290724476911805 iterations/s**. These numbers are not substituted into the unchanged evaluator's output.\n\nThe unchanged UART metadata reports `CoreMark Size: 2000` for the 6 KB run's per-algorithm share, and a hardcoded `-O2` compiler string. The actual build command used `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; the canonical 6 KB CRCs identify the configuration.\n\n## Mapped D/CE cones\n\nInspected both mapped `synth.json` netlists, stopping combinational traversal at registered sources. Candidate enables were also evaluated over all busy/active/count combinations (320 mapped CE truth-table checks):\n\n- EX snapshots: `CE = !div_active_q`, one registered leaf, replacing a launch-qualified cone with 28 leaves.\n- Divisor, dividend, and four flags: `CE = !busy_q`, one registered leaf, replacing 28-leaf launch-qualified cones.\n- Q/R: `CE = !(busy_q && count_q == 7)`, four registered leaves, replacing 31-leaf qualified cones. Their D cones now select idle prefix versus recurrence using busy ownership and have no `div_active_q`/`held_q` request-state dependency. Arithmetic and forwarding data dependencies remain as intended.\n- `result_q` retains its four-leaf busy/count terminal enable. No idle-bank enable depends on held response ownership, opcode validity, load availability, request, or ready. Synchronous reset remains separate from these enables.\n\nNo RTL register was added, but mapping reports seven more physical FFs: two in each EX/MEM and MEM/WB register group and three under the register-file mapping. This physical resource increase is explicitly retained in the result rather than claiming an unchanged FF count.\n\n## All reported routed critical paths\n\nTimes below are nextpnr's printed path totals, rounded by that tool; Fmax above uses its final post-route frequency report. Cell names are the actual mapped endpoints, with architectural aliases verified against the mapped netlist.\n\n| Version/seed | Reported path | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Starting 1 | EX/MEM `rd[0]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.43 | 3.06 | 2.38 |\n| Starting 2 | wrapper `lfsr[20]` -> decode/register-read selection -> ID/EX `rs2_val[3]` | 5.23 | 2.61 | 2.63 |\n| Starting 3 | EX/MEM `rd[0]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.56 | 3.06 | 2.50 |\n| Candidate 1 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` -> normal ALU result selection -> normal address/result `[23]` | 5.25 | 2.35 | 2.90 |\n| Candidate 2 | MEM/WB `rd[1]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.41 | 2.86 | 2.55 |\n| Candidate 3 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` -> normal ALU result selection -> normal address/result `[23]` | 5.93 | 2.35 | 3.58 |\n\nExact candidate endpoints:\n\n- Seeds 1 and 3: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_162.Q` -> `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_72.D`.\n- Seed 2: `cpu.u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_133.Q` -> `cpu.u_if.pc_DFFRE_Q.D`.\n\nExact starting endpoints:\n\n- Seeds 1 and 3: `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_22.Q` -> `cpu.u_if.pc_DFFRE_Q.D`.\n- Seed 2: `lfsr_DFFC_Q_10.Q` -> `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_124.D`.\n\nThese are the modeled paths reported by nextpnr. The absence of a divider endpoint does not establish divider timing margin or coverage of missing/unmodeled arcs. The small median gain cannot be attributed conclusively to a particular removed path; seed 3 regresses, and the two ALU-control paths differ substantially in routing delay. Additional idle switching is an explicit tradeoff and was not power-measured.\n\n## Evidence location\n\nAll scratch builds and evidence are under `$TMPDIR/divider-idle-r11s0/` (the run's existing `.tmp` directory), including `candidate/formal-local.log`, `candidate/units.log`, `candidate/remaining-tests.log`, `protocol-reference/`, `cosim-validation.json`, `sixk-validation.json`, `sixk-full-trace.json`, `fitness-comparison.json`, `mapped-cones.json`, both `fpga-eval.log` files, and all six extracted `*-critical-seed*.txt` reports. Contract files and other cores were not edited.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
