false dependency. The bench history contains only the 282.82\\nbaseline and no prior attempt in this category; LESSONS.md is absent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline hazard unit compares a load destination with both raw instruction\\nfields [19:15] and [24:20], regardless of whether the following instruction\\nactually reads those registers. For example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by\\nADDI x6,x0,5 inserts a bubble because the immediate's low five bits look like\\nrs2=x5. Independent loads and instructions with no register operands can\\nsuffer the same false dependency. The bench history contains only the 282.82\\nbaseline and no prior attempt in this category; LESSONS.md is absent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small opcode-only source-use predecode to load-use detection. OP,\\nSTORE and BRANCH consume both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD and JALR consume rs1\\nonly; LUI, AUIPC, JAL, MISC-MEM and SYSTEM consume neither under this core's\\nexisting decoder contract. Keep conservative checks for unrecognized\\nopcodes. Stall only when a nonzero load destination matches a source that\\nthe following instruction consumes. In particular, all RV32M operations\\nstill consume both sources, and store-data dependencies still require\\nthe existing stall.\\n\\nThis removes one bubble per otherwise exposed false dependency without\\nchanging pipeline depth or adding a forwarding path. Use shallow opcode\\ncomparisons alongside the register comparisons, rather than routing the\\nfull validated decoder through the stall path. Preserve decoder legality,\\nreal load-use interlocks, redirect priority, dmem hold behavior, retained\\nMEM/WB forwarding data, and the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\\n\\nPredict a modest improvement in CoreMark iterations per cycle with nearly\\nunchanged Fmax. The estimated two-percent fitness gain is unmeasured;\\nfalse-hazard frequency and any extra delay on the stall path are the main\\nuncertainties. Validate with directed cycle-count and operand checks,\\nexisting formal and cosim gates, and the unchanged three-seed FPGA fitness\\nevaluation with canonical CoreMark CRCs and timing markers.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass if_id_w.instr[6:0] to a new opcode input on hazard_unit alongside\\nthe existing source fields. Preserve all top-level ports and existing\\npipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from the incoming opcode using the source\\nclasses in the hypothesis. Default unrecognized opcodes conservatively\\nto both sources used. Replace the unconditional two-field match with\\n(uses_rs1 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1) ||\\n(uses_rs2 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2), retaining id_ex_mem_read and the\\nnonzero-destination guard. Preserve every other stall, flush and hold\\nequation and its existing priority. Do not change instruction legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed zero-wait programs where a load destination aliases only\\nimmediate bits in ADDI, a shift-immediate, a following independent LOAD,\\nand LUI/AUIPC/JAL. Compare retirement spacing with equivalent nonaliasing\\ncases to prove removal of the spurious bubble, accounting for the\\nexisting JAL redirect behavior. Verify true rs1 and rs2 dependencies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert correct operands, memory effects, retirement PCs and consecutive\\nRVFI order values. Keep existing assertions and external cosim intact.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass if_id_w.instr[6:0] to a new opcode input on hazard_unit alongside\\nthe existing source fields. Preserve all top-level ports and existing\\npipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from the incoming opcode using the source\\nclasses in the hypothesis. Default unrecognized opcodes conservatively\\nto both sources used. Replace the unconditional two-field match with\\n(uses_rs1 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1) ||\\n(uses_rs2 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2), retaining id_ex_mem_read and the\\nnonzero-destination guard. Preserve every other stall, flush and hold\\nequation and its existing priority. Do not change instruction legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed zero-wait programs where a load destination aliases only\\nimmediate bits in ADDI, a shift-immediate, a following independent LOAD,\\nand LUI/AUIPC/JAL. Compare retirement spacing with equivalent nonaliasing\\ncases to prove removal of the spurious bubble, accounting for the\\nexisting JAL redirect behavior. Verify true rs1 and rs2 dependencies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert correct operands, memory effects, retirement PCs and consecutive\\nRVFI order values. Keep existing assertions and external cosim intact.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.89, \"delta_pct\": 0.73, \"vs_baseline\": 0.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10386, \"ff\": 1877, \"seeds\": [123.75, 127.96, 132.24], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation notes\\n\\nImplemented opcode-only source-use predecode for load-use detection in the\\nbench core. OP (including all RV32M instructions), STORE, BRANCH, and unknown\\nopcodes check both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR check rs1; LUI, AUIPC, JAL,\\nMISC-MEM, and SYSTEM check neither. The nonzero load-destination guard remains.\\nDecoder legality, pipeline depth, bus/redirect priorities, MEM/WB hold behavior,\\nand the nret=1 top-level port set are unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations required by validation\\n\\n- The first formal run passed 52 checks and failed `reg_ch0` at step 20.\\n  Its counterexample retired a SYSTEM instruction with raw rs1=x4 immediately\\n  after an LB to x4. Although SYSTEM consumes no registers, this core's RVFI\\n  and external cosim report and check both raw source values. Eliminating the\\n  bubble exposed the load address instead of its loaded value in RVFI.\\n- `mem_stage.sv` now repairs these retirement metadata fields from the preceding\\n  completed load's MEM/WB data. This path does not feed execution operands or\\n  store data. Retained WB data remains eligible through memory holds.\\n- EX/MEM forwarding excludes loads, whose result is unavailable at that stage.\\n  Real consumers retain their interlock and existing MEM/WB forwarding. This\\n  also preserves the old raw register value after a misaligned load that traps\\n  without writing its destination; such loads are excluded from metadata repair.\\n- A guarded `core_pkg.sv` include in `core.sv` fixes the baseline typedef-order\\n  errors in the exact requested glob-based lint command. Bus-only hazard\\n  equations were separated without changing their values, removing a Verilator\\n  public-signal build's artificial combinational-cycle warning.\\n\\n## Directed tests\\n\\nThe existing pipeline assertions remain. The harness now supports deterministic\\nimem/dmem waits, poisoned undelivered data, sparse jump targets, and stores\\ncommitted only on a ready clock edge. Every program checks consecutive RVFI\\norders, next-PC continuity, and both raw operand values against retired writes.\\n\\nAdded paired alias/nonalias timing checks for ADDI, all three immediate shifts,\\nindependent LOAD, JALR immediate bits, LUI/AUIPC/JAL source-field aliases,\\nFENCE/FENCE.I, illegal SYSTEM, EBREAK, and loads to x0. Independent consumers\\nretire one cycle after the load, with the existing redirect bubble after jumps.\\nTrue rs1/rs2 dependencies retain exactly one additional cycle, including store\\naddress/data, taken/untaken branches, JALR, and both inputs of all eight RV32M\\noperations. Unknown opcodes keep conservative stalls. Added misaligned-load and\\ncombined-wait regressions, including retained MEM/WB forwarding while a branch\\nredirect overlaps both bus stalls.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings.\\n- Formal: PASS, **53 passed / 0 failed**, after **one fix attempt** (two complete\\n  invocations). Used the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh`, `wrapper_si.sv`,\\n  `checks_si.cfg`, and upstream checks, with ALTOPS and the original depths.\\n- Cocotb: PASS, **85 tests** across all five existing runners, including\\n  18 pipeline tests and the actual RV32M arithmetic vectors.\\n- Negative control: the new ADDI and EBREAK timing tests both fail on the\\n  original bench RTL, reporting a two-cycle load/consumer gap instead of one.\\n- Unchanged `test/cosim/run_cosim.py` selftest: PASS, **115 retirements**.\\n  Repeating the same field-by-field comparison with both `--istall --dstall`\\n  also passes all 115 retirements.\\n- Fresh CoreMark build from unchanged sources: `COREMARK_SIZE=6000`,\\n  `COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, `ITERATIONS=10`, both bus-stall flags. PASS: no OOB access,\\n  EBREAK reached, `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, and\\n  the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Timing markers are 56,393 and\\n  32,893,064, giving **32,836,671 bracketed cycles** (not total elapsed cycles).\\n\\nValidation copies, builds, traces, and logs live under\\n`$TMPDIR/load-use-hyp-20260906-001-r1s0/`; protected workspace files were not\\nchanged. Final formal logs are in `formal/last_run-85124.log` and\\n`formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-85124/checks/` under that scratch directory.\\nThe initial counterexample is retained in `formal-attempt1/`.\\n\\n## Remaining concerns\\n\\nThe estimated fitness gain and nearly unchanged Fmax remain unmeasured; the\\norchestrator still needs its unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation. The extra\\nEX/MEM load qualification and RVFI metadata logic must be included in that\\nmeasurement. No multiplier/divider implementation changes were made, and\\nformal-deep was not run.\\n\\nThe prebuilt CoreMark ELF and read-only evaluator constants in this checkout\\nuse the 2 KB configuration. That existing ELF also completed correctly with\\nboth stall flags and its expected 2 KB CRCs. The separate fresh 6 KB run above\\nvalidates the configuration and CRCs requested in the hypothesis. Evaluation\\nconstants, benchmark sources, and the prebuilt ELF were left unchanged. The\\nUART compiler-flags text is hardcoded as `-O2`; the fresh build command used\\n`-O3` as recorded above.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:24:49.837661Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware used by the core\\nwith one shared, blocking iterative quotient/remainder engine. This is\\nthe sole architectural change: retain the current pipeline boundaries\\nand single-cycle RV32I and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU execution.\\n\\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient-bit steps per\\nclock for eight compute clocks. Normalize signed operands at request\\nacceptance, retain the original dividend and both sign flags, and apply\\nquotient/remainder sign correction when producing the held response.\\nSize the trial remainder/subtract path to 33 bits and never use / or %\\nin the synthesized engine. Handle divisor zero explicitly, returning\\nall-ones quotient or the original dividend; preserve INT_MIN/-1 results\\nand the dividend's sign for signed remainder. Bound request-to-response\\nand subsequent dispatch so consecutive divides remain compatible with\\nthe unchanged formal liveness window; avoid gratuitous setup, finish,\\nor cooldown states. Arithmetic and control use the same cycle schedule\\nin real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds. In ALTOPS mode, capture the four\\nexisting subtraction/XOR stand-ins at acceptance and release them on\\nthat same response schedule rather than taking a faster formal path.\\n\\nLaunch only for a valid DIV/REM in ID/EX, once forwarded operands are\\nusable and the older EX/MEM instruction can advance. Capture both\\npost-forward operands, opcode, and the associated RVFI operand values\\nat launch so later WB draining cannot alter an active operation. Hold\\nID/EX and fetch while its result is unavailable. Let older MEM/WB work\\ndrain normally: when EX/MEM is ready, emit a bubble with zeroed control\\nduring the computation, not a repeated copy of the older instruction.\\nAn actual dmem stall still holds its outstanding EX/MEM request and\\nretains the existing WB forwarding payload discipline. Keep a completed\\ndivision response stable until exactly one EX/MEM acceptance, then\\nrelease ID/EX and fetch. Preserve normal forwarding from the completed\\nEX/MEM result to immediately dependent arithmetic, branches, and stores.\\nReset cancels any active transaction; bubbles and killed instructions\\nmust never launch the engine or create an architectural side effect.\\n\\nKeep the decoder's legality rules, all instruction results and traps,\\nbus handshakes, top-level nret=1 ports, and channel-0 RVFI ordering intact.\\nThe gain depends on division being sufficiently uncommon in the timed\\nCoreMark region; its frequency is an assumption to measure, not a known\\nprofile. Evaluate added marker-bracketed cycles against the median Fmax\\ngain, accepting only an increase in their product. For example, a 15%\\nFmax gain with 2.5% additional cycles gives approximately 12% fitness\\nimprovement. Retain every existing correctness gate and arithmetic\\nvector, add protocol/dependency coverage, and run the unchanged formal,\\nstalled cosim, CoreMark CRC/UART checks, and three-seed FPGA evaluation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases, and the existing\\nALTOPS DIV/REM formulas on the same bounded execution schedule.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace combinational division and remainder with the new engine\\nand expose its clock/reset and request/response control to EX.\\nPreserve combinational integer and multiply outputs and all their\\nALTOPS formulas. Select DIV/REM results only from the completed\\nengine response; eliminate the old unrolled / and % hardware.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add division acceptance/completion control and capture the forwarded\\noperand values used for RVFI. Dispatch ordinary operations exactly\\nas before. While a division waits, drain an available EX/MEM slot\\nto a control-zero bubble; preserve a genuinely stalled memory slot.\\nCommit the held divide result and its instruction metadata once,\\nwith the original PC, rd, and operands, and support normal bypass\\nof that result to the following instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX's division wait/acceptance signals to pipeline control while\\npreserving the imem/dmem interfaces, single-channel RVFI ports,\\nretirement boundary, and order counter. Distinguish holding the\\ninstruction in ID/EX from holding an older instruction in EX/MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold fetch and ID/EX for an unfinished DIV/REM without flushing that\\ninstruction. Keep load-use and redirect priorities correct, and\\nreserve EX/MEM holding and WB payload retention for actual memory\\nbackpressure so division waiting cannot duplicate an older memory\\nrequest or retirement. Release the frontend on result acceptance.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt division/remainder checks to the bounded clocked handshake\\nwhile retaining every existing RV32I and RV32M expected-bit vector.\\nAdd randomized signed/unsigned quotient and remainder checks,\\nfull-width trial-remainder cases, response holding, repeated\\nrequests, reset while busy, and an explicit completion bound.\\nContinue checking ordinary integer and multiply latency unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM producer-consumer chains, back-to-back divisions, x0\\ndestinations, preceding WB and load producers, and following\\nbranches/stores. Exercise simultaneous instruction/data stalls,\\nolder stores draining once, reset during division, and flushed\\nwrong-path divisions. Check original RVFI operands, one ordered\\nretirement per accepted instruction, and no duplicate bus effects.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases, and the existing\\nALTOPS DIV/REM formulas on the same bounded execution schedule.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace combinational division and remainder with the new engine\\nand expose its clock/reset and request/response control to EX.\\nPreserve combinational integer and multiply outputs and all their\\nALTOPS formulas. Select DIV/REM results only from the completed\\nengine response; eliminate the old unrolled / and % hardware.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add division acceptance/completion control and capture the forwarded\\noperand values used for RVFI. Dispatch ordinary operations exactly\\nas before. While a division waits, drain an available EX/MEM slot\\nto a control-zero bubble; preserve a genuinely stalled memory slot.\\nCommit the held divide result and its instruction metadata once,\\nwith the original PC, rd, and operands, and support normal bypass\\nof that result to the following instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX's division wait/acceptance signals to pipeline control while\\npreserving the imem/dmem interfaces, single-channel RVFI ports,\\nretirement boundary, and order counter. Distinguish holding the\\ninstruction in ID/EX from holding an older instruction in EX/MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold fetch and ID/EX for an unfinished DIV/REM without flushing that\\ninstruction. Keep load-use and redirect priorities correct, and\\nreserve EX/MEM holding and WB payload retention for actual memory\\nbackpressure so division waiting cannot duplicate an older memory\\nrequest or retirement. Release the frontend on result acceptance.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt division/remainder checks to the bounded clocked handshake\\nwhile retaining every existing RV32I and RV32M expected-bit vector.\\nAdd randomized signed/unsigned quotient and remainder checks,\\nfull-width trial-remainder cases, response holding, repeated\\nrequests, reset while busy, and an explicit completion bound.\\nContinue checking ordinary integer and multiply latency unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM producer-consumer chains, back-to-back divisions, x0\\ndestinations, preceding WB and load producers, and following\\nbranches/stores. Exercise simultaneous instruction/data stalls,\\nolder stores draining once, reset during division, and flushed\\nwrong-path divisions. Check original RVFI operands, one ordered\\nretirement per accepted instruction, and no duplicate bus effects.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 372.62, \"delta_pct\": 31.75, \"vs_baseline\": 31.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5438, \"ff\": 2094, \"seeds\": [165.32, 174.03, 167.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Bounded shared division in cores/bench\\n\\nImplemented a shared unsigned restoring divider with four quotient-bit steps\\nper compute clock, eight compute clocks, and 33-bit trial/subtract paths. Signed\\noperands are normalized at acceptance; original dividend, operand signs and\\nquotient/remainder selection are retained. Zero divisor and INT_MIN/-1 follow\\nRV32M exactly. The synthesized divider contains no division/remainder operators.\\nThe existing four ALTOPS subtraction/XOR formulas are captured at acceptance and\\nuse the same control schedule as real arithmetic. Integer and all four multiply\\noperations retain their combinational ALU path and single-cycle EX dispatch.\\n\\nEX launches only a valid legal DIV/REM with usable forwarded operands and an\\navailable older EX/MEM slot. It captures both post-forward operands for RVFI and\\nholds the instruction metadata in ID/EX. While computing, fetch and ID/EX hold;\\nan available EX/MEM slot becomes a completely zeroed bubble so older loads,\\nstores and retirements drain once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\\nDecoder legality, traps, buses, pipeline boundaries, nret=1 channel-0 ports and\\nthe retirement/order logic remain intact.\\n\\n## Schedule adjustment and formal\\n\\nThe first implementation registered the response after eight compute edges and\\ndispatched it on a separate ninth edge. Local formal passed 52 checks but failed\\n`liveness_ch0` at step 20: a load-use bubble followed by division produced an\\n11-clock gap after the preceding retirement. One RTL fix removed that extra\\ndispatch edge. The response is offered during the eighth compute interval and\\ncan enter EX/MEM on its closing edge. If blocked, the same value is registered\\nand held until acceptance. This is the only deviation from an exclusively\\nregistered response interface; real and ALTOPS schedules remain identical.\\nConsecutive divides now dispatch nine clocks apart, with a worst-case 10-clock\\nretirement gap after load-use or a redirect in the zero-wait formal model.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS after one fix attempt. The unchanged\\n`bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatically selecting `wrapper_si.sv` and\\n`checks_si.cfg`, passed all 53 checks. A final run after the compilation guard\\nadjustment again passed all 53. No checks or depths were modified. Full\\nnon-ALTOPS `formal-deep` was not run; the fast formal suite proves routing and\\nprotocol, while real arithmetic is checked by the vectors and ISS comparisons.\\n\\nThe exact requested glob-order lint command passes without warnings, as does\\nthe same command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`. A guarded core_pkg include in\\ncore.sv accommodates that glob ordering while preserving the existing build\\nflows that already place core_pkg.sv first.\\n\\n## Correctness validation\\n\\n- All six pytest runners passed: ALU real and ALTOPS (30 cocotb tests each),\\n  decoder (30), immediate generator (8), pipeline (18), register file (5).\\n- Every original arithmetic vector remains. Added 1,508 signed/unsigned\\n  DIV/REM edge/random pairs, 512 random multiply checks, precise completion\\n  timing, changing live operands, held responses, busy requests, simultaneous\\n  response consumption/request refill, and reset during busy/response phases.\\n- Pipeline tests compare every RVFI field with the unchanged Python ISS and\\n  independently count accepted memory requests. Coverage includes both operand\\n  forwarding paths, load producers, held WB load payload behind an older store,\\n  12-clock simultaneous bus stalls, dependent/back-to-back divisions, x0,\\n  arithmetic/branch/store consumers, killed divisions, and reset while busy.\\n  Ordinary integer/multiply retirements remain one clock apart.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate passed selftest and CoreMark CRC/UART validation.\\n  Additional selftest runs with both random bus stalls passed seeds\\n  0, 1, 2, 3, 42 and 99 (115 byte-exact retirements each).\\n\\n## FPGA and CoreMark evaluation\\n\\nThe unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`\\ncompleted all three nextpnr seeds. Final routed Fmax values were 165.32,\\n174.03 and 167.36 MHz; median 167.36 MHz. Resource usage was 5,438 LUT4 and\\n2,094 FF, compared with the supplied baseline's 9,563 LUT4 and 1,866 FF.\\nLUT4 usage fell 43.13%.\\n\\nThe checked-in Makefile/evaluator use a 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark\\nconfiguration, despite the hypothesis text describing 6 KB. They were left\\nunchanged. This official gate passed its exact CRCs, UART validation, markers,\\nand OOB checks. Both candidate and a fresh simulation of the baseline RTL from\\nHEAD used 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles. A streamed retirement profile\\nmeasured zero DIV/REM instructions among 2,922,015 timed retirements.\\n\\nOfficial local fitness: **372.62 iterations/s**, versus recorded baseline\\n282.82 iterations/s (**+31.75%**). Median Fmax increased 31.75% with **0% added\\ntimed cycles**, so the frequency-times-iterations-per-cycle acceptance criterion\\nimproves. These are local results; the orchestrator still performs acceptance.\\n\\nThe requested 6 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 configuration was additionally built and\\nrun from unmodified scratch copies. Candidate and baseline both completed in\\n32,836,671 bracketed cycles with no OOB access. Both produced crclist=0xd4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, seedcrc=0x8a02, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal\\n`Correct operation validated.`. At the respective median frequencies, that is\\n50.97 versus 38.69 iterations/s, again a 31.75% improvement with no cycle penalty.\\nIts streamed profile measured zero DIV/REM instructions among 22,052,222 timed\\nretirements; two DIVU instructions executed outside the markers.\\nThe printed compiler-flags string is hardcoded in the unchanged port; the\\nactual build commands used -O3.\\n\\n## Evidence and remaining limits\\n\\nAll build, formal, cosim, profile and P&R outputs are under the isolated scratch\\ndirectory below. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the\\nworktree's formal submodule is a shared symlink; no gate files or other cores\\nwere edited. Only cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this\\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree.\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l`\\n\\nKey evidence: `formal-final-console.log`, `final-unit-tests.log`,\\n`stalled-cosim.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-eval.log`, `division-profile.log`,\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `sixk/*coremark.log`, and\\n`sixk/division-profile.log`.\\n\\nDivision is intentionally blocking and adds eight execution clocks per issued\\nDIV/REM relative to the baseline. Workloads with substantial division traffic\\nmay lose performance despite the FPGA frequency/area improvement. The terminal\\ncompute interval includes sign correction and the EX/MEM acceptance mux; it has\\nbeen included in, and passed, all three routed timing evaluations.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:29:30.038895Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer with a two-bit\\nsaturating direction counter per entry. Index it with fetch PC[5:2], use\\nthe full remaining PC[31:6] as the tag, and store aligned target[31:2].\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\\npredict PC+4. Conditional branches use the counter for direction, while\\nresolved JAL/JALR entries are set strongly taken and learn their latest\\ntarget. Clear valid bits on reset; payloads need not be reset and must\\nnever influence a miss. Use the full architectural PC and target widths.\\n\\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\\nIF/ID into ID/EX. EX computes the actual next PC using the existing\\nforwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush younger\\nwork only when a valid instruction advances out of EX and its actual\\nnext PC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every valid\\ninstruction, including a non-control instruction with a stale table hit.\\nThe repair target is the actual next PC, including PC+4 for a predicted\\ntaken branch that resolves not taken, and the existing PC+4 continuation\\nfor a misaligned target trap. Preserve JAL/JALR link data, trap handling,\\nand the resolved next PC reported through RVFI.\\n\\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never while EX is held by dmem\\nbackpressure. For a matching conditional entry, saturate toward its\\nresolved direction and refresh its aligned branch target. Allocate a\\nmissed conditional entry only when taken, initializing it weakly taken\\n(2'b10). Allocate or refresh legal, aligned JAL/JALR targets with 2'b11.\\nInvalidate a matching entry when the resolved instruction is no longer\\na legal control transfer or its control-transfer target is misaligned.\\nNever allocate or reinforce predictions from bubbles or trap outcomes.\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training to use the pre-edge table\\ncontents for the prediction that is captured; a later table update must\\nnot change recorded metadata.\\n\\nApply a predicted PC advance only when that fetch is actually accepted\\ninto ID/EX. On load-use hazards hold the fetch PC and bubble ID/EX;\\non dmem holds freeze both PC and ID/EX, including its saved prediction.\\nInject the existing invalid bubble on imem unavailability when ID/EX\\nis otherwise able to advance.\\nWhile dmem holds EX, defer both repair and training until EX can advance;\\nretain the existing forwarding state during the hold. An eligible EX\\nrepair must override imem backpressure and any younger prediction.\\nKeep raw lookup independent of flush/stall signals to avoid combinational\\nloops. Correct predictions must not assert a redirect or flush.\\n\\nThis single predictor is expected to improve net fitness by about 8%,\\nsubject to measurement. The small table limits added lookup depth and\\nrouting, but conflicts, changing indirect targets, and additional LUT/FF\\ncost may reduce the gain or lower Fmax. Validate with unchanged formal,\\ncosimulation, CRC, UART, marker-window, and three-seed FPGA evaluation\\ngates. Preserve the nret=1 interface and every evaluation invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 291.2, \"delta_pct\": 2.96, \"vs_baseline\": 2.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10377, \"ff\": 2069, \"seeds\": [129.95, 121.11, 122.77], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Small tagged bimodal predictor\\n\\nImplemented the requested 16-entry direct-mapped predictor in `cores/bench`.\\nThe index is PC[5:2], the tag is PC[31:6], and the stored aligned target is\\ntarget[31:2]. A valid tag hit with counter[1] predicts the target; all misses\\nuse full-width PC+4. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\\nConditional hits refresh the target and saturate the direction counter.\\nTaken conditional misses allocate weakly taken; not-taken misses leave the\\nresident entry untouched. Legal aligned JAL/JALR updates are strongly taken\\nand learn their latest target. Matching stale, illegal, or misaligned entries\\nare invalidated, including misaligned untaken branch targets. Bubbles and\\nheld EX instructions cannot train.\\n\\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC consumed at the accepting\\nedge. EX compares every advancing valid instruction against one actual next\\nPC, also used for RVFI. A mismatch repairs fetch and discards the younger\\ncombinational IF/ID slot. Correct predictions have no repair bubble. JAL/JALR\\nlink writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, misalignment traps, and PC+4 trap\\ncontinuation are preserved. Repairs wait through dmem holds and override\\nimem backpressure when EX advances.\\n\\nLoad-use stalls hold PC and inject an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n## Deviations and scope\\n\\n- Added standalone predictor tests as well as pipeline regressions. The\\n  pipeline harness now models pre-edge bus acceptance and checks RVFI order,\\n  PC continuity, prediction capture, holds, repairs, and store handshakes.\\n- Split hazard equations into independent continuous assignments to avoid\\n  false combinational scheduling cycles in the instrumented Verilator build.\\n- Used `core_pkg.sv` first when linting; compilation-unit typedefs require\\n  this ordering. No lint suppressions were added for combinational loops.\\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This notes file is the requested\\n  exception to the RTL/test editing surface.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\\n\\nThe full formal log is under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-formal/formal-final-console.log`; FPGA seed\\nlogs and the combined result are under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-fpga/`. Cocotb XML results, selftest comparison\\nresults, and the supplemental 6 KB build/run are under the corresponding\\n`-cocotb`, `-selftest-results.json`, and `-6k` scratch paths.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nThe prompt specifies 6 KB CoreMark, but the checked-in Makefile defaults and\\n`tools/eval/fpga.py` CRC gate use the 2 KB fixture. Both workloads were tested\\nseparately without changing the evaluator. The 2 KB measurement must not be\\nreported as a 6 KB fitness result. The measured 10,377 LUT4 exceeds\\n`core.yaml`'s 10,000-LUT target, although all placements succeeded. FPGA\\ncost and reduced Fmax limit the net gain. Direct-map conflicts and changing\\nindirect targets remain expected sources of prediction misses. These are\\nlocal results; orchestrator acceptance and its independent rerun remain\\noutside this implementation step.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:27:51.865455Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    print({k:r.get(k) for k in ('id','category','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds','outcome')})\n    if r['id']=='hyp-20260906-002-r1s1':\n        print(r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    print({k:r.get(k) for k in ('id','category','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds','outcome')})\n    if r['id']=='hyp-20260906-002-r1s1':\n        print(r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{'id': 'baseline-bench-e113d0a', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'fitness': 282.82, 'fmax_mhz': 127.03, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 9563, 'ff': 1866, 'seeds': [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], 'outcome': 'improvement'}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260906-001-r1s0', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'fitness': 284.89, 'fmax_mhz': 127.96, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 10386, 'ff': 1877, 'seeds': [123.75, 127.96, 132.24], 'outcome': 'regression'}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260906-002-r1s1', 'category': 'structural', 'fitness': 372.62, 'fmax_mhz': 167.36, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5438, 'ff': 2094, 'seeds': [165.32, 174.03, 167.36], 'outcome': 'improvement'}\n# Bounded shared division in cores/bench\n\nImplemented a shared unsigned restoring divider with four quotient-bit steps\nper compute clock, eight compute clocks, and 33-bit trial/subtract paths. Signed\noperands are normalized at acceptance; original dividend, operand signs and\nquotient/remainder selection are retained. Zero divisor and INT_MIN/-1 follow\nRV32M exactly. The synthesized divider contains no division/remainder operators.\nThe existing four ALTOPS subtraction/XOR formulas are captured at acceptance and\nuse the same control schedule as real arithmetic. Integer and all four multiply\noperations retain their combinational ALU path and single-cycle EX dispatch.\n\nEX launches only a valid legal DIV/REM with usable forwarded operands and an\navailable older EX/MEM slot. It captures both post-forward operands for RVFI and\nholds the instruction metadata in ID/EX. While computing, fetch and ID/EX hold;\nan available EX/MEM slot becomes a completely zeroed bubble so older loads,\nstores and retirements drain once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\nDecoder legality, traps, buses, pipeline boundaries, nret=1 channel-0 ports and\nthe retirement/order logic remain intact.\n\n## Schedule adjustment and formal\n\nThe first implementation registered the response after eight compute edges and\ndispatched it on a separate ninth edge. Local formal passed 52 checks but failed\n`liveness_ch0` at step 20: a load-use bubble followed by division produced an\n11-clock gap after the preceding retirement. One RTL fix removed that extra\ndispatch edge. The response is offered during the eighth compute interval and\ncan enter EX/MEM on its closing edge. If blocked, the same value is registered\nand held until acceptance. This is the only deviation from an exclusively\nregistered response interface; real and ALTOPS schedules remain identical.\nConsecutive divides now dispatch nine clocks apart, with a worst-case 10-clock\nretirement gap after load-use or a redirect in the zero-wait formal model.\n\nLocal formal status: PASS after one fix attempt. The unchanged\n`bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatically selecting `wrapper_si.sv` and\n`checks_si.cfg`, passed all 53 checks. A final run after the compilation guard\nadjustment again passed all 53. No checks or depths were modified. Full\nnon-ALTOPS `formal-deep` was not run; the fast formal suite proves routing and\nprotocol, while real arithmetic is checked by the vectors and ISS comparisons.\n\nThe exact requested glob-order lint command passes without warnings, as does\nthe same command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`. A guarded core_pkg include in\ncore.sv accommodates that glob ordering while preserving the existing build\nflows that already place core_pkg.sv first.\n\n## Correctness validation\n\n- All six pytest runners passed: ALU real and ALTOPS (30 cocotb tests each),\n  decoder (30), immediate generator (8), pipeline (18), register file (5).\n- Every original arithmetic vector remains. Added 1,508 signed/unsigned\n  DIV/REM edge/random pairs, 512 random multiply checks, precise completion\n  timing, changing live operands, held responses, busy requests, simultaneous\n  response consumption/request refill, and reset during busy/response phases.\n- Pipeline tests compare every RVFI field with the unchanged Python ISS and\n  independently count accepted memory requests. Coverage includes both operand\n  forwarding paths, load producers, held WB load payload behind an older store,\n  12-clock simultaneous bus stalls, dependent/back-to-back divisions, x0,\n  arithmetic/branch/store consumers, killed divisions, and reset while busy.\n  Ordinary integer/multiply retirements remain one clock apart.\n- Unchanged cosim gate passed selftest and CoreMark CRC/UART validation.\n  Additional selftest runs with both random bus stalls passed seeds\n  0, 1, 2, 3, 42 and 99 (115 byte-exact retirements each).\n\n## FPGA and CoreMark evaluation\n\nThe unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`\ncompleted all three nextpnr seeds. Final routed Fmax values were 165.32,\n174.03 and 167.36 MHz; median 167.36 MHz. Resource usage was 5,438 LUT4 and\n2,094 FF, compared with the supplied baseline's 9,563 LUT4 and 1,866 FF.\nLUT4 usage fell 43.13%.\n\nThe checked-in Makefile/evaluator use a 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark\nconfiguration, despite the hypothesis text describing 6 KB. They were left\nunchanged. This official gate passed its exact CRCs, UART validation, markers,\nand OOB checks. Both candidate and a fresh simulation of the baseline RTL from\nHEAD used 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles. A streamed retirement profile\nmeasured zero DIV/REM instructions among 2,922,015 timed retirements.\n\nOfficial local fitness: **372.62 iterations/s**, versus recorded baseline\n282.82 iterations/s (**+31.75%**). Median Fmax increased 31.75% with **0% added\ntimed cycles**, so the frequency-times-iterations-per-cycle acceptance criterion\nimproves. These are local results; the orchestrator still performs acceptance.\n\nThe requested 6 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 configuration was additionally built and\nrun from unmodified scratch copies. Candidate and baseline both completed in\n32,836,671 bracketed cycles with no OOB access. Both produced crclist=0xd4b0,\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, seedcrc=0x8a02, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal\n`Correct operation validated.`. At the respective median frequencies, that is\n50.97 versus 38.69 iterations/s, again a 31.75% improvement with no cycle penalty.\nIts streamed profile measured zero DIV/REM instructions among 22,052,222 timed\nretirements; two DIVU instructions executed outside the markers.\nThe printed compiler-flags string is hardcoded in the unchanged port; the\nactual build commands used -O3.\n\n## Evidence and remaining limits\n\nAll build, formal, cosim, profile and P&R outputs are under the isolated scratch\ndirectory below. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the\nworktree's formal submodule is a shared symlink; no gate files or other cores\nwere edited. Only cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree.\n\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l`\n\nKey evidence: `formal-final-console.log`, `final-unit-tests.log`,\n`stalled-cosim.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-eval.log`, `division-profile.log`,\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `sixk/*coremark.log`, and\n`sixk/division-profile.log`.\n\nDivision is intentionally blocking and adds eight execution clocks per issued\nDIV/REM relative to the baseline. Workloads with substantial division traffic\nmay lose performance despite the FPGA frequency/area improvement. The terminal\ncompute interval includes sign correction and the EX/MEM acceptance mux; it has\nbeen included in, and passed, all three routed timing evaluations.\n\n{'id': 'hyp-20260906-003-r1s2', 'category': 'predictor', 'fitness': 291.2, 'fmax_mhz': 122.77, 'cycles': 4215937, 'lut4': 10377, 'ff': 2069, 'seeds': [129.95, 121.11, 122.77], 'outcome': 'regression'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
