{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    if r.get('category') == 'micro_opt':\n        text=' '.join(str(r.get(k,'')) for k in ('motivation','hypothesis','implementation_notes'))\n        terms=('reset','RAM16','distributed','valid bit','validity','written','storage')\n        for para in text.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n            if any(t.lower() in para.lower() for t in terms) and (('register' in para.lower()) or ('reg_file' in para.lower())):\n                print(r['id'],para[:1800])\n                print()\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 LESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\n {'motivation': 'The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Move ALU result-select decoding across the existing ID/EX register,\\nwithout adding a pipeline stage. Add a 16-bit selector to id_ex_t:\\nbits 0 through 14 select the existing ALU_ADD through ALU_MULHSU results\\nusing their unchanged numeric operation constants, and bit 15 \n\nhyp-20260906-001-r2s0 - `cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`: added 16 named selector positions and a\n  16-bit `alu_result_sel` field only to `id_ex_t`. Positions 0 through 14\n  retain the numeric ADD through MULHSU operation values; position 15\n  selects the shared divider response. Encoded operations, `ctrl_t`,\n  downstream bundles, and RVFI widths are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv`: predecode comes exclusively from\n  `dec_alu_op`. Equality enables for operations 0 through 14 and a shared\n  DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU enable are latched with the existing ID/EX instruction\n  enable. Reset/flush clear the entire bundle and holds preserve it.\n  Illegal decoder outputs retain default ADD; unsupported internal operation\n  values match no selector. Decoder legality logic is untouched.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: passes the registered enables to the ALU\n  and uses bit 15 with the existing valid/legal guards for `is_div`.\n  Divider launch, acceptance, stall, forwarding, captured operands, jump\n  result selection, redirects, traps, and EX/MEM timing are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: replaced the encoded output case with 16 masked\n  result terms and explicit 8/4/2/1 balanced OR levels. Every arithmetic\n  expression, signed/unsigned multiplier expression, and ALTOPS formula is\n  preserved. The divider term remains qualified by `div_resp_valid`.\n  `div_unit.sv` is unchanged, including encoded operation selection and\n  terminal-edge response acceptance. A zero selector produces zero output.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: every operation drive now supplies its\n  selector. All original vectors and timing assertions remain. Added\n  randomized rapid transitions across all combinational classes, zero-hot\n  selection, unsupported operations, and switching result selection while\n  each type of div\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r3s0 LESSONS.md and the micro_opt experiment records rule out simply repeating\nsource-use filtering or whole-ALU one-hot selection: the former left\nmeasured CoreMark cycles unchanged, while the latter reduced median Fmax\n5.98% and increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. The proposed change\ntherefore targets the divider's actual arithmetic carry chains. It can\nimprove clock frequency even when the timed program executes no divides.\n {'motivation': \"The current winner scores 375.78 at 168.78 MHz with 5,470 LUT4s. Its saved\\nrouted reports identify divider-to-result_q critical paths in seeds 1\\nand 3, at 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Seed 1 traverses the quotient sign-correction\\nnegation at div_unit.sv:63; seed 3 traverses a restoring subtraction at\\ndiv_unit.sv:57. Both contain long chains of native carry cells. Seed 2\\ninstead has a 5.925 ns ordinary ALU control-to-result path, so improving\\ndivider arithmetic alone has a competing timing limit.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt experiment records rule out simply repeating\\nsource-use filtering or whole-ALU one-hot selection: the former left\\nmeasured CoreMark cycles unchanged, while the latter reduced median Fmax\\n5.98% and increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. The proposed change\\ntherefore targets the divider's actual arithmetic carry chains. It can\\nimprove clock frequency even when the timed program executes no divides.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Replace only the divider's wide combinational subtract/negate primitives\\nwith one reusable, exact 33-bit block carry-select subtraction circuit.\\nKeep the four restoring steps per clock, eight compute clocks, terminal\\nresponse interval, and all existing pipeline and result-bank registers.\\nThis is a local arithmetic implementation change with no added latency.\\n\\nSplit x and y into f\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r3s0 There is no architectural deviation from the hypothesis. The existing divider\nalways_ff block and its request/response/ALTOPS prefix were checked byte-for-byte\nagainst starting HEAD. Request acceptance, eight compute clocks, terminal\nresponse interval, result_q capture/retention, blocked responses, reset, and\nregistered-result ownership are unchanged. The ordinary ALU and multiplier,\nEX/MEM banks, direct normal-bank memory address, nret=1 IO/RVFI, decoder,\nforwarding, hazards, and all other pipeline RTL are unchanged.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r3s0 - Exact requested glob-order Verilator lint: PASS without warnings, both real\n  arithmetic and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. git diff --check also passes.\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first invocation, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n  No formal counterexamples or fix attempts. The unchanged run_all.sh selected\n  wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl and CORE_NAME=bench.\n  All wrappers, depths, checks, and framework sources remain unchanged.\n- Supplemental combinational Yosys SAT proof: PASS. A separate mathematical\n  33-bit subtraction reference proves the helper exact for all 2^66 input\n  combinations, including bit 32 and every borrow case. This supplements the\n  unchanged ALTOPS core formal gate; full-core formal-deep was not run.\n- All nine pytest runners PASS, containing 133 cocotb tests: ALU real/ALTOPS\n  32 each, divider helper 3, decoder 30, EX banks real/ALTOPS 2 each, immediate\n  generator 8, pipeline 19, and register file 5. The existing pipeline tests\n  include byte-exact RVFI/ISS comparisons, both bus stalls, forwarded divide\n  operands and consumers, held ownership, resets, and completion spacing.\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and the official CoreMark fixture.\n- Unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv: all three\n  routes complete successfully. The installed historical tool versions were\n  used: Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and\n  nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. No timing exceptions, constraints, vendor models,\n  benchmark source, fixture, or evaluation formula changed.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r4s0 The micro_opt history was checked: source-use predecode left measured\nCoreMark cycles unchanged; ALU one-hot selection lost 5.98% and disrupted\nmultiplier sharing; replacing all divider subtractors with block\ncarry-select logic lost 35.87%. The radix-4 structural attempt also lost\n57.65%. Those results argue for preserving the four native restoring\nsubtractors and testing only the unary negation after them. The existing\nterminal logic computes 0-minus-magnitude and then selects its sign.\n {'motivation': \"The current winner reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz with unchanged\\nCoreMark cycles. Its saved seed-1 timing path is 6.018 ns from quotient_q\\nto result_q, with 27 source references to div_unit.sv's terminal quotient\\nnegation. Seed 3 also ends at result_q, while seed 2 is limited by ordinary\\nEX result logic. This makes the final sign-correction cone a concrete,\\nnarrowly scoped timing target, although it cannot explain every limit.\\n\\nThe micro_opt history was checked: source-use predecode left measured\\nCoreMark cycles unchanged; ALU one-hot selection lost 5.98% and disrupted\\nmultiplier sharing; replacing all divider subtractors with block\\ncarry-select logic lost 35.87%. The radix-4 structural attempt also lost\\n57.65%. Those results argue for preserving the four native restoring\\nsubtractors and testing only the unary negation after them. The existing\\nterminal logic computes 0-minus-magnitude and then selects its sign.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Replace only the two terminal conditional negations of quotient_step[4]\\nand remainder_step[4] with a combinational prefix-OR implementation of\\ntwo's-complement sign correction. For a 32-bit magnitude x and negate n,\\ny[0] = x[0], and for each i from 1 through 31,\\ny[i] = x[i] XOR (n AND OR(x[i-1:0])). Negation preserves the\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r4s0 - The exact requested Verilator lint command passes without warnings.\n  The same lint command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` also passes.\n- All nine pytest runners pass: 132 cocotb tests, no failures or skips.\n  This includes every existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic/protocol test,\n  registered result retention, held owner, successive results, forwarding,\n  reset cancellation, and all 19 pipeline regressions including RVFI\n  operands/order, divide consumers, completion spacing, and combined stalls.\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first gate invocation, 53 passed and\n  0 failed. No RTL fix attempts or counterexamples. Ran the unchanged\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`;\n  it selected the nret=1 wrapper/config automatically. This is the standard\n  ALTOPS gate, not a proof of the complete real divider's arithmetic.\n- A separate Yosys SAT miter proves the helper equivalent to a mathematical\n  conditional 32-bit subtraction for every 32-bit magnitude and both values\n  of negate: `sat -verify -prove trigger 0` returned SUCCESS. The reference\n  module was supplied through stdin and was not added to the design.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` passes both ELFs: selftest RVFI comparison\n  and the official CoreMark CRC gate.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with `NRET=1` and\n  `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, completes all three routes and benchmark\n  validation. No placement failures. The evaluator and its acceptance\n  criteria were not edited.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r5s0 LESSONS.md and all prior micro_opt entries in experiments/log.jsonl were\nchecked. None rewrote the register-file read network. False load-use\nremoval left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged; full ALU one-hot\nselection disrupted multiplier sharing; divider carry-select and\nprefix sign-correction changes regressed routed timing. This proposal\ntargets only the combinational integer-register read/bypass network.\n {'motivation': 'The current winner reports 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz median Fmax.\\nIts saved RTL under .tmp/divider-prefix-r4s1-2_i3jztx/cores/bench/rtl\\nmatches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte. The corresponding routed\\ntiming/seed1/nextpnr.log reports a 6.10 ns path from regs[15][27], through\\nthe register-file read selection, into an ID/EX register: 2.51 ns logic\\nand 3.59 ns routing. Seed 3 reports a 5.73 ns fetch/decode-to-ID/EX path,\\nwhile seed 2 reports a 5.82 ns encoded-EX-control-to-EX/MEM path. Thus\\nthe recently improved divider is not the reported worst path in these\\nthree routes, and register-file selection is a concrete local target.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and all prior micro_opt entries in experiments/log.jsonl were\\nchecked. None rewrote the register-file read network. False load-use\\nremoval left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged; full ALU one-hot\\nselection disrupted multiplier sharing; divider carry-select and\\nprefix sign-correction changes regressed routed timing. This proposal\\ntargets only the combinational integer-register read/bypass network.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace each binary-indexed register-file read followed by priority\\nwrite-bypass and x0 selection with one mutually exclusive masked-OR\\nnetwork. Keep the same resettable register storage, synchronous write\\nbehavior, two asynchronous read ports, and module interface.\\n\\nFor each r\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r5s0 Each read port now has its own nonzero write-hit predicate, 31 shared\nword enables, and exactly 32 masked candidates. Candidate zero is the\nwrite bypass; candidates 1 through 31 select stored registers only when\nthere is no write hit. Constant generate indices drive an explicit\n32-to-8-to-2-to-1 OR reduction, with paired ORs within each four-input\ngroup. Reading x0 enables no candidate. The original storage declaration\nand entire synchronous reset/write block are byte-for-byte unchanged.\nHit is deliberately independent of reset, preserving live write-first\nbypass while reset clears storage at the clock edge.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r5s0 The five original register-file tests are retained. The added independent\n32-register architectural scoreboard uses ordinary indexed Python storage\nand write-first rules. It checks distinct initial patterns; all 1024 read\naddress pairs; every write destination with no hit, either single-port\nhit, and both-port hits; reads before and after edges; disabled writes;\nx0; consecutive overwrites; 1024 live, uncommitted write/address changes;\n1500 randomized clocked transitions; and reset of populated storage.\nReset concurrent with a write is covered for all 32 destinations,\nincluding continued bypass on either or both ports after storage clears\nand zero data after write enable drops.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r6s0 LESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries in log.jsonl show regressions from\nbroad ALU one-hot selection, divider carry rewrites, and register-file\nread restructuring. None attempted this narrow JALR alignment path.\nMoving its four-input Boolean computation ahead of the late forwarding\nselector should reduce control-dependent delay with very little area\ncost and identical instruction timing.\n {'motivation': \"The current winner reports 409.80 iter/sec at 184.06 MHz. Its saved\\nthree-seed timing reports identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding\\nselection and the low JALR addition bits to IF pc[1] as the worst path\\nin seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. The current fourteen RTL files\\nmatch that run's SHA-256 manifest. The path serializes register-tag\\nmatching, operand selection, two native arithmetic arcs, and PC logic.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries in log.jsonl show regressions from\\nbroad ALU one-hot selection, divider carry rewrites, and register-file\\nread restructuring. None attempted this narrow JALR alignment path.\\nMoving its four-input Boolean computation ahead of the late forwarding\\nselector should reduce control-dependent delay with very little area\\ncost and identical instruction timing.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"In ex_stage.sv, independently compute bit 1 of base + in.imm for each\\nof the three existing rs1 candidates: in.rs1_val, fwd_ex_mem, and\\nfwd_mem_wb. For each candidate base, use the exact Boolean identity\\nbase[1] ^ in.imm[1] ^ (base[0] & in.imm[0]). This depends on only four\\ninput bits and is independent of the forwarding selector. Select the\\nthree one-bit results using the existing fwd_rs1_sel encoding, including\\nits default selection of in.rs1_val. Preserve forward_unit's existing\\nEX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and all load-use interlocks.\\n\\nKe\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r6s0 There are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. JAL/branch alignment,\nforwarding priority, load-use interlocking, redirect qualification during holds,\nPC+4 links, trap write suppression, linear trap pc_next, raw RVFI operands,\npipeline registers and bubbles, divider/result-bank ownership, normal ALU\narithmetic, directly registered memory addresses, reset, and nret=1 are preserved.\nNo evaluator, benchmark, wrapper, configuration, or other core was modified.\nThis explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\n\nhyp-20260906-001-r7s0 A funct3-aligned binary encoding is an untried, smaller control change.\nThe current winner's saved routes instead limit on forwarding/JALR in\nseeds 1 and 3 and register-file capture in seed 2; consequently, improving\nALU selection alone is not guaranteed to increase its median frequency.\n {'motivation': \"The current ALU operation numbers are sequential by source-list order,\\nrather than by shared hardware controls. MUL through MULHSU occupy\\n11..14, while DIV through REMU occupy 15..18 and cross the high-bit\\nboundary. Decoder outputs and execute classification therefore encode\\nirregular operation sets. Recent r6s1 routes made alu_op-driven normal\\nresult selection critical in every seed. The r2s0 one-hot experiment\\nregressed 5.98% and expanded one MULT36X36 into three, so it does not\\nestablish that a wider control representation helps.\\n\\nA funct3-aligned binary encoding is an untried, smaller control change.\\nThe current winner's saved routes instead limit on forwarding/JALR in\\nseeds 1 and 3 and register-file capture in seed 2; consequently, improving\\nALU selection alone is not guaranteed to increase its median frequency.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Re-encode the existing five-bit ALU operation constants so the low three\\nbits match instruction funct3 for every legal OP and OP-IMM operation.\\nUse these exact decimal values:\\n  ADD=0, SLL=1, SLT=2, SLTU=3, XOR=4, SRL=5, OR=6, AND=7;\\n  SUB=8, SRA=13, LUI=15;\\n  MUL=16, MULH=17, MULHSU=18, MULHU=19;\\n  DIV=20, DIVU=21, REM=22, REMU=23.\\nThus base operations use the low funct3 bits, SUB/SRA add bit 3, and\\nRV32M operations use 16 plus funct3. Codes 9..12, 14, and 24..31 remain\\nunsupported. ADD remains zero, preserving zero-control bubble behavior.\\n\\nThis hypothesis changes only the internal control representation. Keep\\nal\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
