{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor row in rows:\n    print(json.dumps({k:row.get(k) for k in ('id','category','title','outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles')}, ensure_ascii=False))\nfor suffix in ('-r5s1','-r6s0','-r6s1','-r7s0','-r7s1','-r8s1'):\n    for row in rows:\n        if row['id'].endswith(suffix):\n            print('\\\\nNOTES', row['id'])\n            print(row.get('implementation_notes','') or '(none in log)')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor row in rows:\n    print(json.dumps({k:row.get(k) for k in ('id','category','title','outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles')}, ensure_ascii=False))\nfor suffix in ('-r5s1','-r6s0','-r6s1','-r7s0','-r7s1','-r8s1'):\n    for row in rows:\n        if row['id'].endswith(suffix):\n            print('\\\\nNOTES', row['id'])\n            print(row.get('implementation_notes','') or '(none in log)')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-e113d0a\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r1s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Eliminate load-use bubbles caused by immediate bitfields\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.89, \"delta_pct\": 0.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.96, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 372.62, \"delta_pct\": 31.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.36, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 291.2, \"delta_pct\": 2.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77, \"cycles\": 4215937}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r2s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.35, \"delta_pct\": -5.98, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.36, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r2s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 375.78, \"delta_pct\": 0.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 168.78, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r2s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.07, \"delta_pct\": -7.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.48, \"cycles\": 4215937}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r3s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Shorten divider carry chains with block carry-select subtraction\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 240.97, \"delta_pct\": -35.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 108.23, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r3s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Two radix-4 quotient stages per divider clock\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 159.15, \"delta_pct\": -57.65, \"fmax_mhz\": 71.48, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r3s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Stateless backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 361.83, \"delta_pct\": -3.71, \"fmax_mhz\": 154.13, \"cycles\": 4259699}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r4s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Carry-free conditional sign correction at divider completion\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 234.55, \"delta_pct\": -37.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 105.35, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r4s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Retime divider finalization using a narrow request-edge prefix\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 382.48, \"delta_pct\": 1.78, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.79, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r4s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.59, \"delta_pct\": -8.03, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.52, \"cycles\": 4442217}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r5s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Balanced one-hot register-file reads with integrated write bypass\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.03, \"delta_pct\": -7.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 159.01, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r5s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 409.8, \"delta_pct\": 7.14, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.06, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r5s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 338.91, \"delta_pct\": -11.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 146.05, \"cycles\": 4309463}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r6s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Precompute JALR alignment before operand forwarding selection\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 373.37, \"delta_pct\": -8.89, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.7, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r6s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Retime forwarding dependency comparisons into the ID/EX boundary\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 376.98, \"delta_pct\": -8.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.32, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r6s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Tagged target prediction with MEM-stage validation and staged training\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"fitness\": null, \"delta_pct\": null, \"fmax_mhz\": null, \"cycles\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r7s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Align five-bit ALU control codes with instruction funct3\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 336.53, \"delta_pct\": -17.88, \"fmax_mhz\": 151.15, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r7s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 382.21, \"delta_pct\": -6.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.67, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r7s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Single-entry backedge target prediction with decode validation\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 268.66, \"delta_pct\": -34.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 115.85, \"cycles\": 4312067}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r8s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Infer distributed register storage by resetting per-register validity\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 356.56, \"delta_pct\": -12.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.15, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r8s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Absorb ID operand capture into synchronous register-file RAM banks\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 385.46, \"delta_pct\": -5.94, \"fmax_mhz\": 173.13, \"cycles\": 4491485}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r8s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Backward-branch prediction with validity-only EX annul\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 206.45, \"delta_pct\": -49.62, \"fmax_mhz\": 88.97, \"cycles\": 4309463}\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-002-r5s1\nImplemented the pipeline-storage hypothesis. Local evaluation reports **409.80 fitness**, up **7.14%** from 382.48, with unchanged benchmark cycles. Median reported Fmax increases from 171.79 to **184.06 MHz**. Resources are **5,490 LUT4 and 2,112 DFF**, versus 5,513 LUT4 and 2,087 DFF. All local correctness gates passed. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\n\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv` and `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. Each retains its packed output representation but assigns disjoint control and payload fields in separate clocked processes. Every state bit has one procedural owner; Yosys `check` reports zero problems.\n\nID's complete 22-bit control word and valid bit retain reset/flush priority over stall. Unstalled invalid inputs now install zero control. The 175 payload bits reset synchronously only on reset and capture whenever `!stall`. A simultaneous load-use stall and flush clears control while retaining payload; an unstalled redirect captures discarded payload beneath an invalid, control-zero entry. Memory and division holds retain the complete active ID instruction.\n\nEX's control, valid, and registered divider selector retain their original reset, memory-hold, bubble, and acceptance transitions. All normal-result and instruction-payload fields reset synchronously and capture on every `!stall` edge. Neither bubble insertion nor divide acceptance suppresses or zeroes that capture. Saved post-forward divide operands remain selected into accepting metadata; branch targets, resolved next PC, and trap handling retain their existing equations. The registered divider bank remains authoritative on acceptance. `mem_addr` remains directly connected to the normal registered result, and a memory hold freezes that result, payload, controls, and selection.\n\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. Pipeline depth, dispatch timing, eight-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\n\nTests retain all existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response-hold, ownership, ISS/RVFI, retirement-spacing, and accepted-memory-effect assertions. Invalid-cycle whole-bundle-zero comparisons in the EX tests were replaced with valid/control-zero checks; whole-bundle reset checks remain. Added coverage includes a direct ID-stage test and directed pipeline overlaps between memory holds and load-use, division, and redirects, plus unavailable fetches with distinct payloads. The EX test varies inactive normal data through computation and acceptance, then checks a prolonged owner hold and the first dependent consumer. To vary that bank during an active divide, the isolated EX test deliberately combines DIV with PC+4 selection; this is a synthetic interface stress vector. Integrated tests use legal decoded instructions and the unchanged ISS.\n\nLocal validation on the final RTL:\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 140 cases passed, no skips**. Includes 33 real and 33 ALTOPS ALU cases, both EX-bank modes, 30 decoder cases, and all 22 pipeline cases.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, on the first invocation. Includes `liveness_ch0`, unique retirement, cover, and illegal-instruction checks. No counterexample and no formal fix attempts. The unchanged nret=1 wrapper/configuration and all formal depths were used. This is the contract's ALTOPS formal gate; real-arithmetic deep formal was not run because arithmetic did not change.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and evaluator CoreMark.\n- Additional C++ selftest cosimulation with both stall flags and seeds 1, 19, and 99: **PASS**, all 115 retirements compared against the unchanged ISS per run. Starting and final RTL also have identical retirement-cycle sequences for each seed.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: **PASS**, all three routed seeds and the UART, CRC, OOB, and marker gates.\n- Source verification confirms 154 staged source/contract files, all 53 formal PASS results, exact agreement between staged formal RTL and submitted RTL, and agreement between the starting RTL snapshot and HEAD. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\n\nThe synthesized storage cones establish the intended structural change. In module-preserving Yosys `proc; opt` output, all ID payload flops changed from synchronous `reset OR flush` to reset alone, while retaining negative-polarity `stall` enables and direct payload data. EX's normal-result data cone previously included `div_accept`, `div_wait`, and incoming valid; its enable also included those decisions. The final data cone contains only ordinary ALU output, PC, and architectural result selection; its enable is only `!stall` and synchronous reset is only reset. Other EX payload fields likewise lose their bubble-zero data muxes. Saved-operand selection still uses `is_div`, as required for accepting metadata.\n\nThe actual Gowin netlist has 160 unique surviving ID payload DFFRE cells, all with the common capture enable and `cpu.reset` reset. It has 190 unique surviving EX payload DFFR cells with `cpu.reset`; the FPGA wrapper ties memory ready high, so EX's memory-hold enable optimizes away. All 32 normal-result bits are directly registered, and the memory-address vector is identical to those register bits. Other output payload fields directly alias their stored fields; no replacement output-side bubble mux was added. The expected four BSRAMs and one MULT36X36 remain in every routed seed. Counts account for synthesis merging shared fields and pruning unused metadata.\n\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported worst path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.43 | 3.06 / 2.38 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | Wrapper instruction stimulus lfsr[20] to ID rs2_val[3] | 5.23 | 2.61 / 2.63 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.56 | 3.06 / 2.50 |\n\nFigures are rounded independently by nextpnr. Tools are Yosys 0.64 (`d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61`) and nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`. The requested standalone lint used Verilator 5.047; the unit and cosimulation gates used the installed Verilator 5.046 with the Conda Python environment containing cocotb and YAML. The eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\n\nTiming remains a reported tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish complete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multiplier, or other forwarding paths have timing margin.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator uses aggregate **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**, whereas the requested supplemental run uses aggregate **6 KB**. The evaluator and its expected CRCs remain unchanged. The separate 6 KB ELF was built from identical benchmark sources with the existing Makefile options `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`. Starting and final RTL were freshly simulated with the exact same ELF per size and identical `--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed.\n\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both RTL versions | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Starting / final iter/sec |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 382.48 / 409.80 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 52.32 / 56.05 |\n\nRates use the recorded starting 171.79 MHz and measured final 184.06 MHz; starting FPGA routing was not rerun. Both versions reach EBREAK, report no OOB access, provide valid marker brackets, and produce identical UART output for each workload. Both include the literal `Correct operation validated.` and ten iterations. Evaluator CRCs are seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The separately verified **6 KB CRCs are seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART description says -O2; the actual compile commands use -O3.\n\nAll build artifacts and scratch contract copies are under the configured TMPDIR:\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r5s1-storage-78f91x4q`.\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, the nine result XMLs, `cosim-output.log`, `stalled-iss.json`, `fpga-output.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`, `storage-cones.json`, both premap JSONs, `workload-comparison.json`, individual baseline/candidate benchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. This notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5. All remaining changes are confined to bench RTL and test_*.py files.\n\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-001-r6s0\nImplemented the JALR alignment precomputation hypothesis. All local correctness\ngates passed, but the measured fitness **regressed 8.89%, from 409.80 to 373.37**.\nMedian reported Fmax fell from 184.06 to **167.70 MHz** with unchanged benchmark\ncycles. The estimated four percent improvement was not observed. Local gate\nsuccess is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\n\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. It computes three independent\nsum-bit-1 expressions from `in.rs1_val`, `fwd_ex_mem`, and `fwd_mem_wb` using\n`base[1] ^ in.imm[1] ^ (base[0] & in.imm[0])`. A separate one-bit case mux uses\nthe existing forwarding encoding: 1 selects EX/MEM, 2 selects MEM/WB, and both\n0 and 3 default to the ID/EX value. Both JALR target bit 1 and its misalignment\npredicate consume that selected bit directly. The original full-width\n`rs1 + in.imm` still supplies target bits [31:2], including the carry from the\nlow bits and modulo-2^32 wraparound. Target bit 0 remains cleared. No full-width\nadder was replicated and no predicate is computed from already-forwarded rs1.\n\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. JAL/branch alignment,\nforwarding priority, load-use interlocking, redirect qualification during holds,\nPC+4 links, trap write suppression, linear trap pc_next, raw RVFI operands,\npipeline registers and bubbles, divider/result-bank ownership, normal ALU\narithmetic, directly registered memory addresses, reset, and nret=1 are preserved.\nNo evaluator, benchmark, wrapper, configuration, or other core was modified.\nThis explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\n\n`test_ex_result_banks.py` retains every existing ownership/divider assertion and\nadds 1,024 exhaustive combinations of all three candidate low-two-bit values,\nthe immediate low two bits, and all four selector encodings. Expected targets\ncome from an independent Python full-width addition. Further vectors cover\ncarry into bit 2 and high bits, negative immediates, wraparound, PC+4 wrap,\nindependent rs2 forwarding, held owners, invalid metadata, synchronous reset\nover hold, and unchanged PC-relative JAL/branch alignment. Checks cover target,\nredirect, trap/reg_write, PC+4, pc_next, complete captured operand values, and\nthe direct normal-result address bank. All new EX tests run in real and ALTOPS\nmodes.\n\n`test_pipeline.py` adds register-file, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, competing-writer, x0,\nload-use, and divider-result JALR cases with aligned/misaligned sums and negative\nimmediates. Each runs with zero-wait and deterministic random bus stalls.\nTests verify actual forwarding selections, freshest-writer priority, suppressed\ntrap links, discarded stores, consecutive redirects, one-cycle ordinary/divide\nconsumer spacing, the existing two-cycle load-use spacing, and two-cycle target\nretirement spacing. Directed 12-cycle overlapping imem/dmem holds exercise\nrepeated aligned redirects and repeatedly suppressed misaligned redirects while\nMEM/WB retains the needed source. Per-cycle assertions preserve fetch redirect\npriority, all existing bank/metadata holds, and accepted memory effects. Complete\nretirement streams are compared through the unchanged ISS and comparator,\nincluding PC continuity and gap-free order.\n\nLocal validation on the final RTL and tests:\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: **PASS, 9 runners / 148 cocotb cases**,\n  including 24 pipeline cases, 7 EX cases in each arithmetic mode, and all\n  existing ALU, decoder, register-file, and ID checks. No failures or skips.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on the first invocation**. The script\n  auto-selected the unchanged nret=1 wrapper and configuration. Zero formal\n  fix attempts; no counterexample. This is the required ALTOPS gate. Deep\n  arithmetic formal was not run because multiplier/divider arithmetic did\n  not change.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS, selftest and CoreMark**.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\n  **PASS, all three placements and CRC/UART/OOB/marker checks**, with the\n  performance regression reported above.\n- `git diff --check`: **PASS**. SHA-256 verification covers 227 staged source\n  and contract files. Every formal-staged RTL file matches the submitted\n  source; the fresh baseline simulation sources match HEAD.\n\nThe default Python lacks cocotb; the installed Conda environment supplied the\nexisting test dependencies without installation or source changes. Standalone\nlint used Verilator 5.047; the make gates used the installed Verilator 5.046.\nSynthesis used Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61).\nThe eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\nAll generated validation files reside in the configured TMPDIR scratch copy.\n\nAll three complete final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Source -> endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 182.25 | EX/MEM rd[3], through forwarding/control, to saved divide rs2 capture CE | 5.49 | 2.02 / 3.46 |\n| 2 | 165.26 | ID/EX alu_op[2], through ALU result selection, to normal result/address bit 26 | 6.05 | 2.61 / 3.45 |\n| 3 | 167.70 | ID/EX alu_op[3], through ALU result selection, to normal result/address bit 26 | 5.96 | 2.80 / 3.16 |\n\nRounded values are reported independently by nextpnr. The candidate uses\n**5,525 LUT4 and 2,116 DFF**, versus the recorded winner's 5,490 and 2,112.\nThe synthesized design retains **one MULT36X36 and four DPX9B block RAMs**;\nall three packed/routed seeds retain one MULT36X36 and four DP blocks. Every\nmemory-address bit still comes directly from its normal-result DFF.\n\nConnectivity inspection of the actual Gowin netlist found exactly three LUT4\ncells implementing the candidate predicates. Their truth tables were checked\nagainst addition for all 16 base/immediate combinations; synthesis inverted\nthe MEM/WB predicate and absorbed its polarity downstream. Each cell depends\nonly on its own base low bits and the two immediate bits. EX/MEM and MEM/WB\nbase bits retain their existing result-bank/writeback selection. Transitive\nregistered-leaf inspection confirms no forwarding register-tag bits enter any\ncandidate predicate. The three outputs then enter forwarding-controlled LUT/MUX\nselection. Actual connectivity paths from each predicate to IF pc[1], captured\nEX pc_next[1], and the EX trap control contain LUT/MUX logic and no native ALU\ncells. Thus the intended arithmetic-before-forwarding order survives mapping,\nbut different paths and placement dominate the reported result.\n\nA supplemental seed-1 timing/SDF export reproduced the gate's routed netlist\nbyte-for-byte and the same Fmax. All four input timing arcs are present for\neach new predicate LUT4. However, several existing bank selectors and\ndownstream LUT1/2/3 selection/qualification cells have no IOPATH entries. The\ndetailed timing report includes IF pc[1] but omits the EX pc_next[1] and trap\nendpoints. These omissions are **not zero-delay evidence**: no complete physical\ntiming margin is claimed for those paths. An initial attempt to reload the\nrouted JSON for export aborted with nextpnr's array2d bounds assertion; rerunning\nthe unchanged seed from synth.json with export options produced the identical\nroute. No timing model, constraint, or gate was modified.\n\nThe evaluator's recorded **aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ten-iteration fixture** remains\ndistinct from the supplemental **aggregate 6 KB** validation. The latter ELF\nuses the unchanged benchmark Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`.\nFresh baseline and candidate simulations used identical ELFs and identical\n`--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed for each size.\nTheir complete final markers and UART strings match exactly.\n\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both versions | Baseline cycles | Candidate cycles | Baseline / candidate iter/sec |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 409.80 / 373.37 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56.05 / 51.07 |\n\nRates use the recorded winner's 184.06 MHz and the candidate's measured median\n167.70 MHz; baseline FPGA routing was not rerun. Every simulation reaches\nEBREAK, reports no OOB access, has valid timing brackets, reports ten iterations,\nand includes the literal `Correct operation validated.` Evaluator CRCs remain\nseedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\nSupplemental 6 KB CRCs are **seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52,\ncrcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART compiler description says -O2;\nthe actual recorded build commands use -O3. Neither contract was altered.\n\nEvidence directory:\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r6s0-jalr-mivgf56j`\n\nIt contains formal-output.log, unit-tests.log and all nine result XMLs,\ncosim-output.log, fpga-output.log, the three original nextpnr logs and routed\nnetlists, jalr-premap.json, mapped-jalr-connectivity.json, timing-seed1.json/SDF,\ntiming-arc-audit.json, workload-comparison.json, all four baseline/candidate\nbenchmark logs, sixk/build.log, and verified-source-sha256.json. All launched\nvalidation commands have completed.\n\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-002-r6s1\nImplemented the forwarding-comparison retiming hypothesis. Correctness and cycle equivalence checks pass, but the measured three-seed FPGA result regresses: median reported Fmax is 169.32 MHz versus 184.06 MHz, and evaluator fitness is 376.98 versus 409.80 (about -8.01%). The proposed four-percent improvement was not observed. The implementation is left in place for the orchestrator to evaluate.\n\nStarting RTL: commit 4c676b5bcfebb0c7fef236028f4828bd467d81d9. Source changes are limited to cores/bench/rtl/{id_stage,forward_unit,core}.sv and cores/bench/test/test_{id_stage,pipeline}.py, plus these explicitly requested notes.\n\nActual implementation:\n\n- ID accepts the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and captures four independent nonzero raw-source identity matches as separate outputs. They share the payload's synchronous reset and !stall capture enable. Flush and input validity do not gate their capture or reset.\n- Forwarding uses those registered matches with the actual EX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write signals. EX/MEM keeps priority, MEM/WB remains the fallback, and the 00/01/10 interface is unchanged. No valid or wb_w_en qualification was added. Both matches survive a near writer losing permission through a trap.\n- The packed bundles, operand/result banks, direct memory address bank, decoder, hazard unit, divider, arithmetic, redirects, RVFI, ports, and nret=1 configuration are unchanged. An active divide continues to use its existing saved operands; the instruction captured at divide acceptance receives fresh matches.\n- ID tests now cover both raw fields, simultaneous near/far matches, x0, all 256 combinations of four representative source/tag values, changed tags and instruction input during holds, invalid inputs, flush priority, and synchronous reset with all four bits set.\n- Pipeline tests add both operand/producer ages, repeated destinations, JAL/JALR trap suppression with eligible older writers, MEM-stage load-write suppression, ALU/branch/JALR/store consumers, prolonged memory holds with valid-cleared WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, unavailable fetch, and dependent consecutive divisions. Existing ISS/RVFI, eight-clock divide acceptance, nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, reset cancellation, stable requests, and accepted-memory-effect assertions remain. Optional PIPELINE_TRACE_PATH records full directed-run traces for starting/candidate comparison.\n\nLocal formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness_ch0, reg_ch0, unique, cover, illegal-instruction, and all generated instruction checks. This was the first RTL attempt; there were no formal failures, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged command was RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh, which auto-selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config. The normal gate retains RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Real divider arithmetic and latency were exercised by the existing real-mode unit suites and ISS comparisons; formal-deep was not run because arithmetic was untouched.\n\nOther local validation:\n\n- The exact requested verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv command passes without warnings.\n- All nine pytest unit runners pass: 144 cocotb cases, including real and ALTOPS ALU/divider and EX-bank tests, decoder, ID, immediate generator, pipeline, and register file. The final additional all-four-match reset assertion was rechecked with the ID runner: pass.\n- The same expanded pipeline test file passes on starting RTL. All 33 protocol runs are byte-identical between versions, including 5,785 retirement records, every retirement cycle, deterministic stall schedules, and accepted memory effects.\n- Unchanged tools.eval.cosim passes both evaluator ELFs. Supplemental full selftest ISS comparisons pass 115 retirements for each version under zero wait and simultaneous imem/dmem stalls with seeds 1, 2, 3, and 42. Complete emitted RVFI/cycle traces match byte-for-byte. Final retirement cycles are respectively 159, 198, 189, 187, and 198.\n- git diff --check passes. Tested candidate RTL/tests were byte-compared with the submitted files; contract source/config copies were also checked unchanged.\n\nFPGA evaluation used the unchanged synthesis script, core_bench_si wrapper, constraints, nextpnr_run.sh, and tools.eval.fpga evaluator, with seeds 1/2/3 for both versions. Every seed routed successfully. These are reported post-route values:\n\n| Version | Seed 1 MHz | Seed 2 MHz | Seed 3 MHz | Median MHz | Fitness | LUT4 | DFF |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Starting | 184.06 | 191.13 | 179.89 | 184.06 | 409.80 | 5490 | 2112 |\n| Retimed | 169.32 | 191.75 | 160.95 | 169.32 | 376.98 | 5502 | 2093 |\n\nThe evaluator's 2 KB, ITERATIONS=10, -O3 binary and default ~22% imem/dmem stall schedule produce exactly 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles for both versions. Fitness above uses the evaluator's unrounded iteration/cycle calculation, not its rounded ipc_coremark JSON display.\n\nEvery routed critical path was inspected. Starting seeds 1 and 3 reproduce the EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF pc[1] paths at 5.43 ns and 5.56 ns; starting seed 2 reports instruction-input/LFSR -> ID register-file operand capture at 5.23 ns. Candidate seeds 1 and 2 instead report ID/EX alu_op[3] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX/MEM normal alu_result[15], at 5.91 ns and 5.22 ns. Candidate seed 3 reports the corresponding alu_op[2] path at 6.21 ns. Thus the earlier seed-2 register-file path did not remain the reported limit.\n\nStructural inspection of synth.json and each of the three pnr.json files confirms four distinct rs{1,2}_{near,far}_match DFFRE cells with the same clock, reset, and enable as ID payload. Traversing the mapped near/far qualification cones to sequential boundaries yields only the corresponding match bits and actual producer reg_write registers, with no live destination-number comparator layer. Near qualification maps to LUT2; far qualification maps to LUT3 with near priority. The synthesized dmem address still aliases the unselected, directly registered EX normal-result bank. This confirms the intended structural change despite the performance regression.\n\nTiming caveat: these are the unchanged toolchain's timing reports, with its existing timing-model/arc coverage limitations. Omitted paths/arcs were not treated as timing margin. No timing models, constraints, report parsing, or memory-address topology were changed to improve the score.\n\nThe evaluator workload and supplemental canonical 6 KB workload are distinct. Both were built from unchanged sources with -O3 and ITERATIONS=10. For each workload/stall seed, both RTL versions ran the identical ELF and produced identical final retirement records, marker timestamps, and UART. Supplemental results:\n\n| Working set | Stall seed | Bracketed cycles, both RTLs | Final retirement cycle, both RTLs | Retirements, both RTLs |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,490,174 | 4,513,268 | 2,937,037 |\n| 2 KB | 2 | 4,492,556 | 4,515,577 | 2,937,037 |\n| 2 KB | 3 | 4,493,908 | 4,516,723 | 2,937,037 |\n| 6 KB | 1 | 32,838,499 | 32,902,122 | 22,093,920 |\n| 6 KB | 2 | 32,840,744 | 32,904,210 | 22,093,920 |\n| 6 KB | 3 | 32,834,771 | 32,898,145 | 22,093,920 |\n\nEvery CoreMark run reaches EBREAK, reports oob=false and bench_bracketed=true, and contains \"Correct operation validated.\" UART text. The 2 KB runs pass unchanged validate_coremark_uart, including seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, and crcfinal=fcaf. The separate 6 KB runs verify canonical crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47; UART also reports seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949. CoreMark uses the unchanged --bench validation mode, not full per-instruction Python ISS execution; full retirement-cycle comparisons above apply to the directed pipeline and selftest runs. The UART compiler-flags string is pre-existing hardcoded text reporting -O2, while both actual compilation commands used -O3; no benchmark files were changed to correct that display.\n\nELF SHA-256:\n\n- Evaluator 2 KB: 0b7d2c770d8de1e601d1d5610b1048ab73b20cbea0f77e96a348059878ec51ff.\n- Supplemental 6 KB: 7a7e47031b65d93f3111c4d7f0fafdcadbb9f507a1ede7b6aa78bd3d356ecf7f.\n\nExecution details and deviations: validation ran on private starting/candidate copies under the provided TMPDIR so shared formal trees and read-only benchmark/evaluator files were not modified. Conda's existing Python/cocotb environment and the existing OSS CAD Suite/cross compiler were selected through PATH. The FPGA build/evaluation steps were invoked directly with BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv and NRET=1, using the unchanged scripts and evaluator. The supplemental 6 KB compile changed only command-line size/output arguments and wrote a separate ELF. There were no architectural deviations or follow-on tuning changes after observing the regression.\n\nEvidence directory: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/forward-r6s1-1b39xze9. It contains starting/ and candidate/ source/build snapshots, candidate/formal-validation.log, candidate/unit-tests.log, candidate/id-final-test.log, both pipeline-traces.jsonl files, both fpga-eval.json files, per-seed nextpnr logs/netlists, selftest-comparison.json, coremark-comparison.json, and structural-check.json.\n\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-001-r7s0\nImplemented the exact funct3-aligned five-bit ALU encoding. Correctness checks pass, but the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation regresses: median Fmax 184.06 -> 151.15 MHz and fitness 409.80 -> 336.53 iterations/s (-17.88%), with identical marker-bracketed cycles. The proposed three-percent gain is not supported by this evaluation. The requested implementation remains in place for the orchestrator to assess.\n\nChanges:\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`: changed only ALU localparam numbers and added comments describing the groups and unsupported codes. ADD=0, SLL=1, SLT=2, SLTU=3, XOR=4, SRL=5, OR=6, AND=7, SUB=8, SRA=13, LUI=15, MUL=16, MULH=17, MULHSU=18, MULHU=19, DIV=20, DIVU=21, REM=22, REMU=23. Unsupported codes are 9..12, 14, and 24..31.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: independent local numeric expectations; all existing real arithmetic, ALTOPS masks, prefix-state, operand-capture, reset, response, bank-ownership and eight-clock assertions retained. Added combinational checks after every preceding five-bit opcode, with four operand pairs, covering every normal operation and unsupported code. Unsupported controls return zero with the request input low and the divider idle. EX independently checks request generation.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_decoder.py`: independent local numeric expectations, retaining shared branch constants and the unchanged runner. Extended OP funct7 testing to all 128 values and both OP-IMM shift legality sweeps to all 128 values. Check exact base/M/modifier mappings, all OP-IMM funct3 operations, LUI, ADD effective-address defaults, and ADD/no-write/no-memory defaults on illegal instructions. Existing legality and reserved-encoding assertions remain.\n- `cores/bench/test/test_ex_result_banks.py`: local ADD/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expectations. Added all 32 controls under all four valid/illegal combinations, resetting before each case. Only legal, valid codes 20..23 can request division and assert div_wait; all other cases stay idle. Unsupported controls also retain zero normal result/address. All prior ownership, forwarding, hold, reset, direct-address and eight-clock completion tests remain in both arithmetic modes.\n\nNo RTL implementation deviation: decoder legality arms, named comparisons, result-selection cases, multiplier expressions and sharing opportunity, real/ALTOPS formulas, divider schedule, five-bit widths, branch encodings and every packed bundle are unchanged. All RTL other than core_pkg.sv is byte-identical to the starting snapshot. `nret: 1`, core ports and RVFI wiring are unchanged. `_helpers.py`, all other cores, evaluator, formal, FPGA and benchmark source files were not edited.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Exact requested lint command passed without warnings: `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`.\n- `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 /opt/homebrew/Caskroom/miniconda/base/bin/python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cores/bench/test/`: 9 runners passed in 26.46 s, with 145 cocotb cases and no failures. This includes 34 ALU cases per real/ALTOPS mode, 5 EX cases per mode, 31 decoder cases, 1 ID case, 22 pipeline cases, 8 immediate cases and 5 register-file cases.\n- Local formal: PASS on the first gate invocation; 53 passed, 0 failed, including liveness, unique, cover, illegal, register, PC and every RV32IM instruction check. Zero RTL fix attempts or retries; no counterexample. Ran unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4`, auto-detecting `wrapper_si.sv`/`checks_si.cfg`. This is the existing ALTOPS formal gate; formal-deep was not run.\n- Stalled ISS cosimulation: the unmodified harness with `--istall --dstall --seed 1/2/3` and saved selftest ELF passes the unmodified ISS comparison on both starting and candidate RTL. All 115 retirements, every compared RVFI field, and every retirement cycle match between designs. Last retirement cycles are respectively 198, 189 and 187.\n- `git diff --check` passed. Verified the evaluated candidate RTL matches the final worktree RTL and staged contract files match the untouched originals.\n\nValidation setup: the system Python lacks pytest/cocotb, so the installed Conda Python was used. The formal script writes under its adjacent riscv-formal tree, which is a shared symlink in this worktree; therefore an unchanged private copy of the formal framework and contract was staged under the permitted TMPDIR. FPGA and ISS evaluation also used unchanged private contract copies. No helper scripts were created. Starting RTL was captured before editing, from HEAD `53c327aef09bbb852fbebae9b62adc224456ce3e`.\n\nFPGA comparison used the unchanged synth.tcl, single-retirement FPGA wrapper, Tang Nano 20K constraints, nextpnr_run.sh and tools.eval.fpga, with fresh synthesis and routes for seeds 1, 2 and 3 on each design. All six routes completed normally. Both evaluations used identical saved CoreMark ELF bytes and the evaluator's unchanged `--bench --istall --dstall` schedule (default PRNG seed 0xDEADBEEF).\n\n| Metric | Starting RTL | Candidate RTL |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Routed seed 1 Fmax (MHz) | 184.06 | 157.23 |\n| Routed seed 2 Fmax (MHz) | 191.13 | 151.15 |\n| Routed seed 3 Fmax (MHz) | 179.89 | 147.67 |\n| Median Fmax (MHz) | 184.06 | 151.15 |\n| Saved-fixture bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Evaluator fitness (iterations/s) | 409.80 | 336.53 |\n| LUT4 resource count | 5,490 | 5,513 |\n| DFF resource count | 2,112 | 2,091 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\n| Block RAMs | 4 | 4 |\n\nInspected every final routed critical-path report (one synchronous path per seed). Field names below were checked against synthesized net aliases; delays are the printed, rounded values.\n\n| Design / seed | Routed path | Logic ns | Routing ns | Total ns |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Starting / 1 | EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding selection -> JALR target -> IF PC[1] | 3.06 | 2.38 | 5.43 |\n| Starting / 2 | Wrapper LFSR[20] -> register-file read/capture selection -> ID/EX rs2_val[3] | 2.61 | 2.63 | 5.23 |\n| Starting / 3 | EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding selection -> JALR target -> IF PC[1] | 3.06 | 2.50 | 5.56 |\n| Candidate / 1 | EX/MEM rd[3] -> forwarding selection -> divider request/control -> quotient_q[2] | 2.57 | 3.79 | 6.36 |\n| Candidate / 2 | EX/MEM rd[1] -> forwarding selection -> divider request/control -> quotient_q[2] | 2.50 | 4.12 | 6.62 |\n| Candidate / 3 | ID/EX alu_op[0] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX normal-result/address bank[2] | 3.42 | 3.36 | 6.77 |\n\nExact launch/capture cells, in table order:\n\n- Starting 1/3: `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_22.Q` -> `cpu.u_if.pc_DFFRE_Q.D`.\n- Starting 2: `lfsr_DFFC_Q_10.Q` -> `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_124.D`.\n- Candidate 1: `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_19.Q` -> `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.u_div.quotient_q_DFFRE_Q_29.D`.\n- Candidate 2: `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_21.Q` -> `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.u_div.quotient_q_DFFRE_Q_29.D`.\n- Candidate 3: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_165.Q` -> `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_91.D`.\n\nBoth synthesized netlists contain exactly one MULT36X36 and four DPX9B cells; all six route reports confirm one MULT36X36 and four BSRAMs. In both netlists all 32 bits of `cpu.u_ex.mem_addr` are directly driven by DFFR Q ports and alias the external memory-address net; no divide-result selector intervenes. Unit tests also retain the direct-address ownership checks. The reported frequencies describe the unchanged tool's modeled timing paths only. No timing margin is inferred for omitted or unreported tool arcs, including DSP/BRAM arcs.\n\nSaved evaluator fixture and supplemental canonical 6 KB validation are separate:\n\n| Workload | Starting/candidate start marker | Starting/candidate stop marker | Identical bracketed cycles |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Saved 2 KB ELF, ITERATIONS=10 | 15,801 | 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 | 56,393 | 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 |\n\n- The evaluator's saved 2 KB ELF was copied unchanged from the run's bench/programs directory, SHA-256 `2646a2933b6a9142988d36cf1ef08e7ffd3c731d504a7206cd953dd9c42fe0f9`. Its UART passes the unchanged evaluator's expected CRCs: seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The fitness figures above are this fixture's results.\n- The supplemental 6 KB ELF was compiled separately under TMPDIR with the existing source list/link script, `-march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -O3 -static -nostartfiles -specs=nano.specs -specs=nosys.specs -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`, and the unchanged header's ITERATIONS=10. SHA-256 `56ec4cceac34e156f2e9a18e625ed537a75bbc4874cae57694daa5b92646367a`. Explicit checks pass for canonical crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52 and crcstate=5e47; observed seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949. This ELF did not replace the evaluator's fixture.\n- Both workloads, on both designs, reach EBREAK with `oob=false`, `maxcycles_hit=false`, valid start/stop markers, 10 iterations and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Entire benchmark markers, UART output and final retirement records are identical between starting and candidate RTL for each workload.\n- The unchanged benchmark header hardcodes the UART compiler-flags string as `-O2 -march=rv32im`; it also appears in the supplemental -O3 run. Actual supplemental build flags are recorded above; the printed string is not used as build provenance. No benchmark metadata or validation constants were changed.\n\nEvidence directory: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r7s0-funct3-bz9j_w06`. It contains unit-tests.log, formal.log and per-check logs, starting/candidate FPGA evaluation JSON, six nextpnr logs and routed JSONs, resource-audit.json, route-comparison.json, stalled-iss.json, coremark-comparison.json, both workload UART/marker outputs, and the supplemental ELF. Performance regression is the implementation concern; local correctness gates passed.\n\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-002-r7s1\n# Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\n\nImplemented the proposed structural change. Correctness and cycle comparisons pass. The unchanged FPGA evaluator reports a regression: median 171.67 MHz and fitness 382.21, versus the starting core's recorded 184.06 MHz (6.73% lower at identical cycles). The predicted 4% improvement was not observed. The starting frequency is the recorded value in core.yaml, not a new starting-RTL P&R measurement.\n\nStarting HEAD: `53c327aef09bbb852fbebae9b62adc224456ce3e`.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: retained `out` as the integer-only combinational output, with ADD through LUI and default zero. Added `mul_out` with the compact encoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU case and default zero. Original signed/unsigned product expressions and all ALTOPS formulas are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: added one 32-bit `mul_result_q` and one `mul_result_sel_q`. Integer/link and multiply payloads capture independently on the existing `!stall` edge with synchronous reset, without validity, ownership, or divide-accept gating. Legal accepted multiplies acquire ownership; jump-link priority and divide-accept priority are preserved. Reset and accepted bubbles clear both selectors; holds retain them. One encoded selection of registered banks drives the existing forwarding and MEM/WB result consumers. `mem_addr` remains wired directly to `reg_q.alu_result`.\n- Extended the three requested test files. Other RTL, decoder legality, nret=1, IO, divider implementation, MEM/WB hold behavior, hazards, retirement, evaluation scripts, benchmark sources, timing models and constraints are unchanged. No extra multiply edge or consumer bubble was added.\n\n## Correctness and timing comparisons\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- Unmodified `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including liveness, cover, unique, illegal and every instruction check. First gate attempt passed; no RTL fix/retry was needed. Used `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`; nret=1 automatically selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/config. A private copy of the formal framework under the supplied TMPDIR kept generated checks away from the shared framework. The installed conda Python supplied click, YAML and cocotb missing from the default Python environment.\n- All nine pytest runners passed: **151 cocotb cases** across ALU real/ALTOPS (34 each), EX ownership real/ALTOPS (7 each), pipeline (25), decoder (30), ID payload (1), immediate generation (8), and register file (5). Two ownership tests were strengthened after the full run to require distinct consecutive products and synchronous reset of an asserted multiply-owner bit, then passed again in both arithmetic modes.\n- Arithmetic coverage retains every existing integer/divider vector and protocol assertion. Added cross-products of signedness/high-half boundaries, 256 additional random operand pairs, rapid opcode/class/default transitions, and zero on each inactive ALU output. Ownership coverage includes all integer/multiply/divider/bubble class transitions; inactive captures under invalid, illegal and jump stimuli; poisoned inactive multiply inputs during divide computation; prolonged holds; saved divide operands; terminal acceptance; reset; and link priority.\n- Mixed pipeline streams cover all four multiply operations, both forwarding ages, repeated destinations, x0, load operands, dependent ALU, branch, JALR, load/store address, store data, multiply and divide instructions. Directed holds exercise valid-cleared MEM/WB forwarding, redirects and division. Accepted memory effects and all meaningful RVFI fields remain compared with the unchanged Python ISS.\n- Starting versus candidate pipeline comparison: **35 streams, 6,572 retirements, 1,622 accepted memory effects, 29,812 driven cycles**. Retirement cycles, architectural RVFI data, accepted-memory cycles/effects, actual ready schedules, divider launches and dispatches match exactly. Only inactive RVFI memory payload is normalized; all operand values, active memory fields and retirement metadata remain exact. Unstalled multiply consumers retain one-cycle spacing, or nine cycles when the consumer is a divide; consecutive divides retain nine-clock dispatch spacing and eight compute clocks.\n- Unchanged C++ selftest cosimulation: 115 retirements per run, exact ISS match and starting/candidate cycle match. Final retirement cycles are 159 with zero wait and 198/203/198 with both-bus stalls at seeds 1/19/81.\n- Fast formal uses ALTOPS as required. Periodic real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; real arithmetic is covered here by exact-result unit tests, mixed ISS comparisons and CoreMark validation.\n\n## CoreMark: keep the two workloads separate\n\nBoth binaries were built with RV32IM/ILP32, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10. Starting and candidate used the same ELF bytes and deterministic bus schedules. Every run reached EBREAK without OOB access, had valid start/stop MMIO markers, and printed `Correct operation validated.`. Entire UART strings and marker records match between RTL versions.\n\n| Workload | Bus schedule | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Marker-bracketed cycles, both RTL versions |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 | Zero wait | 12,124 | 3,495,706 | 3,483,582 |\n| Evaluator, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 | Both buses ~22% stalls | 15,801 | 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 |\n| Supplemental, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000 | Zero wait | 42,886 | 25,410,161 | 25,367,275 |\n| Supplemental, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000 | Both buses ~22% stalls | 56,393 | 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 |\n\nEvaluator 2 KB UART passes the unchanged `validate_coremark_uart`: seedcrc e9f5, list e714, matrix 1fd7, state 8e3a, final fcaf. Supplemental 6 KB separately matches the requested canonical CRCs: list **d4b0**, matrix **be52**, state **5e47**; seedcrc 8a02, final 5949. The 6 KB UART reports 2000 bytes per algorithm and labels itself a 6k performance run. The unchanged port's static compiler-flags text says -O2; the actual build commands used -O3. No benchmark/reporting code was altered.\n\nELF SHA-256:\n\n- Evaluator 2 KB: `b8346ec25bde132f7f489662ecde763b960c1828d8c7b328e15d83d7b89e7fda`\n- Supplemental 6 KB: `040334f9e71bfa359d78795c031a545e8fd5b9ecb75598df1a801a2307efc982`\n\n## Mapping and routed evaluation\n\nUnmodified `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with NRET=1 and the existing single-issue FPGA wrapper, completed all three seeds. All build products were staged under TMPDIR. Yosys 0.64+149 / nextpnr 0.10-45-g98c18d7f were used.\n\nMapped netlist traversal confirms separate 32-flop integer, multiply and divider banks. The DSP lies in the multiply bank's combinational capture cone and is absent from the integer bank's capture cone. MEM/WB's ALU-data cone ends at exactly the 96 registered data bits plus the two registered class bits, with no live-opcode or completion-data boundary. Forwarding and MEM/WB share the selected source. The intermediate selected wire name is optimized into consumers; disconnected netname aliases were not treated as proof of separation. The direct normal address bank remains separate.\n\nResources: **one MULT36X36, four DPX9B block RAMs, 5,515 routed LUT4 equivalents, 2,124 FF**. The recorded starting resources are 5,490 LUT4 / 2,112 FF. Thus the complete mapped design grows by 25 LUT4 equivalents and 12 FF after optimization, despite the source-level addition of 33 register bits. No product registers per multiply operation or multiplier replication were introduced.\n\n| Seed | Final routed MHz | Reported worst path | Logic / routing |\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 175.25 | Register file x15 bit 11 through read mux to ID/EX operand capture | 2.76 / 2.95 ns |\n| 2 | 171.67 | EX/MEM destination metadata through forwarding selection and JALR target arithmetic to PC | 3.06 / 2.77 ns |\n| 3 | 167.56 | EX/MEM destination metadata through forwarding selection and JALR target arithmetic to PC | 3.04 / 2.93 ns |\n\nEvaluator fitness is **382.21** using 10 / 4,491,485 iterations/cycle, median 171.67 MHz. Recomputing the recorded starting frequency at those identical cycles gives 409.80. The evaluator's displayed `ipc_coremark=2e-06` is rounded; it is not used to recompute fitness here. Applying the same median to the supplemental stalled 6 KB run gives 52.28 iterations/second, separately labeled and not substituted into the evaluator result.\n\n## Timing coverage limitation\n\n**The existing result-bank timing coverage gap continues in all three seeds.** Separate diagnostic runs with SDF/detailed reports reproduced the official routed JSON designs exactly, including identical placement, routing and Fmax. No timing model or constraint was changed.\n\nAll three SDFs contain 37 LUT3 cells under `cpu.u_ex.div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0*` with **no IOPATH entries**. Concrete multiply-bank-to-EX and multiply-bank-to-WB connectivity crosses those cells. For example:\n\n- Multiply Q (`mul_result_q_DFFR_Q_15`) to normal EX result D (`reg_q_DFFR_Q_78`) crosses `div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0_4_I0_LUT3_F.I1->F`, which has no timing arc, followed by forwarding/ALU logic with further missing LUT arcs.\n- Multiply Q (`mul_result_q_DFFR_Q`) to MEM/WB ALU D (`u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_67`) crosses `div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0_9_I0_LUT3_F.I1->F`, also absent, and an unmodeled packing passthrough LUT.\n\nInteger and divider bank paths to the same consumer classes also cross missing arcs. These are topology witnesses, not worst-path delay estimates. **Complete bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB delays are unavailable, not zero.** The reported Fmax/fitness regression is the unchanged evaluator's result under this incomplete timing coverage; it does not establish complete physical timing or any frequency gain from separating the bank.\n\nAttempting to re-import saved P&R JSON for timing export alone hit nextpnr's `array2d.h:78` assertion on all seeds. The subsequent diagnostic reroutes resolved report generation and exactly reproduced the successful official routes; they were not additional fitness samples.\n\n## Evidence and deviations\n\nEvidence directory: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/r7s1-multiply-banks/`.\n\nIt contains the immutable starting RTL, validated candidate snapshot, `formal_gate.log`, unit/pipeline/reset logs, `pipeline_traces/`, `selftest_cosim.json`, `coremark_comparison.json`, both ELF binaries, `mapped_separation.json`, `fpga_result.json`, the official three routed seeds, `routed_bank_timing_coverage.json`, and diagnostic per-seed SDF/timing JSON. Final RTL was byte-compared with the validated candidate snapshot.\n\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Validation used private scratch copies because the formal runner writes into its framework directory and the benchmark Makefile creates ELF files. The explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root, which the orchestrator reads and permits. No tracked contract/evaluator/other-core file was changed. Remaining concerns are the measured fitness regression and incomplete timing arcs, not a known functional failure.\n\n\nNOTES hyp-20260906-002-r8s1\nThe synchronous RAM register-file hypothesis is implemented, but **reject it as a fitness improvement**. The unchanged evaluator reports **385.46**, versus **409.80** for a fresh build and three-seed route of the starting RTL: **-5.94%**, with identical marker-window cycles. The implementation remains in the allowed bench RTL/tests for orchestrator review; it is not an accepted replacement for the starting winner.\n\n`reg_file.sv` now contains two coherent 32-by-32 synchronous-read arrays with `ram_style=\"block\"`. Both receive the same accepted nonzero WB write, independently of read enable. Reads, captured nonzero/initialized qualifications, collision flags, and a shared 32-bit collision-data register use the existing `!stall_id` capture decision. Collision data takes priority over initialized qualification, including a first write after reset. Reset suppresses writes and clears the shared initialized bitmap and four response qualification bits; RAM contents and raw read outputs have no reset. Held responses do not depend on subsequent addresses, writes, or bitmap updates.\n\n`id_stage.sv` stores metadata/control in a local operand-free struct and assembles `id_ex_t` directly from that state and the RF's registered responses. There is no second operand capture. Metadata capture, control-zero bubbles, flush-plus-hold behavior, and invalid-fetch payload capture retain their existing policies. `core.sv` only adds the RF read-enable connection. Forwarding priority, retained MEM/WB forwarding during memory holds, saved DIV/REM operands, direct normal EX/MEM memory-address bank, arithmetic, multiplier sharing, ALTOPS, ports, and `core.yaml`/`nret: 1` are unchanged.\n\nOne mapping refinement beyond the advisory plan is `no_rw_check` on the two raw arrays. The initial inferred read-first memories caused Gowin mapping to insert delayed-write registers and additional transparency emulation, despite the explicit response bypass already hiding every raw collision. This attribute removes that redundant emulation; it does not relax the visible write-first contract. Simulators still use the same behavioral SystemVerilog, and the final formal gate also passes with raw collisions unspecified. This pattern is documented in [Yosys memory handling](https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/v0.50/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html), under synchronous SDP with undefined collision behavior. A single collision-data register serves both ports because they can only collide with the same WB transaction.\n\nThe RF tests retain the original value assertions with edge-based sampling and add an independent architectural scoreboard, all 1,024 read-address pairs after initialization and reset, both-port and individual collisions, first writes, x0, populated-memory resets, suppressed reset-edge writes, changing addresses/WB while held, later initialization behind an unwritten held response, and 4,000 randomized transactions. The ID test drives RF responses after the metadata capture edge, so a redundant operand register would fail. Pipeline tests independently model WB/read capture, exercise producer distances and both operands, ALU/JALR/branch/store dependencies, load-use interlocks, DIV/REM, populated-register reset, imem stalls, long dmem holds, and older WB writes during ID holds. Existing ISS, control/payload retention, ordered retirement, and unique accepted-memory-effect assertions remain active.\n\nLocal validation of the submitted RTL:\n\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS, no warnings**. `git diff --check`: **PASS**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 145 cases passed, 0 failures, 0 skips**, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic and EX-bank modes.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed** on the final implementation. Includes channel-0 liveness, unique, cover, illegal instructions, and register/PC checks. No formal counterexample or failed formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts. Earlier structural forms also passed. The unchanged single-retirement wrapper, depths, and ALTOPS configuration were used. Deep arithmetic formal was not run because arithmetic and divider latency were untouched.\n- Starting and final RTL: **34 identical protocol scenarios, 6,093 complete RVFI records with exact retirement-cycle equality, and 1,515 identical accepted memory transactions**. The same deterministic random seeds and directed bus schedules were used. The integer prefix retires on consecutive cycles through WB/read collisions.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest ISS and evaluator CoreMark CRC/UART gates.\n- Additional C++ selftest runs with `--istall --dstall --seed 1/19/99`: **PASS**, 115 retirements each, field-for-field ISS agreement and byte-identical starting/final full RVFI traces including cycle numbers.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: all three seeds route successfully, CoreMark completes, bounds/CRC/UART/marker checks pass. **The fitness improvement criterion fails.**\n\nAll gates ran from unchanged scratch contract copies under `.tmp/rf-sync/`; final gates used `candidate-norw/`. The staged final RTL and tests were checked against the submitted files, the formal staged RTL matches them, all 53 formal task logs contain PASS, and the starting RTL snapshot matches HEAD `7583066de166bd6cd773a698ecfc285232a61ed3`. No evaluator, benchmark source, other core, or core configuration was edited.\n\nFresh unchanged evaluator results:\n\n| Metric | Starting RTL | Final synchronous RF |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Seed 1 routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Seed 2 routed MHz | 191.13 | 148.68 |\n| Seed 3 routed MHz | 179.89 | 190.11 |\n| Median routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Marker-window cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Iterations | 10 | 10 |\n| Fitness, median MHz × 1,000,000 × 10 / cycles | 409.80 | 385.46 |\n| Routed LUT4 | 5,490 | 2,824 |\n| Routed DFF | 2,112 | 1,105 |\n| BSRAM | 4 | 6 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\n\nMapping inspection confirms exactly two `SDPX9B` RF cells, each configured for 36-bit reads and writes: a 512-by-36 physical geometry using 32 addresses and 32 payload bits. Four existing `DPX9B` memory cells remain. Total BSRAM usage is **6/46**, LUT4 **2,824/20,736**, DFF **1,105/15,552**, and MULT36X36 **1/12**. Both RF clocks are identical, read CE matches the collision-data capture CE, write address/data/enable are broadcast coherently, and both primitive reset inputs are tied low. All 64 raw RF response bits are driven directly by RAM outputs, with no fabric operand-output flops. The shared bitmap maps to 31 resettable flops (x0 constant), qualification/collision state to four resettable flops, and collision data to 32 enabled flops. The final memory-libmap output has no extra read-first/transparency emulation cells. Area fell 48.56% in LUT4 and 47.68% in DFF, but this did not improve the median routed throughput.\n\nAll three final routed limiting paths were inspected. Separate runs adding only SDF/detailed report outputs reproduced each gate Fmax exactly; scoring still uses the unchanged gate.\n\n| Seed | Reported limiting path | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | ID rs1 address bit 1 → forwarding selection → JALR resolution → IF PC bit 1 | 5.776 | 3.037 | 2.739 |\n| 2 | ID ALU operation bit 3 → ALU result selection → EX/MEM normal result bit 2 | 6.726 | 3.420 | 3.306 |\n| 3 | ID ALU operation bit 3 → ALU result selection → EX/MEM normal result bit 0 | 5.260 | 2.886 | 2.374 |\n\n**Timing coverage remains incomplete.** Every RF primitive's SDF contains 36 CLKB-to-DO arcs at **2.259 ns**, plus read-address setup/hold checks. However, each bank's five active ADB address pins is driven through LUT2 cells with no exported IOPATH arcs; those connected read-address endpoints are absent from detailed timing reports in all seeds. On the output side, connectivity walks from DO0 of each bank reach EX registers through LUT3 cells whose input-to-output arcs are also absent in every seed. Example missing arcs are `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.a_LUT3_F_19_I2_MUX2_LUT5_O_I0_LUT3_F.I1→F` and the analogous `b_LUT3_F_11...` cell for port 2. The physical RAM clock-to-output model therefore does not establish complete RAM-output-to-EX timing. Missing arcs are **unknown, not zero delay**; no complete address or operand-path timing claim is made, and no timing library, constraint, or evaluator was changed. This concern is additional to the measured fitness regression.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator uses **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**. The requested **6 KB** run was built separately from byte-identical benchmark sources with the existing `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options. Starting and final binaries ran identical ELFs and `--bench --istall --dstall` schedules for each size. Both sizes produced byte-identical final RVFI, markers, and UART output; both completed with EBREAK and `oob:false`.\n\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both implementations | Marker-window cycles, both | Starting / final iter/sec |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 409.80 / 385.46 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 56.05 / 52.72 |\n\nThe rates use the independently routed medians and the unrounded iteration/cycle ratios, not the evaluator's rounded display of IPC. Both UARTs contain the literal `Correct operation validated.` and ten iterations. Evaluator CRCs are seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The requested **6 KB CRCs are crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47**, additionally seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949. The unchanged UART compiler-description string says -O2; actual recorded compilation commands use -O3. The supplemental measurement is not substituted into the evaluator.\n\nEvidence is under `.tmp/rf-sync/`: `norw-unit.log`, final result XMLs, `norw-formal.log`, `norw-cosim.log`, `norw-fpga.log`, `baseline-fpga.log`, `baseline-protocol-v2.jsonl`, `norw-protocol.jsonl`, `norw-stalled-iss.json`, baseline/final workload JSONL files, `sixk-build.log`, `rf-storage-evidence.json`, `norw-rf-premap.json`, `norw-rf-libmap.json`, `timing-summary.json`, `rf-output-timing-coverage.json`, three `norw-timing-seed*.json/.sdf/.log` exports, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. The notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5; source changes are confined to the six permitted bench RTL/test files.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
