{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat formal/checks_si.cfg cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv fpga/core_bench_si.sv'","aggregated_output":"[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n// rtl/wb_stage.sv\n//\n// Write-back stage. Pure combinational mux that selects between the\n// loaded-data path (mem_to_reg=1) and the ALU-result path. Drives the\n// regfile write port; the regfile itself stalls on x0 and gates on\n// w_en.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds rd_wdata (= w_data when w_en=1, else 0).\nmodule wb_stage (\n  // mem_wb_t fields beyond reg_write/rd/mem_to_reg/read_data/alu_result\n  // (pc, mem_*, rs?_*, instr) are RVFI-only and read at top level.\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  input  mem_wb_t  in,\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  output logic               w_en,\n  output logic [4:0]         w_addr,\n  output logic [31:0]        w_data\n);\n\n  always_comb begin\n    w_en   = in.ctrl.reg_write && in.valid;\n    w_addr = in.rd;\n    w_data = in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? in.read_data : in.alu_result;\n  end\n\nendmodule\n// rtl/mem_stage.sv\n//\n// Memory access stage. Drives the dmem read/write ports combinationally\n// from the EX/MEM register, and sign/zero-extends the loaded data into\n// the MEM/WB register.\n//\n// Byte-lane discipline:\n//   - Write data is replicated across all four byte lanes; the\n//     byte-mask (mem_wmask) selects the actual destination bytes.\n//   - Load: shift the raw word right by `addr[1:0]*8`, then sign- or\n//     zero-extend the low 8/16 bits per the LB/LBU/LH/LHU encoding.\n//   - For RVFI ALIGNED_MEM, mem_addr is reported word-aligned and the\n//     byte position is captured in mem_rmask / mem_wmask.\n//\n// Latency:        1 cycle (MEM/WB register clocked here).\n// RVFI fields:    feeds mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata,\n//                 mem_wdata, plus rd_wdata via the loaded-data path.\nmodule mem_stage (\n  input  logic               clock,\n  input  logic               reset,\n  // hold_wb: dmem stall is in effect. MEM/WB's data fields are RETAINED\n  // (so the previously-retired LOAD's `rd` / `read_data` / `ctrl.reg_write`\n  // remain visible to forward_unit), but `valid` is cleared so:\n  //   - rvfi_order doesn't increment for held cycles\n  //   - wb_stage doesn't write the regfile twice\n  // Zeroing the whole register would lose the LOAD's load_data, breaking\n  // MEM/WB->EX forwarding for any dependent instruction that's also held\n  // in ID/EX during the stall (e.g. a BNE that consumes a LOAD's result).\n  input  logic               hold_wb,\n  // ex_mem_t carries branch_taken / branch_target / pc_next / rs?_val\n  // for downstream RVFI use; mem_stage only consumes a subset, so the\n  // remaining fields look unused from this module's perspective.\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  input  ex_mem_t  in,\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  // Direct normal-result bank: DIV/REM cannot issue a memory access.\n  // The selected in.alu_result still feeds MEM/WB writeback below.\n  input  logic [31:0]        address,\n  // dmem interface\n  output logic [31:0]        dmem_addr,\n  output logic [31:0]        dmem_wdata,\n  input  logic [31:0]        dmem_rdata,\n  output logic [3:0]         dmem_wen,\n  output logic               dmem_ren,\n  // MEM/WB register output\n  output mem_wb_t  out\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] wdata_rep;\n  logic [3:0]  byte_mask;\n  logic [31:0] aligned_addr;\n  // shifted[31:16] is dropped on byte/halfword loads; only [15:0] feeds\n  // the sign/zero-extension mux.\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  logic [31:0] shifted;\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  logic [7:0]  byte_val;\n  logic [15:0] hword_val;\n  logic [31:0] load_data;\n\n  // Misaligned mem-access trap. RV32I requires word-aligned LW/SW and\n  // halfword-aligned LH/LHU/SH; byte ops are always aligned.\n  // riscv-formal's RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM contract demands rvfi_trap=1\n  // when the effective byte address is not aligned to the access width.\n  // On trap, the dmem ports are gated off and ctrl propagates is_illegal.\n  logic mem_misalign;\n  logic mem_op;\n  ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{in.write_data[7:0]}};\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{in.write_data[15:0]}};\n      default: wdata_rep = in.write_data;\n    endcase\n\n    // Byte-lane mask shifted to addr[1:0] * 1.\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n      2'd0:    byte_mask = (4'b0001 << address[1:0]);\n      2'd1:    byte_mask = (4'b0011 << address[1:0]);\n      default: byte_mask = 4'b1111;\n    endcase\n\n    mem_op       = in.ctrl.mem_read || in.ctrl.mem_write;\n    mem_misalign = mem_op && (\n                     (in.ctrl.mem_width == 2'd2 && address[1:0] != 2'b00) ||\n                     (in.ctrl.mem_width == 2'd1 && address[0]   != 1'b0)\n                     // 2'd0 (byte) is never misaligned.\n                   );\n\n    ctrl_with_trap = in.ctrl;\n    if (mem_misalign) begin\n      ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal = 1'b1;\n      ctrl_with_trap.reg_write  = 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    // Combinational dmem outputs — gated off on misalign.\n    dmem_addr  = address;\n    dmem_wdata = wdata_rep;\n    dmem_wen   = (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n    dmem_ren   = in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign;\n\n    // Load extraction: shift right then sign/zero-extend the low N bits.\n    shifted   = dmem_rdata >> (address[1:0] * 8);\n    byte_val  = shifted[7:0];\n    hword_val = shifted[15:0];\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n      2'd0:    load_data = in.ctrl.mem_sext\n                         ? {{24{byte_val[7]}},  byte_val}\n                         : {24'b0,              byte_val};\n      2'd1:    load_data = in.ctrl.mem_sext\n                         ? {{16{hword_val[15]}}, hword_val}\n                         : {16'b0,               hword_val};\n      default: load_data = dmem_rdata;\n    endcase\n\n    // RVFI ALIGNED_MEM expects word-aligned mem_addr; mem_addr=0 if no access.\n    aligned_addr = (mem_op && !mem_misalign)\n                 ? {address[31:2], 2'b00}\n                 : 32'b0;\n  end\n\n  // ── MEM/WB register ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  mem_wb_t reg_q;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      reg_q <= '0;\n    end else if (hold_wb) begin\n      // Clear valid (no double-retire / no double-regfile-write), but\n      // KEEP every other field. Forwarding from MEM/WB to a stalled\n      // dependent in ID/EX needs the held LOAD's rd/read_data/ctrl\n      // alive for as many cycles as the dmem stall lasts.\n      reg_q.valid <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      reg_q.pc         <= in.pc;\n      reg_q.alu_result <= in.alu_result;\n      reg_q.read_data  <= load_data;\n      reg_q.rd         <= in.rd;\n      reg_q.rs1_addr   <= in.rs1_addr;\n      reg_q.rs2_addr   <= in.rs2_addr;\n      reg_q.rs1_val    <= in.rs1_val;\n      reg_q.rs2_val    <= in.rs2_val;\n      reg_q.pc_next    <= in.pc_next;\n      reg_q.mem_addr   <= aligned_addr;\n      reg_q.mem_rdata  <= dmem_rdata;\n      reg_q.mem_wdata  <= wdata_rep;\n      reg_q.mem_wmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n      reg_q.mem_rmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n      reg_q.ctrl       <= ctrl_with_trap;\n      reg_q.instr      <= in.instr;\n      reg_q.valid      <= in.valid;\n    end\n  end\n\n  assign out = reg_q;\n\nendmodule\n// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the full rationale on LFSR-driven imem, dmem\n// model, and the XOR-reduce LED that keeps RVFI fan-out alive through\n// dead-output elimination.\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI fan-out (NRET=1 contract).\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  core cpu (\n    .clock            (clock),\n    .reset            (reset),\n    .io_imemAddr      (imem_addr),\n    .io_imemData      (lfsr),\n    .io_imemReady     (1'b1),\n    .io_dmemAddr      (dmem_addr),\n    .io_dmemWData     (dmem_wdata),\n    .io_dmemRData     (dmem_rdata),\n    .io_dmemWEn       (dmem_wen),\n    .io_dmemREn       (dmem_ren),\n    .io_dmemReady     (1'b1),\n    .io_rvfi_valid_0    (rvfi_valid_0),\n    .io_rvfi_order_0    (rvfi_order_0),\n    .io_rvfi_insn_0     (rvfi_insn_0),\n    .io_rvfi_trap_0     (rvfi_trap_0),\n    .io_rvfi_halt_0     (rvfi_halt_0),\n    .io_rvfi_intr_0     (rvfi_intr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mode_0     (rvfi_mode_0),\n    .io_rvfi_ixl_0      (rvfi_ixl_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0 (rvfi_rs1_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0(rvfi_rs1_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0 (rvfi_rs2_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0(rvfi_rs2_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_addr_0  (rvfi_rd_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0 (rvfi_rd_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0 (rvfi_pc_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0 (rvfi_pc_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_addr_0 (rvfi_mem_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0(rvfi_mem_rmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0(rvfi_mem_wmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0(rvfi_mem_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0(rvfi_mem_wdata_0)\n  );\n\n  // XOR-reduce all CPU outputs to a single LED bit. Without this,\n  // dead-output elimination would prune the RVFI fan-out.\n  assign led = ^{rvfi_valid_0, rvfi_order_0, rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0,\n                 rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0, rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0,\n                 rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0, rvfi_rd_wdata_0,\n                 rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0, rvfi_mem_addr_0,\n                 rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0,\n                 imem_addr, dmem_rdata};\n\nendmodule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -o -i '.{0,110}(writeback|write-back|unified|operand capture|request.stage|setup.stage|mem/wb).{0,180}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -o -i '.{0,110}(writeback|write-back|unified|operand capture|request.stage|setup.stage|mem/wb).{0,180}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"2: path. Preserve decoder legality,\\nreal load-use interlocks, redirect priority, dmem hold behavior, retained\\nMEM/WB forwarding data, and the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\\n\\nPredict a modest improvement in CoreMark iterations per cycle with nearly\\nunchanged Fmax. The estimated two-percen\n2:ther. The nonzero load-destination guard remains.\\nDecoder legality, pipeline depth, bus/redirect priorities, MEM/WB hold behavior,\\nand the nret=1 top-level port set are unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations required by validation\\n\\n- The first formal run passed 52 checks and failed `reg_ch0` at step 20\n2:n RVFI.\\n- `mem_stage.sv` now repairs these retirement metadata fields from the preceding\\n  completed load's MEM/WB data. This path does not feed execution operands or\\n  store data. Retained WB data remains eligible through memory holds.\\n- EX/MEM forwarding excludes loads, whose result is una\n2:vailable at that stage.\\n  Real consumers retain their interlock and existing MEM/WB forwarding. This\\n  also preserves the old raw register value after a misaligned load that traps\\n  without writing its destination; such loads are excluded from metadata repair.\\\n2:wn opcodes keep conservative stalls. Added misaligned-load and\\ncombined-wait regressions, including retained MEM/WB forwarding while a branch\\nredirect overlaps both bus stalls.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings.\\n- Formal: PASS, **53 passed / 0 faile\n3:B draining cannot alter an active operation. Hold\\nID/EX and fetch while its result is unavailable. Let older MEM/WB work\\ndrain normally: when EX/MEM is ready, emit a bubble with zeroed control\\nduring the computation, not a repeated copy of the older instruction.\\nAn actual dmem stall still ho\n3:s,\\nstores and retirements drain once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\\nDecoder legality, traps, buses, pipeline boundaries, nret=1 \n4:an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n## Deviations and scope\\n\\n- Added standa\n4:mbinational loops.\\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This \n5:ls. The latter\\n  compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and explicitly\\n  require EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, selector/bundle\\n  holds, redirects simultaneous with each bus stall, blocking division,\\n  illegal-default ADD, and absence of wrong-path stores/div\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\\nseparated blocking division\n6:nd the two registered data banks. This selected value must feed\\nboth the existing EX/MEM forwarding path and MEM/WB capture, so a dependent\\nALU instruction, branch, JALR, store, multiply, or divide sees the completed\\ndivide at exactly the existing cycle. Never forward the stale normal bank\\nf\n6:acketed cycles; this is an unmeasured estimate. The output bank\\nmux can lengthen EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding and MEM/WB paths, and decode can\\nbecome limiting. Measure those paths and all three routed seeds rather\\nthan assuming the removed input path determines the new median. Preserve\\nthe nret=1\n6:eset as specified. Assemble the\\nselected out.alu_result after the register boundary for both forwarding\\nand MEM/WB, without recapturing the divider's old result on its completion\\nedge. Preserve metadata, operand snapshots, launch/accept timing, link\\nresults, redirects, and existing drain and\n6:eset as specified. Assemble the\\nselected out.alu_result after the register boundary for both forwarding\\nand MEM/WB, without recapturing the divider's old result on its completion\\nedge. Preserve metadata, operand snapshots, launch/accept timing, link\\nresults, redirects, and existing drain and\n6:r-bracketed cycles. The estimated 5% improvement was not reached. Full\\ntiming coverage of the new forwarding/writeback paths remains a concern,\\ndescribed below; the reported fitness is not a complete physical timing proof.\\n\\ndiv_unit now exports result_q directly as registered_result, separately\n6:of the two registered banks controlled only by that registered selector.\\nBoth existing EX/MEM forwarding and MEM/WB result capture consume this value.\\nThe nret=1 external ports, retirement boundary, operand snapshots, division\\nlaunch/accept equations, hazards, redirects, traps, and retained W\n6:ction, and alignment checks. Memory instructions always\\nown the normal bank. The selected result still feeds MEM/WB alu_result and\\nEX/MEM forwarding. This restores four block RAMs without changing an accepted\\nmemory request, adding registers, or adding an output-side opcode decoder.\\nThe inac\n6:les omit IOPATH arcs for the LUT3 cells implementing the\\nbank selector. The detailed JSON reports contain no MEM/WB alu_result\\nendpoints. Complete bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB timing therefore cannot be\\nestablished from these unchanged reports. This is an observed reporting gap,\\nnot evidence th\n6: delays, and setup) are:\\n\\n| Seed | Result banks/selector to normal EX/MEM result | Result banks/selector to MEM/WB result | Divider data bank alone to MEM/WB result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 12.593 ns plus 5 unknown LUT arcs | 2.278 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc | 1.480 ns plus 1 unknown\n7:divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training ev\n7:divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training ev\n8: path.\\n\\nThe preceding result-bank experiment documented missing LUT3 timing arcs and\\nincomplete forwarding/writeback coverage. One supplemental seed-1 route added\\nonly JSON timing and SDF exports to the same route options; it reproduced\\n108.23 MHz and a 9.240 ns reported path. Its SDF has no I\n8:TH arcs for any of\\nthe 32 LUT3 result-bank muxes, and its detailed JSON lists zero D endpoints\\namong the 32 MEM/WB alu_result flops. The coverage gap therefore persists in\\nthe candidate. All Fmax and rate numbers above are the unchanged evaluator's\\nreported measurements, not proof of complet\n9:ths,\\nbut only 30 of 32 completion data endpoints and 7 of 32 normal EX result\\ndata endpoints are listed. No MEM/WB result data endpoint is listed.\\nAll 1,142 LUT3 cells lack exported SDF IOPATH arcs, including the 32 cells\\nimplementing the retained EX result-bank mux. Consequently the reports\n9: do\\nnot establish complete request, iteration, forwarding, writeback or normal\\nEX timing. An omitted path or cell arc is unknown, not zero delay. The\\nheadline fitness regression is the unchanged evaluator's result, not a\\nfully characterized phys\n11:etained.\\n\\nThe winner's saved notes document missing LUT3 bank-selection IOPATH arcs\\nand omitted forwarding/writeback paths in timing exports. Those limitations\\nare not resolved by this change. The complete *reported* paths above do\\nnot establish complete physical timing closure. Unreported pat\n12:ase the gain.\\nInspect request capture, recurrence update, completion, normal EX, and\\nresult-bank forwarding/writeback timing across the unchanged three-seed\\nevaluation; disclose any missing timing coverage rather than assigning\\nomitted arcs zero delay. Compare identical binaries and marker wind\n13:& !in.early_jal. Keep the original ctrl.is_jump and\\nctrl.is_jalr unchanged for architectural execution, link writeback, and\\ntraps. Capture the bit with the instruction and hold it through dmem and\\ndivider stalls. Clear it on reset/flush; the existing invalid-fetch NOP\\ndecodes to zero for this b\n15:ently rely on those controls,\\nso stale payload must never assert a write, request, redirect or launch.\\nKeep MEM/WB unchanged, including its deliberate retention of forwarding\\ndata and reg_write metadata when a dmem hold clears retirement valid.\\n\\nExpect five percent higher fitness from short\n15:-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\\n\\nTests retain all \n15:rted tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish compl\n15:ete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multi\n16:ever act as producers,\\nredirect, write, or issue requests.\\nThe existing EX/MEM bubble behavior and retained MEM/WB forwarding data\\nremain intact. This is localized predictor recovery, not an overhaul of\\nthe downstream pipeline or ALU.\\n\\nThe estimated net gain is 3%, with low confidence. A f\n16:ribe raw invalid payloads and control squashes. The divider,\\nALU, result banks, downstream bubbles, retained MEM/WB forwarding data,\\ndirectly registered memory-address path, nret=1 interface, decoder, and all\\nevaluator contracts are unchanged. No predictor table, training, return stack,\\nor a\n17:1_sel encoding, including\\nits default selection of in.rs1_val. Preserve forward_unit's existing\\nEX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and all load-use interlocks.\\n\\nKeep the existing full-width rs1 + in.imm addition for target bits\\n[31:2], with its original carry propagation and modulo-2^32 semantics.\n17:\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JALR with register-file, immediate EX/MEM, and MEM/WB\\noperands, competing writers, x0, load-use bubbles, and divider-result\\nforwarding. Include aligned and misaligned targets, discarded stores,\\nconsecutive redirects, and overlappi\n17:\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JALR with register-file, immediate EX/MEM, and MEM/WB\\noperands, competing writers, x0, load-use bubbles, and divider-result\\nforwarding. Include aligned and misaligned targets, discarded stores,\\nconsecutive redirects, and overlappi\n17: in.imm[0])`. A separate one-bit case mux uses\\nthe existing forwarding encoding: 1 selects EX/MEM, 2 selects MEM/WB, and both\\n0 and 3 default to the ID/EX value. Both JALR target bit 1 and its misalignment\\npredicate consume that selected bit directly. The original full-width\\n`rs1 + in.imm` s\n17:dress bank. All new EX tests run in real and ALTOPS\\nmodes.\\n\\n`test_pipeline.py` adds register-file, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, competing-writer, x0,\\nload-use, and divider-result JALR cases with aligned/misaligned sums and negative\\nimmediates. Each runs with zero-wait and deterministic random bus stalls\n17:ng imem/dmem holds exercise\\nrepeated aligned redirects and repeatedly suppressed misaligned redirects while\\nMEM/WB retains the needed source. Per-cycle assertions preserve fetch redirect\\npriority, all existing bank/metadata holds, and accepted memory effects. Complete\\nretirement streams are \n17:r truth tables were checked\\nagainst addition for all 16 base/immediate combinations; synthesis inverted\\nthe MEM/WB predicate and absorbed its polarity downstream. Each cell depends\\nonly on its own base low bits and the two immediate bits. EX/MEM and MEM/WB\\nbase bits retain their existing res\n17:ult-bank/writeback selection. Transitive\\nregistered-leaf inspection confirms no forwarding register-tag bits enter any\\ncandidate predicate. The three outputs then enter forwarding-controlled LUT/M\n18:hypothesis\": \"Make one structural change: compute and register forwarding dependency\\nmatches alongside ID/EX operand capture, leaving the pipeline depth,\\noperand-data banks, two-bit forwarding selector interface, and instruction\\nscheduling unchanged. Do not add a predictor or alter arithmetic.\\n\\nFor \n18:ts total for rs1 and rs2. At an actual instruction capture,\\nthose older instructions advance into EX/MEM and MEM/WB respectively. Keep\\nthese bits as separate registered ID-stage outputs rather than changing\\nthe packed pipeline bundles. Give them the same synchronous reset and\\ncapture/hold ru\n18:he registered matches\\nqualified by the actual destination stage's ctrl.reg_write. Select EX/MEM\\nfirst, then MEM/WB, then the captured register-file operand. Preserve both\\nmatches until this selection: a nearer instruction can lose write permission\\nthrough its existing trap logic, and an elig\n18:ot authorize a bypass. Use the\\nsame producer-control qualification as the current implementation, including\\nMEM/WB ctrl.reg_write without an additional valid or wb_w_en gate. A memory\\nhold intentionally clears MEM/WB valid while retaining its forwarding data\\nand control. Keep x0 excluded by \n18:ge relationships under every stall. A dmem hold\\nretains the match bits, ID/EX instruction, EX/MEM owner, and MEM/WB forwarding\\npayload. A load-use flush leaves a control-zero bubble; the consumer's\\nmatches are recomputed when it is finally captured, when its load producer\\nis in EX/MEM and wi\n18:ll next be in MEM/WB. During blocking division, use these\\nmatches for the initial request's forwarded operands, then retain the\\nexisting saved divide operands as authoritative while older stages dra\n18:tch inputs. Qualify each with the corresponding existing\\nstage reg_write control, retaining EX/MEM priority, MEM/WB fallback,\\nand the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding. Preserve forwarding from\\nMEM/WB's retained payload when its retirement valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"des\n18:tch inputs. Qualify each with the corresponding existing\\nstage reg_write control, retaining EX/MEM priority, MEM/WB fallback,\\nand the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding. Preserve forwarding from\\nMEM/WB's retained payload when its retirement valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"des\n18:ty do not gate their capture or reset.\\n- Forwarding uses those registered matches with the actual EX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write signals. EX/MEM keeps priority, MEM/WB remains the fallback, and the 00/01/10 interface is unchanged. No valid or wb_w_en qualification was added. Both matches surv\n18: -> IF pc[1] paths at 5.43 ns and 5.56 ns; starting seed 2 reports instruction-input/LFSR -> ID register-file operand capture at 5.23 ns. Candidate seeds 1 and 2 instead report ID/EX alu_op[3] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX/MEM normal alu_result[15], at 5.91 ns and 5.22 ns. Candidate seed 3 reports\n19: EX/MEM entry instead of accepting the younger EX result.\\nThe current MEM owner still advances normally into MEM/WB, including\\nits legitimate memory transaction and retirement. Add a younger-EX kill\\ninput that suppresses divider request/accept and effective div_wait on\\nthat repair cycle, and\n19:mem hold. Keep the dmem-stall equation independent of repair and\\npreserve load-use interlocking and retained MEM/WB forwarding data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test full tags/targets, all counters, collision replacement, reset,\\ninvalidation, stale training s\n19:mem hold. Keep the dmem-stall equation independent of repair and\\npreserve load-use interlocking and retained MEM/WB forwarding data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test full tags/targets, all counters, collision replacement, reset,\\ninvalidation, stale training s\n21:ll class selection using only\\nregistered selectors and registered data. Both existing EX/MEM forwarding\\nand MEM/WB capture consume that same selected value. There must be no\\nadditional completion edge, live opcode qualification, or extra consumer\\nbubble. Consecutive multiplies and immediate \n21:ompute clocks, nine-clock consecutive-divide dispatch spacing,\\nsaved divide operands and reset cancellation. MEM/WB must continue to\\nretain its forwarding payload and reg_write control when a memory hold\\nclears retirement valid. No hazard or retirement policy changes are needed.\\n\\nThe expect\n21:th selectors; holds retain them. One encoded selection of registered banks drives the existing forwarding and MEM/WB result consumers. `mem_addr` remains wired directly to `reg_q.alu_result`.\\n- Extended the three requested test files. Other RTL, decoder legality, nret=1, IO, divider implementat\n21:ion, MEM/WB hold behavior, hazards, retirement, evaluation scripts, benchmark sources, timing models and constraints are unchanged. No extra multiply edge or consumer bubble was added.\\n\\n## \n21:JALR, load/store address, store data, multiply and divide instructions. Directed holds exercise valid-cleared MEM/WB forwarding, redirects and division. Accepted memory effects and all meaningful RVFI fields remain compared with the unchanged Python ISS.\\n- Starting versus candidate pipeline com\n21:SP lies in the multiply bank's combinational capture cone and is absent from the integer bank's capture cone. MEM/WB's ALU-data cone ends at exactly the 96 registered data bits plus the two registered class bits, with no live-opcode or completion-data boundary. Forwarding and MEM/WB share the se\n21:ic / routing |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 175.25 | Register file x15 bit 11 through read mux to ID/EX operand capture | 2.76 / 2.95 ns |\\n| 2 | 171.67 | EX/MEM destination metadata through forwarding selection and JALR target arithmetic to PC | 3.06 / 2.77 ns |\\n| 3 | 167.56 | EX/MEM destination \n21:arc, followed by forwarding/ALU logic with further missing LUT arcs.\\n- Multiply Q (`mul_result_q_DFFR_Q`) to MEM/WB ALU D (`u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_67`) crosses `div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0_9_I0_LUT3_F.I1->F`, also absent, and an unmodeled packing passthrough LUT.\\n\\nInteger and divider bank paths\n22:d/squash logic, ending at an ID control RESET pin. Seed 1 begins at ID state; seeds 2 and 3 begin at retained MEM/WB instruction/register-address bits. The predictor tag-to-PC path remains present through eight mapped combinational cells including muxes and a packing passthrough. This is a struc\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Absorb ID operand capture into synchronous register-file RAM banks\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural entries in experiments/log.jsonl and LESSONS.md show that\\nchanging storage boun\n24: from\\npipeline control. Its reported seed-2 limit was instruction input through\\nregister-file reading to ID operand capture; seeds 1 and 3 instead limited\\non forwarding through JALR resolution. The r7s1 multiply-bank experiment\\nagain exposed register-file reads and forwarding as competing limits.\\nTh\n24:orwarding-comparison retiming also regressed. No recorded\\nstructural attempt moves register-file storage and operand capture into\\nsynchronous RAM. This experiment removes the large fabric read network\\nby changing where operands are stored and clocked, while retaining the\\ncurrent instruction schedule.\n24: from\\npipeline control. Its reported seed-2 limit was instruction input through\\nregister-file reading to ID operand capture; seeds 1 and 3 instead limited\\non forwarding through JALR resolution. The r7s1 multiply-bank experiment\\nagain exposed register-file reads and forwarding as competing limits.\\nTh\n24:orwarding-comparison retiming also regressed. No recorded\\nstructural attempt moves register-file storage and operand capture into\\nsynchronous RAM. This experiment removes the large fabric read network\\nby changing where operands are stored and clocked, while retaining the\\ncurrent instruction schedule.\n24:ver associated with the preceding instruction.\\nKeep ID metadata/control storage and the existing EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB\\nforwarding priority intact, including retained WB forwarding during\\ndmem stalls and captured DIV/REM operands during execution.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is fewer register-storage\n24:struct and assembles `id_ex_t` directly from that state and the RF's registered responses. There is no second operand capture. Metadata capture, control-zero bubbles, flush-plus-hold behavior, and invalid-fetch payload capture retain their existing policies. `core.sv` only adds the RF read-enable connect\n24:ion. Forwarding priority, retained MEM/WB forwarding during memory holds, saved DIV/REM operands, direct normal EX/MEM memory-address bank, arithmetic, multiplier sharing, ALTOPS, ports, and `core.yaml`/`nret: 1` are unch\n24:\"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r8s1 (regression, -5.94%): Absorbing ID/EX operand capture into two synchronous SDPX9B register-file banks nearly halved LUT/DFF use without adding CoreMark cycles, but forwarding/JALR and ALU-result selection still limited reported Fmax;\n25:warding producers and memory\\nrequests still see zero controls for invalid EX/MEM entries. Preserve\\nretained MEM/WB forwarding data during dmem holds, including valid=0\\nretention after retirement; do not add a MEM/WB-valid forwarding gate.\\n\\nEX shares forwarded equality, signed-less-than, and\n25:ly registered validity. Preserve full synchronous reset, exact\\nheld-owner association, decoder legality, and operand capture.\\nRemove both repair-driven control clears and validity-driven control\\ninput masks; do not reintroduce a whole-bundle output mask.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"descriptio\n25:ly registered validity. Preserve full synchronous reset, exact\\nheld-owner association, decoder legality, and operand capture.\\nRemove both repair-driven control clears and validity-driven control\\ninput masks; do not reintroduce a whole-bundle output mask.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"descriptio\n26:tion, predictor, or forwarding source.\\n\\nFor each raw source register, compute the existing live EX/MEM and\\nMEM/WB matches in parallel:\\n  E = ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 0 && ex_mem_rd == source;\\n  W = mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 0 && mem_wb_rd == source.\\nEmit a three-bit vector with bit 0 \n26: !(E || W), bit 1 = E, and\\nbit 2 = !E && W. Thus exactly one of the captured ID operand, EX/MEM\\nresult, and MEM/WB writeback data is selected, preserving youngest\\neligible writer priority. Remove the two-bit encode/decode interface\\nthroughout the core; do not generate the new enables by deco\n26:d raw-operand RVFI contract.\\n\\nPreserve the original forwarding producer qualifications. In\\nparticular, use MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write rather than retirement valid or\\nwb_w_en: an already-retired writer's retained data must still forward\\nduring a dmem hold. Keep trap-suppressed writers ineligible,\n26:rived from the existing live tag matches. Preserve\\nnonzero destination checks, EX/MEM priority, and retained MEM/WB\\neligibility. Add no registers, valid qualification, or stall gating.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the new forwarding vectors directly between forward_unit\n26:0, true load-use dependencies, and trap-suppressed writes.\\nInclude long dmem holds retaining a valid-cleared MEM/WB source,\\noverlapping imem stalls, and consecutive dependent divides.\\nCompare full RVFI and accepted memory effects against the unchanged\\nISS and verify baseline-equivalent retir\n26:rived from the existing live tag matches. Preserve\\nnonzero destination checks, EX/MEM priority, and retained MEM/WB\\neligibility. Add no registers, valid qualification, or stall gating.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the new forwarding vectors directly between forward_unit\n26:0, true load-use dependencies, and trap-suppressed writes.\\nInclude long dmem holds retaining a valid-cleared MEM/WB source,\\noverlapping imem stalls, and consecutive dependent divides.\\nCompare full RVFI and accepted memory effects against the unchanged\\nISS and verify baseline-equivalent retir\n26: `cores/bench/rtl/{forward_unit,core,ex_stage}.sv` changed in RTL.\\n- `forward_unit` computes live EX/MEM and MEM/WB tag matches independently\\n  and directly emits `{!E && W, E, !(E || W)}` for each source. The two-bit\\n  forwarding interface is removed. EX/MEM wins competing matches; x0 is\\n  \n26:/ `fwd_rs2_en`. Both producer\\n  qualifications remain `ctrl.reg_write`, including retained, valid-cleared\\n  MEM/WB writers during dmem holds. No valid, writeback-enable, load, or\\n  stall qualification was added to the forwarding matches.\\n- `ex_stage` uses three masked candidates for each sem\n27:he IF PC as the limiting\\npath in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. Seed 2 instead limits on\\nregister-file operand capture. The structural r6s1 forwarding retime,\\nr7s1 multiply bank, and r8s1 synchronous register file all regressed;\\nnone inserted a register between JALR target arithmetic and PC reco\n27:he IF PC as the limiting\\npath in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. Seed 2 instead limits on\\nregister-file operand capture. The structural r6s1 forwarding retime,\\nr7s1 multiply bank, and r8s1 synchronous register file all regressed;\\nnone inserted a register between JALR target arithmetic and PC reco\n27:ta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the JALR target/operand capture registers and one pending owner\\nbit, with launch, wait, and completion control. Advance the held\\nJALR and redirect together using only the registered target; retain\\nsaved RVFI \n27:ta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the JALR target/operand capture registers and one pending owner\\nbit, with launch, wait, and completion control. Advance the held\\nJALR and redirect together using only the registered target; retain\\nsaved RVFI \n28: squashed before they acquire\\nan EX/MEM or divider owner. Retain existing EX/MEM control-zero bubbles,\\nheld MEM/WB forwarding data, divider completion-bank ownership and the\\ndirectly registered memory-address bank. No ALU/divider changes belong\\nto this predictor hypothesis.\\n\\nThe measurable\n28:ds retain the\\n  resolving owner; PC repair still overrides unavailable imem. No ALU/divider,\\n  result-bank, MEM/WB forwarding-data, direct memory-address, or retirement\\n  logic changed. `nret: 1` and all core ports remain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviations from the hypothesis. No BTB/h\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'structural': continue\n print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' | '+d['title'])\n print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds')})\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r5s1','r6s1','r7s1')):\n  n=d.get('implementation_notes','')\n  for para in n.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n   if any(w in para.lower() for w in ('timing','critical','liveness','fmax','writeback','write-back')): print(para[:4200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'structural': continue\n print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' | '+d['title'])\n print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds')})\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r5s1','r6s1','r7s1')):\n  n=d.get('implementation_notes','')\n  for para in n.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n   if any(w in para.lower() for w in ('timing','critical','liveness','fmax','writeback','write-back')): print(para[:4200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nhyp-20260906-002-r1s1 | Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\n{'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 372.62, 'fmax_mhz': 167.36, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5438, 'ff': 2094, 'seeds': [165.32, 174.03, 167.36]}\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r2s1 | Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\n{'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 375.78, 'fmax_mhz': 168.78, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5470, 'ff': 2111, 'seeds': [166.17, 168.78, 169.09]}\nImplemented the divide result-bank retiming in cores/bench. The unchanged\nevaluator reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz median routed Fmax, versus the\nstarting implementation's recorded 372.62 and 167.36 MHz: +0.85%, with unchanged\nmarker-bracketed cycles. The estimated 5% improvement was not reached. Full\ntiming coverage of the new forwarding/writeback paths remains a concern,\ndescribed below; the reported fitness is not a complete physical timing proof.\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS without warnings, both real arithmetic\n  and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. git diff --check also passes.\n- Formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including the unchanged liveness window.\n  The initial implementation and the final memory-address correction each\n  passed a complete invocation. No formal counterexamples or failed-check\n  fix retries occurred. wrapper_si.sv, checks_si.cfg, run_all.sh, check depths,\n  and the upstream checks were unchanged. formal-deep was not run.\n- Cocotb: all eight pytest runners PASS, covering 128 cocotb cases: ALU real\n  and ALTOPS (31 each), decoder (30), immediate generator (8), register file\n  (5), pipeline (19), and direct EX real/ALTOPS ownership tests (2 each).\n- Every existing arithmetic vector and handshake assertion remains. Added\n  registered-bank checks before/on/after the terminal edge, immediate and\n  held acceptance, changing live operands/opcodes, distinct successive\n  results, concurrent response consumption/request refill, and resets during\n  computation, held responses, and selected/held ownership.\n- Pipeline regressions retain byte-exact RVFI comparison against the unchanged\n  ISS and independent accepted-memory-request counts. They check selection\n  and metadata on every monitored edge, normal/bubble/memory-hold transitions,\n  load-produced operands, x0, distinct consecutive DIV/REM, and immediate\n  ALU, branch, JALR, store, multiply, and divide consumers. Combined bus waits\n  include twelve-clock forced holds. Divide dispatches remain nine clocks\n  apart; ordinary instructions remain one clock apart; a following ordinary\n  divide consumer has no additional completion bubble.\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and CoreMark. Additional selftest\n  runs passed all 115 RVFI retirements with zero waits, imem-only waits,\n  dmem-only waits, and combined waits at seeds 0, 1, 2, 3, 42, and 99.\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Reported critical delay ns | Critical path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 1 | 166.17 | 6.018 | Divider quotient register to result_q |\n| 2 | 168.78 | 5.925 | ID/EX ALU control to normal EX/MEM result register |\n| 3 | 169.09 | 5.914 | Divider divisor register to result_q |\n| Workload | Starting RTL cycles | Candidate cycles | Candidate iter/s at reported median Fmax |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 375.78 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 51.40 |\nDetailed timing was investigated for all three seeds. Reloading a saved\nroute with --no-pack --no-place --no-route crashes the installed nextpnr in\narray2d.h, so three supplemental routes emitted JSON timing reports and SDF\ndirectly. Each reproduced its corresponding gate Fmax. Summing the exported\nSDF arcs reproduces all three reported critical path delays above.\nHowever, those SDF files omit IOPATH arcs for the LUT3 cells implementing the\nbank selector. The detailed JSON reports contain no MEM/WB alu_result\nendpoints. Complete bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB timing therefore cannot be\nestablished from these unchanged reports. This is an observed reporting gap,\nnot evidence that the mux or its forwarding paths have zero delay.\nThese walks are diagnostic connectivity paths, without sensitization checks\nor complete cell characterization. They are not alternate Fmax or setup-slack\nmeasurements. Their disagreement with the headline clock paths makes full\nforwarding timing coverage an unresolved limitation. No timing model, gate,\nconstraint, or fitness formula was changed to address or hide that limitation.\nThe evaluator's measured gain is small, and this iteration does not establish\nthe proposed five-percent physical frequency improvement.\nEvidence includes formal-console.log, unit-tests.log, the cocotb result XMLs,\ncosim-gate.log, stalled-cosim.log, fpga-eval.log,\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log, coremark-comparison.json,\ncoremark-2k.log, baseline-coremark-2k.log, sixk/*coremark.log,\ntiming/seed*/timing.json, timing/seed*/design.sdf, and\ntiming/bank-paths-partial.json (per-pin known delays and explicit unknowns).\nverified-source-sha256.json records byte-for-byte agreement for 223 staged\nsource/contract files; final formal RTL also matches the submitted RTL.\nOnly bench RTL, bench test_*.py files, and this requested notes file are\nchanged in the worktree. Orchestrator acceptance remains a separate step.\n\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r3s1 | Two radix-4 quotient stages per divider clock\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 159.15, 'fmax_mhz': 71.48, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5651, 'ff': 2127, 'seeds': [71.47, 71.48, 71.96]}\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r4s1 | Retime divider finalization using a narrow request-edge prefix\n{'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 382.48, 'fmax_mhz': 171.79, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5513, 'ff': 2087, 'seeds': [163.85, 171.79, 174.58]}\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r5s1 | Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\n{'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 409.8, 'fmax_mhz': 184.06, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5490, 'ff': 2112, 'seeds': [184.06, 191.13, 179.89]}\nImplemented the pipeline-storage hypothesis. Local evaluation reports **409.80 fitness**, up **7.14%** from 382.48, with unchanged benchmark cycles. Median reported Fmax increases from 171.79 to **184.06 MHz**. Resources are **5,490 LUT4 and 2,112 DFF**, versus 5,513 LUT4 and 2,087 DFF. All local correctness gates passed. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. Pipeline depth, dispatch timing, eight-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 140 cases passed, no skips**. Includes 33 real and 33 ALTOPS ALU cases, both EX-bank modes, 30 decoder cases, and all 22 pipeline cases.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, on the first invocation. Includes `liveness_ch0`, unique retirement, cover, and illegal-instruction checks. No counterexample and no formal fix attempts. The unchanged nret=1 wrapper/configuration and all formal depths were used. This is the contract's ALTOPS formal gate; real-arithmetic deep formal was not run because arithmetic did not change.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and evaluator CoreMark.\n- Additional C++ selftest cosimulation with both stall flags and seeds 1, 19, and 99: **PASS**, all 115 retirements compared against the unchanged ISS per run. Starting and final RTL also have identical retirement-cycle sequences for each seed.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: **PASS**, all three routed seeds and the UART, CRC, OOB, and marker gates.\n- Source verification confirms 154 staged source/contract files, all 53 formal PASS results, exact agreement between staged formal RTL and submitted RTL, and agreement between the starting RTL snapshot and HEAD. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported worst path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.43 | 3.06 / 2.38 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | Wrapper instruction stimulus lfsr[20] to ID rs2_val[3] | 5.23 | 2.61 / 2.63 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.56 | 3.06 / 2.50 |\nTiming remains a reported tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish complete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multiplier, or other forwarding paths have timing margin.\nAll build artifacts and scratch contract copies are under the configured TMPDIR:\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r5s1-storage-78f91x4q`.\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, the nine result XMLs, `cosim-output.log`, `stalled-iss.json`, `fpga-output.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`, `storage-cones.json`, both premap JSONs, `workload-comparison.json`, individual baseline/candidate benchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. This notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5. All remaining changes are confined to bench RTL and test_*.py files.\n\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r6s1 | Retime forwarding dependency comparisons into the ID/EX boundary\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 376.98, 'fmax_mhz': 169.32, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5502, 'ff': 2093, 'seeds': [169.32, 191.75, 160.95]}\nImplemented the forwarding-comparison retiming hypothesis. Correctness and cycle equivalence checks pass, but the measured three-seed FPGA result regresses: median reported Fmax is 169.32 MHz versus 184.06 MHz, and evaluator fitness is 376.98 versus 409.80 (about -8.01%). The proposed four-percent improvement was not observed. The implementation is left in place for the orchestrator to evaluate.\nLocal formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness_ch0, reg_ch0, unique, cover, illegal-instruction, and all generated instruction checks. This was the first RTL attempt; there were no formal failures, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged command was RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh, which auto-selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config. The normal gate retains RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Real divider arithmetic and latency were exercised by the existing real-mode unit suites and ISS comparisons; formal-deep was not run because arithmetic was untouched.\nEvery routed critical path was inspected. Starting seeds 1 and 3 reproduce the EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF pc[1] paths at 5.43 ns and 5.56 ns; starting seed 2 reports instruction-input/LFSR -> ID register-file operand capture at 5.23 ns. Candidate seeds 1 and 2 instead report ID/EX alu_op[3] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX/MEM normal alu_result[15], at 5.91 ns and 5.22 ns. Candidate seed 3 reports the corresponding alu_op[2] path at 6.21 ns. Thus the earlier seed-2 register-file path did not remain the reported limit.\nTiming caveat: these are the unchanged toolchain's timing reports, with its existing timing-model/arc coverage limitations. Omitted paths/arcs were not treated as timing margin. No timing models, constraints, report parsing, or memory-address topology were changed to improve the score.\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r7s1 | Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 382.21, 'fmax_mhz': 171.67, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 5515, 'ff': 2124, 'seeds': [175.25, 171.67, 167.56]}\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: retained `out` as the integer-only combinational output, with ADD through LUI and default zero. Added `mul_out` with the compact encoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU case and default zero. Original signed/unsigned product expressions and all ALTOPS formulas are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: added one 32-bit `mul_result_q` and one `mul_result_sel_q`. Integer/link and multiply payloads capture independently on the existing `!stall` edge with synchronous reset, without validity, ownership, or divide-accept gating. Legal accepted multiplies acquire ownership; jump-link priority and divide-accept priority are preserved. Reset and accepted bubbles clear both selectors; holds retain them. One encoded selection of registered banks drives the existing forwarding and MEM/WB result consumers. `mem_addr` remains wired directly to `reg_q.alu_result`.\n- Extended the three requested test files. Other RTL, decoder legality, nret=1, IO, divider implementation, MEM/WB hold behavior, hazards, retirement, evaluation scripts, benchmark sources, timing models and constraints are unchanged. No extra multiply edge or consumer bubble was added.\n## Correctness and timing comparisons\n- Requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- Unmodified `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including liveness, cover, unique, illegal and every instruction check. First gate attempt passed; no RTL fix/retry was needed. Used `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`; nret=1 automatically selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/config. A private copy of the formal framework under the supplied TMPDIR kept generated checks away from the shared framework. The installed conda Python supplied click, YAML and cocotb missing from the default Python environment.\n- All nine pytest runners passed: **151 cocotb cases** across ALU real/ALTOPS (34 each), EX ownership real/ALTOPS (7 each), pipeline (25), decoder (30), ID payload (1), immediate generation (8), and register file (5). Two ownership tests were strengthened after the full run to require distinct consecutive products and synchronous reset of an asserted multiply-owner bit, then passed again in both arithmetic modes.\n- Arithmetic coverage retains every existing integer/divider vector and protocol assertion. Added cross-products of signedness/high-half boundaries, 256 additional random operand pairs, rapid opcode/class/default transitions, and zero on each inactive ALU output. Ownership coverage includes all integer/multiply/divider/bubble class transitions; inactive captures under invalid, illegal and jump stimuli; poisoned inactive multiply inputs during divide computation; prolonged holds; saved divide operands; terminal acceptance; reset; and link priority.\n- Mixed pipeline streams cover all four multiply operations, both forwarding ages, repeated destinations, x0, load operands, dependent ALU, branch, JALR, load/store address, store data, multiply and divide instructions. Directed holds exercise valid-cleared MEM/WB forwarding, redirects and division. Accepted memory effects and all meaningful RVFI fields remain compared with the unchanged Python ISS.\n- Starting versus candidate pipeline comparison: **35 streams, 6,572 retirements, 1,622 accepted memory effects, 29,812 driven cycles**. Retirement cycles, architectural RVFI data, accepted-memory cycles/effects, actual ready schedules, divider launches and dispatches match exactly. Only inactive RVFI memory payload is normalized; all operand values, active memory fields and retirement metadata remain exact. Unstalled multiply consumers retain one-cycle spacing, or nine cycles when the consumer is a divide; consecutive divides retain nine-clock dispatch spacing and eight compute clocks.\n- Unchanged C++ selftest cosimulation: 115 retirements per run, exact ISS match and starting/candidate cycle match. Final retirement cycles are 159 with zero wait and 198/203/198 with both-bus stalls at seeds 1/19/81.\n- Fast formal uses ALTOPS as required. Periodic real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; real arithmetic is covered here by exact-result unit tests, mixed ISS comparisons and CoreMark validation.\n## Timing coverage limitation\n**The existing result-bank timing coverage gap continues in all three seeds.** Separate diagnostic runs with SDF/detailed reports reproduced the official routed JSON designs exactly, including identical placement, routing and Fmax. No timing model or constraint was changed.\n- Multiply Q (`mul_result_q_DFFR_Q_15`) to normal EX result D (`reg_q_DFFR_Q_78`) crosses `div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0_4_I0_LUT3_F.I1->F`, which has no timing arc, followed by forwarding/ALU logic with further missing LUT arcs.\n- Multiply Q (`mul_result_q_DFFR_Q`) to MEM/WB ALU D (`u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_67`) crosses `div_result_sel_q_MUX2_LUT5_S0_9_I0_LUT3_F.I1->F`, also absent, and an unmodeled packing passthrough LUT.\nInteger and divider bank paths to the same consumer classes also cross missing arcs. These are topology witnesses, not worst-path delay estimates. **Complete bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB delays are unavailable, not zero.** The reported Fmax/fitness regression is the unchanged evaluator's result under this incomplete timing coverage; it does not establish complete physical timing or any frequency gain from separating the bank.\nAttempting to re-import saved P&R JSON for timing export alone hit nextpnr's `array2d.h:78` assertion on all seeds. The subsequent diagnostic reroutes resolved report generation and exactly reproduced the successful official routes; they were not additional fitness samples.\nIt contains the immutable starting RTL, validated candidate snapshot, `formal_gate.log`, unit/pipeline/reset logs, `pipeline_traces/`, `selftest_cosim.json`, `coremark_comparison.json`, both ELF binaries, `mapped_separation.json`, `fpga_result.json`, the official three routed seeds, `routed_bank_timing_coverage.json`, and diagnostic per-seed SDF/timing JSON. Final RTL was byte-compared with the validated candidate snapshot.\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Validation used private scratch copies because the formal runner writes into its framework directory and the benchmark Makefile creates ELF files. The explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root, which the orchestrator reads and permits. No tracked contract/evaluator/other-core file was changed. Remaining concerns are the measured fitness regression and incomplete timing arcs, not a known functional failure.\n\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r8s1 | Absorb ID operand capture into synchronous register-file RAM banks\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 385.46, 'fmax_mhz': 173.13, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 2824, 'ff': 1105, 'seeds': [173.13, 148.68, 190.11]}\n\nhyp-20260906-002-r9s1 | Register JALR targets in a two-cycle execute path\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 341.76, 'fmax_mhz': 153.99, 'cycles': 4505747, 'lut4': 5605, 'ff': 2191, 'seeds': [157.21, 153.99, 146.01]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
