{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if l.strip()]\nfor r in rows:\n print(r['id'],r['category'],r['outcome'],r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('cycles'),r['title'])\nfor r in rows:\n if r['id'] in ('hyp-20260906-001-r11s0','hyp-20260906-002-r8s1','hyp-20260906-002-r11s1'):\n  print('\\\\nDETAIL',r['id'])\n  for k in ('hypothesis','implementation_notes','seeds'):\n   print(k, r.get(k))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if l.strip()]\nfor r in rows:\n print(r['id'],r['category'],r['outcome'],r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('cycles'),r['title'])\nfor r in rows:\n if r['id'] in ('hyp-20260906-001-r11s0','hyp-20260906-002-r8s1','hyp-20260906-002-r11s1'):\n  print('\\\\nDETAIL',r['id'])\n  for k in ('hypothesis','implementation_notes','seeds'):\n   print(k, r.get(k))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-e113d0a micro_opt improvement 127.03 4491485 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260906-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression 127.96 4491485 Eliminate load-use bubbles caused by immediate bitfields\nhyp-20260906-002-r1s1 structural improvement 167.36 4491485 Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\nhyp-20260906-003-r1s2 predictor regression 122.77 4215937 Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\nhyp-20260906-001-r2s0 micro_opt regression 157.36 4491485 Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\nhyp-20260906-002-r2s1 structural improvement 168.78 4491485 Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\nhyp-20260906-003-r2s2 predictor regression 145.48 4215937 Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\nhyp-20260906-001-r3s0 micro_opt regression 108.23 4491485 Shorten divider carry chains with block carry-select subtraction\nhyp-20260906-002-r3s1 structural regression 71.48 4491485 Two radix-4 quotient stages per divider clock\nhyp-20260906-003-r3s2 predictor regression 154.13 4259699 Stateless backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction\nhyp-20260906-001-r4s0 micro_opt regression 105.35 4491485 Carry-free conditional sign correction at divider completion\nhyp-20260906-002-r4s1 structural improvement 171.79 4491485 Retime divider finalization using a narrow request-edge prefix\nhyp-20260906-003-r4s2 predictor regression 153.52 4442217 Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\nhyp-20260906-001-r5s0 micro_opt regression 159.01 4491485 Balanced one-hot register-file reads with integrated write bypass\nhyp-20260906-002-r5s1 structural improvement 184.06 4491485 Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\nhyp-20260906-003-r5s2 predictor regression 146.05 4309463 Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\nhyp-20260906-001-r6s0 micro_opt regression 167.7 4491485 Precompute JALR alignment before operand forwarding selection\nhyp-20260906-002-r6s1 structural regression 169.32 4491485 Retime forwarding dependency comparisons into the ID/EX boundary\nhyp-20260906-003-r6s2 predictor broken None None Tagged target prediction with MEM-stage validation and staged training\nhyp-20260906-001-r7s0 micro_opt regression 151.15 4491485 Align five-bit ALU control codes with instruction funct3\nhyp-20260906-002-r7s1 structural regression 171.67 4491485 Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\nhyp-20260906-003-r7s2 predictor regression 115.85 4312067 Single-entry backedge target prediction with decode validation\nhyp-20260906-001-r8s0 micro_opt regression 160.15 4491485 Infer distributed register storage by resetting per-register validity\nhyp-20260906-002-r8s1 structural regression 173.13 4491485 Absorb ID operand capture into synchronous register-file RAM banks\nhyp-20260906-003-r8s2 predictor regression 88.97 4309463 Backward-branch prediction with validity-only EX annul\nhyp-20260906-001-r9s0 micro_opt regression 176.4 4491485 Flatten EX operand selection with direct one-hot forwarding\nhyp-20260906-002-r9s1 structural regression 153.99 4505747 Register JALR targets in a two-cycle execute path\nhyp-20260906-003-r9s2 predictor regression 94.96 4309463 Backward prediction with alignment-independent EX recovery control\nhyp-20260906-001-r10s0 micro_opt regression 146.63 4491485 LUT-map JALR target bit one after operand forwarding\nhyp-20260906-002-r10s1 structural regression 159.62 4505747 Split JALR forwarding from target addition with an operand register\nhyp-20260906-003-r10s2 predictor regression 78.19 4309463 Backward prediction with balanced EX comparison\nhyp-20260906-001-r11s0 micro_opt improvement 184.84 4491485 Remove launch qualification from idle divider operand storage\nhyp-20260906-002-r11s1 structural regression 135.43 4341607 Decouple fetch from backend interlocks with a two-entry instruction queue\nhyp-20260906-003-r11s2 predictor regression 169.75 4360895 Backward prediction with asymmetric registered exit recovery\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-002-r8s1\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The structural entries in experiments/log.jsonl and LESSONS.md show that\\nchanging storage boundaries can improve fitness without changing cycles:\\nr5s1 reached 409.80 at 184.06 MHz by separating payload capture from\\npipeline control. Its reported seed-2 limit was instruction input through\\nregister-file reading to ID operand capture; seeds 1 and 3 instead limited\\non forwarding through JALR resolution. The r7s1 multiply-bank experiment\\nagain exposed register-file reads and forwarding as competing limits.\\nThese reports establish a real read-path target, not that it dominates\\nevery seed of the current winner.\\n\\nToday reg_file.sv resets the complete 32-by-32 array and performs two\\nasynchronous reads, followed by write-first bypass and ID/EX operand\\nflops. The r5s0 one-hot read-tree rewrite reduced area but regressed routed\\ntiming; r6s1 forwarding-comparison retiming also regressed. No recorded\\nstructural attempt moves register-file storage and operand capture into\\nsynchronous RAM. This experiment removes the large fabric read network\\nby changing where operands are stored and clocked, while retaining the\\ncurrent instruction schedule.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace the asynchronous flop-based integer register file plus its two\\nID/EX operand registers with two coherent inferred synchronous-read RAM\\nbanks, one per source port. Broadcast the existing single WB write to\\nboth banks. Each bank has independent read and write addresses and a\\nsingle shared clock. The RAM read edge must be the existing ID/EX capture\\nedge: instruction N's source addresses before that edge produce its\\nregistered source values alongside instruction N's decoded metadata\\nafter the edge. Do not add a fetch, operand-read, execute, or retirement\\ncycle. This is one structural change to register-file/ID storage ownership.\\n\\nInfer block RAM using portable behavioral SystemVerilog and the RTL\\nram_style attribute, with no vendor-only simulation replacement. Use\\nsynchronous read outputs without bulk array reset. Preserve the existing\\narchitectural reset-to-zero behavior with one resettable initialized-bit\\nbitmap shared by the two coherent copies. Every accepted nonzero WB\\nwrite sets its destination's bit; reset clears the bitmap and suppresses\\nwrites. Capture each read's initialized/nonzero qualification on the\\nsame edge as its RAM read. Unwritten registers and x0 return zero even\\nthough raw RAM contents are unspecified. Reset also clears the captured\\noutput qualifications so a held or previously populated output cannot\\nleak stale data after reset. Do not replace reset with a multi-cycle\\nmemory-clearing sweep or expose unknown data to execution or RVFI.\\n\\nImplement explicit same-edge write-first behavior for each read port.\\nCapture the nonzero WB address-match flag and the matching WB data with\\nthe read transaction; select that registered data on a collision before\\nconsidering the captured initialized bit. A first write after reset must\\ntherefore be visible to a simultaneous read. Neither later WB values,\\nlive read addresses, nor later bitmap updates may change a held operand.\\nDo not depend on an unspecified primitive read/write collision result.\\nKeep raw memory/read-output storage free of reset muxes that would prevent\\nRAM inference; implement required reset visibility in the small response\\nqualification bank. The intended inference pattern is supported by the\\nYosys memory documentation, but actual Gowin mapping must be inspected:\\nhttps://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/v0.50/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html\\n\\nDrive both RAM read enables and all response metadata captures from the\\nsame existing ID payload capture decision, !stall_id, with reset taking\\npriority in qualification state. WB writes remain independent of this\\nread enable, so older instructions can write while ID holds. A dmem or\\ndivider hold retains both operand responses and all instruction metadata.\\nA load-use flush with stall retains payload while clearing control;\\nan unstalled redirect or invalid-fetch bubble follows the current\\nindependent payload-capture policy. Assemble id_ex_t operands directly\\nfrom these registered responses; remove their redundant ID-stage capture\\nso the RAM result is never associated with the preceding instruction.\\nKeep ID metadata/control storage and the existing EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB\\nforwarding priority intact, including retained WB forwarding during\\ndmem stalls and captured DIV/REM operands during execution.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is fewer register-storage flops and fabric read\\nmuxes, a shorter instruction-address-to-read-capture path, and potentially\\nless routing congestion. Target approximately two additional block RAMs,\\nbut verify actual mapped geometry and device capacity. RAM clock-to-output\\ndelay, registered collision selection, fixed RAM placement, and the\\nexisting forwarding/JALR and ALU-control paths may erase the benefit.\\nThe forecast is therefore a low-confidence 5% fitness gain with unchanged\\nmarker-window cycles; it is not a measured improvement.\\n\\nKeep core.yaml nret=1, the external core ports, decoder/trap rules,\\nretirement ordering, memory handshakes, direct EX/MEM memory-address\\nbank, divider latency/arithmetic, multiplier sharing, and all ALTOPS\\nformulas unchanged. Modify only the listed bench RTL and unit tests.\\nRun the existing lint, unit, formal, stalled ISS cosim, CoreMark CRC/UART,\\nbounds, marker, and three-seed FPGA gates without changing their contract.\\nCompare starting and candidate retirement cycles with identical programs\\nand stall sequences to verify that no read bubbles were introduced.\\nVerify the requested 6 KB CRCs and keep any separately reported fixture\\nmeasurements distinct without changing benchmark or evaluator files.\\nInspect mapped RF RAMs, read-output absorption, collision forwarding,\\nreset masking, resources, and all three routed limiting paths. Check RAM\\naddress and RAM-output-to-EX timing coverage; an omitted arc is not zero\\ndelay. Accept only improvement in the unchanged evaluator's median-Fmax\\ntimes marker-bracketed iterations-per-cycle product.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 5, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/reg_file.sv', 'description': 'Implement coherent per-source synchronous RAM banks with a read\\ncapture enable independent of WB writes. Add the resettable shared\\ninitialized bitmap, captured x0/initialized qualifications, and\\ncaptured same-edge WB collision bypass. Hold the complete response\\nwhen reads are disabled. Preserve one-write/two-read bandwidth and\\ndeterministic reset-visible values without resetting RAM contents.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': \"Transfer ownership of the two ID/EX operand registers to the\\nregister file's synchronous read responses. Store instruction\\nmetadata and controls as before, and assemble outgoing operand\\nfields from the matching registered responses without another\\ncapture edge. Preserve independent payload capture, control-zero\\nbubbles, and complete metadata retention during holds.\\n\"}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Connect the register-file read capture enable to the existing\\nID-stage payload enable and route its registered operand responses\\ninto ID/EX assembly. Retain WB write wiring, external ports,\\nforwarding, hazard priorities, memory-address wiring, and RVFI.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_reg_file.py', 'description': 'Adapt read sampling to the intentional synchronous internal\\ninterface while retaining every architectural value assertion.\\nCheck all read-address pairs, both-port write collisions, first\\nwrites after reset, populated-storage reset, x0, independent WB\\nwrites during read holds, and held responses while addresses and\\nwrites change. Use an independent architectural-register scoreboard\\nto verify coherent copies and write-first results at capture edges.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_id_stage.py', 'description': 'Model the synchronous register-file response timing in ID tests\\nand check that instruction metadata and operand responses describe\\nthe same instruction on every capture. Preserve decoder/control\\nand payload/hold assertions; cover reset, invalid fetches, redirect\\nflushes, and load-use flush-plus-hold without adding a read cycle.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add full-RVFI operand and cycle comparisons for consecutive reads,\\nWB/read collisions on either or both ports, producer distances,\\ndependent ALU/JALR/branch/store instructions, load-use interlocks,\\nDIV/REM launch/completion, x0, and reset of populated registers.\\nExercise imem stalls, long dmem holds, and older WB writes while\\nID is frozen. Require unchanged retirement timing for identical\\nstalls, one ordered retirement per instruction, and no duplicate\\nmemory effects while retaining all existing correctness checks.\\n'}]}\nimplementation_notes The synchronous RAM register-file hypothesis is implemented, but **reject it as a fitness improvement**. The unchanged evaluator reports **385.46**, versus **409.80** for a fresh build and three-seed route of the starting RTL: **-5.94%**, with identical marker-window cycles. The implementation remains in the allowed bench RTL/tests for orchestrator review; it is not an accepted replacement for the starting winner.\n\n`reg_file.sv` now contains two coherent 32-by-32 synchronous-read arrays with `ram_style=\"block\"`. Both receive the same accepted nonzero WB write, independently of read enable. Reads, captured nonzero/initialized qualifications, collision flags, and a shared 32-bit collision-data register use the existing `!stall_id` capture decision. Collision data takes priority over initialized qualification, including a first write after reset. Reset suppresses writes and clears the shared initialized bitmap and four response qualification bits; RAM contents and raw read outputs have no reset. Held responses do not depend on subsequent addresses, writes, or bitmap updates.\n\n`id_stage.sv` stores metadata/control in a local operand-free struct and assembles `id_ex_t` directly from that state and the RF's registered responses. There is no second operand capture. Metadata capture, control-zero bubbles, flush-plus-hold behavior, and invalid-fetch payload capture retain their existing policies. `core.sv` only adds the RF read-enable connection. Forwarding priority, retained MEM/WB forwarding during memory holds, saved DIV/REM operands, direct normal EX/MEM memory-address bank, arithmetic, multiplier sharing, ALTOPS, ports, and `core.yaml`/`nret: 1` are unchanged.\n\nOne mapping refinement beyond the advisory plan is `no_rw_check` on the two raw arrays. The initial inferred read-first memories caused Gowin mapping to insert delayed-write registers and additional transparency emulation, despite the explicit response bypass already hiding every raw collision. This attribute removes that redundant emulation; it does not relax the visible write-first contract. Simulators still use the same behavioral SystemVerilog, and the final formal gate also passes with raw collisions unspecified. This pattern is documented in [Yosys memory handling](https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/v0.50/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html), under synchronous SDP with undefined collision behavior. A single collision-data register serves both ports because they can only collide with the same WB transaction.\n\nThe RF tests retain the original value assertions with edge-based sampling and add an independent architectural scoreboard, all 1,024 read-address pairs after initialization and reset, both-port and individual collisions, first writes, x0, populated-memory resets, suppressed reset-edge writes, changing addresses/WB while held, later initialization behind an unwritten held response, and 4,000 randomized transactions. The ID test drives RF responses after the metadata capture edge, so a redundant operand register would fail. Pipeline tests independently model WB/read capture, exercise producer distances and both operands, ALU/JALR/branch/store dependencies, load-use interlocks, DIV/REM, populated-register reset, imem stalls, long dmem holds, and older WB writes during ID holds. Existing ISS, control/payload retention, ordered retirement, and unique accepted-memory-effect assertions remain active.\n\nLocal validation of the submitted RTL:\n\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS, no warnings**. `git diff --check`: **PASS**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 145 cases passed, 0 failures, 0 skips**, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic and EX-bank modes.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed** on the final implementation. Includes channel-0 liveness, unique, cover, illegal instructions, and register/PC checks. No formal counterexample or failed formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts. Earlier structural forms also passed. The unchanged single-retirement wrapper, depths, and ALTOPS configuration were used. Deep arithmetic formal was not run because arithmetic and divider latency were untouched.\n- Starting and final RTL: **34 identical protocol scenarios, 6,093 complete RVFI records with exact retirement-cycle equality, and 1,515 identical accepted memory transactions**. The same deterministic random seeds and directed bus schedules were used. The integer prefix retires on consecutive cycles through WB/read collisions.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest ISS and evaluator CoreMark CRC/UART gates.\n- Additional C++ selftest runs with `--istall --dstall --seed 1/19/99`: **PASS**, 115 retirements each, field-for-field ISS agreement and byte-identical starting/final full RVFI traces including cycle numbers.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: all three seeds route successfully, CoreMark completes, bounds/CRC/UART/marker checks pass. **The fitness improvement criterion fails.**\n\nAll gates ran from unchanged scratch contract copies under `.tmp/rf-sync/`; final gates used `candidate-norw/`. The staged final RTL and tests were checked against the submitted files, the formal staged RTL matches them, all 53 formal task logs contain PASS, and the starting RTL snapshot matches HEAD `7583066de166bd6cd773a698ecfc285232a61ed3`. No evaluator, benchmark source, other core, or core configuration was edited.\n\nFresh unchanged evaluator results:\n\n| Metric | Starting RTL | Final synchronous RF |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Seed 1 routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Seed 2 routed MHz | 191.13 | 148.68 |\n| Seed 3 routed MHz | 179.89 | 190.11 |\n| Median routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Marker-window cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Iterations | 10 | 10 |\n| Fitness, median MHz × 1,000,000 × 10 / cycles | 409.80 | 385.46 |\n| Routed LUT4 | 5,490 | 2,824 |\n| Routed DFF | 2,112 | 1,105 |\n| BSRAM | 4 | 6 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\n\nMapping inspection confirms exactly two `SDPX9B` RF cells, each configured for 36-bit reads and writes: a 512-by-36 physical geometry using 32 addresses and 32 payload bits. Four existing `DPX9B` memory cells remain. Total BSRAM usage is **6/46**, LUT4 **2,824/20,736**, DFF **1,105/15,552**, and MULT36X36 **1/12**. Both RF clocks are identical, read CE matches the collision-data capture CE, write address/data/enable are broadcast coherently, and both primitive reset inputs are tied low. All 64 raw RF response bits are driven directly by RAM outputs, with no fabric operand-output flops. The shared bitmap maps to 31 resettable flops (x0 constant), qualification/collision state to four resettable flops, and collision data to 32 enabled flops. The final memory-libmap output has no extra read-first/transparency emulation cells. Area fell 48.56% in LUT4 and 47.68% in DFF, but this did not improve the median routed throughput.\n\nAll three final routed limiting paths were inspected. Separate runs adding only SDF/detailed report outputs reproduced each gate Fmax exactly; scoring still uses the unchanged gate.\n\n| Seed | Reported limiting path | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | ID rs1 address bit 1 → forwarding selection → JALR resolution → IF PC bit 1 | 5.776 | 3.037 | 2.739 |\n| 2 | ID ALU operation bit 3 → ALU result selection → EX/MEM normal result bit 2 | 6.726 | 3.420 | 3.306 |\n| 3 | ID ALU operation bit 3 → ALU result selection → EX/MEM normal result bit 0 | 5.260 | 2.886 | 2.374 |\n\n**Timing coverage remains incomplete.** Every RF primitive's SDF contains 36 CLKB-to-DO arcs at **2.259 ns**, plus read-address setup/hold checks. However, each bank's five active ADB address pins is driven through LUT2 cells with no exported IOPATH arcs; those connected read-address endpoints are absent from detailed timing reports in all seeds. On the output side, connectivity walks from DO0 of each bank reach EX registers through LUT3 cells whose input-to-output arcs are also absent in every seed. Example missing arcs are `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.a_LUT3_F_19_I2_MUX2_LUT5_O_I0_LUT3_F.I1→F` and the analogous `b_LUT3_F_11...` cell for port 2. The physical RAM clock-to-output model therefore does not establish complete RAM-output-to-EX timing. Missing arcs are **unknown, not zero delay**; no complete address or operand-path timing claim is made, and no timing library, constraint, or evaluator was changed. This concern is additional to the measured fitness regression.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator uses **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**. The requested **6 KB** run was built separately from byte-identical benchmark sources with the existing `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options. Starting and final binaries ran identical ELFs and `--bench --istall --dstall` schedules for each size. Both sizes produced byte-identical final RVFI, markers, and UART output; both completed with EBREAK and `oob:false`.\n\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both implementations | Marker-window cycles, both | Starting / final iter/sec |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 409.80 / 385.46 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 56.05 / 52.72 |\n\nThe rates use the independently routed medians and the unrounded iteration/cycle ratios, not the evaluator's rounded display of IPC. Both UARTs contain the literal `Correct operation validated.` and ten iterations. Evaluator CRCs are seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The requested **6 KB CRCs are crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47**, additionally seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949. The unchanged UART compiler-description string says -O2; actual recorded compilation commands use -O3. The supplemental measurement is not substituted into the evaluator.\n\nEvidence is under `.tmp/rf-sync/`: `norw-unit.log`, final result XMLs, `norw-formal.log`, `norw-cosim.log`, `norw-fpga.log`, `baseline-fpga.log`, `baseline-protocol-v2.jsonl`, `norw-protocol.jsonl`, `norw-stalled-iss.json`, baseline/final workload JSONL files, `sixk-build.log`, `rf-storage-evidence.json`, `norw-rf-premap.json`, `norw-rf-libmap.json`, `timing-summary.json`, `rf-output-timing-coverage.json`, three `norw-timing-seed*.json/.sdf/.log` exports, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. The notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5; source changes are confined to the six permitted bench RTL/test files.\n\nseeds [173.13, 148.68, 190.11]\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-001-r11s0\nhypothesis {'motivation': \"Divider launch qualification still controls wide operand storage in the\\ncurrent winner. In ex_stage.sv, div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q capture only on\\ndiv_req_valid && div_req_ready; in div_unit.sv, the same handshake gates\\ninitialization of quotient/remainder, divisor, dividend and sign metadata.\\nThis connects forwarding-tag comparisons and load-availability decisions\\nto many register enables and data-selection cones. The r7s0 implementation\\nnotes report forwarding-to-quotient_q critical paths in two seeds, and\\nr10s1 reports a forwarding/load-availability path to div_rs2_q CE. These\\nare observed bottlenecks in rejected candidates, not proof that they are\\nthe current winner's worst paths.\\n\\nThe accepted r5s1 change improved fitness 7.14% by separating pipeline\\npayload capture from transaction control. Apply that principle narrowly\\nto the remaining divider operand banks. This avoids repeating the failed\\ndivider arithmetic rewrites, ALU recoding, and JALR timing experiments.\\nNo instruction should gain or lose a cycle; any fitness gain must come\\nfrom a higher three-seed median Fmax.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Separate divider control ownership from idle operand capture, without\\nadding registers, pipeline stages, or an execution shortcut. The sole\\noptimization is to remove request qualification from operand-storage\\nenables and source selection. An unowned operand bank may change freely;\\nthe actual request edge must still capture that edge's exact operands.\\n\\nIn div_unit.sv, retain the existing equations and sequential transitions\\nfor req_ready, resp_valid, busy_q, count_q, held_q and result_q. Put the\\narithmetic storage in disjoint clocked processes with synchronous reset.\\nOn every edge whose pre-edge busy_q is zero, capture the existing magnitude\\nprefix into quotient_q/remainder_q and capture divisor_q, dividend_q and\\nall four operation/sign flags, regardless of req_valid or held_q. While\\nbusy_q is one, retain those operand/flag registers; update quotient_q and\\nremainder_q only on the existing seven !final_cycle recurrence edges.\\nHold Q/R on the eighth, sign-correction edge. In ALTOPS mode, capture the\\nunchanged selected subtraction/XOR formula into alt_result_q on each idle\\nedge and retain it while busy. Give every register exactly one owner.\\n\\nA request can only be accepted while busy_q is zero, so the same edge\\ninitializes its operands and sets busy_q. Idle captures never start an\\noperation or advance the count. A held response remains in result_q and\\ndoes not depend on the now-unowned arithmetic bank; consequently idle\\ncaptures are also safe while held_q is one. Preserve simultaneous held\\nresponse consumption and next-request acceptance, and leave result_q\\nunchanged except for reset and the existing terminal capture.\\n\\nIn ex_stage.sv, retain div_active_q's exact launch/accept state machine\\nbut split its RVFI operand snapshots into a separate process. After reset,\\ncapture forwarded rs1/rs2 on every edge with !div_active_q, without using\\nopcode, load availability, request/ready, stall, or valid as capture\\nqualifiers. Once active, retain both snapshots through computation,\\nblocked response, and the acceptance edge. EX/MEM samples the old snapshots\\non that edge before ownership clears. Later idle snapshot updates cannot\\nalter an already-captured EX/MEM owner or the divider's result bank.\\n\\nPreserve all real arithmetic, corner cases, eight-clock response timing,\\nnine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, request qualification, load-use\\nprotection, forwarding priority, zero-control bubbles, memory holds,\\ntraps, and nret=1 RVFI behavior. Keep the normal registered memory-address\\nbank and multiplier unchanged. Extra idle switching is a tradeoff, and\\nbusy_q fanout or unrelated routing may erase the predicted timing gain.\\n\\nValidate exact accepted operands, results and retirement cycles against\\nthe starting winner under identical programs and bus schedules. Retain\\nthe existing lint, real/ALTOPS unit, formal and stalled cosim gates and\\ncanonical 6 KB CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. Compare the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA fitness using identical binaries and unrounded\\niterations/cycle; keep any existing evaluator working-set discrepancy\\nexplicitly separate. Inspect mapped D/CE cones to confirm that request\\nqualification disappeared from these banks, and report all three routed\\ncritical paths without inferring timing margin from missing arcs.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 3, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/div_unit.sv', 'description': 'Split protocol/result state from arithmetic operand storage. Capture\\nprefix Q/R, normalized divisor, raw dividend and flags on !busy_q;\\nadvance Q/R on busy_q && !final_cycle and hold on final_cycle. Apply\\nthe same idle-capture ownership rule to alt_result_q. Preserve every\\narithmetic expression, reset value, request/response transition and\\nresult_q write condition, including held-response refill behavior.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Give div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q a separate synchronous-reset process with\\ncapture enable !div_active_q and the existing forwarded rs1/rs2 D\\ninputs. Leave div_active_q and all request/accept/wait equations\\nunchanged. Retain snapshots for the complete active transaction and\\npreserve EX/MEM metadata selection, stalls and result-bank ownership.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Retain all arithmetic, prefix-state, exact-latency, reset and held-bank\\nchecks. Add transactions preceded by changing idle operands/opcodes\\nwith req_valid low, followed by different operands on the actual\\nrequest edge. Exercise changed live inputs while busy, idle captures\\nbeneath an unconsumed response, consume/refill, and reset at request,\\nseventh-recurrence and terminal edges in real and ALTOPS modes. Check\\nthat no idle capture produces a response or changes result_q.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_ex_result_banks.py', 'description': 'Extend ownership tests with changing idle forwarded values, delayed\\nlaunch from load unavailability and memory holds, then distinct\\naccepted operands from each forwarding source. Check exact RVFI\\nsnapshots through busy, blocked completion and acceptance, including\\nan EX/MEM divide owner held after div_active_q clears while idle\\nsnapshots change. Preserve all existing result and direct-address\\nassertions in both arithmetic modes.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add directed mixed streams with load/divide producers, competing\\nforwarding writers, retained WB operands, back-to-back DIV/REM and\\nimmediate consumers. Exercise bus stalls, redirects and reset around\\nlaunch/completion. Compare full RVFI, accepted memory effects and\\nretirement-cycle sequences with the unchanged ISS and starting RTL;\\nrequire unchanged ordinary and divide spacing and no extra request\\nor retirement from idle operand capture. Keep all existing checks.\\n'}]}\nimplementation_notes # Idle divider operand storage\n\nImplemented the hypothesis against starting winner `8357476ebbe4f81ec39bae4793d68a4eef9d0eac` (`hyp-20260906-002-r5s1`). Local correctness gates pass. The unchanged three-seed evaluator improves median Fmax from 184.06 to 184.84 MHz, or **0.423774856%**, with identical execution cycles.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv`: separated protocol/result ownership from the arithmetic storage, with synchronous reset and exactly one clocked owner per register. On pre-edge `!busy_q`, Q/R capture the existing four-bit magnitude prefix, and divisor, dividend, and all four flags capture current inputs regardless of request validity or a held response. Q/R advance on the existing seven recurrence edges and hold on the eighth correction edge. ALTOPS captures the unchanged selected subtraction/XOR expression on every idle edge and holds while busy.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: retained the launch/accept state machine verbatim; moved RVFI snapshots to their own synchronous-reset process enabled only by `!div_active_q`. Snapshots retain the accepted operands through blocked completion and the acceptance edge, when EX/MEM samples their old values.\n- No arithmetic expressions, state declarations, pipeline stages, multiplier, normal memory-address bank, request qualification, forwarding priority, result-bank write conditions, or top-level ports changed. `nret` remains 1. Idle captures do not claim ownership, advance count, or produce responses.\n\nNo architectural deviation was required. The old ALU test assertion that arithmetic Q/R remained owned after completion was replaced with an assertion of the requested idle prefix capture. All response/result-bank stability assertions and all arithmetic, prefix, latency, reset, bubble, direct-address, and retirement checks remain.\n\n## Tests and equivalence\n\n- Requested Verilator lint passes with no warnings/errors, in real and ALTOPS modes.\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, using unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config and ALTOPS. Environment: `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`, with the installed conda Python on PATH. The first configured proof run passed; no RTL formal fixes or retries were needed. The initial system-Python setup could not run the gate, so validation used the existing conda installation. Formal was staged under `$TMPDIR` to leave contract files untouched. Real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; arithmetic expressions are unchanged, with real unit, ISS, and full-trace coverage below.\n- All nine pytest runners pass: real ALU 34 cocotb tests, ALTOPS ALU 34, real EX 5, ALTOPS EX 5, decoder 30, ID 1, immediate generator 8, pipeline 24, regfile 5; **146 cocotb tests total**.\n- Added changing idle operands/opcodes, exact different request-edge operands, busy input/request poisoning, captures beneath blocked responses, simultaneous consume/refill, and request/seventh-recurrence/terminal reset checks. EX tests cover all nine forwarding-source combinations, load-unavailable launch delays, memory holds, active snapshot retention, and a held EX/MEM divide owner surviving changing idle snapshots after ownership clears.\n- Added mixed pipeline streams with competing writers, all operand forwarding sources, retained WB load data, dependent and consecutive DIV/REM, immediate consumers, bus waits, redirects, and reset at request, recurrence, terminal, and completed-owner boundaries. Coverage assertions require the intended forwarding and overlap events. Initial test-only assertions were corrected on the starting RTL to include deliberate bus-hold cycles and avoid asking an aborted pre-launch scenario to consume a later hold schedule.\n- The identical protocol suite runs on the starting and candidate RTL. Optional `PIPELINE_TRACE_MODE=record` / `compare`, sharing `PIPELINE_TRACE_DIR`, records complete valid RVFI, bus schedules and accepted effects, accepted divide operands, dispatch results, resets, and cycle numbers. **34 scenarios, 28,706 sampled edges/events, 2,450 launches, and 5,814 retirements match exactly.** Each scenario also passes the unchanged Python ISS comparison. Existing one-clock ordinary/consumer and nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing checks pass; launch-to-accept remains eight clocks.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim.run_cosim` passes on both versions, including selftest and the evaluator's CoreMark configuration. Selftest additionally passes the unchanged ISS with both buses stalled using seeds 1, 2, 3, 19, and 97; all 115 retirements and their cycles match the starting RTL for each schedule.\n- The complete canonical 6 KB stalled CoreMark RVFI streams were compared byte for byte: **22,093,920 retirements and 6,463,986,468 bytes**, including every emitted retirement cycle and operand/result/memory field. No difference. Shared stream SHA256: `34ef03d244b986a66be40660efea1b01b6705ecbf1263bf493d109b7184ac4a0`.\n\n## FPGA evaluation and working-set discrepancy\n\nBoth versions used unchanged synthesis, wrapper, constraints, nextpnr script, seeds 1/2/3, and evaluator. All six routes completed. `make fpga TARGET=bench` used `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`. The starting RTL reproduces the winner's recorded median and resource counts.\n\n| Metric | Starting | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 190.33 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 191.13 | 184.84 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 179.89 | 168.69 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 184.84 |\n| Evaluator bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Exact evaluator iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,491,485 | 10 / 4,491,485 |\n| Evaluator fitness, iterations/s | 409.7976504430049 | 411.5342698461644 |\n| LUT4 | 5,490 | 5,485 |\n| Mapped FF | 2,112 | 2,119 |\n\nThe evaluator still builds **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000**, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and validates its existing 2 KB CRC table. This is a pre-existing discrepancy from the requested 6 KB contract; no evaluator or benchmark source was changed. Its unrounded iteration rate is `2.2264351322558128e-6`, not the rounded JSON field `2e-6`. Both versions ran the identical ELF, SHA256 `9b7d11c69a8a2b0db598fa2156474184488a3b2d22017f3fac05e6113ec74133`.\n\nSeparately built and validated the requested **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10** configuration from unchanged sources, using the same ELF for both versions (SHA256 `f224290c528bdbc3caa7badb285d6b6ae1fab75523bde60110cefe80de23a9e4`). Both runs have:\n\n- Start marker cycle 56,393; stop marker cycle 32,893,064; **32,836,671 bracketed cycles**; final EBREAK cycle 32,900,232; no OOB access.\n- Exact iteration rate `10 / 32836671 = 3.0453757020618807e-7`.\n- CRCs `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, `seedcrc=0x8a02`, `crcfinal=0x5949`, and the literal `Correct operation validated.` UART message. UART and completion records match exactly.\n- Using the same routed medians gives separate 6 KB fitness **56.053185172150975 -> 56.290724476911805 iterations/s**. These numbers are not substituted into the unchanged evaluator's output.\n\nThe unchanged UART metadata reports `CoreMark Size: 2000` for the 6 KB run's per-algorithm share, and a hardcoded `-O2` compiler string. The actual build command used `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; the canonical 6 KB CRCs identify the configuration.\n\n## Mapped D/CE cones\n\nInspected both mapped `synth.json` netlists, stopping combinational traversal at registered sources. Candidate enables were also evaluated over all busy/active/count combinations (320 mapped CE truth-table checks):\n\n- EX snapshots: `CE = !div_active_q`, one registered leaf, replacing a launch-qualified cone with 28 leaves.\n- Divisor, dividend, and four flags: `CE = !busy_q`, one registered leaf, replacing 28-leaf launch-qualified cones.\n- Q/R: `CE = !(busy_q && count_q == 7)`, four registered leaves, replacing 31-leaf qualified cones. Their D cones now select idle prefix versus recurrence using busy ownership and have no `div_active_q`/`held_q` request-state dependency. Arithmetic and forwarding data dependencies remain as intended.\n- `result_q` retains its four-leaf busy/count terminal enable. No idle-bank enable depends on held response ownership, opcode validity, load availability, request, or ready. Synchronous reset remains separate from these enables.\n\nNo RTL register was added, but mapping reports seven more physical FFs: two in each EX/MEM and MEM/WB register group and three under the register-file mapping. This physical resource increase is explicitly retained in the result rather than claiming an unchanged FF count.\n\n## All reported routed critical paths\n\nTimes below are nextpnr's printed path totals, rounded by that tool; Fmax above uses its final post-route frequency report. Cell names are the actual mapped endpoints, with architectural aliases verified against the mapped netlist.\n\n| Version/seed | Reported path | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Starting 1 | EX/MEM `rd[0]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.43 | 3.06 | 2.38 |\n| Starting 2 | wrapper `lfsr[20]` -> decode/register-read selection -> ID/EX `rs2_val[3]` | 5.23 | 2.61 | 2.63 |\n| Starting 3 | EX/MEM `rd[0]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.56 | 3.06 | 2.50 |\n| Candidate 1 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` -> normal ALU result selection -> normal address/result `[23]` | 5.25 | 2.35 | 2.90 |\n| Candidate 2 | MEM/WB `rd[1]` -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF `pc[1]` | 5.41 | 2.86 | 2.55 |\n| Candidate 3 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` -> normal ALU result selection -> normal address/result `[23]` | 5.93 | 2.35 | 3.58 |\n\nExact candidate endpoints:\n\n- Seeds 1 and 3: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_162.Q` -> `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_72.D`.\n- Seed 2: `cpu.u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_133.Q` -> `cpu.u_if.pc_DFFRE_Q.D`.\n\nExact starting endpoints:\n\n- Seeds 1 and 3: `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_22.Q` -> `cpu.u_if.pc_DFFRE_Q.D`.\n- Seed 2: `lfsr_DFFC_Q_10.Q` -> `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_124.D`.\n\nThese are the modeled paths reported by nextpnr. The absence of a divider endpoint does not establish divider timing margin or coverage of missing/unmodeled arcs. The small median gain cannot be attributed conclusively to a particular removed path; seed 3 regresses, and the two ALU-control paths differ substantially in routing delay. Additional idle switching is an explicit tradeoff and was not power-measured.\n\n## Evidence location\n\nAll scratch builds and evidence are under `$TMPDIR/divider-idle-r11s0/` (the run's existing `.tmp` directory), including `candidate/formal-local.log`, `candidate/units.log`, `candidate/remaining-tests.log`, `protocol-reference/`, `cosim-validation.json`, `sixk-validation.json`, `sixk-full-trace.json`, `fitness-comparison.json`, `mapped-cones.json`, both `fpga-eval.log` files, and all six extracted `*-critical-seed*.txt` reports. Contract files and other cores were not edited.\n\nseeds [190.33, 184.84, 168.69]\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-002-r11s1\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The accepted r5s1 structural change achieved 184.06 MHz by separating\\npipeline payload storage from squash and completion control. Later\\nforwarding, register-file, multiply-bank, and two-phase JALR changes\\nregressed routed fitness as other paths became critical. The bench\\nexperiment log contains no previous fetch-queue attempt.\\n\\nThe current IF/ID interface is combinational. hazard_unit freezes the\\nfetch PC for load-use, data-memory, and divider interlocks, while raw\\nio_imemReady still determines instruction availability on every cycle.\\nAn instruction delivered during a backend hold is not retained, so a\\nsubsequent instruction-bus stall can delay issue after that hold ends.\\nA small queue can retain these deliveries and fetch further sequential\\ninstructions while decode waits, overlapping independent stall causes.\\nThis targets iterations per cycle without relying on another increase\\nin the already sensitive execution-path Fmax.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Insert a two-entry, fall-through FIFO of {pc, instr} inside if_stage.\\nDecouple sequential fetch acceptance from consumption by ID/EX. This\\nqueue is the sole architectural experiment; retain the existing issue\\nwidth, execution stages, forwarding, branch policy, and arithmetic.\\n\\nKeep a dedicated registered fetch PC driving io_imemAddr directly.\\nPresent the oldest buffered entry to decode when the FIFO is nonempty;\\notherwise bypass the current {fetch_pc, io_imemData}. An empty queue\\ntherefore adds no mandatory fetch stage or branch-target refill cycle.\\nHead availability is registered occupancy OR a usable current bus\\nresponse. It must not depend on decode consumption or flush outputs.\\nBuffered head data remains available even when io_imemReady is zero,\\nbecause that entry was captured on an earlier ready cycle. Keep the\\nexisting invalid-instruction NOP/control-zero discipline.\\n\\nDefine decode_take as one actual, valid ID/EX capture, excluding reset,\\nredirect, load-use bubbles, data-memory holds, and divider holds. A\\nnormal-cycle fetch acceptance requires io_imemReady and either free\\nFIFO capacity or a simultaneous decode_take. Increment fetch_pc by\\nfour only on this acceptance. If empty and decode takes the live word,\\nadvance the fetch PC without storing a duplicate. Otherwise enqueue\\nthe accepted word with its original PC. With queued work, support\\nsimultaneous head consumption and tail insertion, including when full.\\nA full queue with no consumption holds the fetch PC and accepts nothing.\\nUse two fixed storage slots and a small occupancy state; preserve order\\nand prevent overflow, underflow, loss, and replay through every case.\\n\\nFeed effective head availability to the hazard logic in place of raw\\nimem readiness. Existing backend interlocks block head consumption;\\nthey must not prevent independent filling when capacity remains. Decode,\\nraw-source load-use comparison, immediate generation, and RVFI PC data\\nmust all refer to the same selected head. Do not introduce a readiness\\nloop through flush_if, a fall-through valid signal, or decode_take.\\n\\nReset and the existing EX redirect have priority over all queue events.\\nA redirect discards every queued wrong-path entry and the same-cycle\\nbus response, clears fetch serialization state, and sets fetch_pc to\\nthe resolved target even when the bus is unready or the FIFO is full.\\nRepeated redirects from a memory-held EX owner remain harmless. Preserve\\nthe direct redirect-target PC mux path; queue occupancy must not qualify\\nthe existing redirect or enter target arithmetic. Preserve the r5s1\\nseparation of payload storage from validity/squash control: invalidate\\nqueue ownership without adding redirect reset logic to every data bit.\\n\\nAccount for instruction-fetch serialization introduced by buffering.\\nOn acceptance of a FENCE.I word, stop accepting younger fetch words\\nuntil that same legal instruction advances out of EX after older memory\\nwork can advance. Older queued entries and the fence itself still drain.\\nSince nothing younger is accepted across this barrier, no buffered stale\\ninstruction survives it. Generate the release from existing valid EX\\nmetadata and its actual advancement, without adding a target adder or\\nchanging FENCE.I's existing no-write retirement behavior. Reset or a\\nwrong-path redirect cancels the barrier. Preserve decoder legality.\\n\\nExpect a low-confidence 5% fitness gain. For illustration, 6% fewer\\nmarker-window cycles with 1% less Fmax would improve the product about\\n5.3%; these are estimates, not measurements. The new instruction mux,\\nqueue payload enables, full/pop control, and redirected fetch-PC enables\\nmay instead reduce Fmax enough to erase the cycle saving. Inspect all\\nthree routed seeds, including instruction-to-regfile/decode paths and\\nthe unchanged forwarding/JALR path. Preserve direct registered memory\\naddresses and verify FPGA RAM inference; do not add timing exceptions\\nor interpret missing timing arcs as measured margin.\\n\\nValidate against a fresh accepted-core run with identical ELFs and\\nstall seeds. Require lint, the existing unit suite, unchanged formal\\nincluding liveness and unique retirement, and stalled ISS cosimulation.\\nAdd directed queue tests and measure actual marker-window cycle savings\\nunder both bus stalls, then combine them with median three-seed Fmax.\\nRequire the canonical 6 KB CRCs, bounded accesses, marker-only timing,\\nand the validation UART literal. Prior logs identify 409.80 as the\\nexisting 2 KB evaluator result; report that evaluator separately from\\nthe required 6 KB comparison without modifying either benchmark or\\nevaluator files. Preserve nret=1, all external ports, channel-0 RVFI\\nordering, decoder/trap checks, and the divider's real/ALTOPS schedule.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 5, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': 'Implement two fixed FIFO slots with PC/instruction payloads,\\noccupancy, empty bypass, explicit fetch acceptance and decode\\nconsumption, and simultaneous full pop/push. Keep the fetch address\\ndirectly registered. Handle redirect/reset cancellation and the\\nFENCE.I fetch barrier, separating payload capture from ownership\\ninvalidation. Expose head availability independently of flush.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire raw imem readiness into fetch and effective head availability\\ninto hazard control. Derive decode_take from actual ID/EX capture,\\nroute the selected head consistently to ID and hazard comparisons,\\nand release the fetch fence only when its valid EX owner advances\\npast older memory work. Preserve the existing EX redirect, pipeline\\nbundles, normal-bank data-memory address, retirement, and all ports.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Make frontend availability refer to the selected instruction head,\\nso raw instruction-bus backpressure cannot suppress queued work.\\nApply frontend interlocks to head consumption while fetch capacity\\nindependently controls PC advance. Preserve load-use bubbles,\\nmemory-stall priority, divider holding, redirect flushing, and\\nretained MEM/WB forwarding payloads without combinational loops.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add ISS/RVFI-checked directed programs for empty bypass, both FIFO\\noccupancies, full pop/push, capacity backpressure, long bus stalls,\\nqueue filling during load-use and divider holds, and buffered issue\\nwhile imemReady is low. Test redirects with empty/full queues and\\nunready buses, held redirects, reset, and discarded stores/divides.\\nVerify PC/instruction pairing, source values, exact-once retirement\\nand memory effects, and sustained one-per-cycle zero-wait issue.\\nInclude FENCE.I after a code store with delayed dmem acceptance,\\nqueued and bypassed fences, and wrong-path fence cancellation.\\nUse deterministic stall schedules to demonstrate actual overlap\\nsavings; preserve all existing architectural and arithmetic checks.\\n'}]}\nimplementation_notes # Two-entry fetch queue (r11s1)\n\nImplemented the queue and fetch serialization. Correctness checks pass, but this implementation regresses measured fitness: the required 6 KB comparison drops from 56.05 to 42.55 iterations/second (-24.08%). Its 3.08% cycle saving does not offset the routed Fmax loss. This is a completed implementation experiment, not an acceptance claim.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `if_stage.sv` now holds two fixed PC/instruction slots, a two-bit occupancy, a one-bit head pointer, and a FENCE.I barrier bit. Empty bypass adds no fetch stage. Full simultaneous pop/push replaces the consumed slot and preserves order. Fetch acceptance independently fills free capacity during load-use, memory, and divider holds.\n- The fetch address remains the direct output of a dedicated registered PC. Reset and EX redirect override acceptance and capacity, discard the same-cycle response, and clear queue/barrier ownership. Slot payloads have write enables without reset or redirect data muxes. Repeated redirects from a memory-held EX instruction are harmless.\n- `core.sv` derives consumption from the actual valid ID/EX capture conditions. Hazard readiness uses selected-head availability, which depends only on occupancy, instruction-bus readiness, and the registered barrier. Availability has no dependency on consumption or squash outputs. Decode, immediate generation, raw-source hazard comparisons, and RVFI PC all consume the same head.\n- FENCE.I acceptance blocks younger fetch acceptance until valid, legal EX metadata advances with older memory able to advance. Release reuses existing instruction metadata and advancement controls. Redirect/reset cancel the barrier. The decoder and its legality policy are unchanged; fences retain their no-write retirement behavior.\n- Removed the unused internal `stall_if` output because fetch capacity now controls sequential PC advancement. Updated the pipeline bundle comment. Execution stages, forwarding, arithmetic, divider real/ALTOPS schedule, direct data-memory address bank, retirement channel, `nret: 1`, and all external ports are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe accepted RTL was copied before any edits. Builds, immutable copies of the formal harness, ELFs, and logs live under `$TMPDIR/fetch-queue-r11s1/`; no benchmark, evaluator, formal contract, FPGA wrapper, constraints, or other core was edited.\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, zero warnings.\n- Existing unit suite: accepted 9 pytest runners / 140 cocotb cases PASS; candidate 9 runners / 145 cases PASS. Candidate includes 27 pipeline cases. The final pipeline rerun after adding an exact marker-window assertion passed all 27 cases.\n- Local formal: PASS, 53/53 checks on both accepted and candidate RTL, including `liveness`, `unique`, illegal-instruction, PC, and register checks. Candidate passed its first formal run; zero RTL formal fix retries. Ran unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` in isolated scratch copies with `CORE_NAME=bench RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl JOBS=6` and the existing Miniconda Python on PATH (the default Python lacks the required dependencies). Automatic selection retained `wrapper_si.sv`, `checks_si.cfg`, and their original depths/ALTOPS defines.\n- Unchanged Verilator harness / Python ISS selftest cosimulation: PASS on both cores for both-bus stall seeds `0xDEADBEEF`, 1, 42, and 99, 115 exact retirements each. Directed queue programs also compare all meaningful RVFI fields against the unchanged ISS and check accepted memory transactions and divider launches independently.\n- New edge-by-edge queue scoreboard checks occupancy, head PC/instruction pairing, NOP validity discipline, acceptance, consumption, fetch-PC advance/hold, fence ownership/release, and reset/redirect cancellation. Directed cases cover empty bypass, both occupancies, full pop/push, capacity backpressure, long instruction stalls, filling during all backend hold types, buffered unready issue, full/empty and repeated unready redirects, discarded stores/divides, reset with a full queue and pending fence, queued/bypassed FENCE.I, consecutive fences, and delayed self-modifying-code stores. Existing zero-wait integer/multiply and divide timing checks remain intact.\n- Deterministic two-bus stall program, identical on both cores: start marker cycle 3; accepted stop 35, candidate stop 33. Marker windows are 32 versus 30 cycles (6.25% saving). Candidate regression test requires the measured 30-cycle window.\n\n## Fresh performance comparison\n\nIdentical ELFs: `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000` and separately 2000, `ITERATIONS=10`, actual compiler flag `-O3`, RV32IM/ILP32. Both buses use the unchanged harness's approximately 22% random stalls. Only stop-minus-start MMIO marker cycles count.\n\n| Routed seed | Accepted MHz | Queue MHz |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | 135.43 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | 139.53 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | 128.60 |\n| Median | 184.06 | 135.43 |\n\nAll six canonical routes finished normally. These are final routed frequencies, not placement estimates. LUT4 usage is 5490 -> 5615; FF usage 2112 -> 2223; BSRAM usage remains four blocks.\n\n| Working set | Stall seed | Accepted marker cycles | Queue marker cycles | Saving |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 6 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 32,836,671 | 31,824,980 | 3.0810% |\n| 6 KB | 1 | 32,838,499 | 31,826,861 | 3.0806% |\n| 6 KB | 42 | 32,840,423 | 31,828,643 | 3.0809% |\n| 2 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 4,491,485 | 4,341,607 | 3.3369% |\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,490,174 | 4,339,252 | 3.3612% |\n| 2 KB | 42 | 4,493,775 | 4,343,014 | 3.3549% |\n\nRequired 6 KB fitness, using median routed Fmax and the default stall seed: `184.06e6 * 10 / 32836671 = 56.0532` versus `135.43e6 * 10 / 31824980 = 42.5546` iterations/second. The other two stall seeds show the same approximately 24.08% regression.\n\nThe existing 2 KB evaluator is reported separately: fresh accepted 409.80 versus candidate 311.94 (-23.88%) with its default stall seed. This reproduces the historical 409.80 result; it is not the required 6 KB score. The evaluator and both benchmark source configurations were not modified.\n\nAll twelve CoreMark runs completed with `ebreak:true`, `oob:false`, valid marker brackets, ten iterations, and the exact UART literal `Correct operation validated.`. Every 6 KB run has canonical `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, plus `seedcrc=0x8a02` and `crcfinal=0x5949`. Every 2 KB run passes the unchanged `validate_coremark_uart` evaluator. The port's printed compiler-flags string remains the existing hardcoded `-O2`; the captured build command actually used `-O3`. The 6 KB UART's `CoreMark Size: 2000` is the per-algorithm size and accompanies its `6k performance run parameters` banner.\n\nELF SHA-256 fingerprints:\n\n- 6 KB: `9c360d3fc8064e4ffaadfa2b7c4da1ec64e39c4ab0605c6e95951c87e626dac5`\n- 2 KB: `486c8f9034415384abe02106785d5b9484e1a9e0850d91f575515bf15b79c404`\n\n## Timing and remaining concerns\n\nReviewed all three canonical routed reports. Accepted seeds 1/3 are limited by forwarding through JALR target arithmetic to the fetch-PC register (5.43/5.56 ns); accepted seed 2 by the live instruction-to-ID/register-file path (5.23 ns). Candidate seeds 1/3 are limited by live instruction selection through register-file read logic into ID payload (7.38/7.78 ns); candidate seed 2 by live instruction/load-use/consumption logic into queue occupancy (7.17 ns). The queue therefore exposes exactly the frontend mux and full/pop-control risks identified in the hypothesis. Backend arithmetic and forwarding source files remain byte-identical to the accepted snapshot.\n\nSupplementary routes with `--report --detailed-timing-report` reproduced each candidate seed's canonical Fmax exactly to reported precision. The reports include ID capture, queue occupancy, and the fetch-PC target destination in every seed. Synthesized net aliases confirm the critical ID endpoints in seeds 1/3 are `rs1_val[1]` / `rs1_val[3]`. Absolute net-arrival values were not substituted for the canonical clock-path Fmax. A preliminary attempt to reload an already routed JSON solely for reporting hit a nextpnr internal assertion; the supplementary reports instead came from complete successful routes with the original synthesis input and constraints.\n\nYosys still reports the data-memory read address FF merged into the memory and maps it via `$__GOWIN_DP_`; all routed seeds retain four BSRAMs. The immutable FPGA wrapper supplies instructions from an LFSR, so it provides no instruction-RAM inference measurement. The RTL's instruction address is directly registered. No timing exceptions or constraint changes were added, and unreported paths are not treated as measured timing margin.\n\nDetailed machine-readable measurements and full logs are retained in `$TMPDIR/fetch-queue-r11s1/comparison.json`, `accepted/`, and `candidate/`. This regression should remain visible to the orchestrator's acceptance decision.\n\nseeds [135.43, 139.53, 128.6]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"The prior structural gains came from separating result storage and control paths. Fetch buffering regressed badly on timing, so I’m checking a narrower register-boundary change that preserves pipeline latency."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, and the trap discipline (illegal /\nECALL trap, EBREAK does NOT trap).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.clock import Clock\nfrom cocotb.triggers import RisingEdge, Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\nfrom test_ex_result_banks import _ex_snapshot, _unpack, ID_EX, CTRL\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0110011)\ndef LW  (rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b010, rd, 0b0000011)\ndef SW  (rs2, rs1, imm): return _s(imm & 0xFFF, rs2, rs1, 0b010, 0b0100011)\ndef BEQ (rs1, rs2, imm): return _b(imm, rs2, rs1, 0b000, 0b1100011)\ndef JAL (rd, imm):       return _j(imm, rd, 0b1101111)\ndef JALR(rd, rs1, imm):  return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b1100111)\ndef NOP():               return ADDI(0, 0, 0)        # = 0x00000013\ndef EBREAK():            return 0x00100073\ndef ECALL():             return 0x00000073\ndef DIV (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 4, rd, 0x33)\ndef DIVU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 5, rd, 0x33)\ndef REM (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 6, rd, 0x33)\ndef REMU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 7, rd, 0x33)\ndef MUL (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 0, rd, 0x33)\ndef LUI (rd, value): return (value & 0xFFFFF000) | ((rd & 31) << 7) | 0x37\n\n\n# ── Harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nasync def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200):\n    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n\n    imem is read combinationally each cycle from imemAddr; dmem similarly.\n    Stores get applied to the Python-side dmem dict so subsequent loads\n    see the new value.\n\n    Returns (retirements, dmem) where retirements is a list of dicts\n    sampled on every cycle that rvfi_valid=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    imem = {i * 4: instr & 0xFFFFFFFF for i, instr in enumerate(program)}\n    dmem = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    retirements: list[dict] = []\n\n    cocotb.start_soon(Clock(dut.clock, 10, \"ns\").start())\n\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n    dut.io_dmemRData.value = 0\n    # Zero-wait bus model: ready always asserted. The stall-mode tests\n    # live in vex_main.cpp's --istall/--dstall flags, not in cocotb.\n    dut.io_imemReady.value = 1\n    dut.io_dmemReady.value = 1\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n\n    # Deassert reset and prime the imem read for the first PC=0 fetch.\n    dut.reset.value = 0\n    dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(0, EBREAK())\n\n    for cycle in range(max_cycles):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n        await Timer(1, \"ns\")  # let combinational signals settle\n\n        # Sample the post-edge state.\n        ia        = int(dut.io_imemAddr.value)\n        da        = int(dut.io_dmemAddr.value)\n        wen       = int(dut.io_dmemWEn.value)\n        rvfi_v    = int(dut.io_rvfi_valid_0.value)\n\n        # Apply dmem write side effects.\n        if wen:\n            wdata = int(dut.io_dmemWData.value)\n            wa = da & ~3\n            old = dmem.get(wa, 0)\n            new = old\n            for i in range(4):\n                if (wen >> i) & 1:\n                    bv = (wdata >> (i * 8)) & 0xFF\n                    new = (new & ~(0xFF << (i * 8))) | (bv << (i * 8))\n            dmem[wa] = new\n\n        # Capture RVFI retirement.\n        if rvfi_v:\n            r = {\n                \"order\":     int(dut.io_rvfi_order_0.value),\n                \"insn\":      int(dut.io_rvfi_insn_0.value),\n                \"pc\":        int(dut.io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0.value),\n                \"pc_next\":   int(dut.io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0.value),\n                \"rd\":        int(dut.io_rvfi_rd_addr_0.value),\n                \"rd_wdata\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0.value),\n                \"rs1_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0.value),\n                \"rs1_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0.value),\n                \"rs2_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0.value),\n                \"rs2_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0.value),\n                \"trap\":      int(dut.io_rvfi_trap_0.value),\n                \"mem_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_addr_0.value),\n                \"mem_wmask\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0.value),\n                \"mem_wdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0.value),\n                \"mem_rmask\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0.value),\n                \"mem_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0.value),\n                \"cycle\":     cycle,\n            }\n            retirements.append(r)\n            if r[\"insn\"] == EBREAK():\n                break\n\n        # Drive next cycle's imem fetch and dmem read combinationally.\n        dut.io_imemData.value  = imem.get(ia & ~3, EBREAK())\n        dut.io_dmemRData.value = dmem.get(da & ~3, 0)\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(f\"max_cycles={max_cycles} reached without EBREAK\")\n\n    return retirements, dmem\n\n\ndef _by_insn(retirements, insn):\n    \"\"\"Return all retirements matching a specific instruction word.\"\"\"\n    return [r for r in retirements if r[\"insn\"] == insn]\n\n\nasync def _protocol_tick(dut):\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 1\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -o -i '.{0,80}(store.data|late.store|writeback|MEM/WB|write.back|alu.result.*read.data|register.boundary).{0,140}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | head -90\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -o -i '.{0,80}(store.data|late.store|writeback|MEM/WB|write.back|alu.result.*read.data|register.boundary).{0,140}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | head -90\"","aggregated_output":"2: consumes. In particular, all RV32M operations\\nstill consume both sources, and store-data dependencies still require\\nthe existing stall.\\n\\nThis removes one bubble per otherwise exposed false dependency without\\nchanging pipelin\n2:ty,\\nreal load-use interlocks, redirect priority, dmem hold behavior, retained\\nMEM/WB forwarding data, and the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\\n\\nPredict a modest improvement in CoreMark iterations per cycle with nearly\\n\n2:ncies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert c\n2:ncies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert c\n2:tion guard remains.\\nDecoder legality, pipeline depth, bus/redirect priorities, MEM/WB hold behavior,\\nand the nret=1 top-level port set are unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations required by validation\\n\\n- The first formal run passed 5\n2:repairs these retirement metadata fields from the preceding\\n  completed load's MEM/WB data. This path does not feed execution operands or\\n  store data. Retained WB data remains eligible through memory holds.\\n- EX/MEM forwar\n2:navailable at that stage.\\n  Real consumers retain their interlock and existing MEM/WB forwarding. This\\n  also preserves the old raw register value after a misaligned load that traps\\n  without writing its destination; such l\n2:talls. Added misaligned-load and\\ncombined-wait regressions, including retained MEM/WB forwarding while a branch\\nredirect overlaps both bus stalls.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings\n3:ive operation. Hold\\nID/EX and fetch while its result is unavailable. Let older MEM/WB work\\ndrain normally: when EX/MEM is ready, emit a bubble with zeroed control\\nduring the computation, not a repeated copy of the older ins\n3:in once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\\nDecoder legality, tr\n4:old PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n#\n4:et: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using th\n5:lete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and explicitly\\n  require EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, selector/bundle\\n  holds, redirects simultaneous with each bus stall, blocking division,\\n  illegal-default A\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-202609\n6:eted\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary instructions or divisions another execution clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\":\n6:eted\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary ins