{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md\nrg -n 'predictor|hyp-20260906-003-r1s2|hyp-20260906-003-r2s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\ncat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 (regression, -5.98%): Replacing ALU case selection with registered one-hot masks and a balanced OR tree disrupted multiplier sharing (MULT36X36 count rose from 1 to 3) and left slower selector-to-EX/MEM paths critical, lowering median Fmax 5.98% despite unchanged arithmetic and CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.85%): Routing the post-register DIV/REM result-bank mux into mem_stage.address blocks Yosys BRAM address-register absorption, so memory addresses must use the normal EX/MEM bank directly to avoid expansion from four block RAMs to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks and placement failure.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer with a two-bit\\nsaturating direction counter per entry. Index it with fetch PC[5:2], use\\nthe full remaining PC[31:6] as the tag, and store aligned target[31:2].\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\\npredict PC+4. Conditional branches use the counter for direction, while\\nresolved JAL/JALR entries are set strongly taken and learn their latest\\ntarget. Clear valid bits on reset; payloads need not be reset and must\\nnever influence a miss. Use the full architectural PC and target widths.\\n\\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\\nIF/ID into ID/EX. EX computes the actual next PC using the existing\\nforwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush younger\\nwork only when a valid instruction advances out of EX and its actual\\nnext PC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every valid\\ninstruction, including a non-control instruction with a stale table hit.\\nThe repair target is the actual next PC, including PC+4 for a predicted\\ntaken branch that resolves not taken, and the existing PC+4 continuation\\nfor a misaligned target trap. Preserve JAL/JALR link data, trap handling,\\nand the resolved next PC reported through RVFI.\\n\\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never while EX is held by dmem\\nbackpressure. For a matching conditional entry, saturate toward its\\nresolved direction and refresh its aligned branch target. Allocate a\\nmissed conditional entry only when taken, initializing it weakly taken\\n(2'b10). Allocate or refresh legal, aligned JAL/JALR targets with 2'b11.\\nInvalidate a matching entry when the resolved instruction is no longer\\na legal control transfer or its control-transfer target is misaligned.\\nNever allocate or reinforce predictions from bubbles or trap outcomes.\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training to use the pre-edge table\\ncontents for the prediction that is captured; a later table update must\\nnot change recorded metadata.\\n\\nApply a predicted PC advance only when that fetch is actually accepted\\ninto ID/EX. On load-use hazards hold the fetch PC and bubble ID/EX;\\non dmem holds freeze both PC and ID/EX, including its saved prediction.\\nInject the existing invalid bubble on imem unavailability when ID/EX\\nis otherwise able to advance.\\nWhile dmem holds EX, defer both repair and training until EX can advance;\\nretain the existing forwarding state during the hold. An eligible EX\\nrepair must override imem backpressure and any younger prediction.\\nKeep raw lookup independent of flush/stall signals to avoid combinational\\nloops. Correct predictions must not assert a redirect or flush.\\n\\nThis single predictor is expected to improve net fitness by about 8%,\\nsubject to measurement. The small table limits added lookup depth and\\nrouting, but conflicts, changing indirect targets, and additional LUT/FF\\ncost may reduce the gain or lower Fmax. Validate with unchanged formal,\\ncosimulation, CRC, UART, marker-window, and three-seed FPGA evaluation\\ngates. Preserve the nret=1 interface and every evaluation invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 291.2, \"delta_pct\": 2.96, \"vs_baseline\": 2.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10377, \"ff\": 2069, \"seeds\": [129.95, 121.11, 122.77], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Small tagged bimodal predictor\\n\\nImplemented the requested 16-entry direct-mapped predictor in `cores/bench`.\\nThe index is PC[5:2], the tag is PC[31:6], and the stored aligned target is\\ntarget[31:2]. A valid tag hit with counter[1] predicts the target; all misses\\nuse full-width PC+4. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\\nConditional hits refresh the target and saturate the direction counter.\\nTaken conditional misses allocate weakly taken; not-taken misses leave the\\nresident entry untouched. Legal aligned JAL/JALR updates are strongly taken\\nand learn their latest target. Matching stale, illegal, or misaligned entries\\nare invalidated, including misaligned untaken branch targets. Bubbles and\\nheld EX instructions cannot train.\\n\\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC consumed at the accepting\\nedge. EX compares every advancing valid instruction against one actual next\\nPC, also used for RVFI. A mismatch repairs fetch and discards the younger\\ncombinational IF/ID slot. Correct predictions have no repair bubble. JAL/JALR\\nlink writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, misalignment traps, and PC+4 trap\\ncontinuation are preserved. Repairs wait through dmem holds and override\\nimem backpressure when EX advances.\\n\\nLoad-use stalls hold PC and inject an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n## Deviations and scope\\n\\n- Added standalone predictor tests as well as pipeline regressions. The\\n  pipeline harness now models pre-edge bus acceptance and checks RVFI order,\\n  PC continuity, prediction capture, holds, repairs, and store handshakes.\\n- Split hazard equations into independent continuous assignments to avoid\\n  false combinational scheduling cycles in the instrumented Verilator build.\\n- Used `core_pkg.sv` first when linting; compilation-unit typedefs require\\n  this ordering. No lint suppressions were added for combinational loops.\\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This notes file is the requested\\n  exception to the RTL/test editing surface.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\\n\\nThe full formal log is under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-formal/formal-final-console.log`; FPGA seed\\nlogs and the combined result are under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-fpga/`. Cocotb XML results, selftest comparison\\nresults, and the supplemental 6 KB build/run are under the corresponding\\n`-cocotb`, `-selftest-results.json`, and `-6k` scratch paths.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nThe prompt specifies 6 KB CoreMark, but the checked-in Makefile defaults and\\n`tools/eval/fpga.py` CRC gate use the 2 KB fixture. Both workloads were tested\\nseparately without changing the evaluator. The 2 KB measurement must not be\\nreported as a 6 KB fitness result. The measured 10,377 LUT4 exceeds\\n`core.yaml`'s 10,000-LUT target, although all placements succeeded. FPGA\\ncost and reduced Fmax limit the net gain. Direct-map conflicts and changing\\nindirect targets remain expected sources of prediction misses. These are\\nlocal results; orchestrator acceptance and its independent rerun remain\\noutside this implementation step.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:27:51.865455Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\"}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move ALU result-select decoding across the existing ID/EX register,\\nwithout adding a pipeline stage. Add a 16-bit selector to id_ex_t:\\nbits 0 through 14 select the existing ALU_ADD through ALU_MULHSU results\\nusing their unchanged numeric operation constants, and bit 15 selects\\nthe shared DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU response. Generate these mutually exclusive\\nenables from dec_alu_op in ID and latch them with the instruction.\\nKeep the encoded operation for the divider's signedness, quotient versus\\nremainder choice, and distinct formal ALTOPS substitutions.\\n\\nIn EX, consume the registered enables directly. Implement ALU output\\nselection as masked result terms with a balanced OR reduction, avoiding\\na new priority chain or re-encoding followed by another binary decode.\\nUse selector bit 15, still qualified by in.valid and !is_illegal, for\\nthe existing is_div classification. The division result term retains\\nits existing div_resp_valid qualification. Keep every arithmetic\\nexpression, multiplier, divider recurrence, and ALTOPS formula intact.\\nThis is one control-path micro-optimization, not an arithmetic redesign.\\n\\nDerive selectors from the existing decoder output without changing its\\nlegality decisions. Preserve its default ADD selection even for illegal\\ninstructions; an unsupported internal ALU operation selects no result.\\nReset and flush clear the complete ID/EX bundle, while every hold retains\\nthe selectors with the instruction. Preserve the eight-compute-clock\\ndivider, terminal-edge acceptance, held response, operand snapshots,\\nload interlocks, forwarding, redirect priority, and nret=1 RVFI contract.\\nIn particular, add no completion cycle: the previous divider notes show\\nthat this would violate the unchanged bounded liveness check.\\n\\nExpect identical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and a modest Fmax gain\\nfrom removing binary operation decode from the measured EX paths. The\\nfive-percent fitness estimate is unmeasured. Extra selector fanout, OR\\nrouting, synthesis restructuring, and a newly dominant ID path could\\nerase the gain. Validate actual mapped timing and area with the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA evaluation against the current winner, and compare cycle\\ncounts using identical binaries and bus-stall settings. Run existing\\nlint, formal, arithmetic, pipeline, and cosim gates; retain the required\\n6 KB CRCs, UART validation, memory bounds, and timing markers. If saved\\nevaluator artifacts use a different working-set size, report those\\nmeasurements separately without altering the evaluation contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the 16 result-selector positions and add an alu_result_sel\\nfield only to id_ex_t. Keep the existing five-bit ALU operation\\nvalues, ctrl_t legality fields, downstream bundles, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into the specified result enables before the\\nID/EX register. Latch them with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold on stalls and clear on reset/flush. Include all four division\\noperations in the shared bit-15 class. Preserve decoder defaults\\nand avoid deriving selectors from unvalidated raw instruction bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the registered selectors into alu and use their division-class\\nbit for is_div with the existing valid/legal guards. Preserve the\\nencoded divider operation, all request/response and stall equations,\\ncaptured operands, jump-result mux, traps, and EX/MEM acceptance timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the predecoded selector vector and replace the encoded-op\\noutput case with parallel masked result selection and a balanced OR\\ntree. Preserve all real arithmetic expressions and formal ALTOPS\\nformulas, including each multiply variant. Keep div_unit's encoded\\nop input and handshake unchanged; its result term still outputs zero\\nunless div_resp_valid. A zero selector vector produces zero output.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Update local stimulus helpers to drive the new selector input in\\nstep with every operation change, retaining every existing expected\\nvalue and timing assertion. Exercise all result classes, zero-hot\\nselection, rapid operation transitions, signed and unsigned multiply\\nhigh halves, and the existing division edge cases and held responses\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Keep shared test infrastructure intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed-operation streams to verify that registered\\nselectors stay paired with operands across forwarding, load-use\\nbubbles, imem stalls, dmem holds, redirects, and blocking division.\\nCheck unchanged retirement spacing for ordinary ALU/multiply and\\nconsecutive divides, full RVFI operand/result and order consistency,\\nand absence of wrong-path effects. Retain all existing assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the 16 result-selector positions and add an alu_result_sel\\nfield only to id_ex_t. Keep the existing five-bit ALU operation\\nvalues, ctrl_t legality fields, downstream bundles, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into the specified result enables before the\\nID/EX register. Latch them with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold on stalls and clear on reset/flush. Include all four division\\noperations in the shared bit-15 class. Preserve decoder defaults\\nand avoid deriving selectors from unvalidated raw instruction bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the registered selectors into alu and use their division-class\\nbit for is_div with the existing valid/legal guards. Preserve the\\nencoded divider operation, all request/response and stall equations,\\ncaptured operands, jump-result mux, traps, and EX/MEM acceptance timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the predecoded selector vector and replace the encoded-op\\noutput case with parallel masked result selection and a balanced OR\\ntree. Preserve all real arithmetic expressions and formal ALTOPS\\nformulas, including each multiply variant. Keep div_unit's encoded\\nop input and handshake unchanged; its result term still outputs zero\\nunless div_resp_valid. A zero selector vector produces zero output.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Update local stimulus helpers to drive the new selector input in\\nstep with every operation change, retaining every existing expected\\nvalue and timing assertion. Exercise all result classes, zero-hot\\nselection, rapid operation transitions, signed and unsigned multiply\\nhigh halves, and the existing division edge cases and held responses\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Keep shared test infrastructure intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed-operation streams to verify that registered\\nselectors stay paired with operands across forwarding, load-use\\nbubbles, imem stalls, dmem holds, redirects, and blocking division.\\nCheck unchanged retirement spacing for ordinary ALU/multiply and\\nconsecutive divides, full RVFI operand/result and order consistency,\\nand absence of wrong-path effects. Retain all existing assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.35, \"delta_pct\": -5.98, \"vs_baseline\": 23.88, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5509, \"ff\": 2122, \"seeds\": [141.2, 164.83, 157.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# hyp-20260906-001-r2s0: predecoded ALU result selection\\n\\nImplemented the proposed control change. All local correctness gates pass,\\nbut the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation regresses median Fmax and\\nthroughput by 5.98%. The measured result does not support the estimated gain.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`: added 16 named selector positions and a\\n  16-bit `alu_result_sel` field only to `id_ex_t`. Positions 0 through 14\\n  retain the numeric ADD through MULHSU operation values; position 15\\n  selects the shared divider response. Encoded operations, `ctrl_t`,\\n  downstream bundles, and RVFI widths are unchanged.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv`: predecode comes exclusively from\\n  `dec_alu_op`. Equality enables for operations 0 through 14 and a shared\\n  DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU enable are latched with the existing ID/EX instruction\\n  enable. Reset/flush clear the entire bundle and holds preserve it.\\n  Illegal decoder outputs retain default ADD; unsupported internal operation\\n  values match no selector. Decoder legality logic is untouched.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: passes the registered enables to the ALU\\n  and uses bit 15 with the existing valid/legal guards for `is_div`.\\n  Divider launch, acceptance, stall, forwarding, captured operands, jump\\n  result selection, redirects, traps, and EX/MEM timing are unchanged.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: replaced the encoded output case with 16 masked\\n  result terms and explicit 8/4/2/1 balanced OR levels. Every arithmetic\\n  expression, signed/unsigned multiplier expression, and ALTOPS formula is\\n  preserved. The divider term remains qualified by `div_resp_valid`.\\n  `div_unit.sv` is unchanged, including encoded operation selection and\\n  terminal-edge response acceptance. A zero selector produces zero output.\\n- `cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: every operation drive now supplies its\\n  selector. All original vectors and timing assertions remain. Added\\n  randomized rapid transitions across all combinational classes, zero-hot\\n  selection, unsupported operations, and switching result selection while\\n  each type of divider response is held, in real and ALTOPS builds.\\n- `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`: added dependent streams covering all\\n  16 classes, exact one-clock ordinary/multiply and nine-clock divide\\n  retirement spacing, and mixed streams with both bus stalls. The latter\\n  compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and explicitly\\n  require EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, selector/bundle\\n  holds, redirects simultaneous with each bus stall, blocking division,\\n  illegal-default ADD, and absence of wrong-path stores/divider requests.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Four-bit selector-position\\nconstants explicitly narrow the unchanged five-bit operation constants to\\navoid Verilator array-index width warnings. No source outside the four RTL\\nfiles and two allowed test files was changed, apart from this requested note.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- PASS: the exact requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` command, with no warnings.\\n  The same lint also passes with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\\n- PASS: unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, auto-selecting `nret: 1`,\\n  `wrapper_si.sv`, and `checks_si.cfg`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, first formal\\n  invocation, zero formal retries. Includes non-vacuous channel-0\\n  retirement, liveness, uniqueness, register/PC routing, and M-ext ALTOPS.\\n  No counterexample was produced. Full real-arithmetic formal-deep was\\n  not run; multiplier/divider arithmetic was not redesigned.\\n- PASS: all six pytest runners, **127 cocotb cases**: 32 real ALU, 32 ALTOPS\\n  ALU, 30 decoder, 8 immediate, 20 pipeline, and 5 register-file tests.\\n- PASS: unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` gate, two ELFs. Additional selftest\\n  comparison against the unchanged Python ISS passed all 115 retirements\\n  both zero-wait and with `--istall --dstall`.\\n- PASS: both saved 2 KB and required 6 KB CoreMark binaries complete with\\n  in-bounds memory, start/stop timing markers, 10 iterations, exact CRCs,\\n  and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Both designs were run\\n  locally against the same binary bytes and `--bench --istall --dstall`.\\n\\nAll generated files and copied gate infrastructure are under `$TMPDIR`,\\nnot in protected worktree directories. The installed Miniconda Python was\\nselected for cocotb, click, and YAML dependencies; the default Homebrew\\nPython lacked cocotb/click. No dependency or gate files were edited.\\n\\n## Three-seed mapped comparison\\n\\nRan unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1` and\\n`BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, using Yosys 0.64 and the existing nextpnr\\nscript/constraints. Compared against the saved current divider winner,\\nwhose formal-staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte equal to starting HEAD.\\nThe winner's evaluator sources and FPGA constraints also match this tree.\\n\\n| Measurement | Current winner | This implementation |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 routed Fmax, MHz | 165.32 | 141.20 |\\n| Seed 2 routed Fmax, MHz | 174.03 | 164.83 |\\n| Seed 3 routed Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\\n| LUT4 | 5,438 | 5,509 |\\n| DFF | 2,094 | 2,122 |\\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 3 |\\n| Saved evaluator 2 KB fitness, iter/s | 372.62 | 350.35 |\\n\\nAll three placements succeeded. Median frequency falls 5.98%; LUT4 rises\\n1.31% and DFF rises 1.34%. Although the RTL multiplier expressions are\\nidentical, the different selection network changes synthesis sharing and\\nmaps three MULT36X36 cells instead of one. This is a material area concern.\\n\\nThe final candidate paths are 7.08, 6.07, and 6.36 ns for seeds 1, 2, and 3.\\nThey start at `id_ex_w[46]` for seeds 1/3 and `[44]` for seed 2: registered\\nMULHU and MUL selectors, respectively. All end at EX/MEM `alu_result[4]`\\n(`ex_mem_w[235]`, `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_59`). Binary result decode was\\nremoved, but the mapped result-selection paths remain critical and slower.\\n\\n## Working-set distinction and identical-binary cycle comparison\\n\\nThe repository's unchanged evaluator and default CoreMark Makefile use\\n`TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`, despite the requested 6 KB contract. Its 350.35\\niter/s result above is therefore a separate 2 KB measurement, not a 6 KB\\nfitness claim. Neither evaluator constants nor benchmark sources were\\nmodified to reconcile this discrepancy.\\n\\n| Binary / bus settings | Winner cycles | Candidate cycles | Start / stop cycles, both |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| Saved 2 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 15,801 / 4,507,286 |\\n| Required 6 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56,393 / 32,893,064 |\\n\\nThe saved 6 KB build log confirms `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; its UART\\ncompiler-flags string is a stale hardcoded `-O2` string. The reported\\n`CoreMark Size: 2000` is per algorithm for this 6000-byte total working set.\\nIts exact CRCs pass: seed `0x8a02`, list **`0xd4b0`**, matrix **`0xbe52`**,\\nstate **`0x5e47`**, final `0x5949`. Combining its measured cycles with the\\nthree-seed frequencies gives **50.97 -> 47.92 iter/s** for 6 KB, also -5.98%.\\nThis separate calculation does not replace the unchanged evaluator result.\\n\\nBinary SHA-256 values used for both designs:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `54d433aa39f063a87e7ebc9697bb14dc3a6b855705812d18cbc5ead32cc155b3`\\n- 6 KB: `7b52a15eb9a3de9a8fe8bf6e69abff8810cfe9753be9ee5ce94bbe509dba3955`\\n\\n`make fpga` rebuilt its own 2 KB ELF with different file metadata; every\\nPT_LOAD address, size, and byte hash matches the saved 2 KB ELF, and its\\nmeasured bracket is also exactly 4,491,485 cycles.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\nCandidate scratch root:\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-001-predecode-brw7_gc6/`\\n\\n- `formal-console.log` and `formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-33833/checks/`\\n- `unit-tests.log` and `cores/bench/test/*_runner.result.xml`\\n- `cosim-gate.log` and `selftest-comparison.json`\\n- `fpga-eval.log`, `fpga-result.json`, `timing-paths.json`, and\\n  `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`\\n- `coremark-comparison.json` and `{winner,candidate}-{2k,6k}-coremark.log`\\n\\nSaved winner root: `$TMPDIR/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/`.\\nThe five-percent gain remains unsupported; this implementation is correct\\nunder the local gates but is a measured fitness regression.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:03:10.468534Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 (regression, -5.98%): Replacing ALU case selection with registered one-hot masks and a balanced OR tree disrupted multiplier sharing (MULT36X36 count rose from 1 to 3) and left slower selector-to-EX/MEM paths critical, lowering median Fmax 5.98% despite unchanged arithmetic and CoreMark cycles.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry, direct-mapped, full-width tagged bimodal BTB to the\\ncurrent cores/bench RTL, with training computed exclusively from\\nregistered fetch metadata and the instruction's resolved outcome.\\nThis is one predictor experiment: keep the existing divider, ALU,\\nforwarding, memory system, pipeline boundaries, and nret=1 interface.\\n\\nIndex the BTB by PC[5:2], store the full tag PC[31:6], aligned target\\nbits [31:2], a two-bit saturating counter, and a valid bit. Lookup is\\ncombinational and depends only on the fetch PC. A valid full-tag hit\\nwith counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise predict the\\nfull-width PC+4. Reset clears valid bits; unreset payloads must never\\naffect a miss. Do not introduce an instruction-data target adder or\\nreduce architectural address widths.\\n\\nAlong with the exact predicted_next_pc, capture lookup_hit and the\\nlookup_counter in ID/EX on the same edge that accepts the instruction.\\nA hit includes entries whose direction counter predicts not taken.\\nSupply a defined counter value on a miss. Hold all metadata with a\\nstalled instruction and clear it with a bubble or flush. The predictor\\narrays must have no resolving-PC read port: training must not use\\ntag_q[train_index], counter_q[train_index], or valid_q[train_index]\\nas an input to its next-state/write-enable logic.\\n\\nTrain once when a valid instruction actually advances from EX into\\nEX/MEM: in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. For a legal aligned\\nconditional branch with saved lookup_hit, saturate the saved counter\\ntoward the resolved direction and write a complete entry using this\\ninstruction's actual PC tag and branch target. On a saved miss,\\nallocate only a taken aligned branch, initialized weakly taken (10).\\nLegal aligned JAL/JALR always write the resolved target and counter\\n11. If an instruction with saved lookup_hit is no longer a legal\\ncontrol transfer, or has a misaligned control-transfer target, clear\\nthe addressed valid bit. Invalid instructions and bubbles never\\nallocate. Compute every payload write and enable without rereading\\nresident predictor state at EX; use local index write decoding.\\n\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training using the pre-edge table for\\nboth the consumed next PC and its captured metadata. Deliberately\\naccept a stale counter/hit snapshot when an older resolving instruction\\nupdates the same index at that edge; do not add a training-to-fetch\\nbypass that reconstructs the long feedback path. Such collisions may\\nlose a counter update or evict a newer entry, affecting prediction\\naccuracy only. Every allocation/update writes its own complete actual\\ntag and target, so metadata from different instructions cannot be\\ncombined into a fabricated entry. Full next-PC checking remains the\\ncorrectness mechanism. This bounded approximation must be exercised\\nby consecutive same-index branches and single-instruction loops.\\n\\nEX computes one actual_next_pc with the existing forwarded operands,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and alignment/trap continuation rules. For\\nevery advancing valid instruction, compare it with that instruction's\\nsaved predicted_next_pc, including ordinary instructions with stale\\nBTB hits and completed DIV/REM operations. Redirect only on a mismatch;\\nrepair to actual_next_pc and squash younger work. Correct predictions\\nintroduce no flush. RVFI pc_wdata always reports the resolved next PC;\\nJAL/JALR link writes and trap suppression remain unchanged.\\n\\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when its instruction is accepted.\\nPreserve load-use bubbles, invalid imem bubbles, dmem holds and retained\\nWB forwarding data, and all divider request/response behavior. Defer\\nrepairs and training while EX cannot advance. An eligible repair\\noverrides instruction-bus backpressure and the younger prediction.\\nKeep raw predictor lookup independent of stall/flush/redirect signals\\nand avoid combinational cycles in hazard control.\\n\\nThe prediction benefit is an estimate, not a new measurement. Retaining\\nabout 6% fewer cycles while losing at most about 1% Fmax would improve\\nfitness by roughly 5%. Counter-snapshot collisions, the remaining\\nlookup/repair paths, and FPGA mapping could reduce that gain. Compare\\nagainst the current 372.62 winner using the unchanged marker-bracketed\\nCoreMark and three-seed median-Fmax gates, with all existing formal,\\ncosim, CRC, UART, OOB, and resource checks intact. Inspect routed timing\\nto confirm that predictor training no longer contains a tag-RAM read\\nfollowed by tag comparison and table write enable in the same cycle.\\nKeep the official fixture and any supplemental 6 KB results distinct;\\ndo not modify either the evaluator or benchmark sources.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.07, \"delta_pct\": -7.39, \"vs_baseline\": 22.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6287, \"ff\": 3097, \"seeds\": [143.84, 145.48, 152.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the 16-entry full-width tagged bimodal BTB in cores/bench. Fetch\\nuses PC[5:2], the full PC[31:6] tag, an aligned 30-bit target, validity and a\\ntwo-bit direction counter. A miss exports counter 00 and full-width PC+4.\\nOnly valid bits reset; payloads cannot influence misses.\\n\\nID/EX captures the exact predicted next PC, hit (including not-taken hits),\\nand counter with its instruction. All fields hold during dmem/divider stalls\\nand clear on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. EX computes one resolved\\nnext PC, with forwarded operands, JALR bit-zero clearing and existing trap\\ncontinuation. Every valid EX/MEM acceptance compares that PC with its saved\\nprediction, including ordinary instructions and completed DIV/REM. Only a\\nmismatch repairs fetch and squashes younger work. RVFI retains the resolved\\nPC and the unchanged nret=1 channel-0 retirement/order interface.\\n\\nThe same in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait event drives training.\\nTraining uses only the registered hit/counter and resolved instruction:\\nsaved-hit aligned branches saturate the snapshot, saved misses allocate only\\ntaken aligned branches at 10, and legal aligned JAL/JALR write 11. Stale hits\\non non-control/illegal instructions or misaligned targets invalidate their\\nindex. Every payload update writes its own complete actual tag and target.\\nConstant per-entry write decoding introduces no resolving-PC read port.\\nSimultaneous lookup/training deliberately consumes the pre-edge table with\\nno bypass; stale snapshots can lose updates or evict intervening entries.\\n\\nHazard equations are separate continuous assignments so EX's dmem hold is\\nindependent of its qualified repair. Eligible repairs override imem\\nbackpressure; dmem/divider holds defer both repair and training. Load-use\\nbubbles, retained WB forwarding data, divider request/completion behavior,\\nALU, decoder, memory stages, pipeline boundaries and all top-level ports\\nremain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Local write decoding maps\\nthe table payload into enabled flops rather than relying on a RAM primitive.\\nThe new tests add an instruction-version-aware sparse fetch harness for\\nstale-code scenarios; they use the existing Python ISS and RVFI comparator\\nwithout changing either contract file. Existing tests/assertions remain.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS on the first run, 53 passed and 0 failed; no formal\\nfix attempts were needed. The unchanged bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected\\nwrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl.\\nIt ran from an isolated copy under TMPDIR because the worktree's upstream\\nformal directory is a shared symlink. No checks, depths or wrappers changed.\\nFull non-ALTOPS formal-deep was not run; divider arithmetic is unchanged and\\nboth real and ALTOPS arithmetic tests passed.\\n\\nThe exact requested glob-order Verilator lint command passes without\\nwarnings, as does the ALTOPS variant. All seven pytest runners pass, covering\\n129 cocotb cases: real ALU 30, ALTOPS ALU 30, decoder 30, immediate generator\\n8, register file 5, predictor 3 and pipeline 23. Coverage includes all tag\\nbits/indices/target bits, all saved counters, saved misses, coherent stale\\nreplacement, pre-edge collisions, inactive training, reset, warm loops and\\nexits, both direction errors, changing forwarded JALR targets, illegal and\\nmisaligned stale hits, consecutive same-index branches, single-instruction\\nloops, random imem/dmem stalls, 12-clock holds, retained WB data, deferred\\nDIV/REM repairs, and killed stores/divides. Pipeline monitors check every\\nmetadata capture/hold/bubble, accepted fetch PC, full-PC repair, EX training\\nevent, retirement continuity/order and absence of correct-prediction bubbles.\\n\\nA mapped-netlist combinational-cone audit checked every D and CE input of\\nall 944 predictor storage flops. None has a resident valid/tag/counter/target\\nbit as a combinational input. This confirms the training feedback path was\\nremoved in the mapped hardware, beyond the source-level read-port check.\\n\\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench gate, with NRET=1 and\\nBENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv, completed all three routes. Final Fmax values\\nwere 143.84, 145.48 and 152.72 MHz; median 145.48 MHz. Resource usage was\\n6,287 LUT4 and 3,097 FF, within the 10,000-LUT target. The unchanged cosim\\ngate also passed both selftest and CoreMark, including the official CRC,\\nUART, EBREAK, OOB and marker checks. CoreMark used 4,215,937 bracketed cycles\\nfor 10 iterations with the standard random instruction/data stalls.\\n\\nOfficial local fitness on the current divider-based core is 345.07\\niterations/s versus the current winner's 372.62: a 7.394% regression.\\nTimed cycles decreased 6.135%, but median Fmax decreased 13.074%. LUT4 usage\\nincreased 15.612%. The cycle benefit survived; the estimated <=1% Fmax loss\\ndid not. This implementation therefore does not beat the current winner.\\n\\nAll routed critical paths were inspected. Seed 1 has a 6.95 ns ID/EX\\ncontrol-to-EX/MEM path (3.30 ns logic, 3.65 ns routing). Seed 2 has a 6.87 ns\\npath from ID/EX PC through target-alignment/legal-control qualification to\\na predictor target write enable (2.77 ns logic, 4.10 ns routing). Seed 3\\nhas a 6.55 ns path from ID/EX branch control through resolved training\\nqualification to a predictor target write enable (2.57 ns logic, 3.98 ns\\nrouting). None contains a tag-table read followed by tag comparison and\\ntable write enable. Training qualification and enable fanout remain timing\\nlimitations after removing the original feedback path; the mapped-cone\\naudit above confirms there is no hidden resident-state training dependency.\\n\\nThe official checked-in fixture/evaluator use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3 and\\nITERATIONS=10. They were copied unchanged for evaluation. The recorded\\ncurrent winner is 372.62 iterations/s, 167.36 MHz, 4,491,485 bracketed cycles,\\n5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 FF. These are the comparison values from the existing\\nexperiment log, not new measurements of the baseline.\\n\\nSeparately, an unmodified scratch copy built with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 and -O3\\ncompleted 10 iterations in 30,823,120 marker-bracketed cycles, versus the\\nrecorded winner's supplemental 32,836,671 cycles (6.132% fewer). It reported\\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949,\\nthe literal Correct operation validated., both markers, EBREAK and no OOB\\naccess. At the candidate's 145.48 MHz median this is 47.20 iterations/s.\\nThis is supplemental 6 KB evidence, separate from official fitness.\\nThe UART compiler-flags string is hardcoded; the build command used -O3.\\n\\nEvidence directories, all under the authorized TMPDIR:\\n\\n- predictor-formal-mcapvt3h: run.log and formal/last_run-*.log, all per-check logs.\\n- predictor-tests-qlavcwvr: seven runner XML files and Verilator builds.\\n- predictor-fpga-667ih_e0: gate-*.log, training-cone-check.json, synth.json and\\n  cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log plus routed netlists.\\n- predictor-supplemental-6k-gvvrkbrq: build.log, sim.log and result.json.\\n\\nTMPDIR is /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp.\\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this explicitly\\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree. The orchestrator's\\nacceptance remains independent of these local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:12:02.854732Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path.cwd()\nfor d in [*reversed(root.parents), root]:\n    p=d/'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'\\\\n{p}\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\nfor d in ['cores','cores/bench','cores/bench/experiments','cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses']:\n    p=root/d/'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'\\\\n{p}\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\nPY\ncat CLAUDE.md\ngit status --short\nrg --files --hidden cores/bench/experiments cores/bench/test | head -90\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path.cwd()\nfor d in [*reversed(root.parents), root]:\n    p=d/'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'\\\\n{p}\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\nfor d in ['cores','cores/bench','cores/bench/experiments','cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses']:\n    p=root/d/'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'\\\\n{p}\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\nPY\ncat CLAUDE.md\ngit status --short\nrg --files --hidden cores/bench/experiments cores/bench/test | head -90\"","aggregated_output":"# CLAUDE.md — non-negotiable invariants and don't-touch list\n\nEvery item below was a real bug in the prior Chisel iteration. Each must be\nenforced by a check that fails loudly when violated. **Do not weaken these\nchecks** to make a hypothesis pass.\n\n## Hard invariants\n\n| # | Invariant                                                                                                  | Enforced by                                  |\n|---|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| 1 | Top module `core` exposes an RVFI port set selected by `cores/<target>/core.yaml`'s `nret` field (default 2). **nret=1**: only `io_rvfi_*_0` ports exist; the single retirement always lands on channel 0. **nret=2**: both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` exist; channel 0 carries the older of two simultaneous retirements, channel 1 the younger; single-retire cycles MUST place the retirement on channel 0 with `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`; all channel-1 ports MUST be driven (no X/Z, tie unused fields to `'0`). | cocotb smoke test, `riscv-formal` wrapper (`wrapper.sv` for nret=2, `wrapper_si.sv` for nret=1) |\n| 2 | `rvfi_trap = 1` iff the retiring instruction is illegal per RV32IM (decoder default = illegal)             | `riscv-formal ill` check                     |\n| 3 | EBREAK is the *only* SYSTEM (opcode `0x73`) instruction the core treats as valid; ECALL / CSR / MRET trap  | dedicated decoder unit tests                 |\n| 4 | `rvfi_order` strictly monotonic +1 per retirement across both channels combined (no gaps, no duplicates). When both channels retire in the same cycle, channel-0 order = N and channel-1 order = N+1.                                       | `riscv-formal unique` check                  |\n| 5 | CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic instruction stream                                           | `riscv-formal liveness` check                |\n| 5b | M-extension arithmetic produces RV32M-correct bit results                                                | cocotb `test_alu.py` + `make formal-deep`    |\n| 6 | All memory accesses bounded to `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` plus UART/markers `[0x10000000, 0x10000200)`     | sim emits `oob:true`, eval treats as failure |\n| 7 | CoreMark CRCs match canonical 6 KB perf run: `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`       | `validate_coremark_uart` in fpga eval        |\n| 8 | CoreMark timing brackets `start_time` / `stop_time` markers — total elapsed cycles do NOT count            | MMIO writes at `0x10000100` / `0x10000104`   |\n| 9 | \"Correct operation validated.\" literal must appear in CoreMark UART output                                 | `validate_coremark_uart`                     |\n|10 | Decoder defaults `isIllegal = 1`; a bit is only cleared inside a validated opcode/funct match              | decoder unit tests with reserved encodings   |\n\n## Don't-touch list (the orchestrator never modifies these)\n\nThese directories are part of the eval contract and are *never* modified by\nhypothesis-implementation agents. The hypothesis is \"what RTL changes would\nimprove fitness\", not \"what eval relaxations would let this RTL pass\".\n\n- `tools/` — orchestrator, worktree manager, eval gates.\n- `schemas/` — hypothesis and eval-result JSON schemas.\n- `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg`, `formal/run_all.sh` — they define the\n  correctness contract (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `formal/riscv-formal/` — vendored upstream submodule.\n- `bench/programs/` — selftest, crt0, link.ld, CoreMark sources, portme.\n- `fpga/core_bench.sv`, `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, `fpga/scripts/*`,\n  `fpga/constraints/*` — they define the FPGA fitness contract\n  (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `test/cosim/main.cpp`, `test/cosim/reference.py`, `test/cosim/run_cosim.py`\n  — Verilator harness + Python ISS = the cosim contract.\n- This file (`CLAUDE.md`), `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `README.md`, `setup.sh`,\n  `Makefile`.\n\n## What hypotheses MAY change\n\nEverything under `cores/<TARGET>/rtl/` and the cocotb unit tests under\n`cores/<TARGET>/test/test_*.py`. The agent's `implementation_notes.md`\nlives at `cores/<TARGET>/implementation_notes.md` (gitignored). The\norchestrator may also update `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml` (current:\nsection only).\n\nThe only top-level invariant on `rtl/core.sv` is that it exposes a port\nnamed `core` whose IO matches the RVFI wrapper's expectations: the\nimem/dmem port set (`io_imemAddr`, `io_imemData`, `io_imemReady`,\n`io_dmemAddr`, `io_dmemRData`, `io_dmemWData`, `io_dmemWEn`,\n`io_dmemREn`, `io_dmemReady`), `clock`, `reset`, and the RVFI port\nset determined by `core.yaml`'s `nret` field — only `io_rvfi_*_0`\nfields for `nret: 1`; both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` for\n`nret: 2` (default).\n\nA hypothesis may:\n- Merge stages into a single file or split a module across many.\n- Rename or delete files inside `rtl/`.\n- Add new modules (branch predictors, caches, reservation stations…).\n- Change pipeline depth (add or remove stages).\n- Restructure the pipeline topology end to end.\n- Rewrite any `rtl/` module from scratch.\n\nIt must not:\n- Change `core`'s top-level IO shape relative to the `nret` value declared in `core.yaml` — specifically: the imem/dmem port set, and the RVFI port set (only `_0` fields for `nret: 1`; both `_0` and `_1` for `nret: 2` with channel-0-older convention and the single-retire-on-channel-0 rule).\n- Change `core.yaml`'s `nret` value mid-hypothesis. nret picks the formal/FPGA wrappers used by eval; flipping it is a contract-level change, not a per-hypothesis decision.\n- Weaken any invariant in the table above.\n- Modify any path in the don't-touch list.\n- Edit any other core's directory (cores/<other>/...). Each loop is\n  scoped to a single TARGET.\n\n## Working notes\n\nThis file is the contract. `docs/bootstrap-prompt.md` is the long-form\nplan that produced it.\n\n### RVFI contract: nret=1 vs nret=2\n\nCores declare their retirement width in `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml`\nunder the top-level `nret` field. Two values are supported; the\ndefault is 2 for backward compat with cores authored before this\nfield existed.\n\n- `nret: 1` — single-issue. Top module exposes only `io_rvfi_*_0`\n  ports (no `_1`). Orchestrator wires `formal/wrapper_si.sv` +\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg` for formal and `fpga/core_bench_si.sv` for\n  FPGA Fmax. No `_1` tie-offs to write; no vacuous-pass machinery\n  involved. This is the recommended shape for any in-order or\n  single-retire pipeline.\n- `nret: 2` — dual-issue (or single-issue cores that opt into the\n  wider contract). Top module exposes both `io_rvfi_*_0` and\n  `io_rvfi_*_1` with the channel-0-older convention.\n  `formal/wrapper.sv` + `formal/checks.cfg` and `fpga/core_bench.sv`\n  apply. Single-issue cores declared as `nret: 2` must still tie off\n  channel 1 (`io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0` plus all other `_1` fields driven\n  to `'0`) — about 21 `assign io_rvfi_*_1 = '0;` lines.\n\nTriple-issue or wider would be a future contract bump (NRET=K), not a\nper-hypothesis decision.\n\nContract-side files keyed off `nret`:\n- nret=1: `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks_si.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`.\n- nret=2: `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench.sv`.\n- shared: `formal/run_all.sh`, `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl`,\n  `test/cosim/main.cpp` (per-channel drain that gracefully no-ops\n  on unused channels).\n\nVacuous-pass on channel 1 (`nret: 2` only): `riscv-formal`'s\nper-channel BMC checks assume `rvfi_valid_<ch>=1` to test their\nproperty. When a single-issue hypothesis declared as `nret: 2`\nhardwires `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`, that assumption becomes\nunsatisfiable and SBY exits `Status: PREUNSAT` (rc=16) — technically\na vacuous pass over the empty set of ch1-valid traces.\n`formal/run_all.sh` counts `*_ch1` PREUNSAT as pass, so such\nhypotheses pass formal naturally and true dual-issue hypotheses\n(where ch1 is sometimes valid) get the regular `DONE (PASS` outcome\non the same checks. Caveat: a hypothesis that *claims* dual-issue\nbut whose channel 1 silently never retires would also vacuous-pass\nthese checks; the FPGA-fitness IPC and the cover statement (which\nfires on either channel retiring) catch the fully-stuck case.\nCores declared `nret: 1` sidestep this machinery entirely —\nchecks_si.cfg generates no `_ch1` tasks at all.\n\n## Caveat: `make formal` runs ALTOPS-mode by default\n\n`formal/checks.cfg` defines `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, which substitutes\nthe M-extension `MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU` operations\nwith simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas (riscv-formal docs §7.6). The\nDUT's `alu.sv` mirrors the same substitution under the same `\\`ifdef`.\n\nThis means the per-iteration formal gate proves **M-ext bypassing,\noperand routing, stall/forwarding behavior, and trap discipline** — but\nit does NOT prove that the actual hardware multiplier and divider\nproduce RV32M-correct bit results. M-ext arithmetic correctness is\ncovered separately by:\n\n  1. `test/test_alu.py` (30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results)\n  2. cosim against `test/cosim/reference.py` (each retired MUL/DIV/REM\n     diff'd field-by-field with the Python ISS)\n  3. `make formal-deep` (formal without ALTOPS — slow but the only path\n     that proves real arithmetic via SMT). Run periodically, not in\n     the orchestrator loop.\n\nA hypothesis that touches the multiplier/divider must keep both the\nALTOPS branch and the real branch in `alu.sv` consistent. The cocotb\nsuite is the fast guard against arithmetic regressions.\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py\ncores/bench/test/conftest.py\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py\ncores/bench/test/test_ex_result_banks.py\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py\ncores/bench/test/_helpers.py\ncores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncores/bench/experiments/progress.png\ncores/bench/experiments/.gitkeep\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Both tagged predictors reduced CoreMark cycles by about 6.1%, but their timing cost limited fitness. I’m choosing static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction with direct JAL prediction. It removes the table and training logic; the main risk is the new instruction-to-PC target-adder path."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,225p' fpga/core_bench_si.sv\nsed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\nsed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,225p' fpga/core_bench_si.sv\nsed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\nsed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\"","aggregated_output":"// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the full rationale on LFSR-driven imem, dmem\n// model, and the XOR-reduce LED that keeps RVFI fan-out alive through\n// dead-output elimination.\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI fan-out (NRET=1 contract).\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  core cpu (\n    .clock            (clock),\n    .reset            (reset),\n    .io_imemAddr      (imem_addr),\n    .io_imemData      (lfsr),\n    .io_imemReady     (1'b1),\n    .io_dmemAddr      (dmem_addr),\n    .io_dmemWData     (dmem_wdata),\n    .io_dmemRData     (dmem_rdata),\n    .io_dmemWEn       (dmem_wen),\n    .io_dmemREn       (dmem_ren),\n    .io_dmemReady     (1'b1),\n    .io_rvfi_valid_0    (rvfi_valid_0),\n    .io_rvfi_order_0    (rvfi_order_0),\n    .io_rvfi_insn_0     (rvfi_insn_0),\n    .io_rvfi_trap_0     (rvfi_trap_0),\n    .io_rvfi_halt_0     (rvfi_halt_0),\n    .io_rvfi_intr_0     (rvfi_intr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mode_0     (rvfi_mode_0),\n    .io_rvfi_ixl_0      (rvfi_ixl_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0 (rvfi_rs1_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0(rvfi_rs1_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0 (rvfi_rs2_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0(rvfi_rs2_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_addr_0  (rvfi_rd_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0 (rvfi_rd_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0 (rvfi_pc_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0 (rvfi_pc_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_addr_0 (rvfi_mem_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0(rvfi_mem_rmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0(rvfi_mem_wmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0(rvfi_mem_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0(rvfi_mem_wdata_0)\n  );\n\n  // XOR-reduce all CPU outputs to a single LED bit. Without this,\n  // dead-output elimination would prune the RVFI fan-out.\n  assign led = ^{rvfi_valid_0, rvfi_order_0, rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0,\n                 rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0, rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0,\n                 rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0, rvfi_rd_wdata_0,\n                 rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0, rvfi_mem_addr_0,\n                 rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0,\n                 imem_addr, dmem_rdata};\n\nendmodule\n\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, and the trap discipline (illegal /\nECALL trap, EBREAK does NOT trap).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.clock import Clock\nfrom cocotb.triggers import RisingEdge, Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\nfrom test_ex_result_banks import _ex_snapshot, _unpack, ID_EX\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0110011)\ndef LW  (rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b010, rd, 0b0000011)\ndef SW  (rs2, rs1, imm): return _s(imm & 0xFFF, rs2, rs1, 0b010, 0b0100011)\ndef BEQ (rs1, rs2, imm): return _b(imm, rs2, rs1, 0b000, 0b1100011)\ndef JAL (rd, imm):       return _j(imm, rd, 0b1101111)\ndef JALR(rd, rs1, imm):  return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b1100111)\ndef NOP():               return ADDI(0, 0, 0)        # = 0x00000013\ndef EBREAK():            return 0x00100073\ndef ECALL():             return 0x00000073\ndef DIV (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 4, rd, 0x33)\ndef DIVU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 5, rd, 0x33)\ndef REM (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 6, rd, 0x33)\ndef REMU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 7, rd, 0x33)\ndef MUL (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 0, rd, 0x33)\ndef LUI (rd, value): return (value & 0xFFFFF000) | ((rd & 31) << 7) | 0x37\n\n\n# ── Harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nasync def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200):\n    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n\n    imem is read combinationally each cycle from imemAddr; dmem similarly.\n    Stores get applied to the Python-side dmem dict so subsequent loads\n    see the new value.\n\n    Returns (retirements, dmem) where retirements is a list of dicts\n    sampled on every cycle that rvfi_valid=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    imem = {i * 4: instr & 0xFFFFFFFF for i, instr in enumerate(program)}\n    dmem = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    retirements: list[dict] = []\n\n    cocotb.start_soon(Clock(dut.clock, 10, \"ns\").start())\n\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n    dut.io_dmemRData.value = 0\n    # Zero-wait bus model: ready always asserted. The stall-mode tests\n    # live in vex_main.cpp's --istall/--dstall flags, not in cocotb.\n    dut.io_imemReady.value = 1\n    dut.io_dmemReady.value = 1\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n\n    # Deassert reset and prime the imem read for the first PC=0 fetch.\n    dut.reset.value = 0\n    dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(0, EBREAK())\n\n    for cycle in range(max_cycles):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n        await Timer(1, \"ns\")  # let combinational signals settle\n\n        # Sample the post-edge state.\n        ia        = int(dut.io_imemAddr.value)\n        da        = int(dut.io_dmemAddr.value)\n        wen       = int(dut.io_dmemWEn.value)\n        rvfi_v    = int(dut.io_rvfi_valid_0.value)\n\n        # Apply dmem write side effects.\n        if wen:\n            wdata = int(dut.io_dmemWData.value)\n            wa = da & ~3\n            old = dmem.get(wa, 0)\n            new = old\n            for i in range(4):\n                if (wen >> i) & 1:\n                    bv = (wdata >> (i * 8)) & 0xFF\n                    new = (new & ~(0xFF << (i * 8))) | (bv << (i * 8))\n            dmem[wa] = new\n\n        # Capture RVFI retirement.\n        if rvfi_v:\n            r = {\n                \"order\":     int(dut.io_rvfi_order_0.value),\n                \"insn\":      int(dut.io_rvfi_insn_0.value),\n                \"pc\":        int(dut.io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0.value),\n                \"pc_next\":   int(dut.io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0.value),\n                \"rd\":        int(dut.io_rvfi_rd_addr_0.value),\n                \"rd_wdata\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0.value),\n                \"rs1_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0.value),\n                \"rs1_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0.value),\n                \"rs2_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0.value),\n                \"rs2_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0.value),\n                \"trap\":      int(dut.io_rvfi_trap_0.value),\n                \"mem_addr\":  int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_addr_0.value),\n                \"mem_wmask\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0.value),\n                \"mem_wdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0.value),\n                \"mem_rmask\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0.value),\n                \"mem_rdata\": int(dut.io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0.value),\n                \"cycle\":     cycle,\n            }\n            retirements.append(r)\n            if r[\"insn\"] == EBREAK():\n                break\n\n        # Drive next cycle's imem fetch and dmem read combinationally.\n        dut.io_imemData.value  = imem.get(ia & ~3, EBREAK())\n        dut.io_dmemRData.value = dmem.get(da & ~3, 0)\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(f\"max_cycles={max_cycles} reached without EBREAK\")\n\n    return retirements, dmem\n\n\ndef _by_insn(retirements, insn):\n    \"\"\"Return all retirements matching a specific instruction word.\"\"\"\n    return [r for r in retirements if r[\"insn\"] == insn]\n\n\nasync def _protocol_tick(dut):\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 1\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n\n\ndef _rvfi_snapshot(dut):\n    fields = (\"order insn trap halt intr mode ixl rs1_addr rs1_rdata \"\n              \"rs2_addr rs2_rdata rd_addr rd_wdata pc_rdata pc_wdata \"\n              \"mem_addr mem_rmask mem_wmask mem_rdata mem_wdata\").split()\n    return {f: int(getattr(dut, f\"io_rvfi_{f}_0\").value) for f in fields}\n\n\nasync def _run_protocol(dut, program, dmem_init=None, *, stalls=False, seed=1,\n                        hold_first_store=0, reset_busy_edge=None, max_cycles=20000):\n    \"\"\"Edge-accurate buses and byte-exact comparison with the unchanged ISS.\n\n    Count accepted bus requests separately from RVFI, so replaying an older\n    store/load during a divide is detected even if its retirement is hidden.\n    Observe launch/dispatch to catch wrong-path launches and finish bubbles.\n    \"\"\"\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3] / \"test/cosim\"))\n    from reference import RV32IM\n    from run_cosim import compare\n\n    program = list(program)\n    initial = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    dmem = dict(initial)\n// rtl/if_stage.sv\n//\n// Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n// (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n// flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n// captures everything one cycle later.\n//\n// On flush or redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n// (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n// observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n// a spurious load-use stall the cycle after a taken branch.\n//\n// Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n//                 EX-stage redirect).\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n  input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle\n  input  logic              redirect,         // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  input  logic [31:0]       redirect_target,\n  output logic [31:0]       imem_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0]       imem_data,\n  output if_id_t  out\n);\n\n  localparam logic [31:0] RESET_PC = 32'h0000_0000;\n\n  logic [31:0] pc;\n  logic [31:0] next_pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    next_pc = redirect ? redirect_target : pc + 32'd4;\n  end\n\n  // Redirect must override stall: a BRANCH/JAL/JALR in EX may fire\n  // redirect on the same cycle as imem_stall or dmem_stall — without\n  // this priority the redirect target would be silently dropped, the\n  // PC would hold its old (wrong-path) value, and execution would\n  // resume on the wrong path once the bus unstalls. Verified by the\n  // VexRiscv-binary CoreMark sweep with --istall enabled.\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if      (reset)    pc <= RESET_PC;\n    else if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\n    else if (!stall)   pc <= next_pc;\n  end\n\n  assign imem_addr = pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    out.pc    = pc;\n    out.instr = (flush || redirect) ? 32'h0000_0013 : imem_data;\n    out.valid = !(flush || redirect);\n  end\n\nendmodule\n// rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n//\n// Detects the only data hazard the textbook 5-stage doesn't cover via\n// forwarding: load-use. A LOAD in EX produces its data only after MEM,\n// so an instruction immediately behind that consumes the LOAD's rd\n// must be stalled by exactly one cycle.\n//\n// Outputs:\n//   stall_if / stall_id : freeze the PC reg and the IF/ID combinational\n//                          payload (cleared by ID's flush input).\n//   flush_if / flush_id : on EX redirect, kill the two in-flight\n//                          instructions ahead of the redirect target.\n//   flush_id            : also kills ID's own register on load-use to\n//                          inject a single-cycle bubble between LOAD\n//                          and the dependent instruction.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    n/a (governs validity of subsequent retirements).\nmodule hazard_unit (\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs1,         // IF/ID instr[19:15]  (next rs1)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs2,         // IF/ID instr[24:20]  (next rs2)\n  input  logic       redirect,          // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  // Bus backpressure (default-1 in zero-wait testbenches; VexRiscv-style\n  // random ~22% stall in vex_main.cpp). When low, the corresponding\n  // memory request is NOT serviced this cycle.\n  input  logic       imem_ready,\n  input  logic       dmem_ready,\n  // EX/MEM has a memory op in flight (the LOAD/STORE the dmem stall\n  // would actually be holding up). Computed at top level from the\n  // EX/MEM register's ctrl.mem_read | ctrl.mem_write.\n  input  logic       ex_mem_mem_op,\n  input  logic       div_wait,          // current DIV/REM has not dispatched\n  output logic       stall_if,          // PC reg holds\n  output logic       stall_id,          // ID/EX register holds (vs. bubble)\n  output logic       flush_if,          // IF/ID comb output -> NOP\n  output logic       flush_id,          // ID/EX register captures '0\n  output logic       stall_ex_mem,      // EX/MEM register holds\n  output logic       hold_mem_wb        // MEM/WB clears valid only;\n                                        // data fields stay (for fwd)\n);\n\n  logic load_use_hazard;\n  logic imem_stall;\n  logic dmem_stall;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\n                   && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\n    imem_stall = !imem_ready;\n    // dmem stall only matters if there's actually a memory op in EX/MEM\n    // — otherwise bus-not-ready is irrelevant to the pipeline.\n    dmem_stall = !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op;\n\n    // PC reg holds on any stall reason.\n    stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall || div_wait;\n    // IF/ID combinational payload: NOP whenever we wouldn't have a valid\n    // instruction this cycle (redirect target unknown to IF, or imem\n    // didn't deliver).\n    flush_if      = redirect || imem_stall;\n    // ID/EX register:\n    //   - dmem_stall  -> hold        (preserve in-flight pipeline state)\n    //   - div_wait    -> hold        (keep the divider's instruction)\n    //   - load_use    -> bubble      (1-cycle stall between LOAD + use)\n    //   - redirect    -> bubble      (kill wrong-path)\n    //   - otherwise   -> capture\n    // dmem_stall takes precedence over load_use's bubble: re-evaluate\n    // load_use next cycle when the bus unblocks. flush_id is 1 only when\n    // we want bubble (not hold).\n    stall_id      = dmem_stall || load_use_hazard || div_wait;\n    flush_id      = (load_use_hazard || redirect) && !dmem_stall && !div_wait;\n    // EX/MEM register: holds on dmem_stall (LOAD waits in MEM until the\n    // bus delivers).\n    stall_ex_mem  = dmem_stall;\n    // MEM/WB register: on dmem_stall the previously-retired instruction's\n    // data fields stay alive for forwarding (e.g. a held BNE needs the\n    // LOAD's load_data via fwd_mem_wb), but valid is cleared so we don't\n    // double-retire / double-write the regfile.\n    hold_mem_wb   = dmem_stall;\n  end\n\nendmodule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n        print(json.dumps({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','outcome','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz','lut4','seeds']},indent=2))\n        print(r.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis',''))\n        print('TIMING EVIDENCE')\n        for p in r.get('implementation_notes','').split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n            if any(k in p.lower() for k in ['critical path','timing','fanout']): print(p)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n        print(json.dumps({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','outcome','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz','lut4','seeds']},indent=2))\n        print(r.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis',''))\n        print('TIMING EVIDENCE')\n        for p in r.get('implementation_notes','').split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n            if any(k in p.lower() for k in ['critical path','timing','fanout']): print(p)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 291.2,\n  \"cycles\": 4215937,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77,\n  \"lut4\": 10377,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    129.95,\n    121.11,\n    122.77\n  ]\n}\nAdd one 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer with a two-bit\nsaturating direction counter per entry. Index it with fetch PC[5:2], use\nthe full remaining PC[31:6] as the tag, and store aligned target[31:2].\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\npredict PC+4. Conditional branches use the counter for direction, while\nresolved JAL/JALR entries are set strongly taken and learn their latest\ntarget. Clear valid bits on reset; payloads need not be reset and must\nnever influence a miss. Use the full architectural PC and target widths.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF/ID into ID/EX. EX computes the actual next PC using the existing\nforwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush younger\nwork only when a valid instruction advances out of EX and its actual\nnext PC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every valid\ninstruction, including a non-control instruction with a stale table hit.\nThe repair target is the actual next PC, including PC+4 for a predicted\ntaken branch that resolves not taken, and the existing PC+4 continuation\nfor a misaligned target trap. Preserve JAL/JALR link data, trap handling,\nand the resolved next PC reported through RVFI.\n\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never while EX is held by dmem\nbackpressure. For a matching conditional entry, saturate toward its\nresolved direction and refresh its aligned branch target. Allocate a\nmissed conditional entry only when taken, initializing it weakly taken\n(2'b10). Allocate or refresh legal, aligned JAL/JALR targets with 2'b11.\nInvalidate a matching entry when the resolved instruction is no longer\na legal control transfer or its control-transfer target is misaligned.\nNever allocate or reinforce predictions from bubbles or trap outcomes.\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training to use the pre-edge table\ncontents for the prediction that is captured; a later table update must\nnot change recorded metadata.\n\nApply a predicted PC advance only when that fetch is actually accepted\ninto ID/EX. On load-use hazards hold the fetch PC and bubble ID/EX;\non dmem holds freeze both PC and ID/EX, including its saved prediction.\nInject the existing invalid bubble on imem unavailability when ID/EX\nis otherwise able to advance.\nWhile dmem holds EX, defer both repair and training until EX can advance;\nretain the existing forwarding state during the hold. An eligible EX\nrepair must override imem backpressure and any younger prediction.\nKeep raw lookup independent of flush/stall signals to avoid combinational\nloops. Correct predictions must not assert a redirect or flush.\n\nThis single predictor is expected to improve net fitness by about 8%,\nsubject to measurement. The small table limits added lookup depth and\nrouting, but conflicts, changing indirect targets, and additional LUT/FF\ncost may reduce the gain or lower Fmax. Validate with unchanged formal,\ncosimulation, CRC, UART, marker-window, and three-seed FPGA evaluation\ngates. Preserve the nret=1 interface and every evaluation invariant.\n\nTIMING EVIDENCE\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r2s2\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 345.07,\n  \"cycles\": 4215937,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 145.48,\n  \"lut4\": 6287,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    143.84,\n    145.48,\n    152.72\n  ]\n}\nAdd one 16-entry, direct-mapped, full-width tagged bimodal BTB to the\ncurrent cores/bench RTL, with training computed exclusively from\nregistered fetch metadata and the instruction's resolved outcome.\nThis is one predictor experiment: keep the existing divider, ALU,\nforwarding, memory system, pipeline boundaries, and nret=1 interface.\n\nIndex the BTB by PC[5:2], store the full tag PC[31:6], aligned target\nbits [31:2], a two-bit saturating counter, and a valid bit. Lookup is\ncombinational and depends only on the fetch PC. A valid full-tag hit\nwith counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise predict the\nfull-width PC+4. Reset clears valid bits; unreset payloads must never\naffect a miss. Do not introduce an instruction-data target adder or\nreduce architectural address widths.\n\nAlong with the exact predicted_next_pc, capture lookup_hit and the\nlookup_counter in ID/EX on the same edge that accepts the instruction.\nA hit includes entries whose direction counter predicts not taken.\nSupply a defined counter value on a miss. Hold all metadata with a\nstalled instruction and clear it with a bubble or flush. The predictor\narrays must have no resolving-PC read port: training must not use\ntag_q[train_index], counter_q[train_index], or valid_q[train_index]\nas an input to its next-state/write-enable logic.\n\nTrain once when a valid instruction actually advances from EX into\nEX/MEM: in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. For a legal aligned\nconditional branch with saved lookup_hit, saturate the saved counter\ntoward the resolved direction and write a complete entry using this\ninstruction's actual PC tag and branch target. On a saved miss,\nallocate only a taken aligned branch, initialized weakly taken (10).\nLegal aligned JAL/JALR always write the resolved target and counter\n11. If an instruction with saved lookup_hit is no longer a legal\ncontrol transfer, or has a misaligned control-transfer target, clear\nthe addressed valid bit. Invalid instructions and bubbles never\nallocate. Compute every payload write and enable without rereading\nresident predictor state at EX; use local index write decoding.\n\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training using the pre-edge table for\nboth the consumed next PC and its captured metadata. Deliberately\naccept a stale counter/hit snapshot when an older resolving instruction\nupdates the same index at that edge; do not add a training-to-fetch\nbypass that reconstructs the long feedback path. Such collisions may\nlose a counter update or evict a newer entry, affecting prediction\naccuracy only. Every allocation/update writes its own complete actual\ntag and target, so metadata from different instructions cannot be\ncombined into a fabricated entry. Full next-PC checking remains the\ncorrectness mechanism. This bounded approximation must be exercised\nby consecutive same-index branches and single-instruction loops.\n\nEX computes one actual_next_pc with the existing forwarded operands,\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and alignment/trap continuation rules. For\nevery advancing valid instruction, compare it with that instruction's\nsaved predicted_next_pc, including ordinary instructions with stale\nBTB hits and completed DIV/REM operations. Redirect only on a mismatch;\nrepair to actual_next_pc and squash younger work. Correct predictions\nintroduce no flush. RVFI pc_wdata always reports the resolved next PC;\nJAL/JALR link writes and trap suppression remain unchanged.\n\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when its instruction is accepted.\nPreserve load-use bubbles, invalid imem bubbles, dmem holds and retained\nWB forwarding data, and all divider request/response behavior. Defer\nrepairs and training while EX cannot advance. An eligible repair\noverrides instruction-bus backpressure and the younger prediction.\nKeep raw predictor lookup independent of stall/flush/redirect signals\nand avoid combinational cycles in hazard control.\n\nThe prediction benefit is an estimate, not a new measurement. Retaining\nabout 6% fewer cycles while losing at most about 1% Fmax would improve\nfitness by roughly 5%. Counter-snapshot collisions, the remaining\nlookup/repair paths, and FPGA mapping could reduce that gain. Compare\nagainst the current 372.62 winner using the unchanged marker-bracketed\nCoreMark and three-seed median-Fmax gates, with all existing formal,\ncosim, CRC, UART, OOB, and resource checks intact. Inspect routed timing\nto confirm that predictor training no longer contains a tag-RAM read\nfollowed by tag comparison and table write enable in the same cycle.\nKeep the official fixture and any supplemental 6 KB results distinct;\ndo not modify either the evaluator or benchmark sources.\n\nTIMING EVIDENCE\nAll routed critical paths were inspected. Seed 1 has a 6.95 ns ID/EX\ncontrol-to-EX/MEM path (3.30 ns logic, 3.65 ns routing). Seed 2 has a 6.87 ns\npath from ID/EX PC through target-alignment/legal-control qualification to\na predictor target write enable (2.77 ns logic, 4.10 ns routing). Seed 3\nhas a 6.55 ns path from ID/EX branch control through resolved training\nqualification to a predictor target write enable (2.57 ns logic, 3.98 ns\nrouting). None contains a tag-table read followed by tag comparison and\ntable write enable. Training qualification and enable fanout remain timing\nlimitations after removing the original feedback path; the mapped-cone\naudit above confirms there is no hidden resident-state training dependency.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
