{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'predictor':\n        continue\n    print('\\\\nID',d['id'],d.get('title'))\n    print({k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds']})\n    h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n    if d['id'] in ['hyp-20260906-003-r2s2','hyp-20260906-003-r3s2','hyp-20260906-003-r5s2','hyp-20260906-003-r7s2','hyp-20260906-003-r8s2']:\n        print('PROPOSAL',h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n    notes=d.get('implementation_notes')\n    if notes:\n        print('NOTES',notes)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'predictor':\n        continue\n    print('\\\\nID',d['id'],d.get('title'))\n    print({k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds']})\n    h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n    if d['id'] in ['hyp-20260906-003-r2s2','hyp-20260906-003-r3s2','hyp-20260906-003-r5s2','hyp-20260906-003-r7s2','hyp-20260906-003-r8s2']:\n        print('PROPOSAL',h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n    notes=d.get('implementation_notes')\n    if notes:\n        print('NOTES',notes)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nID hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 291.2, 'fmax_mhz': 122.77, 'cycles': 4215937, 'lut4': 10377, 'ff': 2069, 'seeds': [129.95, 121.11, 122.77]}\nNOTES # Small tagged bimodal predictor\n\nImplemented the requested 16-entry direct-mapped predictor in `cores/bench`.\nThe index is PC[5:2], the tag is PC[31:6], and the stored aligned target is\ntarget[31:2]. A valid tag hit with counter[1] predicts the target; all misses\nuse full-width PC+4. Reset clears only valid bits.\n\nConditional hits refresh the target and saturate the direction counter.\nTaken conditional misses allocate weakly taken; not-taken misses leave the\nresident entry untouched. Legal aligned JAL/JALR updates are strongly taken\nand learn their latest target. Matching stale, illegal, or misaligned entries\nare invalidated, including misaligned untaken branch targets. Bubbles and\nheld EX instructions cannot train.\n\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC consumed at the accepting\nedge. EX compares every advancing valid instruction against one actual next\nPC, also used for RVFI. A mismatch repairs fetch and discards the younger\ncombinational IF/ID slot. Correct predictions have no repair bubble. JAL/JALR\nlink writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, misalignment traps, and PC+4 trap\ncontinuation are preserved. Repairs wait through dmem holds and override\nimem backpressure when EX advances.\n\nLoad-use stalls hold PC and inject an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\n\n## Deviations and scope\n\n- Added standalone predictor tests as well as pipeline regressions. The\n  pipeline harness now models pre-edge bus acceptance and checks RVFI order,\n  PC continuity, prediction capture, holds, repairs, and store handshakes.\n- Split hazard equations into independent continuous assignments to avoid\n  false combinational scheduling cycles in the instrumented Verilator build.\n- Used `core_pkg.sv` first when linting; compilation-unit typedefs require\n  this ordering. No lint suppressions were added for combinational loops.\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This notes file is the requested\n  exception to the RTL/test editing surface.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\n\nThe full formal log is under\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-formal/formal-final-console.log`; FPGA seed\nlogs and the combined result are under\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-fpga/`. Cocotb XML results, selftest comparison\nresults, and the supplemental 6 KB build/run are under the corresponding\n`-cocotb`, `-selftest-results.json`, and `-6k` scratch paths.\n\n## Concerns\n\nThe prompt specifies 6 KB CoreMark, but the checked-in Makefile defaults and\n`tools/eval/fpga.py` CRC gate use the 2 KB fixture. Both workloads were tested\nseparately without changing the evaluator. The 2 KB measurement must not be\nreported as a 6 KB fitness result. The measured 10,377 LUT4 exceeds\n`core.yaml`'s 10,000-LUT target, although all placements succeeded. FPGA\ncost and reduced Fmax limit the net gain. Direct-map conflicts and changing\nindirect targets remain expected sources of prediction misses. These are\nlocal results; orchestrator acceptance and its independent rerun remain\noutside this implementation step.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 345.07, 'fmax_mhz': 145.48, 'cycles': 4215937, 'lut4': 6287, 'ff': 3097, 'seeds': [143.84, 145.48, 152.72]}\nPROPOSAL Add one 16-entry, direct-mapped, full-width tagged bimodal BTB to the\ncurrent cores/bench RTL, with training computed exclusively from\nregistered fetch metadata and the instruction's resolved outcome.\nThis is one predictor experiment: keep the existing divider, ALU,\nforwarding, memory system, pipeline boundaries, and nret=1 interface.\n\nIndex the BTB by PC[5:2], store the full tag PC[31:6], aligned target\nbits [31:2], a two-bit saturating counter, and a valid bit. Lookup is\ncombinational and depends only on the fetch PC. A valid full-tag hit\nwith counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise predict the\nfull-width PC+4. Reset clears valid bits; unreset payloads must never\naffect a miss. Do not introduce an instruction-data target adder or\nreduce architectural address widths.\n\nAlong with the exact predicted_next_pc, capture lookup_hit and the\nlookup_counter in ID/EX on the same edge that accepts the instruction.\nA hit includes entries whose direction counter predicts not taken.\nSupply a defined counter value on a miss. Hold all metadata with a\nstalled instruction and clear it with a bubble or flush. The predictor\narrays must have no resolving-PC read port: training must not use\ntag_q[train_index], counter_q[train_index], or valid_q[train_index]\nas an input to its next-state/write-enable logic.\n\nTrain once when a valid instruction actually advances from EX into\nEX/MEM: in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. For a legal aligned\nconditional branch with saved lookup_hit, saturate the saved counter\ntoward the resolved direction and write a complete entry using this\ninstruction's actual PC tag and branch target. On a saved miss,\nallocate only a taken aligned branch, initialized weakly taken (10).\nLegal aligned JAL/JALR always write the resolved target and counter\n11. If an instruction with saved lookup_hit is no longer a legal\ncontrol transfer, or has a misaligned control-transfer target, clear\nthe addressed valid bit. Invalid instructions and bubbles never\nallocate. Compute every payload write and enable without rereading\nresident predictor state at EX; use local index write decoding.\n\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training using the pre-edge table for\nboth the consumed next PC and its captured metadata. Deliberately\naccept a stale counter/hit snapshot when an older resolving instruction\nupdates the same index at that edge; do not add a training-to-fetch\nbypass that reconstructs the long feedback path. Such collisions may\nlose a counter update or evict a newer entry, affecting prediction\naccuracy only. Every allocation/update writes its own complete actual\ntag and target, so metadata from different instructions cannot be\ncombined into a fabricated entry. Full next-PC checking remains the\ncorrectness mechanism. This bounded approximation must be exercised\nby consecutive same-index branches and single-instruction loops.\n\nEX computes one actual_next_pc with the existing forwarded operands,\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and alignment/trap continuation rules. For\nevery advancing valid instruction, compare it with that instruction's\nsaved predicted_next_pc, including ordinary instructions with stale\nBTB hits and completed DIV/REM operations. Redirect only on a mismatch;\nrepair to actual_next_pc and squash younger work. Correct predictions\nintroduce no flush. RVFI pc_wdata always reports the resolved next PC;\nJAL/JALR link writes and trap suppression remain unchanged.\n\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when its instruction is accepted.\nPreserve load-use bubbles, invalid imem bubbles, dmem holds and retained\nWB forwarding data, and all divider request/response behavior. Defer\nrepairs and training while EX cannot advance. An eligible repair\noverrides instruction-bus backpressure and the younger prediction.\nKeep raw predictor lookup independent of stall/flush/redirect signals\nand avoid combinational cycles in hazard control.\n\nThe prediction benefit is an estimate, not a new measurement. Retaining\nabout 6% fewer cycles while losing at most about 1% Fmax would improve\nfitness by roughly 5%. Counter-snapshot collisions, the remaining\nlookup/repair paths, and FPGA mapping could reduce that gain. Compare\nagainst the current 372.62 winner using the unchanged marker-bracketed\nCoreMark and three-seed median-Fmax gates, with all existing formal,\ncosim, CRC, UART, OOB, and resource checks intact. Inspect routed timing\nto confirm that predictor training no longer contains a tag-RAM read\nfollowed by tag comparison and table write enable in the same cycle.\nKeep the official fixture and any supplemental 6 KB results distinct;\ndo not modify either the evaluator or benchmark sources.\n\nNOTES Implemented the 16-entry full-width tagged bimodal BTB in cores/bench. Fetch\nuses PC[5:2], the full PC[31:6] tag, an aligned 30-bit target, validity and a\ntwo-bit direction counter. A miss exports counter 00 and full-width PC+4.\nOnly valid bits reset; payloads cannot influence misses.\n\nID/EX captures the exact predicted next PC, hit (including not-taken hits),\nand counter with its instruction. All fields hold during dmem/divider stalls\nand clear on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. EX computes one resolved\nnext PC, with forwarded operands, JALR bit-zero clearing and existing trap\ncontinuation. Every valid EX/MEM acceptance compares that PC with its saved\nprediction, including ordinary instructions and completed DIV/REM. Only a\nmismatch repairs fetch and squashes younger work. RVFI retains the resolved\nPC and the unchanged nret=1 channel-0 retirement/order interface.\n\nThe same in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait event drives training.\nTraining uses only the registered hit/counter and resolved instruction:\nsaved-hit aligned branches saturate the snapshot, saved misses allocate only\ntaken aligned branches at 10, and legal aligned JAL/JALR write 11. Stale hits\non non-control/illegal instructions or misaligned targets invalidate their\nindex. Every payload update writes its own complete actual tag and target.\nConstant per-entry write decoding introduces no resolving-PC read port.\nSimultaneous lookup/training deliberately consumes the pre-edge table with\nno bypass; stale snapshots can lose updates or evict intervening entries.\n\nHazard equations are separate continuous assignments so EX's dmem hold is\nindependent of its qualified repair. Eligible repairs override imem\nbackpressure; dmem/divider holds defer both repair and training. Load-use\nbubbles, retained WB forwarding data, divider request/completion behavior,\nALU, decoder, memory stages, pipeline boundaries and all top-level ports\nremain unchanged.\n\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Local write decoding maps\nthe table payload into enabled flops rather than relying on a RAM primitive.\nThe new tests add an instruction-version-aware sparse fetch harness for\nstale-code scenarios; they use the existing Python ISS and RVFI comparator\nwithout changing either contract file. Existing tests/assertions remain.\n\nLocal formal status: PASS on the first run, 53 passed and 0 failed; no formal\nfix attempts were needed. The unchanged bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected\nwrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl.\nIt ran from an isolated copy under TMPDIR because the worktree's upstream\nformal directory is a shared symlink. No checks, depths or wrappers changed.\nFull non-ALTOPS formal-deep was not run; divider arithmetic is unchanged and\nboth real and ALTOPS arithmetic tests passed.\n\nThe exact requested glob-order Verilator lint command passes without\nwarnings, as does the ALTOPS variant. All seven pytest runners pass, covering\n129 cocotb cases: real ALU 30, ALTOPS ALU 30, decoder 30, immediate generator\n8, register file 5, predictor 3 and pipeline 23. Coverage includes all tag\nbits/indices/target bits, all saved counters, saved misses, coherent stale\nreplacement, pre-edge collisions, inactive training, reset, warm loops and\nexits, both direction errors, changing forwarded JALR targets, illegal and\nmisaligned stale hits, consecutive same-index branches, single-instruction\nloops, random imem/dmem stalls, 12-clock holds, retained WB data, deferred\nDIV/REM repairs, and killed stores/divides. Pipeline monitors check every\nmetadata capture/hold/bubble, accepted fetch PC, full-PC repair, EX training\nevent, retirement continuity/order and absence of correct-prediction bubbles.\n\nA mapped-netlist combinational-cone audit checked every D and CE input of\nall 944 predictor storage flops. None has a resident valid/tag/counter/target\nbit as a combinational input. This confirms the training feedback path was\nremoved in the mapped hardware, beyond the source-level read-port check.\n\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench gate, with NRET=1 and\nBENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv, completed all three routes. Final Fmax values\nwere 143.84, 145.48 and 152.72 MHz; median 145.48 MHz. Resource usage was\n6,287 LUT4 and 3,097 FF, within the 10,000-LUT target. The unchanged cosim\ngate also passed both selftest and CoreMark, including the official CRC,\nUART, EBREAK, OOB and marker checks. CoreMark used 4,215,937 bracketed cycles\nfor 10 iterations with the standard random instruction/data stalls.\n\nOfficial local fitness on the current divider-based core is 345.07\niterations/s versus the current winner's 372.62: a 7.394% regression.\nTimed cycles decreased 6.135%, but median Fmax decreased 13.074%. LUT4 usage\nincreased 15.612%. The cycle benefit survived; the estimated <=1% Fmax loss\ndid not. This implementation therefore does not beat the current winner.\n\nAll routed critical paths were inspected. Seed 1 has a 6.95 ns ID/EX\ncontrol-to-EX/MEM path (3.30 ns logic, 3.65 ns routing). Seed 2 has a 6.87 ns\npath from ID/EX PC through target-alignment/legal-control qualification to\na predictor target write enable (2.77 ns logic, 4.10 ns routing). Seed 3\nhas a 6.55 ns path from ID/EX branch control through resolved training\nqualification to a predictor target write enable (2.57 ns logic, 3.98 ns\nrouting). None contains a tag-table read followed by tag comparison and\ntable write enable. Training qualification and enable fanout remain timing\nlimitations after removing the original feedback path; the mapped-cone\naudit above confirms there is no hidden resident-state training dependency.\n\nThe official checked-in fixture/evaluator use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3 and\nITERATIONS=10. They were copied unchanged for evaluation. The recorded\ncurrent winner is 372.62 iterations/s, 167.36 MHz, 4,491,485 bracketed cycles,\n5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 FF. These are the comparison values from the existing\nexperiment log, not new measurements of the baseline.\n\nSeparately, an unmodified scratch copy built with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 and -O3\ncompleted 10 iterations in 30,823,120 marker-bracketed cycles, versus the\nrecorded winner's supplemental 32,836,671 cycles (6.132% fewer). It reported\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949,\nthe literal Correct operation validated., both markers, EBREAK and no OOB\naccess. At the candidate's 145.48 MHz median this is 47.20 iterations/s.\nThis is supplemental 6 KB evidence, separate from official fitness.\nThe UART compiler-flags string is hardcoded; the build command used -O3.\n\nEvidence directories, all under the authorized TMPDIR:\n\n- predictor-formal-mcapvt3h: run.log and formal/last_run-*.log, all per-check logs.\n- predictor-tests-qlavcwvr: seven runner XML files and Verilator builds.\n- predictor-fpga-667ih_e0: gate-*.log, training-cone-check.json, synth.json and\n  cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log plus routed netlists.\n- predictor-supplemental-6k-gvvrkbrq: build.log, sim.log and result.json.\n\nTMPDIR is /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp.\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this explicitly\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree. The orchestrator's\nacceptance remains independent of these local results.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r3s2 Stateless backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 361.83, 'fmax_mhz': 154.13, 'cycles': 4259699, 'lut4': 5548, 'ff': 2092, 'seeds': [157.95, 153.59, 154.13]}\nPROPOSAL Add one static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor with direct JAL\nprediction to cores/bench. Use the current combinational fetch/decode\nboundary; do not add a pipeline stage. Predict legal conditional branches\nwith negative B-immediates as taken, and predict legal direct JALs as taken\nin either direction. Predict all other instructions sequentially, including\nJALR. This is a single stateless direct-control predictor: no BTB, history\ntable, direction counters, return stack, or training interface.\n\nIn if_stage, predecode the raw imem_data independently of flushed output,\nstall, and redirect. Recognize BRANCH opcode 0x63 only for funct3 values\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, and 7; its immediate sign is instruction bit 31. Recognize\nJAL opcode 0x6f. Require an aligned fetch PC and aligned displacement before\npredicting taken. B-immediate bit 1 is instruction bit 8; J-immediate bit 1\nis instruction bit 21; bit 0 is zero for both. Check these low bits directly\nrather than putting a wide target-adder result on the prediction-enable\npath. Reserved branch encodings and all JALRs predict PC+4. This predecode\nonly chooses speculation; the existing decoder remains authoritative for\ninstruction legality, including the default-illegal and SYSTEM rules.\n\nConstruct the sign-extended B/J displacement from instruction wires and\nselect it before one PC-relative target adder. Compute PC+4 independently.\nPreserve full 32-bit address arithmetic and modulo-2^32 carry behavior;\ndo not truncate addresses to the benchmark's memory size. On an accepted\nfetch, choose the aligned direct target when predicted taken, otherwise\nPC+4. Keep imem_addr driven directly by the PC register. A prediction must\nnot flush the instruction that generated it.\n\nAdd only a predicted_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Capture it on the\nsame edge as the instruction whose prediction actually advances the PC.\nHold the bit with ID/EX through dmem and divider stalls; clear it with\nreset, flushes, and invalid advancing bubbles. No predicted target or\n32-bit predicted_next_pc register is needed: every predicted target is\nthe exact PC-relative target of that same captured instruction, so there\nare no target aliases or stale table entries to repair. Preserve that\ncorrespondence even when instruction data changes while a fetch is held.\n\nEX retains its forwarded branch comparison, full target calculation,\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and existing misalignment/trap continuation.\nDefine resolved_taken as an aligned, nontrapping taken branch or jump.\nOn in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait, redirect only when\npredicted_taken differs from resolved_taken. This one-bit comparison\nreplaces the unconditional taken-transfer redirect without adding a\nfull-next-PC equality cone. Correctly predicted branches and direct JALs\ndo not flush. A backward-branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken\nforward branch repairs to its target. An aligned JALR remains unpredicted\nand uses its existing EX redirect, even when its target happens to equal\nPC+4. A misaligned transfer retains its existing trap and PC+4 continuation.\nUse the architectural resolved next PC for RVFI and repairs; preserve\nJAL/JALR link writes and their suppression on a target-alignment trap.\n\nReset has highest PC priority, then an eligible EX repair, then an accepted\nfetch prediction, otherwise hold. Repairs are deferred while EX is held\nand override imem backpressure when EX advances. Preserve load-use bubbles,\nimem invalidation, dmem holds and retained WB forwarding data, and divider\nrequest/completion behavior. Keep the dmem-hold equations independent of\nredirect, using separate assignments if necessary to avoid combinational\nscheduling loops when redirect is qualified by EX advancement. A repair\nmust squash the younger slot before it can retire, store, or launch DIV/REM.\n\nExpected net fitness improvement is about 4%, not a measured result. A\nnominal 5% cycle reduction with a 1% Fmax reduction gives about 4.2% higher\nthroughput. Static direction errors may reduce that cycle benefit, and\nthe new instruction-to-PC target-adder path may become critical. Inspect\nall three routed timing reports and compare marker-window cycles times\nmedian Fmax against the current winner; cycle savings alone are not success.\nRun the unchanged formal, cosim, canonical CRC, UART, OOB, marker-window,\nand three-seed FPGA gates. Keep any supplemental workload results distinct\nfrom official fitness. Preserve nret=1, every invariant, the existing\ndivider/result banks and direct registered memory-address path, and all\nevaluation files. Only the predictor integration and its tests are in scope.\n\nNOTES Implemented stateless backward-taken/forward-not-taken branch prediction and\ndirect-JAL prediction. Local correctness gates pass, but measured throughput\nregresses: 361.83 iter/s versus the current winner's 375.78 iter/s (-3.71%).\nThe expected four-percent improvement was not achieved.\n\nChanges:\n\n- IF predecodes raw instruction data independently of flush, stall, and\n  redirect. Only legal BRANCH funct3 values with a negative displacement,\n  and JAL in either direction, can predict taken. Fetch-PC alignment and\n  instruction bits 8/21 qualify alignment before the target adder.\n- One selected, sign-extended B/J displacement feeds one full-width\n  PC-relative adder. PC+4 is independent. Both preserve modulo-2^32 address\n  arithmetic; imem_addr remains directly driven by the PC register.\n- Added only predicted_taken to IF/ID and ID/EX. ID captures it with the\n  accepted instruction, holds it through memory/divider stalls, and clears\n  it on reset, flush, and invalid advancing bubbles. No table, training\n  interface, saved target, or saved next-PC was added.\n- EX compares the saved bit with the aligned, nontrapping transfer outcome\n  only on in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. Repairs and RVFI share\n  the architectural resolved next PC. Correct predictions do not flush;\n  backward exits repair to PC+4. JALR remains unpredicted, including a\n  target equal to PC+4. Link writes and alignment-trap suppression remain.\n- Separated the dmem hold assignments from redirect-dependent hazard logic.\n  Reset takes PC priority, followed by eligible EX repair, accepted fetch,\n  and hold. An EX repair squashes the single younger combinational slot.\n- Updated the packed EX test layout and added pipeline and standalone IF\n  coverage. The decoder, divider, ALU/result banks, direct registered dmem\n  address path, core top-level ports, nret=1, and evaluation files are intact.\n\nValidation on the submitted RTL:\n\n- Requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings, both normal and ALTOPS modes.\n  git diff --check: PASS.\n- Local formal: PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed. No failed\n  checks, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged run_all.sh used\n  wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg. formal-deep was not run; divider and\n  multiplier RTL were not changed.\n- All nine pytest runners pass, totaling 135 cocotb cases. The initial full\n  suite passed, then the added IF suite and final pipeline reset coverage\n  passed. Every pre-existing assertion remains.\n- Directed tests cover all six branch conditions in both directions and\n  both outcomes, backward loops/exits, forward branches, JAL both directions,\n  self-JAL, forwarded/changing JALR, and JALR targeting PC+4. They check\n  reserved branches, illegal JALR/SYSTEM, target-alignment traps and link\n  suppression, B/J sign extension, full-address carry/wrap, and unaligned PCs.\n- Protocol checks compare each accepted prediction with the captured\n  instruction and actual PC advance. They verify ID/EX holds and bubbles,\n  fourteen-cycle memory holds with changing instruction data, retained WB\n  forwarding, load-dependent branches, divider waits/resets, repair despite\n  unavailable imem, and repair overriding a younger direct prediction.\n  Correct direct predictions have no added retirement bubble. RVFI order,\n  PC continuity, links, and all compared fields match the unchanged ISS;\n  accepted bus requests and divider launches exclude wrong-path operations.\n- Unchanged make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1: PASS for selftest and CoreMark.\n  CoreMark reached EBREAK, passed canonical UART/CRC checks, produced valid\n  start/stop markers, and reported no OOB access.\n\nFPGA measurement uses the unchanged single-issue wrapper, constraints,\nsynthesis script, three seeds, and marker-window fitness calculation. The\nfirst complete gate used Yosys 0.64+149 (2dc69a757-dirty) and\nnextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. A second complete gate used the winner's recorded Yosys 0.64\n(d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and the same nextpnr. Both synthesized\nidentical cell types, parameters, connectivity, and net-bit mappings. Both\ngates completed all three routes successfully and returned identical\nper-seed Fmax, resources, marker-window cycles, and fitness.\n\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Critical path | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\n| 1 | 157.95 | ID/EX register through EX selection to EX/MEM data | 3.69 / 2.64 |\n| 2 | 153.59 | ID/EX register through hazard control to ID/EX reset | 2.67 / 3.84 |\n| 3 | 154.13 | ID/EX register through forwarding/request control to divider operand CE | 1.67 / 4.82 |\n\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected. The endpoints\nare respectively cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_31.D,\ncpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_72.RESET, and cpu.u_ex.div_rs2_q_DFFRE_Q_6.CE.\nThe reports show approximately 6.33, 6.51, and 6.49 ns total delay. None of\nthese reported worst paths identifies the IF target-adder path as critical;\nthe headline reports do not establish complete timing coverage of every\nforwarding or memory path.\n\nMedian Fmax is 154.13 MHz versus 168.78 MHz (-8.68%). Resources are 5,548\nLUT4, 2,092 DFF, and four block RAMs, versus 5,470 LUT4, 2,111 DFF, and four\nblock RAMs for the winner. The cycle savings do not offset the Fmax loss.\nThe candidate needs\nmore than 160.07 MHz at its measured cycle count to exceed the winner.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator builds aggregate 2 KB CoreMark, despite the prompt's\naggregate 6 KB description. Its unchanged CRC contract is seedcrc=e9f5,\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The requested 6 KB\nworkload was built separately with the unchanged Makefile's\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 options. It is supplemental, not official\nfitness. Each comparison below uses a fresh simulation of the starting\nHEAD RTL and the identical ELF, ten iterations, and --istall --dstall.\n\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Candidate cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / candidate iter/s |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3 | 4,491,485 | 4,259,699 | 5.16% | 375.78 / 361.83 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3 | 32,836,671 | 31,086,275 | 5.33% | 51.40 / 49.58 |\n\nThe rates use the winner's recorded 168.78 MHz and the candidate's measured\n154.13 MHz. The 6 KB throughput also regresses, by 3.54%.\n\nMarker windows (start, stop): 2 KB starting (15,801, 4,507,286), candidate\n(15,189, 4,274,888); 6 KB starting (56,393, 32,893,064), candidate\n(54,040, 31,140,315). Both 6 KB UARTs contain seedcrc=0x8a02,\ncrclist=0xd4b0, crcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, crcfinal=0x5949, ten\niterations, and the literal \"Correct operation validated.\" Both complete\nwith EBREAK, valid markers, and no OOB access. The unchanged UART compiler\ndescription says -O2; actual build commands use -O3.\n\nAll generated artifacts and unchanged evaluation scratch copies are under\n$TMPDIR/stateless-r3s2. Evidence includes formal-console.log, unit-tests.log,\nprediction-tests.log, core test result XMLs, cosim-gate.log, fpga-gate.log,\nstable/fpga-gate.log, both sets of cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log,\ntiming-summary.json, workload-comparison.json, the individual CoreMark logs,\nand sixk/build.log. verified-source-sha256.json verifies 145 staged source\nfiles; the formal-stage RTL was also checked byte-for-byte against the\nsubmitted RTL. Only scoped bench RTL/tests and this requested notes file\nare modified in the worktree. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 345.59, 'fmax_mhz': 153.52, 'cycles': 4442217, 'lut4': 5512, 'ff': 2096, 'seeds': [164.07, 153.52, 151.88]}\nNOTES # Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\n\nImplemented the hypothesis. Final correctness gates pass, but measured fitness\nregresses: **345.59 iter/sec versus 375.78 (-8.03%)**. The 1.10% reduction in\ntimed cycles does not offset the 9.04% reduction in median Fmax. This is not a\nfitness improvement.\n\nOnly four RTL files changed: `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`, `id_stage.sv`, and\n`ex_stage.sv`. IF predecodes raw `imem_data` for opcode 0x6f, aligned PC, and\ninstruction bit 21 clear. The sign-extended J displacement feeds one full-width\nPC-relative adder; PC+4 remains independent. Both wrap modulo 2^32.\n`imem_addr` remains directly assigned from the PC register. Branches, JALR,\nand all other opcodes predict sequentially.\n\nPC updates prioritize reset, the existing EX redirect, and then an accepted\nfetch (`!stall && !flush`); otherwise PC holds. The target mux selects using\nraw JAL eligibility, while `early_jal` certifies actual acceptance and is zero\nduring reset, hold, flush, or redirect. The explicit flush condition also\nmakes an isolated IF flush hold PC. In the integrated winner, flush already\nimplies an EX redirect or imem stall, so this adds no new integrated behavior.\n\nID/EX registers `dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal` as `jump_needs_redirect`, using\nthe existing reset/flush and hold policy. Invalid fetches retain the existing\ndecoded-NOP payload policy; no invalid-fetch-wide reset was added. EX changes\nonly the jump term in its redirect predicate. Architectural jump controls,\nPC+4 link results, resolved RVFI targets, alignment traps, and JALR forwarding\nand bit-zero clearing remain intact. The divider, result banks, registered\ndmem address, forwarding, hazard unit, retirement logic, and nret=1 IO are\nunchanged. There is no predictor state, training, saved target, target/direction\ncomparison, fallback repair mux, or new EX-advance/div_wait qualification.\n\nTests add direct IF and ID coverage and extend the existing pipeline and EX\nresult-bank tests without removing their assertions. Coverage includes signed\nJAL displacements and extremes, full-address carry/wrap, every non-JAL opcode,\nalignment, changing instructions during holds, reset/redirect priority,\nconsecutive/self JALs, x0/x1/x5 links and forwarding, imem stalls, an 18-cycle\nolder memory hold, preceding divider waits, load-use bubbles, misaligned-JAL\ncontinuation, forwarded JALR, and older redirects killing younger JAL/store/divide.\nPipeline tests compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and\ncheck accepted memory transactions and divider launches separately.\n\nAn opt-in `test_coremark_profile.py` observes accepted early JALs directly,\nusing the unchanged ELF and the C++ harness's exact xorshift stall sequence.\nIt adds no RTL counters and does not replace any evaluator gate. The final\nprofile matches the unchanged C++ simulation's markers exactly:\n\n- Start: 15,665; stop: 4,457,882; timed cycles: **4,442,217**.\n- Accepted early JALs: **49,629 total; 49,367 inside the marker window**.\n- EBREAK reached; UART/CRC validation passed; no OOB access.\n\nFinal local validation:\n\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings.\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, 10 runners; the opt-in profile is skipped\n  by default and separately PASS when enabled. Includes real and ALTOPS\n  arithmetic/result-bank tests and all 25 pipeline cases.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, using the unchanged single-channel wrapper\n  and configuration. Both local formal invocations passed. No formal\n  counterexample or formal fix attempt occurred. All final generated checks\n  have DONE (PASS), and all 14 staged RTL files match the submitted RTL.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: PASS, selftest and CoreMark.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\n  completed all three seeds, CRC/UART, OOB, and marker-window gates.\n- `git diff --check`: PASS. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\n\nFinal routed results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61)\nand nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, the winner's recorded tools. The median includes\nall three final seeds:\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst reported path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 164.07 | Divider divisor_q to result_q data | 6.09 | 3.72 / 2.38 |\n| 2 | 153.52 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.51 | 2.39 / 4.12 |\n| 3 | 151.88 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.58 | 2.39 / 4.19 |\n\nMedian Fmax is **153.52 MHz**, versus 168.78 MHz. Final resources are 5,512\nLUT4 and 2,096 DFF, versus 5,470 LUT4 and 2,111 DFF. At the measured cycle\ncount the candidate needs more than **166.93 MHz** to exceed 375.78 iter/sec.\nThe reported worst paths do not identify the IF J-target adder as limiting;\nthey are not evidence that every IF, forwarding, or memory path has margin.\n\nOne local implementation refinement separated target selection from the\nacceptance-qualified metadata bit: the first version used `out.early_jal`\ninside the PC data mux and measured 78.73 MHz median, with long paths to\nID/EX reset. The final version uses raw predecode in the data mux and\nqualifies acceptance in the PC update enable. All gates were rerun on that\nfinal version; the first route's results are archived separately.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB** CoreMark despite the\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its unmodified CRC contract is\nseedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\nA separate **6 KB, -O3** ELF was built using the unchanged benchmark\nMakefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options. It is supplemental,\nnot a replacement for official fitness. Both comparisons below use fresh\nsimulation of starting HEAD and final RTL, identical ELFs, ten iterations,\nand `--istall --dstall`:\n\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / final iter/sec |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3 | 4,491,485 | 4,442,217 | 1.10% | 375.78 / 345.59 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3 | 32,836,671 | 32,485,445 | 1.07% | 51.40 / 47.26 |\n\nRates use the winner's recorded 168.78 MHz and the final measured 153.52 MHz.\nThe 6 KB rate regresses 8.06%. Its starting marker window is\n(56,393, 32,893,064); the final window is (55,880, 32,541,325). Both 6 KB\nUARTs have seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47,\ncrcfinal=5949, ten iterations, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`\nBoth runs reach EBREAK, have valid markers, and report no OOB access. The\nUART compiler-description string says -O2; the actual build command is -O3.\n\nScratch copies and generated evidence are under\n`$TMPDIR/early-jal-hove0D` (the run's configured TMPDIR, outside other cores).\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `profile-tests.log`,\n`coremark-profile.json`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`,\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`,\n`workload-validation.json`, the individual benchmark logs, and\n`sixk/build.log`. `verified-source-sha256.json` verifies 169 staged RTL,\ntest, and unchanged contract source files against the worktree. This\nrequested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\nOrchestrator acceptance remains separate from these local results.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r5s2 Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 338.91, 'fmax_mhz': 146.05, 'cycles': 4309463, 'lut4': 5602, 'ff': 2106, 'seeds': [153.21, 138.68, 146.05]}\nPROPOSAL Implement one static backward-taken/forward-not-taken conditional-branch\npredictor with decode-encoded repair polarity and control-only squashes.\nThe architectural claim is that roughly four percent fewer cycles can\nsurvive FPGA routing when prediction adds neither a second outcome XOR\nin the EX repair path nor repair-driven reset of the ID/EX data payload.\nThis is a refinement of the failed r3s2 control path, not a claim that\nits already-tested predictor policy alone fixes timing. Keep JAL and\nJALR sequentially predicted. Add no target table, direction table,\ntraining mechanism, return stack, or pipeline stage.\n\nIn IF, predecode raw imem_data for opcode 0x63 and legal funct3 values\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7. Predict taken only for instruction bit 31 set, aligned\nfetch PC, and instruction bit 8 clear, which ensures an aligned negative\nB displacement. Construct only the sign-extended B immediate and one\nfull-width PC-relative target adder; compute PC+4 independently. Select\nthe target with raw eligibility, never an acceptance-qualified signal.\nApply that selection only when the fetch advances into ID/EX. Preserve\nreset, existing EX redirect, accepted fetch advance, then hold priority.\nKeep imem_addr directly connected to the PC register and preserve\nmodulo-2^32 arithmetic. Carry raw predicted_taken alongside the fetch;\nit has meaning only for the same valid instruction accepted on that edge.\nA prediction must not flush the branch that produced it.\n\nIn ID, retain the architectural decoded branch_op and separately register\nrepair_invert = dec_branch_op[0] XOR in.predicted_taken. Also register\nbranch_repair_enable = dec_is_branch AND target_word_aligned, computing\nalignment from the two-bit sum of in.pc[1:0] and imm[1:0], independent of\nthe full target adder. Capture predicted_taken, repair_invert, and this\nenable with the instruction and hold them during pipeline stalls.\n\nEX shares the existing forwarded equality, signed-less-than, and\nunsigned-less-than comparisons. Select base_cond using branch_op[2:1]:\nequality for 00, signed less-than for 10, unsigned less-than for 11;\nreserved class 01 is disabled. For legal branches, architectural\nbranch_cond is base_cond XOR the original branch_op[0], whereas the\ndirection-mismatch condition is base_cond XOR registered repair_invert.\nThus predicted direction is folded into the polarity before ID/EX;\ndo not form architectural branch_cond XOR predicted_taken in the global\nrepair cone. Keep the original outcome for traps and resolved RVFI PC.\n\nThe branch contribution to redirect is branch_repair_enable AND the\ndirection-mismatch condition. Combine it with the existing legal,\naligned JAL/JALR redirect contribution and qualify with in.valid. A\npredicted backward-branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken\naligned branch repairs to its branch target. Select repair target using\nregistered predicted_taken and instruction class, independently of the\nlive comparison result. Correct predictions cause no redirect or bubble.\nThe prequalified branch enable suppresses redirect for an unpredicted\nmisaligned target, preserving the current taken-only trap and PC+4\ncontinuation. JAL/JALR links, JALR bit-zero clearing, jump alignment\nchecks, and actual pc_next remain architectural, never predicted values.\nPreserve the winner's existing redirect priority during imem and dmem\nstalls. A held EX branch may keep asserting the same repair until it\nadvances; add no div_wait or generic EX-accept dependency to this cone.\n\nMake narrow squash distribution an integral part of this predictor's\nrecovery mechanism. IF should emit raw instruction bits with validity\nindicating flush/unavailability, instead of placing a redirect-driven\nNOP mux across the instruction word. In ID/EX, reset still initializes\nthe complete register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete\nctrl_t bank, and the three predictor control bits. An invalid advancing\nfetch likewise produces zero controls and valid=0, regardless of its raw\nopcode. All PC, operand, immediate, register-address, and instruction\npayload flops use the ordinary !stall capture enable independently of\ndynamic flush; they may capture discarded data when a repair advances.\nDo not reintroduce repair as a wide payload clear, enable, or output mask.\nDuring holds retain the complete accepted instruction and its metadata;\npreserve the existing flush-versus-hold priority from hazard_unit.\nZero bubble controls are essential because hazards and forwarding inspect\nsome controls without a valid gate. Check that invalid ID/EX bubbles,\neven with retained load/store/jump/divide payloads, never act as producers,\nredirect, write, or issue requests.\nThe existing EX/MEM bubble behavior and retained MEM/WB forwarding data\nremain intact. This is localized predictor recovery, not an overhaul of\nthe downstream pipeline or ALU.\n\nThe estimated net gain is 3%, with low confidence. A four-percent cycle\nreduction and one-percent Fmax loss would give approximately 3.1% higher\nfitness. The current comparison is 382.48 iter/sec, 171.79 MHz, and\n4,491,485 marker-window cycles; at exactly four-percent fewer cycles the\ncandidate needs approximately 164.92 MHz just to break even. Static\ndirection errors, the remaining fetch adder, operand enables, and FPGA\nplacement could still erase the gain. Measure the actual branch-only\ncycle reduction and all three routed Fmax seeds; inspect whether the\nrepair net still reaches wide ID/EX payload reset, CE, or data muxes.\nTreat the unchanged evaluator as authoritative and keep any supplemental\n6 KB measurement distinct, as the prior logs document a fixture-size\ndiscrepancy. Run existing lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB,\nmarker-window, resource, and three-seed FPGA gates without modification.\nPreserve nret=1, all hard invariants, divider/result-bank ownership, and\nthe directly registered dmem address path. Scope is cores/bench only.\n\nNOTES # Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\n\nImplemented the hypothesis. All local correctness gates pass. The branch-only\npredictor reduces the unchanged evaluator's marker-window cycles by **4.05%**,\nbut median routed Fmax falls from 171.79 to **146.05 MHz**. Fitness is\n**338.91 iter/sec**, versus 382.48 for the starting winner (**-11.39%**).\nThe proposed timing-preservation claim did not survive routing.\n\nIF predecodes raw opcode 0x63 and funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7. Negative displacement,\ninstruction bit 8 clear, and aligned PC qualify static taken prediction.\nOne sign-extended B immediate and full-width target adder are independent of\nPC+4; the target mux uses raw eligibility. PC priority is reset, existing EX\nredirect, accepted fetch (`!stall && !flush`), then hold. All arithmetic wraps\nmodulo 2^32 and `imem_addr` remains directly connected to the PC register.\nJAL/JALR and all other opcodes predict sequentially. Raw `predicted_taken`\ntravels with raw instruction bits, with validity identifying discarded or\nunavailable fetches. Predictions do not flush their producing branch.\n\nID retains architectural `branch_op` and registers the prediction, its\ndecode-folded repair polarity, and legal-branch target alignment. Alignment\nuses only the two-bit sum of PC and immediate low bits. Reset clears the\ncomplete ID/EX register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete control\nbank, and all three predictor bits. An advancing invalid fetch also clears\nthose controls. Payload capture depends only on ordinary `!stall`, including\non repair edges; a load-use flush during a hold retains payload and clears\ncontrols. The existing hazard priority preserves the complete accepted\ninstruction during dmem/divider holds.\n\nEX shares forwarded equality/signed-less/unsigned-less comparisons and selects\ntheir base condition with architectural `branch_op[2:1]`. The architectural\noutcome uses original polarity; repair uses the registered polarity directly,\nwithout a second architectural-outcome XOR with prediction. Decode-qualified\nbranch alignment gates the repair contribution. Predicted branch exits repair\nto PC+4; unpredicted taken branches repair to the branch target. Recovery target\nselection is independent of the live comparison. Jump redirects, taken-only\nalignment traps, JAL/JALR link values, JALR bit-zero clearing, and resolved RVFI\nPC values remain architectural. No EX-accept or div_wait qualifier was added\nto redirect; held branches continue asserting their repair until advancement.\n\nThe functional changes are confined to `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`,\n`id_stage.sv`, and `ex_stage.sv`. `core.sv` and `hazard_unit.sv` have comment-only\nupdates to describe raw invalid payloads and control squashes. The divider,\nALU, result banks, downstream bubbles, retained MEM/WB forwarding data,\ndirectly registered memory-address path, nret=1 interface, decoder, and all\nevaluator contracts are unchanged. No predictor table, training, return stack,\nor additional pipeline stage was added.\n\nTests update the EX constructor's packed layout, defaulting the three added\nbits to zero. New IF tests cover all eight funct3 values, displacement bits\nand extremes, alignment, full-width carry/wrap, every non-branch opcode,\nchanging held/unavailable instruction words, raw metadata, reset, and redirect\npriority. New ID tests directly check the two-bit alignment sum, both repair\npolarities, full metadata holds, reset, and independent payload capture during\nflush and invalid advances. Pipeline tests preserve every original assertion\nand compare complete retirement streams against the unchanged Python ISS.\nThey cover all six branch conditions in both directions and outcomes, exact\nzero-bubble prediction and one-slot repair timing, loop exits, forwarding and\nload dependencies, branches held behind divides, 18-cycle memory holds,\nimem stalls with changing raw data, consecutive repairs, reset during repair,\nkilled loads/stores/divides/jumps/branches, reserved encodings, misalignment\ntraps, and unchanged jump links. Per-cycle assertions check zero bubble\ncontrols, metadata/payload capture, fetch priority, accepted memory requests,\ndivider launches, result-bank ownership, and RVFI PC/order continuity.\n\nLocal validation used scratch copies under the configured TMPDIR, avoiding\ngenerated writes to protected evaluator directories in the submitted tree.\nThe available Conda Python environment supplied cocotb, pytest, YAML, and\nclick; the default Python lacked cocotb/click. No evaluator or dependency\nsource was modified.\n\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS**, no warnings.\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: **PASS**, all 10 runners, including real and\n  ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank checks. The final expanded pipeline runner\n  was rerun after adding fetch-priority and additional hold coverage:\n  **26 pipeline cases passed**. Final XML totals are 145 cocotb cases,\n  zero failures across the 10 runners.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  **PASS: 53 passed, 0 failed**. The unchanged script auto-selected nret=1\n  wrappers/checks. One local formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts,\n  no counterexample. Every staged RTL source matches the final worktree.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and CoreMark.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\n  **PASS** for all three placements and the CRC/UART, OOB, marker-window\n  and resource evaluation; fitness regresses as reported above.\n- `git diff --check`: **PASS**. SHA-256 evidence verifies 328 staged source,\n  test, and contract files against the worktree. No other core or core.yaml\n  was edited. The explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root.\n\nRouted results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61).\nEvery reported frequency below is the final post-route value from the\nunchanged nextpnr seed script, with no excluded seed.\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 153.21 | ID/EX ctrl.is_branch RESET | 6.53 | 2.84 / 3.69 |\n| 2 | 138.68 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[2] RESET | 7.21 | 2.67 / 4.54 |\n| 3 | 146.05 | ID/EX valid RESET | 6.85 | 2.84 / 4.01 |\n\nThe worst paths start at the FPGA wrapper's LFSR instruction source and pass\nthrough the raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold control logic before the\nnarrow ID/EX reset bank. The mapped netlist has 160 physical ID/EX payload\nflops reset only by the top-level reset and 24 physical control/valid/predictor\nflops using the dynamic reset. A combinational fanout traversal from registered\n`repair_invert` and `branch_repair_enable` reaches those 24 RESET pins and the\nvalid flop's D input, **no payload RESET, CE, or D pin**. All 184 physical\nID/EX flops share the ordinary stall-derived CE, which is outside that repair\nfanout. Synthesis can merge duplicated bundle bits, hence physical counts are\nsmaller than the 201-bit logical bundle. This verifies narrow recovery\ndistribution, but does not establish timing margin for other paths.\n\nResources are **5,602 LUT4 and 2,106 DFF**, versus the winner's 5,513 LUT4 and\n2,087 DFF. Four DPX9B block RAMs and one MULT36X36 remain; the memory-address\nbank has not been replaced by the divider-result mux. At 4,309,463 cycles,\nbreak-even Fmax against the winner is **164.83 MHz**, well above 146.05 MHz.\n\nThe unchanged evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB, -O3** CoreMark despite the\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its CRC contract is seedcrc=e9f5,\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. This evaluator\nresult is authoritative. A separately built aggregate **6 KB, -O3** ELF\nuses the unchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options\nand is supplemental only. Both comparisons use fresh baseline-HEAD and\ncandidate simulations, identical ELFs, ten iterations, and `--istall --dstall`.\n\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Candidate cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / candidate iter/sec |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 4,491,485 | 4,309,463 | 4.05% | 382.48 / 338.91 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 32,836,671 | 31,436,060 | 4.27% | 52.32 / 46.46 |\n\nThe 2 KB start/stop markers are baseline (15,801, 4,507,286) and candidate\n(15,406, 4,324,869). The 6 KB markers are baseline (56,393, 32,893,064) and\ncandidate (54,555, 31,490,615). Both 6 KB UARTs report seedcrc=8a02,\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, ten iterations,\nand `Correct operation validated.` All four fresh runs reach EBREAK with\nvalid markers and no OOB access. UART's compiler-description string says\n-O2; the actual recorded build commands use -O3. Rates use the winner's\nrecorded 171.79 MHz and the candidate's measured median 146.05 MHz.\n\nEvidence is under:\n`$TMPDIR/branch-repair-r5s2-93q3k2v2/`.\nIt includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `pipeline-final.log`,\n`cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`,\n`repair-fanout.json`, `workload-validation.json`, individual baseline/candidate\nbenchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`.\nLocal correctness success is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r6s2 Tagged target prediction with MEM-stage validation and staged training\n{'outcome': 'broken', 'fitness': None, 'fmax_mhz': None, 'cycles': None, 'lut4': None, 'ff': None, 'seeds': None}\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r7s2 Single-entry backedge target prediction with decode validation\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 268.66, 'fmax_mhz': 115.85, 'cycles': 4312067, 'lut4': 5696, 'ff': 2201, 'seeds': [108.48, 116.96, 115.85]}\nPROPOSAL Add one fully tagged last-taken-backedge target entry. It contains valid,\nsource PC[31:2], target[31:2], and the exact B displacement bits [12:1]:\n73 logical state bits before constant-bit optimization. A lookup hit\nrequires valid, word-aligned fetch PC, and equality of all source-PC\nbits [31:2]. On a hit predict the stored full-width aligned target;\notherwise predict full-width PC+4. Use no direction counter, indexed\narray, return stack, or separate jump predictor. Only an accepted legal,\ntaken conditional branch with negative displacement, aligned source PC,\nand aligned target can replace the entry. Not-taken branches, forward\nbranches, and jumps leave it unchanged. It therefore retains a hot\ninner-loop backedge across intervening non-backedge control transfers.\nNested backedges can evict one another; cold starts and loop exits cost\nordinary EX repairs. No address-range truncation or workload PC constants\nare permitted. Addition remains modulo 2^32, including page crossings.\n\nLookup and target selection depend only on registered PC and entry\ncontents. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC register. Select the\nraw lookup result in the PC data mux, and apply it only on actual fetch\nacceptance into ID/EX; do not put acceptance, flush, or div_wait in the\nprediction-select data cone. Reset and existing EX repair retain priority\nover prediction. Hold fetch on ordinary load-use, bus, and divider stalls.\nA prediction never squashes the instruction that produced it.\n\nCarry prediction_used and the cached displacement snapshot with the\naccepting IF/ID instruction. Simultaneous lookup/update consumes the\npre-edge entry, with no update bypass. In ID, certify a predicted target\nonly if the CURRENT instruction decodes as a legal conditional branch\nand its complete B displacement equals the saved displacement. The\nsource-PC tag already matched the exact accepted PC, and training wrote\nsource, target, and displacement coherently, so this equality proves the\npredicted target equals this instruction's architectural branch target.\nIt needs only immediate-bit comparison, not another PC-relative adder.\nChanges in branch condition or source registers do not invalidate that\nproof; EX must evaluate the current condition using forwarded operands.\nRegister prediction_used, certified_backedge, and the repair polarity\nwith ID/EX. Keep the architectural branch_op unchanged. For a certified\nprediction, fold inversion of the outcome into the registered branch\nrepair polarity, as in the earlier decode-folded comparator design.\n\nKeep recovery in EX and specify all three cases explicitly:\n(1) No consumed prediction: retain the existing branch/jump redirect\nand target-alignment behavior.\n(2) Certified backedge: redirect only when its condition is false, to\nPC+4; a taken outcome has no flush or bubble.\n(3) Consumed but uncertified prediction: force repair to the current\ninstruction's actual architectural next PC, for EVERY instruction class.\nThat includes changed branch displacements, JAL/JALR, ordinary ALU,\nload/store, DIV/REM, illegal encodings, and alignment traps. Use the\nexisting PC+4 trap continuation and suppress trapped jump link writes.\nThis conservative stale-entry repair needs no full predicted-PC field\nor full-PC mismatch comparator in EX. A stale branch's actual outcome\nstill selects target versus fall-through correctly; stale jumps use\nthe current forwarded target. Keep this exceptional target selection\nseparate from the common certified-exit PC+4 and ordinary redirect paths.\n\nQualify repair with valid and reset, retaining the existing level-style\nEX repair behavior through holds. A held owner may repeatedly request\nthe same repair; it must retain its operands and prediction flags until\nadvancement. Do not add a generic EX-accept/div_wait gate to the common\nbranch repair cone. In particular, an uncertified prediction on DIV/REM\nmust not flush that owner or cancel its divide: existing divider-hold\npriority retains ID/EX until completion, then the repair clears the\nyounger slot on the accepting edge. Memory holds similarly defer owner\nadvancement. Repairs override unavailable imem. Flush only the younger\ncombinational fetch/decode slot, preserving the current recovery depth.\n\nStage predictor writes independently of recovery. Register a one-cycle\ntrain_pending pulse when the qualifying backward branch actually enters\nEX/MEM, using valid, legality, alignment, taken outcome, and !stall.\nClear that pulse on every other clock, including holds and bubbles.\nOn the following edge the predictor writes the coherent source, target,\nand displacement from the existing EX/MEM pc, branch_target, and instr\npayload. Those fields were captured on the same edge as train_pending;\nnonblocking update semantics preserve their association on the write\nedge. Reuse these payload fields instead of adding another wide training\nbank. Only registered train_pending enables entry writes, with no live\nEX comparator, resident-entry match, or memory-ready signal in that\nwrite-enable cone. Each accepted branch trains once even when subsequent\npipeline work stalls. Reset cancels pending training and clears entry\nvalidity; inactive uninitialized entry payload cannot affect a miss.\n\nPreserve the winner's independent pipeline payload capture and narrow\ncontrol/valid squashes. Hold accepted prediction metadata with its owner;\ninvalid entries have inert repair/training controls. Preserve the decoder,\narithmetic, divider completion bank, retained WB forwarding data, direct\nregistered dmem address, actual RVFI pc_next, and nret=1 external ports.\nThis experiment changes only backedge prediction and its integration.\n\nEstimate 2% higher fitness with low confidence. The explicit working\nassumption is 2.5% fewer marker-window cycles with at most 0.5% median\nFmax loss, yielding about 2.05% net gain. This is a forecast, not a\nmeasurement; the one-entry coverage may be too small. Against 184.06 MHz\nand 409.80 iter/sec, a 2.5% cycle reduction breaks even near 179.46 MHz.\nCount useful hits, loop-exit/stale repairs, and backedge replacements\ninside the existing timing markers. Run unchanged lint, unit, formal\nincluding liveness, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker, resource, and three-seed\nFPGA gates. Inspect all routed seeds for lookup-to-PC, repair-to-squash,\nand training-enable timing, including unchanged paths displaced by\nrouting. Keep evaluator fitness authoritative and any separately built\n6 KB measurement distinct, as prior notes document a fixture discrepancy.\nDo not change any evaluator, formal bound, other core, or hard invariant.\n\nNOTES Implemented the single-entry backedge predictor as specified. Correctness gates pass, but the measured timing loss rejects the forecasted fitness gain: the unchanged local evaluator reports 115.85 MHz median and 268.66 iter/sec, versus the supplied winner's 184.06 MHz and 409.80 iter/sec. These are approximately 37.06% lower Fmax and 34.44% lower fitness. No acceptance decision or evaluator record was changed.\n\nThe RTL changes are confined to cores/bench/rtl:\n\n- Added branch_predictor.sv with one valid bit, 30 source bits, 30 target bits, and the complete 12-bit B displacement. Synthesis retains all 73 entry flops. Only validity resets; reset cancels a pending payload write. Miss outputs are zero. Lookup requires exact source equality and word alignment, uses full addresses, and has no update bypass.\n- IF selects the raw lookup target versus full-width PC+4. Reset and existing EX repair have priority; normal advancement requires fetch acceptance. imem_addr still comes directly from the PC register. A prediction does not flush its own instruction.\n- IF/ID carries the lookup-use bit and displacement snapshot. ID compares every B displacement bit against a current legal branch decode, preserving architectural branch_op and source-register fields. ID/EX carries prediction_used, certified_backedge, and branch_op[0] XOR certified_backedge as registered repair polarity. Invalid/flush controls are inert, holds retain all owner metadata, and independent payload capture remains intact.\n- EX shares equality/signed-less-than/unsigned-less-than predicates between actual branch resolution and the decode-folded repair polarity. No prediction uses ordinary EX recovery; certified predictions repair only false outcomes to PC+4; stale predictions always repair to the current architectural next PC, including illegal encodings, alignment traps, memory operations, and DIV/REM. The exceptional actual-next-PC selection remains separate from the ordinary redirect and certified-exit selections. RVFI always carries the actual next PC.\n- EX repair is qualified by valid and reset, with no new EX-accept/div_wait gate. The unchanged hazard priority retains held memory/divider owners and clears only the younger combinational slot when the owner advances. Trapped jumps still suppress link writes.\n- A registered train_pending pulse is generated only for an accepted legal aligned taken negative-displacement conditional branch. It clears on every other clock, including holds. The following edge writes the existing EX/MEM pc, branch_target and instruction displacement. There is no additional training payload bank, resident-state feedback, or live EX/memory-ready write enable.\n\nNo architectural deviations were needed. The decoder, arithmetic/divider implementation, divider completion bank, retained WB forwarding, hazard priority, directly registered dmem address, and nret=1 external port set are unchanged. Test-only additions include dynamic instruction versions and optional marker-window counters; there are no hardware event counters, workload PC constants, address truncations, new prediction classes, or evaluator/formal-bound changes.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Requested verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv: PASS, no warnings.\n- Unchanged RVFI channel-0 pattern gate: PASS.\n- Unit suite: 10 pytest runners pass. Final XML aggregate: 153 cocotb cases pass; the optional CoreMark measurement is skipped in normal runs and was also run separately to completion. Both real and ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank tests pass. The final pipeline-only rerun after adding reset coverage passes.\n- Directed coverage includes all tag/target bits, all displacement comparison bits, page crossings and modulo-2^32 wraparound, pre-edge lookup/write collisions, valid-only reset and canceled pending writes, once-per-owner staged training across holds, nested eviction, and cold/warm/exit timing. Warm-loop branch-to-successor gaps are exactly [2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2] clocks.\n- Full RVFI comparison against the unchanged ISS passes for changed displacements, all six branch conditions and changed registers, forwarded loaded operands, ALU/multiply, legal and misaligned loads/stores/JAL/JALR, illegal instructions, and DIV/REM replacements. Independent accepted bus request and divider launch checks catch wrong-path effects and duplication. Long memory holds, unavailable imem, consecutive repairs, completion holds, and reset of a hot entry plus a stale dividing owner pass.\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first RTL attempt, 53 checks passed and 0 failed, including liveness, pc_fwd/pc_bwd, unique, ill, and cover. No counterexample and no formal fix/retry were needed. The exact unchanged run_all.sh/checks_si.cfg/wrapper_si.sv were run in an isolated scratch copy with RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6. Actual divider arithmetic remains covered by real unit tests and cosim; formal-deep was not run because divider arithmetic was unchanged.\n- Unchanged make cosim TARGET=bench: PASS, both selftest full-trace ISS comparison and CoreMark CRC validation.\n- Unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench with NRET=1 and BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv: all three seeds route successfully. An initial invocation selected Makefile's default two-channel synthesis wrapper and stopped before routing; rerunning with the existing declared single-channel wrapper resolved that setup mismatch without modifying the contract.\n\n| Seed | Routed Fmax (MHz) | Critical period (ns) | Logic / routing (ns) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 1 | 108.48 | 9.218 | 4.17 / 5.05 |\n| 2 | 116.96 | 8.550 | 3.59 / 4.96 |\n| 3 | 115.85 | 8.632 | 4.15 / 4.48 |\n\nThe authoritative local evaluator reports 4,312,067 marker-window cycles, 10 iterations, 268.66 iter/sec, 5,696 LUT4 and 2,201 FF. All resource capacities pass; BRAM usage remains 4 and MULT36X36 usage remains 1. UART validation, EBREAK completion, bracket markers and oob=false pass. Relative to the supplied rounded baseline numbers, the inferred cycle reduction is approximately 3.99%; this comparison is inferred from 184.06 MHz / 409.80 iter/sec, not a newly measured baseline run.\n\nAll three routed seeds were inspected, including the existing forwarding/JALR/alignment paths displaced by routing. Each worst path passes through forwarding and low JALR target alignment, then repair/hold/squash logic, ending at an ID control RESET pin. Seed 1 begins at ID state; seeds 2 and 3 begin at retained MEM/WB instruction/register-address bits. The predictor tag-to-PC path remains present through eight mapped combinational cells including muxes and a packing passthrough. This is a structural depth count, not an isolated path-period measurement. PC data endpoints were also inspected in each detailed timing report.\n\nThe predictor payload CE cone was checked in every routed netlist: all 72 payload flops share a single LUT2 driven only by registered train_pending and reset. Validity is enabled directly by train_pending. There is no live EX comparator, resident match, or memory-ready input to those enables. Detailed reports contain the training-pulse D and validity-CE endpoint arrivals; the full payload-enable connectivity was checked separately. No claim is made that this wide physical net is free of routing cost.\n\nDetailed reporting required replaying the same three seeds with only --report and --detailed-timing-report added. All three reproduced the evaluator's routed Fmax exactly to its reported precision. A preliminary attempt to reload an already routed JSON for report-only analysis crashed inside the installed nextpnr array2d assertion; it did not affect the completed gate or its artifacts.\n\nOptional unit-harness CoreMark counters used the unchanged ELF and the exact C++ harness xorshift stall sequence. The measured start/stop cycles were 15,666 and 4,327,733, reproducing the evaluator's 4,312,067-cycle window exactly:\n\n| Marker-window event | Count |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Useful certified taken hits | 192,341 |\n| Certified loop-exit repairs | 12,211 |\n| Stale repairs | 0 |\n| Ordinary repairs | 165,186 |\n| Staged entry writes | 235,191 |\n| Entry replacements (resident tuple changed) | 42,850 |\n\nThe live fixture discrepancy remains explicit: Makefile/evaluator defaults use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 and CRCs e714/1fd7/8e3a. The evaluator fitness above uses that unchanged fixture. A separately compiled TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 ELF passed d4b0/be52/5e47, the literal validation message, EBREAK, OOB and marker checks. Its window is 55,554 to 31,597,703, or 31,542,149 cycles; 36.73 iter/sec at this candidate's median Fmax is a separate derived 6 KB measurement, not evaluator fitness. The UART compiler-flags string still says -O2 in both binaries; the captured actual compile commands use -O3.\n\nRemaining concern: small storage and staged writes did not preserve timing. The roughly 4% cycle improvement cannot offset the measured 37% median Fmax loss. This implementation is functionally validated but is not a fitness improvement over the supplied winner.\n\nValidation artifacts are preserved under /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/r7s2-validation-_8mhz1xm. Relevant files are formal-gate.log, formal/last_run-15123.log, cosim-gate.log, fpga-gate.log, predictor-counts.json, predictor-measurement.log, sixkb-build.log, sixkb-coremark.log, timing-inspection.json, routed-cone-inspection.json, and cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/{nextpnr.log,timing.json,timing-full.log}. Unit logs are under $TMPDIR/r7s2-unit-tests-final.log and $TMPDIR/r7s2-pipeline-final.log. Scratch contract copies and generated outputs stay under $TMPDIR; no protected worktree files or other cores were changed.\n\n\nID hyp-20260906-003-r8s2 Backward-branch prediction with validity-only EX annul\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 206.45, 'fmax_mhz': 88.97, 'cycles': 4309463, 'lut4': 5578, 'ff': 2095, 'seeds': [90.53, 88.97, 87.28]}\nPROPOSAL Implement one static backward-taken conditional-branch predictor whose\nEX recovery annuls the younger slot solely through its registered valid\nbit. The new architectural claim is that validity-qualified consumers\ncan make the previously measured branch-cycle saving profitable by\nremoving repair from the entire ID/EX decode-control bank as well as\nits data payload. Retain the existing pipeline depth and one-slot EX\nrepair penalty. Use the branch-only policy from r5s2 to isolate this\nchange in predictor recovery; JAL/JALR remain sequentially predicted.\n\nIF predecodes raw imem_data for opcode 0x63 and legal funct3 values\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, and 7. Predict taken only for a negative B displacement\n(instruction bit 31), word-aligned fetch PC, and instruction bit 8 clear.\nConstruct the sign-extended B displacement and add it to the full PC;\npreserve all 32 address bits and modulo-2^32 carry behavior. Compute\nPC+4 independently. Select between these values using raw eligibility,\nindependent of stall, flush, redirect, and acceptance qualification.\nUpdate the PC only on the existing accepted fetch edge, with reset and\nEX repair taking priority. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC\nregister. There is no table, training, saved target, or additional stage.\n\nCarry predicted_taken with the exact raw instruction used to choose\nthe fetch next PC. IF always exposes raw instruction and prediction\nbits; flush and instruction unavailability affect validity only.\nRemove the redirect-driven NOP mux so repair cannot reach the decoded\ncontrol D inputs through instruction masking. In ID, keep architectural\nbranch_op unchanged and register repair_invert = branch_op[0] XOR\npredicted_taken. Register branch_repair_enable for a decoded legal\nbranch with an aligned target, using only the two-bit sum of PC and\nimmediate low bits. These three metadata bits use ordinary payload\ncapture and hold rules and are meaningful only when ID/EX is valid.\n\nMake ID/EX valid the sole dynamically squashed ID/EX field. Its existing\nreset, flush, and stall priority remains: an eligible flush clears valid,\nan ordinary capture takes fetch validity, and a memory/divider hold\nretains the current owner. The complete ctrl_t, prediction metadata,\nand existing instruction/data fields have synchronous reset and the\nordinary !stall capture enable only. Capture raw decoded controls on\nan advancing edge even when fetch is invalid or a repair discards it.\nA load-use flush clears valid while its ordinary stall retains payload.\nDo not zero or mask the control bank with flush or fetch validity, and\ndo not mask the ID/EX output bundle with valid. Invalid raw controls\nare intentionally unspecified as instructions but remain defined bits.\n\nQualify every action that can consume these raw ID/EX controls with\nthe registered owner validity. Specifically, pass ID/EX valid into\nhazard_unit and compute load_use_hazard from valid AND mem_read before\ncomparing rd with the raw fetched register addresses. Do not gate this\ncomparison with repair-dependent fetch validity or masked instructions;\nthe current valid branch/jump cannot simultaneously be an EX load.\nKeep EX redirect and divider request/wait/accept qualified by in.valid;\naudit that no invalid opcode can stall, redirect, or launch arithmetic.\nPreserve EX/MEM's existing control-zero bubble conversion whenever\n!in.valid or div_wait. Thus downstream forwarding producers and memory\nrequests still see zero controls for invalid EX/MEM entries. Preserve\nretained MEM/WB forwarding data during dmem holds, including valid=0\nretention after retirement; do not add a MEM/WB-valid forwarding gate.\n\nEX shares forwarded equality, signed-less-than, and unsigned-less-than\npredicates. Select base_cond with architectural branch_op[2:1]; use\noriginal branch_op[0] for architectural direction and the registered\nrepair_invert for direction mismatch. Branch repair is in.valid AND\nbranch_repair_enable AND (base_cond XOR repair_invert). Combine it with\nthe current valid, legal, aligned JAL/JALR redirect contribution.\nA predicted branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken branch\nrepairs to its branch target. Select the branch repair target from\nregistered predicted_taken, independently of the live comparison.\nCorrect predictions preserve the useful younger instruction without\na bubble. Keep taken-only alignment traps, JALR bit-zero clearing,\nlink-write suppression on traps, and actual resolved RVFI pc_next.\n\nPreserve the current EX redirect priority over imem backpressure and\nthe current hold priority for an owner blocked by dmem. A held branch\ncan keep asserting its repair until advancement; it must retain its\ncomplete instruction and prediction metadata. When it advances, only\nthe younger combinational fetch slot is annulled. Do not add div_wait\nor a generic EX-accept qualifier to the branch recovery cone. Recovery\nremains in EX and cannot let a wrong-path instruction reach a memory\nowner, retire, or launch DIV/REM. No extra recovery cycle is permitted.\n\nThis tests a different recovery boundary from r5s2's control-bank\nsquashes: the repair-to-ID cone should terminate only at the valid\nflop, with no path to decode-control, prediction-metadata, or payload\nRESET, CE, or D pins. PC repair remains required. Inspect the mapped\nfanout and all three routed seeds to verify this physical claim;\nsource-level separation alone is insufficient. Also inspect the new\nvalid-qualified hazard path and the instruction-to-PC target adder.\nThey may become critical even after the control-reset paths disappear.\n\nEstimate a 3% net fitness improvement with low confidence. Repeating\nthe prior 4.05% cycle reduction requires about 176.61 MHz to break even\nagainst the supplied 184.06 MHz, 409.80 iter/sec winner. A 1% Fmax loss\nwith that cycle reduction would yield about 3.18% greater fitness.\nThese are conditional estimates, not measured results. Measure actual\nmarker-window cycles and median routed Fmax; do not accept cycle savings\nalone. Run unchanged lint, unit, formal including liveness, ISS cosim,\nCRC/UART, OOB, marker, resource, and three-seed FPGA checks. Compare\nidentical workload builds and disclose the actual evaluator workload\nif it differs from the prompt; do not change the evaluation contract.\nPreserve nret=1, all hard invariants, arithmetic/result-bank ownership,\nand the directly registered dmem address. Scope is cores/bench only.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The prior predictors saved roughly 4–6% of CoreMark cycles, but their recovery logic reduced Fmax. I’m focusing on registering prediction recovery so the branch and JALR calculation no longer drives pipeline squash in the same cycle."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' cores/bench/test/conftest.py; sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport importlib.util\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor name in ['yaml','jsonschema']:\n print(name, bool(importlib.util.find_spec(name)))\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses')\nprint('hypotheses exists:',p.exists())\nif p.exists(): print([str(x) for x in p.iterdir()])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' cores/bench/test/conftest.py; sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport importlib.util\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor name in ['yaml','jsonschema']:\n print(name, bool(importlib.util.find_spec(name)))\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses')\nprint('hypotheses exists:',p.exists())\nif p.exists(): print([str(x) for x in p.iterdir()])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"pytest configuration for cocotb unit tests.\n\nAdds this directory to sys.path so test_*.py modules can `import _helpers`\nduring pytest collection. Cocotb sets PYTHONPATH separately for the\nsimulator-side import.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n_HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nif str(_HERE) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HERE))\n\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, and the trap discipline (illegal /\nECALL trap, EBREAK does NOT trap).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.clock import Clock\nfrom cocotb.triggers import RisingEdge, Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\nfrom test_ex_result_banks import _ex_snapshot, _unpack, ID_EX, CTRL\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0110011)\ndef LW  (rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b010, rd, 0b0000011)\ndef SW  (rs2, rs1, imm): return _s(imm & 0xFFF, rs2, rs1, 0b010, 0b0100011)\ndef BEQ (rs1, rs2, imm): return _b(imm, rs2, rs1, 0b000, 0b1100011)\ndef JAL (rd, imm):       return _j(imm, rd, 0b1101111)\ndef JALR(rd, rs1, imm):  return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b1100111)\ndef NOP():               return ADDI(0, 0, 0)        # = 0x00000013\ndef EBREAK():            return 0x00100073\ndef ECALL():             return 0x00000073\ndef DIV (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 4, rd, 0x33)\ndef DIVU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 5, rd, 0x33)\ndef REM (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 6, rd, 0x33)\ndef REMU(rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 7, rd, 0x33)\ndef MUL (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, 0, rd, 0x33)\ndef LUI (rd, value): return (value & 0xFFFFF000) | ((rd & 31) << 7) | 0x37\n\n\n# ── Harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nasync def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200):\n    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n\n    imem is read combinationally each cycle from imemAddr; dmem similarly.\n    Stores get applied to the Python-side dmem dict so subsequent loads\n    see the new value.\n\n    Returns (retirements, dmem) where retirements is a list of dicts\n    sampled on every cycle that rvfi_valid=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    imem = {i * 4: instr & 0xFFFFFFFF for i, instr in enumerate(program)}\n    dmem = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    retirements: list[dict] = []\n\n    cocotb.start_soon(Clock(dut.clock, 10, \"ns\").start())\n\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n    dut.io_dmemRData.value = 0\n    # Zero-wait bus model: ready always asserted. The stall-mode tests\n    # live in vex_main.cpp's --istall/--dstall flags, not in cocotb.\nyaml False\njsonschema False\nhypotheses exists: True\n['cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r3s0.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r6s0.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r1s0.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-002-r3s1.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r5s0.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-003-r8s2.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r8s0.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-002-r1s1.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r4s0.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-003-r2s2.last', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-002-r8s1.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-001-r7s0.log', 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260906-002-r5s1.last', 'cores/be