{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'micro_opt|hyp-20260906-001-r11s0|false|operand|source|hazard' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-e113d0a\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T06:48:58.053601Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Eliminate load-use bubbles caused by immediate bitfields\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline hazard unit compares a load destination with both raw instruction\\nfields [19:15] and [24:20], regardless of whether the following instruction\\nactually reads those registers. For example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by\\nADDI x6,x0,5 inserts a bubble because the immediate's low five bits look like\\nrs2=x5. Independent loads and instructions with no register operands can\\nsuffer the same false dependency. The bench history contains only the 282.82\\nbaseline and no prior attempt in this category; LESSONS.md is absent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline hazard unit compares a load destination with both raw instruction\\nfields [19:15] and [24:20], regardless of whether the following instruction\\nactually reads those registers. For example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by\\nADDI x6,x0,5 inserts a bubble because the immediate's low five bits look like\\nrs2=x5. Independent loads and instructions with no register operands can\\nsuffer the same false dependency. The bench history contains only the 282.82\\nbaseline and no prior attempt in this category; LESSONS.md is absent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small opcode-only source-use predecode to load-use detection. OP,\\nSTORE and BRANCH consume both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD and JALR consume rs1\\nonly; LUI, AUIPC, JAL, MISC-MEM and SYSTEM consume neither under this core's\\nexisting decoder contract. Keep conservative checks for unrecognized\\nopcodes. Stall only when a nonzero load destination matches a source that\\nthe following instruction consumes. In particular, all RV32M operations\\nstill consume both sources, and store-data dependencies still require\\nthe existing stall.\\n\\nThis removes one bubble per otherwise exposed false dependency without\\nchanging pipeline depth or adding a forwarding path. Use shallow opcode\\ncomparisons alongside the register comparisons, rather than routing the\\nfull validated decoder through the stall path. Preserve decoder legality,\\nreal load-use interlocks, redirect priority, dmem hold behavior, retained\\nMEM/WB forwarding data, and the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\\n\\nPredict a modest improvement in CoreMark iterations per cycle with nearly\\nunchanged Fmax. The estimated two-percent fitness gain is unmeasured;\\nfalse-hazard frequency and any extra delay on the stall path are the main\\nuncertainties. Validate with directed cycle-count and operand checks,\\nexisting formal and cosim gates, and the unchanged three-seed FPGA fitness\\nevaluation with canonical CoreMark CRCs and timing markers.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass if_id_w.instr[6:0] to a new opcode input on hazard_unit alongside\\nthe existing source fields. Preserve all top-level ports and existing\\npipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from the incoming opcode using the source\\nclasses in the hypothesis. Default unrecognized opcodes conservatively\\nto both sources used. Replace the unconditional two-field match with\\n(uses_rs1 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1) ||\\n(uses_rs2 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2), retaining id_ex_mem_read and the\\nnonzero-destination guard. Preserve every other stall, flush and hold\\nequation and its existing priority. Do not change instruction legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed zero-wait programs where a load destination aliases only\\nimmediate bits in ADDI, a shift-immediate, a following independent LOAD,\\nand LUI/AUIPC/JAL. Compare retirement spacing with equivalent nonaliasing\\ncases to prove removal of the spurious bubble, accounting for the\\nexisting JAL redirect behavior. Verify true rs1 and rs2 dependencies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert correct operands, memory effects, retirement PCs and consecutive\\nRVFI order values. Keep existing assertions and external cosim intact.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass if_id_w.instr[6:0] to a new opcode input on hazard_unit alongside\\nthe existing source fields. Preserve all top-level ports and existing\\npipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from the incoming opcode using the source\\nclasses in the hypothesis. Default unrecognized opcodes conservatively\\nto both sources used. Replace the unconditional two-field match with\\n(uses_rs1 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1) ||\\n(uses_rs2 && id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2), retaining id_ex_mem_read and the\\nnonzero-destination guard. Preserve every other stall, flush and hold\\nequation and its existing priority. Do not change instruction legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed zero-wait programs where a load destination aliases only\\nimmediate bits in ADDI, a shift-immediate, a following independent LOAD,\\nand LUI/AUIPC/JAL. Compare retirement spacing with equivalent nonaliasing\\ncases to prove removal of the spurious bubble, accounting for the\\nexisting JAL redirect behavior. Verify true rs1 and rs2 dependencies\\nstill insert the required bubble and produce correct results, including\\nstore data, branch operands and an RV32M consumer. Add deterministic\\nimem/dmem wait sequences around representative false and true matches;\\nassert correct operands, memory effects, retirement PCs and consecutive\\nRVFI order values. Keep existing assertions and external cosim intact.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.89, \"delta_pct\": 0.73, \"vs_baseline\": 0.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10386, \"ff\": 1877, \"seeds\": [123.75, 127.96, 132.24], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation notes\\n\\nImplemented opcode-only source-use predecode for load-use detection in the\\nbench core. OP (including all RV32M instructions), STORE, BRANCH, and unknown\\nopcodes check both sources; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR check rs1; LUI, AUIPC, JAL,\\nMISC-MEM, and SYSTEM check neither. The nonzero load-destination guard remains.\\nDecoder legality, pipeline depth, bus/redirect priorities, MEM/WB hold behavior,\\nand the nret=1 top-level port set are unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations required by validation\\n\\n- The first formal run passed 52 checks and failed `reg_ch0` at step 20.\\n  Its counterexample retired a SYSTEM instruction with raw rs1=x4 immediately\\n  after an LB to x4. Although SYSTEM consumes no registers, this core's RVFI\\n  and external cosim report and check both raw source values. Eliminating the\\n  bubble exposed the load address instead of its loaded value in RVFI.\\n- `mem_stage.sv` now repairs these retirement metadata fields from the preceding\\n  completed load's MEM/WB data. This path does not feed execution operands or\\n  store data. Retained WB data remains eligible through memory holds.\\n- EX/MEM forwarding excludes loads, whose result is unavailable at that stage.\\n  Real consumers retain their interlock and existing MEM/WB forwarding. This\\n  also preserves the old raw register value after a misaligned load that traps\\n  without writing its destination; such loads are excluded from metadata repair.\\n- A guarded `core_pkg.sv` include in `core.sv` fixes the baseline typedef-order\\n  errors in the exact requested glob-based lint command. Bus-only hazard\\n  equations were separated without changing their values, removing a Verilator\\n  public-signal build's artificial combinational-cycle warning.\\n\\n## Directed tests\\n\\nThe existing pipeline assertions remain. The harness now supports deterministic\\nimem/dmem waits, poisoned undelivered data, sparse jump targets, and stores\\ncommitted only on a ready clock edge. Every program checks consecutive RVFI\\norders, next-PC continuity, and both raw operand values against retired writes.\\n\\nAdded paired alias/nonalias timing checks for ADDI, all three immediate shifts,\\nindependent LOAD, JALR immediate bits, LUI/AUIPC/JAL source-field aliases,\\nFENCE/FENCE.I, illegal SYSTEM, EBREAK, and loads to x0. Independent consumers\\nretire one cycle after the load, with the existing redirect bubble after jumps.\\nTrue rs1/rs2 dependencies retain exactly one additional cycle, including store\\naddress/data, taken/untaken branches, JALR, and both inputs of all eight RV32M\\noperations. Unknown opcodes keep conservative stalls. Added misaligned-load and\\ncombined-wait regressions, including retained MEM/WB forwarding while a branch\\nredirect overlaps both bus stalls.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings.\\n- Formal: PASS, **53 passed / 0 failed**, after **one fix attempt** (two complete\\n  invocations). Used the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh`, `wrapper_si.sv`,\\n  `checks_si.cfg`, and upstream checks, with ALTOPS and the original depths.\\n- Cocotb: PASS, **85 tests** across all five existing runners, including\\n  18 pipeline tests and the actual RV32M arithmetic vectors.\\n- Negative control: the new ADDI and EBREAK timing tests both fail on the\\n  original bench RTL, reporting a two-cycle load/consumer gap instead of one.\\n- Unchanged `test/cosim/run_cosim.py` selftest: PASS, **115 retirements**.\\n  Repeating the same field-by-field comparison with both `--istall --dstall`\\n  also passes all 115 retirements.\\n- Fresh CoreMark build from unchanged sources: `COREMARK_SIZE=6000`,\\n  `COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, `ITERATIONS=10`, both bus-stall flags. PASS: no OOB access,\\n  EBREAK reached, `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, and\\n  the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Timing markers are 56,393 and\\n  32,893,064, giving **32,836,671 bracketed cycles** (not total elapsed cycles).\\n\\nValidation copies, builds, traces, and logs live under\\n`$TMPDIR/load-use-hyp-20260906-001-r1s0/`; protected workspace files were not\\nchanged. Final formal logs are in `formal/last_run-85124.log` and\\n`formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-85124/checks/` under that scratch directory.\\nThe initial counterexample is retained in `formal-attempt1/`.\\n\\n## Remaining concerns\\n\\nThe estimated fitness gain and nearly unchanged Fmax remain unmeasured; the\\norchestrator still needs its unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation. The extra\\nEX/MEM load qualification and RVFI metadata logic must be included in that\\nmeasurement. No multiplier/divider implementation changes were made, and\\nformal-deep was not run.\\n\\nThe prebuilt CoreMark ELF and read-only evaluator constants in this checkout\\nuse the 2 KB configuration. That existing ELF also completed correctly with\\nboth stall flags and its expected 2 KB CRCs. The separate fresh 6 KB run above\\nvalidates the configuration and CRCs requested in the hypothesis. Evaluation\\nconstants, benchmark sources, and the prebuilt ELF were left unchanged. The\\nUART compiler-flags text is hardcoded as `-O2`; the fresh build command used\\n`-O3` as recorded above.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:24:49.837661Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware used by the core\\nwith one shared, blocking iterative quotient/remainder engine. This is\\nthe sole architectural change: retain the current pipeline boundaries\\nand single-cycle RV32I and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU execution.\\n\\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient-bit steps per\\nclock for eight compute clocks. Normalize signed operands at request\\nacceptance, retain the original dividend and both sign flags, and apply\\nquotient/remainder sign correction when producing the held response.\\nSize the trial remainder/subtract path to 33 bits and never use / or %\\nin the synthesized engine. Handle divisor zero explicitly, returning\\nall-ones quotient or the original dividend; preserve INT_MIN/-1 results\\nand the dividend's sign for signed remainder. Bound request-to-response\\nand subsequent dispatch so consecutive divides remain compatible with\\nthe unchanged formal liveness window; avoid gratuitous setup, finish,\\nor cooldown states. Arithmetic and control use the same cycle schedule\\nin real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds. In ALTOPS mode, capture the four\\nexisting subtraction/XOR stand-ins at acceptance and release them on\\nthat same response schedule rather than taking a faster formal path.\\n\\nLaunch only for a valid DIV/REM in ID/EX, once forwarded operands are\\nusable and the older EX/MEM instruction can advance. Capture both\\npost-forward operands, opcode, and the associated RVFI operand values\\nat launch so later WB draining cannot alter an active operation. Hold\\nID/EX and fetch while its result is unavailable. Let older MEM/WB work\\ndrain normally: when EX/MEM is ready, emit a bubble with zeroed control\\nduring the computation, not a repeated copy of the older instruction.\\nAn actual dmem stall still holds its outstanding EX/MEM request and\\nretains the existing WB forwarding payload discipline. Keep a completed\\ndivision response stable until exactly one EX/MEM acceptance, then\\nrelease ID/EX and fetch. Preserve normal forwarding from the completed\\nEX/MEM result to immediately dependent arithmetic, branches, and stores.\\nReset cancels any active transaction; bubbles and killed instructions\\nmust never launch the engine or create an architectural side effect.\\n\\nKeep the decoder's legality rules, all instruction results and traps,\\nbus handshakes, top-level nret=1 ports, and channel-0 RVFI ordering intact.\\nThe gain depends on division being sufficiently uncommon in the timed\\nCoreMark region; its frequency is an assumption to measure, not a known\\nprofile. Evaluate added marker-bracketed cycles against the median Fmax\\ngain, accepting only an increase in their product. For example, a 15%\\nFmax gain with 2.5% additional cycles gives approximately 12% fitness\\nimprovement. Retain every existing correctness gate and arithmetic\\nvector, add protocol/dependency coverage, and run the unchanged formal,\\nstalled cosim, CoreMark CRC/UART checks, and three-seed FPGA evaluation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases, and the existing\\nALTOPS DIV/REM formulas on the same bounded execution schedule.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace combinational division and remainder with the new engine\\nand expose its clock/reset and request/response control to EX.\\nPreserve combinational integer and multiply outputs and all their\\nALTOPS formulas. Select DIV/REM results only from the completed\\nengine response; eliminate the old unrolled / and % hardware.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add division acceptance/completion control and capture the forwarded\\noperand values used for RVFI. Dispatch ordinary operations exactly\\nas before. While a division waits, drain an available EX/MEM slot\\nto a control-zero bubble; preserve a genuinely stalled memory slot.\\nCommit the held divide result and its instruction metadata once,\\nwith the original PC, rd, and operands, and support normal bypass\\nof that result to the following instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX's division wait/acceptance signals to pipeline control while\\npreserving the imem/dmem interfaces, single-channel RVFI ports,\\nretirement boundary, and order counter. Distinguish holding the\\ninstruction in ID/EX from holding an older instruction in EX/MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold fetch and ID/EX for an unfinished DIV/REM without flushing that\\ninstruction. Keep load-use and redirect priorities correct, and\\nreserve EX/MEM holding and WB payload retention for actual memory\\nbackpressure so division waiting cannot duplicate an older memory\\nrequest or retirement. Release the frontend on result acceptance.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt division/remainder checks to the bounded clocked handshake\\nwhile retaining every existing RV32I and RV32M expected-bit vector.\\nAdd randomized signed/unsigned quotient and remainder checks,\\nfull-width trial-remainder cases, response holding, repeated\\nrequests, reset while busy, and an explicit completion bound.\\nContinue checking ordinary integer and multiply latency unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM producer-consumer chains, back-to-back divisions, x0\\ndestinations, preceding WB and load producers, and following\\nbranches/stores. Exercise simultaneous instruction/data stalls,\\nolder stores draining once, reset during division, and flushed\\nwrong-path divisions. Check original RVFI operands, one ordered\\nretirement per accepted instruction, and no duplicate bus effects.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases, and the existing\\nALTOPS DIV/REM formulas on the same bounded execution schedule.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace combinational division and remainder with the new engine\\nand expose its clock/reset and request/response control to EX.\\nPreserve combinational integer and multiply outputs and all their\\nALTOPS formulas. Select DIV/REM results only from the completed\\nengine response; eliminate the old unrolled / and % hardware.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add division acceptance/completion control and capture the forwarded\\noperand values used for RVFI. Dispatch ordinary operations exactly\\nas before. While a division waits, drain an available EX/MEM slot\\nto a control-zero bubble; preserve a genuinely stalled memory slot.\\nCommit the held divide result and its instruction metadata once,\\nwith the original PC, rd, and operands, and support normal bypass\\nof that result to the following instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX's division wait/acceptance signals to pipeline control while\\npreserving the imem/dmem interfaces, single-channel RVFI ports,\\nretirement boundary, and order counter. Distinguish holding the\\ninstruction in ID/EX from holding an older instruction in EX/MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold fetch and ID/EX for an unfinished DIV/REM without flushing that\\ninstruction. Keep load-use and redirect priorities correct, and\\nreserve EX/MEM holding and WB payload retention for actual memory\\nbackpressure so division waiting cannot duplicate an older memory\\nrequest or retirement. Release the frontend on result acceptance.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt division/remainder checks to the bounded clocked handshake\\nwhile retaining every existing RV32I and RV32M expected-bit vector.\\nAdd randomized signed/unsigned quotient and remainder checks,\\nfull-width trial-remainder cases, response holding, repeated\\nrequests, reset while busy, and an explicit completion bound.\\nContinue checking ordinary integer and multiply latency unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM producer-consumer chains, back-to-back divisions, x0\\ndestinations, preceding WB and load producers, and following\\nbranches/stores. Exercise simultaneous instruction/data stalls,\\nolder stores draining once, reset during division, and flushed\\nwrong-path divisions. Check original RVFI operands, one ordered\\nretirement per accepted instruction, and no duplicate bus effects.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 372.62, \"delta_pct\": 31.75, \"vs_baseline\": 31.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5438, \"ff\": 2094, \"seeds\": [165.32, 174.03, 167.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Bounded shared division in cores/bench\\n\\nImplemented a shared unsigned restoring divider with four quotient-bit steps\\nper compute clock, eight compute clocks, and 33-bit trial/subtract paths. Signed\\noperands are normalized at acceptance; original dividend, operand signs and\\nquotient/remainder selection are retained. Zero divisor and INT_MIN/-1 follow\\nRV32M exactly. The synthesized divider contains no division/remainder operators.\\nThe existing four ALTOPS subtraction/XOR formulas are captured at acceptance and\\nuse the same control schedule as real arithmetic. Integer and all four multiply\\noperations retain their combinational ALU path and single-cycle EX dispatch.\\n\\nEX launches only a valid legal DIV/REM with usable forwarded operands and an\\navailable older EX/MEM slot. It captures both post-forward operands for RVFI and\\nholds the instruction metadata in ID/EX. While computing, fetch and ID/EX hold;\\nan available EX/MEM slot becomes a completely zeroed bubble so older loads,\\nstores and retirements drain once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\\nDecoder legality, traps, buses, pipeline boundaries, nret=1 channel-0 ports and\\nthe retirement/order logic remain intact.\\n\\n## Schedule adjustment and formal\\n\\nThe first implementation registered the response after eight compute edges and\\ndispatched it on a separate ninth edge. Local formal passed 52 checks but failed\\n`liveness_ch0` at step 20: a load-use bubble followed by division produced an\\n11-clock gap after the preceding retirement. One RTL fix removed that extra\\ndispatch edge. The response is offered during the eighth compute interval and\\ncan enter EX/MEM on its closing edge. If blocked, the same value is registered\\nand held until acceptance. This is the only deviation from an exclusively\\nregistered response interface; real and ALTOPS schedules remain identical.\\nConsecutive divides now dispatch nine clocks apart, with a worst-case 10-clock\\nretirement gap after load-use or a redirect in the zero-wait formal model.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS after one fix attempt. The unchanged\\n`bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatically selecting `wrapper_si.sv` and\\n`checks_si.cfg`, passed all 53 checks. A final run after the compilation guard\\nadjustment again passed all 53. No checks or depths were modified. Full\\nnon-ALTOPS `formal-deep` was not run; the fast formal suite proves routing and\\nprotocol, while real arithmetic is checked by the vectors and ISS comparisons.\\n\\nThe exact requested glob-order lint command passes without warnings, as does\\nthe same command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`. A guarded core_pkg include in\\ncore.sv accommodates that glob ordering while preserving the existing build\\nflows that already place core_pkg.sv first.\\n\\n## Correctness validation\\n\\n- All six pytest runners passed: ALU real and ALTOPS (30 cocotb tests each),\\n  decoder (30), immediate generator (8), pipeline (18), register file (5).\\n- Every original arithmetic vector remains. Added 1,508 signed/unsigned\\n  DIV/REM edge/random pairs, 512 random multiply checks, precise completion\\n  timing, changing live operands, held responses, busy requests, simultaneous\\n  response consumption/request refill, and reset during busy/response phases.\\n- Pipeline tests compare every RVFI field with the unchanged Python ISS and\\n  independently count accepted memory requests. Coverage includes both operand\\n  forwarding paths, load producers, held WB load payload behind an older store,\\n  12-clock simultaneous bus stalls, dependent/back-to-back divisions, x0,\\n  arithmetic/branch/store consumers, killed divisions, and reset while busy.\\n  Ordinary integer/multiply retirements remain one clock apart.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate passed selftest and CoreMark CRC/UART validation.\\n  Additional selftest runs with both random bus stalls passed seeds\\n  0, 1, 2, 3, 42 and 99 (115 byte-exact retirements each).\\n\\n## FPGA and CoreMark evaluation\\n\\nThe unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`\\ncompleted all three nextpnr seeds. Final routed Fmax values were 165.32,\\n174.03 and 167.36 MHz; median 167.36 MHz. Resource usage was 5,438 LUT4 and\\n2,094 FF, compared with the supplied baseline's 9,563 LUT4 and 1,866 FF.\\nLUT4 usage fell 43.13%.\\n\\nThe checked-in Makefile/evaluator use a 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark\\nconfiguration, despite the hypothesis text describing 6 KB. They were left\\nunchanged. This official gate passed its exact CRCs, UART validation, markers,\\nand OOB checks. Both candidate and a fresh simulation of the baseline RTL from\\nHEAD used 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles. A streamed retirement profile\\nmeasured zero DIV/REM instructions among 2,922,015 timed retirements.\\n\\nOfficial local fitness: **372.62 iterations/s**, versus recorded baseline\\n282.82 iterations/s (**+31.75%**). Median Fmax increased 31.75% with **0% added\\ntimed cycles**, so the frequency-times-iterations-per-cycle acceptance criterion\\nimproves. These are local results; the orchestrator still performs acceptance.\\n\\nThe requested 6 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 configuration was additionally built and\\nrun from unmodified scratch copies. Candidate and baseline both completed in\\n32,836,671 bracketed cycles with no OOB access. Both produced crclist=0xd4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, seedcrc=0x8a02, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal\\n`Correct operation validated.`. At the respective median frequencies, that is\\n50.97 versus 38.69 iterations/s, again a 31.75% improvement with no cycle penalty.\\nIts streamed profile measured zero DIV/REM instructions among 22,052,222 timed\\nretirements; two DIVU instructions executed outside the markers.\\nThe printed compiler-flags string is hardcoded in the unchanged port; the\\nactual build commands used -O3.\\n\\n## Evidence and remaining limits\\n\\nAll build, formal, cosim, profile and P&R outputs are under the isolated scratch\\ndirectory below. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the\\nworktree's formal submodule is a shared symlink; no gate files or other cores\\nwere edited. Only cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this\\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree.\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l`\\n\\nKey evidence: `formal-final-console.log`, `final-unit-tests.log`,\\n`stalled-cosim.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-eval.log`, `division-profile.log`,\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `sixk/*coremark.log`, and\\n`sixk/division-profile.log`.\\n\\nDivision is intentionally blocking and adds eight execution clocks per issued\\nDIV/REM relative to the baseline. Workloads with substantial division traffic\\nmay lose performance despite the FPGA frequency/area improvement. The terminal\\ncompute interval includes sign correction and the EX/MEM acceptance mux; it has\\nbeen included in, and passed, all three routed timing evaluations.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:29:30.038895Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer with a two-bit\\nsaturating direction counter per entry. Index it with fetch PC[5:2], use\\nthe full remaining PC[31:6] as the tag, and store aligned target[31:2].\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\\npredict PC+4. Conditional branches use the counter for direction, while\\nresolved JAL/JALR entries are set strongly taken and learn their latest\\ntarget. Clear valid bits on reset; payloads need not be reset and must\\nnever influence a miss. Use the full architectural PC and target widths.\\n\\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\\nIF/ID into ID/EX. EX computes the actual next PC using the existing\\nforwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush younger\\nwork only when a valid instruction advances out of EX and its actual\\nnext PC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every valid\\ninstruction, including a non-control instruction with a stale table hit.\\nThe repair target is the actual next PC, including PC+4 for a predicted\\ntaken branch that resolves not taken, and the existing PC+4 continuation\\nfor a misaligned target trap. Preserve JAL/JALR link data, trap handling,\\nand the resolved next PC reported through RVFI.\\n\\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never while EX is held by dmem\\nbackpressure. For a matching conditional entry, saturate toward its\\nresolved direction and refresh its aligned branch target. Allocate a\\nmissed conditional entry only when taken, initializing it weakly taken\\n(2'b10). Allocate or refresh legal, aligned JAL/JALR targets with 2'b11.\\nInvalidate a matching entry when the resolved instruction is no longer\\na legal control transfer or its control-transfer target is misaligned.\\nNever allocate or reinforce predictions from bubbles or trap outcomes.\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training to use the pre-edge table\\ncontents for the prediction that is captured; a later table update must\\nnot change recorded metadata.\\n\\nApply a predicted PC advance only when that fetch is actually accepted\\ninto ID/EX. On load-use hazards hold the fetch PC and bubble ID/EX;\\non dmem holds freeze both PC and ID/EX, including its saved prediction.\\nInject the existing invalid bubble on imem unavailability when ID/EX\\nis otherwise able to advance.\\nWhile dmem holds EX, defer both repair and training until EX can advance;\\nretain the existing forwarding state during the hold. An eligible EX\\nrepair must override imem backpressure and any younger prediction.\\nKeep raw lookup independent of flush/stall signals to avoid combinational\\nloops. Correct predictions must not assert a redirect or flush.\\n\\nThis single predictor is expected to improve net fitness by about 8%,\\nsubject to measurement. The small table limits added lookup depth and\\nrouting, but conflicts, changing indirect targets, and additional LUT/FF\\ncost may reduce the gain or lower Fmax. Validate with unchanged formal,\\ncosimulation, CRC, UART, marker-window, and three-seed FPGA evaluation\\ngates. Preserve the nret=1 interface and every evaluation invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 291.2, \"delta_pct\": 2.96, \"vs_baseline\": 2.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10377, \"ff\": 2069, \"seeds\": [129.95, 121.11, 122.77], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Small tagged bimodal predictor\\n\\nImplemented the requested 16-entry direct-mapped predictor in `cores/bench`.\\nThe index is PC[5:2], the tag is PC[31:6], and the stored aligned target is\\ntarget[31:2]. A valid tag hit with counter[1] predicts the target; all misses\\nuse full-width PC+4. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\\nConditional hits refresh the target and saturate the direction counter.\\nTaken conditional misses allocate weakly taken; not-taken misses leave the\\nresident entry untouched. Legal aligned JAL/JALR updates are strongly taken\\nand learn their latest target. Matching stale, illegal, or misaligned entries\\nare invalidated, including misaligned untaken branch targets. Bubbles and\\nheld EX instructions cannot train.\\n\\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC consumed at the accepting\\nedge. EX compares every advancing valid instruction against one actual next\\nPC, also used for RVFI. A mismatch repairs fetch and discards the younger\\ncombinational IF/ID slot. Correct predictions have no repair bubble. JAL/JALR\\nlink writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, misalignment traps, and PC+4 trap\\ncontinuation are preserved. Repairs wait through dmem holds and override\\nimem backpressure when EX advances.\\n\\nLoad-use stalls hold PC and inject an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n## Deviations and scope\\n\\n- Added standalone predictor tests as well as pipeline regressions. The\\n  pipeline harness now models pre-edge bus acceptance and checks RVFI order,\\n  PC continuity, prediction capture, holds, repairs, and store handshakes.\\n- Split hazard equations into independent continuous assignments to avoid\\n  false combinational scheduling cycles in the instrumented Verilator build.\\n- Used `core_pkg.sv` first when linting; compilation-unit typedefs require\\n  this ordering. No lint suppressions were added for combinational loops.\\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This notes file is the requested\\n  exception to the RTL/test editing surface.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\\n\\nThe full formal log is under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-formal/formal-final-console.log`; FPGA seed\\nlogs and the combined result are under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-fpga/`. Cocotb XML results, selftest comparison\\nresults, and the supplemental 6 KB build/run are under the corresponding\\n`-cocotb`, `-selftest-results.json`, and `-6k` scratch paths.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nThe prompt specifies 6 KB CoreMark, but the checked-in Makefile defaults and\\n`tools/eval/fpga.py` CRC gate use the 2 KB fixture. Both workloads were tested\\nseparately without changing the evaluator. The 2 KB measurement must not be\\nreported as a 6 KB fitness result. The measured 10,377 LUT4 exceeds\\n`core.yaml`'s 10,000-LUT target, although all placements succeeded. FPGA\\ncost and reduced Fmax limit the net gain. Direct-map conflicts and changing\\nindirect targets remain expected sources of prediction misses. These are\\nlocal results; orchestrator acceptance and its independent rerun remain\\noutside this implementation step.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:27:51.865455Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\"}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode ALU result selection at the existing ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The winning divider implementation reports 372.62 iter/s at a median\\n167.36 MHz. Its saved routed timing reports under\\n.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log\\nshow 6.05 ns and 5.97 ns critical paths for seeds 1 and 3, respectively.\\nBoth start at ID/EX bit 53, which the saved synthesis netlist identifies\\nas alu_op[3], and end at EX/MEM result registers. This supports targeting\\noperation decoding and result selection inside EX rather than assuming\\nan arithmetic carry chain is the remaining bottleneck. Seed 2 instead\\nhas a 5.75 ns fetch/decode path, so moving decode work earlier has a risk.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the experiment log were checked. The previous micro_opt\\nattempt, hyp-20260906-001-r1s0, left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged\\nand required additional RVFI operand repair. The predictor lesson also\\nshows that cycle savings can be offset by timing and area costs. A small\\nchange to the existing ALU control representation offers a distinct\\nopportunity to raise frequency while preserving instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move ALU result-select decoding across the existing ID/EX register,\\nwithout adding a pipeline stage. Add a 16-bit selector to id_ex_t:\\nbits 0 through 14 select the existing ALU_ADD through ALU_MULHSU results\\nusing their unchanged numeric operation constants, and bit 15 selects\\nthe shared DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU response. Generate these mutually exclusive\\nenables from dec_alu_op in ID and latch them with the instruction.\\nKeep the encoded operation for the divider's signedness, quotient versus\\nremainder choice, and distinct formal ALTOPS substitutions.\\n\\nIn EX, consume the registered enables directly. Implement ALU output\\nselection as masked result terms with a balanced OR reduction, avoiding\\na new priority chain or re-encoding followed by another binary decode.\\nUse selector bit 15, still qualified by in.valid and !is_illegal, for\\nthe existing is_div classification. The division result term retains\\nits existing div_resp_valid qualification. Keep every arithmetic\\nexpression, multiplier, divider recurrence, and ALTOPS formula intact.\\nThis is one control-path micro-optimization, not an arithmetic redesign.\\n\\nDerive selectors from the existing decoder output without changing its\\nlegality decisions. Preserve its default ADD selection even for illegal\\ninstructions; an unsupported internal ALU operation selects no result.\\nReset and flush clear the complete ID/EX bundle, while every hold retains\\nthe selectors with the instruction. Preserve the eight-compute-clock\\ndivider, terminal-edge acceptance, held response, operand snapshots,\\nload interlocks, forwarding, redirect priority, and nret=1 RVFI contract.\\nIn particular, add no completion cycle: the previous divider notes show\\nthat this would violate the unchanged bounded liveness check.\\n\\nExpect identical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and a modest Fmax gain\\nfrom removing binary operation decode from the measured EX paths. The\\nfive-percent fitness estimate is unmeasured. Extra selector fanout, OR\\nrouting, synthesis restructuring, and a newly dominant ID path could\\nerase the gain. Validate actual mapped timing and area with the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA evaluation against the current winner, and compare cycle\\ncounts using identical binaries and bus-stall settings. Run existing\\nlint, formal, arithmetic, pipeline, and cosim gates; retain the required\\n6 KB CRCs, UART validation, memory bounds, and timing markers. If saved\\nevaluator artifacts use a different working-set size, report those\\nmeasurements separately without altering the evaluation contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the 16 result-selector positions and add an alu_result_sel\\nfield only to id_ex_t. Keep the existing five-bit ALU operation\\nvalues, ctrl_t legality fields, downstream bundles, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into the specified result enables before the\\nID/EX register. Latch them with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold on stalls and clear on reset/flush. Include all four division\\noperations in the shared bit-15 class. Preserve decoder defaults\\nand avoid deriving selectors from unvalidated raw instruction bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the registered selectors into alu and use their division-class\\nbit for is_div with the existing valid/legal guards. Preserve the\\nencoded divider operation, all request/response and stall equations,\\ncaptured operands, jump-result mux, traps, and EX/MEM acceptance timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the predecoded selector vector and replace the encoded-op\\noutput case with parallel masked result selection and a balanced OR\\ntree. Preserve all real arithmetic expressions and formal ALTOPS\\nformulas, including each multiply variant. Keep div_unit's encoded\\nop input and handshake unchanged; its result term still outputs zero\\nunless div_resp_valid. A zero selector vector produces zero output.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Update local stimulus helpers to drive the new selector input in\\nstep with every operation change, retaining every existing expected\\nvalue and timing assertion. Exercise all result classes, zero-hot\\nselection, rapid operation transitions, signed and unsigned multiply\\nhigh halves, and the existing division edge cases and held responses\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Keep shared test infrastructure intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed-operation streams to verify that registered\\nselectors stay paired with operands across forwarding, load-use\\nbubbles, imem stalls, dmem holds, redirects, and blocking division.\\nCheck unchanged retirement spacing for ordinary ALU/multiply and\\nconsecutive divides, full RVFI operand/result and order consistency,\\nand absence of wrong-path effects. Retain all existing assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the 16 result-selector positions and add an alu_result_sel\\nfield only to id_ex_t. Keep the existing five-bit ALU operation\\nvalues, ctrl_t legality fields, downstream bundles, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into the specified result enables before the\\nID/EX register. Latch them with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold on stalls and clear on reset/flush. Include all four division\\noperations in the shared bit-15 class. Preserve decoder defaults\\nand avoid deriving selectors from unvalidated raw instruction bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the registered selectors into alu and use their division-class\\nbit for is_div with the existing valid/legal guards. Preserve the\\nencoded divider operation, all request/response and stall equations,\\ncaptured operands, jump-result mux, traps, and EX/MEM acceptance timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the predecoded selector vector and replace the encoded-op\\noutput case with parallel masked result selection and a balanced OR\\ntree. Preserve all real arithmetic expressions and formal ALTOPS\\nformulas, including each multiply variant. Keep div_unit's encoded\\nop input and handshake unchanged; its result term still outputs zero\\nunless div_resp_valid. A zero selector vector produces zero output.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Update local stimulus helpers to drive the new selector input in\\nstep with every operation change, retaining every existing expected\\nvalue and timing assertion. Exercise all result classes, zero-hot\\nselection, rapid operation transitions, signed and unsigned multiply\\nhigh halves, and the existing division edge cases and held responses\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Keep shared test infrastructure intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed-operation streams to verify that registered\\nselectors stay paired with operands across forwarding, load-use\\nbubbles, imem stalls, dmem holds, redirects, and blocking division.\\nCheck unchanged retirement spacing for ordinary ALU/multiply and\\nconsecutive divides, full RVFI operand/result and order consistency,\\nand absence of wrong-path effects. Retain all existing assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.35, \"delta_pct\": -5.98, \"vs_baseline\": 23.88, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5509, \"ff\": 2122, \"seeds\": [141.2, 164.83, 157.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# hyp-20260906-001-r2s0: predecoded ALU result selection\\n\\nImplemented the proposed control change. All local correctness gates pass,\\nbut the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation regresses median Fmax and\\nthroughput by 5.98%. The measured result does not support the estimated gain.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`: added 16 named selector positions and a\\n  16-bit `alu_result_sel` field only to `id_ex_t`. Positions 0 through 14\\n  retain the numeric ADD through MULHSU operation values; position 15\\n  selects the shared divider response. Encoded operations, `ctrl_t`,\\n  downstream bundles, and RVFI widths are unchanged.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv`: predecode comes exclusively from\\n  `dec_alu_op`. Equality enables for operations 0 through 14 and a shared\\n  DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU enable are latched with the existing ID/EX instruction\\n  enable. Reset/flush clear the entire bundle and holds preserve it.\\n  Illegal decoder outputs retain default ADD; unsupported internal operation\\n  values match no selector. Decoder legality logic is untouched.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: passes the registered enables to the ALU\\n  and uses bit 15 with the existing valid/legal guards for `is_div`.\\n  Divider launch, acceptance, stall, forwarding, captured operands, jump\\n  result selection, redirects, traps, and EX/MEM timing are unchanged.\\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: replaced the encoded output case with 16 masked\\n  result terms and explicit 8/4/2/1 balanced OR levels. Every arithmetic\\n  expression, signed/unsigned multiplier expression, and ALTOPS formula is\\n  preserved. The divider term remains qualified by `div_resp_valid`.\\n  `div_unit.sv` is unchanged, including encoded operation selection and\\n  terminal-edge response acceptance. A zero selector produces zero output.\\n- `cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: every operation drive now supplies its\\n  selector. All original vectors and timing assertions remain. Added\\n  randomized rapid transitions across all combinational classes, zero-hot\\n  selection, unsupported operations, and switching result selection while\\n  each type of divider response is held, in real and ALTOPS builds.\\n- `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`: added dependent streams covering all\\n  16 classes, exact one-clock ordinary/multiply and nine-clock divide\\n  retirement spacing, and mixed streams with both bus stalls. The latter\\n  compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and explicitly\\n  require EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, selector/bundle\\n  holds, redirects simultaneous with each bus stall, blocking division,\\n  illegal-default ADD, and absence of wrong-path stores/divider requests.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Four-bit selector-position\\nconstants explicitly narrow the unchanged five-bit operation constants to\\navoid Verilator array-index width warnings. No source outside the four RTL\\nfiles and two allowed test files was changed, apart from this requested note.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- PASS: the exact requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` command, with no warnings.\\n  The same lint also passes with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\\n- PASS: unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, auto-selecting `nret: 1`,\\n  `wrapper_si.sv`, and `checks_si.cfg`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, first formal\\n  invocation, zero formal retries. Includes non-vacuous channel-0\\n  retirement, liveness, uniqueness, register/PC routing, and M-ext ALTOPS.\\n  No counterexample was produced. Full real-arithmetic formal-deep was\\n  not run; multiplier/divider arithmetic was not redesigned.\\n- PASS: all six pytest runners, **127 cocotb cases**: 32 real ALU, 32 ALTOPS\\n  ALU, 30 decoder, 8 immediate, 20 pipeline, and 5 register-file tests.\\n- PASS: unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` gate, two ELFs. Additional selftest\\n  comparison against the unchanged Python ISS passed all 115 retirements\\n  both zero-wait and with `--istall --dstall`.\\n- PASS: both saved 2 KB and required 6 KB CoreMark binaries complete with\\n  in-bounds memory, start/stop timing markers, 10 iterations, exact CRCs,\\n  and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Both designs were run\\n  locally against the same binary bytes and `--bench --istall --dstall`.\\n\\nAll generated files and copied gate infrastructure are under `$TMPDIR`,\\nnot in protected worktree directories. The installed Miniconda Python was\\nselected for cocotb, click, and YAML dependencies; the default Homebrew\\nPython lacked cocotb/click. No dependency or gate files were edited.\\n\\n## Three-seed mapped comparison\\n\\nRan unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1` and\\n`BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, using Yosys 0.64 and the existing nextpnr\\nscript/constraints. Compared against the saved current divider winner,\\nwhose formal-staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte equal to starting HEAD.\\nThe winner's evaluator sources and FPGA constraints also match this tree.\\n\\n| Measurement | Current winner | This implementation |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 routed Fmax, MHz | 165.32 | 141.20 |\\n| Seed 2 routed Fmax, MHz | 174.03 | 164.83 |\\n| Seed 3 routed Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 167.36 | 157.36 |\\n| LUT4 | 5,438 | 5,509 |\\n| DFF | 2,094 | 2,122 |\\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 3 |\\n| Saved evaluator 2 KB fitness, iter/s | 372.62 | 350.35 |\\n\\nAll three placements succeeded. Median frequency falls 5.98%; LUT4 rises\\n1.31% and DFF rises 1.34%. Although the RTL multiplier expressions are\\nidentical, the different selection network changes synthesis sharing and\\nmaps three MULT36X36 cells instead of one. This is a material area concern.\\n\\nThe final candidate paths are 7.08, 6.07, and 6.36 ns for seeds 1, 2, and 3.\\nThey start at `id_ex_w[46]` for seeds 1/3 and `[44]` for seed 2: registered\\nMULHU and MUL selectors, respectively. All end at EX/MEM `alu_result[4]`\\n(`ex_mem_w[235]`, `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_59`). Binary result decode was\\nremoved, but the mapped result-selection paths remain critical and slower.\\n\\n## Working-set distinction and identical-binary cycle comparison\\n\\nThe repository's unchanged evaluator and default CoreMark Makefile use\\n`TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`, despite the requested 6 KB contract. Its 350.35\\niter/s result above is therefore a separate 2 KB measurement, not a 6 KB\\nfitness claim. Neither evaluator constants nor benchmark sources were\\nmodified to reconcile this discrepancy.\\n\\n| Binary / bus settings | Winner cycles | Candidate cycles | Start / stop cycles, both |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| Saved 2 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 15,801 / 4,507,286 |\\n| Required 6 KB, ~22% imem+dmem stalls | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56,393 / 32,893,064 |\\n\\nThe saved 6 KB build log confirms `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; its UART\\ncompiler-flags string is a stale hardcoded `-O2` string. The reported\\n`CoreMark Size: 2000` is per algorithm for this 6000-byte total working set.\\nIts exact CRCs pass: seed `0x8a02`, list **`0xd4b0`**, matrix **`0xbe52`**,\\nstate **`0x5e47`**, final `0x5949`. Combining its measured cycles with the\\nthree-seed frequencies gives **50.97 -> 47.92 iter/s** for 6 KB, also -5.98%.\\nThis separate calculation does not replace the unchanged evaluator result.\\n\\nBinary SHA-256 values used for both designs:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `54d433aa39f063a87e7ebc9697bb14dc3a6b855705812d18cbc5ead32cc155b3`\\n- 6 KB: `7b52a15eb9a3de9a8fe8bf6e69abff8810cfe9753be9ee5ce94bbe509dba3955`\\n\\n`make fpga` rebuilt its own 2 KB ELF with different file metadata; every\\nPT_LOAD address, size, and byte hash matches the saved 2 KB ELF, and its\\nmeasured bracket is also exactly 4,491,485 cycles.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\nCandidate scratch root:\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-001-predecode-brw7_gc6/`\\n\\n- `formal-console.log` and `formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-33833/checks/`\\n- `unit-tests.log` and `cores/bench/test/*_runner.result.xml`\\n- `cosim-gate.log` and `selftest-comparison.json`\\n- `fpga-eval.log`, `fpga-result.json`, `timing-paths.json`, and\\n  `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`\\n- `coremark-comparison.json` and `{winner,candidate}-{2k,6k}-coremark.log`\\n\\nSaved winner root: `$TMPDIR/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l/`.\\nThe five-percent gain remains unsupported; this implementation is correct\\nunder the local gates but is a measured fitness regression.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:03:10.468534Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 (regression, -5.98%): Replacing ALU case selection with registered one-hot masks and a balanced OR tree disrupted multiplier sharing (MULT36X36 count rose from 1 to 3) and left slower selector-to-EX/MEM paths critical, lowering median Fmax 5.98% despite unchanged arithmetic and CoreMark cycles.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\\nseparated blocking division from ordinary execution, reaching 372.62\\nfitness and 167.36 MHz median Fmax with unchanged measured CoreMark cycles.\\nLESSONS.md attributes that gain to frequency, with no timed DIV/REM traffic.\\nThe saved winning implementation's seed-1 and seed-3 critical paths still\\nstart at ID/EX ALU control and traverse divider-associated result-selection\\nlogic before reaching EX/MEM registers, taking 6.05 and 5.97 ns respectively.\\nSeed 2 instead ends in decode, so further frequency headroom is uncertain.\\nThese are historical timing observations, not proof of a new result.\\n\\nIn the current RTL, the terminal divide result passes through div_unit's\\nlive/held response mux, the common ALU result selection, and EX's result\\ncapture logic even though div_unit already registers that same completed\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary instructions or divisions another execution clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\\nseparated blocking division from ordinary execution, reaching 372.62\\nfitness and 167.36 MHz median Fmax with unchanged measured CoreMark cycles.\\nLESSONS.md attributes that gain to frequency, with no timed DIV/REM traffic.\\nThe saved winning implementation's seed-1 and seed-3 critical paths still\\nstart at ID/EX ALU control and traverse divider-associated result-selection\\nlogic before reaching EX/MEM registers, taking 6.05 and 5.97 ns respectively.\\nSeed 2 instead ends in decode, so further frequency headroom is uncertain.\\nThese are historical timing observations, not proof of a new result.\\n\\nIn the current RTL, the terminal divide result passes through div_unit's\\nlive/held response mux, the common ALU result selection, and EX's result\\ncapture logic even though div_unit already registers that same completed\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary instructions or divisions another execution clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the EX/MEM execution result into two parallel register banks: the\\nexisting normal ALU/link-result register and div_unit's existing result_q.\\nThis retiming of result ownership is the single structural change. Keep\\ninteger and multiply execution, the divider arithmetic and eight-compute-\\nclock schedule, pipeline instruction boundaries, and single retirement.\\n\\nExport result_q directly from div_unit as a separate registered completion\\ndata output. It must have no combinational dependency on final_cycle,\\ncompleted_result, live operands, or the live opcode. Keep the existing\\nterminal response-valid and held-response protocol. On the final compute\\nedge, result_q captures completed_result regardless of whether EX accepts\\nimmediately or the response must wait. Consuming a response must not clear\\nits data; retain result_q until a subsequent completion or reset.\\n\\nGive EX independent normal-ALU and registered-divider data paths. Remove\\nthe live divide response from the common ALU output consumed by EX/MEM.\\nRegister a one-bit divide-result selector alongside EX/MEM metadata. On\\ndiv_accept, capture the divide instruction's metadata and set this bit on\\nthe very same edge that captures the terminal result in result_q; do not\\nsample result_q into another data register on that edge, which would read\\nthe preceding operation's value. Acceptance of an already-held response\\nuses its existing stable result_q. Normal instructions clear the selector\\nand capture their ordinary ALU or PC+4 link result as before. Reset and\\nbubbles clear the selector; an actual EX/MEM hold retains it with metadata.\\n\\nReconstruct out.alu_result after these registers using just the registered\\nselector and the two registered data banks. This selected value must feed\\nboth the existing EX/MEM forwarding path and MEM/WB capture, so a dependent\\nALU instruction, branch, JALR, store, multiply, or divide sees the completed\\ndivide at exactly the existing cycle. Never forward the stale normal bank\\nfor a divide. Preserve the divider bank while its EX/MEM owner is held;\\ndemonstrate that the blocking issue protocol cannot allow a newer divide\\ncompletion to overwrite it before the owner advances. Avoid an additional\\nopcode decoder or live arithmetic on the output side of the bank selector.\\n\\nLeave request launch, operand/RVFI snapshots, div_wait, older-instruction\\ndraining, load interlocks, bus acceptance, redirects, trap handling, and\\nretained WB forwarding discipline intact. In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mode use\\nthe existing formulas and exactly the same register ownership, response\\nschedule, and forwarding as real arithmetic. The formerly failing extra\\ndispatch edge must not return: back-to-back divides still dispatch nine\\nclocks apart and the unchanged formal liveness window must pass.\\n\\nExpect approximately five percent higher fitness from Fmax with unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed cycles; this is an unmeasured estimate. The output bank\\nmux can lengthen EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding and MEM/WB paths, and decode can\\nbecome limiting. Measure those paths and all three routed seeds rather\\nthan assuming the removed input path determines the new median. Preserve\\nthe nret=1 ports and every existing correctness check. Run the unchanged\\nlint, arithmetic tests, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA fitness gates;\\nverify canonical 6 KB CoreMark CRCs, validation text, bounds, and markers.\\nThe history distinguishes the evaluator's 2 KB fixture from a supplemental\\n6 KB run: compare matching workloads and label them accurately, leaving\\nall evaluation files and benchmark sources unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose result_q directly as the registered completion data bank while\\npreserving its final-compute-edge capture, held-response handshake,\\nreset behavior, arithmetic, and ALTOPS schedule. Retain completion data\\nafter response consumption, including while a subsequent request is\\nlaunched. Keep the live response data distinct from this registered\\noutput; no terminal-cycle bypass is allowed onto the registered port.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the normal integer/multiply result consumed by EX from the\\nlive DIV/REM response and exported registered completion data. Preserve\\nrequest/response controls and all arithmetic results and ALTOPS formulas.\\nThe synthesized normal result cone must not select a live divide result.\\nKeep a distinct live response data interface for protocol verification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use independent normal-result and registered-divide-result banks with\\nan EX/MEM-aligned divide selector. Update selector ownership on normal\\nadvance, div_accept, bubble, hold, and reset as specified. Assemble the\\nselected out.alu_result after the register boundary for both forwarding\\nand MEM/WB, without recapturing the divider's old result on its completion\\nedge. Preserve metadata, operand snapshots, launch/accept timing, link\\nresults, redirects, and existing drain and hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve every existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic vector and handshake\\nassertion, adapting DIV/REM observations to the separate response data\\nport. Check that registered completion data changes on the final edge,\\nequals the offered result after that edge, and remains stable through\\ndelayed acceptance and subsequent request launch. Cover immediate and\\nheld acceptance, changing live inputs, distinct successive results,\\nsigned corner cases, and reset during computation and held response.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add regressions for result-bank ownership and selection across normal,\\ndivide, bubble, reset, and memory-hold transitions. Exercise distinguishable\\nback-to-back DIV/REM results, x0 destinations, load-produced operands,\\nimmediate ALU/branch/JALR/store/multiply/divide consumers, and simultaneous\\nbus waits. Retain full RVFI operand/result/order/PC and accepted-memory-\\nrequest checks. Assert the existing nine-clock divide dispatch spacing,\\nunchanged ordinary instruction spacing, and no extra completion bubble.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose result_q directly as the registered completion data bank while\\npreserving its final-compute-edge capture, held-response handshake,\\nreset behavior, arithmetic, and ALTOPS schedule. Retain completion data\\nafter response consumption, including while a subsequent request is\\nlaunched. Keep the live response data distinct from this registered\\noutput; no terminal-cycle bypass is allowed onto the registered port.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the normal integer/multiply result consumed by EX from the\\nlive DIV/REM response and exported registered completion data. Preserve\\nrequest/response controls and all arithmetic results and ALTOPS formulas.\\nThe synthesized normal result cone must not select a live divide result.\\nKeep a distinct live response data interface for protocol verification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use independent normal-result and registered-divide-result banks with\\nan EX/MEM-aligned divide selector. Update selector ownership on normal\\nadvance, div_accept, bubble, hold, and reset as specified. Assemble the\\nselected out.alu_result after the register boundary for both forwarding\\nand MEM/WB, without recapturing the divider's old result on its completion\\nedge. Preserve metadata, operand snapshots, launch/accept timing, link\\nresults, redirects, and existing drain and hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve every existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic vector and handshake\\nassertion, adapting DIV/REM observations to the separate response data\\nport. Check that registered completion data changes on the final edge,\\nequals the offered result after that edge, and remains stable through\\ndelayed acceptance and subsequent request launch. Cover immediate and\\nheld acceptance, changing live inputs, distinct successive results,\\nsigned corner cases, and reset during computation and held response.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add regressions for result-bank ownership and selection across normal,\\ndivide, bubble, reset, and memory-hold transitions. Exercise distinguishable\\nback-to-back DIV/REM results, x0 destinations, load-produced operands,\\nimmediate ALU/branch/JALR/store/multiply/divide consumers, and simultaneous\\nbus waits. Retain full RVFI operand/result/order/PC and accepted-memory-\\nrequest checks. Assert the existing nine-clock divide dispatch spacing,\\nunchanged ordinary instruction spacing, and no extra completion bubble.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 375.78, \"delta_pct\": 0.85, \"vs_baseline\": 32.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 168.78, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5470, \"ff\": 2111, \"seeds\": [166.17, 168.78, 169.09], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the divide result-bank retiming in cores/bench. The unchanged\\nevaluator reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz median routed Fmax, versus the\\nstarting implementation's recorded 372.62 and 167.36 MHz: +0.85%, with unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed cycles. The estimated 5% improvement was not reached. Full\\ntiming coverage of the new forwarding/writeback paths remains a concern,\\ndescribed below; the reported fitness is not a complete physical timing proof.\\n\\ndiv_unit now exports result_q directly as registered_result, separately from\\nits existing live/held response data. Its arithmetic, eight compute clocks,\\nrequest/response handshake, final-edge capture, and reset behavior are\\nunchanged. Consumption and subsequent launch retain result_q. alu exposes\\nboth divider data ports, while its ordinary output contains only integer,\\nmultiply, and default-zero results. All real and ALTOPS arithmetic formulas\\nare preserved.\\n\\nEX/MEM now owns a registered divide selector alongside the existing metadata.\\ndiv_accept sets it on the same edge that div_unit captures the terminal result,\\nor accepts an already-held result. EX does not recapture the divider bank.\\nNormal instructions clear the selector and capture the usual ALU/PC+4 result;\\nreset and bubbles clear it, and an EX/MEM hold retains it. out.alu_result is a\\nmux of the two registered banks controlled only by that registered selector.\\nBoth existing EX/MEM forwarding and MEM/WB result capture consume this value.\\nThe nret=1 external ports, retirement boundary, operand snapshots, division\\nlaunch/accept equations, hazards, redirects, traps, and retained WB forwarding\\ndiscipline remain intact.\\n\\nThe ownership argument is enforced by the existing blocking issue protocol:\\nID/EX retains the dividing instruction until acceptance, so no younger divide\\ncan already be computing while that result becomes the EX/MEM owner. The next\\nlaunch requires !stall and therefore occurs on an edge that also advances the\\nprevious owner. Launch itself cannot change result_q, and the next completion\\nis eight compute edges later. Holding an owner suppresses all new launches.\\nAt core level, dmem backpressure only holds memory instructions; direct EX\\ntests additionally hold a divide owner for twelve clocks with a younger\\ndivide presented, proving bank and metadata retention at that interface.\\n\\nOne implementation deviation was necessary for FPGA placement. Sending the\\nselected output through the memory address interface prevented Yosys from\\nabsorbing the normal address register into the wrapper's block RAM. Both\\navailable Yosys builds mapped the wrapper to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks, exceeding\\n648 available blocks, and all three seeds failed placement. EX now exposes\\nthe normal bank directly as mem_addr; core passes it to mem_stage.address for\\naddressing, lane selection, and alignment checks. Memory instructions always\\nown the normal bank. The selected result still feeds MEM/WB alu_result and\\nEX/MEM forwarding. This restores four block RAMs without changing an accepted\\nmemory request, adding registers, or adding an output-side opcode decoder.\\nThe inactive dmem address during a divide can now show the unused normal bank;\\nDIV/REM never assert either memory request enable.\\n\\nLocal validation on the final RTL:\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS without warnings, both real arithmetic\\n  and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. git diff --check also passes.\\n- Formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including the unchanged liveness window.\\n  The initial implementation and the final memory-address correction each\\n  passed a complete invocation. No formal counterexamples or failed-check\\n  fix retries occurred. wrapper_si.sv, checks_si.cfg, run_all.sh, check depths,\\n  and the upstream checks were unchanged. formal-deep was not run.\\n- Cocotb: all eight pytest runners PASS, covering 128 cocotb cases: ALU real\\n  and ALTOPS (31 each), decoder (30), immediate generator (8), register file\\n  (5), pipeline (19), and direct EX real/ALTOPS ownership tests (2 each).\\n- Every existing arithmetic vector and handshake assertion remains. Added\\n  registered-bank checks before/on/after the terminal edge, immediate and\\n  held acceptance, changing live operands/opcodes, distinct successive\\n  results, concurrent response consumption/request refill, and resets during\\n  computation, held responses, and selected/held ownership.\\n- Pipeline regressions retain byte-exact RVFI comparison against the unchanged\\n  ISS and independent accepted-memory-request counts. They check selection\\n  and metadata on every monitored edge, normal/bubble/memory-hold transitions,\\n  load-produced operands, x0, distinct consecutive DIV/REM, and immediate\\n  ALU, branch, JALR, store, multiply, and divide consumers. Combined bus waits\\n  include twelve-clock forced holds. Divide dispatches remain nine clocks\\n  apart; ordinary instructions remain one clock apart; a following ordinary\\n  divide consumer has no additional completion bubble.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and CoreMark. Additional selftest\\n  runs passed all 115 RVFI retirements with zero waits, imem-only waits,\\n  dmem-only waits, and combined waits at seeds 0, 1, 2, 3, 42, and 99.\\n\\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv gate\\ncompleted all three seeds using the historical run's installed Yosys 0.64\\n(d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f.\\nFinal resources are 5,470 LUT4, 2,111 DFF, and four block RAMs, versus the\\nstarting record's 5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 DFF.\\n\\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Reported critical delay ns | Critical path |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 166.17 | 6.018 | Divider quotient register to result_q |\\n| 2 | 168.78 | 5.925 | ID/EX ALU control to normal EX/MEM result register |\\n| 3 | 169.09 | 5.914 | Divider divisor register to result_q |\\n\\nThe evaluator uses aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark. The requested\\naggregate 6 KB workload was built separately with the existing Makefile's\\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 options. Both candidate and fresh starting\\nRTL simulations used the identical ELF and approximately 22% random stalls on\\nboth buses within each comparison. No benchmark source or evaluator changed.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting RTL cycles | Candidate cycles | Candidate iter/s at reported median Fmax |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 375.78 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 51.40 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB markers are start=15,801 and stop=4,507,286; the 6 KB markers are\\nstart=56,393 and stop=32,893,064. Both sides of each comparison have identical\\nmarkers, clean EBREAK completion, no OOB access, ten iterations, and the\\nliteral Correct operation validated. The evaluator's five 2 KB CRC checks\\npass. Both 6 KB runs produce seedcrc=0x8a02, crclist=0xd4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, and crcfinal=0x5949. The UART's compiler-flags\\ndescription is hardcoded as -O2 in the unchanged port; the actual build\\ncommands use -O3. The 2 KB fitness must not be described as a 6 KB result.\\n\\nDetailed timing was investigated for all three seeds. Reloading a saved\\nroute with --no-pack --no-place --no-route crashes the installed nextpnr in\\narray2d.h, so three supplemental routes emitted JSON timing reports and SDF\\ndirectly. Each reproduced its corresponding gate Fmax. Summing the exported\\nSDF arcs reproduces all three reported critical path delays above.\\n\\nHowever, those SDF files omit IOPATH arcs for the LUT3 cells implementing the\\nbank selector. The detailed JSON reports contain no MEM/WB alu_result\\nendpoints. Complete bank-to-EX and bank-to-WB timing therefore cannot be\\nestablished from these unchanged reports. This is an observed reporting gap,\\nnot evidence that the mux or its forwarding paths have zero delay.\\n\\nFor additional inspection, structural walks through the placed connectivity\\nretain missing LUT arcs as explicit unknowns. Their largest known subtotals\\n(registered launch, available cell/routing delays, and setup) are:\\n\\n| Seed | Result banks/selector to normal EX/MEM result | Result banks/selector to MEM/WB result | Divider data bank alone to MEM/WB result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 12.593 ns plus 5 unknown LUT arcs | 2.278 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc | 1.480 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc |\\n| 2 | 12.233 ns plus 4 unknown LUT arcs | 1.876 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc | 1.410 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc |\\n| 3 | 13.832 ns plus 5 unknown LUT arcs | 2.278 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc | 1.344 ns plus 1 unknown LUT arc |\\n\\nThese walks are diagnostic connectivity paths, without sensitization checks\\nor complete cell characterization. They are not alternate Fmax or setup-slack\\nmeasurements. Their disagreement with the headline clock paths makes full\\nforwarding timing coverage an unresolved limitation. No timing model, gate,\\nconstraint, or fitness formula was changed to address or hide that limitation.\\nThe evaluator's measured gain is small, and this iteration does not establish\\nthe proposed five-percent physical frequency improvement.\\n\\nAll builds and scratch copies are under:\\n\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-002-r2-result-banks-anuzylcu`\\n\\nEvidence includes formal-console.log, unit-tests.log, the cocotb result XMLs,\\ncosim-gate.log, stalled-cosim.log, fpga-eval.log,\\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log, coremark-comparison.json,\\ncoremark-2k.log, baseline-coremark-2k.log, sixk/*coremark.log,\\ntiming/seed*/timing.json, timing/seed*/design.sdf, and\\ntiming/bank-paths-partial.json (per-pin known delays and explicit unknowns).\\nverified-source-sha256.json records byte-for-byte agreement for 223 staged\\nsource/contract files; final formal RTL also matches the submitted RTL.\\nOnly bench RTL, bench test_*.py files, and this requested notes file are\\nchanged in the worktree. Orchestrator acceptance remains a separate step.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:18:02.872179Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.85%): Routing the post-register DIV/REM result-bank mux into mem_stage.address blocks Yosys BRAM address-register absorption, so memory addresses must use the normal EX/MEM bank directly to avoid expansion from four block RAMs to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks and placement failure.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry, direct-mapped, full-width tagged bimodal BTB to the\\ncurrent cores/bench RTL, with training computed exclusively from\\nregistered fetch metadata and the instruction's resolved outcome.\\nThis is one predictor experiment: keep the existing divider, ALU,\\nforwarding, memory system, pipeline boundaries, and nret=1 interface.\\n\\nIndex the BTB by PC[5:2], store the full tag PC[31:6], aligned target\\nbits [31:2], a two-bit saturating counter, and a valid bit. Lookup is\\ncombinational and depends only on the fetch PC. A valid full-tag hit\\nwith counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise predict the\\nfull-width PC+4. Reset clears valid bits; unreset payloads must never\\naffect a miss. Do not introduce an instruction-data target adder or\\nreduce architectural address widths.\\n\\nAlong with the exact predicted_next_pc, capture lookup_hit and the\\nlookup_counter in ID/EX on the same edge that accepts the instruction.\\nA hit includes entries whose direction counter predicts not taken.\\nSupply a defined counter value on a miss. Hold all metadata with a\\nstalled instruction and clear it with a bubble or flush. The predictor\\narrays must have no resolving-PC read port: training must not use\\ntag_q[train_index], counter_q[train_index], or valid_q[train_index]\\nas an input to its next-state/write-enable logic.\\n\\nTrain once when a valid instruction actually advances from EX into\\nEX/MEM: in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. For a legal aligned\\nconditional branch with saved lookup_hit, saturate the saved counter\\ntoward the resolved direction and write a complete entry using this\\ninstruction's actual PC tag and branch target. On a saved miss,\\nallocate only a taken aligned branch, initialized weakly taken (10).\\nLegal aligned JAL/JALR always write the resolved target and counter\\n11. If an instruction with saved lookup_hit is no longer a legal\\ncontrol transfer, or has a misaligned control-transfer target, clear\\nthe addressed valid bit. Invalid instructions and bubbles never\\nallocate. Compute every payload write and enable without rereading\\nresident predictor state at EX; use local index write decoding.\\n\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training using the pre-edge table for\\nboth the consumed next PC and its captured metadata. Deliberately\\naccept a stale counter/hit snapshot when an older resolving instruction\\nupdates the same index at that edge; do not add a training-to-fetch\\nbypass that reconstructs the long feedback path. Such collisions may\\nlose a counter update or evict a newer entry, affecting prediction\\naccuracy only. Every allocation/update writes its own complete actual\\ntag and target, so metadata from different instructions cannot be\\ncombined into a fabricated entry. Full next-PC checking remains the\\ncorrectness mechanism. This bounded approximation must be exercised\\nby consecutive same-index branches and single-instruction loops.\\n\\nEX computes one actual_next_pc with the existing forwarded operands,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and alignment/trap continuation rules. For\\nevery advancing valid instruction, compare it with that instruction's\\nsaved predicted_next_pc, including ordinary instructions with stale\\nBTB hits and completed DIV/REM operations. Redirect only on a mismatch;\\nrepair to actual_next_pc and squash younger work. Correct predictions\\nintroduce no flush. RVFI pc_wdata always reports the resolved next PC;\\nJAL/JALR link writes and trap suppression remain unchanged.\\n\\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when its instruction is accepted.\\nPreserve load-use bubbles, invalid imem bubbles, dmem holds and retained\\nWB forwarding data, and all divider request/response behavior. Defer\\nrepairs and training while EX cannot advance. An eligible repair\\noverrides instruction-bus backpressure and the younger prediction.\\nKeep raw predictor lookup independent of stall/flush/redirect signals\\nand avoid combinational cycles in hazard control.\\n\\nThe prediction benefit is an estimate, not a new measurement. Retaining\\nabout 6% fewer cycles while losing at most about 1% Fmax would improve\\nfitness by roughly 5%. Counter-snapshot collisions, the remaining\\nlookup/repair paths, and FPGA mapping could reduce that gain. Compare\\nagainst the current 372.62 winner using the unchanged marker-bracketed\\nCoreMark and three-seed median-Fmax gates, with all existing formal,\\ncosim, CRC, UART, OOB, and resource checks intact. Inspect routed timing\\nto confirm that predictor training no longer contains a tag-RAM read\\nfollowed by tag comparison and table write enable in the same cycle.\\nKeep the official fixture and any supplemental 6 KB results distinct;\\ndo not modify either the evaluator or benchmark sources.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.07, \"delta_pct\": -7.39, \"vs_baseline\": 22.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6287, \"ff\": 3097, \"seeds\": [143.84, 145.48, 152.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the 16-entry full-width tagged bimodal BTB in cores/bench. Fetch\\nuses PC[5:2], the full PC[31:6] tag, an aligned 30-bit target, validity and a\\ntwo-bit direction counter. A miss exports counter 00 and full-width PC+4.\\nOnly valid bits reset; payloads cannot influence misses.\\n\\nID/EX captures the exact predicted next PC, hit (including not-taken hits),\\nand counter with its instruction. All fields hold during dmem/divider stalls\\nand clear on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. EX computes one resolved\\nnext PC, with forwarded operands, JALR bit-zero clearing and existing trap\\ncontinuation. Every valid EX/MEM acceptance compares that PC with its saved\\nprediction, including ordinary instructions and completed DIV/REM. Only a\\nmismatch repairs fetch and squashes younger work. RVFI retains the resolved\\nPC and the unchanged nret=1 channel-0 retirement/order interface.\\n\\nThe same in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait event drives training.\\nTraining uses only the registered hit/counter and resolved instruction:\\nsaved-hit aligned branches saturate the snapshot, saved misses allocate only\\ntaken aligned branches at 10, and legal aligned JAL/JALR write 11. Stale hits\\non non-control/illegal instructions or misaligned targets invalidate their\\nindex. Every payload update writes its own complete actual tag and target.\\nConstant per-entry write decoding introduces no resolving-PC read port.\\nSimultaneous lookup/training deliberately consumes the pre-edge table with\\nno bypass; stale snapshots can lose updates or evict intervening entries.\\n\\nHazard equations are separate continuous assignments so EX's dmem hold is\\nindependent of its qualified repair. Eligible repairs override imem\\nbackpressure; dmem/divider holds defer both repair and training. Load-use\\nbubbles, retained WB forwarding data, divider request/completion behavior,\\nALU, decoder, memory stages, pipeline boundaries and all top-level ports\\nremain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Local write decoding maps\\nthe table payload into enabled flops rather than relying on a RAM primitive.\\nThe new tests add an instruction-version-aware sparse fetch harness for\\nstale-code scenarios; they use the existing Python ISS and RVFI comparator\\nwithout changing either contract file. Existing tests/assertions remain.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS on the first run, 53 passed and 0 failed; no formal\\nfix attempts were needed. The unchanged bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected\\nwrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl.\\nIt ran from an isolated copy under TMPDIR because the worktree's upstream\\nformal directory is a shared symlink. No checks, depths or wrappers changed.\\nFull non-ALTOPS formal-deep was not run; divider arithmetic is unchanged and\\nboth real and ALTOPS arithmetic tests passed.\\n\\nThe exact requested glob-order Verilator lint command passes without\\nwarnings, as does the ALTOPS variant. All seven pytest runners pass, covering\\n129 cocotb cases: real ALU 30, ALTOPS ALU 30, decoder 30, immediate generator\\n8, register file 5, predictor 3 and pipeline 23. Coverage includes all tag\\nbits/indices/target bits, all saved counters, saved misses, coherent stale\\nreplacement, pre-edge collisions, inactive training, reset, warm loops and\\nexits, both direction errors, changing forwarded JALR targets, illegal and\\nmisaligned stale hits, consecutive same-index branches, single-instruction\\nloops, random imem/dmem stalls, 12-clock holds, retained WB data, deferred\\nDIV/REM repairs, and killed stores/divides. Pipeline monitors check every\\nmetadata capture/hold/bubble, accepted fetch PC, full-PC repair, EX training\\nevent, retirement continuity/order and absence of correct-prediction bubbles.\\n\\nA mapped-netlist combinational-cone audit checked every D and CE input of\\nall 944 predictor storage flops. None has a resident valid/tag/counter/target\\nbit as a combinational input. This confirms the training feedback path was\\nremoved in the mapped hardware, beyond the source-level read-port check.\\n\\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench gate, with NRET=1 and\\nBENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv, completed all three routes. Final Fmax values\\nwere 143.84, 145.48 and 152.72 MHz; median 145.48 MHz. Resource usage was\\n6,287 LUT4 and 3,097 FF, within the 10,000-LUT target. The unchanged cosim\\ngate also passed both selftest and CoreMark, including the official CRC,\\nUART, EBREAK, OOB and marker checks. CoreMark used 4,215,937 bracketed cycles\\nfor 10 iterations with the standard random instruction/data stalls.\\n\\nOfficial local fitness on the current divider-based core is 345.07\\niterations/s versus the current winner's 372.62: a 7.394% regression.\\nTimed cycles decreased 6.135%, but median Fmax decreased 13.074%. LUT4 usage\\nincreased 15.612%. The cycle benefit survived; the estimated <=1% Fmax loss\\ndid not. This implementation therefore does not beat the current winner.\\n\\nAll routed critical paths were inspected. Seed 1 has a 6.95 ns ID/EX\\ncontrol-to-EX/MEM path (3.30 ns logic, 3.65 ns routing). Seed 2 has a 6.87 ns\\npath from ID/EX PC through target-alignment/legal-control qualification to\\na predictor target write enable (2.77 ns logic, 4.10 ns routing). Seed 3\\nhas a 6.55 ns path from ID/EX branch control through resolved training\\nqualification to a predictor target write enable (2.57 ns logic, 3.98 ns\\nrouting). None contains a tag-table read followed by tag comparison and\\ntable write enable. Training qualification and enable fanout remain timing\\nlimitations after removing the original feedback path; the mapped-cone\\naudit above confirms there is no hidden resident-state training dependency.\\n\\nThe official checked-in fixture/evaluator use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3 and\\nITERATIONS=10. They were copied unchanged for evaluation. The recorded\\ncurrent winner is 372.62 iterations/s, 167.36 MHz, 4,491,485 bracketed cycles,\\n5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 FF. These are the comparison values from the existing\\nexperiment log, not new measurements of the baseline.\\n\\nSeparately, an unmodified scratch copy built with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 and -O3\\ncompleted 10 iterations in 30,823,120 marker-bracketed cycles, versus the\\nrecorded winner's supplemental 32,836,671 cycles (6.132% fewer). It reported\\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949,\\nthe literal Correct operation validated., both markers, EBREAK and no OOB\\naccess. At the candidate's 145.48 MHz median this is 47.20 iterations/s.\\nThis is supplemental 6 KB evidence, separate from official fitness.\\nThe UART compiler-flags string is hardcoded; the build command used -O3.\\n\\nEvidence directories, all under the authorized TMPDIR:\\n\\n- predictor-formal-mcapvt3h: run.log and formal/last_run-*.log, all per-check logs.\\n- predictor-tests-qlavcwvr: seven runner XML files and Verilator builds.\\n- predictor-fpga-667ih_e0: gate-*.log, training-cone-check.json, synth.json and\\n  cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log plus routed netlists.\\n- predictor-supplemental-6k-gvvrkbrq: build.log, sim.log and result.json.\\n\\nTMPDIR is /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp.\\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this explicitly\\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree. The orchestrator's\\nacceptance remains independent of these local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:12:02.854732Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Shorten divider carry chains with block carry-select subtraction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 375.78 at 168.78 MHz with 5,470 LUT4s. Its saved\\nrouted reports identify divider-to-result_q critical paths in seeds 1\\nand 3, at 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Seed 1 traverses the quotient sign-correction\\nnegation at div_unit.sv:63; seed 3 traverses a restoring subtraction at\\ndiv_unit.sv:57. Both contain long chains of native carry cells. Seed 2\\ninstead has a 5.925 ns ordinary ALU control-to-result path, so improving\\ndivider arithmetic alone has a competing timing limit.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt experiment records rule out simply repeating\\nsource-use filtering or whole-ALU one-hot selection: the former left\\nmeasured CoreMark cycles unchanged, while the latter reduced median Fmax\\n5.98% and increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. The proposed change\\ntherefore targets the divider's actual arithmetic carry chains. It can\\nimprove clock frequency even when the timed program executes no divides.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 375.78 at 168.78 MHz with 5,470 LUT4s. Its saved\\nrouted reports identify divider-to-result_q critical paths in seeds 1\\nand 3, at 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Seed 1 traverses the quotient sign-correction\\nnegation at div_unit.sv:63; seed 3 traverses a restoring subtraction at\\ndiv_unit.sv:57. Both contain long chains of native carry cells. Seed 2\\ninstead has a 5.925 ns ordinary ALU control-to-result path, so improving\\ndivider arithmetic alone has a competing timing limit.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt experiment records rule out simply repeating\\nsource-use filtering or whole-ALU one-hot selection: the former left\\nmeasured CoreMark cycles unchanged, while the latter reduced median Fmax\\n5.98% and increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. The proposed change\\ntherefore targets the divider's actual arithmetic carry chains. It can\\nimprove clock frequency even when the timed program executes no divides.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the divider's wide combinational subtract/negate primitives\\nwith one reusable, exact 33-bit block carry-select subtraction circuit.\\nKeep the four restoring steps per clock, eight compute clocks, terminal\\nresponse interval, and all existing pipeline and result-bank registers.\\nThis is a local arithmetic implementation change with no added latency.\\n\\nSplit x and y into four little-endian blocks of widths 8, 8, 8, and 9.\\nFor each block independently compute both candidate differences, x-y\\nand x-y-1, modulo that block's width, before its incoming borrow arrives.\\nAlso compute generate g=(x<y) and propagate p=(x==y); generate may reuse\\nthe correctly widened no-borrow subtraction's outgoing borrow. With\\ninitial borrow b0=0, use explicit parallel block-prefix expressions:\\nb1=g0; b2=g1|(p1&g0);\\nb3=g2|(p2&g1)|(p2&p1&g0).\\nEach block selects its precomputed candidate using its incoming borrow.\\nConcatenate the four selected blocks to produce exactly (x-y) mod 2^33.\\nKeep local arithmetic unsigned and explicitly widened; in particular,\\ny=max_block with borrow-in one must not wrap before subtraction. Avoid\\na final full-width increment/decrement after the block selections.\\n\\nUse this circuit for each of the four trial-minus-divisor operations.\\nPreserve every trial bit and the existing difference[32] restore decision;\\ndo not substitute the helper's unsigned borrow beyond bit 32 for that\\ndecision. Use the same arithmetic structure for both final conditional\\nnegations by subtracting the zero-extended 32-bit magnitude from zero\\nand taking bits [31:0]. Preserve the existing quotient/remainder choice,\\nsign enables, zero-divisor overrides, and all RV32M corner-case results.\\nThe intended mapping trades some short parallel adders and LUT muxes for\\nshorter serial carry propagation. Do not add timing exceptions, vendor\\ntiming-model changes, or false/multicycle path constraints.\\n\\nThe request/response controller and registered_result ownership remain\\nbit- and cycle-equivalent: request and consumption retain result_q,\\ncompletion writes it on the same terminal edge, blocked responses hold,\\nand reset cancels pending work. Leave ALTOPS formulas and its identical\\ncontrol schedule intact. Keep the ordinary ALU case and multiplier\\nexpressions unchanged, and retain the direct normal-bank memory address\\npath required for block-RAM address-register absorption. The nret=1 IO,\\ndecoder legality, forwarding, hazards, and all correctness gates stay intact.\\n\\nPredict unchanged marker-bracketed cycles and an unmeasured three-percent\\nfitness improvement from Fmax, with low confidence: native FPGA carry\\nchains are already efficient, extra mux routing can erase the benefit,\\nand the ordinary ALU may become limiting. Run existing lint, real and\\nALTOPS arithmetic/ownership/pipeline tests, unchanged formal and cosim,\\nand the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation. Inspect mapped arithmetic,\\nresource counts, and reported paths to establish whether carry depth\\nactually fell; do not infer physical timing closure from missing arcs.\\nThe preceding result-bank experiment documented incomplete forwarding\\ntiming coverage, which this hypothesis does not resolve by assumption.\\nPreserve the official fixture and benchmark sources; report the requested\\ncanonical 6 KB CRC/marker validation separately from the existing log's\\n2 KB evaluator numbers, as the prior implementation notes distinguish them.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_sub33.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a purely combinational unsigned 33-bit subtractor with the specified\\n8/8/8/9 blocks, parallel borrow-zero/borrow-one candidate results, and\\nexplicit block-prefix borrow generation. Expose only x, y, and the\\nexact 33-bit modular difference. Use synthesizable portable RTL and\\nbounded local carry chains, with no registers or timing attributes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the helper for all four existing restoring subtractions\\nand the two final magnitude negations in the real-arithmetic branch.\\nPreserve the 33-bit trials, difference[32] decisions, signed and zero\\noverrides, eight-clock controller, both response data interfaces,\\nresult_q retention, and unchanged ALTOPS branch. No other execution\\ndatapath or pipeline boundary changes belong to this hypothesis.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_sub33.py\", \"description\": \"Add a cocotb runner and independent Python-integer reference checks for\\nexact (x-y) modulo 2^33. Exercise randomized full-width operands, bit\\n32, equality, underflow, all block boundaries, borrow generation and\\npropagation through equal blocks, and maximal blocks with incoming\\nborrow. Check boundary values immediately below, at, and above powers\\nof two rather than duplicating the RTL prefix equations in the oracle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic and handshake assertion. Extend real\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU vectors around 8/16/24/32-bit boundaries, equal and\\nadjacent magnitudes, both signs, high-bit unsigned divisors, zero,\\nINT_MIN/-1, and negative remainders. Use the existing mathematical\\nreference and exact eight-clock/held-response/registered-bank checks;\\nretain corresponding unchanged ALTOPS behavior. Run existing pipeline\\nand EX result-bank tests to verify integration without changing their\\nacceptance criteria.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_sub33.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a purely combinational unsigned 33-bit subtractor with the specified\\n8/8/8/9 blocks, parallel borrow-zero/borrow-one candidate results, and\\nexplicit block-prefix borrow generation. Expose only x, y, and the\\nexact 33-bit modular difference. Use synthesizable portable RTL and\\nbounded local carry chains, with no registers or timing attributes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the helper for all four existing restoring subtractions\\nand the two final magnitude negations in the real-arithmetic branch.\\nPreserve the 33-bit trials, difference[32] decisions, signed and zero\\noverrides, eight-clock controller, both response data interfaces,\\nresult_q retention, and unchanged ALTOPS branch. No other execution\\ndatapath or pipeline boundary changes belong to this hypothesis.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_sub33.py\", \"description\": \"Add a cocotb runner and independent Python-integer reference checks for\\nexact (x-y) modulo 2^33. Exercise randomized full-width operands, bit\\n32, equality, underflow, all block boundaries, borrow generation and\\npropagation through equal blocks, and maximal blocks with incoming\\nborrow. Check boundary values immediately below, at, and above powers\\nof two rather than duplicating the RTL prefix equations in the oracle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic and handshake assertion. Extend real\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU vectors around 8/16/24/32-bit boundaries, equal and\\nadjacent magnitudes, both signs, high-bit unsigned divisors, zero,\\nINT_MIN/-1, and negative remainders. Use the existing mathematical\\nreference and exact eight-clock/held-response/registered-bank checks;\\nretain corresponding unchanged ALTOPS behavior. Run existing pipeline\\nand EX result-bank tests to verify integration without changing their\\nacceptance criteria.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 240.97, \"delta_pct\": -35.87, \"vs_baseline\": -14.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 108.23, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5810, \"ff\": 2112, \"seeds\": [108.23, 107.12, 112.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the specified divider block carry-select arithmetic in cores/bench.\\nThe unchanged three-seed evaluator reports 240.97 fitness at 108.23 MHz median\\nFmax, versus the starting winner's recorded 375.78 at 168.78 MHz: a 35.87%\\nregression with unchanged CoreMark cycles. The predicted 3% improvement was\\nnot observed. The implementation is retained for the orchestrator to evaluate.\\n\\ndiv_sub33.sv implements unsigned (x-y) modulo 2^33 in little-endian 8/8/8/9-bit\\nblocks. Each block computes widened local candidates before its incoming\\nborrow arrives. The borrow-one candidate uses x + ~y, complementing only the\\nlocal y bits before zero extension. Its low block-width bits are exactly\\nx-y-1; this avoids a wrapping y+1 and does not decrement the selected result.\\nThe widened borrow-zero subtraction supplies g, equality supplies p, and\\nthe three incoming borrows use the requested explicit parallel expressions.\\nOnly g/p bits needed by those expressions are retained. The module has no\\nregisters, attributes affecting synthesis/timing, or outgoing-borrow port.\\n\\ndiv_unit.sv instantiates the helper for all four restoring subtractions and\\nboth final conditional magnitude negations. The four-step combinational loop\\nis expressed as a generate loop with continuous assignments and module\\ninstances. Every 33-bit trial and difference[32] restore decision is preserved;\\nno bit-33 unsigned borrow replaces that decision. Sign enables, quotient versus\\nremainder selection, zero overrides, INT_MIN/-1, and negative remainders retain\\ntheir existing behavior. Input magnitude conversion is unchanged.\\n\\nThere is no architectural deviation from the hypothesis. The existing divider\\nalways_ff block and its request/response/ALTOPS prefix were checked byte-for-byte\\nagainst starting HEAD. Request acceptance, eight compute clocks, terminal\\nresponse interval, result_q capture/retention, blocked responses, reset, and\\nregistered-result ownership are unchanged. The ordinary ALU and multiplier,\\nEX/MEM banks, direct normal-bank memory address, nret=1 IO/RVFI, decoder,\\nforwarding, hazards, and all other pipeline RTL are unchanged.\\n\\nTest changes preserve all existing arithmetic and handshake assertions.\\ntest_div_sub33.py checks 38,530 pairs using Python's unbounded (x-y) masked to\\n33 bits: full-width random operands, all powers-of-two neighborhoods through\\nbit 33, equality, underflow, every combination of representative block operand\\nrelations, equal zero/maximal block borrow propagation, and maximal y blocks\\nwith an incoming borrow. test_alu.py adds signed and unsigned DIV/REM cross\\nproducts around 8/16/24/31/32-bit boundaries, with both signs, equal/adjacent\\nmagnitudes, high unsigned divisors, zero, and overflow. These use the existing\\nmathematical reference and exact latency/bank checks in both real and ALTOPS\\nbuilds. The EX ownership and pipeline test files only add the new RTL file to\\ntheir source lists; their acceptance criteria are unchanged.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Exact requested glob-order Verilator lint: PASS without warnings, both real\\n  arithmetic and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. git diff --check also passes.\\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first invocation, 53 passed / 0 failed.\\n  No formal counterexamples or fix attempts. The unchanged run_all.sh selected\\n  wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl and CORE_NAME=bench.\\n  All wrappers, depths, checks, and framework sources remain unchanged.\\n- Supplemental combinational Yosys SAT proof: PASS. A separate mathematical\\n  33-bit subtraction reference proves the helper exact for all 2^66 input\\n  combinations, including bit 32 and every borrow case. This supplements the\\n  unchanged ALTOPS core formal gate; full-core formal-deep was not run.\\n- All nine pytest runners PASS, containing 133 cocotb tests: ALU real/ALTOPS\\n  32 each, divider helper 3, decoder 30, EX banks real/ALTOPS 2 each, immediate\\n  generator 8, pipeline 19, and register file 5. The existing pipeline tests\\n  include byte-exact RVFI/ISS comparisons, both bus stalls, forwarded divide\\n  operands and consumers, held ownership, resets, and completion spacing.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and the official CoreMark fixture.\\n- Unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv: all three\\n  routes complete successfully. The installed historical tool versions were\\n  used: Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and\\n  nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. No timing exceptions, constraints, vendor models,\\n  benchmark source, fixture, or evaluation formula changed.\\n\\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Reported path ns | Logic / routing ns | Path |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 108.23 | 9.24 | 5.99 / 3.25 | divisor_q to remainder_q |\\n| 2 | 107.12 | 9.34 | 6.02 / 3.31 | divisor_q to remainder_q |\\n| 3 | 112.61 | 8.88 | 6.00 / 2.88 | quotient_q to remainder_q |\\n\\nReported resources are 5,810 LUT4 (+340, +6.22%) and 2,112 DFF (+1), with one\\nMULT36X36 and four block RAMs retained. No logical register was added; the\\none-flop mapped difference is a synthesis optimization difference.\\n\\nMapped connectivity establishes that native carry depth actually fell in the\\ntargeted arithmetic. The starting netlist has four 33-ALU restoring chains and\\ntwo 32-ALU final negation chains. The candidate helper maps to 36 independent\\nchains: ten of length 8 and twenty-six of length 9. The two unchanged input\\nmagnitude conversions still have 32-ALU chains. Total native ALU cells increase\\nfrom 546 to 665. Starting RTL was checked against the saved baseline synthesis\\nsource and matches HEAD; the synthesis tool version also matches.\\n\\nAll three reported candidate critical paths contain two short native carry\\nruns (8+8, 9+8, and 9+8 cells respectively), seven LUT4 logic arcs, and one\\nMUX2_LUT5 arc between launch and capture. The added selection/prefix logic and\\nits routing outweigh the shorter individual carry chains in these reports.\\nThe divider remains the reported limit; the ordinary ALU did not become the\\ncritical path. Shorter contiguous carry chains do not imply a shorter complete\\nfour-restoring-step combinational path.\\n\\nThe preceding result-bank experiment documented missing LUT3 timing arcs and\\nincomplete forwarding/writeback coverage. One supplemental seed-1 route added\\nonly JSON timing and SDF exports to the same route options; it reproduced\\n108.23 MHz and a 9.240 ns reported path. Its SDF has no IOPATH arcs for any of\\nthe 32 LUT3 result-bank muxes, and its detailed JSON lists zero D endpoints\\namong the 32 MEM/WB alu_result flops. The coverage gap therefore persists in\\nthe candidate. All Fmax and rate numbers above are the unchanged evaluator's\\nreported measurements, not proof of complete physical timing closure.\\nMissing arcs are not treated as zero-delay paths, and the headline regression\\ndoes not establish a fully characterized forwarding-path timing comparison.\\n\\nThe official fixture uses aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10, and approximately\\n22% random instruction/data bus stalls. Its 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles\\nmatch the starting experiment log exactly: start=15,801, stop=4,507,286.\\nAll five official CRC checks pass: seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714,\\ncrcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. It finishes at EBREAK without OOB\\naccess and prints the literal Correct operation validated.\\n\\nSeparately, an unmodified scratch copy of the benchmark was built through its\\nexisting Makefile with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3. The requested\\naggregate 6 KB, ten-iteration validation passes: seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, the validation literal, both\\nmarkers, EBREAK, and no OOB access. Start=56,393 and stop=32,893,064 give\\n32,836,671 bracketed cycles, also identical to the starting log's supplemental\\n6 KB measurement. At the candidate's reported median Fmax this is 32.96\\niterations/s. It is separate from the official 2 KB fitness of 240.97.\\nThe unmodified UART compiler-flags description says -O2; the captured build\\ncommands use -O3. UART CoreMark Size reports the per-algorithm size, not the\\naggregate TOTAL_DATA_SIZE supplied to the compiler.\\n\\nValidation builds and generated evidence are isolated under:\\n\\n`$TMPDIR/div-carry-select-zed8wek3`\\n\\nEvidence includes unit-tests.log and runner XMLs, formal-console.log and all\\nper-check logs, sub33-sat-proof.log, cosim-gate.log, fpga-eval.log,\\nfpga-result.json, cores/bench/generated/synth.json and pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log,\\ncarry-chain-audit.json, reported-path-audit.json, coremark-2k.log,\\ncoremark-2k-result.json, and sixk/build.log, sim.log, and result.json.\\ntiming-seed1/ contains the supplemental route log, routed netlist, timing.json,\\ndesign.sdf, bank-mux-cells.json, and coverage-audit.json.\\nverified-source-sha256.json checks 120 staged source/contract files and exact\\nagreement between the final formal RTL and submitted RTL. Scratch staging\\navoids writes through the worktree's shared formal/riscv-formal symlink.\\nOnly bench RTL, bench test_*.py files, and this explicitly requested note are\\nchanged in the submitted worktree. Orchestrator acceptance is independent of\\nthese local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:49:44.510548Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r3s0 (regression, -35.87%): In div_unit.sv's four-step restoring divider, 8/8/8/9-bit carry-select subtraction shortened native carry chains but added prefix/mux logic and routing across serial steps, reducing reported median Fmax 35.87% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two radix-4 quotient stages per divider clock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two previous structural experiments isolated division and then retimed\\nits result bank. The current winner reports 375.78 fitness, 168.78 MHz\\nmedian Fmax, and 5470 LUT4s. Its saved routed reports place the critical\\npath inside the divider for seeds 1 and 3: quotient/divisor registers to\\nresult_q in 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Each compute interval currently cascades\\nfour dependent restoring subtract/select steps, and the last interval\\nalso performs sign correction. The normal execute control/result path\\nlimits seed 2 at 5.925 ns, so divider acceleration alone has a competing\\nceiling and a large frequency gain is not assured.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and log.jsonl contain no previous radix restructuring attempt.\\nThey show that adding a completion clock violates the unchanged liveness\\nwindow, and that altering ALU selection can disrupt multiplier sharing.\\nA divider-local topology change can reduce serial arithmetic depth while\\npreserving the existing compute count, ordinary instruction latency,\\nsingle multiplier, and direct registered memory-address path. Prior\\nprofiles found no DIV/REM inside either measured CoreMark timing bracket.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two previous structural experiments isolated division and then retimed\\nits result bank. The current winner reports 375.78 fitness, 168.78 MHz\\nmedian Fmax, and 5470 LUT4s. Its saved routed reports place the critical\\npath inside the divider for seeds 1 and 3: quotient/divisor registers to\\nresult_q in 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Each compute interval currently cascades\\nfour dependent restoring subtract/select steps, and the last interval\\nalso performs sign correction. The normal execute control/result path\\nlimits seed 2 at 5.925 ns, so divider acceleration alone has a competing\\nceiling and a large frequency gain is not assured.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and log.jsonl contain no previous radix restructuring attempt.\\nThey show that adding a completion clock violates the unchanged liveness\\nwindow, and that altering ALU selection can disrupt multiplier sharing.\\nA divider-local topology change can reduce serial arithmetic depth while\\npreserving the existing compute count, ordinary instruction latency,\\nsingle multiplier, and direct registered memory-address path. Prior\\nprofiles found no DIV/REM inside either measured CoreMark timing bracket.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the four cascaded radix-2 restoring steps in div_unit.sv with\\ntwo cascaded radix-4 stages. Each stage generates two quotient bits using\\nthree parallel trial subtractions. This is one structural change to the\\nshared divider datapath; retain the existing blocking pipeline protocol\\nand the request edge followed by eight compute edges for every DIV/REM.\\n\\nAt request acceptance, normalize the signed operands exactly as today.\\nRegister the unsigned divisor magnitude D and its 34-bit multiple 3D;\\nform 2D by a zero-extended shift of registered D. Compute 3D as an\\nexplicitly widened shift plus add on the existing request edge, without\\na multiplier or an additional setup state. Keep these values stable\\nwhile busy. Preserve the original dividend, operand sign flags,\\nzero-divisor flag, and quotient-versus-remainder selection.\\n\\nFor each radix-4 stage, form the 34-bit trial T = {R, Q[31:30]}.\\nIn parallel compute the 35-bit zero-extended differences T-D, T-2D,\\nand T-3D. Difference bit 34 is the unsigned underflow indicator;\\nneither bit 31 nor a truncated divisor multiple is a valid substitute.\\nSelect the largest digit d in 0..3 whose subtraction does not underflow.\\nUse mutually exclusive selection enables derived from these three\\nunderflow bits and a shallow result mux, avoiding a serial chain of\\ncompare-then-subtract operations. Select R_next = T-d*D from the already\\ncomputed differences, or T for digit zero; the d*D notation describes\\nthat selection and must not infer another multiplier or subtractor.\\nAppend d to Q with Q_next = {Q[29:0], d[1:0]}. Cascade exactly two such\\nstages before updating quotient_q and remainder_q on each compute edge.\\nFor nonzero D, 0 <= R_next < D guarantees that the selected remainder\\nfits 32 bits despite the wider trial and difference calculations.\\n\\nAfter eight compute edges all 32 quotient bits have been generated.\\nKeep the existing final-interval sign correction and explicit divisor-zero\\noverride, including INT_MIN/-1, truncation toward zero, and the dividend's\\nsign for REM. Zero-divisor requests use the same fixed schedule. Preserve\\nthe live final-cycle response, acceptance on the eighth compute edge,\\nheld-response behavior, simultaneous held-response consumption/request\\nrefill, and reset cancellation. result_q must still update only on\\ncompletion and survive consumption and the next request so the existing\\nEX/MEM result-bank ownership remains valid.\\n\\nKeep the four RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS subtraction/XOR results unchanged and\\nrelease them on exactly the same control schedule as real arithmetic.\\nThe existing ALU, EX/MEM bank mux, dedicated normal memory-address bank,\\nforwarding, load-use interlock, bus holds, decoder/trap discipline,\\nretirement boundary, and nret=1 top-level ports require no redesign.\\nConsecutive divides must remain nine clocks apart; do not add a finish\\nstate, change formal depths, or weaken any existing assertion.\\n\\nThe intended benefit is less combinational depth between divider\\nregisters with identical instruction-cycle behavior. A proposal-level\\ninteger model checked both signed and unsigned quotient/remainder results\\nand per-digit bounds for 40800 operand cases; this is arithmetic design\\nevidence, not RTL validation or measured fitness. Estimate a 3 percent\\nfitness gain. Additional parallel subtractors, divisor-multiple setup\\ndelay, digit-selection routing, and the competing normal EX path can\\nerase that gain. Keep the complete core below the 10000-LUT target.\\n\\nValidate with existing and extended real/ALTOPS arithmetic tests, direct\\nEX bank-ownership tests, unchanged formal and cosim gates, and unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA evaluation. Inspect request-to-divisor-multiple paths,\\nboth radix stages, terminal sign correction, and normal EX paths. The\\nprior result-bank implementation notes flag missing timing arcs; retain\\nthat limitation explicitly and do not claim that an unreported path has\\nzero delay or change the timing model or constraints. Compare identical\\nbenchmark binaries and marker-bracketed cycles. Keep the evaluator's\\nrecorded 2 KB fitness separate from the requested 6 KB CRC-validated run;\\nbenchmark sources, evaluator constants, and all contract files stay fixed.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two radix-4 stages with parallel widened trial\\nsubtractions, explicit digit selection, and a request-registered\\n3D magnitude. Retain the module ports, eight-compute-edge counter,\\nfinal/held response protocol, registered-result ownership, signed\\nand exceptional-case semantics, and identical ALTOPS scheduling.\\nKeep all divisor multiples wide enough for unsigned 0xffffffff.\\nDo not modify the ordinary ALU or EX/MEM memory-address routing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic and exact handshake-timing assertion.\\nAdd DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases stressing quotient digits 0 through 3,\\nboundaries around D, 2D and 3D, repeated and alternating quotient\\ndigits, powers of two, high unsigned operands, and signed extrema.\\nInclude divisors around 0x40000000, 0x55555555, 0x80000000 and\\n0xffffffff to expose multiple-width and underflow errors. Compare\\nagainst the independent integer reference. Exercise changing live\\noperands, held responses, consume/refill, and reset with distinct\\ndivisor multiples; retain the same latency checks in ALTOPS builds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add compact RVFI/ISS-checked streams using the new arithmetic boundary\\noperands, including load-produced operands, consecutive dependent\\nDIV/REM operations and immediate arithmetic, branch and store\\nconsumers. Exercise instruction/data waits and reset without changing\\nexisting assertions. Require correct raw RVFI operands, one accepted\\nmemory effect per request, consecutive retirement orders, nine-clock\\nzero-wait spacing for consecutive divides, and unchanged ordinary\\ninstruction spacing. Run the existing direct EX result-bank tests\\nunchanged to check terminal acceptance and prolonged ownership holds.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two radix-4 stages with parallel widened trial\\nsubtractions, explicit digit selection, and a request-registered\\n3D magnitude. Retain the module ports, eight-compute-edge counter,\\nfinal/held response protocol, registered-result ownership, signed\\nand exceptional-case semantics, and identical ALTOPS scheduling.\\nKeep all divisor multiples wide enough for unsigned 0xffffffff.\\nDo not modify the ordinary ALU or EX/MEM memory-address routing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic and exact handshake-timing assertion.\\nAdd DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases stressing quotient digits 0 through 3,\\nboundaries around D, 2D and 3D, repeated and alternating quotient\\ndigits, powers of two, high unsigned operands, and signed extrema.\\nInclude divisors around 0x40000000, 0x55555555, 0x80000000 and\\n0xffffffff to expose multiple-width and underflow errors. Compare\\nagainst the independent integer reference. Exercise changing live\\noperands, held responses, consume/refill, and reset with distinct\\ndivisor multiples; retain the same latency checks in ALTOPS builds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add compact RVFI/ISS-checked streams using the new arithmetic boundary\\noperands, including load-produced operands, consecutive dependent\\nDIV/REM operations and immediate arithmetic, branch and store\\nconsumers. Exercise instruction/data waits and reset without changing\\nexisting assertions. Require correct raw RVFI operands, one accepted\\nmemory effect per request, consecutive retirement orders, nine-clock\\nzero-wait spacing for consecutive divides, and unchanged ordinary\\ninstruction spacing. Run the existing direct EX result-bank tests\\nunchanged to check terminal acceptance and prolonged ownership holds.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 159.15, \"delta_pct\": -57.65, \"vs_baseline\": -43.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 71.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5651, \"ff\": 2127, \"seeds\": [71.47, 71.48, 71.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the two-radix-4-stage divider in cores/bench. Functional validation\\npasses, but the unchanged three-seed evaluator reports 159.15 fitness at\\n71.48 MHz median Fmax, versus the current winner's recorded 375.78 and\\n168.78 MHz: a 57.65% regression. The estimated 3% gain was not achieved.\\nThe implementation is retained as the requested hypothesis experiment;\\nno evaluator, timing model, constraints, or acceptance checks were changed.\\n\\nOnly div_unit.sv changed in RTL. Each compute interval now cascades exactly\\ntwo radix-4 stages. Each stage forms a 34-bit trial, performs three parallel\\n35-bit zero-extended subtractions, and uses bit 34 for unsigned underflow.\\nMutually exclusive digit enables select the already-computed remainder\\nthrough a masked OR mux and append two quotient bits. The divisor magnitude\\nand its explicitly widened 34-bit shift-plus-add 3D are captured on the\\nexisting request edge. 2D is a zero-extended shift of registered D. There is\\nno additional setup/completion state, arithmetic multiplier, or subtraction\\nafter digit selection.\\n\\nThe counter, request/response equations, terminal-edge result capture, held\\nresponse, simultaneous held consumption/refill, reset cancellation, signed\\nnormalization, final sign correction, divisor-zero override, and four ALTOPS\\nformulas retain their original control schedule. result_q updates only on\\ncompletion, apart from reset, and survives consumption and the next request.\\nINT_MIN/-1, signed truncation toward zero, and the dividend sign for remainder\\nare preserved. Zero-divisor operations take the same eight compute edges.\\nThe ALU, EX result-bank selection, direct normal memory-address bank,\\nforwarding, hazards, traps, retirement boundary, core.yaml and nret=1 ports\\nare unchanged. There were no architectural deviations from the proposal.\\n\\nThe existing tests and assertions were retained. test_alu.py adds 4,216\\noperand/operation cases per arithmetic mode covering radix-4 digit patterns,\\nD/2D/3D boundaries, powers of two, high unsigned values, signed extrema,\\nand divisors around 0x40000000, 0x55555555, 0x80000000 and 0xffffffff.\\nThe real build checks each clock's quotient/remainder against integer\\ndivision of the processed dividend prefix, including R < D, and checks that\\nD and full-width 3D remain captured while live operands change. Both real\\nand ALTOPS builds check the same exact response timing and bank ownership.\\nAdditional cases exercise distinct divisor multiples across held responses,\\nsimultaneous consume/refill and resets before, during and after computation.\\n\\ntest_pipeline.py adds boundary streams using load-produced operands,\\nconsecutive dependent divides, immediate arithmetic, branch and store\\nconsumers, killed divides, combined instruction/data waits, forced\\ntwelve-clock memory holds, and reset with wide divisor multiples. They use\\nthe unchanged RVFI/ISS comparator and independent accepted-bus-effect count.\\nAssertions retain raw operands, consecutive retirement orders, nine-clock\\nspacing for adjacent divides and one-clock ordinary consumer spacing.\\ntest_ex_result_banks.py was run unchanged in real and ALTOPS modes, including\\nterminal acceptance and prolonged ownership/response holds.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- The exact requested Verilator glob-order lint command passes without\\n  warnings, as does its RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS variant. git diff --check passes.\\n- Formal status: PASS on the first invocation, 53 passed and 0 failed.\\n  No counterexamples or formal fix attempts occurred. The unchanged\\n  bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg from\\n  CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl. Depths and liveness assertions\\n  were unchanged. This is ALTOPS formal; formal-deep was not run, so no\\n  exhaustive formal proof of real divider arithmetic is claimed.\\n- All eight pytest runners pass: 134 cocotb tests, comprising ALU real and\\n  ALTOPS (33 each), decoder (30), immediate generator (8), register file (5),\\n  pipeline (21), and unchanged direct EX ownership tests (2 per mode).\\n- The unchanged cosim gate passes both selftest and CoreMark. Twelve extra\\n  selftest runs pass all 115 RVFI retirements with no waits, instruction-only\\n  waits, data-only waits, and combined waits at seeds 1, 42 and 99.\\n- A Yosys structural audit finds exactly six 35-bit trial subtractors. The\\n  remaining four 32-bit subtractors implement operand normalization and\\n  final sign correction. The divider has no multiply, divide or modulo\\n  cells. Full FPGA synthesis retains one MULT36X36 and four block RAMs.\\n\\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv\\nevaluation completed all three routes. Synthesis used the previous winner's\\ninstalled Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61), and routing\\nused nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. The design uses 5,651 LUT4 and 2,127 DFF,\\nversus 5,470 and 2,111 in the winner's record. LUT use increased 3.31% and\\nremains below the 10,000-LUT target.\\n\\n| Seed | Final Fmax MHz | Reported critical delay ns | Critical path |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 71.47 | 13.991 | remainder_q[4] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\\n| 2 | 71.48 | 13.989 | divisor_three_q[2] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\\n| 3 | 71.96 | 13.896 | divisor_three_q[7] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\\n\\nThree supplemental routes using identical synthesis, seeds and constraints\\nexported detailed JSON and SDF reports and reproduced the gate frequencies.\\nOn the reported critical paths, the first radix-stage selection is reached\\nat cumulative delays 5.260/4.785/5.163 ns, the second at\\n9.601/10.113/10.021 ns, and the terminal result then crosses remainder sign\\ncorrection and result selection. Arithmetic-cell propagation contributes\\n5.734/5.369/5.579 ns, while routing contributes 5.851/6.142/5.839 ns.\\nThe source-level reduction in dependent subtract/select stages did not\\nproduce the intended benefit under this mapping and evaluation.\\n\\nTiming coverage remains incomplete. Request normalization and the 34-bit\\n3D setup adder were inspected in RTL and the mapped netlist, but none of\\nthe 34 3D-register or 32 D-register data endpoints appears in the detailed\\ntiming reports. Q/R update data endpoints are also absent. Both radix stages\\nand terminal sign correction are visible on the reported completion paths,\\nbut only 30 of 32 completion data endpoints and 7 of 32 normal EX result\\ndata endpoints are listed. No MEM/WB result data endpoint is listed.\\nAll 1,142 LUT3 cells lack exported SDF IOPATH arcs, including the 32 cells\\nimplementing the retained EX result-bank mux. Consequently the reports do\\nnot establish complete request, iteration, forwarding, writeback or normal\\nEX timing. An omitted path or cell arc is unknown, not zero delay. The\\nheadline fitness regression is the unchanged evaluator's result, not a\\nfully characterized physical timing comparison. No timing coverage gap was\\nhidden or addressed by changing the contract.\\n\\nBenchmark comparisons used freshly built starting-RTL and candidate\\nsimulations, with the identical ELF for both sides of each workload and the\\nunchanged approximately 22% random instruction/data stall model. The official\\nevaluator uses aggregate 2 KB, -O3 and ITERATIONS=10. The requested aggregate\\n6 KB run was built separately from unchanged sources using the existing\\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 build options.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting RTL timed cycles | Candidate timed cycles | Candidate iter/s at reported median Fmax |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 159.15 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 21.77 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB start/stop markers are 15,801/4,507,286; the 6 KB markers are\\n56,393/32,893,064. Both RTL versions produce identical complete benchmark\\nmarkers, including clean EBREAK completion, no OOB access, ten iterations,\\nand the literal Correct operation validated. All five official 2 KB CRC\\nchecks pass. Both 6 KB runs report seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47 and crcfinal=5949. The unchanged UART compiler\\nflags string says -O2; the actual build commands used -O3. The supplemental\\n6 KB result is separate from the evaluator's recorded 2 KB fitness.\\n\\nAll build artifacts and unchanged scratch copies are under:\\n\\n$TMPDIR/radix4-r3s1-_xy90800\\n\\nEvidence includes formal-console.log, formal/last_run-*.log, unit-tests.log,\\ncocotb result XMLs, cosim-gate.log, stalled-cosim.json, fpga-eval.log,\\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log, divider-structure.json,\\ncoremark-comparison.json, candidate-coremark-*.log, baseline-coremark-*.log,\\ntiming/seed*/timing.json, timing/seed*/design.sdf and timing/summary.json.\\nverified-source-sha256.json verifies 73 staged source/contract files against\\nthe submitted worktree; the final formal RTL also matches. Scratch staging\\nwas necessary because the worktree's formal/riscv-formal is a shared symlink.\\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv, the two bench test_*.py files and this\\nexplicitly requested notes file changed in the submitted worktree.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:51:37.449932Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r3s1 (regression, -57.65%): Halving divider quotient stages does not guarantee better mapped timing: two radix-4 stages in div_unit.sv cascaded 35-bit trial subtraction and underflow-driven masked-OR selection into terminal sign correction, yielding a reported ~14 ns completion path with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Stateless backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch currently chooses PC+4 and EX redirects every taken branch or jump.\\nSince IF/ID is combinational, each normally advancing taken transfer\\ndiscards one useful fetch/decode slot. LESSONS.md and the predictor entries\\nin experiments/log.jsonl show that both 16-entry tagged BTBs reduced timed\\nCoreMark cycles by about 6.1%, but added timing and area costs. In\\nhyp-20260906-003-r2s2, registered lookup metadata removed table-read training\\nfeedback, yet EX qualification and predictor write-enable fanout still\\nreduced median Fmax by 13.07%. The current winner is 375.78 iter/sec at\\n168.78 MHz. A stateless predictor can capture recurring direct transfers\\nwithout table lookup, training fanout, or full-next-PC comparison in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch currently chooses PC+4 and EX redirects every taken branch or jump.\\nSince IF/ID is combinational, each normally advancing taken transfer\\ndiscards one useful fetch/decode slot. LESSONS.md and the predictor entries\\nin experiments/log.jsonl show that both 16-entry tagged BTBs reduced timed\\nCoreMark cycles by about 6.1%, but added timing and area costs. In\\nhyp-20260906-003-r2s2, registered lookup metadata removed table-read training\\nfeedback, yet EX qualification and predictor write-enable fanout still\\nreduced median Fmax by 13.07%. The current winner is 375.78 iter/sec at\\n168.78 MHz. A stateless predictor can capture recurring direct transfers\\nwithout table lookup, training fanout, or full-next-PC comparison in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor with direct JAL\\nprediction to cores/bench. Use the current combinational fetch/decode\\nboundary; do not add a pipeline stage. Predict legal conditional branches\\nwith negative B-immediates as taken, and predict legal direct JALs as taken\\nin either direction. Predict all other instructions sequentially, including\\nJALR. This is a single stateless direct-control predictor: no BTB, history\\ntable, direction counters, return stack, or training interface.\\n\\nIn if_stage, predecode the raw imem_data independently of flushed output,\\nstall, and redirect. Recognize BRANCH opcode 0x63 only for funct3 values\\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, and 7; its immediate sign is instruction bit 31. Recognize\\nJAL opcode 0x6f. Require an aligned fetch PC and aligned displacement before\\npredicting taken. B-immediate bit 1 is instruction bit 8; J-immediate bit 1\\nis instruction bit 21; bit 0 is zero for both. Check these low bits directly\\nrather than putting a wide target-adder result on the prediction-enable\\npath. Reserved branch encodings and all JALRs predict PC+4. This predecode\\nonly chooses speculation; the existing decoder remains authoritative for\\ninstruction legality, including the default-illegal and SYSTEM rules.\\n\\nConstruct the sign-extended B/J displacement from instruction wires and\\nselect it before one PC-relative target adder. Compute PC+4 independently.\\nPreserve full 32-bit address arithmetic and modulo-2^32 carry behavior;\\ndo not truncate addresses to the benchmark's memory size. On an accepted\\nfetch, choose the aligned direct target when predicted taken, otherwise\\nPC+4. Keep imem_addr driven directly by the PC register. A prediction must\\nnot flush the instruction that generated it.\\n\\nAdd only a predicted_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Capture it on the\\nsame edge as the instruction whose prediction actually advances the PC.\\nHold the bit with ID/EX through dmem and divider stalls; clear it with\\nreset, flushes, and invalid advancing bubbles. No predicted target or\\n32-bit predicted_next_pc register is needed: every predicted target is\\nthe exact PC-relative target of that same captured instruction, so there\\nare no target aliases or stale table entries to repair. Preserve that\\ncorrespondence even when instruction data changes while a fetch is held.\\n\\nEX retains its forwarded branch comparison, full target calculation,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and existing misalignment/trap continuation.\\nDefine resolved_taken as an aligned, nontrapping taken branch or jump.\\nOn in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait, redirect only when\\npredicted_taken differs from resolved_taken. This one-bit comparison\\nreplaces the unconditional taken-transfer redirect without adding a\\nfull-next-PC equality cone. Correctly predicted branches and direct JALs\\ndo not flush. A backward-branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken\\nforward branch repairs to its target. An aligned JALR remains unpredicted\\nand uses its existing EX redirect, even when its target happens to equal\\nPC+4. A misaligned transfer retains its existing trap and PC+4 continuation.\\nUse the architectural resolved next PC for RVFI and repairs; preserve\\nJAL/JALR link writes and their suppression on a target-alignment trap.\\n\\nReset has highest PC priority, then an eligible EX repair, then an accepted\\nfetch prediction, otherwise hold. Repairs are deferred while EX is held\\nand override imem backpressure when EX advances. Preserve load-use bubbles,\\nimem invalidation, dmem holds and retained WB forwarding data, and divider\\nrequest/completion behavior. Keep the dmem-hold equations independent of\\nredirect, using separate assignments if necessary to avoid combinational\\nscheduling loops when redirect is qualified by EX advancement. A repair\\nmust squash the younger slot before it can retire, store, or launch DIV/REM.\\n\\nExpected net fitness improvement is about 4%, not a measured result. A\\nnominal 5% cycle reduction with a 1% Fmax reduction gives about 4.2% higher\\nthroughput. Static direction errors may reduce that cycle benefit, and\\nthe new instruction-to-PC target-adder path may become critical. Inspect\\nall three routed timing reports and compare marker-window cycles times\\nmedian Fmax against the current winner; cycle savings alone are not success.\\nRun the unchanged formal, cosim, canonical CRC, UART, OOB, marker-window,\\nand three-seed FPGA gates. Keep any supplemental workload results distinct\\nfrom official fitness. Preserve nret=1, every invariant, the existing\\ndivider/result banks and direct registered memory-address path, and all\\nevaluation files. Only the predictor integration and its tests are in scope.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement raw-instruction static BRANCH/JAL predecode, cheap low-bit\\nalignment qualification, one full-width B/J target adder, and the\\naccepted-fetch next-PC selection. Emit predicted_taken with the\\ninstruction. Keep raw prediction independent of flush/stall control,\\nretain direct registered imem_addr, and prioritize reset and EX repair.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Keep the\\nexisting resolved pc_next fields and all architectural/RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predicted_taken with its accepted instruction, retain it during\\nholds, and clear it with reset, flushes, and invalid advancing bubbles.\\nPreserve the existing decoder and regfile interfaces and control rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare saved predicted_taken with the actual aligned transfer outcome\\nonly when EX advances. Repair to the resolved target or PC+4 as needed;\\npreserve branch forwarding, JAL/JALR link results, trap behavior,\\ndivider protocol, separate result banks, and architectural RVFI pc_next.\\nAvoid a full predicted-next-PC comparator and any training logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate redirect as a qualified static-prediction repair. Preserve\\nall existing stall and bubble behavior, with no correct-prediction\\nflush. Express dmem hold independently of redirect to avoid a control\\nloop, and document the actual single younger combinational fetch slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward-loop/exit, forward taken/not-taken, aligned JAL\\nin both directions, self-JAL, and forwarded/changing JALR cases. Check\\nreserved BRANCH encodings, misaligned direct targets, sign extension,\\nand trap continuation. Exercise load-dependent branches, simultaneous\\nimem unavailability and repair, long dmem/divider holds, reset, and\\na repair overriding a younger prediction. Assert accepted prediction\\nmetadata, no bubble on correct direct predictions, exact RVFI PC/order\\ncontinuity and link data, and no wrong-path stores or divider launches.\\nRetain existing assertions and use the unchanged ISS for comparisons.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the test's packed ID/EX layout and instruction constructors for\\nthe new prediction bit, initializing it sequential for non-control\\ninstructions. Preserve every existing divider, forwarding, result-bank,\\nand registered-memory-address assertion so predictor integration cannot\\ninvalidate coverage of the current winning datapath.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement raw-instruction static BRANCH/JAL predecode, cheap low-bit\\nalignment qualification, one full-width B/J target adder, and the\\naccepted-fetch next-PC selection. Emit predicted_taken with the\\ninstruction. Keep raw prediction independent of flush/stall control,\\nretain direct registered imem_addr, and prioritize reset and EX repair.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Keep the\\nexisting resolved pc_next fields and all architectural/RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predicted_taken with its accepted instruction, retain it during\\nholds, and clear it with reset, flushes, and invalid advancing bubbles.\\nPreserve the existing decoder and regfile interfaces and control rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare saved predicted_taken with the actual aligned transfer outcome\\nonly when EX advances. Repair to the resolved target or PC+4 as needed;\\npreserve branch forwarding, JAL/JALR link results, trap behavior,\\ndivider protocol, separate result banks, and architectural RVFI pc_next.\\nAvoid a full predicted-next-PC comparator and any training logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate redirect as a qualified static-prediction repair. Preserve\\nall existing stall and bubble behavior, with no correct-prediction\\nflush. Express dmem hold independently of redirect to avoid a control\\nloop, and document the actual single younger combinational fetch slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward-loop/exit, forward taken/not-taken, aligned JAL\\nin both directions, self-JAL, and forwarded/changing JALR cases. Check\\nreserved BRANCH encodings, misaligned direct targets, sign extension,\\nand trap continuation. Exercise load-dependent branches, simultaneous\\nimem unavailability and repair, long dmem/divider holds, reset, and\\na repair overriding a younger prediction. Assert accepted prediction\\nmetadata, no bubble on correct direct predictions, exact RVFI PC/order\\ncontinuity and link data, and no wrong-path stores or divider launches.\\nRetain existing assertions and use the unchanged ISS for comparisons.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the test's packed ID/EX layout and instruction constructors for\\nthe new prediction bit, initializing it sequential for non-control\\ninstructions. Preserve every existing divider, forwarding, result-bank,\\nand registered-memory-address assertion so predictor integration cannot\\ninvalidate coverage of the current winning datapath.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 361.83, \"delta_pct\": -3.71, \"vs_baseline\": 27.94, \"fmax_mhz\": 154.13, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5548, \"ff\": 2092, \"seeds\": [157.95, 153.59, 154.13], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented stateless backward-taken/forward-not-taken branch prediction and\\ndirect-JAL prediction. Local correctness gates pass, but measured throughput\\nregresses: 361.83 iter/s versus the current winner's 375.78 iter/s (-3.71%).\\nThe expected four-percent improvement was not achieved.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- IF predecodes raw instruction data independently of flush, stall, and\\n  redirect. Only legal BRANCH funct3 values with a negative displacement,\\n  and JAL in either direction, can predict taken. Fetch-PC alignment and\\n  instruction bits 8/21 qualify alignment before the target adder.\\n- One selected, sign-extended B/J displacement feeds one full-width\\n  PC-relative adder. PC+4 is independent. Both preserve modulo-2^32 address\\n  arithmetic; imem_addr remains directly driven by the PC register.\\n- Added only predicted_taken to IF/ID and ID/EX. ID captures it with the\\n  accepted instruction, holds it through memory/divider stalls, and clears\\n  it on reset, flush, and invalid advancing bubbles. No table, training\\n  interface, saved target, or saved next-PC was added.\\n- EX compares the saved bit with the aligned, nontrapping transfer outcome\\n  only on in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. Repairs and RVFI share\\n  the architectural resolved next PC. Correct predictions do not flush;\\n  backward exits repair to PC+4. JALR remains unpredicted, including a\\n  target equal to PC+4. Link writes and alignment-trap suppression remain.\\n- Separated the dmem hold assignments from redirect-dependent hazard logic.\\n  Reset takes PC priority, followed by eligible EX repair, accepted fetch,\\n  and hold. An EX repair squashes the single younger combinational slot.\\n- Updated the packed EX test layout and added pipeline and standalone IF\\n  coverage. The decoder, divider, ALU/result banks, direct registered dmem\\n  address path, core top-level ports, nret=1, and evaluation files are intact.\\n\\nValidation on the submitted RTL:\\n\\n- Requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings, both normal and ALTOPS modes.\\n  git diff --check: PASS.\\n- Local formal: PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed. No failed\\n  checks, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged run_all.sh used\\n  wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg. formal-deep was not run; divider and\\n  multiplier RTL were not changed.\\n- All nine pytest runners pass, totaling 135 cocotb cases. The initial full\\n  suite passed, then the added IF suite and final pipeline reset coverage\\n  passed. Every pre-existing assertion remains.\\n- Directed tests cover all six branch conditions in both directions and\\n  both outcomes, backward loops/exits, forward branches, JAL both directions,\\n  self-JAL, forwarded/changing JALR, and JALR targeting PC+4. They check\\n  reserved branches, illegal JALR/SYSTEM, target-alignment traps and link\\n  suppression, B/J sign extension, full-address carry/wrap, and unaligned PCs.\\n- Protocol checks compare each accepted prediction with the captured\\n  instruction and actual PC advance. They verify ID/EX holds and bubbles,\\n  fourteen-cycle memory holds with changing instruction data, retained WB\\n  forwarding, load-dependent branches, divider waits/resets, repair despite\\n  unavailable imem, and repair overriding a younger direct prediction.\\n  Correct direct predictions have no added retirement bubble. RVFI order,\\n  PC continuity, links, and all compared fields match the unchanged ISS;\\n  accepted bus requests and divider launches exclude wrong-path operations.\\n- Unchanged make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1: PASS for selftest and CoreMark.\\n  CoreMark reached EBREAK, passed canonical UART/CRC checks, produced valid\\n  start/stop markers, and reported no OOB access.\\n\\nFPGA measurement uses the unchanged single-issue wrapper, constraints,\\nsynthesis script, three seeds, and marker-window fitness calculation. The\\nfirst complete gate used Yosys 0.64+149 (2dc69a757-dirty) and\\nnextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. A second complete gate used the winner's recorded Yosys 0.64\\n(d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and the same nextpnr. Both synthesized\\nidentical cell types, parameters, connectivity, and net-bit mappings. Both\\ngates completed all three routes successfully and returned identical\\nper-seed Fmax, resources, marker-window cycles, and fitness.\\n\\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Critical path | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\\n| 1 | 157.95 | ID/EX register through EX selection to EX/MEM data | 3.69 / 2.64 |\\n| 2 | 153.59 | ID/EX register through hazard control to ID/EX reset | 2.67 / 3.84 |\\n| 3 | 154.13 | ID/EX register through forwarding/request control to divider operand CE | 1.67 / 4.82 |\\n\\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected. The endpoints\\nare respectively cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_31.D,\\ncpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_72.RESET, and cpu.u_ex.div_rs2_q_DFFRE_Q_6.CE.\\nThe reports show approximately 6.33, 6.51, and 6.49 ns total delay. None of\\nthese reported worst paths identifies the IF target-adder path as critical;\\nthe headline reports do not establish complete timing coverage of every\\nforwarding or memory path.\\n\\nMedian Fmax is 154.13 MHz versus 168.78 MHz (-8.68%). Resources are 5,548\\nLUT4, 2,092 DFF, and four block RAMs, versus 5,470 LUT4, 2,111 DFF, and four\\nblock RAMs for the winner. The cycle savings do not offset the Fmax loss.\\nThe candidate needs\\nmore than 160.07 MHz at its measured cycle count to exceed the winner.\\n\\nThe checked-in evaluator builds aggregate 2 KB CoreMark, despite the prompt's\\naggregate 6 KB description. Its unchanged CRC contract is seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The requested 6 KB\\nworkload was built separately with the unchanged Makefile's\\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 options. It is supplemental, not official\\nfitness. Each comparison below uses a fresh simulation of the starting\\nHEAD RTL and the identical ELF, ten iterations, and --istall --dstall.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Candidate cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / candidate iter/s |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3 | 4,491,485 | 4,259,699 | 5.16% | 375.78 / 361.83 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3 | 32,836,671 | 31,086,275 | 5.33% | 51.40 / 49.58 |\\n\\nThe rates use the winner's recorded 168.78 MHz and the candidate's measured\\n154.13 MHz. The 6 KB throughput also regresses, by 3.54%.\\n\\nMarker windows (start, stop): 2 KB starting (15,801, 4,507,286), candidate\\n(15,189, 4,274,888); 6 KB starting (56,393, 32,893,064), candidate\\n(54,040, 31,140,315). Both 6 KB UARTs contain seedcrc=0x8a02,\\ncrclist=0xd4b0, crcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, crcfinal=0x5949, ten\\niterations, and the literal \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\" Both complete\\nwith EBREAK, valid markers, and no OOB access. The unchanged UART compiler\\ndescription says -O2; actual build commands use -O3.\\n\\nAll generated artifacts and unchanged evaluation scratch copies are under\\n$TMPDIR/stateless-r3s2. Evidence includes formal-console.log, unit-tests.log,\\nprediction-tests.log, core test result XMLs, cosim-gate.log, fpga-gate.log,\\nstable/fpga-gate.log, both sets of cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log,\\ntiming-summary.json, workload-comparison.json, the individual CoreMark logs,\\nand sixk/build.log. verified-source-sha256.json verifies 145 staged source\\nfiles; the formal-stage RTL was also checked byte-for-byte against the\\nsubmitted RTL. Only scoped bench RTL/tests and this requested notes file\\nare modified in the worktree. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:48:06.406113Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r3s2 (regression, -3.71%): Removing BTB state is insufficient to recover predictor timing: stateless backward-taken/direct-JAL prediction with one-bit EX repair cut CoreMark cycles 5.16% but lost 8.68% median Fmax, with routed worst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\"}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Carry-free conditional sign correction at divider completion\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz with unchanged\\nCoreMark cycles. Its saved seed-1 timing path is 6.018 ns from quotient_q\\nto result_q, with 27 source references to div_unit.sv's terminal quotient\\nnegation. Seed 3 also ends at result_q, while seed 2 is limited by ordinary\\nEX result logic. This makes the final sign-correction cone a concrete,\\nnarrowly scoped timing target, although it cannot explain every limit.\\n\\nThe micro_opt history was checked: source-use predecode left measured\\nCoreMark cycles unchanged; ALU one-hot selection lost 5.98% and disrupted\\nmultiplier sharing; replacing all divider subtractors with block\\ncarry-select logic lost 35.87%. The radix-4 structural attempt also lost\\n57.65%. Those results argue for preserving the four native restoring\\nsubtractors and testing only the unary negation after them. The existing\\nterminal logic computes 0-minus-magnitude and then selects its sign.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz with unchanged\\nCoreMark cycles. Its saved seed-1 timing path is 6.018 ns from quotient_q\\nto result_q, with 27 source references to div_unit.sv's terminal quotient\\nnegation. Seed 3 also ends at result_q, while seed 2 is limited by ordinary\\nEX result logic. This makes the final sign-correction cone a concrete,\\nnarrowly scoped timing target, although it cannot explain every limit.\\n\\nThe micro_opt history was checked: source-use predecode left measured\\nCoreMark cycles unchanged; ALU one-hot selection lost 5.98% and disrupted\\nmultiplier sharing; replacing all divider subtractors with block\\ncarry-select logic lost 35.87%. The radix-4 structural attempt also lost\\n57.65%. Those results argue for preserving the four native restoring\\nsubtractors and testing only the unary negation after them. The existing\\nterminal logic computes 0-minus-magnitude and then selects its sign.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the two terminal conditional negations of quotient_step[4]\\nand remainder_step[4] with a combinational prefix-OR implementation of\\ntwo's-complement sign correction. For a 32-bit magnitude x and negate n,\\ny[0] = x[0], and for each i from 1 through 31,\\ny[i] = x[i] XOR (n AND OR(x[i-1:0])). Negation preserves the lowest set\\nbit and complements every higher bit, so this identity produces exactly\\nn ? ((0-x) modulo 2^32) : x, including zero and 0x80000000.\\n\\nBuild the lower-bit-nonzero terms with explicit balanced inclusive\\nprefix-OR levels at distances 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16, then use prefix[i-1]\\nin the final conditional XOR. Use constant generate indices and ordinary\\nsynthesizable logic, with no sequential prefix chain, arithmetic adder,\\ngeneral carry-select subtraction, vendor primitive, or timing attribute.\\nThe sign enable enters the final XOR mask, folding the old sign-selection\\nmux into the correction. This trades the terminal 32-bit negation carry\\ndependency for a shallow reduction network; routed timing must determine\\nwhether its additional LUTs and wiring are worthwhile on this FPGA.\\n\\nInstantiate the helper separately for quotient and remainder, using the\\nexisting dividend_negative_q XOR divisor_negative_q and\\ndividend_negative_q enables. Keep the quotient/remainder selection and\\ndivide-by-zero override unchanged. Preserve all four 33-bit restoring\\nsubtractions and restore muxes, input magnitude conversion, counters,\\neight compute clocks, terminal-edge response acceptance, result_q capture\\nand retention, held responses, reset cancellation, and ALTOPS formulas\\nand schedule. The EX/MEM divide bank and its ownership protocol, direct\\nnormal-bank memory address, ordinary ALU/multiplier, pipeline, hazards,\\ndecoder legality, and nret=1 IO/RVFI remain unchanged.\\n\\nPredict unchanged marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and an unmeasured\\nthree-percent fitness improvement through Fmax, with low confidence.\\nPrefix routing may erase the benefit, and the ordinary EX path or the\\nremaining restoring chain may set the new limit. Validate the helper\\nagainst mathematical modular negation, retain all real and ALTOPS\\ndivider protocol tests, and run unchanged lint, formal, cosim, and\\nthree-seed FPGA gates. Check mapped sign-correction logic, complete\\nreported paths, multiplier and block-RAM counts, and LUT headroom.\\nThe prior notes document missing timing arcs in result-bank forwarding;\\ndo not interpret absent paths as zero delay or proof of physical closure.\\nKeep the official evaluator intact and distinguish its logged 2 KB\\nresults from separate canonical 6 KB CRC and marker validation, as in\\nthe existing implementation notes.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_sign32.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a purely combinational 32-bit conditional-negation helper with\\nmagnitude, negate, and corrected ports. Implement the balanced\\nprefix-OR identity specified above, including the special bit-zero\\nassignment. Do not infer a ripple incrementer, add registers, or use\\nsynthesis/timing exceptions. Keep widths explicit and the logic\\ncompatible with the existing Yosys and Verilator frontends.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"In the real-arithmetic branch, drive quotient_signed and\\nremainder_signed from two div_sign32 instances and remove their old\\nconditional subtraction assignments. Leave the restoring recurrence,\\ninput normalization, completed_result selection/zero override, all\\nsequential logic, public interfaces, and ALTOPS branch unchanged.\\nOnly the terminal sign-correction implementation changes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_sign32.py\", \"description\": \"Add a standalone cocotb runner and compare both negate settings with\\nan independent Python oracle: (-x if negate else x) & 0xffffffff.\\nCover zero, all ones, INT_MIN, every one-hot bit and powers-of-two\\nneighbors, long runs of low zeros/ones, every possible lowest-set-bit\\nposition with varied upper bits, and randomized full-width values.\\nDo not reproduce the RTL prefix equations in the expected results.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_sign32.sv in both real and ALTOPS source lists. Retain\\nevery existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response, and registered\\nresult-bank assertion. Add mathematical DIV/REM vectors that yield\\nzero, signed extrema, powers-of-two and neighboring quotient or\\nremainder magnitudes, including negative dividends, both divisor\\nsigns, divide by zero, INT_MIN/-1, and high-bit unsigned operands.\\nExercise immediate and held acceptance with changing live inputs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add the helper to compilation sources for the existing real and\\nALTOPS ownership runners; retain all assertions and schedules.\\nRe-run held-owner, successive-result, forwarding, and reset coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add the helper to the existing pipeline runner's compilation sources\\nwithout changing any acceptance criteria. Re-run full RVFI operand,\\nretirement-order, divide-consumer, and combined bus-stall regressions\\nto establish unchanged integration and completion spacing.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_sign32.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a purely combinational 32-bit conditional-negation helper with\\nmagnitude, negate, and corrected ports. Implement the balanced\\nprefix-OR identity specified above, including the special bit-zero\\nassignment. Do not infer a ripple incrementer, add registers, or use\\nsynthesis/timing exceptions. Keep widths explicit and the logic\\ncompatible with the existing Yosys and Verilator frontends.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"In the real-arithmetic branch, drive quotient_signed and\\nremainder_signed from two div_sign32 instances and remove their old\\nconditional subtraction assignments. Leave the restoring recurrence,\\ninput normalization, completed_result selection/zero override, all\\nsequential logic, public interfaces, and ALTOPS branch unchanged.\\nOnly the terminal sign-correction implementation changes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_sign32.py\", \"description\": \"Add a standalone cocotb runner and compare both negate settings with\\nan independent Python oracle: (-x if negate else x) & 0xffffffff.\\nCover zero, all ones, INT_MIN, every one-hot bit and powers-of-two\\nneighbors, long runs of low zeros/ones, every possible lowest-set-bit\\nposition with varied upper bits, and randomized full-width values.\\nDo not reproduce the RTL prefix equations in the expected results.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_sign32.sv in both real and ALTOPS source lists. Retain\\nevery existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response, and registered\\nresult-bank assertion. Add mathematical DIV/REM vectors that yield\\nzero, signed extrema, powers-of-two and neighboring quotient or\\nremainder magnitudes, including negative dividends, both divisor\\nsigns, divide by zero, INT_MIN/-1, and high-bit unsigned operands.\\nExercise immediate and held acceptance with changing live inputs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add the helper to compilation sources for the existing real and\\nALTOPS ownership runners; retain all assertions and schedules.\\nRe-run held-owner, successive-result, forwarding, and reset coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add the helper to the existing pipeline runner's compilation sources\\nwithout changing any acceptance criteria. Re-run full RVFI operand,\\nretirement-order, divide-consumer, and combined bus-stall regressions\\nto establish unchanged integration and completion spacing.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 234.55, \"delta_pct\": -37.58, \"vs_baseline\": -17.07, \"fmax_mhz\": 105.35, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5411, \"ff\": 2116, \"seeds\": [105.35, 130.29, 104.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the requested carry-free terminal sign correction. Correctness\\nchecks pass, but the local three-seed FPGA result regresses: median Fmax is\\n105.35 MHz and official fitness is 234.55, versus the recorded winner's\\n168.78 MHz and 375.78. The fitness change is -37.58%. The predicted 3%\\nimprovement is not supported by this measurement. Marker-bracketed cycles\\nare exactly unchanged in both the official 2 KB and supplemental 6 KB runs.\\n\\nChanges and scope:\\n\\n- Added `cores/bench/rtl/div_sign32.sv`, with explicit inclusive prefix-OR\\n  levels at distances 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16, constant generate indices,\\n  `corrected[0] = magnitude[0]`, and the conditional XOR for bits 1..31.\\n  Prefix vectors cover bits 0..30 because no output needs the inclusive\\n  prefix through bit 31. This omits unused logic without changing the\\n  specified identity or depth. There are no arithmetic operators on signal\\n  values, registers, vendor primitives, or synthesis/timing attributes.\\n- Instantiated separate quotient and remainder helpers inside the real\\n  arithmetic branch of `div_unit.sv`; removed only the two terminal\\n  conditional subtraction assignments. The four 33-bit restoring\\n  subtractions, restore muxes, input magnitude conversion, result selection,\\n  divide-by-zero override, sequential logic, eight compute edges, response\\n  protocol, and ALTOPS formulas/schedule are unchanged.\\n- Added a standalone cocotb helper runner using the independent Python\\n  oracle `(-x if negate else x) & 0xffffffff`. It checks 1,105 structured\\n  magnitudes and 8,192 randomized values, each with both negate settings:\\n  18,594 comparisons. Coverage includes zero, all ones, INT_MIN, all one-hot\\n  bits and their neighbors, long low zero/one runs, and every possible\\n  lowest-set-bit position with varied upper bits.\\n- Added mathematical quotient/remainder boundary vectors to `test_alu.py`:\\n  1,592 signed operand pairs and 472 unsigned pairs, each checked with both\\n  relevant operations and immediate versus three-cycle held acceptance.\\n  These add 8,256 transactions per real/ALTOPS build. Live opcode and operands\\n  change during computation and response holding; blocked requests cannot\\n  disturb the completed bank. Existing assertions and tests were retained.\\n- Added the helper to both ALU runners, both EX result-bank runners, and the\\n  pipeline runner. No other RTL changed. EX/MEM ownership, its direct normal\\n  memory-address bank, ordinary ALU/multiplier, pipeline, hazards, legality,\\n  and the nret=1 I/O/RVFI contract remain unchanged.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- The exact requested Verilator lint command passes without warnings.\\n  The same lint command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` also passes.\\n- All nine pytest runners pass: 132 cocotb tests, no failures or skips.\\n  This includes every existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic/protocol test,\\n  registered result retention, held owner, successive results, forwarding,\\n  reset cancellation, and all 19 pipeline regressions including RVFI\\n  operands/order, divide consumers, completion spacing, and combined stalls.\\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first gate invocation, 53 passed and\\n  0 failed. No RTL fix attempts or counterexamples. Ran the unchanged\\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`;\\n  it selected the nret=1 wrapper/config automatically. This is the standard\\n  ALTOPS gate, not a proof of the complete real divider's arithmetic.\\n- A separate Yosys SAT miter proves the helper equivalent to a mathematical\\n  conditional 32-bit subtraction for every 32-bit magnitude and both values\\n  of negate: `sat -verify -prove trigger 0` returned SUCCESS. The reference\\n  module was supplied through stdin and was not added to the design.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` passes both ELFs: selftest RVFI comparison\\n  and the official CoreMark CRC gate.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with `NRET=1` and\\n  `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, completes all three routes and benchmark\\n  validation. No placement failures. The evaluator and its acceptance\\n  criteria were not edited.\\n\\nValidation used unchanged scratch copies under\\n`$TMPDIR/div-sign32-r4s0-34k2_reo`. The installed conda Python provided\\ncocotb, pytest, YAML, and click; the default Homebrew Python lacks cocotb\\nand cannot run the installed sby without click. No environment packages\\nwere installed and no contract scripts were patched. Tool versions were\\nVerilator 5.047 devel, Yosys 0.64\\n`d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61`, and\\nnextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`, matching the recorded winner's synthesis/P&R\\nversions. `verified-source-sha256.json` verifies 224 staged source files\\nagainst the submitted worktree; all formal-staged RTL was also compared\\nbyte-for-byte. Generated outputs are outside the submitted worktree.\\n\\nMeasured FPGA results:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported path ns | Logic ns | Routing ns | Launch -> capture |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 105.35 | 9.49 | 5.45 | 4.05 | divisor_q_DFFRE_Q_31.Q -> result_q_DFFRE_Q_15.D |\\n| 2 | 130.29 | 7.68 | 5.25 | 2.43 | divisor_q_DFFRE_Q_31.Q -> result_q_DFFRE_Q_15.D |\\n| 3 | 104.55 | 9.56 | 5.11 | 4.46 | divisor_q_DFFRE_Q_27.Q -> result_q_DFFRE_Q_15.D |\\n\\nNames in the table share the prefix `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.u_div.`. Delay columns\\nare rounded independently by nextpnr. Mean Fmax is 113.40 MHz, sample SD\\n14.64 MHz; the official statistic remains the 105.35 MHz median. All three\\nreported paths traverse a restoring subtraction before the mapped completion\\nlogic and end at the same result register. Seeds 1 and 2 report 32 carry\\noutput arcs plus a sum arc; seed 3 reports 28 plus a sum arc. Each then\\ntraverses seven LUT arcs and one LUT5 mux arc before register setup.\\n\\nComplete source-to-register path blocks, including every carry, logic,\\nrouting, and setup entry, are retained in `critical-path-seed1.txt`,\\n`critical-path-seed2.txt`, and `critical-path-seed3.txt`, and every named\\ndelay is preserved in `timing-paths.json`. The corresponding original\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log` line ranges are 239-516,\\n245-522, and 267-516. The mapped completion network and its routing do not\\ndeliver the hypothesized timing benefit in these routes.\\n\\nMapped logic and headroom:\\n\\n| Resource | Starting RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Placed LUT4 utilization | 5,470 (recorded winner) | 5,411 / 20,736 |\\n| Flip-flops | 2,111 | 2,116 |\\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\\n| Block RAM (DPX9B / BSRAM) | 4 | 4 |\\n| Synthesized ALU carry cells | 546 | 482 |\\n\\nThe starting RTL was read from HEAD\\n`a99797c57d396792c8e7d728f54bb041d22401fd` and synthesized separately with\\nthe same tools to compare mapped resources. Removing the two terminal\\n32-bit carry chains removes 64 ALU cells. The helper alone maps to 77\\nLUT1..LUT4 cells and six MUX2_LUT5 cells, with no ALU or register cells;\\nthese standalone counts are not additive per-instance core utilization\\nbecause full-core optimization merges logic across module boundaries.\\nYosys structure inspection confirms exactly four (33,33,33)-width\\nrestoring subtractions and two (32,32,32)-width input magnitude subtractions\\nremain. The helper RTL contains only 124 OR, 31 AND, and 31 XOR cells.\\nThere are no added RTL state elements, although the mapped FF count rises\\nby five. Device LUT headroom is 15,325; headroom below core.yaml's 10,000-LUT\\ntarget is 4,589. Multiplier sharing and block-RAM inference are retained.\\n\\nThe winner's saved notes document missing LUT3 bank-selection IOPATH arcs\\nand omitted forwarding/writeback paths in timing exports. Those limitations\\nare not resolved by this change. The complete *reported* paths above do\\nnot establish complete physical timing closure. Unreported paths and\\nmissing arcs must not be treated as zero delay. No timing exceptions or\\nattributes were added to hide them.\\n\\nCoreMark configuration and cycle comparison:\\n\\nThe unchanged evaluator actually builds aggregate 2 KB CoreMark, ten\\niterations, -O3. Its official CRC expectations remain seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, and crcfinal=fcaf. Official\\nfitness 234.55 uses 10 / 4,491,485 iterations per cycle and 105.35 MHz;\\nthe evaluator's separately printed IPC field is rounded.\\n\\nThe requested aggregate 6 KB workload was built separately with the\\nunchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` overrides.\\nThe official 2 KB ELF was retained. Both sizes were run on freshly built\\nstarting-RTL and candidate simulators with identical ELFs and\\n`--bench --istall --dstall`.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting bracketed cycles | Candidate bracketed cycles | Start marker | Stop marker |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Official 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 15,801 | 4,507,286 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56,393 | 32,893,064 |\\n\\nBoth 6 KB runs have seedcrc=0x8a02, crclist=0xd4b0, crcmatrix=0xbe52,\\ncrcstate=0x5e47, and crcfinal=0x5949. All four runs complete with EBREAK,\\nvalid timing brackets, no out-of-bounds access, ten iterations, and the\\nliteral `Correct operation validated.` The existing UART compiler-flags\\ndescription says -O2; the actual build commands use -O3. The reported\\nCoreMark Size is the per-algorithm partition (666 or 2000), not the total\\nworking set. Supplemental 6 KB validation is not the logged official\\nfitness result.\\n\\nEvidence in the scratch directory includes `unit-tests.log`, `formal.log`,\\n`cosim.log`, `fpga.log`, both synthesized netlists and synthesis logs,\\n`sign-mapped.json`, `div-structure.json`, the three complete P&R logs and\\npath extracts, `cycle-comparison.json`, all four `candidate-/baseline-`\\nCoreMark logs, `canonical/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`.\\nOnly the scoped bench RTL/tests and this requested notes file were changed\\nin the worktree. No official evaluator, other core, metadata, or experiment\\nlog was modified. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T09:20:41.336420Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r4s0 (regression, -37.58%): Carry-free prefix-OR/XOR sign correction in div_unit.sv removed 64 carry cells but left restoring carry chains feeding seven LUT arcs and a LUT5 mux, lowering reported median Fmax 37.58% despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Retime divider finalization using a narrow request-edge prefix\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner records 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz. Its r2s1 notes\\nidentify divider-state-to-result_q completion paths as critical in two\\nof three routed seeds; the remaining seed is limited by normal EX result\\nselection. The divider currently cascades four restoring steps into\\nsigned-result correction on its last compute clock. The r3 carry-select\\nand radix-4 attempts made mapped timing substantially worse, so replacing\\nthe proven wide recurrence with another arithmetic network is unattractive.\\nThe original r1s1 notes also document a liveness failure when completion\\ngained one clock. A useful structural experiment must separate final\\ncorrection from the recurrence while retaining the existing latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner records 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz. Its r2s1 notes\\nidentify divider-state-to-result_q completion paths as critical in two\\nof three routed seeds; the remaining seed is limited by normal EX result\\nselection. The divider currently cascades four restoring steps into\\nsigned-result correction on its last compute clock. The r3 carry-select\\nand radix-4 attempts made mapped timing substantially worse, so replacing\\nthe proven wide recurrence with another arithmetic network is unattractive.\\nThe original r1s1 notes also document a liveness failure when completion\\ngained one clock. A useful structural experiment must separate final\\ncorrection from the recurrence while retaining the existing latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the first four dividend-bit iterations onto the request edge using\\na narrow initial-prefix circuit, and move final sign correction behind\\nthe existing quotient/remainder registers. This is one retiming of the\\nblocking divider, with no new pipeline stage visible to the core and\\nno change to its request/response schedule or eight post-request clocks.\\n\\nAt request acceptance, form the existing unsigned magnitudes A and D\\nfrom the fully forwarded operands. Let P = A[31:28]. Since P is at most\\n15, D >= 16 implies an initial four-bit quotient of zero and remainder P.\\nDetect that case with the reduction OR of D[31:4]. Otherwise perform\\nexactly four ordinary restoring steps on four-bit P and four-bit D,\\nstarting from zero remainder, using five-bit trial/subtract values and\\nexplicit borrow selection. This produces four-bit prefix quotient Q4\\nand remainder R4 without a wide subtract cascade at launch. Use native\\nsubtract/select expressions, not division operators, carry-select logic,\\nradix-4 candidate selection, or an additional wide arithmetic resource.\\nThe zero-divisor case retains the existing explicit final-result override.\\n\\nInitialize quotient_q to {A[27:0], Q4} and remainder_q to {28'b0, R4}\\non that same request edge. Preserve the captured divisor, original\\ndividend, operation selection, and signedness flags. The first seven\\nsubsequent clocks each run the existing four serial 33-bit restoring\\nsteps, consuming the remaining 28 dividend bits. After the seventh\\nclock the registered quotient and remainder contain the complete\\nunsigned magnitudes. During the eighth interval, hold those registers\\nand compute the existing quotient/remainder sign corrections and\\nzero-divisor selection directly from their registered outputs. Nothing\\nin completed_result may depend on quotient_step[4], remainder_step[4],\\nor a live operand during this final interval.\\n\\nKeep final_cycle at busy_q && count_q == 7. Offer the response during\\nthat eighth interval and capture result_q on its closing edge, exactly\\nwhen EX/MEM may accept its metadata. Keep registered_result connected\\ndirectly to result_q, with no combinational bypass. Preserve immediate\\nacceptance, held-response stability, simultaneous held consumption and\\nnext-request acceptance, reset cancellation, and result_q retention\\nthrough consumption and subsequent launches. All operations, including\\ndivisor zero, still use the same fixed schedule. Consecutive divides\\nremain nine clocks apart, and an ordinary dependent consumer acquires\\nno additional bubble. Do not introduce a ninth completion clock.\\n\\nIn RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, retain all four existing subtraction/XOR formulas\\ncaptured on the request edge and use the identical counter, handshake,\\nand result-bank update schedule. Real arithmetic must retain signed\\ntruncation toward zero, the dividend sign for REM, full-width unsigned\\noperands, divide-by-zero values, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior. ALTOPS does\\nnot establish correctness of the new real-arithmetic prefix circuit.\\n\\nPredict a modest three-percent fitness improvement from shorter divider\\ncompletion timing with unchanged instruction-cycle behavior. This is\\nunmeasured: the added magnitude-to-prefix launch path or the untouched\\nnormal EX cone may limit Fmax, and placement changes may erase the gain.\\nInspect request capture, recurrence update, completion, normal EX, and\\nresult-bank forwarding/writeback timing across the unchanged three-seed\\nevaluation; disclose any missing timing coverage rather than assigning\\nomitted arcs zero delay. Compare identical binaries and marker windows.\\nKeep the separately requested 6 KB CRC validation distinct from the\\nrecorded evaluator workload if its previously documented 2 KB setting\\nremains present. Do not alter benchmark sources or evaluation settings.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1 and every external port, decoder/trap rule, memory and\\nretirement invariant, and all formal depths and checks. Keep the normal\\nEX/MEM memory-address bank directly connected to mem_stage.address so\\nBRAM address-register absorption remains possible. Leave the multiplier,\\nordinary ALU, result-bank selection, forwarding, hazards, and redirects\\nunchanged. Run the existing lint, arithmetic unit tests, formal, cosim,\\ncanonical UART/CRC/OOB/marker checks, and three-seed FPGA gates.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the magnitude-based four-bit initial-prefix circuit and capture\\nits quotient/remainder state on the existing request edge. Retain\\nthe current wide four-step recurrence for count values 0 through 6;\\nat count 7 hold Q/R and form completed_result from their registered\\nvalues and captured sign/zero-divisor flags. Preserve final_cycle,\\nall ports, counter duration, response acceptance, result-bank\\nownership, reset behavior, and the matching ALTOPS schedule. Update\\nthe module's timing comments to describe request preprocessing,\\nseven wide recurrence clocks, and one final correction clock.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic vector and exact eight-clock\\nresponse/bank assertion in both real and ALTOPS builds. Add exhaustive\\ncoverage of all 16 initial magnitude nibbles against divisors 0..15,\\nand directed divisors 16, 17, powers of two, and high-bit unsigned\\nvalues. Vary the lower 28 dividend bits and signed operand forms,\\nincluding INT_MIN/-1 and zero divisors. Check captured prefix state\\nagainst an independent integer-arithmetic reference, complete Q/R\\nafter seven recurrence edges, and no early result-bank update.\\nChange live inputs after launch and test immediate/held acceptance,\\nconsume/refill, and reset at the request, seventh, and eighth edges.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve the exact nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, existing\\nno-extra-completion-bubble assertions, and full RVFI/ISS comparisons.\\nAdd small-divisor prefix-boundary streams with load-produced operands\\nand redirect-target divides, specifically exercising the predecessor\\npatterns that previously failed liveness with one added clock.\\nCover dependent ALU, branch, JALR, store and divide consumers, combined\\nbus waits, reset, x0 destinations, and killed younger divides. Require\\nexact retirement order/PC continuity and accepted memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Keep all existing bank ownership and latency assertions. Add checks\\nthat the previous result remains owned through prefix setup and the\\nseventh recurrence edge, and that the corrected result and EX/MEM\\nmetadata become visible together only on the unchanged eighth edge.\\nExercise held completion and prolonged owner stalls with changing\\nyounger operands in both arithmetic modes, preserving the direct\\nnormal-bank memory-address checks.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the magnitude-based four-bit initial-prefix circuit and capture\\nits quotient/remainder state on the existing request edge. Retain\\nthe current wide four-step recurrence for count values 0 through 6;\\nat count 7 hold Q/R and form completed_result from their registered\\nvalues and captured sign/zero-divisor flags. Preserve final_cycle,\\nall ports, counter duration, response acceptance, result-bank\\nownership, reset behavior, and the matching ALTOPS schedule. Update\\nthe module's timing comments to describe request preprocessing,\\nseven wide recurrence clocks, and one final correction clock.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic vector and exact eight-clock\\nresponse/bank assertion in both real and ALTOPS builds. Add exhaustive\\ncoverage of all 16 initial magnitude nibbles against divisors 0..15,\\nand directed divisors 16, 17, powers of two, and high-bit unsigned\\nvalues. Vary the lower 28 dividend bits and signed operand forms,\\nincluding INT_MIN/-1 and zero divisors. Check captured prefix state\\nagainst an independent integer-arithmetic reference, complete Q/R\\nafter seven recurrence edges, and no early result-bank update.\\nChange live inputs after launch and test immediate/held acceptance,\\nconsume/refill, and reset at the request, seventh, and eighth edges.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve the exact nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, existing\\nno-extra-completion-bubble assertions, and full RVFI/ISS comparisons.\\nAdd small-divisor prefix-boundary streams with load-produced operands\\nand redirect-target divides, specifically exercising the predecessor\\npatterns that previously failed liveness with one added clock.\\nCover dependent ALU, branch, JALR, store and divide consumers, combined\\nbus waits, reset, x0 destinations, and killed younger divides. Require\\nexact retirement order/PC continuity and accepted memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Keep all existing bank ownership and latency assertions. Add checks\\nthat the previous result remains owned through prefix setup and the\\nseventh recurrence edge, and that the corrected result and EX/MEM\\nmetadata become visible together only on the unchanged eighth edge.\\nExercise held completion and prolonged owner stalls with changing\\nyounger operands in both arithmetic modes, preserving the direct\\nnormal-bank memory-address checks.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 382.48, \"delta_pct\": 1.78, \"vs_baseline\": 35.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5513, \"ff\": 2087, \"seeds\": [163.85, 171.79, 174.58], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T09:31:31.255685Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r4s1 (improvement, +1.78%): Precomputing the first four restoring bits with a narrow request-edge prefix in div_unit.sv separates sign correction from the wide recurrence by using registered quotient_q/remainder_q, improving fitness without extending the eight-clock response schedule.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core fetches sequentially and discards one younger fetch/decode\\nslot for every normally advancing taken control transfer. LESSONS.md and\\nthe predictor records in experiments/log.jsonl show that the tagged BTBs\\nsaved about 6.1% of timed cycles, while the stateless backward-taken/JAL\\npredictor saved 5.16%; their timing costs prevented beating the winner.\\nIn hyp-20260906-003-r3s2, median Fmax fell from 168.78 to 154.13 MHz, with\\nworst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\\nDirect JAL has a target fully determined by the accepted instruction and\\nits PC. Predicting this transfer alone can remove its bubble without\\nintroducing conditional-direction repair into the existing EX control cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core fetches sequentially and discards one younger fetch/decode\\nslot for every normally advancing taken control transfer. LESSONS.md and\\nthe predictor records in experiments/log.jsonl show that the tagged BTBs\\nsaved about 6.1% of timed cycles, while the stateless backward-taken/JAL\\npredictor saved 5.16%; their timing costs prevented beating the winner.\\nIn hyp-20260906-003-r3s2, median Fmax fell from 168.78 to 154.13 MHz, with\\nworst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\\nDirect JAL has a target fully determined by the accepted instruction and\\nits PC. Predicting this transfer alone can remove its bubble without\\nintroducing conditional-direction repair into the existing EX control cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one stateless direct-JAL-only predictor in cores/bench. Predict\\nopcode 0x6f early when the fetch PC is word-aligned and the J-immediate is\\nword-aligned. Predict conditional branches, JALR, and every other opcode\\nsequentially. This is deliberately one narrow predictor, with no BTB,\\ndirection table, history, return stack, or training interface.\\n\\nPredecode raw imem_data independently of flushed instruction output and\\nof stall/redirect controls. JAL needs no funct-field qualification; check\\nPC[1:0] == 0 and instruction bit 21 == 0 before predicting, since that bit\\nis J-immediate bit 1 and bit 0 is implicit zero. Build the sign-extended\\nJ displacement and add it directly to the full 32-bit PC. Keep PC+4\\nindependent and preserve modulo-2^32 arithmetic. There is no B/J immediate\\nselector. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the existing PC register.\\n\\nApply the early target only on the edge that accepts this JAL into ID/EX.\\nPreserve the current PC priority: reset, existing EX redirect, accepted\\nfetch advance, otherwise hold. Early prediction must not flush the JAL\\nitself. Emit an early_jal bit with the IF/ID instruction so decode knows\\nwhich accepted instruction actually caused this PC advance. A held or\\nunavailable fetch must not consume a prediction; changing imem_data during\\na hold must leave the next accepted instruction and its target consistent.\\n\\nAt the ID/EX boundary, register a separate jump_needs_redirect bit equal\\nto dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal. Keep the original ctrl.is_jump and\\nctrl.is_jalr unchanged for architectural execution, link writeback, and\\ntraps. Capture the bit with the instruction and hold it through dmem and\\ndivider stalls. Clear it on reset/flush; the existing invalid-fetch NOP\\ndecodes to zero for this bit. Retain the current ID/EX payload reset and\\ninvalid-fetch policy: do not add an invalid-fetch condition to the entire\\npayload reset or add a new wide flush mux.\\n\\nIn EX, replace only the jump contribution to the existing redirect\\npredicate with the registered jump_needs_redirect bit. Thus redirect is\\nin.valid && (branch_taken || in.jump_needs_redirect) && !misalign_fault.\\nThe branch comparator, redirect target selection, and alignment logic\\notherwise keep their current behavior. An early aligned JAL cannot need\\ntarget or direction repair: the target used at its accepting edge is\\nexactly the PC-relative target EX computes from that same instruction.\\nNo saved target, predicted-next-PC comparator, outcome XOR, or fallback\\nPC+4 repair mux is required. Do not introduce a generic EX-advance or\\ndiv_wait qualification into this redirect cone; retain the winner's\\nexisting redirect/hold semantics for unpredicted branches and JALRs.\\n\\nEX must still execute every predicted JAL normally, write PC+4 to its\\nnonzero destination, and report its resolved target through RVFI pc_next.\\nMisaligned direct transfers are not predicted and retain the existing\\ntrap, suppressed link write, and PC+4 continuation. JALR keeps its current\\nforwarded target computation and bit-zero clearing. Preserve all current\\nload-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, retained WB forwarding,\\ndivider protocol, and suppression of wrong-path side effects. A correct\\nearly JAL must also remain correct when held in EX behind an older memory\\nrequest; its already selected fetch target simply stays held.\\n\\nThe estimated net fitness gain is 2%, with low confidence because the\\ntimed direct-JAL frequency and routed timing of this narrower design have\\nnot been measured. The premise is a smaller cycle gain with much less\\ndisturbance to EX control than the previous combined branch/JAL predictor.\\nThe instruction-to-PC adder and placement changes remain timing risks.\\nCount accepted early JALs and marker-window cycles in simulation, then\\nevaluate cycles together with the median of all three FPGA Fmax seeds\\nagainst 375.78 iter/sec; fewer cycles alone do not establish improvement.\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker-window,\\nand FPGA gates. Preserve the nret=1 IO contract, all hard invariants, the\\nwinning divider/result banks, and the direct registered dmem address path.\\nAll evaluator files and other core directories remain outside this change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw-instruction direct-JAL predecode, low-bit alignment checks,\\none full-width J-target adder, and early_jal metadata. Select the\\nearly target only on an accepted fetch, preserving the existing\\nreset/redirect/stall priority and directly registered imem address.\\nLeave every conditional branch and JALR prediction sequential.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add early_jal to if_id_t and jump_needs_redirect to id_ex_t. The\\nlatter is a decode-generated redirect permission, not a prediction\\nbit for a new EX comparison. Preserve all existing architectural\\ncontrol fields, resolved PC fields, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal with the instruction as\\njump_needs_redirect. Preserve the original jump controls for link\\ndata and traps, retain the new bit on holds, and clear it with the\\nexisting reset/flush behavior. Keep the winner's invalid-fetch and\\nwide-payload reset policy to avoid a new high-fanout control path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered jump_needs_redirect bit instead of ctrl.is_jump\\nonly in the redirect predicate. Keep branch resolution, target muxes,\\ntrap qualification, JAL/JALR results, and architectural pc_next as\\nthey are. Add no direction comparison, target comparison, generic\\nEX-advance dependency, or divider-dependent redirect qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused tests for positive/negative/self JALs, displacement\\nextremes, full-address carry/wrap, PC/target alignment, and sequential\\nprediction for all non-JAL opcodes. Verify prediction consumption\\nonly on accepted fetches and priority of reset and older redirects\\nover a younger early JAL, including instruction changes during holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Cover zero-bubble accepted JALs, consecutive and self JALs, x0/x1/x5\\nlink destinations, forwarded use of the link, unpredicted branches\\nand JALRs, and misaligned-JAL trap continuation. Exercise imem stalls,\\nlong older-memory holds, a preceding divider wait, load-use stalls,\\nand an older redirect killing a younger JAL/store/divide. Check the\\naccepted target and decode certificate, exact RVFI PC/order/link\\nvalues, no wrong-path side effects, and all existing assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout and constructors for the new redirect\\npermission bit, setting it to zero for non-jumps. Retain every current\\ndivider, forwarding, result-bank, and registered-address assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw-instruction direct-JAL predecode, low-bit alignment checks,\\none full-width J-target adder, and early_jal metadata. Select the\\nearly target only on an accepted fetch, preserving the existing\\nreset/redirect/stall priority and directly registered imem address.\\nLeave every conditional branch and JALR prediction sequential.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add early_jal to if_id_t and jump_needs_redirect to id_ex_t. The\\nlatter is a decode-generated redirect permission, not a prediction\\nbit for a new EX comparison. Preserve all existing architectural\\ncontrol fields, resolved PC fields, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal with the instruction as\\njump_needs_redirect. Preserve the original jump controls for link\\ndata and traps, retain the new bit on holds, and clear it with the\\nexisting reset/flush behavior. Keep the winner's invalid-fetch and\\nwide-payload reset policy to avoid a new high-fanout control path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered jump_needs_redirect bit instead of ctrl.is_jump\\nonly in the redirect predicate. Keep branch resolution, target muxes,\\ntrap qualification, JAL/JALR results, and architectural pc_next as\\nthey are. Add no direction comparison, target comparison, generic\\nEX-advance dependency, or divider-dependent redirect qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused tests for positive/negative/self JALs, displacement\\nextremes, full-address carry/wrap, PC/target alignment, and sequential\\nprediction for all non-JAL opcodes. Verify prediction consumption\\nonly on accepted fetches and priority of reset and older redirects\\nover a younger early JAL, including instruction changes during holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Cover zero-bubble accepted JALs, consecutive and self JALs, x0/x1/x5\\nlink destinations, forwarded use of the link, unpredicted branches\\nand JALRs, and misaligned-JAL trap continuation. Exercise imem stalls,\\nlong older-memory holds, a preceding divider wait, load-use stalls,\\nand an older redirect killing a younger JAL/store/divide. Check the\\naccepted target and decode certificate, exact RVFI PC/order/link\\nvalues, no wrong-path side effects, and all existing assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout and constructors for the new redirect\\npermission bit, setting it to zero for non-jumps. Retain every current\\ndivider, forwarding, result-bank, and registered-address assertion.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.59, \"delta_pct\": -8.03, \"vs_baseline\": 22.19, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.52, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4442217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5512, \"ff\": 2096, \"seeds\": [164.07, 153.52, 151.88], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis. Final correctness gates pass, but measured fitness\\nregresses: **345.59 iter/sec versus 375.78 (-8.03%)**. The 1.10% reduction in\\ntimed cycles does not offset the 9.04% reduction in median Fmax. This is not a\\nfitness improvement.\\n\\nOnly four RTL files changed: `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`, `id_stage.sv`, and\\n`ex_stage.sv`. IF predecodes raw `imem_data` for opcode 0x6f, aligned PC, and\\ninstruction bit 21 clear. The sign-extended J displacement feeds one full-width\\nPC-relative adder; PC+4 remains independent. Both wrap modulo 2^32.\\n`imem_addr` remains directly assigned from the PC register. Branches, JALR,\\nand all other opcodes predict sequentially.\\n\\nPC updates prioritize reset, the existing EX redirect, and then an accepted\\nfetch (`!stall && !flush`); otherwise PC holds. The target mux selects using\\nraw JAL eligibility, while `early_jal` certifies actual acceptance and is zero\\nduring reset, hold, flush, or redirect. The explicit flush condition also\\nmakes an isolated IF flush hold PC. In the integrated winner, flush already\\nimplies an EX redirect or imem stall, so this adds no new integrated behavior.\\n\\nID/EX registers `dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal` as `jump_needs_redirect`, using\\nthe existing reset/flush and hold policy. Invalid fetches retain the existing\\ndecoded-NOP payload policy; no invalid-fetch-wide reset was added. EX changes\\nonly the jump term in its redirect predicate. Architectural jump controls,\\nPC+4 link results, resolved RVFI targets, alignment traps, and JALR forwarding\\nand bit-zero clearing remain intact. The divider, result banks, registered\\ndmem address, forwarding, hazard unit, retirement logic, and nret=1 IO are\\nunchanged. There is no predictor state, training, saved target, target/direction\\ncomparison, fallback repair mux, or new EX-advance/div_wait qualification.\\n\\nTests add direct IF and ID coverage and extend the existing pipeline and EX\\nresult-bank tests without removing their assertions. Coverage includes signed\\nJAL displacements and extremes, full-address carry/wrap, every non-JAL opcode,\\nalignment, changing instructions during holds, reset/redirect priority,\\nconsecutive/self JALs, x0/x1/x5 links and forwarding, imem stalls, an 18-cycle\\nolder memory hold, preceding divider waits, load-use bubbles, misaligned-JAL\\ncontinuation, forwarded JALR, and older redirects killing younger JAL/store/divide.\\nPipeline tests compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and\\ncheck accepted memory transactions and divider launches separately.\\n\\nAn opt-in `test_coremark_profile.py` observes accepted early JALs directly,\\nusing the unchanged ELF and the C++ harness's exact xorshift stall sequence.\\nIt adds no RTL counters and does not replace any evaluator gate. The final\\nprofile matches the unchanged C++ simulation's markers exactly:\\n\\n- Start: 15,665; stop: 4,457,882; timed cycles: **4,442,217**.\\n- Accepted early JALs: **49,629 total; 49,367 inside the marker window**.\\n- EBREAK reached; UART/CRC validation passed; no OOB access.\\n\\nFinal local validation:\\n\\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings.\\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, 10 runners; the opt-in profile is skipped\\n  by default and separately PASS when enabled. Includes real and ALTOPS\\n  arithmetic/result-bank tests and all 25 pipeline cases.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, using the unchanged single-channel wrapper\\n  and configuration. Both local formal invocations passed. No formal\\n  counterexample or formal fix attempt occurred. All final generated checks\\n  have DONE (PASS), and all 14 staged RTL files match the submitted RTL.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: PASS, selftest and CoreMark.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\\n  completed all three seeds, CRC/UART, OOB, and marker-window gates.\\n- `git diff --check`: PASS. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\\n\\nFinal routed results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61)\\nand nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, the winner's recorded tools. The median includes\\nall three final seeds:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst reported path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 164.07 | Divider divisor_q to result_q data | 6.09 | 3.72 / 2.38 |\\n| 2 | 153.52 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.51 | 2.39 / 4.12 |\\n| 3 | 151.88 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.58 | 2.39 / 4.19 |\\n\\nMedian Fmax is **153.52 MHz**, versus 168.78 MHz. Final resources are 5,512\\nLUT4 and 2,096 DFF, versus 5,470 LUT4 and 2,111 DFF. At the measured cycle\\ncount the candidate needs more than **166.93 MHz** to exceed 375.78 iter/sec.\\nThe reported worst paths do not identify the IF J-target adder as limiting;\\nthey are not evidence that every IF, forwarding, or memory path has margin.\\n\\nOne local implementation refinement separated target selection from the\\nacceptance-qualified metadata bit: the first version used `out.early_jal`\\ninside the PC data mux and measured 78.73 MHz median, with long paths to\\nID/EX reset. The final version uses raw predecode in the data mux and\\nqualifies acceptance in the PC update enable. All gates were rerun on that\\nfinal version; the first route's results are archived separately.\\n\\nThe checked-in evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB** CoreMark despite the\\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its unmodified CRC contract is\\nseedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\\nA separate **6 KB, -O3** ELF was built using the unchanged benchmark\\nMakefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options. It is supplemental,\\nnot a replacement for official fitness. Both comparisons below use fresh\\nsimulation of starting HEAD and final RTL, identical ELFs, ten iterations,\\nand `--istall --dstall`:\\n\\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / final iter/sec |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3 | 4,491,485 | 4,442,217 | 1.10% | 375.78 / 345.59 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3 | 32,836,671 | 32,485,445 | 1.07% | 51.40 / 47.26 |\\n\\nRates use the winner's recorded 168.78 MHz and the final measured 153.52 MHz.\\nThe 6 KB rate regresses 8.06%. Its starting marker window is\\n(56,393, 32,893,064); the final window is (55,880, 32,541,325). Both 6 KB\\nUARTs have seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47,\\ncrcfinal=5949, ten iterations, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`\\nBoth runs reach EBREAK, have valid markers, and report no OOB access. The\\nUART compiler-description string says -O2; the actual build command is -O3.\\n\\nScratch copies and generated evidence are under\\n`$TMPDIR/early-jal-hove0D` (the run's configured TMPDIR, outside other cores).\\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `profile-tests.log`,\\n`coremark-profile.json`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`,\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`,\\n`workload-validation.json`, the individual benchmark logs, and\\n`sixk/build.log`. `verified-source-sha256.json` verifies 169 staged RTL,\\ntest, and unchanged contract source files against the worktree. This\\nrequested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\\nOrchestrator acceptance remains separate from these local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T09:29:41.572098Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 (regression, -8.03%): Using acceptance-qualified early_jal as the IF PC mux select pulled stall/flush/redirect controls into the data path; selecting with raw predecode instead recovered median Fmax from 78.73 to 153.52 MHz, but JAL-only prediction's 1.10% CoreMark cycle saving could not cover the remaining 9.04% Fmax loss.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Balanced one-hot register-file reads with integrated write bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz median Fmax.\\nIts saved RTL under .tmp/divider-prefix-r4s1-2_i3jztx/cores/bench/rtl\\nmatches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte. The corresponding routed\\ntiming/seed1/nextpnr.log reports a 6.10 ns path from regs[15][27], through\\nthe register-file read selection, into an ID/EX register: 2.51 ns logic\\nand 3.59 ns routing. Seed 3 reports a 5.73 ns fetch/decode-to-ID/EX path,\\nwhile seed 2 reports a 5.82 ns encoded-EX-control-to-EX/MEM path. Thus\\nthe recently improved divider is not the reported worst path in these\\nthree routes, and register-file selection is a concrete local target.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and all prior micro_opt entries in experiments/log.jsonl were\\nchecked. None rewrote the register-file read network. False load-use\\nremoval left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged; full ALU one-hot\\nselection disrupted multiplier sharing; divider carry-select and\\nprefix sign-correction changes regressed routed timing. This proposal\\ntargets only the combinational integer-register read/bypass network.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz median Fmax.\\nIts saved RTL under .tmp/divider-prefix-r4s1-2_i3jztx/cores/bench/rtl\\nmatches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte. The corresponding routed\\ntiming/seed1/nextpnr.log reports a 6.10 ns path from regs[15][27], through\\nthe register-file read selection, into an ID/EX register: 2.51 ns logic\\nand 3.59 ns routing. Seed 3 reports a 5.73 ns fetch/decode-to-ID/EX path,\\nwhile seed 2 reports a 5.82 ns encoded-EX-control-to-EX/MEM path. Thus\\nthe recently improved divider is not the reported worst path in these\\nthree routes, and register-file selection is a concrete local target.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and all prior micro_opt entries in experiments/log.jsonl were\\nchecked. None rewrote the register-file read network. False load-use\\nremoval left measured CoreMark cycles unchanged; full ALU one-hot\\nselection disrupted multiplier sharing; divider carry-select and\\nprefix sign-correction changes regressed routed timing. This proposal\\ntargets only the combinational integer-register read/bypass network.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace each binary-indexed register-file read followed by priority\\nwrite-bypass and x0 selection with one mutually exclusive masked-OR\\nnetwork. Keep the same resettable register storage, synchronous write\\nbehavior, two asynchronous read ports, and module interface.\\n\\nFor each read port independently, compute\\nhit = w_en && (read_addr != 0) && (w_addr == read_addr).\\nDecode the read address into shared per-word enables for x1 through\\nx31. Define exactly 32 candidate words: candidate[0] is w_data masked\\nby hit, and candidate[k] for k=1..31 is regs[k] masked by\\n((read_addr == k) && !hit). OR these candidates with explicit balanced\\nlevels: eight four-candidate groups, two four-group reductions, then\\nthe final two-input OR. Use constant generate indices and fully driven\\nintermediate wires, so no procedural priority or serial OR accumulator\\nis introduced. Share each address comparison across its 32 data bits.\\n\\nThis makes write-first bypass a peer input to the read reduction and\\nmakes a read of x0 select no input, removing the separate output muxes\\nfrom the stored-data path. On a write hit, every stored-data candidate\\nis disabled and only w_data contributes. On a nonzero read without a\\nhit, exactly one stored register contributes. Both outputs remain zero\\nfor x0 even with w_en and w_addr=0. Do not gate combinational hit with\\nreset: preserve the current bypass behavior while synchronous reset\\nis asserted, as well as reset's priority over writes at the clock edge.\\n\\nThis is a combinational micro-optimization with no new state, pipeline\\nstages, hazards, or instruction-dependent scheduling. Keep decoder\\nlegality, raw RVFI operand values, forwarding, bus stalls, all DIV/REM\\narithmetic and its eight-clock completion schedule, ALTOPS behavior,\\nALU/multiplier selection, the direct registered memory-address bank,\\nand the nret=1 external and retirement contracts unchanged.\\n\\nExpect identical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles and an unmeasured\\nthree-percent fitness improvement through Fmax. Confidence is low:\\none-hot enable fanout, extra LUTs, placement changes, or the competing\\nEX-control path may erase the benefit. Verify the mapped read network\\nand complete reported paths instead of assuming RTL tree depth proves\\na timing gain. Preserve one MULT36X36 and four block RAMs and remain\\nbelow the 10000-LUT target; introduce no timing exceptions or synthesis\\nattributes that conceal paths. Existing notes document incomplete\\ntiming-arc coverage, so absent paths are not evidence of zero delay.\\n\\nValidate with a reference-model register-file test, existing pipeline\\noperand/forwarding/stall tests, unchanged lint, formal and cosim gates,\\nand the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation. Compare identical\\nCoreMark binaries and bus-stall settings against the starting RTL.\\nPrior implementation notes distinguish the evaluator's logged 2 KB\\nworkload from supplemental canonical 6 KB validation; keep those\\nmeasurements distinct and leave all evaluation files unchanged.\\nThe required 6 KB run must retain its timing markers, bounded accesses,\\ncanonical d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, and Correct operation validated. output.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace only the two combinational read implementations with the\\nper-port one-hot enables, mutually exclusive stored-data/bypass\\ncandidates, and balanced 32-to-8-to-2-to-1 OR tree specified above.\\nPreserve the existing regs array and entire synchronous reset/write\\nblock, port names and widths, x0 behavior, and combinational bypass\\nsemantics. Use portable synthesizable logic and explicit widths;\\nadd no flops, vendor primitives, or synthesis/timing attributes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing test and add an independent 32-register Python\\nscoreboard. Initialize all writable registers to distinct patterns,\\ncover all 32-by-32 read-address pairs on both ports, and exercise\\neach possible write destination with no hit, one-port hit, and\\nsimultaneous two-port hits. Check write-first data before the edge\\nand stored data after the edge, disabled writes, x0 writes/reads,\\nback-to-back overwrites, randomized data/address transitions, and\\nreset after nonzero contents. Include reset concurrent with w_en:\\nstorage clears at the edge while a nonzero matching read preserves\\nthe existing combinational bypass until w_en drops. Derive expected\\nvalues from architectural storage and write-first rules, not from\\nthe proposed masked-OR equations. Re-run the unchanged pipeline\\nRVFI/forwarding/combined-stall regressions for integration coverage.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace only the two combinational read implementations with the\\nper-port one-hot enables, mutually exclusive stored-data/bypass\\ncandidates, and balanced 32-to-8-to-2-to-1 OR tree specified above.\\nPreserve the existing regs array and entire synchronous reset/write\\nblock, port names and widths, x0 behavior, and combinational bypass\\nsemantics. Use portable synthesizable logic and explicit widths;\\nadd no flops, vendor primitives, or synthesis/timing attributes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing test and add an independent 32-register Python\\nscoreboard. Initialize all writable registers to distinct patterns,\\ncover all 32-by-32 read-address pairs on both ports, and exercise\\neach possible write destination with no hit, one-port hit, and\\nsimultaneous two-port hits. Check write-first data before the edge\\nand stored data after the edge, disabled writes, x0 writes/reads,\\nback-to-back overwrites, randomized data/address transitions, and\\nreset after nonzero contents. Include reset concurrent with w_en:\\nstorage clears at the edge while a nonzero matching read preserves\\nthe existing combinational bypass until w_en drops. Derive expected\\nvalues from architectural storage and write-first rules, not from\\nthe proposed masked-OR equations. Re-run the unchanged pipeline\\nRVFI/forwarding/combined-stall regressions for integration coverage.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.03, \"delta_pct\": -7.44, \"vs_baseline\": 25.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 159.01, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5266, \"ff\": 2090, \"seeds\": [159.01, 143.86, 188.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the balanced one-hot register-file read hypothesis. All local\\ncorrectness gates pass, but the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation\\nregresses: median Fmax 171.79 -> 159.01 MHz and fitness 382.48 -> 354.03\\n(-7.44%), with identical CoreMark cycles. The proposed three-percent\\nimprovement was not observed. The implementation is retained for the\\norchestrator's evaluation; no unrelated optimization was attempted. Only\\ncores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv and cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py changed,\\napart from this requested report.\\n\\nEach read port now has its own nonzero write-hit predicate, 31 shared\\nword enables, and exactly 32 masked candidates. Candidate zero is the\\nwrite bypass; candidates 1 through 31 select stored registers only when\\nthere is no write hit. Constant generate indices drive an explicit\\n32-to-8-to-2-to-1 OR reduction, with paired ORs within each four-input\\ngroup. Reading x0 enables no candidate. The original storage declaration\\nand entire synchronous reset/write block are byte-for-byte unchanged.\\nHit is deliberately independent of reset, preserving live write-first\\nbypass while reset clears storage at the clock edge.\\n\\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed architecture, new state,\\npipeline changes, vendor primitives, synthesis attributes, or timing\\nexceptions. All other RTL, nret=1 metadata, decoder, forwarding/stalls,\\nRVFI, ALU/multiplier/ALTOPS logic, eight-clock DIV/REM schedule, and direct\\nregistered memory-address connection remain unchanged.\\n\\nThe five original register-file tests are retained. The added independent\\n32-register architectural scoreboard uses ordinary indexed Python storage\\nand write-first rules. It checks distinct initial patterns; all 1024 read\\naddress pairs; every write destination with no hit, either single-port\\nhit, and both-port hits; reads before and after edges; disabled writes;\\nx0; consecutive overwrites; 1024 live, uncommitted write/address changes;\\n1500 randomized clocked transitions; and reset of populated storage.\\nReset concurrent with a write is covered for all 32 destinations,\\nincluding continued bypass on either or both ports after storage clears\\nand zero data after write enable drops.\\n\\nLocal correctness gates:\\n\\n- Exact requested command passed without warnings:\\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`.\\n- The new register-file suite also passed against the saved starting RTL:\\n  all six cocotb tests, including the scoreboard.\\n- Candidate `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cores/bench/test/`:\\n  8 runners passed, 137 cocotb cases, no failures. This includes all 21\\n  unchanged pipeline RVFI/forwarding/combined-stall regressions, real and\\n  ALTOPS arithmetic tests, and both result-bank suites.\\n- Local formal status: PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed;\\n  zero RTL fix attempts and no counterexample. Ran the unchanged\\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl`, `CORE_NAME=bench`,\\n  and `JOBS=6`; it auto-selected wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg. This is\\n  the existing ALTOPS gate, not a claim of formal-deep arithmetic proof.\\n- Both unchanged Verilator cosim builds passed with NRET=1. The unchanged\\n  `python -m tools.eval.cosim . bench` gate passed on both baseline and\\n  candidate, testing selftest.elf against the Python ISS and validating\\n  the evaluator's 2 KB CoreMark CRCs under both bus stalls.\\n\\nValidation ran in isolated scratch copies under the supplied TMPDIR:\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/regfile-onehot-r5s0-zgbbezdd`.\\nSubdirectories baseline/ and candidate/ contain the respective RTL,\\nunmodified validation sources, logs, synthesized netlists, and routes.\\nThe formal framework was copied, so the gate did not write into the\\nshared vendored formal tree. The candidate RTL was byte-compared with\\nthe working tree after validation. Evaluation source copies were checked\\nagainst the originals. Existing Conda Python 3.13/cocotb 2.0.1 was used\\nbecause the default Homebrew Python lacks cocotb; no evaluation file was\\nedited to accommodate the environment.\\n\\nThe checked-in evaluator actually measures a 2 KB total working set,\\ndespite the prompt's general 6 KB fitness description. That evaluator\\nand its CRC expectations were left unchanged. The starting RTL's full\\nthree-seed evaluation reproduced the supplied winner exactly:\\n\\n| Measurement | Starting RTL | One-hot candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 163.85 | 159.01 |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 171.79 | 143.86 |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 174.58 | 188.96 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 171.79 | 159.01 |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\\n| Evaluator fitness | 382.48 | 354.03 |\\n| LUT4 resources | 5,513 | 5,266 |\\n| DFF resources | 2,087 | 2,090 |\\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\\n| Block RAMs | 4 | 4 |\\n\\nBoth designs were synthesized with the unchanged fpga/scripts/synth.tcl,\\nusing BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv, then evaluated with the unchanged\\ntools.eval.fpga three-seed entry point and nextpnr_run.sh. The same\\ncompiled CoreMark ELF was copied to both evaluation trees. The existing\\n`--bench --istall --dstall` flags and default 0xDEADBEEF bus-stall seed\\nwere preserved. The candidate remains below the 10,000-LUT target.\\nThere are no extra RTL flops; mapping changes the total physical DFF\\ncount by three, while unused x0 storage disappears (993 to 992 RF DFFs).\\nBoth synthesis logs contain the same eight pre-existing divider-array\\nlowering warnings, with no new warning category.\\n\\nMapped read-network inspection traversed the synthesized combinational\\ncone from every nonzero register bit into both ID/EX operand fields.\\nEach of the 64 output bits retained all 31 stored-register sources.\\nAcross the 992 source/output combinations on each port, longest mapped\\nstored-data paths changed from 4-6 cells on rs1 and 3-7 cells on rs2 to\\n3-4 cells on both ports. Candidate depth counts are 255 combinations at\\nthree cells and 737 at four cells, per port. Yosys maps the reduction\\nusing LUTs and MUX2_LUT5 primitives; the physical network is not assumed\\nto retain the literal RTL OR-tree boundaries. Detailed examples and\\ndepth histograms are in mapped_read_network.json in the scratch root.\\n\\nAll six routes completed normally. Inspected each complete final\\nclock-to-clock critical path, including clock-to-Q, every combinational\\nand routing arc, and destination setup. The reports are preserved in\\nrouted_paths.txt and summarized in routed_summary.json in the scratch\\nroot; the original per-seed nextpnr.log files remain alongside pnr.json.\\n\\n| Design / seed | Reported critical cone | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Starting / 1 | regs[15][27] -> RF read -> ID/EX | 6.10 | 2.51 | 3.59 |\\n| Starting / 2 | id_ex_w[53] -> EX control -> EX/MEM | 5.82 | 3.15 | 2.67 |\\n| Starting / 3 | fetch LFSR[20] -> read selection -> ID/EX | 5.73 | 2.12 | 3.61 |\\n| Candidate / 1 | id_ex_w[53] -> EX control -> EX/MEM | 6.29 | 3.15 | 3.14 |\\n| Candidate / 2 | id_ex_w[53] -> EX control -> EX/MEM | 6.95 | 3.15 | 3.80 |\\n| Candidate / 3 | id_ex_w[54] -> EX control -> EX/MEM | 5.29 | 3.13 | 2.16 |\\n\\nCandidate seeds 1 and 2 start at cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_144.Q and\\nend at cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_17.D. Seed 3 starts at\\ncpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_143.Q and ends at\\ncpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_24.D. Each path traverses six LUT4 arcs and\\none MUX2_LUT5 arc. In seed 2, a single EX-control connection from\\n(4,19) to (17,17) contributes 1.95 ns of routing. The seven-arc\\nEX-control cone remains critical even though its RTL is unchanged.\\n\\nThe register-file network became smaller and structurally shallower,\\nbut the competing EX-control path and placement variation erased that\\nbenefit in the required median. Candidate seed Fmax ranges from 143.86\\nto 188.96 MHz (sample SD 22.95 MHz; starting SD 5.57 MHz), so the best\\nseed does not justify claiming an improvement. LUT resources decreased\\n247 (-4.48%). No further seeds were substituted or timing flags changed.\\nThe known timing-arc coverage limitation still applies: unreported paths\\nare not evidence of zero delay, including divider and multiplier paths.\\n\\nThe separate required canonical 6 KB run passed on both designs. Built\\nonce with the unchanged benchmark Makefile and explicit\\n`COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, with ITERATIONS=10, then ran the\\nsame ELF on both simulators with `--bench --istall --dstall`. Both full\\noutput records are identical:\\n\\n- Start marker: cycle 56,393; stop marker: cycle 32,893,064.\\n- Marker-bracketed cycles: 32,836,671; final EBREAK cycle: 32,900,232.\\n- `ebreak:true`, `maxcycles_hit:false`, `oob:false`, `bench_bracketed:true`.\\n- seedcrc=0x8a02; crclist=0xd4b0; crcmatrix=0xbe52;\\n  crcstate=0x5e47; crcfinal=0x5949; iterations=10.\\n- UART includes the exact `Correct operation validated.` literal.\\n\\nThe 6 KB UART's `CoreMark Size : 2000` is the per-algorithm size after\\ndivision of the total working set among three algorithms. Its printed\\n`Compiler flags : -O2 -march=rv32im` is existing hardcoded metadata in\\ncore_portme.h; the actual saved compiler command uses -O3 and\\n-DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000. Neither string was changed. These supplemental\\n6 KB cycles are separate from the evaluator's 2 KB fitness measurement.\\n\\nELF SHA-256 identifiers:\\n\\n- Shared evaluator 2 KB CoreMark:\\n  `c842bd5ae85d17ca8161f1f70e6c81a6778d950fb3aa76cafba88af6601a55f2`.\\n- Shared supplemental 6 KB CoreMark:\\n  `ae8a1e5ec542bc090ae8dbc1af8245d4d0bf21ea6c79f7923e58ae1ba6720c3a`.\\n- Shared selftest:\\n  `f3580679e10d4db8222450d65f41205266ef49366ed2fd2250bbfa5c69a5848a`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:02:00.640594Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r5s0 (regression, -7.44%): Balanced one-hot read/bypass trees in reg_file.sv reduced LUTs and mapped read depth yet lost 7.44% median Fmax as placement worsened the unchanged EX-control-to-EX/MEM path, showing that local mux simplification can regress routed timing despite identical CoreMark cycles.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz with 5,513 LUT4.\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment records show gains from divider\\nisolation and retiming, regressions from replacing its arithmetic, and\\na liveness failure when completion acquired one extra clock. The saved\\nr4s1 timing reports, whose RTL matches the current source, now report\\ndifferent worst paths: register-file data to an ID operand register\\n(6.103 ns), ID/EX control through execution selection to a normal EX/MEM\\nresult register (5.821 ns), and instruction-bus stimulus to an ID operand\\nregister (5.728 ns). These paths motivate changing pipeline storage\\ncontrol rather than adding another divider arithmetic transformation.\\nID currently clears its entire packed register on flush, and EX clears\\nits entire register on a bubble or divide wait while also conditionally\\nholding the normal result on divide acceptance. Those actions couple\\nwide data storage to instruction-validity and completion decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz with 5,513 LUT4.\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment records show gains from divider\\nisolation and retiming, regressions from replacing its arithmetic, and\\na liveness failure when completion acquired one extra clock. The saved\\nr4s1 timing reports, whose RTL matches the current source, now report\\ndifferent worst paths: register-file data to an ID operand register\\n(6.103 ns), ID/EX control through execution selection to a normal EX/MEM\\nresult register (5.821 ns), and instruction-bus stimulus to an ID operand\\nregister (5.728 ns). These paths motivate changing pipeline storage\\ncontrol rather than adding another divider arithmetic transformation.\\nID currently clears its entire packed register on flush, and EX clears\\nits entire register on a bubble or divide wait while also conditionally\\nholding the normal result on divide acceptance. Those actions couple\\nwide data storage to instruction-validity and completion decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the existing ID/EX and EX/MEM boundaries into independently\\nupdated payload and control banks. Bubble insertion should clear valid\\nand the complete control word, while wide payload registers retain\\ntheir normal capture/hold behavior. This is one pipeline-storage change:\\npreserve the pipeline depth, instruction dispatch schedule, arithmetic,\\nforwarding priority, and existing eight-clock divider protocol.\\n\\nIn ID, retain the exact reset/flush/stall precedence for valid and ctrl.\\nCapture a decoded control word only with a valid incoming instruction;\\nan accepted invalid input installs zero control and valid=0. Separate\\npc, instr, immediates, source values and register addresses from that\\nbubble-clearing bank. Give the payload its existing synchronous reset\\nto zero, followed by capture when !stall and retention when stalled.\\nFlush must not appear in the payload reset, data mux, or write enable.\\nIn particular, a simultaneous load-use stall and flush clears control\\nwhile retaining inactive payload; a dmem or division hold preserves\\nthe complete active instruction. A redirect may capture discarded\\npayload, but it must install an invalid, control-zero entry.\\n\\nIn EX, preserve the current control-bank transitions: reset clears it;\\ndmem stall holds it; otherwise div_wait or !in.valid installs a bubble;\\notherwise capture ctrl_with_trap and valid. Preserve the registered\\ndivider-bank selector and its ownership rules. Independently capture\\nthe normal ALU-or-PC+4 result and instruction payload on every !stall\\nedge, with synchronous reset to zero. Allow this normal result bank to\\nupdate during invalid cycles, divide computation and divide acceptance.\\nRemove div_wait, div_accept and in.valid from its capture enable and\\nbubble-zero mux. On divide acceptance, the registered divider selector\\nmakes the divider bank authoritative, so the simultaneously captured\\nnormal result is inactive. Preserve saved post-forward divide operands\\nin the accepting metadata and all existing resolved-PC/trap behavior.\\nEach state bit must have exactly one procedural owner after the split.\\n\\nA memory stall must still freeze all EX/MEM payload, controls and bank\\nselection, keeping address, write data and byte enables stable until\\nacceptance. Keep mem_addr connected directly to the normal registered\\nresult, without validity or divider-selection muxes on its output, so\\nthe wrapper can still absorb its address register into block RAM.\\nAn inactive address may change only when no memory request is owned.\\nInvalid ID/EX and EX/MEM entries must retain zero control: downstream\\nhazard, forwarding and memory logic currently rely on those controls,\\nso stale payload must never assert a write, request, redirect or launch.\\nKeep MEM/WB unchanged, including its deliberate retention of forwarding\\ndata and reg_write metadata when a dmem hold clears retirement valid.\\n\\nExpect five percent higher fitness from shorter payload capture paths\\nwith unchanged marker-bracketed instruction cycles. This is a prediction,\\nnot a measured gain: capture-enable mapping, routing, the regfile read\\ntree, ordinary ALU selection or divider recurrence may still limit Fmax.\\nCheck the synthesized data/reset/enable cones to establish that bubble\\nand completion decisions were actually removed from wide payload\\nstorage; do not merely move those decisions to an output-side mux.\\nInspect all three routed seeds and preserve the known timing-report\\nlimitations rather than treating omitted arcs as zero delay.\\n\\nKeep nret=1, the external port set, decoder legality, all traps, ordered\\nretirement, RV32M arithmetic and both real/ALTOPS schedules unchanged.\\nRetain all correctness gates and formal depths. Run existing real and\\nALTOPS unit suites, formal including liveness, stalled ISS cosimulation,\\nUART/CRC/OOB/marker checks and the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation.\\nUse identical binaries and stall schedules for cycle comparisons. The\\nprior implementation notes distinguish a 2 KB evaluator workload from\\nthe requested canonical 6 KB run; preserve that distinction and verify\\nthe 6 KB CRCs without modifying the benchmark or evaluator contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split control/valid updates from payload updates at the existing\\nID/EX boundary. Preserve control flush priority and true holds;\\nmake accepted invalid inputs control-zero. Reset payload only on\\nreset, capture it on !stall, and remove flush from its storage\\ncontrols. Preserve all valid instruction fields and cycle timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the EX/MEM control/bank-selector state from payload and\\nnormal-result storage. Capture payload and the normal result on\\nevery non-memory-stalled edge, including divide and bubble edges,\\nwhile maintaining control-zero bubbles, accepted divide metadata,\\nsaved operands, exact bank ownership and synchronous reset values.\\nPreserve complete memory holds and the direct registered address\\nconnection. Do not change the divider or arithmetic datapaths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all RVFI/ISS comparisons, arithmetic vectors, exact divide\\nlatency and retirement-spacing checks, and accepted-bus-effect\\ncounts. Add distinct stale payloads around load-use bubbles,\\nunavailable instruction responses, redirects, division waits and\\noverlapping memory holds. Check invalid ID/EX and EX/MEM control\\nwords are zero, active held payload is stable, and only valid\\ninstructions produce ordered retirements and memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every accepted-result, operand, latency, response-hold and\\ndivider-bank ownership assertion. Replace representation-specific\\nwhole-bundle-zero comparisons during invalid cycles with explicit\\nvalid/control-zero checks and checks that inactive payload cannot\\nbecome an architectural effect. Exercise changing normal-bank data\\nthrough divide completion, the first dependent consumer, reset and\\nprolonged owner stalls. Require stable direct memory addresses for\\nheld memory owners and correct addresses for every valid access.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split control/valid updates from payload updates at the existing\\nID/EX boundary. Preserve control flush priority and true holds;\\nmake accepted invalid inputs control-zero. Reset payload only on\\nreset, capture it on !stall, and remove flush from its storage\\ncontrols. Preserve all valid instruction fields and cycle timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the EX/MEM control/bank-selector state from payload and\\nnormal-result storage. Capture payload and the normal result on\\nevery non-memory-stalled edge, including divide and bubble edges,\\nwhile maintaining control-zero bubbles, accepted divide metadata,\\nsaved operands, exact bank ownership and synchronous reset values.\\nPreserve complete memory holds and the direct registered address\\nconnection. Do not change the divider or arithmetic datapaths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all RVFI/ISS comparisons, arithmetic vectors, exact divide\\nlatency and retirement-spacing checks, and accepted-bus-effect\\ncounts. Add distinct stale payloads around load-use bubbles,\\nunavailable instruction responses, redirects, division waits and\\noverlapping memory holds. Check invalid ID/EX and EX/MEM control\\nwords are zero, active held payload is stable, and only valid\\ninstructions produce ordered retirements and memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every accepted-result, operand, latency, response-hold and\\ndivider-bank ownership assertion. Replace representation-specific\\nwhole-bundle-zero comparisons during invalid cycles with explicit\\nvalid/control-zero checks and checks that inactive payload cannot\\nbecome an architectural effect. Exercise changing normal-bank data\\nthrough divide completion, the first dependent consumer, reset and\\nprolonged owner stalls. Require stable direct memory addresses for\\nheld memory owners and correct addresses for every valid access.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 409.8, \"delta_pct\": 7.14, \"vs_baseline\": 44.9, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5490, \"ff\": 2112, \"seeds\": [184.06, 191.13, 179.89], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the pipeline-storage hypothesis. Local evaluation reports **409.80 fitness**, up **7.14%** from 382.48, with unchanged benchmark cycles. Median reported Fmax increases from 171.79 to **184.06 MHz**. Resources are **5,490 LUT4 and 2,112 DFF**, versus 5,513 LUT4 and 2,087 DFF. All local correctness gates passed. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\\n\\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv` and `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. Each retains its packed output representation but assigns disjoint control and payload fields in separate clocked processes. Every state bit has one procedural owner; Yosys `check` reports zero problems.\\n\\nID's complete 22-bit control word and valid bit retain reset/flush priority over stall. Unstalled invalid inputs now install zero control. The 175 payload bits reset synchronously only on reset and capture whenever `!stall`. A simultaneous load-use stall and flush clears control while retaining payload; an unstalled redirect captures discarded payload beneath an invalid, control-zero entry. Memory and division holds retain the complete active ID instruction.\\n\\nEX's control, valid, and registered divider selector retain their original reset, memory-hold, bubble, and acceptance transitions. All normal-result and instruction-payload fields reset synchronously and capture on every `!stall` edge. Neither bubble insertion nor divide acceptance suppresses or zeroes that capture. Saved post-forward divide operands remain selected into accepting metadata; branch targets, resolved next PC, and trap handling retain their existing equations. The registered divider bank remains authoritative on acceptance. `mem_addr` remains directly connected to the normal registered result, and a memory hold freezes that result, payload, controls, and selection.\\n\\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. Pipeline depth, dispatch timing, eight-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\\n\\nTests retain all existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response-hold, ownership, ISS/RVFI, retirement-spacing, and accepted-memory-effect assertions. Invalid-cycle whole-bundle-zero comparisons in the EX tests were replaced with valid/control-zero checks; whole-bundle reset checks remain. Added coverage includes a direct ID-stage test and directed pipeline overlaps between memory holds and load-use, division, and redirects, plus unavailable fetches with distinct payloads. The EX test varies inactive normal data through computation and acceptance, then checks a prolonged owner hold and the first dependent consumer. To vary that bank during an active divide, the isolated EX test deliberately combines DIV with PC+4 selection; this is a synthetic interface stress vector. Integrated tests use legal decoded instructions and the unchanged ISS.\\n\\nLocal validation on the final RTL:\\n\\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 140 cases passed, no skips**. Includes 33 real and 33 ALTOPS ALU cases, both EX-bank modes, 30 decoder cases, and all 22 pipeline cases.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, on the first invocation. Includes `liveness_ch0`, unique retirement, cover, and illegal-instruction checks. No counterexample and no formal fix attempts. The unchanged nret=1 wrapper/configuration and all formal depths were used. This is the contract's ALTOPS formal gate; real-arithmetic deep formal was not run because arithmetic did not change.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and evaluator CoreMark.\\n- Additional C++ selftest cosimulation with both stall flags and seeds 1, 19, and 99: **PASS**, all 115 retirements compared against the unchanged ISS per run. Starting and final RTL also have identical retirement-cycle sequences for each seed.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: **PASS**, all three routed seeds and the UART, CRC, OOB, and marker gates.\\n- Source verification confirms 154 staged source/contract files, all 53 formal PASS results, exact agreement between staged formal RTL and submitted RTL, and agreement between the starting RTL snapshot and HEAD. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\\n\\nThe synthesized storage cones establish the intended structural change. In module-preserving Yosys `proc; opt` output, all ID payload flops changed from synchronous `reset OR flush` to reset alone, while retaining negative-polarity `stall` enables and direct payload data. EX's normal-result data cone previously included `div_accept`, `div_wait`, and incoming valid; its enable also included those decisions. The final data cone contains only ordinary ALU output, PC, and architectural result selection; its enable is only `!stall` and synchronous reset is only reset. Other EX payload fields likewise lose their bubble-zero data muxes. Saved-operand selection still uses `is_div`, as required for accepting metadata.\\n\\nThe actual Gowin netlist has 160 unique surviving ID payload DFFRE cells, all with the common capture enable and `cpu.reset` reset. It has 190 unique surviving EX payload DFFR cells with `cpu.reset`; the FPGA wrapper ties memory ready high, so EX's memory-hold enable optimizes away. All 32 normal-result bits are directly registered, and the memory-address vector is identical to those register bits. Other output payload fields directly alias their stored fields; no replacement output-side bubble mux was added. The expected four BSRAMs and one MULT36X36 remain in every routed seed. Counts account for synthesis merging shared fields and pruning unused metadata.\\n\\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported worst path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 184.06 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.43 | 3.06 / 2.38 |\\n| 2 | 191.13 | Wrapper instruction stimulus lfsr[20] to ID rs2_val[3] | 5.23 | 2.61 / 2.63 |\\n| 3 | 179.89 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.56 | 3.06 / 2.50 |\\n\\nFigures are rounded independently by nextpnr. Tools are Yosys 0.64 (`d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61`) and nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`. The requested standalone lint used Verilator 5.047; the unit and cosimulation gates used the installed Verilator 5.046 with the Conda Python environment containing cocotb and YAML. The eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\\n\\nTiming remains a reported tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish complete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multiplier, or other forwarding paths have timing margin.\\n\\nThe checked-in evaluator uses aggregate **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**, whereas the requested supplemental run uses aggregate **6 KB**. The evaluator and its expected CRCs remain unchanged. The separate 6 KB ELF was built from identical benchmark sources with the existing Makefile options `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`. Starting and final RTL were freshly simulated with the exact same ELF per size and identical `--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed.\\n\\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both RTL versions | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Starting / final iter/sec |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 382.48 / 409.80 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 52.32 / 56.05 |\\n\\nRates use the recorded starting 171.79 MHz and measured final 184.06 MHz; starting FPGA routing was not rerun. Both versions reach EBREAK, report no OOB access, provide valid marker brackets, and produce identical UART output for each workload. Both include the literal `Correct operation validated.` and ten iterations. Evaluator CRCs are seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. The separately verified **6 KB CRCs are seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART description says -O2; the actual compile commands use -O3.\\n\\nAll build artifacts and scratch contract copies are under the configured TMPDIR:\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r5s1-storage-78f91x4q`.\\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, the nine result XMLs, `cosim-output.log`, `stalled-iss.json`, `fpga-output.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`, `storage-cones.json`, both premap JSONs, `workload-comparison.json`, individual baseline/candidate benchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`. This notes file is at the worktree root as explicitly requested in instruction 5. All remaining changes are confined to bench RTL and test_*.py files.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:04:12.733398Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r5s1 (improvement, +7.14%): Separating ID/EX and EX/MEM payload capture from bubble/flush and divide-completion control removed those decisions from wide data/reset/enable cones, raising reported median Fmax 7.14% with unchanged CoreMark cycles while requiring zero control on invalid entries and full payload/control retention during memory stalls.\"}\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner still fetches sequentially and loses one useful\\nfetch/decode slot on an advancing taken branch. The predictor records\\nhyp-20260906-003-r1s2 through hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 and LESSONS.md show\\nuseful cycle savings but insufficient timing preservation. The combined\\nstatic backward-branch/JAL predictor saved 5.16% of measured cycles,\\nwhereas JAL alone saved only 1.10%. These separate experiments suggest\\nconditional branches offer the larger opportunity; their difference is\\nan estimate, not a measured branch-only result. Tagged BTBs introduced\\ntraining-enable timing paths, and both static designs exposed paths\\nending at the wide ID/EX payload reset. The next predictor should move\\nprediction-dependent decision work into decode and confine recovery\\nfanout to the small pipeline control bank.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner still fetches sequentially and loses one useful\\nfetch/decode slot on an advancing taken branch. The predictor records\\nhyp-20260906-003-r1s2 through hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 and LESSONS.md show\\nuseful cycle savings but insufficient timing preservation. The combined\\nstatic backward-branch/JAL predictor saved 5.16% of measured cycles,\\nwhereas JAL alone saved only 1.10%. These separate experiments suggest\\nconditional branches offer the larger opportunity; their difference is\\nan estimate, not a measured branch-only result. Tagged BTBs introduced\\ntraining-enable timing paths, and both static designs exposed paths\\nending at the wide ID/EX payload reset. The next predictor should move\\nprediction-dependent decision work into decode and confine recovery\\nfanout to the small pipeline control bank.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one static backward-taken/forward-not-taken conditional-branch\\npredictor with decode-encoded repair polarity and control-only squashes.\\nThe architectural claim is that roughly four percent fewer cycles can\\nsurvive FPGA routing when prediction adds neither a second outcome XOR\\nin the EX repair path nor repair-driven reset of the ID/EX data payload.\\nThis is a refinement of the failed r3s2 control path, not a claim that\\nits already-tested predictor policy alone fixes timing. Keep JAL and\\nJALR sequentially predicted. Add no target table, direction table,\\ntraining mechanism, return stack, or pipeline stage.\\n\\nIn IF, predecode raw imem_data for opcode 0x63 and legal funct3 values\\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7. Predict taken only for instruction bit 31 set, aligned\\nfetch PC, and instruction bit 8 clear, which ensures an aligned negative\\nB displacement. Construct only the sign-extended B immediate and one\\nfull-width PC-relative target adder; compute PC+4 independently. Select\\nthe target with raw eligibility, never an acceptance-qualified signal.\\nApply that selection only when the fetch advances into ID/EX. Preserve\\nreset, existing EX redirect, accepted fetch advance, then hold priority.\\nKeep imem_addr directly connected to the PC register and preserve\\nmodulo-2^32 arithmetic. Carry raw predicted_taken alongside the fetch;\\nit has meaning only for the same valid instruction accepted on that edge.\\nA prediction must not flush the branch that produced it.\\n\\nIn ID, retain the architectural decoded branch_op and separately register\\nrepair_invert = dec_branch_op[0] XOR in.predicted_taken. Also register\\nbranch_repair_enable = dec_is_branch AND target_word_aligned, computing\\nalignment from the two-bit sum of in.pc[1:0] and imm[1:0], independent of\\nthe full target adder. Capture predicted_taken, repair_invert, and this\\nenable with the instruction and hold them during pipeline stalls.\\n\\nEX shares the existing forwarded equality, signed-less-than, and\\nunsigned-less-than comparisons. Select base_cond using branch_op[2:1]:\\nequality for 00, signed less-than for 10, unsigned less-than for 11;\\nreserved class 01 is disabled. For legal branches, architectural\\nbranch_cond is base_cond XOR the original branch_op[0], whereas the\\ndirection-mismatch condition is base_cond XOR registered repair_invert.\\nThus predicted direction is folded into the polarity before ID/EX;\\ndo not form architectural branch_cond XOR predicted_taken in the global\\nrepair cone. Keep the original outcome for traps and resolved RVFI PC.\\n\\nThe branch contribution to redirect is branch_repair_enable AND the\\ndirection-mismatch condition. Combine it with the existing legal,\\naligned JAL/JALR redirect contribution and qualify with in.valid. A\\npredicted backward-branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken\\naligned branch repairs to its branch target. Select repair target using\\nregistered predicted_taken and instruction class, independently of the\\nlive comparison result. Correct predictions cause no redirect or bubble.\\nThe prequalified branch enable suppresses redirect for an unpredicted\\nmisaligned target, preserving the current taken-only trap and PC+4\\ncontinuation. JAL/JALR links, JALR bit-zero clearing, jump alignment\\nchecks, and actual pc_next remain architectural, never predicted values.\\nPreserve the winner's existing redirect priority during imem and dmem\\nstalls. A held EX branch may keep asserting the same repair until it\\nadvances; add no div_wait or generic EX-accept dependency to this cone.\\n\\nMake narrow squash distribution an integral part of this predictor's\\nrecovery mechanism. IF should emit raw instruction bits with validity\\nindicating flush/unavailability, instead of placing a redirect-driven\\nNOP mux across the instruction word. In ID/EX, reset still initializes\\nthe complete register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete\\nctrl_t bank, and the three predictor control bits. An invalid advancing\\nfetch likewise produces zero controls and valid=0, regardless of its raw\\nopcode. All PC, operand, immediate, register-address, and instruction\\npayload flops use the ordinary !stall capture enable independently of\\ndynamic flush; they may capture discarded data when a repair advances.\\nDo not reintroduce repair as a wide payload clear, enable, or output mask.\\nDuring holds retain the complete accepted instruction and its metadata;\\npreserve the existing flush-versus-hold priority from hazard_unit.\\nZero bubble controls are essential because hazards and forwarding inspect\\nsome controls without a valid gate. Check that invalid ID/EX bubbles,\\neven with retained load/store/jump/divide payloads, never act as producers,\\nredirect, write, or issue requests.\\nThe existing EX/MEM bubble behavior and retained MEM/WB forwarding data\\nremain intact. This is localized predictor recovery, not an overhaul of\\nthe downstream pipeline or ALU.\\n\\nThe estimated net gain is 3%, with low confidence. A four-percent cycle\\nreduction and one-percent Fmax loss would give approximately 3.1% higher\\nfitness. The current comparison is 382.48 iter/sec, 171.79 MHz, and\\n4,491,485 marker-window cycles; at exactly four-percent fewer cycles the\\ncandidate needs approximately 164.92 MHz just to break even. Static\\ndirection errors, the remaining fetch adder, operand enables, and FPGA\\nplacement could still erase the gain. Measure the actual branch-only\\ncycle reduction and all three routed Fmax seeds; inspect whether the\\nrepair net still reaches wide ID/EX payload reset, CE, or data muxes.\\nTreat the unchanged evaluator as authoritative and keep any supplemental\\n6 KB measurement distinct, as the prior logs document a fixture-size\\ndiscrepancy. Run existing lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB,\\nmarker-window, resource, and three-seed FPGA gates without modification.\\nPreserve nret=1, all hard invariants, divider/result-bank ownership, and\\nthe directly registered dmem address path. Scope is cores/bench only.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw legal backward-branch predecode, a B-immediate-only target\\nadder, raw predicted_taken metadata, and accepted-fetch PC selection.\\nEmit raw instruction payload with validity for flush and imem bubbles\\nso predictor repairs do not mask the instruction word. Keep direct\\nPC-to-imem addressing and existing redirect/stall priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to if_id_t, and predicted_taken, repair_invert,\\nand branch_repair_enable to id_ex_t. Preserve architectural branch\\ncontrols, all resolved PC fields, and every RVFI interface width.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch repair polarity and low-bit target alignment at\\ndecode. Separate ID/EX payload capture from control/valid squashing:\\nreset initializes everything, flush or an advancing invalid fetch\\nzeros controls and predictor bits, and data capture depends only on\\nthe ordinary stall enable. Keep the complete instruction on holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Share forwarded comparison primitives between architectural branch\\nresolution and the independently registered repair polarity. Use\\ndecode-certified branch alignment in the redirect contribution and\\npredicted_taken for fallthrough-versus-target recovery. Preserve\\nactual branch outcomes, trap/link/RVFI behavior, existing jump\\nredirects, divider protocol, result banks, and registered mem_addr.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused IF tests for every legal/reserved branch funct3, backward\\nand forward offsets, target alignment, full-width carry/wrap, and\\nsequential prediction of jumps and other opcodes. Check raw payload\\nvalidity, accepted prediction correspondence, reset/repair priority,\\nchanging instructions during holds, and unavailable instruction data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline tests for all six branch conditions and both outcomes,\\nloop exits, zero-bubble correct predictions, forwarded/load-dependent\\noperands, misaligned traps, and unchanged JAL/JALR behavior. Exercise\\nrepair during long dmem holds, imem stalls, and divider waits; killed\\nraw loads/stores/divides; consecutive repairs; and reset. Preserve\\narchitectural assertions and check control-zero bubbles, held metadata,\\nexact RVFI order/PC/traps, accepted stores, and divider launches against\\nthe unchanged ISS. Add directed ID checks that squash does not govern\\npayload capture while zero controls prevent all invalid side effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout for the three added predictor bits,\\ndefaulting them to zero in ordinary ALU/divider/jump constructors.\\nRetain every result-bank, divider, forwarding, reset, and directly\\nregistered memory-address assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw legal backward-branch predecode, a B-immediate-only target\\nadder, raw predicted_taken metadata, and accepted-fetch PC selection.\\nEmit raw instruction payload with validity for flush and imem bubbles\\nso predictor repairs do not mask the instruction word. Keep direct\\nPC-to-imem addressing and existing redirect/stall priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to if_id_t, and predicted_taken, repair_invert,\\nand branch_repair_enable to id_ex_t. Preserve architectural branch\\ncontrols, all resolved PC fields, and every RVFI interface width.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch repair polarity and low-bit target alignment at\\ndecode. Separate ID/EX payload capture from control/valid squashing:\\nreset initializes everything, flush or an advancing invalid fetch\\nzeros controls and predictor bits, and data capture depends only on\\nthe ordinary stall enable. Keep the complete instruction on holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Share forwarded comparison primitives between architectural branch\\nresolution and the independently registered repair polarity. Use\\ndecode-certified branch alignment in the redirect contribution and\\npredicted_taken for fallthrough-versus-target recovery. Preserve\\nactual branch outcomes, trap/link/RVFI behavior, existing jump\\nredirects, divider protocol, result banks, and registered mem_addr.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused IF tests for every legal/reserved branch funct3, backward\\nand forward offsets, target alignment, full-width carry/wrap, and\\nsequential prediction of jumps and other opcodes. Check raw payload\\nvalidity, accepted prediction correspondence, reset/repair priority,\\nchanging instructions during holds, and unavailable instruction data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline tests for all six branch conditions and both outcomes,\\nloop exits, zero-bubble correct predictions, forwarded/load-dependent\\noperands, misaligned traps, and unchanged JAL/JALR behavior. Exercise\\nrepair during long dmem holds, imem stalls, and divider waits; killed\\nraw loads/stores/divides; consecutive repairs; and reset. Preserve\\narchitectural assertions and check control-zero bubbles, held metadata,\\nexact RVFI order/PC/traps, accepted stores, and divider launches against\\nthe unchanged ISS. Add directed ID checks that squash does not govern\\npayload capture while zero controls prevent all invalid side effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout for the three added predictor bits,\\ndefaulting them to zero in ordinary ALU/divider/jump constructors.\\nRetain every result-bank, divider, forwarding, reset, and directly\\nregistered memory-address assertion.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 338.91, \"delta_pct\": -11.39, \"vs_baseline\": 19.83, \"fmax_mhz\": 146.05, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5602, \"ff\": 2106, \"seeds\": [153.21, 138.68, 146.05], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis. All local correctness gates pass. The branch-only\\npredictor reduces the unchanged evaluator's marker-window cycles by **4.05%**,\\nbut median routed Fmax falls from 171.79 to **146.05 MHz**. Fitness is\\n**338.91 iter/sec**, versus 382.48 for the starting winner (**-11.39%**).\\nThe proposed timing-preservation claim did not survive routing.\\n\\nIF predecodes raw opcode 0x63 and funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7. Negative displacement,\\ninstruction bit 8 clear, and aligned PC qualify static taken prediction.\\nOne sign-extended B immediate and full-width target adder are independent of\\nPC+4; the target mux uses raw eligibility. PC priority is reset, existing EX\\nredirect, accepted fetch (`!stall && !flush`), then hold. All arithmetic wraps\\nmodulo 2^32 and `imem_addr` remains directly connected to the PC register.\\nJAL/JALR and all other opcodes predict sequentially. Raw `predicted_taken`\\ntravels with raw instruction bits, with validity identifying discarded or\\nunavailable fetches. Predictions do not flush their producing branch.\\n\\nID retains architectural `branch_op` and registers the prediction, its\\ndecode-folded repair polarity, and legal-branch target alignment. Alignment\\nuses only the two-bit sum of PC and immediate low bits. Reset clears the\\ncomplete ID/EX register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete control\\nbank, and all three predictor bits. An advancing invalid fetch also clears\\nthose controls. Payload capture depends only on ordinary `!stall`, including\\non repair edges; a load-use flush during a hold retains payload and clears\\ncontrols. The existing hazard priority preserves the complete accepted\\ninstruction during dmem/divider holds.\\n\\nEX shares forwarded equality/signed-less/unsigned-less comparisons and selects\\ntheir base condition with architectural `branch_op[2:1]`. The architectural\\noutcome uses original polarity; repair uses the registered polarity directly,\\nwithout a second architectural-outcome XOR with prediction. Decode-qualified\\nbranch alignment gates the repair contribution. Predicted branch exits repair\\nto PC+4; unpredicted taken branches repair to the branch target. Recovery target\\nselection is independent of the live comparison. Jump redirects, taken-only\\nalignment traps, JAL/JALR link values, JALR bit-zero clearing, and resolved RVFI\\nPC values remain architectural. No EX-accept or div_wait qualifier was added\\nto redirect; held branches continue asserting their repair until advancement.\\n\\nThe functional changes are confined to `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`,\\n`id_stage.sv`, and `ex_stage.sv`. `core.sv` and `hazard_unit.sv` have comment-only\\nupdates to describe raw invalid payloads and control squashes. The divider,\\nALU, result banks, downstream bubbles, retained MEM/WB forwarding data,\\ndirectly registered memory-address path, nret=1 interface, decoder, and all\\nevaluator contracts are unchanged. No predictor table, training, return stack,\\nor additional pipeline stage was added.\\n\\nTests update the EX constructor's packed layout, defaulting the three added\\nbits to zero. New IF tests cover all eight funct3 values, displacement bits\\nand extremes, alignment, full-width carry/wrap, every non-branch opcode,\\nchanging held/unavailable instruction words, raw metadata, reset, and redirect\\npriority. New ID tests directly check the two-bit alignment sum, both repair\\npolarities, full metadata holds, reset, and independent payload capture during\\nflush and invalid advances. Pipeline tests preserve every original assertion\\nand compare complete retirement streams against the unchanged Python ISS.\\nThey cover all six branch conditions in both directions and outcomes, exact\\nzero-bubble prediction and one-slot repair timing, loop exits, forwarding and\\nload dependencies, branches held behind divides, 18-cycle memory holds,\\nimem stalls with changing raw data, consecutive repairs, reset during repair,\\nkilled loads/stores/divides/jumps/branches, reserved encodings, misalignment\\ntraps, and unchanged jump links. Per-cycle assertions check zero bubble\\ncontrols, metadata/payload capture, fetch priority, accepted memory requests,\\ndivider launches, result-bank ownership, and RVFI PC/order continuity.\\n\\nLocal validation used scratch copies under the configured TMPDIR, avoiding\\ngenerated writes to protected evaluator directories in the submitted tree.\\nThe available Conda Python environment supplied cocotb, pytest, YAML, and\\nclick; the default Python lacked cocotb/click. No evaluator or dependency\\nsource was modified.\\n\\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS**, no warnings.\\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: **PASS**, all 10 runners, including real and\\n  ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank checks. The final expanded pipeline runner\\n  was rerun after adding fetch-priority and additional hold coverage:\\n  **26 pipeline cases passed**. Final XML totals are 145 cocotb cases,\\n  zero failures across the 10 runners.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  **PASS: 53 passed, 0 failed**. The unchanged script auto-selected nret=1\\n  wrappers/checks. One local formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts,\\n  no counterexample. Every staged RTL source matches the final worktree.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and CoreMark.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\\n  **PASS** for all three placements and the CRC/UART, OOB, marker-window\\n  and resource evaluation; fitness regresses as reported above.\\n- `git diff --check`: **PASS**. SHA-256 evidence verifies 328 staged source,\\n  test, and contract files against the worktree. No other core or core.yaml\\n  was edited. The explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root.\\n\\nRouted results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61).\\nEvery reported frequency below is the final post-route value from the\\nunchanged nextpnr seed script, with no excluded seed.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 153.21 | ID/EX ctrl.is_branch RESET | 6.53 | 2.84 / 3.69 |\\n| 2 | 138.68 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[2] RESET | 7.21 | 2.67 / 4.54 |\\n| 3 | 146.05 | ID/EX valid RESET | 6.85 | 2.84 / 4.01 |\\n\\nThe worst paths start at the FPGA wrapper's LFSR instruction source and pass\\nthrough the raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold control logic before the\\nnarrow ID/EX reset bank. The mapped netlist has 160 physical ID/EX payload\\nflops reset only by the top-level reset and 24 physical control/valid/predictor\\nflops using the dynamic reset. A combinational fanout traversal from registered\\n`repair_invert` and `branch_repair_enable` reaches those 24 RESET pins and the\\nvalid flop's D input, **no payload RESET, CE, or D pin**. All 184 physical\\nID/EX flops share the ordinary stall-derived CE, which is outside that repair\\nfanout. Synthesis can merge duplicated bundle bits, hence physical counts are\\nsmaller than the 201-bit logical bundle. This verifies narrow recovery\\ndistribution, but does not establish timing margin for other paths.\\n\\nResources are **5,602 LUT4 and 2,106 DFF**, versus the winner's 5,513 LUT4 and\\n2,087 DFF. Four DPX9B block RAMs and one MULT36X36 remain; the memory-address\\nbank has not been replaced by the divider-result mux. At 4,309,463 cycles,\\nbreak-even Fmax against the winner is **164.83 MHz**, well above 146.05 MHz.\\n\\nThe unchanged evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB, -O3** CoreMark despite the\\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its CRC contract is seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. This evaluator\\nresult is authoritative. A separately built aggregate **6 KB, -O3** ELF\\nuses the unchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options\\nand is supplemental only. Both comparisons use fresh baseline-HEAD and\\ncandidate simulations, identical ELFs, ten iterations, and `--istall --dstall`.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Candidate cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / candidate iter/sec |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 4,491,485 | 4,309,463 | 4.05% | 382.48 / 338.91 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 32,836,671 | 31,436,060 | 4.27% | 52.32 / 46.46 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB start/stop markers are baseline (15,801, 4,507,286) and candidate\\n(15,406, 4,324,869). The 6 KB markers are baseline (56,393, 32,893,064) and\\ncandidate (54,555, 31,490,615). Both 6 KB UARTs report seedcrc=8a02,\\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, ten iterations,\\nand `Correct operation validated.` All four fresh runs reach EBREAK with\\nvalid markers and no OOB access. UART's compiler-description string says\\n-O2; the actual recorded build commands use -O3. Rates use the winner's\\nrecorded 171.79 MHz and the candidate's measured median 146.05 MHz.\\n\\nEvidence is under:\\n`$TMPDIR/branch-repair-r5s2-93q3k2v2/`.\\nIt includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `pipeline-final.log`,\\n`cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`,\\n`repair-fanout.json`, `workload-validation.json`, individual baseline/candidate\\nbenchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`.\\nLocal correctness success is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:05:37.270079Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r5s2 (regression, -11.39%): Control-only ID/EX squashes and decode-folded repair polarity do not guarantee predictor timing: raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold paths to control-bank RESET remained critical, lowering median Fmax from 171.79 to 146.05 MHz and outweighing branch-only prediction's 4.05% CoreMark cycle saving.\"}\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Precompute JALR alignment before operand forwarding selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 409.80 iter/sec at 184.06 MHz. Its saved\\nthree-seed timing reports identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding\\nselection and the low JALR addition bits to IF pc[1] as the worst path\\nin seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. The current fourteen RTL files\\nmatch that run's SHA-256 manifest. The path serializes register-tag\\nmatching, operand selection, two native arithmetic arcs, and PC logic.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries in log.jsonl show regressions from\\nbroad ALU one-hot selection, divider carry rewrites, and register-file\\nread restructuring. None attempted this narrow JALR alignment path.\\nMoving its four-input Boolean computation ahead of the late forwarding\\nselector should reduce control-dependent delay with very little area\\ncost and identical instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reports 409.80 iter/sec at 184.06 MHz. Its saved\\nthree-seed timing reports identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding\\nselection and the low JALR addition bits to IF pc[1] as the worst path\\nin seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. The current fourteen RTL files\\nmatch that run's SHA-256 manifest. The path serializes register-tag\\nmatching, operand selection, two native arithmetic arcs, and PC logic.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries in log.jsonl show regressions from\\nbroad ALU one-hot selection, divider carry rewrites, and register-file\\nread restructuring. None attempted this narrow JALR alignment path.\\nMoving its four-input Boolean computation ahead of the late forwarding\\nselector should reduce control-dependent delay with very little area\\ncost and identical instruction timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"In ex_stage.sv, independently compute bit 1 of base + in.imm for each\\nof the three existing rs1 candidates: in.rs1_val, fwd_ex_mem, and\\nfwd_mem_wb. For each candidate base, use the exact Boolean identity\\nbase[1] ^ in.imm[1] ^ (base[0] & in.imm[0]). This depends on only four\\ninput bits and is independent of the forwarding selector. Select the\\nthree one-bit results using the existing fwd_rs1_sel encoding, including\\nits default selection of in.rs1_val. Preserve forward_unit's existing\\nEX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and all load-use interlocks.\\n\\nKeep the existing full-width rs1 + in.imm addition for target bits\\n[31:2], with its original carry propagation and modulo-2^32 semantics.\\nConstruct the JALR target as {jalr_sum[31:2], selected_bit1, 1'b0}.\\nUse selected_bit1 directly for the JALR contribution to misalign_jump;\\nJAL and branch alignment continue using their existing target bits.\\nBoth the target bit and its alignment predicate must use the selected\\nprecomputed bit, so neither consumer retains the old low-sum path.\\nDo not compute the predicate from already-multiplexed rs1, replicate\\nfull-width target adders, or truncate the carry into target bit 2.\\n\\nThis is a combinational micro-optimization within the existing EX\\nstage. Preserve reset, validity, trap qualification, PC+4 links,\\nmisaligned-jump write suppression and linear pc_next, repeated redirect\\nbehavior during holds, raw RVFI operand values, and nret=1. Preserve\\nall pipeline registers, bubble controls, divider/result-bank ownership,\\nordinary ALU arithmetic, and the directly registered memory address.\\nNo ISA, predictor, latency, bus, or evaluation-contract change is needed.\\n\\nExpect approximately four percent higher fitness from higher Fmax with\\nunchanged marker-window cycles. This estimate is unmeasured. Register\\nfile timing already limits seed 2, and placement or upper target-bit\\npaths may limit the gain. Run the unchanged lint, unit, formal, cosim,\\nand three-seed FPGA gates. Inspect mapped connectivity and complete\\nreported timing paths to confirm that low-bit arithmetic precedes\\nforwarding selection; missing timing arcs are not zero-delay evidence.\\nRequire unchanged benchmark cycles, correct CRC/UART/OOB/marker checks,\\nand preserved multiplier sharing and block-RAM inference. Keep the\\nevaluator's recorded 2 KB fixture result distinct from supplemental\\n6 KB validation with the required d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, as documented in\\nprior implementation notes; do not alter either benchmark contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute three candidate JALR sum-bit-1 predicates before rs1 bypass\\nselection and select their one-bit result with fwd_rs1_sel. Use\\nthat result for JALR target bit 1 and alignment detection while\\nretaining the original full addition for bits [31:2], bit-zero\\nclearing, all other target logic, and existing sequential behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add JALR checks against an independent full-width addition reference.\\nExhaust all 1024 combinations of the three candidates' low two\\nbits, immediate low two bits, and all four selector encodings.\\nAdd full-width carry, negative-immediate, and wraparound vectors.\\nCheck targets, redirect suppression, trap and reg_write controls,\\nPC+4 links, pc_next, and unchanged forwarded RVFI source values.\\nPreserve existing divider, bubble, reset, and ownership assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JALR with register-file, immediate EX/MEM, and MEM/WB\\noperands, competing writers, x0, load-use bubbles, and divider-result\\nforwarding. Include aligned and misaligned targets, discarded stores,\\nconsecutive redirects, and overlapping imem/dmem holds. Compare\\ncomplete retirement traces with the unchanged ISS and assert the\\nexisting retirement spacing, hold behavior, PC continuity, and order.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute three candidate JALR sum-bit-1 predicates before rs1 bypass\\nselection and select their one-bit result with fwd_rs1_sel. Use\\nthat result for JALR target bit 1 and alignment detection while\\nretaining the original full addition for bits [31:2], bit-zero\\nclearing, all other target logic, and existing sequential behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add JALR checks against an independent full-width addition reference.\\nExhaust all 1024 combinations of the three candidates' low two\\nbits, immediate low two bits, and all four selector encodings.\\nAdd full-width carry, negative-immediate, and wraparound vectors.\\nCheck targets, redirect suppression, trap and reg_write controls,\\nPC+4 links, pc_next, and unchanged forwarded RVFI source values.\\nPreserve existing divider, bubble, reset, and ownership assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JALR with register-file, immediate EX/MEM, and MEM/WB\\noperands, competing writers, x0, load-use bubbles, and divider-result\\nforwarding. Include aligned and misaligned targets, discarded stores,\\nconsecutive redirects, and overlapping imem/dmem holds. Compare\\ncomplete retirement traces with the unchanged ISS and assert the\\nexisting retirement spacing, hold behavior, PC continuity, and order.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 373.37, \"delta_pct\": -8.89, \"vs_baseline\": 32.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.7, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5525, \"ff\": 2116, \"seeds\": [182.25, 165.26, 167.7], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the JALR alignment precomputation hypothesis. All local correctness\\ngates passed, but the measured fitness **regressed 8.89%, from 409.80 to 373.37**.\\nMedian reported Fmax fell from 184.06 to **167.70 MHz** with unchanged benchmark\\ncycles. The estimated four percent improvement was not observed. Local gate\\nsuccess is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\\n\\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. It computes three independent\\nsum-bit-1 expressions from `in.rs1_val`, `fwd_ex_mem`, and `fwd_mem_wb` using\\n`base[1] ^ in.imm[1] ^ (base[0] & in.imm[0])`. A separate one-bit case mux uses\\nthe existing forwarding encoding: 1 selects EX/MEM, 2 selects MEM/WB, and both\\n0 and 3 default to the ID/EX value. Both JALR target bit 1 and its misalignment\\npredicate consume that selected bit directly. The original full-width\\n`rs1 + in.imm` still supplies target bits [31:2], including the carry from the\\nlow bits and modulo-2^32 wraparound. Target bit 0 remains cleared. No full-width\\nadder was replicated and no predicate is computed from already-forwarded rs1.\\n\\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. JAL/branch alignment,\\nforwarding priority, load-use interlocking, redirect qualification during holds,\\nPC+4 links, trap write suppression, linear trap pc_next, raw RVFI operands,\\npipeline registers and bubbles, divider/result-bank ownership, normal ALU\\narithmetic, directly registered memory addresses, reset, and nret=1 are preserved.\\nNo evaluator, benchmark, wrapper, configuration, or other core was modified.\\nThis explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\\n\\n`test_ex_result_banks.py` retains every existing ownership/divider assertion and\\nadds 1,024 exhaustive combinations of all three candidate low-two-bit values,\\nthe immediate low two bits, and all four selector encodings. Expected targets\\ncome from an independent Python full-width addition. Further vectors cover\\ncarry into bit 2 and high bits, negative immediates, wraparound, PC+4 wrap,\\nindependent rs2 forwarding, held owners, invalid metadata, synchronous reset\\nover hold, and unchanged PC-relative JAL/branch alignment. Checks cover target,\\nredirect, trap/reg_write, PC+4, pc_next, complete captured operand values, and\\nthe direct normal-result address bank. All new EX tests run in real and ALTOPS\\nmodes.\\n\\n`test_pipeline.py` adds register-file, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, competing-writer, x0,\\nload-use, and divider-result JALR cases with aligned/misaligned sums and negative\\nimmediates. Each runs with zero-wait and deterministic random bus stalls.\\nTests verify actual forwarding selections, freshest-writer priority, suppressed\\ntrap links, discarded stores, consecutive redirects, one-cycle ordinary/divide\\nconsumer spacing, the existing two-cycle load-use spacing, and two-cycle target\\nretirement spacing. Directed 12-cycle overlapping imem/dmem holds exercise\\nrepeated aligned redirects and repeatedly suppressed misaligned redirects while\\nMEM/WB retains the needed source. Per-cycle assertions preserve fetch redirect\\npriority, all existing bank/metadata holds, and accepted memory effects. Complete\\nretirement streams are compared through the unchanged ISS and comparator,\\nincluding PC continuity and gap-free order.\\n\\nLocal validation on the final RTL and tests:\\n\\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: **PASS, 9 runners / 148 cocotb cases**,\\n  including 24 pipeline cases, 7 EX cases in each arithmetic mode, and all\\n  existing ALU, decoder, register-file, and ID checks. No failures or skips.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on the first invocation**. The script\\n  auto-selected the unchanged nret=1 wrapper and configuration. Zero formal\\n  fix attempts; no counterexample. This is the required ALTOPS gate. Deep\\n  arithmetic formal was not run because multiplier/divider arithmetic did\\n  not change.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS, selftest and CoreMark**.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\\n  **PASS, all three placements and CRC/UART/OOB/marker checks**, with the\\n  performance regression reported above.\\n- `git diff --check`: **PASS**. SHA-256 verification covers 227 staged source\\n  and contract files. Every formal-staged RTL file matches the submitted\\n  source; the fresh baseline simulation sources match HEAD.\\n\\nThe default Python lacks cocotb; the installed Conda environment supplied the\\nexisting test dependencies without installation or source changes. Standalone\\nlint used Verilator 5.047; the make gates used the installed Verilator 5.046.\\nSynthesis used Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61).\\nThe eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\\nAll generated validation files reside in the configured TMPDIR scratch copy.\\n\\nAll three complete final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Source -> endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 182.25 | EX/MEM rd[3], through forwarding/control, to saved divide rs2 capture CE | 5.49 | 2.02 / 3.46 |\\n| 2 | 165.26 | ID/EX alu_op[2], through ALU result selection, to normal result/address bit 26 | 6.05 | 2.61 / 3.45 |\\n| 3 | 167.70 | ID/EX alu_op[3], through ALU result selection, to normal result/address bit 26 | 5.96 | 2.80 / 3.16 |\\n\\nRounded values are reported independently by nextpnr. The candidate uses\\n**5,525 LUT4 and 2,116 DFF**, versus the recorded winner's 5,490 and 2,112.\\nThe synthesized design retains **one MULT36X36 and four DPX9B block RAMs**;\\nall three packed/routed seeds retain one MULT36X36 and four DP blocks. Every\\nmemory-address bit still comes directly from its normal-result DFF.\\n\\nConnectivity inspection of the actual Gowin netlist found exactly three LUT4\\ncells implementing the candidate predicates. Their truth tables were checked\\nagainst addition for all 16 base/immediate combinations; synthesis inverted\\nthe MEM/WB predicate and absorbed its polarity downstream. Each cell depends\\nonly on its own base low bits and the two immediate bits. EX/MEM and MEM/WB\\nbase bits retain their existing result-bank/writeback selection. Transitive\\nregistered-leaf inspection confirms no forwarding register-tag bits enter any\\ncandidate predicate. The three outputs then enter forwarding-controlled LUT/MUX\\nselection. Actual connectivity paths from each predicate to IF pc[1], captured\\nEX pc_next[1], and the EX trap control contain LUT/MUX logic and no native ALU\\ncells. Thus the intended arithmetic-before-forwarding order survives mapping,\\nbut different paths and placement dominate the reported result.\\n\\nA supplemental seed-1 timing/SDF export reproduced the gate's routed netlist\\nbyte-for-byte and the same Fmax. All four input timing arcs are present for\\neach new predicate LUT4. However, several existing bank selectors and\\ndownstream LUT1/2/3 selection/qualification cells have no IOPATH entries. The\\ndetailed timing report includes IF pc[1] but omits the EX pc_next[1] and trap\\nendpoints. These omissions are **not zero-delay evidence**: no complete physical\\ntiming margin is claimed for those paths. An initial attempt to reload the\\nrouted JSON for export aborted with nextpnr's array2d bounds assertion; rerunning\\nthe unchanged seed from synth.json with export options produced the identical\\nroute. No timing model, constraint, or gate was modified.\\n\\nThe evaluator's recorded **aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ten-iteration fixture** remains\\ndistinct from the supplemental **aggregate 6 KB** validation. The latter ELF\\nuses the unchanged benchmark Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`.\\nFresh baseline and candidate simulations used identical ELFs and identical\\n`--bench --istall --dstall` flags/default deterministic seed for each size.\\nTheir complete final markers and UART strings match exactly.\\n\\n| Workload | Start / stop markers, both versions | Baseline cycles | Candidate cycles | Baseline / candidate iter/sec |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 15,801 / 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 409.80 / 373.37 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 56,393 / 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 56.05 / 51.07 |\\n\\nRates use the recorded winner's 184.06 MHz and the candidate's measured median\\n167.70 MHz; baseline FPGA routing was not rerun. Every simulation reaches\\nEBREAK, reports no OOB access, has valid timing brackets, reports ten iterations,\\nand includes the literal `Correct operation validated.` Evaluator CRCs remain\\nseedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\\nSupplemental 6 KB CRCs are **seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52,\\ncrcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949**. The unchanged UART compiler description says -O2;\\nthe actual recorded build commands use -O3. Neither contract was altered.\\n\\nEvidence directory:\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-r6s0-jalr-mivgf56j`\\n\\nIt contains formal-output.log, unit-tests.log and all nine result XMLs,\\ncosim-output.log, fpga-output.log, the three original nextpnr logs and routed\\nnetlists, jalr-premap.json, mapped-jalr-connectivity.json, timing-seed1.json/SDF,\\ntiming-arc-audit.json, workload-comparison.json, all four baseline/candidate\\nbenchmark logs, sixk/build.log, and verified-source-sha256.json. All launched\\nvalidation commands have completed.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:34:45.310610Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r6s0 (regression, -8.89%): Precomputing JALR sum bit 1 for each rs1 forwarding candidate in ex_stage.sv removed native arithmetic from the mapped alignment cone, but routed ALU-result selection and divider-operand enables became critical, reducing median Fmax from 184.06 to 167.70 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Retime forwarding dependency comparisons into the ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural history shows that removing control decisions from wide\\nexecute paths has helped: r5s1 improved fitness to 409.80 at a reported\\nmedian 184.06 MHz with unchanged marker-bracketed cycles. Its implementation\\nnotes identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding selection and the JALR target\\nadder to IF pc[1] as the worst path in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns.\\nThe remaining seed instead limits on instruction-input to register-file\\nread capture. The current forward_unit performs four five-bit dependency\\ncomparisons after the ID/EX boundary, serially ahead of operand selection\\nand execute arithmetic. The same producer identities are available one\\nstage earlier, so registering their matches may shorten the measured\\nredirect path without another execute cycle. This differs from the failed\\none-hot register-file rewrite and predictor experiments: it relocates\\ndependency detection across an existing pipeline boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The structural history shows that removing control decisions from wide\\nexecute paths has helped: r5s1 improved fitness to 409.80 at a reported\\nmedian 184.06 MHz with unchanged marker-bracketed cycles. Its implementation\\nnotes identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding selection and the JALR target\\nadder to IF pc[1] as the worst path in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns.\\nThe remaining seed instead limits on instruction-input to register-file\\nread capture. The current forward_unit performs four five-bit dependency\\ncomparisons after the ID/EX boundary, serially ahead of operand selection\\nand execute arithmetic. The same producer identities are available one\\nstage earlier, so registering their matches may shorten the measured\\nredirect path without another execute cycle. This differs from the failed\\none-hot register-file rewrite and predictor experiments: it relocates\\ndependency detection across an existing pipeline boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make one structural change: compute and register forwarding dependency\\nmatches alongside ID/EX operand capture, leaving the pipeline depth,\\noperand-data banks, two-bit forwarding selector interface, and instruction\\nscheduling unchanged. Do not add a predictor or alter arithmetic.\\n\\nFor each incoming raw instruction source field s, capture two independent\\nidentity matches on ID's existing !stall capture edge:\\n  near_match = (s != 0) && (s == current_ID_EX.rd)\\n  far_match  = (s != 0) && (s == current_EX_MEM.rd)\\nThere are four bits total for rs1 and rs2. At an actual instruction capture,\\nthose older instructions advance into EX/MEM and MEM/WB respectively. Keep\\nthese bits as separate registered ID-stage outputs rather than changing\\nthe packed pipeline bundles. Give them the same synchronous reset and\\ncapture/hold rule as ID payload, independent of flush; invalid instructions\\nstill have the existing zero control word and cannot act on stale matches.\\nUse raw source fields exactly as the current forwarding logic does, without\\nchanging source-use decoding or the load-use interlock.\\n\\nIn EX, replace live register-number comparisons with the registered matches\\nqualified by the actual destination stage's ctrl.reg_write. Select EX/MEM\\nfirst, then MEM/WB, then the captured register-file operand. Preserve both\\nmatches until this selection: a nearer instruction can lose write permission\\nthrough its existing trap logic, and an eligible farther writer must remain\\navailable. Identity matching alone must not authorize a bypass. Use the\\nsame producer-control qualification as the current implementation, including\\nMEM/WB ctrl.reg_write without an additional valid or wb_w_en gate. A memory\\nhold intentionally clears MEM/WB valid while retaining its forwarding data\\nand control. Keep x0 excluded by construction.\\n\\nPreserve the existing age relationships under every stall. A dmem hold\\nretains the match bits, ID/EX instruction, EX/MEM owner, and MEM/WB forwarding\\npayload. A load-use flush leaves a control-zero bubble; the consumer's\\nmatches are recomputed when it is finally captured, when its load producer\\nis in EX/MEM and will next be in MEM/WB. During blocking division, use these\\nmatches for the initial request's forwarded operands, then retain the\\nexisting saved divide operands as authoritative while older stages drain.\\nDo not reinterpret frozen matches as fresh producer identities during an\\nactive divide. The instruction captured on divide acceptance receives new\\nmatches and can bypass the registered divider result from EX/MEM normally.\\nRetain the existing load-address guard on divide launch, reset cancellation,\\nredirect priorities, and all eight-clock real/ALTOPS divider behavior.\\n\\nThis removes the rd-to-rs comparator layer from the execute-to-redirect\\npath rather than moving operand data or adding latency. Expect about four\\npercent higher fitness with identical cycles for identical binaries and\\nstall schedules. This is unmeasured: the added ID comparison paths, late\\nwrite qualification, register-file path, arithmetic, or placement may limit\\nimprovement. Check that synthesis retains the four match registers and\\nthat routed EX forwarding no longer depends on live destination-number\\nequality; inspect every seed rather than assuming the current second path\\nstays fixed. Preserve direct registered memory addressing and the known\\ntiming-report limitations; omitted timing arcs are not evidence of margin.\\n\\nKeep nret=1, all external ports, decoder legality and traps, ordered RVFI,\\nmemory handshakes, arithmetic, and every evaluation gate unchanged. Run\\nexisting unit suites, unchanged formal including liveness, stalled ISS\\ncosimulation, and three-seed FPGA evaluation. Compare retirement cycles\\nand marker-bracketed cycles against the starting RTL under identical\\ninputs. Retain UART, OOB, and canonical 6 KB CRC validation. Prior notes\\ndistinguish an evaluator 2 KB workload from the supplemental 6 KB run;\\nreport them separately and do not change evaluator or benchmark files.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and register four\\nindependent nonzero-source identity matches on the existing payload\\ncapture edge. Expose them as separate near/far match outputs for both\\nsources. Reset synchronously, retain on stall, and preserve all current\\npayload/control separation, flush priority, and instruction timing.\\nLeave final writer eligibility to the actual destination stages.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four live five-bit equality comparisons with registered\\nnear/far match inputs. Qualify each with the corresponding existing\\nstage reg_write control, retaining EX/MEM priority, MEM/WB fallback,\\nand the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding. Preserve forwarding from\\nMEM/WB's retained payload when its retirement valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the current ID/EX and EX/MEM rd tags into ID's early comparison\\ninputs and connect the registered match outputs to forward_unit.\\nKeep the current producer reg_write qualifications and EX operand\\nmux interfaces. Preserve hazard wiring, divider/result-bank ownership,\\nthe direct memory-address bank, nret=1 ports, and retirement logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Initialize the new producer-tag inputs and extend capture, reset,\\nflush, invalid-input, and hold coverage to the match outputs. Check\\nboth sources, zero-register exclusion, independent simultaneous near\\nand far matches, and changing tags while stalled. Retain every existing\\npayload/control assertion and verify metadata follows its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency chains covering both operands and both producer ages,\\nsuccessive writes to the same rd, nearer-writer suppression by traps,\\nand consumers using ALU operations, JALR, branches, and store data.\\nExercise load-use bubbles, unavailable fetches, prolonged dmem holds\\nwith valid-cleared WB forwarding, divider launch and completion,\\nback-to-back divisions, redirects, and reset. Retain ISS/RVFI operand\\nand result comparisons, exact divide latency and retirement spacing,\\nstable held memory requests, and accepted-memory-effect counts.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and register four\\nindependent nonzero-source identity matches on the existing payload\\ncapture edge. Expose them as separate near/far match outputs for both\\nsources. Reset synchronously, retain on stall, and preserve all current\\npayload/control separation, flush priority, and instruction timing.\\nLeave final writer eligibility to the actual destination stages.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four live five-bit equality comparisons with registered\\nnear/far match inputs. Qualify each with the corresponding existing\\nstage reg_write control, retaining EX/MEM priority, MEM/WB fallback,\\nand the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding. Preserve forwarding from\\nMEM/WB's retained payload when its retirement valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the current ID/EX and EX/MEM rd tags into ID's early comparison\\ninputs and connect the registered match outputs to forward_unit.\\nKeep the current producer reg_write qualifications and EX operand\\nmux interfaces. Preserve hazard wiring, divider/result-bank ownership,\\nthe direct memory-address bank, nret=1 ports, and retirement logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Initialize the new producer-tag inputs and extend capture, reset,\\nflush, invalid-input, and hold coverage to the match outputs. Check\\nboth sources, zero-register exclusion, independent simultaneous near\\nand far matches, and changing tags while stalled. Retain every existing\\npayload/control assertion and verify metadata follows its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency chains covering both operands and both producer ages,\\nsuccessive writes to the same rd, nearer-writer suppression by traps,\\nand consumers using ALU operations, JALR, branches, and store data.\\nExercise load-use bubbles, unavailable fetches, prolonged dmem holds\\nwith valid-cleared WB forwarding, divider launch and completion,\\nback-to-back divisions, redirects, and reset. Retain ISS/RVFI operand\\nand result comparisons, exact divide latency and retirement spacing,\\nstable held memory requests, and accepted-memory-effect counts.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 376.98, \"delta_pct\": -8.01, \"vs_baseline\": 33.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5502, \"ff\": 2093, \"seeds\": [169.32, 191.75, 160.95], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the forwarding-comparison retiming hypothesis. Correctness and cycle equivalence checks pass, but the measured three-seed FPGA result regresses: median reported Fmax is 169.32 MHz versus 184.06 MHz, and evaluator fitness is 376.98 versus 409.80 (about -8.01%). The proposed four-percent improvement was not observed. The implementation is left in place for the orchestrator to evaluate.\\n\\nStarting RTL: commit 4c676b5bcfebb0c7fef236028f4828bd467d81d9. Source changes are limited to cores/bench/rtl/{id_stage,forward_unit,core}.sv and cores/bench/test/test_{id_stage,pipeline}.py, plus these explicitly requested notes.\\n\\nActual implementation:\\n\\n- ID accepts the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and captures four independent nonzero raw-source identity matches as separate outputs. They share the payload's synchronous reset and !stall capture enable. Flush and input validity do not gate their capture or reset.\\n- Forwarding uses those registered matches with the actual EX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write signals. EX/MEM keeps priority, MEM/WB remains the fallback, and the 00/01/10 interface is unchanged. No valid or wb_w_en qualification was added. Both matches survive a near writer losing permission through a trap.\\n- The packed bundles, operand/result banks, direct memory address bank, decoder, hazard unit, divider, arithmetic, redirects, RVFI, ports, and nret=1 configuration are unchanged. An active divide continues to use its existing saved operands; the instruction captured at divide acceptance receives fresh matches.\\n- ID tests now cover both raw fields, simultaneous near/far matches, x0, all 256 combinations of four representative source/tag values, changed tags and instruction input during holds, invalid inputs, flush priority, and synchronous reset with all four bits set.\\n- Pipeline tests add both operand/producer ages, repeated destinations, JAL/JALR trap suppression with eligible older writers, MEM-stage load-write suppression, ALU/branch/JALR/store consumers, prolonged memory holds with valid-cleared WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, unavailable fetch, and dependent consecutive divisions. Existing ISS/RVFI, eight-clock divide acceptance, nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, reset cancellation, stable requests, and accepted-memory-effect assertions remain. Optional PIPELINE_TRACE_PATH records full directed-run traces for starting/candidate comparison.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness_ch0, reg_ch0, unique, cover, illegal-instruction, and all generated instruction checks. This was the first RTL attempt; there were no formal failures, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged command was RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh, which auto-selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config. The normal gate retains RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Real divider arithmetic and latency were exercised by the existing real-mode unit suites and ISS comparisons; formal-deep was not run because arithmetic was untouched.\\n\\nOther local validation:\\n\\n- The exact requested verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv command passes without warnings.\\n- All nine pytest unit runners pass: 144 cocotb cases, including real and ALTOPS ALU/divider and EX-bank tests, decoder, ID, immediate generator, pipeline, and register file. The final additional all-four-match reset assertion was rechecked with the ID runner: pass.\\n- The same expanded pipeline test file passes on starting RTL. All 33 protocol runs are byte-identical between versions, including 5,785 retirement records, every retirement cycle, deterministic stall schedules, and accepted memory effects.\\n- Unchanged tools.eval.cosim passes both evaluator ELFs. Supplemental full selftest ISS comparisons pass 115 retirements for each version under zero wait and simultaneous imem/dmem stalls with seeds 1, 2, 3, and 42. Complete emitted RVFI/cycle traces match byte-for-byte. Final retirement cycles are respectively 159, 198, 189, 187, and 198.\\n- git diff --check passes. Tested candidate RTL/tests were byte-compared with the submitted files; contract source/config copies were also checked unchanged.\\n\\nFPGA evaluation used the unchanged synthesis script, core_bench_si wrapper, constraints, nextpnr_run.sh, and tools.eval.fpga evaluator, with seeds 1/2/3 for both versions. Every seed routed successfully. These are reported post-route values:\\n\\n| Version | Seed 1 MHz | Seed 2 MHz | Seed 3 MHz | Median MHz | Fitness | LUT4 | DFF |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Starting | 184.06 | 191.13 | 179.89 | 184.06 | 409.80 | 5490 | 2112 |\\n| Retimed | 169.32 | 191.75 | 160.95 | 169.32 | 376.98 | 5502 | 2093 |\\n\\nThe evaluator's 2 KB, ITERATIONS=10, -O3 binary and default ~22% imem/dmem stall schedule produce exactly 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles for both versions. Fitness above uses the evaluator's unrounded iteration/cycle calculation, not its rounded ipc_coremark JSON display.\\n\\nEvery routed critical path was inspected. Starting seeds 1 and 3 reproduce the EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF pc[1] paths at 5.43 ns and 5.56 ns; starting seed 2 reports instruction-input/LFSR -> ID register-file operand capture at 5.23 ns. Candidate seeds 1 and 2 instead report ID/EX alu_op[3] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX/MEM normal alu_result[15], at 5.91 ns and 5.22 ns. Candidate seed 3 reports the corresponding alu_op[2] path at 6.21 ns. Thus the earlier seed-2 register-file path did not remain the reported limit.\\n\\nStructural inspection of synth.json and each of the three pnr.json files confirms four distinct rs{1,2}_{near,far}_match DFFRE cells with the same clock, reset, and enable as ID payload. Traversing the mapped near/far qualification cones to sequential boundaries yields only the corresponding match bits and actual producer reg_write registers, with no live destination-number comparator layer. Near qualification maps to LUT2; far qualification maps to LUT3 with near priority. The synthesized dmem address still aliases the unselected, directly registered EX normal-result bank. This confirms the intended structural change despite the performance regression.\\n\\nTiming caveat: these are the unchanged toolchain's timing reports, with its existing timing-model/arc coverage limitations. Omitted paths/arcs were not treated as timing margin. No timing models, constraints, report parsing, or memory-address topology were changed to improve the score.\\n\\nThe evaluator workload and supplemental canonical 6 KB workload are distinct. Both were built from unchanged sources with -O3 and ITERATIONS=10. For each workload/stall seed, both RTL versions ran the identical ELF and produced identical final retirement records, marker timestamps, and UART. Supplemental results:\\n\\n| Working set | Stall seed | Bracketed cycles, both RTLs | Final retirement cycle, both RTLs | Retirements, both RTLs |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,490,174 | 4,513,268 | 2,937,037 |\\n| 2 KB | 2 | 4,492,556 | 4,515,577 | 2,937,037 |\\n| 2 KB | 3 | 4,493,908 | 4,516,723 | 2,937,037 |\\n| 6 KB | 1 | 32,838,499 | 32,902,122 | 22,093,920 |\\n| 6 KB | 2 | 32,840,744 | 32,904,210 | 22,093,920 |\\n| 6 KB | 3 | 32,834,771 | 32,898,145 | 22,093,920 |\\n\\nEvery CoreMark run reaches EBREAK, reports oob=false and bench_bracketed=true, and contains \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\" UART text. The 2 KB runs pass unchanged validate_coremark_uart, including seedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, and crcfinal=fcaf. The separate 6 KB runs verify canonical crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47; UART also reports seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949. CoreMark uses the unchanged --bench validation mode, not full per-instruction Python ISS execution; full retirement-cycle comparisons above apply to the directed pipeline and selftest runs. The UART compiler-flags string is pre-existing hardcoded text reporting -O2, while both actual compilation commands used -O3; no benchmark files were changed to correct that display.\\n\\nELF SHA-256:\\n\\n- Evaluator 2 KB: 0b7d2c770d8de1e601d1d5610b1048ab73b20cbea0f77e96a348059878ec51ff.\\n- Supplemental 6 KB: 7a7e47031b65d93f3111c4d7f0fafdcadbb9f507a1ede7b6aa78bd3d356ecf7f.\\n\\nExecution details and deviations: validation ran on private starting/candidate copies under the provided TMPDIR so shared formal trees and read-only benchmark/evaluator files were not modified. Conda's existing Python/cocotb environment and the existing OSS CAD Suite/cross compiler were selected through PATH. The FPGA build/evaluation steps were invoked directly with BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv and NRET=1, using the unchanged scripts and evaluator. The supplemental 6 KB compile changed only command-line size/output arguments and wrote a separate ELF. There were no architectural deviations or follow-on tuning changes after observing the regression.\\n\\nEvidence directory: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/forward-r6s1-1b39xze9. It contains starting/ and candidate/ source/build snapshots, candidate/formal-validation.log, candidate/unit-tests.log, candidate/id-final-test.log, both pipeline-traces.jsonl files, both fpga-eval.json files, per-seed nextpnr logs/netlists, selftest-comparison.json, coremark-comparison.json, and structural-check.json.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:40:19.721587Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r6s1 (regression, -8.01%): Registering near/far forwarding matches at ID/EX removed live rd comparisons from EX, but alu_op-driven normal-result selection into EX/MEM became critical in all three seeds, lowering reported median Fmax from 184.06 to 169.32 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; comparator retiming still requires checking other routed paths across seeds.\"}\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Tagged target prediction with MEM-stage validation and staged training\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 409.80 iter/sec at 184.06 MHz and 4,491,485\\nmarker-window cycles. The 16-entry predictor in r2s2 saved 6.135% of\\ncycles, but lost 13.074% of Fmax. Its routed paths included EX target\\nalignment and branch qualification feeding predictor payload enables.\\nStatic predictors in r3s2 through r5s2 also lost more frequency than\\nthey saved cycles; even control-only squashing left long recovery and\\nhazard paths. A predictor should move resolution off the same-cycle\\nrecovery and table-write paths while retaining the current winner's\\nindependent pipeline payload capture. The measured small-BTB cycle\\nbenefit makes delayed validation a worthwhile single predictor experiment.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 409.80 iter/sec at 184.06 MHz and 4,491,485\\nmarker-window cycles. The 16-entry predictor in r2s2 saved 6.135% of\\ncycles, but lost 13.074% of Fmax. Its routed paths included EX target\\nalignment and branch qualification feeding predictor payload enables.\\nStatic predictors in r3s2 through r5s2 also lost more frequency than\\nthey saved cycles; even control-only squashing left long recovery and\\nhazard paths. A predictor should move resolution off the same-cycle\\nrecovery and table-write paths while retaining the current winner's\\nindependent pipeline payload capture. The measured small-BTB cycle\\nbenefit makes delayed validation a worthwhile single predictor experiment.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped bimodal BTB, and resolve prediction errors\\nfrom registered EX/MEM state in MEM. This moves recovery one cycle later\\nwithout adding a stage to ordinary instruction execution or retirement.\\nCorrect predictions still have no control-flow bubble; a misprediction\\ndiscards two younger slots instead of the existing one. The hypothesis\\nis that shorter recovery and training paths preserve enough Fmax to\\noutweigh that extra miss penalty.\\n\\nIndex the BTB with fetch PC[5:2], compare the full PC[31:6] tag, and store\\naligned target[31:2], a valid bit, and a two-bit saturating counter.\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set selects the stored target; otherwise\\nselect full-width PC+4. Legal conditional branches train direction;\\nJAL and JALR learn their latest aligned target and use counter 11.\\nReset clears validity; uninitialized payload must never affect a miss.\\nLookup depends only on PC and table state, without instruction predecode,\\nan instruction-immediate fetch adder, or stall/flush qualification in\\nthe target-select data cone. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC\\nregister. Prediction is applied only on actual fetch acceptance.\\n\\nCarry the exact consumed predicted_next_pc, lookup hit, and counter\\nsnapshot into ID/EX. Metadata follows the accepted instruction and\\nholds through load/decode holds, memory backpressure, and divider waits.\\nPreserve the winner's separation of payload capture from control/valid\\nclearing: dynamic squashes and invalid bubbles clear controls, while\\npayload flops retain their ordinary capture/hold enables. Metadata on\\ninvalid entries is inert. Lookup and a simultaneous table update use\\npre-edge table contents, with no combinational update bypass.\\n\\nEX continues computing architectural pc_next, including forwarded JALR,\\nPC+4 on the existing misalignment-trap continuation, and unchanged link\\nresults. Register the consumed predicted_next_pc alongside that actual\\npc_next in EX/MEM. Remove the immediate EX redirect. In MEM, compare\\nthese two registered full-width values for EVERY valid instruction,\\nincluding ordinary instructions, loads/stores, trapped instructions,\\nand completed DIV/REM with a stale BTB prediction. Assert repair only\\nwhen this EX/MEM owner can advance: valid, not reset, and no older dmem\\nhold. Drive repair_target directly from registered architectural\\npc_next. Do not put forwarded operands, live branch condition, target\\naddition, instruction legality, or div_wait in this comparison cone.\\nPreserve actual pc_next as the RVFI source. A held memory owner keeps\\nboth PCs until its first accepting edge; it must not consume a repair\\nor retire twice while blocked.\\n\\nA MEM repair wins over every younger fetch prediction and EX operation.\\nOn its accepting edge, redirect fetch even if imem is unavailable,\\ninvalidate the younger IF/ID slot and ID/EX entry, and inject a control-\\nzero invalid EX/MEM entry instead of accepting the younger EX result.\\nThe current MEM owner still advances normally into MEM/WB, including\\nits legitimate memory transaction and retirement. Add a younger-EX kill\\ninput that suppresses divider request/accept and effective div_wait on\\nthat repair cycle, and gives squash priority over the younger divide\\nhold. Do not reset the divider or its completion bank on repair: the\\nrepairing MEM owner can itself be a completed DIV/REM using that bank.\\nAn older memory hold already prevents a younger divide from launching;\\nonce the hold ends, pending MEM repair must kill it before launch.\\nCompute dmem hold independently of repair to avoid combinational loops.\\nWrong-path loads/stores must be killed before becoming memory owners.\\n\\nRetiming training is part of this same predictor. EX computes a small\\nupdate action and final counter from its accepted instruction and saved\\nlookup snapshot, then registers them with EX/MEM. Register the source\\nPC and target payload with ordinary payload enables, reusing existing\\nEX/MEM fields where possible. Only the registered action, registered\\nindex, and MEM-owner advancement may drive table write enables; no\\nsame-cycle EX outcome, target-alignment test, or generic EX-accept net\\nmay directly enable the wide BTB payload bank. An aligned conditional\\nhit saturates its saved counter and refreshes its direct target. An\\naligned taken conditional miss allocates at 10; a not-taken miss does\\nnothing. Legal aligned jumps write 11. Invalidate a saved hit for a\\nnon-control, illegal, or misaligned-target instruction, including an\\nuntaken conditional whose target is misaligned; never allocate it.\\nInvalid and killed owners have action NONE. Apply each registered action\\nonce on that owner's MEM advance, including the owner causing repair.\\nA stale snapshot may replace intervening state, but every allocation\\nwrites a coherent complete tag/target/counter tuple. Prediction errors\\nare always recoverable by the full-PC comparison.\\n\\nEstimate a 3% fitness improvement with low confidence. The earlier\\n6.135% cycle saving is evidence for early validation only; the extra\\nrecovery cycle must be measured, and may erase the benefit. A candidate\\nwith 3% fewer cycles breaks even at approximately 178.54 MHz against\\nthe current winner, and gains about 3.09% if Fmax stays at 184.06 MHz.\\nInspect all three routed seeds for MEM comparison-to-squash timing,\\nregistered-action-to-BTB enables, and unchanged raw load-use paths.\\nReport actual marker-window cycles, miss/recovery observations, median\\nFmax, LUT4/FF use, and net fitness. Preserve nret=1, all invariants,\\narithmetic/result-bank ownership, and the directly registered dmem\\naddress. Run unchanged lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker,\\nresource, and three-seed FPGA gates. Prior logs identify a 2 KB versus\\n6 KB fixture discrepancy; keep evaluator results authoritative and any\\nseparate 6 KB measurement clearly supplemental without modifying either\\ncontract. This proposal changes only the bench predictor and its recovery.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry fully tagged direct-mapped BTB with two-bit direction\\ncounters, PC-only lookup, valid-only reset, and synchronous writes\\ndriven exclusively by registered training records. Implement coherent\\nallocation, invalidation, and pre-edge lookup/update semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add consumed prediction and lookup-snapshot metadata to IF/ID and\\nID/EX, and predicted_next_pc plus the small registered training record\\nto EX/MEM. Reuse its existing actual pc_next and source PC. Keep all\\narchitectural controls, downstream RVFI fields, and nret=1 unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the raw BTB prediction for accepted PC advances and emit its\\nexact metadata. Preserve direct registered imem addressing, normal\\nstall behavior, invalid fetch bubbles, and reset/repair priority.\\nA prediction never squashes its producing instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and lookup snapshots with instruction payload and\\nretain them across holds. Preserve independent payload capture and\\ncontrol-zero invalid bubbles so metadata adds no dynamic wide reset.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve architectural resolution while registering predicted_pc and\\na training record with EX/MEM; remove same-cycle branch redirect.\\nAdd MEM-repair kill with control-zero younger EX/MEM squashing and\\ndivider-launch suppression, preserving the current MEM owner, held\\nforwarding data, divider completion bank, and direct mem_addr bank.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire PC-only predictor lookup and registered training. Generate MEM\\nrepair by comparing valid advancing EX/MEM actual/predicted PCs, use\\nits registered actual PC as target, and distribute repair to fetch,\\ndecode, and younger EX kill. Keep the external IO/RVFI shape intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make MEM repair squash both younger stages and override younger\\ndivider holds, while deferring it through the current MEM owner's\\ndmem hold. Keep the dmem-stall equation independent of repair and\\npreserve load-use interlocking and retained MEM/WB forwarding data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test full tags/targets, all counters, collision replacement, reset,\\ninvalidation, stale training snapshots, and simultaneous lookup/write.\\nCheck that inactive or held update records never train repeatedly.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify zero-bubble warm predictions and two-slot miss recovery with\\ncomplete RVFI comparison against the unchanged ISS. Cover direction\\nand target mistakes, changing JALR targets, loop exits, stale hits on\\nordinary/memory/divide/illegal instructions, alignment traps, long\\ndmem holds, imem unavailability, load forwarding, consecutive repairs,\\nand reset. Observe actual accepted stores and divider launches to\\nprove MEM repair kills younger work before any 