{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 (regression, -5.98%): Replacing ALU case selection with registered one-hot masks and a balanced OR tree disrupted multiplier sharing (MULT36X36 count rose from 1 to 3) and left slower selector-to-EX/MEM paths critical, lowering median Fmax 5.98% despite unchanged arithmetic and CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.85%): Routing the post-register DIV/REM result-bank mux into mem_stage.address blocks Yosys BRAM address-register absorption, so memory addresses must use the normal EX/MEM bank directly to avoid expansion from four block RAMs to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks and placement failure.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r3s0 (regression, -35.87%): In div_unit.sv's four-step restoring divider, 8/8/8/9-bit carry-select subtraction shortened native carry chains but added prefix/mux logic and routing across serial steps, reducing reported median Fmax 35.87% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r3s1 (regression, -57.65%): Halving divider quotient stages does not guarantee better mapped timing: two radix-4 stages in div_unit.sv cascaded 35-bit trial subtraction and underflow-driven masked-OR selection into terminal sign correction, yielding a reported ~14 ns completion path with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r3s2 (regression, -3.71%): Removing BTB state is insufficient to recover predictor timing: stateless backward-taken/direct-JAL prediction with one-bit EX repair cut CoreMark cycles 5.16% but lost 8.68% median Fmax, with routed worst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r4s0 (regression, -37.58%): Carry-free prefix-OR/XOR sign correction in div_unit.sv removed 64 carry cells but left restoring carry chains feeding seven LUT arcs and a LUT5 mux, lowering reported median Fmax 37.58% despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r4s1 (improvement, +1.78%): Precomputing the first four restoring bits with a narrow request-edge prefix in div_unit.sv separates sign correction from the wide recurrence by using registered quotient_q/remainder_q, improving fitness without extending the eight-clock response schedule.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 (regression, -8.03%): Using acceptance-qualified early_jal as the IF PC mux select pulled stall/flush/redirect controls into the data path; selecting with raw predecode instead recovered median Fmax from 78.73 to 153.52 MHz, but JAL-only prediction's 1.10% CoreMark cycle saving could not cover the remaining 9.04% Fmax loss.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r5s0 (regression, -7.44%): Balanced one-hot read/bypass trees in reg_file.sv reduced LUTs and mapped read depth yet lost 7.44% median Fmax as placement worsened the unchanged EX-control-to-EX/MEM path, showing that local mux simplification can regress routed timing despite identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r5s1 (improvement, +7.14%): Separating ID/EX and EX/MEM payload capture from bubble/flush and divide-completion control removed those decisions from wide data/reset/enable cones, raising reported median Fmax 7.14% with unchanged CoreMark cycles while requiring zero control on invalid entries and full payload/control retention during memory stalls.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r5s2 (regression, -11.39%): Control-only ID/EX squashes and decode-folded repair polarity do not guarantee predictor timing: raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold paths to control-bank RESET remained critical, lowering median Fmax from 171.79 to 146.05 MHz and outweighing branch-only prediction's 4.05% CoreMark cycle saving.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r6s0 (regression, -8.89%): Precomputing JALR sum bit 1 for each rs1 forwarding candidate in ex_stage.sv removed native arithmetic from the mapped alignment cone, but routed ALU-result selection and divider-operand enables became critical, reducing median Fmax from 184.06 to 167.70 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r6s1 (regression, -8.01%): Registering near/far forwarding matches at ID/EX removed live rd comparisons from EX, but alu_op-driven normal-result selection into EX/MEM became critical in all three seeds, lowering reported median Fmax from 184.06 to 169.32 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; comparator retiming still requires checking other routed paths across seeds.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r7s0 (regression, -17.88%): Funct3-aligned five-bit ALU codes do not guarantee better routed control timing: forwarding-to-divider request/quotient capture became routing-dominated in two seeds and alu_op-to-normal-result selection limited the third, cutting median Fmax 17.88% despite unchanged CoreMark cycles and a single MULT36X36.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r7s1 (regression, -6.73%): Separating MUL into an EX/MEM bank left EX/MEM rd-to-JALR forwarding and register-file reads limiting reported Fmax despite unchanged CoreMark cycles and one MULT36X36; missing result-bank mux arcs prevent attributing the regression to bank-to-EX/WB delay.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.44%): Registering train_pending isolated predictor payload enables from live EX qualification, but single-entry backedge prediction with decode displacement certification still left forwarding/JALR alignment through repair/hold/squash to ID control RESET critical in all three seeds, losing 37.06% median Fmax for an inferred ~4% CoreMark cycle saving.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r8s0 (regression, -12.99%): Replacing reg_file.sv's data reset with written_q validity inferred 32 RAM16SDP4 cells and eliminated 1,014 data flops, but validity-to-ID/EX selection limited two routed seeds and forwarding/JALR the third, showing that smaller register storage can reduce median Fmax despite identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r8s1 (regression, -5.94%): Absorbing ID/EX operand capture into two synchronous SDPX9B register-file banks nearly halved LUT/DFF use without adding CoreMark cycles, but forwarding/JALR and ALU-result selection still limited reported Fmax; missing RAM address/output logic arcs prevent attributing the regression to RAM latency.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r8s2 (regression, -49.62%): Validity-only ID/EX annul isolated repair from the control/payload bank, but forwarding through 27-31 carry arcs to the remaining valid RESET still limited all three routed seeds, dropping median Fmax from 184.06 to 88.97 MHz despite 4.05% fewer CoreMark cycles; reducing squash fanout alone does not shorten recovery logic.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r9s0 (regression, -4.16%): Direct one-hot forwarding and balanced masked-OR ALU inputs in ex_stage.sv do not guarantee shallower mapping: synthesis retained final operand muxes and five-cell tag-to-ALU depth, while routing-dominated register-read paths became critical in all three seeds, reducing median Fmax 4.16% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r9s1 (regression, -16.60%): Inserting jalr_target_q after the full forwarding and addition cone shifted a critical path to its input while divider operand enables and EX result selection limited other seeds, reducing reported median Fmax 16.34% despite only 0.32% more 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r9s2 (regression, -46.23%): Removing target-alignment/JALR adders from EX squash control still left EX/MEM rd-driven forwarding through branch-comparison carry chains (two seeds) and recovery/decode LUTs (third seed) dominating routed timing, cutting median Fmax 48.41% despite backward prediction's 4.05% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r10s0 (regression, -20.34%): Mapping JALR target bit 1 to a single LUT4 after the unchanged rs1 forwarding mux in ex_stage.sv still shifted all three routed critical paths to slower alu_op-to-normal-EX/MEM result selection, so avoiding speculative per-source fanout did not prevent a median Fmax drop from 184.06 to 146.63 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r10s1 (regression, -13.55%): Registering raw forwarded JALR operands before target addition isolated completion from live forwarding, but PC+immediate alignment/squash to ID/EX alu_op and forwarding/load-availability to div_rs2_q CE became critical, lowering median Fmax from 184.06 to 159.62 MHz despite only 0.32% more 6 KB CoreMark cycles; a target-path cut must also account for shared squash and launch controls.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r10s2 (regression, -55.72%): A balanced Boolean tree in branch_compare.sv eliminated branch-comparison carry cells but left a 16-cell LUT/mux path through forwarding and EX repair to ID control RESET in all three routed seeds, showing that carry removal alone cannot ensure faster recovery: median Fmax fell to 78.19 MHz despite 4.05% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r11s1 (regression, -23.88%): A two-entry fall-through FIFO in if_stage.sv saved 3.08% of stalled 6 KB CoreMark cycles, but its live-head mux through register-file reads and load-use/decode_take-to-occupancy paths cut median routed Fmax from 184.06 to 135.43 MHz, showing that empty bypass can put queue selection and consumption control on the critical path.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r11s2 (regression, -5.01%): Registering only predicted-taken exit recovery isolated the inverted branch-outcome path, but two-slot exit repairs left only 2.91% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles while load-use control to ID/EX RESET and ordinary data paths limited median Fmax to 169.75 MHz, below the 178.71 MHz break-even point.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r12s0 (regression, -4.31%): Explicit signed 33x33 multiply sharing in alu.sv kept one MULT36X36 and removed a result-path MUX2_LUT5 but left five combinational cells, while EX/MEM rd-to-JALR/PC forwarding limited all three seeds; reducing multiply mux count did not improve routed timing with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r12s2 (regression, -48.86%): Certifying cached backedge targets from raw fetch data and registering exits removed the IF target adder and generic stale-hit repair, but EX/MEM forwarding through ordinary immediate recovery to ID/EX control RESET remained critical in every routed seed, limiting median Fmax to 91.00 MHz despite 3.73% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20260906-002-r12s1|hyp-20260906-001-r11s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in e.items() if k not in (\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"eval_result\\\",\\\"evaluation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\")},ensure_ascii=False)),print(\\\"HYPOTHESIS\\\",str(e.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\"))[:4200]),print(\\\"NOTES\\\",str(e.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))[:7500])) for e in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20260906-002-r12s1|hyp-20260906-001-r11s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in e.items() if k not in (\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"eval_result\\\",\\\"evaluation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\")},ensure_ascii=False)),print(\\\"HYPOTHESIS\\\",str(e.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\"))[:4200]),print(\\\"NOTES\\\",str(e.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))[:7500])) for e in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Separate bounded multicycle division from the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases, and the existing\\nALTOPS DIV/REM formulas on the same bounded execution schedule.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace combinational division and remainder with the new engine\\nand expose its clock/reset and request/response control to EX.\\nPreserve combinational integer and multiply outputs and all their\\nALTOPS formulas. Select DIV/REM results only from the completed\\nengine response; eliminate the old unrolled / and % hardware.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add division acceptance/completion control and capture the forwarded\\noperand values used for RVFI. Dispatch ordinary operations exactly\\nas before. While a division waits, drain an available EX/MEM slot\\nto a control-zero bubble; preserve a genuinely stalled memory slot.\\nCommit the held divide result and its instruction metadata once,\\nwith the original PC, rd, and operands, and support normal bypass\\nof that result to the following instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX's division wait/acceptance signals to pipeline control while\\npreserving the imem/dmem interfaces, single-channel RVFI ports,\\nretirement boundary, and order counter. Distinguish holding the\\ninstruction in ID/EX from holding an older instruction in EX/MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold fetch and ID/EX for an unfinished DIV/REM without flushing that\\ninstruction. Keep load-use and redirect priorities correct, and\\nreserve EX/MEM holding and WB payload retention for actual memory\\nbackpressure so division waiting cannot duplicate an older memory\\nrequest or retirement. Release the frontend on result acceptance.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt division/remainder checks to the bounded clocked handshake\\nwhile retaining every existing RV32I and RV32M expected-bit vector.\\nAdd randomized signed/unsigned quotient and remainder checks,\\nfull-width trial-remainder cases, response holding, repeated\\nrequests, reset while busy, and an explicit completion bound.\\nContinue checking ordinary integer and multiply latency unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM producer-consumer chains, back-to-back divisions, x0\\ndestinations, preceding WB and load producers, and following\\nbranches/stores. Exercise simultaneous instruction/data stalls,\\nolder stores draining once, reset during division, and flushed\\nwrong-path divisions. Check original RVFI operands, one ordered\\nretirement per accepted instruction, and no duplicate bus effects.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 372.62, \"delta_pct\": 31.75, \"vs_baseline\": 31.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5438, \"ff\": 2094, \"seeds\": [165.32, 174.03, 167.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:29:30.038895Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The only recorded cores/bench experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is\\nabsent and the experiment log contains no previous structural attempts.\\nThe baseline uses 9563 LUT4s and achieves 282.82 iterations/second at a\\nmedian 127.03 MHz. Its saved seed-2 timing report shows a 7.87 ns path\\nfrom ID/EX control through execution logic to EX/MEM alu_result bit 22,\\ncomprising 4.43 ns logic and 3.44 ns routing. The RTL places fully\\ncombinational signed and unsigned division/remainder in this execution\\ncone together with the common integer operations. Factoring division\\ninto a small sequential resource could shorten this cone and reduce\\nrouting pressure without increasing the latency of ordinary ALU, load,\\nbranch, or multiply instructions. This timing attribution motivates an\\nexperiment; it does not establish how much delay division alone causes.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware used by the core\\nwith one shared, blocking iterative quotient/remainder engine. This is\\nthe sole architectural change: retain the current pipeline boundaries\\nand single-cycle RV32I and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU execution.\\n\\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient-bit steps per\\nclock for eight compute clocks. Normalize signed operands at request\\nacceptance, retain the original dividend and both sign flags, and apply\\nquotient/remainder sign correction when producing the held response.\\nSize the trial remainder/subtract path to 33 bits and never use / or %\\nin the synthesized engine. Handle divisor zero explicitly, returning\\nall-ones quotient or the original dividend; preserve INT_MIN/-1 results\\nand the dividend's sign for signed remainder. Bound request-to-response\\nand subsequent dispatch so consecutive divides remain compatible with\\nthe unchanged formal liveness window; avoid gratuitous setup, finish,\\nor cooldown states. Arithmetic and control use the same cycle schedule\\nin real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds. In ALTOPS mode, capture the four\\nexisting subtraction/XOR stand-ins at acceptance and release them on\\nthat same response schedule rather than taking a faster formal path.\\n\\nLaunch only for a valid DIV/REM in ID/EX, once forwarded operands are\\nusable and the older EX/MEM instruction can advance. Capture both\\npost-forward operands, opcode, and the associated RVFI operand values\\nat launch so later WB draining cannot alter an active operation. Hold\\nID/EX and fetch while its result is unavailable. Let older MEM/WB work\\ndrain normally: when EX/MEM is ready, emit a bubble with zeroed control\\nduring the computation, not a repeated copy of the older instruction.\\nAn actual dmem stall still holds its outstanding EX/MEM request and\\nretains the existing WB forwarding payload discipline. Keep a completed\\ndivision response stable until exactly one EX/MEM acceptance, then\\nrelease ID/EX and fetch. Preserve normal forwarding from the completed\\nEX/MEM result to immediately dependent arithmetic, branches, and stores.\\nReset cancels any active transaction; bubbles and killed instructions\\nmust never launch the engine or create an architectural side effect.\\n\\nKeep the decoder's legality rules, all instruction results and traps,\\nbus handshakes, top-level nret=1 ports, and channel-0 RVFI ordering intact.\\nThe gain depends on division being sufficiently uncommon in the timed\\nCoreMark region; its frequency is an assumption to measure, not a known\\nprofile. Evaluate added marker-bracketed cycles against the median Fmax\\ngain, accepting only an increase in their product. For example, a 15%\\nFmax gain with 2.5% additional cycles gives approximately 12% fitness\\nimprovement. Retain every existing correctness gate and arithmetic\\nvector, add protocol/dependency coverage, and run the unchanged formal,\\nstalled cosim, CoreMark CRC/UART checks, and three-seed FPGA evaluation.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 12, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/div_unit.sv', 'description': 'Add the shared four-steps-per-clock restoring divider with latched\\nsignedness and original operands, request/held-response handshake,\\nreset cancellation, exact RV32M corner cases\nNOTES # Bounded shared division in cores/bench\n\nImplemented a shared unsigned restoring divider with four quotient-bit steps\nper compute clock, eight compute clocks, and 33-bit trial/subtract paths. Signed\noperands are normalized at acceptance; original dividend, operand signs and\nquotient/remainder selection are retained. Zero divisor and INT_MIN/-1 follow\nRV32M exactly. The synthesized divider contains no division/remainder operators.\nThe existing four ALTOPS subtraction/XOR formulas are captured at acceptance and\nuse the same control schedule as real arithmetic. Integer and all four multiply\noperations retain their combinational ALU path and single-cycle EX dispatch.\n\nEX launches only a valid legal DIV/REM with usable forwarded operands and an\navailable older EX/MEM slot. It captures both post-forward operands for RVFI and\nholds the instruction metadata in ID/EX. While computing, fetch and ID/EX hold;\nan available EX/MEM slot becomes a completely zeroed bubble so older loads,\nstores and retirements drain once. Only actual dmem backpressure holds EX/MEM\nand preserves the existing MEM/WB forwarding payload. A completed divide\nforwards through the usual EX/MEM path. Reset cancels requests and responses.\nDecoder legality, traps, buses, pipeline boundaries, nret=1 channel-0 ports and\nthe retirement/order logic remain intact.\n\n## Schedule adjustment and formal\n\nThe first implementation registered the response after eight compute edges and\ndispatched it on a separate ninth edge. Local formal passed 52 checks but failed\n`liveness_ch0` at step 20: a load-use bubble followed by division produced an\n11-clock gap after the preceding retirement. One RTL fix removed that extra\ndispatch edge. The response is offered during the eighth compute interval and\ncan enter EX/MEM on its closing edge. If blocked, the same value is registered\nand held until acceptance. This is the only deviation from an exclusively\nregistered response interface; real and ALTOPS schedules remain identical.\nConsecutive divides now dispatch nine clocks apart, with a worst-case 10-clock\nretirement gap after load-use or a redirect in the zero-wait formal model.\n\nLocal formal status: PASS after one fix attempt. The unchanged\n`bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatically selecting `wrapper_si.sv` and\n`checks_si.cfg`, passed all 53 checks. A final run after the compilation guard\nadjustment again passed all 53. No checks or depths were modified. Full\nnon-ALTOPS `formal-deep` was not run; the fast formal suite proves routing and\nprotocol, while real arithmetic is checked by the vectors and ISS comparisons.\n\nThe exact requested glob-order lint command passes without warnings, as does\nthe same command with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`. A guarded core_pkg include in\ncore.sv accommodates that glob ordering while preserving the existing build\nflows that already place core_pkg.sv first.\n\n## Correctness validation\n\n- All six pytest runners passed: ALU real and ALTOPS (30 cocotb tests each),\n  decoder (30), immediate generator (8), pipeline (18), register file (5).\n- Every original arithmetic vector remains. Added 1,508 signed/unsigned\n  DIV/REM edge/random pairs, 512 random multiply checks, precise completion\n  timing, changing live operands, held responses, busy requests, simultaneous\n  response consumption/request refill, and reset during busy/response phases.\n- Pipeline tests compare every RVFI field with the unchanged Python ISS and\n  independently count accepted memory requests. Coverage includes both operand\n  forwarding paths, load producers, held WB load payload behind an older store,\n  12-clock simultaneous bus stalls, dependent/back-to-back divisions, x0,\n  arithmetic/branch/store consumers, killed divisions, and reset while busy.\n  Ordinary integer/multiply retirements remain one clock apart.\n- Unchanged cosim gate passed selftest and CoreMark CRC/UART validation.\n  Additional selftest runs with both random bus stalls passed seeds\n  0, 1, 2, 3, 42 and 99 (115 byte-exact retirements each).\n\n## FPGA and CoreMark evaluation\n\nThe unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`\ncompleted all three nextpnr seeds. Final routed Fmax values were 165.32,\n174.03 and 167.36 MHz; median 167.36 MHz. Resource usage was 5,438 LUT4 and\n2,094 FF, compared with the supplied baseline's 9,563 LUT4 and 1,866 FF.\nLUT4 usage fell 43.13%.\n\nThe checked-in Makefile/evaluator use a 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark\nconfiguration, despite the hypothesis text describing 6 KB. They were left\nunchanged. This official gate passed its exact CRCs, UART validation, markers,\nand OOB checks. Both candidate and a fresh simulation of the baseline RTL from\nHEAD used 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles. A streamed retirement profile\nmeasured zero DIV/REM instructions among 2,922,015 timed retirements.\n\nOfficial local fitness: **372.62 iterations/s**, versus recorded baseline\n282.82 iterations/s (**+31.75%**). Median Fmax increased 31.75% with **0% added\ntimed cycles**, so the frequency-times-iterations-per-cycle acceptance criterion\nimproves. These are local results; the orchestrator still performs acceptance.\n\nThe requested 6 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 configuration was additionally built and\nrun from unmodified scratch copies. Candidate and baseline both completed in\n32,836,671 bracketed cycles with no OOB access. Both produced crclist=0xd4b0,\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, seedcrc=0x8a02, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal\n`Correct operation validated.`. At the respective median frequencies, that is\n50.97 versus 38.69 iterations/s, again a 31.75% improvement with no cycle penalty.\nIts streamed profile measured zero DIV/REM instructions among 22,052,222 timed\nretirements; two DIVU instructions executed outside the markers.\nThe printed compiler-flags string is hardcoded in the unchanged port; the\nactual build commands used -O3.\n\n## Evidence and remaining limits\n\nAll build, formal, cosim, profile and P&R outputs are under the isolated scratch\ndirectory below. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the\nworktree's formal submodule is a shared symlink; no gate files or other cores\nwere edited. Only cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree.\n\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/hyp-002-divider-1hlm295l`\n\nKey evidence: `formal-final-console.log`, `final-unit-tests.log`,\n`stalled-cosim.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-eval.log`, `division-profile.log`,\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `sixk/*coremark.log`, and\n`sixk/division-profile.log`.\n\nDivision is intentionally blocking and adds eight execution clocks per issued\nDIV/REM relative to the baseline. Workloads with substantial division traffic\nmay lose performance despite the FPGA frequency/area improvement. The terminal\ncompute interval includes sign correction and the EX/MEM acceptance mux; it has\nbeen included in, and passed, all three routed timing evaluations.\n\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming divide writeback into a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\\nseparated blocking division from ordinary execution, reaching 372.62\\nfitness and 167.36 MHz median Fmax with unchanged measured CoreMark cycles.\\nLESSONS.md attributes that gain to frequency, with no timed DIV/REM traffic.\\nThe saved winning implementation's seed-1 and seed-3 critical paths still\\nstart at ID/EX ALU control and traverse divider-associated result-selection\\nlogic before reaching EX/MEM registers, taking 6.05 and 5.97 ns respectively.\\nSeed 2 instead ends in decode, so further frequency headroom is uncertain.\\nThese are historical timing observations, not proof of a new result.\\n\\nIn the current RTL, the terminal divide result passes through div_unit's\\nlive/held response mux, the common ALU result selection, and EX's result\\ncapture logic even though div_unit already registers that same completed\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary instructions or divisions another execution clock.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose result_q directly as the registered completion data bank while\\npreserving its final-compute-edge capture, held-response handshake,\\nreset behavior, arithmetic, and ALTOPS schedule. Retain completion data\\nafter response consumption, including while a subsequent request is\\nlaunched. Keep the live response data distinct from this registered\\noutput; no terminal-cycle bypass is allowed onto the registered port.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the normal integer/multiply result consumed by EX from the\\nlive DIV/REM response and exported registered completion data. Preserve\\nrequest/response controls and all arithmetic results and ALTOPS formulas.\\nThe synthesized normal result cone must not select a live divide result.\\nKeep a distinct live response data interface for protocol verification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use independent normal-result and registered-divide-result banks with\\nan EX/MEM-aligned divide selector. Update selector ownership on normal\\nadvance, div_accept, bubble, hold, and reset as specified. Assemble the\\nselected out.alu_result after the register boundary for both forwarding\\nand MEM/WB, without recapturing the divider's old result on its completion\\nedge. Preserve metadata, operand snapshots, launch/accept timing, link\\nresults, redirects, and existing drain and hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve every existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic vector and handshake\\nassertion, adapting DIV/REM observations to the separate response data\\nport. Check that registered completion data changes on the final edge,\\nequals the offered result after that edge, and remains stable through\\ndelayed acceptance and subsequent request launch. Cover immediate and\\nheld acceptance, changing live inputs, distinct successive results,\\nsigned corner cases, and reset during computation and held response.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add regressions for result-bank ownership and selection across normal,\\ndivide, bubble, reset, and memory-hold transitions. Exercise distinguishable\\nback-to-back DIV/REM results, x0 destinations, load-produced operands,\\nimmediate ALU/branch/JALR/store/multiply/divide consumers, and simultaneous\\nbus waits. Retain full RVFI operand/result/order/PC and accepted-memory-\\nrequest checks. Assert the existing nine-clock divide dispatch spacing,\\nunchanged ordinary instruction spacing, and no extra completion bubble.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 375.78, \"delta_pct\": 0.85, \"vs_baseline\": 32.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 168.78, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5470, \"ff\": 2111, \"seeds\": [166.17, 168.78, 169.09], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:18:02.872179Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.85%): Routing the post-register DIV/REM result-bank mux into mem_stage.address blocks Yosys BRAM address-register absorption, so memory addresses must use the normal EX/MEM bank directly to avoid expansion from four block RAMs to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks and placement failure.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"The bench history contains one structural attempt: hyp-20260906-002-r1s1\\nseparated blocking division from ordinary execution, reaching 372.62\\nfitness and 167.36 MHz median Fmax with unchanged measured CoreMark cycles.\\nLESSONS.md attributes that gain to frequency, with no timed DIV/REM traffic.\\nThe saved winning implementation's seed-1 and seed-3 critical paths still\\nstart at ID/EX ALU control and traverse divider-associated result-selection\\nlogic before reaching EX/MEM registers, taking 6.05 and 5.97 ns respectively.\\nSeed 2 instead ends in decode, so further frequency headroom is uncertain.\\nThese are historical timing observations, not proof of a new result.\\n\\nIn the current RTL, the terminal divide result passes through div_unit's\\nlive/held response mux, the common ALU result selection, and EX's result\\ncapture logic even though div_unit already registers that same completed\\nresult on the acceptance edge. Moving the selection across this existing\\nregister boundary could remove a redundant combinational path without\\ncharging ordinary instructions or divisions another execution clock.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Split the EX/MEM execution result into two parallel register banks: the\\nexisting normal ALU/link-result register and div_unit's existing result_q.\\nThis retiming of result ownership is the single structural change. Keep\\ninteger and multiply execution, the divider arithmetic and eight-compute-\\nclock schedule, pipeline instruction boundaries, and single retirement.\\n\\nExport result_q directly from div_unit as a separate registered completion\\ndata output. It must have no combinational dependency on final_cycle,\\ncompleted_result, live operands, or the live opcode. Keep the existing\\nterminal response-valid and held-response protocol. On the final compute\\nedge, result_q captures completed_result regardless of whether EX accepts\\nimmediately or the response must wait. Consuming a response must not clear\\nits data; retain result_q until a subsequent completion or reset.\\n\\nGive EX independent normal-ALU and registered-divider data paths. Remove\\nthe live divide response from the common ALU output consumed by EX/MEM.\\nRegister a one-bit divide-result selector alongside EX/MEM metadata. On\\ndiv_accept, capture the divide instruction's metadata and set this bit on\\nthe very same edge that captures the terminal result in result_q; do not\\nsample result_q into another data register on that edge, which would read\\nthe preceding operation's value. Acceptance of an already-held response\\nuses its existing stable result_q. Normal instructions clear the selector\\nand capture their ordinary ALU or PC+4 link result as before. Reset and\\nbubbles clear the selector; an actual EX/MEM hold retains it with metadata.\\n\\nReconstruct out.alu_result after these registers using just the registered\\nselector and the two registered data banks. This selected value must feed\\nboth the existing EX/MEM forwarding path and MEM/WB capture, so a dependent\\nALU instruction, branch, JALR, store, multiply, or divide sees the completed\\ndivide at exactly the existing cycle. Never forward the stale normal bank\\nfor a divide. Preserve the divider bank while its EX/MEM owner is held;\\ndemonstrate that the blocking issue protocol cannot allow a newer divide\\ncompletion to overwrite it before the owner advances. Avoid an additional\\nopcode decoder or live arithmetic on the output side of the bank selector.\\n\\nLeave request launch, operand/RVFI snapshots, div_wait, older-instruction\\ndraining, load interlocks, bus acceptance, redirects, trap handling, and\\nretained WB forwarding discipline intact. In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mode use\\nthe existing formulas and exactly the same register ownership, response\\nschedule, and forwarding as real arithmetic. The formerly failing extra\\ndispatch edge must not return: back-to-back divides still dispatch nine\\nclocks apart and the unchanged formal liveness window must pass.\\n\\nExpect approximately five percent higher fitness from Fmax with unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed cycles; this is an unmeasured estimate. The output bank\\nmux can lengthen EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding and MEM/WB paths, and decode can\\nbecome\nNOTES Implemented the divide result-bank retiming in cores/bench. The unchanged\nevaluator reports 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz median routed Fmax, versus the\nstarting implementation's recorded 372.62 and 167.36 MHz: +0.85%, with unchanged\nmarker-bracketed cycles. The estimated 5% improvement was not reached. Full\ntiming coverage of the new forwarding/writeback paths remains a concern,\ndescribed below; the reported fitness is not a complete physical timing proof.\n\ndiv_unit now exports result_q directly as registered_result, separately from\nits existing live/held response data. Its arithmetic, eight compute clocks,\nrequest/response handshake, final-edge capture, and reset behavior are\nunchanged. Consumption and subsequent launch retain result_q. alu exposes\nboth divider data ports, while its ordinary output contains only integer,\nmultiply, and default-zero results. All real and ALTOPS arithmetic formulas\nare preserved.\n\nEX/MEM now owns a registered divide selector alongside the existing metadata.\ndiv_accept sets it on the same edge that div_unit captures the terminal result,\nor accepts an already-held result. EX does not recapture the divider bank.\nNormal instructions clear the selector and capture the usual ALU/PC+4 result;\nreset and bubbles clear it, and an EX/MEM hold retains it. out.alu_result is a\nmux of the two registered banks controlled only by that registered selector.\nBoth existing EX/MEM forwarding and MEM/WB result capture consume this value.\nThe nret=1 external ports, retirement boundary, operand snapshots, division\nlaunch/accept equations, hazards, redirects, traps, and retained WB forwarding\ndiscipline remain intact.\n\nThe ownership argument is enforced by the existing blocking issue protocol:\nID/EX retains the dividing instruction until acceptance, so no younger divide\ncan already be computing while that result becomes the EX/MEM owner. The next\nlaunch requires !stall and therefore occurs on an edge that also advances the\nprevious owner. Launch itself cannot change result_q, and the next completion\nis eight compute edges later. Holding an owner suppresses all new launches.\nAt core level, dmem backpressure only holds memory instructions; direct EX\ntests additionally hold a divide owner for twelve clocks with a younger\ndivide presented, proving bank and metadata retention at that interface.\n\nOne implementation deviation was necessary for FPGA placement. Sending the\nselected output through the memory address interface prevented Yosys from\nabsorbing the normal address register into the wrapper's block RAM. Both\navailable Yosys builds mapped the wrapper to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks, exceeding\n648 available blocks, and all three seeds failed placement. EX now exposes\nthe normal bank directly as mem_addr; core passes it to mem_stage.address for\naddressing, lane selection, and alignment checks. Memory instructions always\nown the normal bank. The selected result still feeds MEM/WB alu_result and\nEX/MEM forwarding. This restores four block RAMs without changing an accepted\nmemory request, adding registers, or adding an output-side opcode decoder.\nThe inactive dmem address during a divide can now show the unused normal bank;\nDIV/REM never assert either memory request enable.\n\nLocal validation on the final RTL:\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: PASS without warnings, both real arithmetic\n  and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. git diff --check also passes.\n- Formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including the unchanged liveness window.\n  The initial implementation and the final memory-address correction each\n  passed a complete invocation. No formal counterexamples or failed-check\n  fix retries occurred. wrapper_si.sv, checks_si.cfg, run_all.sh, check depths,\n  and the upstream checks were unchanged. formal-deep was not run.\n- Cocotb: all eight pytest runners PASS, covering 128 cocotb cases: ALU real\n  and ALTOPS (31 each), decoder (30), immediate generator (8), register file\n  (5), pipeline (19), and direct EX real/ALTOPS ownership tests (2 each).\n- Every existing arithmetic vector and handshake assertion remains. Added\n  registered-bank checks before/on/after the terminal edge, immediate and\n  held acceptance, changing live operands/opcodes, distinct successive\n  results, concurrent response consumption/request refill, and resets during\n  computation, held responses, and selected/held ownership.\n- Pipeline regressions retain byte-exact RVFI comparison against the unchanged\n  ISS and independent accepted-memory-request counts. They check selection\n  and metadata on every monitored edge, normal/bubble/memory-hold transitions,\n  load-produced operands, x0, distinct consecutive DIV/REM, and immediate\n  ALU, branch, JALR, store, multiply, and divide consumers. Combined bus waits\n  include twelve-clock forced holds. Divide dispatches remain nine clocks\n  apart; ordinary instructions remain one clock apart; a following ordinary\n  divide consumer has no additional completion bubble.\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and CoreMark. Additional selftest\n  runs passed all 115 RVFI retirements with zero waits, imem-only waits,\n  dmem-only waits, and combined waits at seeds 0, 1, 2, 3, 42, and 99.\n\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv gate\ncompleted all three seeds using the historical run's installed Yosys 0.64\n(d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61) and nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f.\nFinal resources are 5,470 LUT4, 2,111 DFF, and four block RAMs, versus the\nstarting record's 5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 DFF.\n\n| Routed seed | Fmax MHz | Reported critical delay ns | Critical path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 1 | 166.17 | 6.018 | Divider quotient register to result_q |\n| 2 | 168.78 | 5.925 | ID/EX ALU control to normal EX/MEM result register |\n| 3 | 169.09 | 5.914 | Divider divisor register to result_q |\n\nThe evaluator uses aggregate 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 CoreMark. The requested\naggregate 6 KB workload was built separately with the existing Makefile's\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 options. Both candidate and fresh starting\nRTL simulations used the identical ELF and approximately 22% random stalls on\nboth buses within each comparison. No benchmark source or evaluator changed.\n\n| Workload | Starting RTL cycles | Candidate cycles | Candidate iter/s at reported median Fmax |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 | 375.78 |\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 32,836,671 | 32,836,671 | 51.40 |\n\nThe 2 KB markers are start=15,801 and stop=4,507,286; the 6 KB markers are\nstart=56,393 and stop=32,893,064. Both sides of each comparison have identical\nmarkers, clean EBREAK completion, no OOB access, ten iterations, and the\nliteral Correct operation validated. The evaluator's five 2 KB CRC checks\npass. Both 6 KB runs produce seedcrc=0x8a02, crclist=0xd4b0,\ncrcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, and crcfinal=0x5949. The UART's compiler-flags\ndescription is hardcoded as -O2 in the unchanged port; the actual build\ncommands use -O3. The 2 KB fitness must not be described as a 6 KB result.\n\nDetailed timing was investigated for all three seeds. Reloading a saved\nroute with --no-pack --no-place --no-route crashes the installed nextpnr in\narray2d.h, so three supplemental routes emitted JSON timing reports and SDF\ndirectly. Each reproduced its corresponding gate Fmax. Summing the exported\nSDF arcs reproduces all three reported critical path delays above.\n\nHowever, those SDF files omit IOPATH arcs for the LUT3 cells i\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two radix-4 quotient stages per divider clock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two previous structural experiments isolated division and then retimed\\nits result bank. The current winner reports 375.78 fitness, 168.78 MHz\\nmedian Fmax, and 5470 LUT4s. Its saved routed reports place the critical\\npath inside the divider for seeds 1 and 3: quotient/divisor registers to\\nresult_q in 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Each compute interval currently cascades\\nfour dependent restoring subtract/select steps, and the last interval\\nalso performs sign correction. The normal execute control/result path\\nlimits seed 2 at 5.925 ns, so divider acceleration alone has a competing\\nceiling and a large frequency gain is not assured.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and log.jsonl contain no previous radix restructuring attempt.\\nThey show that adding a completion clock violates the unchanged liveness\\nwindow, and that altering ALU selection can disrupt multiplier sharing.\\nA divider-local topology change can reduce serial arithmetic depth while\\npreserving the existing compute count, ordinary instruction latency,\\nsingle multiplier, and direct registered memory-address path. Prior\\nprofiles found no DIV/REM inside either measured CoreMark timing bracket.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two radix-4 stages with parallel widened trial\\nsubtractions, explicit digit selection, and a request-registered\\n3D magnitude. Retain the module ports, eight-compute-edge counter,\\nfinal/held response protocol, registered-result ownership, signed\\nand exceptional-case semantics, and identical ALTOPS scheduling.\\nKeep all divisor multiples wide enough for unsigned 0xffffffff.\\nDo not modify the ordinary ALU or EX/MEM memory-address routing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic and exact handshake-timing assertion.\\nAdd DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases stressing quotient digits 0 through 3,\\nboundaries around D, 2D and 3D, repeated and alternating quotient\\ndigits, powers of two, high unsigned operands, and signed extrema.\\nInclude divisors around 0x40000000, 0x55555555, 0x80000000 and\\n0xffffffff to expose multiple-width and underflow errors. Compare\\nagainst the independent integer reference. Exercise changing live\\noperands, held responses, consume/refill, and reset with distinct\\ndivisor multiples; retain the same latency checks in ALTOPS builds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add compact RVFI/ISS-checked streams using the new arithmetic boundary\\noperands, including load-produced operands, consecutive dependent\\nDIV/REM operations and immediate arithmetic, branch and store\\nconsumers. Exercise instruction/data waits and reset without changing\\nexisting assertions. Require correct raw RVFI operands, one accepted\\nmemory effect per request, consecutive retirement orders, nine-clock\\nzero-wait spacing for consecutive divides, and unchanged ordinary\\ninstruction spacing. Run the existing direct EX result-bank tests\\nunchanged to check terminal acceptance and prolonged ownership holds.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 159.15, \"delta_pct\": -57.65, \"vs_baseline\": -43.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 71.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5651, \"ff\": 2127, \"seeds\": [71.47, 71.48, 71.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:51:37.449932Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r3s1 (regression, -57.65%): Halving divider quotient stages does not guarantee better mapped timing: two radix-4 stages in div_unit.sv cascaded 35-bit trial subtraction and underflow-driven masked-OR selection into terminal sign correction, yielding a reported ~14 ns completion path with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The two previous structural experiments isolated division and then retimed\\nits result bank. The current winner reports 375.78 fitness, 168.78 MHz\\nmedian Fmax, and 5470 LUT4s. Its saved routed reports place the critical\\npath inside the divider for seeds 1 and 3: quotient/divisor registers to\\nresult_q in 6.018 and 5.914 ns. Each compute interval currently cascades\\nfour dependent restoring subtract/select steps, and the last interval\\nalso performs sign correction. The normal execute control/result path\\nlimits seed 2 at 5.925 ns, so divider acceleration alone has a competing\\nceiling and a large frequency gain is not assured.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and log.jsonl contain no previous radix restructuring attempt.\\nThey show that adding a completion clock violates the unchanged liveness\\nwindow, and that altering ALU selection can disrupt multiplier sharing.\\nA divider-local topology change can reduce serial arithmetic depth while\\npreserving the existing compute count, ordinary instruction latency,\\nsingle multiplier, and direct registered memory-address path. Prior\\nprofiles found no DIV/REM inside either measured CoreMark timing bracket.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace the four cascaded radix-2 restoring steps in div_unit.sv with\\ntwo cascaded radix-4 stages. Each stage generates two quotient bits using\\nthree parallel trial subtractions. This is one structural change to the\\nshared divider datapath; retain the existing blocking pipeline protocol\\nand the request edge followed by eight compute edges for every DIV/REM.\\n\\nAt request acceptance, normalize the signed operands exactly as today.\\nRegister the unsigned divisor magnitude D and its 34-bit multiple 3D;\\nform 2D by a zero-extended shift of registered D. Compute 3D as an\\nexplicitly widened shift plus add on the existing request edge, without\\na multiplier or an additional setup state. Keep these values stable\\nwhile busy. Preserve the original dividend, operand sign flags,\\nzero-divisor flag, and quotient-versus-remainder selection.\\n\\nFor each radix-4 stage, form the 34-bit trial T = {R, Q[31:30]}.\\nIn parallel compute the 35-bit zero-extended differences T-D, T-2D,\\nand T-3D. Difference bit 34 is the unsigned underflow indicator;\\nneither bit 31 nor a truncated divisor multiple is a valid substitute.\\nSelect the largest digit d in 0..3 whose subtraction does not underflow.\\nUse mutually exclusive selection enables derived from these three\\nunderflow bits and a shallow result mux, avoiding a serial chain of\\ncompare-then-subtract operations. Select R_next = T-d*D from the already\\ncomputed differences, or T for digit zero; the d*D notation describes\\nthat selection and must not infer another multiplier or subtractor.\\nAppend d to Q with Q_next = {Q[29:0], d[1:0]}. Cascade exactly two such\\nstages before updating quotient_q and remainder_q on each compute edge.\\nFor nonzero D, 0 <= R_next < D guarantees that the selected remainder\\nfits 32 bits despite the wider trial and difference calculations.\\n\\nAfter eight compute edges all 32 quotient bits have been generated.\\nKeep the existing final-interval sign correction and explicit divisor-zero\\noverride, including INT_MIN/-1, truncation toward zero, and the dividend's\\nsign for REM. Zero-divisor requests use the same fixed schedule. Preserve\\nthe live final-cycle response, acceptance on the eighth compute edge,\\nheld-response behavior, simultaneous held-response consumption/request\\nrefill, and reset cancellation. result_q must still update only on\\ncompletion and survive consumption and the next request so the existing\\nEX/MEM result-bank ownership remains valid.\\n\\nKeep the four RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS subtraction/XOR results unchanged and\\nrelease them on exactly the same control schedule as real arithmetic.\\nThe existing ALU, EX/MEM bank mux, dedicated normal memory-address bank,\\nforwarding, load-use interlock, bus holds, decoder/trap discipline,\\nretirement boundary, and nret=1 top-level ports require no redesign.\\nConsecutive divides must remain nine clocks apart; do not add a finish\\nstate, change formal depths, or weaken any existing assertion.\\n\\nThe intended benefit is less combinational depth between divide\nNOTES Implemented the two-radix-4-stage divider in cores/bench. Functional validation\npasses, but the unchanged three-seed evaluator reports 159.15 fitness at\n71.48 MHz median Fmax, versus the current winner's recorded 375.78 and\n168.78 MHz: a 57.65% regression. The estimated 3% gain was not achieved.\nThe implementation is retained as the requested hypothesis experiment;\nno evaluator, timing model, constraints, or acceptance checks were changed.\n\nOnly div_unit.sv changed in RTL. Each compute interval now cascades exactly\ntwo radix-4 stages. Each stage forms a 34-bit trial, performs three parallel\n35-bit zero-extended subtractions, and uses bit 34 for unsigned underflow.\nMutually exclusive digit enables select the already-computed remainder\nthrough a masked OR mux and append two quotient bits. The divisor magnitude\nand its explicitly widened 34-bit shift-plus-add 3D are captured on the\nexisting request edge. 2D is a zero-extended shift of registered D. There is\nno additional setup/completion state, arithmetic multiplier, or subtraction\nafter digit selection.\n\nThe counter, request/response equations, terminal-edge result capture, held\nresponse, simultaneous held consumption/refill, reset cancellation, signed\nnormalization, final sign correction, divisor-zero override, and four ALTOPS\nformulas retain their original control schedule. result_q updates only on\ncompletion, apart from reset, and survives consumption and the next request.\nINT_MIN/-1, signed truncation toward zero, and the dividend sign for remainder\nare preserved. Zero-divisor operations take the same eight compute edges.\nThe ALU, EX result-bank selection, direct normal memory-address bank,\nforwarding, hazards, traps, retirement boundary, core.yaml and nret=1 ports\nare unchanged. There were no architectural deviations from the proposal.\n\nThe existing tests and assertions were retained. test_alu.py adds 4,216\noperand/operation cases per arithmetic mode covering radix-4 digit patterns,\nD/2D/3D boundaries, powers of two, high unsigned values, signed extrema,\nand divisors around 0x40000000, 0x55555555, 0x80000000 and 0xffffffff.\nThe real build checks each clock's quotient/remainder against integer\ndivision of the processed dividend prefix, including R < D, and checks that\nD and full-width 3D remain captured while live operands change. Both real\nand ALTOPS builds check the same exact response timing and bank ownership.\nAdditional cases exercise distinct divisor multiples across held responses,\nsimultaneous consume/refill and resets before, during and after computation.\n\ntest_pipeline.py adds boundary streams using load-produced operands,\nconsecutive dependent divides, immediate arithmetic, branch and store\nconsumers, killed divides, combined instruction/data waits, forced\ntwelve-clock memory holds, and reset with wide divisor multiples. They use\nthe unchanged RVFI/ISS comparator and independent accepted-bus-effect count.\nAssertions retain raw operands, consecutive retirement orders, nine-clock\nspacing for adjacent divides and one-clock ordinary consumer spacing.\ntest_ex_result_banks.py was run unchanged in real and ALTOPS modes, including\nterminal acceptance and prolonged ownership/response holds.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- The exact requested Verilator glob-order lint command passes without\n  warnings, as does its RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS variant. git diff --check passes.\n- Formal status: PASS on the first invocation, 53 passed and 0 failed.\n  No counterexamples or formal fix attempts occurred. The unchanged\n  bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg from\n  CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl. Depths and liveness assertions\n  were unchanged. This is ALTOPS formal; formal-deep was not run, so no\n  exhaustive formal proof of real divider arithmetic is claimed.\n- All eight pytest runners pass: 134 cocotb tests, comprising ALU real and\n  ALTOPS (33 each), decoder (30), immediate generator (8), register file (5),\n  pipeline (21), and unchanged direct EX ownership tests (2 per mode).\n- The unchanged cosim gate passes both selftest and CoreMark. Twelve extra\n  selftest runs pass all 115 RVFI retirements with no waits, instruction-only\n  waits, data-only waits, and combined waits at seeds 1, 42 and 99.\n- A Yosys structural audit finds exactly six 35-bit trial subtractors. The\n  remaining four 32-bit subtractors implement operand normalization and\n  final sign correction. The divider has no multiply, divide or modulo\n  cells. Full FPGA synthesis retains one MULT36X36 and four block RAMs.\n\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv\nevaluation completed all three routes. Synthesis used the previous winner's\ninstalled Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61), and routing\nused nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f. The design uses 5,651 LUT4 and 2,127 DFF,\nversus 5,470 and 2,111 in the winner's record. LUT use increased 3.31% and\nremains below the 10,000-LUT target.\n\n| Seed | Final Fmax MHz | Reported critical delay ns | Critical path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 1 | 71.47 | 13.991 | remainder_q[4] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\n| 2 | 71.48 | 13.989 | divisor_three_q[2] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\n| 3 | 71.96 | 13.896 | divisor_three_q[7] through both stages and remainder sign correction to result_q |\n\nThree supplemental routes using identical synthesis, seeds and constraints\nexported detailed JSON and SDF reports and reproduced the gate frequencies.\nOn the reported critical paths, the first radix-stage selection is reached\nat cumulative delays 5.260/4.785/5.163 ns, the second at\n9.601/10.113/10.021 ns, and the terminal result then crosses remainder sign\ncorrection and result selection. Arithmetic-cell propagation contributes\n5.734/5.369/5.579 ns, while routing contributes 5.851/6.142/5.839 ns.\nThe source-level reduction in dependent subtract/select stages did not\nproduce the intended benefit under this mapping and evaluation.\n\nTiming coverage remains incomplete. Request normalization and the 34-bit\n3D setup adder were inspected in RTL and the mapped netlist, but none of\nthe 34 3D-register or 32 D-register data endpoints appears in the detailed\ntiming reports. Q/R update data endpoints are also absent. Both radix stages\nand terminal sign correction are visible on the reported completion paths,\nbut only 30 of 32 completion data endpoints and 7 of 32 normal EX result\ndata endpoints are listed. No MEM/WB result data endpoint is listed.\nAll 1,142 LUT3 cells lack exported SDF IOPATH arcs, including the 32 cells\nimplementing the retained EX result-bank mux. Consequently the reports do\nnot establish complete request, iteration, forwarding, writeback or normal\nEX timing. An omitted path or cell arc is unknown, not zero delay. The\nheadline fitness regression is the unchanged evaluator's result, not a\nfully characterized physical timing comparison. No timing coverage gap was\nhidden or addressed by changing the contract.\n\nBenchmark comparisons used freshly built starting-RTL and candidate\nsimulations, with the identical ELF for both sides of each workload and the\nunchanged approximately 22% random instruction/data stall model. The official\nevaluator uses aggregate 2 KB, -O3 and ITERATIONS=10. The requested aggregate\n6 KB run was built separately from unchanged sources using the existing\nCOREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 build options.\n\n| Workload | Starting RTL timed cycles | Candidate timed cycles | Candidate\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Retime divider finalization using a narrow request-edge prefix\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner records 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz. Its r2s1 notes\\nidentify divider-state-to-result_q completion paths as critical in two\\nof three routed seeds; the remaining seed is limited by normal EX result\\nselection. The divider currently cascades four restoring steps into\\nsigned-result correction on its last compute clock. The r3 carry-select\\nand radix-4 attempts made mapped timing substantially worse, so replacing\\nthe proven wide recurrence with another arithmetic network is unattractive.\\nThe original r1s1 notes also document a liveness failure when completion\\ngained one clock. A useful structural experiment must separate final\\ncorrection from the recurrence while retaining the existing latency.\\n\", \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the magnitude-based four-bit initial-prefix circuit and capture\\nits quotient/remainder state on the existing request edge. Retain\\nthe current wide four-step recurrence for count values 0 through 6;\\nat count 7 hold Q/R and form completed_result from their registered\\nvalues and captured sign/zero-divisor flags. Preserve final_cycle,\\nall ports, counter duration, response acceptance, result-bank\\nownership, reset behavior, and the matching ALTOPS schedule. Update\\nthe module's timing comments to describe request preprocessing,\\nseven wide recurrence clocks, and one final correction clock.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing arithmetic vector and exact eight-clock\\nresponse/bank assertion in both real and ALTOPS builds. Add exhaustive\\ncoverage of all 16 initial magnitude nibbles against divisors 0..15,\\nand directed divisors 16, 17, powers of two, and high-bit unsigned\\nvalues. Vary the lower 28 dividend bits and signed operand forms,\\nincluding INT_MIN/-1 and zero divisors. Check captured prefix state\\nagainst an independent integer-arithmetic reference, complete Q/R\\nafter seven recurrence edges, and no early result-bank update.\\nChange live inputs after launch and test immediate/held acceptance,\\nconsume/refill, and reset at the request, seventh, and eighth edges.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve the exact nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, existing\\nno-extra-completion-bubble assertions, and full RVFI/ISS comparisons.\\nAdd small-divisor prefix-boundary streams with load-produced operands\\nand redirect-target divides, specifically exercising the predecessor\\npatterns that previously failed liveness with one added clock.\\nCover dependent ALU, branch, JALR, store and divide consumers, combined\\nbus waits, reset, x0 destinations, and killed younger divides. Require\\nexact retirement order/PC continuity and accepted memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Keep all existing bank ownership and latency assertions. Add checks\\nthat the previous result remains owned through prefix setup and the\\nseventh recurrence edge, and that the corrected result and EX/MEM\\nmetadata become visible together only on the unchanged eighth edge.\\nExercise held completion and prolonged owner stalls with changing\\nyounger operands in both arithmetic modes, preserving the direct\\nnormal-bank memory-address checks.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 382.48, \"delta_pct\": 1.78, \"vs_baseline\": 35.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5513, \"ff\": 2087, \"seeds\": [163.85, 171.79, 174.58], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T09:31:31.255685Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r4s1 (improvement, +1.78%): Precomputing the first four restoring bits with a narrow request-edge prefix in div_unit.sv separates sign correction from the wide recurrence by using registered quotient_q/remainder_q, improving fitness without extending the eight-clock response schedule.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The current winner records 375.78 fitness at 168.78 MHz. Its r2s1 notes\\nidentify divider-state-to-result_q completion paths as critical in two\\nof three routed seeds; the remaining seed is limited by normal EX result\\nselection. The divider currently cascades four restoring steps into\\nsigned-result correction on its last compute clock. The r3 carry-select\\nand radix-4 attempts made mapped timing substantially worse, so replacing\\nthe proven wide recurrence with another arithmetic network is unattractive.\\nThe original r1s1 notes also document a liveness failure when completion\\ngained one clock. A useful structural experiment must separate final\\ncorrection from the recurrence while retaining the existing latency.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Move the first four dividend-bit iterations onto the request edge using\\na narrow initial-prefix circuit, and move final sign correction behind\\nthe existing quotient/remainder registers. This is one retiming of the\\nblocking divider, with no new pipeline stage visible to the core and\\nno change to its request/response schedule or eight post-request clocks.\\n\\nAt request acceptance, form the existing unsigned magnitudes A and D\\nfrom the fully forwarded operands. Let P = A[31:28]. Since P is at most\\n15, D >= 16 implies an initial four-bit quotient of zero and remainder P.\\nDetect that case with the reduction OR of D[31:4]. Otherwise perform\\nexactly four ordinary restoring steps on four-bit P and four-bit D,\\nstarting from zero remainder, using five-bit trial/subtract values and\\nexplicit borrow selection. This produces four-bit prefix quotient Q4\\nand remainder R4 without a wide subtract cascade at launch. Use native\\nsubtract/select expressions, not division operators, carry-select logic,\\nradix-4 candidate selection, or an additional wide arithmetic resource.\\nThe zero-divisor case retains the existing explicit final-result override.\\n\\nInitialize quotient_q to {A[27:0], Q4} and remainder_q to {28'b0, R4}\\non that same request edge. Preserve the captured divisor, original\\ndividend, operation selection, and signedness flags. The first seven\\nsubsequent clocks each run the existing four serial 33-bit restoring\\nsteps, consuming the remaining 28 dividend bits. After the seventh\\nclock the registered quotient and remainder contain the complete\\nunsigned magnitudes. During the eighth interval, hold those registers\\nand compute the existing quotient/remainder sign corrections and\\nzero-divisor selection directly from their registered outputs. Nothing\\nin completed_result may depend on quotient_step[4], remainder_step[4],\\nor a live operand during this final interval.\\n\\nKeep final_cycle at busy_q && count_q == 7. Offer the response during\\nthat eighth interval and capture result_q on its closing edge, exactly\\nwhen EX/MEM may accept its metadata. Keep registered_result connected\\ndirectly to result_q, with no combinational bypass. Preserve immediate\\nacceptance, held-response stability, simultaneous held consumption and\\nnext-request acceptance, reset cancellation, and result_q retention\\nthrough consumption and subsequent launches. All operations, including\\ndivisor zero, still use the same fixed schedule. Consecutive divides\\nremain nine clocks apart, and an ordinary dependent consumer acquires\\nno additional bubble. Do not introduce a ninth completion clock.\\n\\nIn RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, retain all four existing subtraction/XOR formulas\\ncaptured on the request edge and use the identical counter, handshake,\\nand result-bank update schedule. Real arithmetic must retain signed\\ntruncation toward zero, the dividend sign for REM, full-width unsigned\\noperands, divide-by-zero values, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior. ALTOPS does\\nnot establish correctness of the new real-arithmetic prefix circuit.\\n\\nPredict a modest three-percent fitness improvement from shorter divider\\ncompletion timing with unchanged instruction-cycle behavior. This is\\nunmeasured: the added magnitude-to-prefix launch path or the untouched\\nnormal EX cone may limit Fmax, and placement changes may erase the gain.\\nInspect request capture, recurrence update, completion, normal EX, and\\nresult-bank forwarding/writeback timi\nNOTES \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate pipeline payload storage from bubble and divide control\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz with 5,513 LUT4.\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment records show gains from divider\\nisolation and retiming, regressions from replacing its arithmetic, and\\na liveness failure when completion acquired one extra clock. The saved\\nr4s1 timing reports, whose RTL matches the current source, now report\\ndifferent worst paths: register-file data to an ID operand register\\n(6.103 ns), ID/EX control through execution selection to a normal EX/MEM\\nresult register (5.821 ns), and instruction-bus stimulus to an ID operand\\nregister (5.728 ns). These paths motivate changing pipeline storage\\ncontrol rather than adding another divider arithmetic transformation.\\nID currently clears its entire packed register on flush, and EX clears\\nits entire register on a bubble or divide wait while also conditionally\\nholding the normal result on divide acceptance. Those actions couple\\nwide data storage to instruction-validity and completion decisions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split control/valid updates from payload updates at the existing\\nID/EX boundary. Preserve control flush priority and true holds;\\nmake accepted invalid inputs control-zero. Reset payload only on\\nreset, capture it on !stall, and remove flush from its storage\\ncontrols. Preserve all valid instruction fields and cycle timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the EX/MEM control/bank-selector state from payload and\\nnormal-result storage. Capture payload and the normal result on\\nevery non-memory-stalled edge, including divide and bubble edges,\\nwhile maintaining control-zero bubbles, accepted divide metadata,\\nsaved operands, exact bank ownership and synchronous reset values.\\nPreserve complete memory holds and the direct registered address\\nconnection. Do not change the divider or arithmetic datapaths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all RVFI/ISS comparisons, arithmetic vectors, exact divide\\nlatency and retirement-spacing checks, and accepted-bus-effect\\ncounts. Add distinct stale payloads around load-use bubbles,\\nunavailable instruction responses, redirects, division waits and\\noverlapping memory holds. Check invalid ID/EX and EX/MEM control\\nwords are zero, active held payload is stable, and only valid\\ninstructions produce ordered retirements and memory effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every accepted-result, operand, latency, response-hold and\\ndivider-bank ownership assertion. Replace representation-specific\\nwhole-bundle-zero comparisons during invalid cycles with explicit\\nvalid/control-zero checks and checks that inactive payload cannot\\nbecome an architectural effect. Exercise changing normal-bank data\\nthrough divide completion, the first dependent consumer, reset and\\nprolonged owner stalls. Require stable direct memory addresses for\\nheld memory owners and correct addresses for every valid access.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 409.8, \"delta_pct\": 7.14, \"vs_baseline\": 44.9, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5490, \"ff\": 2112, \"seeds\": [184.06, 191.13, 179.89], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:04:12.733398Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r5s1 (improvement, +7.14%): Separating ID/EX and EX/MEM payload capture from bubble/flush and divide-completion control removed those decisions from wide data/reset/enable cones, raising reported median Fmax 7.14% with unchanged CoreMark cycles while requiring zero control on invalid entries and full payload/control retention during memory stalls.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The current winner reaches 382.48 fitness at 171.79 MHz with 5,513 LUT4.\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment records show gains from divider\\nisolation and retiming, regressions from replacing its arithmetic, and\\na liveness failure when completion acquired one extra clock. The saved\\nr4s1 timing reports, whose RTL matches the current source, now report\\ndifferent worst paths: register-file data to an ID operand register\\n(6.103 ns), ID/EX control through execution selection to a normal EX/MEM\\nresult register (5.821 ns), and instruction-bus stimulus to an ID operand\\nregister (5.728 ns). These paths motivate changing pipeline storage\\ncontrol rather than adding another divider arithmetic transformation.\\nID currently clears its entire packed register on flush, and EX clears\\nits entire register on a bubble or divide wait while also conditionally\\nholding the normal result on divide acceptance. Those actions couple\\nwide data storage to instruction-validity and completion decisions.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Restructure the existing ID/EX and EX/MEM boundaries into independently\\nupdated payload and control banks. Bubble insertion should clear valid\\nand the complete control word, while wide payload registers retain\\ntheir normal capture/hold behavior. This is one pipeline-storage change:\\npreserve the pipeline depth, instruction dispatch schedule, arithmetic,\\nforwarding priority, and existing eight-clock divider protocol.\\n\\nIn ID, retain the exact reset/flush/stall precedence for valid and ctrl.\\nCapture a decoded control word only with a valid incoming instruction;\\nan accepted invalid input installs zero control and valid=0. Separate\\npc, instr, immediates, source values and register addresses from that\\nbubble-clearing bank. Give the payload its existing synchronous reset\\nto zero, followed by capture when !stall and retention when stalled.\\nFlush must not appear in the payload reset, data mux, or write enable.\\nIn particular, a simultaneous load-use stall and flush clears control\\nwhile retaining inactive payload; a dmem or division hold preserves\\nthe complete active instruction. A redirect may capture discarded\\npayload, but it must install an invalid, control-zero entry.\\n\\nIn EX, preserve the current control-bank transitions: reset clears it;\\ndmem stall holds it; otherwise div_wait or !in.valid installs a bubble;\\notherwise capture ctrl_with_trap and valid. Preserve the registered\\ndivider-bank selector and its ownership rules. Independently capture\\nthe normal ALU-or-PC+4 result and instruction payload on every !stall\\nedge, with synchronous reset to zero. Allow this normal result bank to\\nupdate during invalid cycles, divide computation and divide acceptance.\\nRemove div_wait, div_accept and in.valid from its capture enable and\\nbubble-zero mux. On divide acceptance, the registered divider selector\\nmakes the divider bank authoritative, so the simultaneously captured\\nnormal result is inactive. Preserve saved post-forward divide operands\\nin the accepting metadata and all existing resolved-PC/trap behavior.\\nEach state bit must have exactly one procedural owner after the split.\\n\\nA memory stall must still freeze all EX/MEM payload, controls and bank\\nselection, keeping address, write data and byte enables stable until\\nacceptance. Keep mem_addr connected directly to the normal registered\\nresult, without validity or divider-selection muxes on its output, so\\nthe wrapper can still absorb its address register into block RAM.\\nAn inactive address may change only when no memory request is owned.\\nInvalid ID/EX and EX/MEM entries must retain zero control: downstream\\nhazard, forwarding and memory logic currently rely on those controls,\\nso stale payload must never assert a write, request, redirect or launch.\\nKeep MEM/WB unchanged, including its deliberate retention of forwarding\\ndata and reg_write metadata when a dmem hold clears retirement valid.\\n\\nExpect five percent higher fitness from shorter payload capture paths\\nwith unchanged marker-bracketed instruction cycles. This is a prediction,\\nnot a measured gain: capture-enable mapping, routing, the regfile read\\ntree, ordinary ALU selection or \nNOTES Implemented the pipeline-storage hypothesis. Local evaluation reports **409.80 fitness**, up **7.14%** from 382.48, with unchanged benchmark cycles. Median reported Fmax increases from 171.79 to **184.06 MHz**. Resources are **5,490 LUT4 and 2,112 DFF**, versus 5,513 LUT4 and 2,087 DFF. All local correctness gates passed. Orchestrator acceptance remains separate.\n\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv` and `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` changed in RTL. Each retains its packed output representation but assigns disjoint control and payload fields in separate clocked processes. Every state bit has one procedural owner; Yosys `check` reports zero problems.\n\nID's complete 22-bit control word and valid bit retain reset/flush priority over stall. Unstalled invalid inputs now install zero control. The 175 payload bits reset synchronously only on reset and capture whenever `!stall`. A simultaneous load-use stall and flush clears control while retaining payload; an unstalled redirect captures discarded payload beneath an invalid, control-zero entry. Memory and division holds retain the complete active ID instruction.\n\nEX's control, valid, and registered divider selector retain their original reset, memory-hold, bubble, and acceptance transitions. All normal-result and instruction-payload fields reset synchronously and capture on every `!stall` edge. Neither bubble insertion nor divide acceptance suppresses or zeroes that capture. Saved post-forward divide operands remain selected into accepting metadata; branch targets, resolved next PC, and trap handling retain their existing equations. The registered divider bank remains authoritative on acceptance. `mem_addr` remains directly connected to the normal registered result, and a memory hold freezes that result, payload, controls, and selection.\n\nThere are no RTL deviations from the proposed scope. Pipeline depth, dispatch timing, eight-clock divider protocol, divider arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, forwarding priority, decoder, nret=1 ports, and MEM/WB are unchanged. MEM/WB's deliberate forwarding-data/control retention when a hold clears retirement valid is explicitly checked by the expanded pipeline tests.\n\nTests retain all existing arithmetic, exact-latency, response-hold, ownership, ISS/RVFI, retirement-spacing, and accepted-memory-effect assertions. Invalid-cycle whole-bundle-zero comparisons in the EX tests were replaced with valid/control-zero checks; whole-bundle reset checks remain. Added coverage includes a direct ID-stage test and directed pipeline overlaps between memory holds and load-use, division, and redirects, plus unavailable fetches with distinct payloads. The EX test varies inactive normal data through computation and acceptance, then checks a prolonged owner hold and the first dependent consumer. To vary that bank during an active divide, the isolated EX test deliberately combines DIV with PC+4 selection; this is a synthetic interface stress vector. Integrated tests use legal decoded instructions and the unchanged ISS.\n\nLocal validation on the final RTL:\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: **PASS, no warnings**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 140 cases passed, no skips**. Includes 33 real and 33 ALTOPS ALU cases, both EX-bank modes, 30 decoder cases, and all 22 pipeline cases.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, on the first invocation. Includes `liveness_ch0`, unique retirement, cover, and illegal-instruction checks. No counterexample and no formal fix attempts. The unchanged nret=1 wrapper/configuration and all formal depths were used. This is the contract's ALTOPS formal gate; real-arithmetic deep formal was not run because arithmetic did not change.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and evaluator CoreMark.\n- Additional C++ selftest cosimulation with both stall flags and seeds 1, 19, and 99: **PASS**, all 115 retirements compared against the unchanged ISS per run. Starting and final RTL also have identical retirement-cycle sequences for each seed.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: **PASS**, all three routed seeds and the UART, CRC, OOB, and marker gates.\n- Source verification confirms 154 staged source/contract files, all 53 formal PASS results, exact agreement between staged formal RTL and submitted RTL, and agreement between the starting RTL snapshot and HEAD. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\n\nThe synthesized storage cones establish the intended structural change. In module-preserving Yosys `proc; opt` output, all ID payload flops changed from synchronous `reset OR flush` to reset alone, while retaining negative-polarity `stall` enables and direct payload data. EX's normal-result data cone previously included `div_accept`, `div_wait`, and incoming valid; its enable also included those decisions. The final data cone contains only ordinary ALU output, PC, and architectural result selection; its enable is only `!stall` and synchronous reset is only reset. Other EX payload fields likewise lose their bubble-zero data muxes. Saved-operand selection still uses `is_div`, as required for accepting metadata.\n\nThe actual Gowin netlist has 160 unique surviving ID payload DFFRE cells, all with the common capture enable and `cpu.reset` reset. It has 190 unique surviving EX payload DFFR cells with `cpu.reset`; the FPGA wrapper ties memory ready high, so EX's memory-hold enable optimizes away. All 32 normal-result bits are directly registered, and the memory-address vector is identical to those register bits. Other output payload fields directly alias their stored fields; no replacement output-side bubble mux was added. The expected four BSRAMs and one MULT36X36 remain in every routed seed. Counts account for synthesis merging shared fields and pruning unused metadata.\n\nAll three final routed critical-path reports were inspected:\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Reported worst path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.43 | 3.06 / 2.38 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | Wrapper instruction stimulus lfsr[20] to ID rs2_val[3] | 5.23 | 2.61 / 2.63 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | EX/MEM rd[0], through forwarding selection and JALR target, to IF pc[1] | 5.56 | 3.06 / 2.50 |\n\nFigures are rounded independently by nextpnr. Tools are Yosys 0.64 (`d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61`) and nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`. The requested standalone lint used Verilator 5.047; the unit and cosimulation gates used the installed Verilator 5.046 with the Conda Python environment containing cocotb and YAML. The eight Yosys array-to-register warnings originate in the unchanged divider.\n\nTiming remains a reported tool estimate. Prior result-bank experiments observed missing LUT3 bank-selector IOPATH arcs and omitted MEM/WB result endpoints in detailed exports. This run inspects all three unchanged routed gate reports and connectivity, but does not recharacterize those missing arcs or establish complete bank-to-forwarding/MEM/WB physical timing. No omitted arc is treated as zero delay; no timing model, constraint, or evaluation criterion was changed. The new critical paths do not prove that divider, multiplier, or other forwarding paths have timing margin.\n\nThe checked-in evaluator uses aggregate **2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**, whereas the requested supplemental run uses aggregate **6 KB**. The evaluator an\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Retime forwarding dependency comparisons into the ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural history shows that removing control decisions from wide\\nexecute paths has helped: r5s1 improved fitness to 409.80 at a reported\\nmedian 184.06 MHz with unchanged marker-bracketed cycles. Its implementation\\nnotes identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding selection and the JALR target\\nadder to IF pc[1] as the worst path in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns.\\nThe remaining seed instead limits on instruction-input to register-file\\nread capture. The current forward_unit performs four five-bit dependency\\ncomparisons after the ID/EX boundary, serially ahead of operand selection\\nand execute arithmetic. The same producer identities are available one\\nstage earlier, so registering their matches may shorten the measured\\nredirect path without another execute cycle. This differs from the failed\\none-hot register-file rewrite and predictor experiments: it relocates\\ndependency detection across an existing pipeline boundary.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and register four\\nindependent nonzero-source identity matches on the existing payload\\ncapture edge. Expose them as separate near/far match outputs for both\\nsources. Reset synchronously, retain on stall, and preserve all current\\npayload/control separation, flush priority, and instruction timing.\\nLeave final writer eligibility to the actual destination stages.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four live five-bit equality comparisons with registered\\nnear/far match inputs. Qualify each with the corresponding existing\\nstage reg_write control, retaining EX/MEM priority, MEM/WB fallback,\\nand the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding. Preserve forwarding from\\nMEM/WB's retained payload when its retirement valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the current ID/EX and EX/MEM rd tags into ID's early comparison\\ninputs and connect the registered match outputs to forward_unit.\\nKeep the current producer reg_write qualifications and EX operand\\nmux interfaces. Preserve hazard wiring, divider/result-bank ownership,\\nthe direct memory-address bank, nret=1 ports, and retirement logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Initialize the new producer-tag inputs and extend capture, reset,\\nflush, invalid-input, and hold coverage to the match outputs. Check\\nboth sources, zero-register exclusion, independent simultaneous near\\nand far matches, and changing tags while stalled. Retain every existing\\npayload/control assertion and verify metadata follows its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency chains covering both operands and both producer ages,\\nsuccessive writes to the same rd, nearer-writer suppression by traps,\\nand consumers using ALU operations, JALR, branches, and store data.\\nExercise load-use bubbles, unavailable fetches, prolonged dmem holds\\nwith valid-cleared WB forwarding, divider launch and completion,\\nback-to-back divisions, redirects, and reset. Retain ISS/RVFI operand\\nand result comparisons, exact divide latency and retirement spacing,\\nstable held memory requests, and accepted-memory-effect counts.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 376.98, \"delta_pct\": -8.01, \"vs_baseline\": 33.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5502, \"ff\": 2093, \"seeds\": [169.32, 191.75, 160.95], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:40:19.721587Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r6s1 (regression, -8.01%): Registering near/far forwarding matches at ID/EX removed live rd comparisons from EX, but alu_op-driven normal-result selection into EX/MEM became critical in all three seeds, lowering reported median Fmax from 184.06 to 169.32 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; comparator retiming still requires checking other routed paths across seeds.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The structural history shows that removing control decisions from wide\\nexecute paths has helped: r5s1 improved fitness to 409.80 at a reported\\nmedian 184.06 MHz with unchanged marker-bracketed cycles. Its implementation\\nnotes identify EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding selection and the JALR target\\nadder to IF pc[1] as the worst path in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns.\\nThe remaining seed instead limits on instruction-input to register-file\\nread capture. The current forward_unit performs four five-bit dependency\\ncomparisons after the ID/EX boundary, serially ahead of operand selection\\nand execute arithmetic. The same producer identities are available one\\nstage earlier, so registering their matches may shorten the measured\\nredirect path without another execute cycle. This differs from the failed\\none-hot register-file rewrite and predictor experiments: it relocates\\ndependency detection across an existing pipeline boundary.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Make one structural change: compute and register forwarding dependency\\nmatches alongside ID/EX operand capture, leaving the pipeline depth,\\noperand-data banks, two-bit forwarding selector interface, and instruction\\nscheduling unchanged. Do not add a predictor or alter arithmetic.\\n\\nFor each incoming raw instruction source field s, capture two independent\\nidentity matches on ID's existing !stall capture edge:\\n  near_match = (s != 0) && (s == current_ID_EX.rd)\\n  far_match  = (s != 0) && (s == current_EX_MEM.rd)\\nThere are four bits total for rs1 and rs2. At an actual instruction capture,\\nthose older instructions advance into EX/MEM and MEM/WB respectively. Keep\\nthese bits as separate registered ID-stage outputs rather than changing\\nthe packed pipeline bundles. Give them the same synchronous reset and\\ncapture/hold rule as ID payload, independent of flush; invalid instructions\\nstill have the existing zero control word and cannot act on stale matches.\\nUse raw source fields exactly as the current forwarding logic does, without\\nchanging source-use decoding or the load-use interlock.\\n\\nIn EX, replace live register-number comparisons with the registered matches\\nqualified by the actual destination stage's ctrl.reg_write. Select EX/MEM\\nfirst, then MEM/WB, then the captured register-file operand. Preserve both\\nmatches until this selection: a nearer instruction can lose write permission\\nthrough its existing trap logic, and an eligible farther writer must remain\\navailable. Identity matching alone must not authorize a bypass. Use the\\nsame producer-control qualification as the current implementation, including\\nMEM/WB ctrl.reg_write without an additional valid or wb_w_en gate. A memory\\nhold intentionally clears MEM/WB valid while retaining its forwarding data\\nand control. Keep x0 excluded by construction.\\n\\nPreserve the existing age relationships under every stall. A dmem hold\\nretains the match bits, ID/EX instruction, EX/MEM owner, and MEM/WB forwarding\\npayload. A load-use flush leaves a control-zero bubble; the consumer's\\nmatches are recomputed when it is finally captured, when its load producer\\nis in EX/MEM and will next be in MEM/WB. During blocking division, use these\\nmatches for the initial request's forwarded operands, then retain the\\nexisting saved divide operands as authoritative while older stages drain.\\nDo not reinterpret frozen matches as fresh producer identities during an\\nactive divide. The instruction captured on divide acceptance receives new\\nmatches and can bypass the registered divider result from EX/MEM normally.\\nRetain the existing load-address guard on divide launch, reset cancellation,\\nredirect priorities, and all eight-clock real/ALTOPS divider behavior.\\n\\nThis removes the rd-to-rs comparator layer from the execute-to-redirect\\npath rather than moving operand data or adding latency. Expect about four\\npercent higher fitness with identical cycles for identical binaries and\\nstall schedules. This is unmeasured: the added ID comparison paths, late\\nwrite qualification, register-file path, arithmetic, or placement may limit\\nimprovement. Check that synthesis retains the four match registers and\\nthat routed EX forwarding n\nNOTES Implemented the forwarding-comparison retiming hypothesis. Correctness and cycle equivalence checks pass, but the measured three-seed FPGA result regresses: median reported Fmax is 169.32 MHz versus 184.06 MHz, and evaluator fitness is 376.98 versus 409.80 (about -8.01%). The proposed four-percent improvement was not observed. The implementation is left in place for the orchestrator to evaluate.\n\nStarting RTL: commit 4c676b5bcfebb0c7fef236028f4828bd467d81d9. Source changes are limited to cores/bench/rtl/{id_stage,forward_unit,core}.sv and cores/bench/test/test_{id_stage,pipeline}.py, plus these explicitly requested notes.\n\nActual implementation:\n\n- ID accepts the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and captures four independent nonzero raw-source identity matches as separate outputs. They share the payload's synchronous reset and !stall capture enable. Flush and input validity do not gate their capture or reset.\n- Forwarding uses those registered matches with the actual EX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write signals. EX/MEM keeps priority, MEM/WB remains the fallback, and the 00/01/10 interface is unchanged. No valid or wb_w_en qualification was added. Both matches survive a near writer losing permission through a trap.\n- The packed bundles, operand/result banks, direct memory address bank, decoder, hazard unit, divider, arithmetic, redirects, RVFI, ports, and nret=1 configuration are unchanged. An active divide continues to use its existing saved operands; the instruction captured at divide acceptance receives fresh matches.\n- ID tests now cover both raw fields, simultaneous near/far matches, x0, all 256 combinations of four representative source/tag values, changed tags and instruction input during holds, invalid inputs, flush priority, and synchronous reset with all four bits set.\n- Pipeline tests add both operand/producer ages, repeated destinations, JAL/JALR trap suppression with eligible older writers, MEM-stage load-write suppression, ALU/branch/JALR/store consumers, prolonged memory holds with valid-cleared WB forwarding, load-use bubbles, unavailable fetch, and dependent consecutive divisions. Existing ISS/RVFI, eight-clock divide acceptance, nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, reset cancellation, stable requests, and accepted-memory-effect assertions remain. Optional PIPELINE_TRACE_PATH records full directed-run traces for starting/candidate comparison.\n\nLocal formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness_ch0, reg_ch0, unique, cover, illegal-instruction, and all generated instruction checks. This was the first RTL attempt; there were no formal failures, counterexamples, or fix retries. The unchanged command was RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh, which auto-selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config. The normal gate retains RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Real divider arithmetic and latency were exercised by the existing real-mode unit suites and ISS comparisons; formal-deep was not run because arithmetic was untouched.\n\nOther local validation:\n\n- The exact requested verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv command passes without warnings.\n- All nine pytest unit runners pass: 144 cocotb cases, including real and ALTOPS ALU/divider and EX-bank tests, decoder, ID, immediate generator, pipeline, and register file. The final additional all-four-match reset assertion was rechecked with the ID runner: pass.\n- The same expanded pipeline test file passes on starting RTL. All 33 protocol runs are byte-identical between versions, including 5,785 retirement records, every retirement cycle, deterministic stall schedules, and accepted memory effects.\n- Unchanged tools.eval.cosim passes both evaluator ELFs. Supplemental full selftest ISS comparisons pass 115 retirements for each version under zero wait and simultaneous imem/dmem stalls with seeds 1, 2, 3, and 42. Complete emitted RVFI/cycle traces match byte-for-byte. Final retirement cycles are respectively 159, 198, 189, 187, and 198.\n- git diff --check passes. Tested candidate RTL/tests were byte-compared with the submitted files; contract source/config copies were also checked unchanged.\n\nFPGA evaluation used the unchanged synthesis script, core_bench_si wrapper, constraints, nextpnr_run.sh, and tools.eval.fpga evaluator, with seeds 1/2/3 for both versions. Every seed routed successfully. These are reported post-route values:\n\n| Version | Seed 1 MHz | Seed 2 MHz | Seed 3 MHz | Median MHz | Fitness | LUT4 | DFF |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Starting | 184.06 | 191.13 | 179.89 | 184.06 | 409.80 | 5490 | 2112 |\n| Retimed | 169.32 | 191.75 | 160.95 | 169.32 | 376.98 | 5502 | 2093 |\n\nThe evaluator's 2 KB, ITERATIONS=10, -O3 binary and default ~22% imem/dmem stall schedule produce exactly 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles for both versions. Fitness above uses the evaluator's unrounded iteration/cycle calculation, not its rounded ipc_coremark JSON display.\n\nEvery routed critical path was inspected. Starting seeds 1 and 3 reproduce the EX/MEM rd[0] -> forwarding -> JALR target -> IF pc[1] paths at 5.43 ns and 5.56 ns; starting seed 2 reports instruction-input/LFSR -> ID register-file operand capture at 5.23 ns. Candidate seeds 1 and 2 instead report ID/EX alu_op[3] -> normal ALU result selection -> EX/MEM normal alu_result[15], at 5.91 ns and 5.22 ns. Candidate seed 3 reports the corresponding alu_op[2] path at 6.21 ns. Thus the earlier seed-2 register-file path did not remain the reported limit.\n\nStructural inspection of synth.json and each of the three pnr.json files confirms four distinct rs{1,2}_{near,far}_match DFFRE cells with the same clock, reset, and enable as ID payload. Traversing the mapped near/far qualification cones to sequential boundaries yields only the corresponding match bits and actual producer reg_write registers, with no live destination-number comparator layer. Near qualification maps to LUT2; far qualification maps to LUT3 with near priority. The synthesized dmem address still aliases the unselected, directly registered EX normal-result bank. This confirms the intended structural change despite the performance regression.\n\nTiming caveat: these are the unchanged toolchain's timing reports, with its existing timing-model/arc coverage limitations. Omitted paths/arcs were not treated as timing margin. No timing models, constraints, report parsing, or memory-address topology were changed to improve the score.\n\nThe evaluator workload and supplemental canonical 6 KB workload are distinct. Both were built from unchanged sources with -O3 and ITERATIONS=10. For each workload/stall seed, both RTL versions ran the identical ELF and produced identical final retirement records, marker timestamps, and UART. Supplemental results:\n\n| Working set | Stall seed | Bracketed cycles, both RTLs | Final retirement cycle, both RTLs | Retirements, both RTLs |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,490,174 | 4,513,268 | 2,937,037 |\n| 2 KB | 2 | 4,492,556 | 4,515,577 | 2,937,037 |\n| 2 KB | 3 | 4,493,908 | 4,516,723 | 2,937,037 |\n| 6 KB | 1 | 32,838,499 | 32,902,122 | 22,093,920 |\n| 6 KB | 2 | 32,840,744 | 32,904,210 | 22,093,920 |\n| 6 KB | 3 | 32,834,771 | 32,898,145 | 22,093,920 |\n\nEvery CoreMark run reaches EBREAK, reports oob=false and bench_bracketed=true, and contains \"Correct operation validated.\" UART text. The 2 KB runs pass unchanged validate_coremark_uart, includi\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Result selection is a recurring routed bottleneck in this core. The\\nr6s1 structural attempt removed live forwarding comparisons, but all\\nthree candidate seeds became limited by ID/EX alu_op to the normal\\nEX/MEM result, producing an 8.01% regression. In r2s0, multiply selector\\npaths remained critical and replacing the complete ALU case with\\none-hot masking increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. Meanwhile,\\nr2s1's separate divider completion bank and r5s1's independent payload\\nstorage improved fitness without changing CoreMark cycles. These results\\nmotivate separating multiply result ownership at the existing register\\nboundary. They do not establish that multiplier arithmetic limits the\\ncurrent 184.06 MHz winner, whose reported worst paths also include JALR\\nforwarding and register-file reads.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the ordinary integer output from a dedicated combinational\\nmultiply output. Preserve all arithmetic expressions, the compact\\nencoded multiply case and DSP sharing, every ALTOPS formula, and the\\nexisting divider interfaces. Remove multiplication from the output\\nconsumed by the normal EX/MEM bank.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the parallel multiply-result payload register and registered\\nmultiply ownership. Capture it with synchronous reset and !stall,\\nindependent of validity or completion. Select registered integer,\\nmultiply or divider banks for out.alu_result at the existing cycle;\\npreserve jump-link priority, bubble/hold rules, divider ownership,\\nmetadata, and the unselected normal bank's direct mem_addr connection.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Route existing multiply checks to the new multiply output without\\nremoving any exact-result vector. Check all four real and ALTOPS\\nmultiply operations, signedness and high-half boundaries, random\\noperands and rapid operation transitions against independent expected\\narithmetic. Retain every integer and divider result/protocol assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ownership tests to distinguish integer, multiply and divider\\nbanks. Cover distinct consecutive multiply results, all class\\ntransitions, inactive payload capture, synchronous reset, bubbles,\\nprolonged holds, jump-link priority and existing divide completion\\ntiming in real and ALTOPS modes. Verify the selected result and its\\nmetadata stay coherent while the direct memory-address bank remains\\nindependent of multiply/divide selection.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add mixed multiply producer/consumer streams across both forwarding\\nages, repeated destinations, x0, load-produced operands and dependent\\nALU, branch, JALR, memory and M-extension instructions. Exercise both\\nbus stalls, valid-cleared WB forwarding, redirects and reset. Retain\\nISS/RVFI comparisons and accepted-memory-effect checks; compare with\\nstarting RTL to assert unchanged instruction spacing and no added\\nmultiply latency or consumer bubble.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 382.21, \"delta_pct\": -6.73, \"vs_baseline\": 35.14, \"fmax_mhz\": 171.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5515, \"ff\": 2124, \"seeds\": [175.25, 171.67, 167.56], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T11:22:01.176786Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r7s1 (regression, -6.73%): Separating MUL into an EX/MEM bank left EX/MEM rd-to-JALR forwarding and register-file reads limiting reported Fmax despite unchanged CoreMark cycles and one MULT36X36; missing result-bank mux arcs prevent attributing the regression to bank-to-EX/WB delay.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"Result selection is a recurring routed bottleneck in this core. The\\nr6s1 structural attempt removed live forwarding comparisons, but all\\nthree candidate seeds became limited by ID/EX alu_op to the normal\\nEX/MEM result, producing an 8.01% regression. In r2s0, multiply selector\\npaths remained critical and replacing the complete ALU case with\\none-hot masking increased MULT36X36 usage from one to three. Meanwhile,\\nr2s1's separate divider completion bank and r5s1's independent payload\\nstorage improved fitness without changing CoreMark cycles. These results\\nmotivate separating multiply result ownership at the existing register\\nboundary. They do not establish that multiplier arithmetic limits the\\ncurrent 184.06 MHz winner, whose reported worst paths also include JALR\\nforwarding and register-file reads.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Add one parallel 32-bit multiply-result register at the existing EX/MEM\\nboundary. Retain the ordinary integer/link-result bank and the existing\\ndivider completion bank. Select among these registered owners after\\nEX/MEM using registered result-class metadata. This is the sole structural\\nchange: every RV32I and MUL instruction keeps its existing execution,\\nforwarding and retirement cycle, and DIV/REM keeps its current schedule.\\n\\nIn alu.sv, expose separate combinational integer and multiply results.\\nThe integer output case retains ADD through LUI and the current default\\nzero behavior, with no multiply term or multiply-result mux feeding it.\\nThe multiply output uses a compact encoded case for MUL, MULH, MULHU and\\nMULHSU, with zero as its default. Keep the existing signed/unsigned product\\nexpressions and exact real and ALTOPS results. Preserve synthesis sharing\\nwithin this multiply case; do not create individual per-operation product\\nregisters, replicate multipliers, or introduce the failed full-ALU one-hot\\nmasked-OR network. The core must not recombine these two outputs before\\ntheir EX/MEM registers.\\n\\nEX captures the integer result or PC+4 in its current normal bank and\\ncaptures the selected multiply result in the new bank on the same edge.\\nGive both banks synchronous reset and the existing !stall payload capture\\nenable. Capture inactive multiply data freely during bubbles, division\\ncomputation and other instructions; validity, multiply recognition and\\ndivide acceptance must not enter either bank's data-zero or enable cone.\\nPreserve the winner's separation of payload from bubble/flush controls.\\n\\nRegister a multiply-owner bit alongside the existing divider-owner bit.\\nReset and an accepted bubble clear ownership, a real memory hold retains\\nit, and an accepted legal multiply selects the new bank. Preserve PC+4\\npriority for a jump and the existing divide-accept priority. The bits\\nmust be mutually exclusive; ordinary instructions select the normal bank.\\nReconstruct out.alu_result with one small class selection using only\\nregistered selectors and registered data. Both existing EX/MEM forwarding\\nand MEM/WB capture consume that same selected value. There must be no\\nadditional completion edge, live opcode qualification, or extra consumer\\nbubble. Consecutive multiplies and immediate dependent ALU, branch, JALR,\\nload-address, store-data, multiply and divide consumers retain their\\nexisting schedules and exact post-forward RVFI operands.\\n\\nKeep mem_addr directly connected to the normal registered integer/link\\nbank. Loads and stores always own that bank; the multiply/divide output\\nselection must never enter the dmem address, lane-selection or alignment\\npath. A memory hold freezes all EX/MEM banks, selectors and metadata so\\nits request remains stable. Preserve the existing divider bank lifetime,\\neight compute clocks, nine-clock consecutive-divide dispatch spacing,\\nsaved divide operands and reset cancellation. MEM/WB must continue to\\nretain its forwarding payload and reg_write control when a memory hold\\nclears retirement valid. No hazard or retirement policy changes are needed.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is a smaller integer-result selection cone and\\nindependent placement of multiply capture, at a cost of about 32 data\\nflops and one ownership bit before synthesi\nNOTES # Give multiplication a separate EX/MEM result bank\n\nImplemented the proposed structural change. Correctness and cycle comparisons pass. The unchanged FPGA evaluator reports a regression: median 171.67 MHz and fitness 382.21, versus the starting core's recorded 184.06 MHz (6.73% lower at identical cycles). The predicted 4% improvement was not observed. The starting frequency is the recorded value in core.yaml, not a new starting-RTL P&R measurement.\n\nStarting HEAD: `53c327aef09bbb852fbebae9b62adc224456ce3e`.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: retained `out` as the integer-only combinational output, with ADD through LUI and default zero. Added `mul_out` with the compact encoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU case and default zero. Original signed/unsigned product expressions and all ALTOPS formulas are unchanged.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: added one 32-bit `mul_result_q` and one `mul_result_sel_q`. Integer/link and multiply payloads capture independently on the existing `!stall` edge with synchronous reset, without validity, ownership, or divide-accept gating. Legal accepted multiplies acquire ownership; jump-link priority and divide-accept priority are preserved. Reset and accepted bubbles clear both selectors; holds retain them. One encoded selection of registered banks drives the existing forwarding and MEM/WB result consumers. `mem_addr` remains wired directly to `reg_q.alu_result`.\n- Extended the three requested test files. Other RTL, decoder legality, nret=1, IO, divider implementation, MEM/WB hold behavior, hazards, retirement, evaluation scripts, benchmark sources, timing models and constraints are unchanged. No extra multiply edge or consumer bubble was added.\n\n## Correctness and timing comparisons\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- Unmodified `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including liveness, cover, unique, illegal and every instruction check. First gate attempt passed; no RTL fix/retry was needed. Used `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`; nret=1 automatically selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/config. A private copy of the formal framework under the supplied TMPDIR kept generated checks away from the shared framework. The installed conda Python supplied click, YAML and cocotb missing from the default Python environment.\n- All nine pytest runners passed: **151 cocotb cases** across ALU real/ALTOPS (34 each), EX ownership real/ALTOPS (7 each), pipeline (25), decoder (30), ID payload (1), immediate generation (8), and register file (5). Two ownership tests were strengthened after the full run to require distinct consecutive products and synchronous reset of an asserted multiply-owner bit, then passed again in both arithmetic modes.\n- Arithmetic coverage retains every existing integer/divider vector and protocol assertion. Added cross-products of signedness/high-half boundaries, 256 additional random operand pairs, rapid opcode/class/default transitions, and zero on each inactive ALU output. Ownership coverage includes all integer/multiply/divider/bubble class transitions; inactive captures under invalid, illegal and jump stimuli; poisoned inactive multiply inputs during divide computation; prolonged holds; saved divide operands; terminal acceptance; reset; and link priority.\n- Mixed pipeline streams cover all four multiply operations, both forwarding ages, repeated destinations, x0, load operands, dependent ALU, branch, JALR, load/store address, store data, multiply and divide instructions. Directed holds exercise valid-cleared MEM/WB forwarding, redirects and division. Accepted memory effects and all meaningful RVFI fields remain compared with the unchanged Python ISS.\n- Starting versus candidate pipeline comparison: **35 streams, 6,572 retirements, 1,622 accepted memory effects, 29,812 driven cycles**. Retirement cycles, architectural RVFI data, accepted-memory cycles/effects, actual ready schedules, divider launches and dispatches match exactly. Only inactive RVFI memory payload is normalized; all operand values, active memory fields and retirement metadata remain exact. Unstalled multiply consumers retain one-cycle spacing, or nine cycles when the consumer is a divide; consecutive divides retain nine-clock dispatch spacing and eight compute clocks.\n- Unchanged C++ selftest cosimulation: 115 retirements per run, exact ISS match and starting/candidate cycle match. Final retirement cycles are 159 with zero wait and 198/203/198 with both-bus stalls at seeds 1/19/81.\n- Fast formal uses ALTOPS as required. Periodic real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; real arithmetic is covered here by exact-result unit tests, mixed ISS comparisons and CoreMark validation.\n\n## CoreMark: keep the two workloads separate\n\nBoth binaries were built with RV32IM/ILP32, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10. Starting and candidate used the same ELF bytes and deterministic bus schedules. Every run reached EBREAK without OOB access, had valid start/stop MMIO markers, and printed `Correct operation validated.`. Entire UART strings and marker records match between RTL versions.\n\n| Workload | Bus schedule | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Marker-bracketed cycles, both RTL versions |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Evaluator, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 | Zero wait | 12,124 | 3,495,706 | 3,483,582 |\n| Evaluator, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 | Both buses ~22% stalls | 15,801 | 4,507,286 | 4,491,485 |\n| Supplemental, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000 | Zero wait | 42,886 | 25,410,161 | 25,367,275 |\n| Supplemental, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000 | Both buses ~22% stalls | 56,393 | 32,893,064 | 32,836,671 |\n\nEvaluator 2 KB UART passes the unchanged `validate_coremark_uart`: seedcrc e9f5, list e714, matrix 1fd7, state 8e3a, final fcaf. Supplemental 6 KB separately matches the requested canonical CRCs: list **d4b0**, matrix **be52**, state **5e47**; seedcrc 8a02, final 5949. The 6 KB UART reports 2000 bytes per algorithm and labels itself a 6k performance run. The unchanged port's static compiler-flags text says -O2; the actual build commands used -O3. No benchmark/reporting code was altered.\n\nELF SHA-256:\n\n- Evaluator 2 KB: `b8346ec25bde132f7f489662ecde763b960c1828d8c7b328e15d83d7b89e7fda`\n- Supplemental 6 KB: `040334f9e71bfa359d78795c031a545e8fd5b9ecb75598df1a801a2307efc982`\n\n## Mapping and routed evaluation\n\nUnmodified `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with NRET=1 and the existing single-issue FPGA wrapper, completed all three seeds. All build products were staged under TMPDIR. Yosys 0.64+149 / nextpnr 0.10-45-g98c18d7f were used.\n\nMapped netlist traversal confirms separate 32-flop integer, multiply and divider banks. The DSP lies in the multiply bank's combinational capture cone and is absent from the integer bank's capture cone. MEM/WB's ALU-data cone ends at exactly the 96 registered data bits plus the two registered class bits, with no live-opcode or completion-data boundary. Forwarding and MEM/WB share the selected source. The intermediate selected wire name is optimized into consumers; disconnected netname aliases were not treated as proof of separation. The direct normal address bank remains separate.\n\nResources: **one MULT36X36, four DPX9B block RAMs, 5,515 routed LUT4 equivalents, 2,124 FF**. The recorded starting resources are 5,490 LUT4 / 2,112 FF. Thus the complete mapped design grows by 25 LUT4 equivalents and 12 FF after optimization, despite the source-level addition of 33 register bits. No product registers per multiply operation or multiplier\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Absorb ID operand capture into synchronous register-file RAM banks\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural entries in experiments/log.jsonl and LESSONS.md show that\\nchanging storage boundaries can improve fitness without changing cycles:\\nr5s1 reached 409.80 at 184.06 MHz by separating payload capture from\\npipeline control. Its reported seed-2 limit was instruction input through\\nregister-file reading to ID operand capture; seeds 1 and 3 instead limited\\non forwarding through JALR resolution. The r7s1 multiply-bank experiment\\nagain exposed register-file reads and forwarding as competing limits.\\nThese reports establish a real read-path target, not that it dominates\\nevery seed of the current winner.\\n\\nToday reg_file.sv resets the complete 32-by-32 array and performs two\\nasynchronous reads, followed by write-first bypass and ID/EX operand\\nflops. The r5s0 one-hot read-tree rewrite reduced area but regressed routed\\ntiming; r6s1 forwarding-comparison retiming also regressed. No recorded\\nstructural attempt moves register-file storage and operand capture into\\nsynchronous RAM. This experiment removes the large fabric read network\\nby changing where operands are stored and clocked, while retaining the\\ncurrent instruction schedule.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement coherent per-source synchronous RAM banks with a read\\ncapture enable independent of WB writes. Add the resettable shared\\ninitialized bitmap, captured x0/initialized qualifications, and\\ncaptured same-edge WB collision bypass. Hold the complete response\\nwhen reads are disabled. Preserve one-write/two-read bandwidth and\\ndeterministic reset-visible values without resetting RAM contents.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Transfer ownership of the two ID/EX operand registers to the\\nregister file's synchronous read responses. Store instruction\\nmetadata and controls as before, and assemble outgoing operand\\nfields from the matching registered responses without another\\ncapture edge. Preserve independent payload capture, control-zero\\nbubbles, and complete metadata retention during holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the register-file read capture enable to the existing\\nID-stage payload enable and route its registered operand responses\\ninto ID/EX assembly. Retain WB write wiring, external ports,\\nforwarding, hazard priorities, memory-address wiring, and RVFI.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt read sampling to the intentional synchronous internal\\ninterface while retaining every architectural value assertion.\\nCheck all read-address pairs, both-port write collisions, first\\nwrites after reset, populated-storage reset, x0, independent WB\\nwrites during read holds, and held responses while addresses and\\nwrites change. Use an independent architectural-register scoreboard\\nto verify coherent copies and write-first results at capture edges.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Model the synchronous register-file response timing in ID tests\\nand check that instruction metadata and operand responses describe\\nthe same instruction on every capture. Preserve decoder/control\\nand payload/hold assertions; cover reset, invalid fetches, redirect\\nflushes, and load-use flush-plus-hold without adding a read cycle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add full-RVFI operand and cycle comparisons for consecutive reads,\\nWB/read collisions on either or both ports, producer distances,\\ndependent ALU/JALR/branch/store instructions, load-use interlocks,\\nDIV/REM launch/completion, x0, and reset of populated registers.\\nExercise imem stalls, long dmem holds, and older WB writes while\\nID is frozen. Require unchanged retirement timing for identical\\nstalls, one ordered retirement per instruction, and no duplicate\\nmemory effects while retaining all existing correctness checks.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 385.46, \"delta_pct\": -5.94, \"vs_baseline\": 36.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 173.13, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2824, \"ff\": 1105, \"seeds\": [173.13, 148.68, 190.11], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T12:08:01.603323Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r8s1 (regression, -5.94%): Absorbing ID/EX operand capture into two synchronous SDPX9B register-file banks nearly halved LUT/DFF use without adding CoreMark cycles, but forwarding/JALR and ALU-result selection still limited reported Fmax; missing RAM address/output logic arcs prevent attributing the regression to RAM latency.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The structural entries in experiments/log.jsonl and LESSONS.md show that\\nchanging storage boundaries can improve fitness without changing cycles:\\nr5s1 reached 409.80 at 184.06 MHz by separating payload capture from\\npipeline control. Its reported seed-2 limit was instruction input through\\nregister-file reading to ID operand capture; seeds 1 and 3 instead limited\\non forwarding through JALR resolution. The r7s1 multiply-bank experiment\\nagain exposed register-file reads and forwarding as competing limits.\\nThese reports establish a real read-path target, not that it dominates\\nevery seed of the current winner.\\n\\nToday reg_file.sv resets the complete 32-by-32 array and performs two\\nasynchronous reads, followed by write-first bypass and ID/EX operand\\nflops. The r5s0 one-hot read-tree rewrite reduced area but regressed routed\\ntiming; r6s1 forwarding-comparison retiming also regressed. No recorded\\nstructural attempt moves register-file storage and operand capture into\\nsynchronous RAM. This experiment removes the large fabric read network\\nby changing where operands are stored and clocked, while retaining the\\ncurrent instruction schedule.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace the asynchronous flop-based integer register file plus its two\\nID/EX operand registers with two coherent inferred synchronous-read RAM\\nbanks, one per source port. Broadcast the existing single WB write to\\nboth banks. Each bank has independent read and write addresses and a\\nsingle shared clock. The RAM read edge must be the existing ID/EX capture\\nedge: instruction N's source addresses before that edge produce its\\nregistered source values alongside instruction N's decoded metadata\\nafter the edge. Do not add a fetch, operand-read, execute, or retirement\\ncycle. This is one structural change to register-file/ID storage ownership.\\n\\nInfer block RAM using portable behavioral SystemVerilog and the RTL\\nram_style attribute, with no vendor-only simulation replacement. Use\\nsynchronous read outputs without bulk array reset. Preserve the existing\\narchitectural reset-to-zero behavior with one resettable initialized-bit\\nbitmap shared by the two coherent copies. Every accepted nonzero WB\\nwrite sets its destination's bit; reset clears the bitmap and suppresses\\nwrites. Capture each read's initialized/nonzero qualification on the\\nsame edge as its RAM read. Unwritten registers and x0 return zero even\\nthough raw RAM contents are unspecified. Reset also clears the captured\\noutput qualifications so a held or previously populated output cannot\\nleak stale data after reset. Do not replace reset with a multi-cycle\\nmemory-clearing sweep or expose unknown data to execution or RVFI.\\n\\nImplement explicit same-edge write-first behavior for each read port.\\nCapture the nonzero WB address-match flag and the matching WB data with\\nthe read transaction; select that registered data on a collision before\\nconsidering the captured initialized bit. A first write after reset must\\ntherefore be visible to a simultaneous read. Neither later WB values,\\nlive read addresses, nor later bitmap updates may change a held operand.\\nDo not depend on an unspecified primitive read/write collision result.\\nKeep raw memory/read-output storage free of reset muxes that would prevent\\nRAM inference; implement required reset visibility in the small response\\nqualification bank. The intended inference pattern is supported by the\\nYosys memory documentation, but actual Gowin mapping must be inspected:\\nhttps://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/v0.50/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html\\n\\nDrive both RAM read enables and all response metadata captures from the\\nsame existing ID payload capture decision, !stall_id, with reset taking\\npriority in qualification state. WB writes remain independent of this\\nread enable, so older instructions can write while ID holds. A dmem or\\ndivider hold retains both operand responses and all instruction metadata.\\nA load-use flush with stall retains payload while clearing control;\\nan unstalled redirect or invalid-fetch bubble follows the current\\nindependent payload-capture policy. Assemble id_ex_t operands directly\\nfrom these registered respons\nNOTES The synchronous RAM register-file hypothesis is implemented, but **reject it as a fitness improvement**. The unchanged evaluator reports **385.46**, versus **409.80** for a fresh build and three-seed route of the starting RTL: **-5.94%**, with identical marker-window cycles. The implementation remains in the allowed bench RTL/tests for orchestrator review; it is not an accepted replacement for the starting winner.\n\n`reg_file.sv` now contains two coherent 32-by-32 synchronous-read arrays with `ram_style=\"block\"`. Both receive the same accepted nonzero WB write, independently of read enable. Reads, captured nonzero/initialized qualifications, collision flags, and a shared 32-bit collision-data register use the existing `!stall_id` capture decision. Collision data takes priority over initialized qualification, including a first write after reset. Reset suppresses writes and clears the shared initialized bitmap and four response qualification bits; RAM contents and raw read outputs have no reset. Held responses do not depend on subsequent addresses, writes, or bitmap updates.\n\n`id_stage.sv` stores metadata/control in a local operand-free struct and assembles `id_ex_t` directly from that state and the RF's registered responses. There is no second operand capture. Metadata capture, control-zero bubbles, flush-plus-hold behavior, and invalid-fetch payload capture retain their existing policies. `core.sv` only adds the RF read-enable connection. Forwarding priority, retained MEM/WB forwarding during memory holds, saved DIV/REM operands, direct normal EX/MEM memory-address bank, arithmetic, multiplier sharing, ALTOPS, ports, and `core.yaml`/`nret: 1` are unchanged.\n\nOne mapping refinement beyond the advisory plan is `no_rw_check` on the two raw arrays. The initial inferred read-first memories caused Gowin mapping to insert delayed-write registers and additional transparency emulation, despite the explicit response bypass already hiding every raw collision. This attribute removes that redundant emulation; it does not relax the visible write-first contract. Simulators still use the same behavioral SystemVerilog, and the final formal gate also passes with raw collisions unspecified. This pattern is documented in [Yosys memory handling](https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/v0.50/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html), under synchronous SDP with undefined collision behavior. A single collision-data register serves both ports because they can only collide with the same WB transaction.\n\nThe RF tests retain the original value assertions with edge-based sampling and add an independent architectural scoreboard, all 1,024 read-address pairs after initialization and reset, both-port and individual collisions, first writes, x0, populated-memory resets, suppressed reset-edge writes, changing addresses/WB while held, later initialization behind an unwritten held response, and 4,000 randomized transactions. The ID test drives RF responses after the metadata capture edge, so a redundant operand register would fail. Pipeline tests independently model WB/read capture, exercise producer distances and both operands, ALU/JALR/branch/store dependencies, load-use interlocks, DIV/REM, populated-register reset, imem stalls, long dmem holds, and older WB writes during ID holds. Existing ISS, control/payload retention, ordered retirement, and unique accepted-memory-effect assertions remain active.\n\nLocal validation of the submitted RTL:\n\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS, no warnings**. `git diff --check`: **PASS**.\n- Complete cocotb suite: **9 runners, 145 cases passed, 0 failures, 0 skips**, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic and EX-bank modes.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed** on the final implementation. Includes channel-0 liveness, unique, cover, illegal instructions, and register/PC checks. No formal counterexample or failed formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts. Earlier structural forms also passed. The unchanged single-retirement wrapper, depths, and ALTOPS configuration were used. Deep arithmetic formal was not run because arithmetic and divider latency were untouched.\n- Starting and final RTL: **34 identical protocol scenarios, 6,093 complete RVFI records with exact retirement-cycle equality, and 1,515 identical accepted memory transactions**. The same deterministic random seeds and directed bus schedules were used. The integer prefix retires on consecutive cycles through WB/read collisions.\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest ISS and evaluator CoreMark CRC/UART gates.\n- Additional C++ selftest runs with `--istall --dstall --seed 1/19/99`: **PASS**, 115 retirements each, field-for-field ISS agreement and byte-identical starting/final full RVFI traces including cycle numbers.\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: all three seeds route successfully, CoreMark completes, bounds/CRC/UART/marker checks pass. **The fitness improvement criterion fails.**\n\nAll gates ran from unchanged scratch contract copies under `.tmp/rf-sync/`; final gates used `candidate-norw/`. The staged final RTL and tests were checked against the submitted files, the formal staged RTL matches them, all 53 formal task logs contain PASS, and the starting RTL snapshot matches HEAD `7583066de166bd6cd773a698ecfc285232a61ed3`. No evaluator, benchmark source, other core, or core configuration was edited.\n\nFresh unchanged evaluator results:\n\n| Metric | Starting RTL | Final synchronous RF |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Seed 1 routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Seed 2 routed MHz | 191.13 | 148.68 |\n| Seed 3 routed MHz | 179.89 | 190.11 |\n| Median routed MHz | 184.06 | 173.13 |\n| Marker-window cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Iterations | 10 | 10 |\n| Fitness, median MHz × 1,000,000 × 10 / cycles | 409.80 | 385.46 |\n| Routed LUT4 | 5,490 | 2,824 |\n| Routed DFF | 2,112 | 1,105 |\n| BSRAM | 4 | 6 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\n\nMapping inspection confirms exactly two `SDPX9B` RF cells, each configured for 36-bit reads and writes: a 512-by-36 physical geometry using 32 addresses and 32 payload bits. Four existing `DPX9B` memory cells remain. Total BSRAM usage is **6/46**, LUT4 **2,824/20,736**, DFF **1,105/15,552**, and MULT36X36 **1/12**. Both RF clocks are identical, read CE matches the collision-data capture CE, write address/data/enable are broadcast coherently, and both primitive reset inputs are tied low. All 64 raw RF response bits are driven directly by RAM outputs, with no fabric operand-output flops. The shared bitmap maps to 31 resettable flops (x0 constant), qualification/collision state to four resettable flops, and collision data to 32 enabled flops. The final memory-libmap output has no extra read-first/transparency emulation cells. Area fell 48.56% in LUT4 and 47.68% in DFF, but this did not improve the median routed throughput.\n\nAll three final routed limiting paths were inspected. Separate runs adding only SDF/detailed report outputs reproduced each gate Fmax exactly; scoring still uses the unchanged gate.\n\n| Seed | Reported limiting path | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | ID rs1 address bit 1 → forwarding selection → JALR resolution → IF PC bit 1 | 5.776 | 3.037 | 2.739 |\n| 2 | ID ALU operation bit 3 → ALU result selection → EX/MEM normal result bit 2 | 6.726 \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Register JALR targets in a two-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The accepted r5s1 design reaches 409.80 fitness at 184.06 MHz. Its saved\\nrouted reports identify EX/MEM destination-tag comparison, operand\\nforwarding, and JALR target generation into the IF PC as the limiting\\npath in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. Seed 2 instead limits on\\nregister-file operand capture. The structural r6s1 forwarding retime,\\nr7s1 multiply bank, and r8s1 synchronous register file all regressed;\\nnone inserted a register between JALR target arithmetic and PC recovery.\\nThe r6s0 alignment precomputation also regressed, so this experiment\\nchanges execution latency rather than replicating alignment logic.\\nA dedicated target register could remove the complete live JALR operand\\ncone from redirect and squash control, at the cost of one extra execute\\ncycle for each JALR. Whether frequency gain exceeds that cost must be\\nmeasured; JALR frequency in the required timed workload is not assumed\\nto be zero.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the JALR target/operand capture registers and one pending owner\\nbit, with launch, wait, and completion control. Advance the held\\nJALR and redirect together using only the registered target; retain\\nsaved RVFI operands and precise misalignment/link suppression.\\nExclude JALR from live jump resolution. Preserve ordinary execution,\\ndivider ownership, direct memory addressing, payload capture policy,\\ndmem holds, and control-zero bubbles during the extra execute cycle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate JALR wait into fetch and ID/EX holding, while preserving\\nload-use, dmem, divider, and redirect priorities. Allow launch to\\ndrain the older EX/MEM owner once and completion to flush a younger\\ninstruction. Keep WB retention tied to actual memory backpressure.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the new EX JALR-wait signal to hazard control. Preserve the\\nexisting redirect interface, pipeline retirement boundary, order\\ncounter, normal-bank memory address, and all nret=1 external ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add direct checks of JALR launch/completion ownership, registered\\ntarget isolation while live operands change, saved RVFI operands,\\nblocked completion, reset cancellation, aligned/misaligned targets,\\nand transitions among JALR, ordinary, divider, and bubble owners.\\nRetain all existing arithmetic, divider, and result-bank assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add full ISS/RVFI comparisons for JALR with RF, EX/MEM, retained WB,\\nload, and divider producers; repeated destination writers; rd=rs1;\\nx0; negative immediates; aligned, bit-0-set, and bit-1-set targets;\\nconsecutive jumps; and JALR targets beginning with DIV/REM. Exercise\\nimem stalls at completion, long older-memory holds, reset while\\npending, and wrong-path loads/stores/divides. Require one ordered\\nretirement and no duplicate memory effects, exactly one added JALR\\nexecute cycle without stalls, unchanged ordinary/divider timing,\\nand the existing liveness bound. Update only internal timing and\\nownership expectations affected by the intentional JALR latency.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 341.76, \"delta_pct\": -16.6, \"vs_baseline\": 20.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4505747, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5605, \"ff\": 2191, \"seeds\": [157.21, 153.99, 146.01], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T12:50:35.832285Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r9s1 (regression, -16.60%): Inserting jalr_target_q after the full forwarding and addition cone shifted a critical path to its input while divider operand enables and EX result selection limited other seeds, reducing reported median Fmax 16.34% despite only 0.32% more 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The accepted r5s1 design reaches 409.80 fitness at 184.06 MHz. Its saved\\nrouted reports identify EX/MEM destination-tag comparison, operand\\nforwarding, and JALR target generation into the IF PC as the limiting\\npath in seeds 1 and 3, at 5.43 and 5.56 ns. Seed 2 instead limits on\\nregister-file operand capture. The structural r6s1 forwarding retime,\\nr7s1 multiply bank, and r8s1 synchronous register file all regressed;\\nnone inserted a register between JALR target arithmetic and PC recovery.\\nThe r6s0 alignment precomputation also regressed, so this experiment\\nchanges execution latency rather than replicating alignment logic.\\nA dedicated target register could remove the complete live JALR operand\\ncone from redirect and squash control, at the cost of one extra execute\\ncycle for each JALR. Whether frequency gain exceeds that cost must be\\nmeasured; JALR frequency in the required timed workload is not assumed\\nto be zero.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Give legal JALR a blocking, two-cycle EX path. Keep ordinary integer,\\nmultiply, load/store, conditional-branch, and direct-JAL execution at\\ntheir existing latency. Use the existing forwarding network and hold\\nthe JALR instruction in ID/EX until completion. This is one structural\\nexperiment in control-transfer execution, with no prediction state.\\n\\nOn the first unstalled EX edge, accept a valid, legal JALR exactly once.\\nCapture (forwarded_rs1 + immediate) modulo 2^32 with bit 0 cleared into\\na dedicated target register, capture the actual forwarded operands for\\nRVFI, and set a pending bit. Never capture a load address as its rs1\\noperand; respect the existing load-use interlock and unavailable-load\\nforwarding qualification. Launch requires the older EX/MEM owner to\\nbe able to advance. It must not depend on target alignment or a live\\ntarget-based redirect. Hold fetch and ID/EX on this launch cycle while\\nallowing older instructions to drain, emitting a control-zero EX/MEM\\nbubble when that register is available.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, form JALR redirect_target and the alignment\\ndecision exclusively from the registered target. On the next available\\nEX/MEM edge, consume the pending operation once, emit its instruction\\nmetadata and PC+4 link, and redirect on that same edge if aligned. With\\nzero bus waits, completion is exactly one clock after launch, adding\\none cycle relative to the current JALR. Hold the target, saved operands,\\nand ID/EX owner if completion is blocked. Reset cancels pending work.\\nDo not allow live forwarding data or the current JALR adder output to\\nbypass the target register into PC, squash, trap, or link-write control.\\n\\nPreserve the current JALR semantics: bit 0 is cleared before alignment\\nchecking, target bit 1 causes the existing misalignment trap, a trapped\\nJALR suppresses its link and redirect and reports pc_next=PC+4, and an\\naligned JALR reports the registered target. A misaligned completion\\nreleases the held sequential fetch normally. Use saved launch operands\\nfor RVFI even after forwarding producers have drained; rd=rs1 and x0\\ncases must still use the pre-link source value. Illegal JALR encodings\\ncontinue through the existing illegal-instruction path without launch.\\n\\nAdd a separate JALR wait input to hazard control. It holds fetch and\\nID/EX without retiring or issuing the held instruction twice. Actual\\ndmem backpressure retains its current priority and preserves the older\\nrequest and WB forwarding payload. At aligned completion, clear the\\nJALR wait condition so redirect can flush the younger instruction and\\noverride an imem stall on the same edge. Restrict the existing live\\njump-resolution path to direct JAL; branch behavior is unchanged.\\nPreserve r5s1's separation between wide payload capture and bubble\\ncontrol, and retain control-zero invalid EX/MEM entries.\\n\\nCrucially, advance JALR into EX/MEM only when its registered redirect\\nis consumed. The r6s2 MEM-stage predictor experiment failed liveness;\\ndo not retire a jump first and redirect one stage later. The proposed\\nschedule delays JALR retirement together with its redirect, retaining\\nthe existing redirect-to-target-DIV/REM retirement spacing. Ver\nNOTES \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split JALR forwarding from target addition with an operand register\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The accepted r5s1 design reports 409.80 fitness at 184.06 MHz. Its\\nrouted critical paths in seeds 1 and 3 start at EX/MEM destination\\ntags, pass through forwarding and JALR target generation, and end at\\nthe IF PC. Structural r6s1, r7s1, and r8s1 regressed after moving the\\nreported limit to ALU selection, forwarding, or register-file reads.\\nThe most recent structural attempt, r9s1, added a JALR target register\\nbut left forwarding and the full addition together BEFORE that new\\nregister. It lost 16.60% fitness despite only 0.32% more cycles in\\nthe evaluator's recorded 2 KB workload. Its saved timing analysis\\nstill contains forwarding-plus-carry paths into jalr_target_q.\\nThat result motivates testing the location of the execution boundary:\\nregister the forwarded source before target arithmetic so neither\\nexecution phase contains the entire original dependency chain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw forwarded JALR operand registers, a pending owner, and\\nlaunch/wait/completion control. Calculate the target exclusively\\nfrom saved rs1 and the held immediate during completion; omit a\\ntarget-result register and remove live JALR operand paths to\\nredirect, alignment, and pc_next. Dispatch and redirect together,\\nretaining saved RVFI operands, trap/link semantics, drain/hold\\nbehavior, and reset cancellation. Preserve ordinary execution,\\ndivider ownership and latency, the direct memory address bank,\\nindependent payload capture, and control-zero bubbles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate JALR wait into fetch and ID/EX holding and flush\\nqualification. Preserve memory-stall priority and WB payload\\nretention, load-use and divider interlocks, and redirect priority.\\nRelease the held instruction exactly at JALR completion, allowing\\nan aligned redirect to override imem backpressure on that edge.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new JALR wait signal between EX and hazard control.\\nRetain the existing pipeline bundles, forwarding sources,\\nretirement boundary, order counter, direct normal-bank memory\\naddressing, and complete nret=1 external interface.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Add raw-operand capture and completion checks, including changing\\nlive forwarded values after launch to prove target isolation.\\nCover saved RVFI operands, source-plus-immediate carry and wrap,\\nbit-0 clearing, bit-1 misalignment, blocked completion, reset,\\nand transitions among JALR, divide, ordinary, and bubble owners.\\nRetain existing real/ALTOPS arithmetic and result-bank assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add full ISS/RVFI comparisons for RF, EX/MEM, retained WB, load,\\nand divide JALR producers; both operands; repeated destination\\nwriters; rd=rs1; x0; negative immediates; aligned and misaligned\\ntargets; consecutive JALRs; and targets beginning with DIV/REM.\\nExercise imem stalls at completion, long older-memory holds,\\nreset while pending, and wrong-path stores and divides. Require\\nunique retirement and memory effects, saved source values, the\\nexisting progress bound, exactly one extra unstalled JALR cycle,\\nand unchanged timing for other instructions. Adapt only internal\\ntiming expectations affected by the intentional JALR latency.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.26, \"delta_pct\": -13.55, \"vs_baseline\": 25.26, \"fmax_mhz\": 159.62, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4505747, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5586, \"ff\": 2160, \"seeds\": [165.21, 159.62, 158.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T13:41:02.192562Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r10s1 (regression, -13.55%): Registering raw forwarded JALR operands before target addition isolated completion from live forwarding, but PC+immediate alignment/squash to ID/EX alu_op and forwarding/load-availability to div_rs2_q CE became critical, lowering median Fmax from 184.06 to 159.62 MHz despite only 0.32% more 6 KB CoreMark cycles; a target-path cut must also account for shared squash and launch controls.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"The accepted r5s1 design reports 409.80 fitness at 184.06 MHz. Its\\nrouted critical paths in seeds 1 and 3 start at EX/MEM destination\\ntags, pass through forwarding and JALR target generation, and end at\\nthe IF PC. Structural r6s1, r7s1, and r8s1 regressed after moving the\\nreported limit to ALU selection, forwarding, or register-file reads.\\nThe most recent structural attempt, r9s1, added a JALR target register\\nbut left forwarding and the full addition together BEFORE that new\\nregister. It lost 16.60% fitness despite only 0.32% more cycles in\\nthe evaluator's recorded 2 KB workload. Its saved timing analysis\\nstill contains forwarding-plus-carry paths into jalr_target_q.\\nThat result motivates testing the location of the execution boundary:\\nregister the forwarded source before target arithmetic so neither\\nexecution phase contains the entire original dependency chain.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Give legal JALR two execution phases separated immediately after\\noperand forwarding. Starting from the current accepted RTL, capture\\nraw forwarded operands in the first phase and calculate the target\\nfrom the saved rs1 in the second. This is one structural change to\\nJALR execution. Keep the existing latency of all other instructions.\\n\\nOn the first unstalled EX edge for a valid legal JALR, capture rs1 and\\nrs2 after the existing forwarding muxes into jalr_rs1_q/jalr_rs2_q\\nand set a pending owner bit. Capture RAW rs1, with no immediate\\naddition or alignment operation on the operand-register D input.\\nRespect load-use interlocking and operand availability; never use a\\npreceding load's effective address as the JALR source. Launch only\\nwhen the older EX/MEM instruction can advance. Hold ID/EX and fetch\\nwhile allowing older instructions to drain, and inject a control-zero\\nEX/MEM bubble when it is available. Launch and payload capture enables\\nmust depend on ownership and backpressure, not target arithmetic or\\ntarget alignment. Preserve the r5s1 payload/control separation.\\n\\nDuring the next cycle compute (jalr_rs1_q + in.imm) modulo 2^32,\\nthen clear bit 0. The immediate, PC, instruction, and controls remain\\nstable in the held ID/EX owner. Drive JALR target, alignment checking,\\nredirect, and pc_next from this saved-operand calculation. There must\\nbe no live forwarded-rs1 bypass into this calculation, including its\\nlow alignment bits. Use the saved operands for eventual RVFI data.\\nDo not add a target-result register: the existing IF PC and EX/MEM\\nregisters capture the completed target and instruction on the same\\ncompletion edge. Reusing the saved RVFI rs1 as the adder input avoids\\nthe separate 32-bit target bank used in r9s1.\\n\\nComplete and consume the pending JALR exactly once on the next\\navailable EX/MEM edge. With no bus waits, this edge is exactly one\\ncycle after operand capture, adding one execute cycle relative to\\nthe accepted core and no further finish or recovery cycle. Redirect\\nand EX/MEM dispatch must occur together, before retirement. An aligned\\ncompletion writes PC+4 as the link and reports the calculated target.\\nA target with bit 1 set retains the current misalignment trap,\\nsuppresses link writing and redirect, and reports pc_next=PC+4.\\nBit 0 alone never causes a JALR alignment trap. Illegal encodings\\nfollow the existing decoder trap path without acquiring ownership.\\n\\nAdd JALR wait to frontend holding and EX/MEM bubble control, with\\nactual dmem backpressure retaining its current priority and held WB\\nforwarding payload. Saved operands and ownership survive a blocked\\ncompletion. On aligned completion, release JALR wait and allow the\\nredirect to flush the younger instruction and override imem stalls.\\nOn misaligned completion, release the sequential instruction normally.\\nReset cancels pending work. Wrong-path or invalid JALRs cannot launch.\\nKeep direct JAL and conditional branches on their current paths;\\nexclude JALR from every live forwarded-operand jump-resolution path.\\n\\nThe proposed timing cut is destination-tag/forwarding to operand D,\\nfollowed by operand Q/target addition to PC and EX/MEM. Verify this\\nseparation in the mapped netlist, including the operand-register\\ne\nNOTES Implemented the JALR raw-operand execution boundary. Correctness checks pass, but this implementation regresses the measured product and should not replace r5s1. The candidate RTL is retained for orchestrator evaluation; no acceptance or evaluator metadata was changed.\n\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/{ex_stage,hazard_unit,core}.sv and the two requested test_*.py files changed, plus this required notes file. The accepted reference was freshly copied from the initially clean worktree and its RTL was checked byte-for-byte against HEAD. Final candidate RTL and tests were checked byte-for-byte against the validated scratch copies.\n\nThe first available legal, valid JALR edge captures raw post-forwarding rs1/rs2 in jalr_rs1_q/jalr_rs2_q and acquires jalr_pending_q. Capture waits for older EX/MEM advancement and for either selected load source to reach WB. Operand D inputs have no addition or bit clearing. The held ID/EX instruction supplies the immediate, PC, instruction, addresses, and controls. The next available edge simultaneously dispatches EX/MEM and redirects IF from (jalr_rs1_q + imm) modulo 2^32 with bit 0 cleared. There is no target-result register, finish state, or recovery cycle.\n\nJALR wait participates in frontend holding and control-zero EX/MEM bubbles. Actual dmem holds keep their existing priority and WB forwarding payload. Pending operands survive a blocked completion; reset cancels them. Alignment and link/trap behavior use the saved-source sum, including its low bits. Bit 1 traps without link writing or redirect, with pc_next=PC+4; bit 0 alone does not trap. JALR is excluded from direct-jump and live branch-comparator resolution. Other instruction latencies, divider ownership/eight-clock response schedule, result banks, normal-bank memory addressing, payload/control separation, nret=1, and all external ports remain intact. Decoder, ALU, divider, WB, and retirement logic are unchanged.\n\nValidation results:\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors. git diff --check: PASS.\n- Full unit suite: 9 pytest runners passed. Final cocotb XMLs contain 150 passing cases and no failures. The initial full run contained 149 cases; the additional retained-load-WB test was then added and the complete 26-case pipeline runner passed again.\n- Direct EX tests run in real arithmetic and ALTOPS modes. They cover raw operands, changed live forwarding after capture, carry/wrap/negative immediate, bit-0 clearing, bit-1 trapping, x0/link destinations, both load-source availability checks, long older holds, blocked completion, reset, illegal/invalid owners, and JALR/divide/ordinary/bubble transitions. Existing arithmetic and result-bank assertions remain.\n- Pipeline tests compare all applicable RVFI fields and accepted memory effects with the unchanged ISS. They cover RF, EX/MEM, WB, retained load WB on either raw source, load/divide producers, repeated writers, rd=rs1, x0, negative immediates, consecutive JALRs, traps, reserved funct3, reset while pending, forced imem unavailability at completion, older memory holds, and wrong-path stores/divides. JALR targets beginning with DIV/REM retain the existing <=10-clock unstalled retirement progress bound. A matched direct-JAL/JALR program proves exactly one additional cycle, with all subsequent target instructions shifted by precisely that cycle.\n- Local formal: PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness_ch0, unique, ordering, PC, register, and instruction checks. No RTL fixes or formal retries were needed. The unchanged run_all.sh auto-selected wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg with their original depths and ALTOPS configuration. Real arithmetic is covered by unit tests and ISS; formal-deep was not run because arithmetic was unchanged.\n- Unchanged Verilator harness selftest with both bus stalls, seeds 1, 17, and 91: byte-exact ISS PASS, 115 retirements each, clean completion and bounded accesses.\n- Fresh synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr script seeds completed successfully for both accepted and candidate RTL. Both used Yosys 0.64 d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61 and the nret=1 FPGA wrapper. The eight synthesis messages about expanding divider arrays also occur in the accepted build; no new synthesis warnings were introduced.\n\nFresh routed Fmax comparison, MHz:\n\n| Seed | Accepted r5s1 | Candidate |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | 165.21 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | 159.62 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | 158.98 |\n| Median | 184.06 | 159.62 |\n\nResource use changes from 5,490 to 5,664 LUT4s and from 2,112 to 2,160 FFs. The mapped candidate contains 64 JALR operand FFs and one pending FF, with no JALR target bank; other synthesis optimizations make the overall FF increase 48.\n\nBoth CoreMark sizes were freshly built with -O3 and ITERATIONS=10 from unchanged benchmark sources. Each accepted/candidate pair executes the identical ELF with the unchanged --istall --dstall harness schedule, default seed 0xDEADBEEF. Timing uses only bench_stop_cycle minus bench_start_cycle, never total elapsed cycles. Separate streaming runs of the unchanged full trace count retired JALRs inside the same physical marker interval; final cycle and retirement counts match the bench-mode runs.\n\n| Workload | Accepted marker cycles | Candidate marker cycles | Timed JALRs, each build | Cycle increase | Accepted product | Candidate product | Product change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Required 6 KB validation | 32,836,671 | 32,941,244 | 104,164 | 0.318464% | 56.053185 | 48.455972 | -13.553580% |\n| Existing 2 KB evaluator | 4,491,485 | 4,505,747 | 13,735 | 0.317534% | 409.797650 | 354.258683 | -13.552778% |\n\nProduct = median routed MHz * 1e6 * 10 / marker cycles. The independently measured 6 KB cost is not inferred from the 2 KB result. The forecast +3% fitness improvement was not achieved.\n\nBoth 6 KB runs reached EBREAK with oob=false and bench_bracketed=true, and reported seedcrc=0x8a02, crclist=0xd4b0, crcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal \"Correct operation validated.\" The UART identifies the 6k performance parameters. Its CoreMark Size=2000 is the per-algorithm partition of TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000. Its compiler-flags text is hardcoded to -O2 in the untouched port; the saved actual compiler command uses -O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000. The 2 KB runs separately passed the unchanged evaluator's five expected CRCs. Neither evaluator nor benchmark files were changed to reconcile the contract's size discrepancy.\n\nMapped-netlist evidence and remaining timing concerns:\n\n- An unconstrained combinational fan-in traversal stopping at FF outputs found no ALU/carry or multiplier cells in either the operand/pending D cones or their CE cones. Capture CE roots exclude source data, immediate, PC, and saved operand bits. They depend on controls, source/destination tags, pending ownership, reset/backpressure, and load availability.\n- Truth-table cofactoring of the mapped LUT/MUX/ALU graph for a legal pending JALR with reset=stall=0 verified that actual IF-PC D, EX/MEM pc_next D, trap/link-control D, and ID-valid squash D depend only on saved rs1 and held metadata/current PC as applicable. No live forwarded data or forwarding tags remain in those completion cones. Trap/link and squash each reduce to the four saved-source/immediate low-bit roots. Saved RVFI operand values are additionally checked by the direct EX and full ISS tests.\n- Accepted seeds 1 and 3 start at EX/MEM destination tags and end at \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Remove launch qualification from idle divider operand storage\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Divider launch qualification still controls wide operand storage in the\\ncurrent winner. In ex_stage.sv, div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q capture only on\\ndiv_req_valid && div_req_ready; in div_unit.sv, the same handshake gates\\ninitialization of quotient/remainder, divisor, dividend and sign metadata.\\nThis connects forwarding-tag comparisons and load-availability decisions\\nto many register enables and data-selection cones. The r7s0 implementation\\nnotes report forwarding-to-quotient_q critical paths in two seeds, and\\nr10s1 reports a forwarding/load-availability path to div_rs2_q CE. These\\nare observed bottlenecks in rejected candidates, not proof that they are\\nthe current winner's worst paths.\\n\\nThe accepted r5s1 change improved fitness 7.14% by separating pipeline\\npayload capture from transaction control. Apply that principle narrowly\\nto the remaining divider operand banks. This avoids repeating the failed\\ndivider arithmetic rewrites, ALU recoding, and JALR timing experiments.\\nNo instruction should gain or lose a cycle; any fitness gain must come\\nfrom a higher three-seed median Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Split protocol/result state from arithmetic operand storage. Capture\\nprefix Q/R, normalized divisor, raw dividend and flags on !busy_q;\\nadvance Q/R on busy_q && !final_cycle and hold on final_cycle. Apply\\nthe same idle-capture ownership rule to alt_result_q. Preserve every\\narithmetic expression, reset value, request/response transition and\\nresult_q write condition, including held-response refill behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Give div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q a separate synchronous-reset process with\\ncapture enable !div_active_q and the existing forwarded rs1/rs2 D\\ninputs. Leave div_active_q and all request/accept/wait equations\\nunchanged. Retain snapshots for the complete active transaction and\\npreserve EX/MEM metadata selection, stalls and result-bank ownership.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all arithmetic, prefix-state, exact-latency, reset and held-bank\\nchecks. Add transactions preceded by changing idle operands/opcodes\\nwith req_valid low, followed by different operands on the actual\\nrequest edge. Exercise changed live inputs while busy, idle captures\\nbeneath an unconsumed response, consume/refill, and reset at request,\\nseventh-recurrence and terminal edges in real and ALTOPS modes. Check\\nthat no idle capture produces a response or changes result_q.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ownership tests with changing idle forwarded values, delayed\\nlaunch from load unavailability and memory holds, then distinct\\naccepted operands from each forwarding source. Check exact RVFI\\nsnapshots through busy, blocked completion and acceptance, including\\nan EX/MEM divide owner held after div_active_q clears while idle\\nsnapshots change. Preserve all existing result and direct-address\\nassertions in both arithmetic modes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed streams with load/divide producers, competing\\nforwarding writers, retained WB operands, back-to-back DIV/REM and\\nimmediate consumers. Exercise bus stalls, redirects and reset around\\nlaunch/completion. Compare full RVFI, accepted memory effects and\\nretirement-cycle sequences with the unchanged ISS and starting RTL;\\nrequire unchanged ordinary and divide spacing and no extra request\\nor retirement from idle operand capture. Keep all existing checks.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 411.53, \"delta_pct\": 0.42, \"vs_baseline\": 45.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.84, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5485, \"ff\": 2119, \"seeds\": [190.33, 184.84, 168.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T14:20:27.333901Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"Divider launch qualification still controls wide operand storage in the\\ncurrent winner. In ex_stage.sv, div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q capture only on\\ndiv_req_valid && div_req_ready; in div_unit.sv, the same handshake gates\\ninitialization of quotient/remainder, divisor, dividend and sign metadata.\\nThis connects forwarding-tag comparisons and load-availability decisions\\nto many register enables and data-selection cones. The r7s0 implementation\\nnotes report forwarding-to-quotient_q critical paths in two seeds, and\\nr10s1 reports a forwarding/load-availability path to div_rs2_q CE. These\\nare observed bottlenecks in rejected candidates, not proof that they are\\nthe current winner's worst paths.\\n\\nThe accepted r5s1 change improved fitness 7.14% by separating pipeline\\npayload capture from transaction control. Apply that principle narrowly\\nto the remaining divider operand banks. This avoids repeating the failed\\ndivider arithmetic rewrites, ALU recoding, and JALR timing experiments.\\nNo instruction should gain or lose a cycle; any fitness gain must come\\nfrom a higher three-seed median Fmax.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Separate divider control ownership from idle operand capture, without\\nadding registers, pipeline stages, or an execution shortcut. The sole\\noptimization is to remove request qualification from operand-storage\\nenables and source selection. An unowned operand bank may change freely;\\nthe actual request edge must still capture that edge's exact operands.\\n\\nIn div_unit.sv, retain the existing equations and sequential transitions\\nfor req_ready, resp_valid, busy_q, count_q, held_q and result_q. Put the\\narithmetic storage in disjoint clocked processes with synchronous reset.\\nOn every edge whose pre-edge busy_q is zero, capture the existing magnitude\\nprefix into quotient_q/remainder_q and capture divisor_q, dividend_q and\\nall four operation/sign flags, regardless of req_valid or held_q. While\\nbusy_q is one, retain those operand/flag registers; update quotient_q and\\nremainder_q only on the existing seven !final_cycle recurrence edges.\\nHold Q/R on the eighth, sign-correction edge. In ALTOPS mode, capture the\\nunchanged selected subtraction/XOR formula into alt_result_q on each idle\\nedge and retain it while busy. Give every register exactly one owner.\\n\\nA request can only be accepted while busy_q is zero, so the same edge\\ninitializes its operands and sets busy_q. Idle captures never start an\\noperation or advance the count. A held response remains in result_q and\\ndoes not depend on the now-unowned arithmetic bank; consequently idle\\ncaptures are also safe while held_q is one. Preserve simultaneous held\\nresponse consumption and next-request acceptance, and leave result_q\\nunchanged except for reset and the existing terminal capture.\\n\\nIn ex_stage.sv, retain div_active_q's exact launch/accept state machine\\nbut split its RVFI operand snapshots into a separate process. After reset,\\ncapture forwarded rs1/rs2 on every edge with !div_active_q, without using\\nopcode, load availability, request/ready, stall, or valid as capture\\nqualifiers. Once active, retain both snapshots through computation,\\nblocked response, and the acceptance edge. EX/MEM samples the old snapshots\\non that edge before ownership clears. Later idle snapshot updates cannot\\nalter an already-captured EX/MEM owner or the divider's result bank.\\n\\nPreserve all real arithmetic, corner cases, eight-clock response timing,\\nnine-clock consecutive-divide spacing, request qualification, load-use\\nprotection, forwarding priority, zero-control bubbles, memory holds,\\ntraps, and nret=1 RVFI behavior. Keep the normal registered memory-address\\nbank and multiplier unchanged. Extra idle switching is a tradeoff, and\\nbusy_q fanout or unrelated routing may erase the predicted timing gain.\\n\\nValidate exact accepted operands, results and retirement cycles against\\nthe starting winner under identical programs and bus schedules. Retain\\nthe existing lint, real/ALTOPS unit, formal and stalled cosim gates and\\ncanonical 6 KB CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. Compare the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA fitness using identical binaries and unrounded\\niterations/cycle;\nNOTES # Idle divider operand storage\n\nImplemented the hypothesis against starting winner `8357476ebbe4f81ec39bae4793d68a4eef9d0eac` (`hyp-20260906-002-r5s1`). Local correctness gates pass. The unchanged three-seed evaluator improves median Fmax from 184.06 to 184.84 MHz, or **0.423774856%**, with identical execution cycles.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv`: separated protocol/result ownership from the arithmetic storage, with synchronous reset and exactly one clocked owner per register. On pre-edge `!busy_q`, Q/R capture the existing four-bit magnitude prefix, and divisor, dividend, and all four flags capture current inputs regardless of request validity or a held response. Q/R advance on the existing seven recurrence edges and hold on the eighth correction edge. ALTOPS captures the unchanged selected subtraction/XOR expression on every idle edge and holds while busy.\n- `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`: retained the launch/accept state machine verbatim; moved RVFI snapshots to their own synchronous-reset process enabled only by `!div_active_q`. Snapshots retain the accepted operands through blocked completion and the acceptance edge, when EX/MEM samples their old values.\n- No arithmetic expressions, state declarations, pipeline stages, multiplier, normal memory-address bank, request qualification, forwarding priority, result-bank write conditions, or top-level ports changed. `nret` remains 1. Idle captures do not claim ownership, advance count, or produce responses.\n\nNo architectural deviation was required. The old ALU test assertion that arithmetic Q/R remained owned after completion was replaced with an assertion of the requested idle prefix capture. All response/result-bank stability assertions and all arithmetic, prefix, latency, reset, bubble, direct-address, and retirement checks remain.\n\n## Tests and equivalence\n\n- Requested Verilator lint passes with no warnings/errors, in real and ALTOPS modes.\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, using unchanged single-retirement wrapper/config and ALTOPS. Environment: `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`, with the installed conda Python on PATH. The first configured proof run passed; no RTL formal fixes or retries were needed. The initial system-Python setup could not run the gate, so validation used the existing conda installation. Formal was staged under `$TMPDIR` to leave contract files untouched. Real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run; arithmetic expressions are unchanged, with real unit, ISS, and full-trace coverage below.\n- All nine pytest runners pass: real ALU 34 cocotb tests, ALTOPS ALU 34, real EX 5, ALTOPS EX 5, decoder 30, ID 1, immediate generator 8, pipeline 24, regfile 5; **146 cocotb tests total**.\n- Added changing idle operands/opcodes, exact different request-edge operands, busy input/request poisoning, captures beneath blocked responses, simultaneous consume/refill, and request/seventh-recurrence/terminal reset checks. EX tests cover all nine forwarding-source combinations, load-unavailable launch delays, memory holds, active snapshot retention, and a held EX/MEM divide owner surviving changing idle snapshots after ownership clears.\n- Added mixed pipeline streams with competing writers, all operand forwarding sources, retained WB load data, dependent and consecutive DIV/REM, immediate consumers, bus waits, redirects, and reset at request, recurrence, terminal, and completed-owner boundaries. Coverage assertions require the intended forwarding and overlap events. Initial test-only assertions were corrected on the starting RTL to include deliberate bus-hold cycles and avoid asking an aborted pre-launch scenario to consume a later hold schedule.\n- The identical protocol suite runs on the starting and candidate RTL. Optional `PIPELINE_TRACE_MODE=record` / `compare`, sharing `PIPELINE_TRACE_DIR`, records complete valid RVFI, bus schedules and accepted effects, accepted divide operands, dispatch results, resets, and cycle numbers. **34 scenarios, 28,706 sampled edges/events, 2,450 launches, and 5,814 retirements match exactly.** Each scenario also passes the unchanged Python ISS comparison. Existing one-clock ordinary/consumer and nine-clock consecutive-divide spacing checks pass; launch-to-accept remains eight clocks.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim.run_cosim` passes on both versions, including selftest and the evaluator's CoreMark configuration. Selftest additionally passes the unchanged ISS with both buses stalled using seeds 1, 2, 3, 19, and 97; all 115 retirements and their cycles match the starting RTL for each schedule.\n- The complete canonical 6 KB stalled CoreMark RVFI streams were compared byte for byte: **22,093,920 retirements and 6,463,986,468 bytes**, including every emitted retirement cycle and operand/result/memory field. No difference. Shared stream SHA256: `34ef03d244b986a66be40660efea1b01b6705ecbf1263bf493d109b7184ac4a0`.\n\n## FPGA evaluation and working-set discrepancy\n\nBoth versions used unchanged synthesis, wrapper, constraints, nextpnr script, seeds 1/2/3, and evaluator. All six routes completed. `make fpga TARGET=bench` used `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`. The starting RTL reproduces the winner's recorded median and resource counts.\n\n| Metric | Starting | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 190.33 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 191.13 | 184.84 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 179.89 | 168.69 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.06 | 184.84 |\n| Evaluator bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Exact evaluator iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,491,485 | 10 / 4,491,485 |\n| Evaluator fitness, iterations/s | 409.7976504430049 | 411.5342698461644 |\n| LUT4 | 5,490 | 5,485 |\n| Mapped FF | 2,112 | 2,119 |\n\nThe evaluator still builds **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000**, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and validates its existing 2 KB CRC table. This is a pre-existing discrepancy from the requested 6 KB contract; no evaluator or benchmark source was changed. Its unrounded iteration rate is `2.2264351322558128e-6`, not the rounded JSON field `2e-6`. Both versions ran the identical ELF, SHA256 `9b7d11c69a8a2b0db598fa2156474184488a3b2d22017f3fac05e6113ec74133`.\n\nSeparately built and validated the requested **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10** configuration from unchanged sources, using the same ELF for both versions (SHA256 `f224290c528bdbc3caa7badb285d6b6ae1fab75523bde60110cefe80de23a9e4`). Both runs have:\n\n- Start marker cycle 56,393; stop marker cycle 32,893,064; **32,836,671 bracketed cycles**; final EBREAK cycle 32,900,232; no OOB access.\n- Exact iteration rate `10 / 32836671 = 3.0453757020618807e-7`.\n- CRCs `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, `seedcrc=0x8a02`, `crcfinal=0x5949`, and the literal `Correct operation validated.` UART message. UART and completion records match exactly.\n- Using the same routed medians gives separate 6 KB fitness **56.053185172150975 -> 56.290724476911805 iterations/s**. These numbers are not substituted into the unchanged evaluator's output.\n\nThe unchanged UART metadata reports `CoreMark Size: 2000` for the 6 KB run's per-algorithm share, and a hardcoded `-O2` compiler string. The actual build command used `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`; the canonical 6 KB CRCs identify the configuration.\n\n## Mapped D/CE cones\n\nInspected both mapped `synth.json` netlists, stopping combinational traversal at registered sources. Candidate enables were also evaluated over all busy/active/count combinations (320 mapped CE truth-table\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch from backend interlocks with a two-entry instruction queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The accepted r5s1 structural change achieved 184.06 MHz by separating\\npipeline payload storage from squash and completion control. Later\\nforwarding, register-file, multiply-bank, and two-phase JALR changes\\nregressed routed fitness as other paths became critical. The bench\\nexperiment log contains no previous fetch-queue attempt.\\n\\nThe current IF/ID interface is combinational. hazard_unit freezes the\\nfetch PC for load-use, data-memory, and divider interlocks, while raw\\nio_imemReady still determines instruction availability on every cycle.\\nAn instruction delivered during a backend hold is not retained, so a\\nsubsequent instruction-bus stall can delay issue after that hold ends.\\nA small queue can retain these deliveries and fetch further sequential\\ninstructions while decode waits, overlapping independent stall causes.\\nThis targets iterations per cycle without relying on another increase\\nin the already sensitive execution-path Fmax.\\n\", \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement two fixed FIFO slots with PC/instruction payloads,\\noccupancy, empty bypass, explicit fetch acceptance and decode\\nconsumption, and simultaneous full pop/push. Keep the fetch address\\ndirectly registered. Handle redirect/reset cancellation and the\\nFENCE.I fetch barrier, separating payload capture from ownership\\ninvalidation. Expose head availability independently of flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire raw imem readiness into fetch and effective head availability\\ninto hazard control. Derive decode_take from actual ID/EX capture,\\nroute the selected head consistently to ID and hazard comparisons,\\nand release the fetch fence only when its valid EX owner advances\\npast older memory work. Preserve the existing EX redirect, pipeline\\nbundles, normal-bank data-memory address, retirement, and all ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make frontend availability refer to the selected instruction head,\\nso raw instruction-bus backpressure cannot suppress queued work.\\nApply frontend interlocks to head consumption while fetch capacity\\nindependently controls PC advance. Preserve load-use bubbles,\\nmemory-stall priority, divider holding, redirect flushing, and\\nretained MEM/WB forwarding payloads without combinational loops.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add ISS/RVFI-checked directed programs for empty bypass, both FIFO\\noccupancies, full pop/push, capacity backpressure, long bus stalls,\\nqueue filling during load-use and divider holds, and buffered issue\\nwhile imemReady is low. Test redirects with empty/full queues and\\nunready buses, held redirects, reset, and discarded stores/divides.\\nVerify PC/instruction pairing, source values, exact-once retirement\\nand memory effects, and sustained one-per-cycle zero-wait issue.\\nInclude FENCE.I after a code store with delayed dmem acceptance,\\nqueued and bypassed fences, and wrong-path fence cancellation.\\nUse deterministic stall schedules to demonstrate actual overlap\\nsavings; preserve all existing architectural and arithmetic checks.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 311.94, \"delta_pct\": -23.88, \"vs_baseline\": 10.3, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.43, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4341607, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5615, \"ff\": 2223, \"seeds\": [135.43, 139.53, 128.6], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T14:22:14.877964Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r11s1 (regression, -23.88%): A two-entry fall-through FIFO in if_stage.sv saved 3.08% of stalled 6 KB CoreMark cycles, but its live-head mux through register-file reads and load-use/decode_take-to-occupancy paths cut median routed Fmax from 184.06 to 135.43 MHz, showing that empty bypass can put queue selection and consumption control on the critical path.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': 'The accepted r5s1 structural change achieved 184.06 MHz by separating\\npipeline payload storage from squash and completion control. Later\\nforwarding, register-file, multiply-bank, and two-phase JALR changes\\nregressed routed fitness as other paths became critical. The bench\\nexperiment log contains no previous fetch-queue attempt.\\n\\nThe current IF/ID interface is combinational. hazard_unit freezes the\\nfetch PC for load-use, data-memory, and divider interlocks, while raw\\nio_imemReady still determines instruction availability on every cycle.\\nAn instruction delivered during a backend hold is not retained, so a\\nsubsequent instruction-bus stall can delay issue after that hold ends.\\nA small queue can retain these deliveries and fetch further sequential\\ninstructions while decode waits, overlapping independent stall causes.\\nThis targets iterations per cycle without relying on another increase\\nin the already sensitive execution-path Fmax.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Insert a two-entry, fall-through FIFO of {pc, instr} inside if_stage.\\nDecouple sequential fetch acceptance from consumption by ID/EX. This\\nqueue is the sole architectural experiment; retain the existing issue\\nwidth, execution stages, forwarding, branch policy, and arithmetic.\\n\\nKeep a dedicated registered fetch PC driving io_imemAddr directly.\\nPresent the oldest buffered entry to decode when the FIFO is nonempty;\\notherwise bypass the current {fetch_pc, io_imemData}. An empty queue\\ntherefore adds no mandatory fetch stage or branch-target refill cycle.\\nHead availability is registered occupancy OR a usable current bus\\nresponse. It must not depend on decode consumption or flush outputs.\\nBuffered head data remains available even when io_imemReady is zero,\\nbecause that entry was captured on an earlier ready cycle. Keep the\\nexisting invalid-instruction NOP/control-zero discipline.\\n\\nDefine decode_take as one actual, valid ID/EX capture, excluding reset,\\nredirect, load-use bubbles, data-memory holds, and divider holds. A\\nnormal-cycle fetch acceptance requires io_imemReady and either free\\nFIFO capacity or a simultaneous decode_take. Increment fetch_pc by\\nfour only on this acceptance. If empty and decode takes the live word,\\nadvance the fetch PC without storing a duplicate. Otherwise enqueue\\nthe accepted word with its original PC. With queued work, support\\nsimultaneous head consumption and tail insertion, including when full.\\nA full queue with no consumption holds the fetch PC and accepts nothing.\\nUse two fixed storage slots and a small occupancy state; preserve order\\nand prevent overflow, underflow, loss, and replay through every case.\\n\\nFeed effective head availability to the hazard logic in place of raw\\nimem readiness. Existing backend interlocks block head consumption;\\nthey must not prevent independent filling when capacity remains. Decode,\\nraw-source load-use comparison, immediate generation, and RVFI PC data\\nmust all refer to the same selected head. Do not introduce a readiness\\nloop through flush_if, a fall-through valid signal, or decode_take.\\n\\nReset and the existing EX redirect have priority over all queue events.\\nA redirect discards every queued wrong-path entry and the same-cycle\\nbus response, clears fetch serialization state, and sets fetch_pc to\\nthe resolved target even when the bus is unready or the FIFO is full.\\nRepeated redirects from a memory-held EX owner remain harmless. Preserve\\nthe direct redirect-target PC mux path; queue occupancy must not qualify\\nthe existing redirect or enter target arithmetic. Preserve the r5s1\\nseparation of payload storage from validity/squash control: invalidate\\nqueue ownership without adding redirect reset logic to every data bit.\\n\\nAccount for instruction-fetch serialization introduced by buffering.\\nOn acceptance of a FENCE.I word, stop accepting younger fetch words\\nuntil that same legal instruction advances out of EX after older memory\\nwork can advance. Older queued entries and the fence itself still drain.\\nSince nothing younger is accepted across this barrier, no buffered stale\\ninstruction survives it. Generate the release from existing valid EX\\nmetadata and its actual a\nNOTES # Two-entry fetch queue (r11s1)\n\nImplemented the queue and fetch serialization. Correctness checks pass, but this implementation regresses measured fitness: the required 6 KB comparison drops from 56.05 to 42.55 iterations/second (-24.08%). Its 3.08% cycle saving does not offset the routed Fmax loss. This is a completed implementation experiment, not an acceptance claim.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `if_stage.sv` now holds two fixed PC/instruction slots, a two-bit occupancy, a one-bit head pointer, and a FENCE.I barrier bit. Empty bypass adds no fetch stage. Full simultaneous pop/push replaces the consumed slot and preserves order. Fetch acceptance independently fills free capacity during load-use, memory, and divider holds.\n- The fetch address remains the direct output of a dedicated registered PC. Reset and EX redirect override acceptance and capacity, discard the same-cycle response, and clear queue/barrier ownership. Slot payloads have write enables without reset or redirect data muxes. Repeated redirects from a memory-held EX instruction are harmless.\n- `core.sv` derives consumption from the actual valid ID/EX capture conditions. Hazard readiness uses selected-head availability, which depends only on occupancy, instruction-bus readiness, and the registered barrier. Availability has no dependency on consumption or squash outputs. Decode, immediate generation, raw-source hazard comparisons, and RVFI PC all consume the same head.\n- FENCE.I acceptance blocks younger fetch acceptance until valid, legal EX metadata advances with older memory able to advance. Release reuses existing instruction metadata and advancement controls. Redirect/reset cancel the barrier. The decoder and its legality policy are unchanged; fences retain their no-write retirement behavior.\n- Removed the unused internal `stall_if` output because fetch capacity now controls sequential PC advancement. Updated the pipeline bundle comment. Execution stages, forwarding, arithmetic, divider real/ALTOPS schedule, direct data-memory address bank, retirement channel, `nret: 1`, and all external ports are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe accepted RTL was copied before any edits. Builds, immutable copies of the formal harness, ELFs, and logs live under `$TMPDIR/fetch-queue-r11s1/`; no benchmark, evaluator, formal contract, FPGA wrapper, constraints, or other core was edited.\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, zero warnings.\n- Existing unit suite: accepted 9 pytest runners / 140 cocotb cases PASS; candidate 9 runners / 145 cases PASS. Candidate includes 27 pipeline cases. The final pipeline rerun after adding an exact marker-window assertion passed all 27 cases.\n- Local formal: PASS, 53/53 checks on both accepted and candidate RTL, including `liveness`, `unique`, illegal-instruction, PC, and register checks. Candidate passed its first formal run; zero RTL formal fix retries. Ran unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` in isolated scratch copies with `CORE_NAME=bench RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl JOBS=6` and the existing Miniconda Python on PATH (the default Python lacks the required dependencies). Automatic selection retained `wrapper_si.sv`, `checks_si.cfg`, and their original depths/ALTOPS defines.\n- Unchanged Verilator harness / Python ISS selftest cosimulation: PASS on both cores for both-bus stall seeds `0xDEADBEEF`, 1, 42, and 99, 115 exact retirements each. Directed queue programs also compare all meaningful RVFI fields against the unchanged ISS and check accepted memory transactions and divider launches independently.\n- New edge-by-edge queue scoreboard checks occupancy, head PC/instruction pairing, NOP validity discipline, acceptance, consumption, fetch-PC advance/hold, fence ownership/release, and reset/redirect cancellation. Directed cases cover empty bypass, both occupancies, full pop/push, capacity backpressure, long instruction stalls, filling during all backend hold types, buffered unready issue, full/empty and repeated unready redirects, discarded stores/divides, reset with a full queue and pending fence, queued/bypassed FENCE.I, consecutive fences, and delayed self-modifying-code stores. Existing zero-wait integer/multiply and divide timing checks remain intact.\n- Deterministic two-bus stall program, identical on both cores: start marker cycle 3; accepted stop 35, candidate stop 33. Marker windows are 32 versus 30 cycles (6.25% saving). Candidate regression test requires the measured 30-cycle window.\n\n## Fresh performance comparison\n\nIdentical ELFs: `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000` and separately 2000, `ITERATIONS=10`, actual compiler flag `-O3`, RV32IM/ILP32. Both buses use the unchanged harness's approximately 22% random stalls. Only stop-minus-start MMIO marker cycles count.\n\n| Routed seed | Accepted MHz | Queue MHz |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 184.06 | 135.43 |\n| 2 | 191.13 | 139.53 |\n| 3 | 179.89 | 128.60 |\n| Median | 184.06 | 135.43 |\n\nAll six canonical routes finished normally. These are final routed frequencies, not placement estimates. LUT4 usage is 5490 -> 5615; FF usage 2112 -> 2223; BSRAM usage remains four blocks.\n\n| Working set | Stall seed | Accepted marker cycles | Queue marker cycles | Saving |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 6 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 32,836,671 | 31,824,980 | 3.0810% |\n| 6 KB | 1 | 32,838,499 | 31,826,861 | 3.0806% |\n| 6 KB | 42 | 32,840,423 | 31,828,643 | 3.0809% |\n| 2 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 4,491,485 | 4,341,607 | 3.3369% |\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,490,174 | 4,339,252 | 3.3612% |\n| 2 KB | 42 | 4,493,775 | 4,343,014 | 3.3549% |\n\nRequired 6 KB fitness, using median routed Fmax and the default stall seed: `184.06e6 * 10 / 32836671 = 56.0532` versus `135.43e6 * 10 / 31824980 = 42.5546` iterations/second. The other two stall seeds show the same approximately 24.08% regression.\n\nThe existing 2 KB evaluator is reported separately: fresh accepted 409.80 versus candidate 311.94 (-23.88%) with its default stall seed. This reproduces the historical 409.80 result; it is not the required 6 KB score. The evaluator and both benchmark source configurations were not modified.\n\nAll twelve CoreMark runs completed with `ebreak:true`, `oob:false`, valid marker brackets, ten iterations, and the exact UART literal `Correct operation validated.`. Every 6 KB run has canonical `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, plus `seedcrc=0x8a02` and `crcfinal=0x5949`. Every 2 KB run passes the unchanged `validate_coremark_uart` evaluator. The port's printed compiler-flags string remains the existing hardcoded `-O2`; the captured build command actually used `-O3`. The 6 KB UART's `CoreMark Size: 2000` is the per-algorithm size and accompanies its `6k performance run parameters` banner.\n\nELF SHA-256 fingerprints:\n\n- 6 KB: `9c360d3fc8064e4ffaadfa2b7c4da1ec64e39c4ab0605c6e95951c87e626dac5`\n- 2 KB: `486c8f9034415384abe02106785d5b9484e1a9e0850d91f575515bf15b79c404`\n\n## Timing and remaining concerns\n\nReviewed all three canonical routed reports. Accepted seeds 1/3 are limited by forwarding through JALR target arithmetic to the fetch-PC register (5.43/5.56 ns); accepted seed 2 by the live instruction-to-ID/register-file path (5.23 ns). Candidate seeds 1/3 are limited by live instruction selection through register-file read logic into ID payload (7.38/7.78 ns); candidate seed 2 by live instruction/load-use/consumption logic into queue occupancy (7.17 ns). The queue therefore exposes exactly the frontend mux and full/pop-control risks identified in the hypothesis. Backend arithmetic and forwarding source files r\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Factor multiply signedness into one product and one result selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner's r11s0 timing reports put ID/EX alu_op[3] to\\nEX/MEM normal result bit 23 on the worst path in seeds 1 and 3.\\nInspection of the saved mapped netlist shows the path entering a\\nMUL decode, combining the shared product's low and high halves with\\nother ALU results, and traversing two MUX2_LUT5 cells before the\\nnormal result register. The RTL describes three signedness variants\\nof multiplication and four independently selected multiply results.\\nYosys already maps them to ONE MULT36X36, so there is no claim that\\nthis proposal removes redundant DSPs. The opportunity is to simplify\\nmultiply control and its contribution to the result-selection cone.\\n\\nThe micro_opt history warns against a broad selector rewrite:\\nr2s0's registered one-hot ALU tree duplicated the multiplier and lost\\n5.98% fitness, while r7s0's opcode recoding lost 17.88%. The separate\\nmultiplier register bank in structural attempt r7s1 also regressed.\\nThis experiment instead keeps the existing encoded controls and\\nregister boundaries and changes only the combinational multiply cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement explicit signed 33-bit operand extension and one signed\\n66-bit real product for the four multiply operations. Collapse their\\nreal-arithmetic output cases into a single grouped arm with one\\nlow/high-half choice. Retain exact ALTOPS formulas, all nonmultiply\\narithmetic and default behavior, the module interface, and every\\ndivider connection. Handle unused product bits with the existing\\nnarrow lint-suppression convention. Check inferred DSP sharing and\\nthe actual mapped result cone instead of assuming source factoring\\nsurvives synthesis.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all existing assertions. Extend real multiply checks with\\ncross-products of zero, one, minus one, INT_MIN, INT_MAX, unsigned\\nmaximum, and values around the 16-bit and 31-bit boundaries, plus\\ndeterministic random full-width pairs. Use independent Python signed\\nand unsigned products to check the low/high halves for all four ops.\\nExercise rapid transitions between multiply signedness variants,\\ninteger operations and unsupported controls; retain exact ALTOPS\\nexpectations and all divider latency and held-result checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add short mixed programs exercising every multiply variant with\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB operand forwarding, load-fed operands, dependent\\nconsumers, repeated destinations and writes to x0. Verify exact\\nresults and RVFI source values against the unchanged ISS, unchanged\\nretirement spacing under zero waits, and correct ownership under\\ndeterministic imem/dmem holds and adjacent DIV/REM instructions.\\nRetain strict retirement order, PC continuity and existing assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 393.79, \"delta_pct\": -4.31, \"vs_baseline\": 39.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 176.87, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5489, \"ff\": 2095, \"seeds\": [172.47, 177.65, 176.87], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T16:11:09.600635Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r12s0 (regression, -4.31%): Explicit signed 33x33 multiply sharing in alu.sv kept one MULT36X36 and removed a result-path MUX2_LUT5 but left five combinational cells, while EX/MEM rd-to-JALR/PC forwarding limited all three seeds; reducing multiply mux count did not improve routed timing with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"The current winner's r11s0 timing reports put ID/EX alu_op[3] to\\nEX/MEM normal result bit 23 on the worst path in seeds 1 and 3.\\nInspection of the saved mapped netlist shows the path entering a\\nMUL decode, combining the shared product's low and high halves with\\nother ALU results, and traversing two MUX2_LUT5 cells before the\\nnormal result register. The RTL describes three signedness variants\\nof multiplication and four independently selected multiply results.\\nYosys already maps them to ONE MULT36X36, so there is no claim that\\nthis proposal removes redundant DSPs. The opportunity is to simplify\\nmultiply control and its contribution to the result-selection cone.\\n\\nThe micro_opt history warns against a broad selector rewrite:\\nr2s0's registered one-hot ALU tree duplicated the multiplier and lost\\n5.98% fitness, while r7s0's opcode recoding lost 17.88%. The separate\\nmultiplier register bank in structural attempt r7s1 also regressed.\\nThis experiment instead keeps the existing encoded controls and\\nregister boundaries and changes only the combinational multiply cone.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Replace the three real multiply expressions with one explicitly shared\\nsigned 33-by-33-bit multiplication. Form signed_a only for MULH and\\nMULHSU, and signed_b only for MULH. Construct signed 33-bit operands\\nAext = {signed_a && a[31], a} and\\nBext = {signed_b && b[31], b}, then compute one signed 66-bit product\\nP = Aext * Bext with explicit SystemVerilog widths and signedness.\\nA zero extension represents every unsigned RV32 operand as a positive\\nsigned 33-bit value; a sign extension preserves its signed value.\\nThus P[31:0] supplies MUL and P[63:32] supplies MULH, MULHU and MULHSU.\\nBits 65:64 are unused; do not accidentally select P[65:34].\\n\\nIn the real-arithmetic ALU case, group all four multiply opcodes into\\none result arm, selecting P[31:0] for MUL and P[63:32] otherwise.\\nResolve the high-half signedness entirely at the product inputs:\\nthere should be no post-product selection between three separately\\nnamed signedness results. Leave the existing nonmultiply case arms\\nand default-zero behavior intact. Keep the four exact existing\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS add/subtract-XOR formulas in their guarded branch;\\nordinary ALTOPS formal alone cannot establish real-product correctness.\\n\\nThe intended mechanism is fewer distinct multiply terms in the normal\\nALU result selection, with sharing explicit before synthesis instead\\nof dependent on mutually exclusive consumers. No register or pipeline\\nstage is added. Preserve single-cycle multiply execution, forwarding,\\njump-link priority, direct registered memory addressing, all divider\\nrequest/response and result-bank behavior, and the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\nDo not recode ALU operations or add whole-ALU one-hot selection.\\n\\nPredict unchanged marker-window cycles and a modest Fmax gain. This\\nis unmeasured and low confidence: synthesis may reconstruct the old\\nmux topology, input signedness may become critical, or placement may\\nworsen other paths. Check that the mapped design still has exactly\\none MULT36X36 and four memory block RAMs, and inspect connectivity\\nfrom both opcode and forwarded operand sources to the product and\\nEX/MEM result across all three routed seeds. Absence of a modeled DSP\\ntiming arc must not be presented as proof of timing margin.\\n\\nValidate all four real multiply operations against independent integer\\nreferences, then run unchanged lint, unit, formal and ISS cosim gates.\\nCompare predecessor and candidate with identical binaries and bus\\nschedules; ordinary instruction and multiply retirement timing should\\nmatch. Run the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation and separately\\nverify the requested 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration CoreMark run with its\\ncanonical CRCs, markers and validation literal. Prior logs and the\\ncurrent benchmark Makefile identify an existing 2 KB evaluator\\ndefault; report the two configurations separately and do not alter\\nbenchmark sources or validators to reconcile that discrepancy.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 3, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Implement explicit sign\nNOTES # Shared multiply product and result selection\n\nImplemented the requested hypothesis against `hyp-20260906-001-r11s0`, whose RTL commit is `b528e69487651d83e1c0c4b349aac815291d9464`. Local correctness gates pass. The unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation **regresses median Fmax and fitness by 4.311837265%**, with identical execution cycles. The implementation remains available for the orchestrator's evaluation; the predicted Fmax gain was not observed.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`: replaced three real multiplication expressions with signed 33-bit `mul_a_ext`/`mul_b_ext` operands and one signed 66-bit `mul_product`. Operand a is signed only for MULH/MULHSU; operand b only for MULH. The four real multiply case labels share one arm selecting `[31:0]` for MUL and `[63:32]` otherwise. Bits 65:64 are unused under the existing narrowly scoped UNUSEDSIGNAL convention.\n- Retained the four exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas, all nonmultiply case arms, default zero, encoded opcodes, interface, and divider connections. No other RTL file changed. No register or pipeline stage was added. Single-cycle multiply, forwarding, jump-link priority, direct registered memory addressing, divider protocol/result banks, and `nret: 1` remain intact.\n- `test_alu.py`: retained every existing assertion and added all four multiply operations over a 16-value boundary cross-product and 1,024 deterministic full-width random pairs. Independent Python signed/unsigned integer products check the low/high halves. A second test interleaves multiply variants with every ordinary ALU control and unsupported controls 19 through 31. Both tests also run with the existing exact ALTOPS expectations; all divider latency, arithmetic-state, reset, and held-bank checks remain.\n- `test_pipeline.py`: added named high-half instruction encoders, forwarding coverage assertions, and mixed programs for every multiply variant. Coverage includes both EX/MEM and MEM/WB sources on both operands, competing writers, repeated destinations, dependent consumers, x0 writes/reads, load-fed operands, retained WB data, deterministic instruction/data holds, and adjacent DIV/REM operations. Exact ISS, retirement order, PC continuity, accepted memory effects, and existing state-ownership assertions remain mandatory.\n\nNo architectural deviation was needed. One new test initially requested a memory/load-use overlap without holding the preceding store. It failed on the unchanged predecessor. Added that store hold to the stimulus and retained the coverage assertion; predecessor and candidate then passed. There were no RTL correctness fixes or formal retries.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Requested Verilator lint command: pass without warnings/errors, in real and ALTOPS modes.\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed on the first proof run**. Used `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6` and the installed conda Python. The unchanged script auto-selected `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg`. Staged the formal tree under `$TMPDIR` so contract files and the shared upstream checkout were not written.\n- All nine candidate pytest runners pass: real ALU 36 cocotb tests, ALTOPS ALU 36, real EX/result-bank 5, ALTOPS EX/result-bank 5, decoder 30, ID 1, immediate generator 8, pipeline 26, register file 5: **152 cocotb tests total**.\n- Identical pipeline suites ran on predecessor and candidate using the existing optional trace recorder/comparator. **40 scenarios, 29,936 sampled edges/events, 2,498 launches, and 6,300 retirements match exactly**, including valid RVFI fields, source operands, memory acceptance, bus schedules, resets, dispatches, and cycle numbers. Reset-aborted requests account for launches without dispatch. Both versions independently pass the unchanged Python ISS comparison. Ordinary multiply/integer retirement spacing is one clock; load-use and divide spacing retain their exact previous values.\n- The unchanged `tools.eval.cosim.run_cosim` gate passes on both versions for selftest and the evaluator CoreMark binary. Additional selftest runs pass the unchanged ISS at zero waits and with both buses stalled using seeds 1, 2, 3, 19, and 97; all 115 retirements and their cycles match exactly for each schedule.\n- The complete real-arithmetic 6 KB CoreMark RVFI streams were compared byte for byte while streaming: **22,093,920 retirements, 6,463,986,468 bytes**, including every emitted operand, result, memory field, and retirement cycle. No difference. Shared SHA256: `34ef03d244b986a66be40660efea1b01b6705ecbf1263bf493d109b7184ac4a0`.\n\nFast formal uses ALTOPS and does not prove the real product. Real arithmetic is supported here by the independent integer vectors, ISS comparisons, canonical CRCs, and predecessor trace equivalence. `formal-deep` was not run; no claim of a complete real-arithmetic SMT proof is made.\n\n## Three-seed FPGA evaluation\n\nRan unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` on both staged versions with `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`. All six routes completed normally. Synthesis, FPGA wrapper, constraints, route script, seeds 1/2/3, evaluator, benchmark sources, and validators were unchanged. The predecessor reproduces its recorded Fmax and resource counts.\n\n| Metric | Predecessor | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 190.33 | 172.47 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 184.84 | 177.65 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 168.69 | 176.87 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.84 | 176.87 |\n| Evaluator marker-window cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Exact iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,491,485 | 10 / 4,491,485 |\n| Exact evaluator fitness, iterations/s | 411.5342698461644 | 393.7895818420856 |\n| Evaluator LUT4 count | 5,485 | 5,489 |\n| Evaluator FF count | 2,119 | 2,095 |\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 1 |\n| Memory block RAMs | 4 | 4 |\n\nThe exact iteration rate is `2.2264351322558128e-6`; the evaluator's rounded JSON field `2e-6` was not used to recompute fitness. Physical FF count fell by 24 (DFFE mapping), despite unchanged RTL register boundaries. Total mapped MUX2_LUT5 count fell from 488 to 456; this is not an overall LUT or timing improvement.\n\n## Evaluator 2 KB versus requested 6 KB\n\nThe existing benchmark Makefile defaults to **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10**, and the unchanged evaluator validates its existing 2 KB CRC table. The FPGA numbers above use that configuration. The shared evaluator ELF SHA256 is `6987dfe6f861a637b9d26b1d0138a0a4a450f6ea5e4bdd1cc3a8b2f90bc8e8e1`. Separate preliminary builds had identical PT_LOAD bytes, addresses, sizes, and flags but different nonloaded ELF metadata; the comparisons use a shared byte-identical ELF.\n\nSeparately built **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10** from unchanged sources using the Makefile's existing command-line options. Both versions consumed the same ELF, SHA256 `6ce5ce8581a7c02c54ff0939acbddd63bc27e37c3658f66a8d76b23a069cf522`, with identical `--istall --dstall` schedules. Both report:\n\n- Start marker cycle 56,393; stop marker cycle 32,893,064; **32,836,671 marker-window cycles**; final EBREAK cycle 32,900,232. No OOB access or cycle-limit termination.\n- Canonical `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, plus `seedcrc=0x8a02`, `crcfinal=0x5949`, ten iterations, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`\n- Exact iteration rate `10 / 32836671 = 3.0453757020618807e-7`. Using the routed medians gives separate 6 KB fitness **56.290724476911805 -> 53.86356004236848 iterations/s**. These values were not substituted into the 2 KB evaluator.\n\nThe unch\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Retime writeback selection into a unified MEM/WB result register\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural history in cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl shows gains\\nfrom changing storage boundaries and ownership: r2s1 separated divider\\ncompletion storage, r4s1 moved divider finalization behind registers,\\nand r5s1 separated pipeline payload capture from squash control. Later\\nmultiplier-bank, register-file, forwarding, JALR, and fetch-queue\\nexperiments regressed routed fitness. In particular, r11s1's frontend\\nmux and consumption paths erased its modest cycle saving.\\n\\nThe current MEM/WB bundle registers both alu_result and extended\\nread_data, then wb_stage selects between them with registered\\nmem_to_reg. That late 32-bit mux feeds the register-file write bus,\\nsame-cycle ID bypass, and MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding. The accepted r11s0\\nnotes report normal EX result selection as critical in two seeds and\\nMEM/WB tag forwarding through JALR as critical in the other. These\\nobservations do not prove the writeback data mux is the bottleneck,\\nbut motivate reducing downstream data selection and result storage\\nwithout adding issue bubbles. No logged structural attempt has moved\\nthis load-versus-execution selection before the MEM/WB boundary.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace mem_wb_t.alu_result and mem_wb_t.read_data with one\\n32-bit wb_result field at the existing retirement boundary.\\nPreserve the other bundles, shared control encoding, and all\\nRVFI metadata, including the distinct raw mem_rdata field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the current EX/MEM owner's existing extended load_data\\nor selected in.alu_result before capturing wb_result on the\\nunchanged MEM/WB edge. Preserve synchronous reset and full\\nforwarding-payload retention while hold_wb clears valid.\\nLeave the direct address bank, memory outputs, load extraction,\\nalignment traps, metadata capture, and valid timing intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive w_data directly from in.wb_result, eliminating the\\npost-register mem_to_reg mux. Retain the current destination\\nand write-enable behavior so core.sv's existing writeback,\\nforwarding, register-file bypass, and RVFI wiring all consume\\nthe same already selected registered result.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend existing ISS/RVFI and protocol-trace checks with\\nalternating load, integer, multiply, divide, and link results\\ntargeting the same register, plus immediate and WB-distance\\nconsumers. Cover signed and unsigned byte/halfword lanes,\\nword loads, x0, misaligned accesses, reset, bubbles, and\\nsimultaneous bus stalls. Explicitly hold a dependent EX\\ninstruction behind a stalled memory owner while its needed\\nWB value remains available with valid=0; test both load and\\nnon-load retained results and assert no duplicate retirement\\nor accepted memory transaction. Preserve every existing check\\nand compare valid RVFI fields and event cycles against the\\naccepted-core trace using identical external inputs.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 353.74, \"delta_pct\": -14.04, \"vs_baseline\": 25.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 158.88, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5496, \"ff\": 2062, \"seeds\": [155.26, 158.88, 162.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T14:45:56.604885Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12}\nHYPOTHESIS {'motivation': \"The structural history in cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl shows gains\\nfrom changing storage boundaries and ownership: r2s1 separated divider\\ncompletion storage, r4s1 moved divider finalization behind registers,\\nand r5s1 separated pipeline payload capture from squash control. Later\\nmultiplier-bank, register-file, forwarding, JALR, and fetch-queue\\nexperiments regressed routed fitness. In particular, r11s1's frontend\\nmux and consumption paths erased its modest cycle saving.\\n\\nThe current MEM/WB bundle registers both alu_result and extended\\nread_data, then wb_stage selects between them with registered\\nmem_to_reg. That late 32-bit mux feeds the register-file write bus,\\nsame-cycle ID bypass, and MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding. The accepted r11s0\\nnotes report normal EX result selection as critical in two seeds and\\nMEM/WB tag forwarding through JALR as critical in the other. These\\nobservations do not prove the writeback data mux is the bottleneck,\\nbut motivate reducing downstream data selection and result storage\\nwithout adding issue bubbles. No logged structural attempt has moved\\nthis load-versus-execution selection before the MEM/WB boundary.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Replace the two architectural-result fields at MEM/WB with one 32-bit\\nwb_result register. Perform load-versus-execution result selection on\\nits D input in mem_stage, using the current EX/MEM owner's mem_to_reg.\\nwb_stage then exposes wb_result directly as w_data. This is the sole\\nstructural experiment: move one result-selection mux across an\\nexisting register boundary and consolidate its storage, with no new\\nstage, changed instruction latency, or changed hazard policy.\\n\\nOn each existing !hold_wb capture edge, assign wb_result the value\\nin.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result. load_data is the\\nexisting byte/halfword/word extraction and sign-extension result;\\nin.alu_result already selects the authoritative normal or divider\\ncompletion bank. Use the older EX/MEM metadata, never a younger live\\nopcode or divider response signal. Remove the obsolete registered\\nalu_result and read_data fields from mem_wb_t rather than retaining\\nduplicate functional result registers. Keep ctrl_t and its decoding\\nintact; retaining mem_to_reg in the shared control bundle is harmless\\nas long as no post-MEM/WB writeback mux still consumes it.\\n\\nKeep reset synchronous and clear wb_result to zero. A dmem hold must\\nretain wb_result, destination, and forwarding eligibility along with\\nthe existing MEM/WB payload while clearing retirement valid only.\\nIn particular, never clear or qualify the retained result by valid,\\nwb_w_en, or a bubble: a stalled EX consumer can still require that\\npreviously retired writer's value. Capture bubbles on the same edges\\nas before and preserve control-zero invalid EX/MEM entries. Regfile\\nwrites remain gated by valid, reg_write, and the existing x0 rule;\\nforwarding retains its current producer qualifications and EX/MEM\\npriority. All consumers, including RVFI rd_wdata, must use the same\\nunified registered value.\\n\\nPreserve the exact memory bus transaction and trap behavior. Retain\\nraw mem_rdata, aligned mem_addr, masks, replicated mem_wdata, and\\nsource operands as independent RVFI metadata; wb_result does not\\nreplace the raw load word. Misaligned loads still suppress writes\\nand accesses through the existing trap controls. Leave the direct\\nnormal EX/MEM address bank and mem_stage.address connection untouched\\nso this experiment does not repeat r2s1's RAM-inference failure.\\nKeep the divider's real and ALTOPS arithmetic, completion bank,\\nrequest schedule, top-level nret=1 IO, and retirement order unchanged.\\n\\nPredict a low-confidence 3% fitness improvement from removing one\\ndownstream mux and up to 32 redundant result-storage bits. With\\nidentical marker-window cycles, any median Fmax gain transfers\\ndirectly to fitness. The extra selection before wb_result may instead\\nmake the BRAM-load-to-MEM/WB or execution-bank-to-MEM/WB path critical;\\nunchanged EX result and JALR paths can also cap or reverse the gain.\\nThese are hypotheses, not measured savings. Inspect mapped result\\nstorage, all three routed critical p\nNOTES \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
