{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json; rg -n 'hyp-20260906-003-r(1|4|11|13|14)s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r1s1 (improvement, +31.75%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the single-cycle execute path into a shared blocking divider raised median Fmax 31.75% with no CoreMark cycle penalty because the timed region executed no DIV/REM, so the throughput gain depends on the workload's division frequency.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r2s0 (regression, -5.98%): Replacing ALU case selection with registered one-hot masks and a balanced OR tree disrupted multiplier sharing (MULT36X36 count rose from 1 to 3) and left slower selector-to-EX/MEM paths critical, lowering median Fmax 5.98% despite unchanged arithmetic and CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.85%): Routing the post-register DIV/REM result-bank mux into mem_stage.address blocks Yosys BRAM address-register absorption, so memory addresses must use the normal EX/MEM bank directly to avoid expansion from four block RAMs to 1,024 RAM16SDP4 blocks and placement failure.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r3s0 (regression, -35.87%): In div_unit.sv's four-step restoring divider, 8/8/8/9-bit carry-select subtraction shortened native carry chains but added prefix/mux logic and routing across serial steps, reducing reported median Fmax 35.87% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r3s1 (regression, -57.65%): Halving divider quotient stages does not guarantee better mapped timing: two radix-4 stages in div_unit.sv cascaded 35-bit trial subtraction and underflow-driven masked-OR selection into terminal sign correction, yielding a reported ~14 ns completion path with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r3s2 (regression, -3.71%): Removing BTB state is insufficient to recover predictor timing: stateless backward-taken/direct-JAL prediction with one-bit EX repair cut CoreMark cycles 5.16% but lost 8.68% median Fmax, with routed worst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r4s0 (regression, -37.58%): Carry-free prefix-OR/XOR sign correction in div_unit.sv removed 64 carry cells but left restoring carry chains feeding seven LUT arcs and a LUT5 mux, lowering reported median Fmax 37.58% despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r4s1 (improvement, +1.78%): Precomputing the first four restoring bits with a narrow request-edge prefix in div_unit.sv separates sign correction from the wide recurrence by using registered quotient_q/remainder_q, improving fitness without extending the eight-clock response schedule.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 (regression, -8.03%): Using acceptance-qualified early_jal as the IF PC mux select pulled stall/flush/redirect controls into the data path; selecting with raw predecode instead recovered median Fmax from 78.73 to 153.52 MHz, but JAL-only prediction's 1.10% CoreMark cycle saving could not cover the remaining 9.04% Fmax loss.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r5s0 (regression, -7.44%): Balanced one-hot read/bypass trees in reg_file.sv reduced LUTs and mapped read depth yet lost 7.44% median Fmax as placement worsened the unchanged EX-control-to-EX/MEM path, showing that local mux simplification can regress routed timing despite identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r5s1 (improvement, +7.14%): Separating ID/EX and EX/MEM payload capture from bubble/flush and divide-completion control removed those decisions from wide data/reset/enable cones, raising reported median Fmax 7.14% with unchanged CoreMark cycles while requiring zero control on invalid entries and full payload/control retention during memory stalls.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r5s2 (regression, -11.39%): Control-only ID/EX squashes and decode-folded repair polarity do not guarantee predictor timing: raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold paths to control-bank RESET remained critical, lowering median Fmax from 171.79 to 146.05 MHz and outweighing branch-only prediction's 4.05% CoreMark cycle saving.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r6s0 (regression, -8.89%): Precomputing JALR sum bit 1 for each rs1 forwarding candidate in ex_stage.sv removed native arithmetic from the mapped alignment cone, but routed ALU-result selection and divider-operand enables became critical, reducing median Fmax from 184.06 to 167.70 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r6s1 (regression, -8.01%): Registering near/far forwarding matches at ID/EX removed live rd comparisons from EX, but alu_op-driven normal-result selection into EX/MEM became critical in all three seeds, lowering reported median Fmax from 184.06 to 169.32 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; comparator retiming still requires checking other routed paths across seeds.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r7s0 (regression, -17.88%): Funct3-aligned five-bit ALU codes do not guarantee better routed control timing: forwarding-to-divider request/quotient capture became routing-dominated in two seeds and alu_op-to-normal-result selection limited the third, cutting median Fmax 17.88% despite unchanged CoreMark cycles and a single MULT36X36.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r7s1 (regression, -6.73%): Separating MUL into an EX/MEM bank left EX/MEM rd-to-JALR forwarding and register-file reads limiting reported Fmax despite unchanged CoreMark cycles and one MULT36X36; missing result-bank mux arcs prevent attributing the regression to bank-to-EX/WB delay.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.44%): Registering train_pending isolated predictor payload enables from live EX qualification, but single-entry backedge prediction with decode displacement certification still left forwarding/JALR alignment through repair/hold/squash to ID control RESET critical in all three seeds, losing 37.06% median Fmax for an inferred ~4% CoreMark cycle saving.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r8s0 (regression, -12.99%): Replacing reg_file.sv's data reset with written_q validity inferred 32 RAM16SDP4 cells and eliminated 1,014 data flops, but validity-to-ID/EX selection limited two routed seeds and forwarding/JALR the third, showing that smaller register storage can reduce median Fmax despite identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r8s1 (regression, -5.94%): Absorbing ID/EX operand capture into two synchronous SDPX9B register-file banks nearly halved LUT/DFF use without adding CoreMark cycles, but forwarding/JALR and ALU-result selection still limited reported Fmax; missing RAM address/output logic arcs prevent attributing the regression to RAM latency.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r8s2 (regression, -49.62%): Validity-only ID/EX annul isolated repair from the control/payload bank, but forwarding through 27-31 carry arcs to the remaining valid RESET still limited all three routed seeds, dropping median Fmax from 184.06 to 88.97 MHz despite 4.05% fewer CoreMark cycles; reducing squash fanout alone does not shorten recovery logic.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r9s0 (regression, -4.16%): Direct one-hot forwarding and balanced masked-OR ALU inputs in ex_stage.sv do not guarantee shallower mapping: synthesis retained final operand muxes and five-cell tag-to-ALU depth, while routing-dominated register-read paths became critical in all three seeds, reducing median Fmax 4.16% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r9s1 (regression, -16.60%): Inserting jalr_target_q after the full forwarding and addition cone shifted a critical path to its input while divider operand enables and EX result selection limited other seeds, reducing reported median Fmax 16.34% despite only 0.32% more 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r9s2 (regression, -46.23%): Removing target-alignment/JALR adders from EX squash control still left EX/MEM rd-driven forwarding through branch-comparison carry chains (two seeds) and recovery/decode LUTs (third seed) dominating routed timing, cutting median Fmax 48.41% despite backward prediction's 4.05% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r10s0 (regression, -20.34%): Mapping JALR target bit 1 to a single LUT4 after the unchanged rs1 forwarding mux in ex_stage.sv still shifted all three routed critical paths to slower alu_op-to-normal-EX/MEM result selection, so avoiding speculative per-source fanout did not prevent a median Fmax drop from 184.06 to 146.63 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r10s1 (regression, -13.55%): Registering raw forwarded JALR operands before target addition isolated completion from live forwarding, but PC+immediate alignment/squash to ID/EX alu_op and forwarding/load-availability to div_rs2_q CE became critical, lowering median Fmax from 184.06 to 159.62 MHz despite only 0.32% more 6 KB CoreMark cycles; a target-path cut must also account for shared squash and launch controls.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r10s2 (regression, -55.72%): A balanced Boolean tree in branch_compare.sv eliminated branch-comparison carry cells but left a 16-cell LUT/mux path through forwarding and EX repair to ID control RESET in all three routed seeds, showing that carry removal alone cannot ensure faster recovery: median Fmax fell to 78.19 MHz despite 4.05% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r11s1 (regression, -23.88%): A two-entry fall-through FIFO in if_stage.sv saved 3.08% of stalled 6 KB CoreMark cycles, but its live-head mux through register-file reads and load-use/decode_take-to-occupancy paths cut median routed Fmax from 184.06 to 135.43 MHz, showing that empty bypass can put queue selection and consumption control on the critical path.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r11s2 (regression, -5.01%): Registering only predicted-taken exit recovery isolated the inverted branch-outcome path, but two-slot exit repairs left only 2.91% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles while load-use control to ID/EX RESET and ordinary data paths limited median Fmax to 169.75 MHz, below the 178.71 MHz break-even point.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r12s0 (regression, -4.31%): Explicit signed 33x33 multiply sharing in alu.sv kept one MULT36X36 and removed a result-path MUX2_LUT5 but left five combinational cells, while EX/MEM rd-to-JALR/PC forwarding limited all three seeds; reducing multiply mux count did not improve routed timing with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r12s2 (regression, -48.86%): Certifying cached backedge targets from raw fetch data and registering exits removed the IF target adder and generic stale-hit repair, but EX/MEM forwarding through ordinary immediate recovery to ID/EX control RESET remained critical in every routed seed, limiting median Fmax to 91.00 MHz despite 3.73% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r13s0 (regression, -13.38%): Fetch payload/control separation can worsen operand-read timing: removing if_stage.sv's NOP mask with validity-qualified load-use checks made raw source-address paths through register-file read/bypass into ID/EX critical in all three routed seeds, dropping median Fmax from 184.84 to 160.10 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r13s1 (regression, -4.38%): Moving MULH/MULHU/MULHSU into div_unit doubled MULT36X36 use without changing 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles, but normal ALU selection remained critical in two routed seeds and LFSR-to-ID/EX capture in the third, lowering reported median Fmax from 184.84 to 176.74 MHz.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r13s2 (regression, -19.90%): Fully registered recovery removed live EX-to-squash paths but left EX/MEM rd-driven forwarding and branch comparison critical at recovery_pending.D in all three routed seeds, so the 64-entry bimodal predictor's 5.02% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles could not offset median Fmax falling from 184.84 to 140.61 MHz.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r14s0 (regression, -4.35%): Sharing SRL/SRA through a sign-injected signed 33-bit shifter in alu.sv reduced mux counts but deepened the structural alu_op[3]-to-result cone from seven to nine cells, while forwarding/JALR and register reads limited routed Fmax, so fewer shift expressions do not guarantee better timing with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r14s1 (regression, -6.36%): Blocking all multiplication behind div_unit.sv operand registers retained one MULT36X36 but added 2.09%/3.86% cycles in 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark, while forwarding/JALR and LFSR-to-ID/EX capture still limited routed timing and reduced reported median Fmax 4.40%; DSP isolation must overcome both multiply bubbles and the remaining nonmultiply paths.\n- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r14s2 (regression, -9.88%): EX/MEM byte-summary flops cut the forwarding-to-full-branch-comparison path, but residual forwarding-to-byte-comparison and ordinary ALU-result selection limited median routed Fmax to 158.20 MHz, below the 175.55 MHz needed for the bimodal predictor's 5.02% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles to break even.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch currently always advances to PC+4, and EX flushes fetch/decode on\\nevery taken branch or jump. Since IF/ID is combinational, a normally\\nadvancing taken transfer suppresses one useful fetch/decode slot. A hot\\ncontrol transfer can avoid that bubble if its next PC is predicted at\\nfetch. The local experiment log contains only the 282.82 iter/sec baseline\\n(4,491,485 measured cycles, 127.03 MHz median Fmax), with no previous\\npredictor attempts and no LESSONS.md. A small PC-indexed target table is\\nattractive because its lookup does not require instruction decoding or\\nan instruction-immediate target adder on the fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer with a two-bit\\nsaturating direction counter per entry. Index it with fetch PC[5:2], use\\nthe full remaining PC[31:6] as the tag, and store aligned target[31:2].\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\\npredict PC+4. Conditional branches use the counter for direction, while\\nresolved JAL/JALR entries are set strongly taken and learn their latest\\ntarget. Clear valid bits on reset; payloads need not be reset and must\\nnever influence a miss. Use the full architectural PC and target widths.\\n\\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\\nIF/ID into ID/EX. EX computes the actual next PC using the existing\\nforwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush younger\\nwork only when a valid instruction advances out of EX and its actual\\nnext PC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every valid\\ninstruction, including a non-control instruction with a stale table hit.\\nThe repair target is the actual next PC, including PC+4 for a predicted\\ntaken branch that resolves not taken, and the existing PC+4 continuation\\nfor a misaligned target trap. Preserve JAL/JALR link data, trap handling,\\nand the resolved next PC reported through RVFI.\\n\\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never while EX is held by dmem\\nbackpressure. For a matching conditional entry, saturate toward its\\nresolved direction and refresh its aligned branch target. Allocate a\\nmissed conditional entry only when taken, initializing it weakly taken\\n(2'b10). Allocate or refresh legal, aligned JAL/JALR targets with 2'b11.\\nInvalidate a matching entry when the resolved instruction is no longer\\na legal control transfer or its control-transfer target is misaligned.\\nNever allocate or reinforce predictions from bubbles or trap outcomes.\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training to use the pre-edge table\\ncontents for the prediction that is captured; a later table update must\\nnot change recorded metadata.\\n\\nApply a predicted PC advance only when that fetch is actually accepted\\ninto ID/EX. On load-use hazards hold the fetch PC and bubble ID/EX;\\non dmem holds freeze both PC and ID/EX, including its saved prediction.\\nInject the existing invalid bubble on imem unavailability when ID/EX\\nis otherwise able to advance.\\nWhile dmem holds EX, defer both repair and training until EX can advance;\\nretain the existing forwarding state during the hold. An eligible EX\\nrepair must override imem backpressure and any younger prediction.\\nKeep raw lookup independent of flush/stall signals to avoid combinational\\nloops. Correct predictions must not assert a redirect or flush.\\n\\nThis single predictor is expected to improve net fitness by about 8%,\\nsubject to measurement. The small table limits added lookup depth and\\nrouting, but conflicts, changing indirect targets, and additional LUT/FF\\ncost may reduce the gain or lower Fmax. Validate with unchanged formal,\\ncosimulation, CRC, UART, marker-window, and three-seed FPGA evaluation\\ngates. Preserve the nret=1 interface and every evaluation invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry PC-indexed tagged target table, valid bits, and\\ntwo-bit saturating counters with the specified reset, miss,\\nreplacement, invalidation, and once-per-EX-advance training behavior.\\nProvide a combinational PC-only lookup and synchronous training;\\nkeep prediction independent of instruction data and pipeline flush.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t so EX\\nchecks the prediction actually consumed by fetch. Keep the existing\\nresolved pc_next fields and single-retirement contract intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup to the fetch PC and connect qualified EX\\ntraining information back to the predictor. Wire predicted next PC\\ninto fetch and misprediction repair into the existing redirect path.\\nPreserve all top-level imem, dmem, and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the predictor next PC on accepted fetches and emit that exact\\nprediction in the IF/ID bundle. Give reset and qualified EX repair\\npriority; retain PC on ordinary stalls and suppress invalid fetches\\nas before. A prediction itself must not squash the current instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc with the same enable as the instruction;\\nhold it with ID/EX during dmem stalls and clear it with bubbles and\\nflushes. Do not recompute a prediction after its instruction is held.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive one actual_next_pc from existing branch/jump resolution and\\ntrap semantics. Qualify repair with in.valid and EX advancement,\\ncompare actual_next_pc against in.predicted_next_pc, and redirect\\nonly on a mismatch. Export resolved control-transfer information\\nand a single training enable per EX advance; retain operand\\nforwarding, link writes, alignment checks, and RVFI pc_next.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified prediction repair, preserving the\\nexisting load-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, and WB\\nforwarding retention. Flush younger instructions only for repair,\\nwith no extra bubble for a correctly predicted control transfer.\\nUpdate comments to reflect the actual combinational IF/ID boundary.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline regressions for cold misses, trained taken loops,\\nloop exits, both direction mistakes, taken-to-wrong-target repair,\\ndirect JAL and changing JALR targets, full-tag index collisions,\\nreset, stale non-control hits, and misaligned control transfers.\\nExercise load-dependent branches and repairs coincident with imem\\nstalls and multi-cycle dmem holds. Check architectural state,\\nmonotonic RVFI order/PC continuity, and absence of wrong-path stores\\nor retirements. Confirm correct predictions cause no repair bubble\\nand held EX instructions do not train repeatedly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 291.2, \"delta_pct\": 2.96, \"vs_baseline\": 2.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10377, \"ff\": 2069, \"seeds\": [129.95, 121.11, 122.77], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Small tagged bimodal predictor\\n\\nImplemented the requested 16-entry direct-mapped predictor in `cores/bench`.\\nThe index is PC[5:2], the tag is PC[31:6], and the stored aligned target is\\ntarget[31:2]. A valid tag hit with counter[1] predicts the target; all misses\\nuse full-width PC+4. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\\nConditional hits refresh the target and saturate the direction counter.\\nTaken conditional misses allocate weakly taken; not-taken misses leave the\\nresident entry untouched. Legal aligned JAL/JALR updates are strongly taken\\nand learn their latest target. Matching stale, illegal, or misaligned entries\\nare invalidated, including misaligned untaken branch targets. Bubbles and\\nheld EX instructions cannot train.\\n\\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC consumed at the accepting\\nedge. EX compares every advancing valid instruction against one actual next\\nPC, also used for RVFI. A mismatch repairs fetch and discards the younger\\ncombinational IF/ID slot. Correct predictions have no repair bubble. JAL/JALR\\nlink writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, misalignment traps, and PC+4 trap\\ncontinuation are preserved. Repairs wait through dmem holds and override\\nimem backpressure when EX advances.\\n\\nLoad-use stalls hold PC and inject an ID/EX bubble. Dmem stalls hold PC and\\nthe complete ID/EX payload, including prediction metadata; existing MEM/WB\\nforwarding retention remains intact. Invalid imem fetches now clear the\\nwhole advancing ID/EX bubble, including prediction metadata.\\n\\n## Deviations and scope\\n\\n- Added standalone predictor tests as well as pipeline regressions. The\\n  pipeline harness now models pre-edge bus acceptance and checks RVFI order,\\n  PC continuity, prediction capture, holds, repairs, and store handshakes.\\n- Split hazard equations into independent continuous assignments to avoid\\n  false combinational scheduling cycles in the instrumented Verilator build.\\n- Used `core_pkg.sv` first when linting; compilation-unit typedefs require\\n  this ordering. No lint suppressions were added for combinational loops.\\n- Kept `nret: 1`, the channel-0-only top-level ports, ALU, decoder, forwarding,\\n  memory/writeback logic, and all evaluation contract files unchanged.\\n- Build artifacts and unchanged formal scratch copies are under `$TMPDIR`,\\n  using the prefix `hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-`. This notes file is the requested\\n  exception to the RTL/test editing surface.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Strict Verilator lint: PASS, both ordinary and `--public-flat-rw` builds;\\n  no warnings. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Cocotb: all 6 pytest runners / 87 cocotb cases PASS, including 17 pipeline\\n  cases and 3 predictor cases. Coverage includes cold/warm loops, exits,\\n  both direction mistakes, direct and changing indirect targets, every tag\\n  bit, all slots, reset, stale ordinary/illegal hits, misaligned transfers,\\n  load-dependent branches, random bus stalls, and multi-cycle held repairs.\\n- Selftest cosimulation using the unchanged Python ISS comparison: all 115\\n  retirements match in each of six runs (zero-wait, imem-only, dmem-only,\\n  combined stalls, and two additional combined-stall seeds).\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on both the initial run and the\\n  final full run after hazard scheduling cleanup. No counterexamples or\\n  formal fix retries. The staged RTL was verified byte-for-byte against\\n  the final worktree sources; the single-retirement wrapper and all check\\n  depths/definitions were unchanged.\\n- Unchanged evaluator CoreMark validation: PASS, 10 iterations, 4,215,937\\n  marker-window cycles, canonical UART/CRC validation and no OOB accesses.\\n- Supplemental 6 KB / `-O3` CoreMark: PASS, 10 iterations, 30,823,120 measured\\n  cycles; CRCs `d4b0`, `be52`, `5e47`, the required validation text, both timing\\n  markers, and no OOB accesses. Built from unchanged sources with the\\n  existing Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options.\\n- FPGA synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs: PASS, exit 0.\\n  Seeds 1/2/3: 129.95 / 121.11 / 122.77 MHz; median 122.77 MHz.\\n  Resources: 10,377 LUT4 and 2,069 DFF. Using the unchanged evaluator's\\n  fixture and fitness formula gives 291.20 iter/sec, +2.96% against the\\n  supplied 282.82 baseline. Measured cycles fell 6.13%; median Fmax fell\\n  3.35%. The projected 8% net improvement was not achieved locally.\\n\\nThe full formal log is under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-formal/formal-final-console.log`; FPGA seed\\nlogs and the combined result are under\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-20260906-003-r1s2-fpga/`. Cocotb XML results, selftest comparison\\nresults, and the supplemental 6 KB build/run are under the corresponding\\n`-cocotb`, `-selftest-results.json`, and `-6k` scratch paths.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nThe prompt specifies 6 KB CoreMark, but the checked-in Makefile defaults and\\n`tools/eval/fpga.py` CRC gate use the 2 KB fixture. Both workloads were tested\\nseparately without changing the evaluator. The 2 KB measurement must not be\\nreported as a 6 KB fitness result. The measured 10,377 LUT4 exceeds\\n`core.yaml`'s 10,000-LUT target, although all placements succeeded. FPGA\\ncost and reduced Fmax limit the net gain. Direct-map conflicts and changing\\nindirect targets remain expected sources of prediction misses. These are\\nlocal results; orchestrator acceptance and its independent rerun remain\\noutside this implementation step.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T07:27:51.865455Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.96%): Small BTBs still need timing and area headroom: this 16-entry full-width tagged predictor cut CoreMark cycles 6.13%, but a 3.35% median Fmax loss limited throughput gain to 2.96%, with total area reaching 10,377 LUT4 against a 10,000-LUT target.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current divider-based winner has no predictor and scores 372.62\\niterations/s at 167.36 MHz with 5,438 LUT4. Its combinational IF/ID\\nboundary loses one fetch/decode opportunity on an advancing taken\\nbranch or jump. LESSONS.md and experiment hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 show\\nthat a 16-entry tagged bimodal BTB reduced measured CoreMark cycles\\nby 6.13%, but achieved only 291.20 iterations/s on the older baseline:\\nmedian Fmax was 122.77 MHz and area was 10,377 LUT4. Those absolute\\nresults are not measurements of a predictor on the current winner.\\n\\nThe saved r1s2 seed-2 nextpnr report identifies a specific obstacle:\\nan 8.26 ns critical path starts at the predictor's registered tag-read\\naddress, traverses tag RAM and training-hit/counter logic, and ends at\\na tag RAM write enable. That implementation reads the table again at\\nthe resolving instruction's PC to decide how to train it. Merely\\nadding that predictor to the faster winner risks making this feedback\\npath dominant. Capturing lookup metadata with the instruction can\\nremove the second table read from training while preserving the\\nuseful target and direction predictions demonstrated previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry, direct-mapped, full-width tagged bimodal BTB to the\\ncurrent cores/bench RTL, with training computed exclusively from\\nregistered fetch metadata and the instruction's resolved outcome.\\nThis is one predictor experiment: keep the existing divider, ALU,\\nforwarding, memory system, pipeline boundaries, and nret=1 interface.\\n\\nIndex the BTB by PC[5:2], store the full tag PC[31:6], aligned target\\nbits [31:2], a two-bit saturating counter, and a valid bit. Lookup is\\ncombinational and depends only on the fetch PC. A valid full-tag hit\\nwith counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise predict the\\nfull-width PC+4. Reset clears valid bits; unreset payloads must never\\naffect a miss. Do not introduce an instruction-data target adder or\\nreduce architectural address widths.\\n\\nAlong with the exact predicted_next_pc, capture lookup_hit and the\\nlookup_counter in ID/EX on the same edge that accepts the instruction.\\nA hit includes entries whose direction counter predicts not taken.\\nSupply a defined counter value on a miss. Hold all metadata with a\\nstalled instruction and clear it with a bubble or flush. The predictor\\narrays must have no resolving-PC read port: training must not use\\ntag_q[train_index], counter_q[train_index], or valid_q[train_index]\\nas an input to its next-state/write-enable logic.\\n\\nTrain once when a valid instruction actually advances from EX into\\nEX/MEM: in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait. For a legal aligned\\nconditional branch with saved lookup_hit, saturate the saved counter\\ntoward the resolved direction and write a complete entry using this\\ninstruction's actual PC tag and branch target. On a saved miss,\\nallocate only a taken aligned branch, initialized weakly taken (10).\\nLegal aligned JAL/JALR always write the resolved target and counter\\n11. If an instruction with saved lookup_hit is no longer a legal\\ncontrol transfer, or has a misaligned control-transfer target, clear\\nthe addressed valid bit. Invalid instructions and bubbles never\\nallocate. Compute every payload write and enable without rereading\\nresident predictor state at EX; use local index write decoding.\\n\\nDefine simultaneous lookup and training using the pre-edge table for\\nboth the consumed next PC and its captured metadata. Deliberately\\naccept a stale counter/hit snapshot when an older resolving instruction\\nupdates the same index at that edge; do not add a training-to-fetch\\nbypass that reconstructs the long feedback path. Such collisions may\\nlose a counter update or evict a newer entry, affecting prediction\\naccuracy only. Every allocation/update writes its own complete actual\\ntag and target, so metadata from different instructions cannot be\\ncombined into a fabricated entry. Full next-PC checking remains the\\ncorrectness mechanism. This bounded approximation must be exercised\\nby consecutive same-index branches and single-instruction loops.\\n\\nEX computes one actual_next_pc with the existing forwarded operands,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, and alignment/trap continuation rules. For\\nevery advancing valid instruction, compare it with that instruction's\\nsaved predicted_next_pc, including ordinary instructions with stale\\nBTB hits and completed DIV/REM operations. Redirect only on a mismatch;\\nrepair to actual_next_pc and squash younger work. Correct predictions\\nintroduce no flush. RVFI pc_wdata always reports the resolved next PC;\\nJAL/JALR link writes and trap suppression remain unchanged.\\n\\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when its instruction is accepted.\\nPreserve load-use bubbles, invalid imem bubbles, dmem holds and retained\\nWB forwarding data, and all divider request/response behavior. Defer\\nrepairs and training while EX cannot advance. An eligible repair\\noverrides instruction-bus backpressure and the younger prediction.\\nKeep raw predictor lookup independent of stall/flush/redirect signals\\nand avoid combinational cycles in hazard control.\\n\\nThe prediction benefit is an estimate, not a new measurement. Retaining\\nabout 6% fewer cycles while losing at most about 1% Fmax would improve\\nfitness by roughly 5%. Counter-snapshot collisions, the remaining\\nlookup/repair paths, and FPGA mapping could reduce that gain. Compare\\nagainst the current 372.62 winner using the unchanged marker-bracketed\\nCoreMark and three-seed median-Fmax gates, with all existing formal,\\ncosim, CRC, UART, OOB, and resource checks intact. Inspect routed timing\\nto confirm that predictor training no longer contains a tag-RAM read\\nfollowed by tag comparison and table write enable in the same cycle.\\nKeep the official fixture and any supplemental 6 KB results distinct;\\ndo not modify either the evaluator or benchmark sources.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the specified 16-entry PC-only tagged BTB with one fetch lookup\\nand synchronous writes. Export hit/counter metadata for capture.\\nGenerate training from supplied registered hit/counter snapshots,\\nresolved PC/target/outcome, and EX advancement, with no dynamic\\ntraining-side array reads. Implement complete-entry writes, reset\\nvalidity, stale-hit invalidation, and pre-edge collision semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with predicted_next_pc, lookup_hit, and\\nlookup_counter. Preserve resolved pc_next and every existing RVFI,\\ncontrol, and division payload field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the PC-only prediction on accepted fetches and attach its exact\\nnext PC and lookup snapshot to the combinational IF/ID bundle.\\nPreserve reset/eligible-repair priority, ordinary PC holds, and\\ninvalid fetch behavior. A prediction must not flush its instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction and training metadata under exactly the same\\nenable as the instruction; hold it through dmem/divider stalls and\\nclear it on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. Preserve decoder\\nlegality and existing operand capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the saved prediction with the full actual next PC for every\\nvalid EX advance and export one qualified repair/training event.\\nSupply the saved hit/counter to training rather than reading the\\ntable at the resolving PC. Preserve divider completion, forwarding,\\ntrap continuation, link results, and resolved RVFI information.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch lookup, captured predictor metadata, resolved training,\\nand qualified misprediction repair. Preserve the existing divider\\nand all top-level imem/dmem and channel-0-only RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as a qualified EX prediction repair and preserve\\nload-use, instruction/data stalls, divider holds, and WB forwarding\\nretention. Flush only on repair or the existing bubble conditions;\\nkeep independent hazard equations free of combinational cycles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb coverage for all tag bits and indices, counter states,\\nsaved-hit versus saved-miss training, complete-entry replacement,\\nstale-hit invalidation, reset, and explicitly pre-edge lookup/write\\ncollisions. Exercise stale snapshots after an intervening same-index\\nwrite and verify that the resulting entry has one coherent tag and\\ntarget. Hold training inactive for multiple clocks to check stability.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain existing assertions and add ISS/RVFI-checked warm loops, loop\\nexits, both direction errors, stale non-control hits, changing JALR\\ntargets, misalignment traps, full-tag collisions, consecutive\\nsame-index branches, and reset. Exercise load-dependent repairs,\\nimem backpressure, multicycle dmem holds, divider waits/completion,\\nand killed wrong-path stores/divides. Check PC continuity, ordered\\nsingle retirement, one training pulse per EX advance, held metadata,\\nand no repair bubble on a correct prediction.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.07, \"delta_pct\": -7.39, \"vs_baseline\": 22.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6287, \"ff\": 3097, \"seeds\": [143.84, 145.48, 152.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the 16-entry full-width tagged bimodal BTB in cores/bench. Fetch\\nuses PC[5:2], the full PC[31:6] tag, an aligned 30-bit target, validity and a\\ntwo-bit direction counter. A miss exports counter 00 and full-width PC+4.\\nOnly valid bits reset; payloads cannot influence misses.\\n\\nID/EX captures the exact predicted next PC, hit (including not-taken hits),\\nand counter with its instruction. All fields hold during dmem/divider stalls\\nand clear on flushes or invalid advancing bubbles. EX computes one resolved\\nnext PC, with forwarded operands, JALR bit-zero clearing and existing trap\\ncontinuation. Every valid EX/MEM acceptance compares that PC with its saved\\nprediction, including ordinary instructions and completed DIV/REM. Only a\\nmismatch repairs fetch and squashes younger work. RVFI retains the resolved\\nPC and the unchanged nret=1 channel-0 retirement/order interface.\\n\\nThe same in.valid && !reset && !stall && !div_wait event drives training.\\nTraining uses only the registered hit/counter and resolved instruction:\\nsaved-hit aligned branches saturate the snapshot, saved misses allocate only\\ntaken aligned branches at 10, and legal aligned JAL/JALR write 11. Stale hits\\non non-control/illegal instructions or misaligned targets invalidate their\\nindex. Every payload update writes its own complete actual tag and target.\\nConstant per-entry write decoding introduces no resolving-PC read port.\\nSimultaneous lookup/training deliberately consumes the pre-edge table with\\nno bypass; stale snapshots can lose updates or evict intervening entries.\\n\\nHazard equations are separate continuous assignments so EX's dmem hold is\\nindependent of its qualified repair. Eligible repairs override imem\\nbackpressure; dmem/divider holds defer both repair and training. Load-use\\nbubbles, retained WB forwarding data, divider request/completion behavior,\\nALU, decoder, memory stages, pipeline boundaries and all top-level ports\\nremain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis. Local write decoding maps\\nthe table payload into enabled flops rather than relying on a RAM primitive.\\nThe new tests add an instruction-version-aware sparse fetch harness for\\nstale-code scenarios; they use the existing Python ISS and RVFI comparator\\nwithout changing either contract file. Existing tests/assertions remain.\\n\\nLocal formal status: PASS on the first run, 53 passed and 0 failed; no formal\\nfix attempts were needed. The unchanged bash formal/run_all.sh auto-selected\\nwrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg with CORE_NAME=bench and RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl.\\nIt ran from an isolated copy under TMPDIR because the worktree's upstream\\nformal directory is a shared symlink. No checks, depths or wrappers changed.\\nFull non-ALTOPS formal-deep was not run; divider arithmetic is unchanged and\\nboth real and ALTOPS arithmetic tests passed.\\n\\nThe exact requested glob-order Verilator lint command passes without\\nwarnings, as does the ALTOPS variant. All seven pytest runners pass, covering\\n129 cocotb cases: real ALU 30, ALTOPS ALU 30, decoder 30, immediate generator\\n8, register file 5, predictor 3 and pipeline 23. Coverage includes all tag\\nbits/indices/target bits, all saved counters, saved misses, coherent stale\\nreplacement, pre-edge collisions, inactive training, reset, warm loops and\\nexits, both direction errors, changing forwarded JALR targets, illegal and\\nmisaligned stale hits, consecutive same-index branches, single-instruction\\nloops, random imem/dmem stalls, 12-clock holds, retained WB data, deferred\\nDIV/REM repairs, and killed stores/divides. Pipeline monitors check every\\nmetadata capture/hold/bubble, accepted fetch PC, full-PC repair, EX training\\nevent, retirement continuity/order and absence of correct-prediction bubbles.\\n\\nA mapped-netlist combinational-cone audit checked every D and CE input of\\nall 944 predictor storage flops. None has a resident valid/tag/counter/target\\nbit as a combinational input. This confirms the training feedback path was\\nremoved in the mapped hardware, beyond the source-level read-port check.\\n\\nThe unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench gate, with NRET=1 and\\nBENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv, completed all three routes. Final Fmax values\\nwere 143.84, 145.48 and 152.72 MHz; median 145.48 MHz. Resource usage was\\n6,287 LUT4 and 3,097 FF, within the 10,000-LUT target. The unchanged cosim\\ngate also passed both selftest and CoreMark, including the official CRC,\\nUART, EBREAK, OOB and marker checks. CoreMark used 4,215,937 bracketed cycles\\nfor 10 iterations with the standard random instruction/data stalls.\\n\\nOfficial local fitness on the current divider-based core is 345.07\\niterations/s versus the current winner's 372.62: a 7.394% regression.\\nTimed cycles decreased 6.135%, but median Fmax decreased 13.074%. LUT4 usage\\nincreased 15.612%. The cycle benefit survived; the estimated <=1% Fmax loss\\ndid not. This implementation therefore does not beat the current winner.\\n\\nAll routed critical paths were inspected. Seed 1 has a 6.95 ns ID/EX\\ncontrol-to-EX/MEM path (3.30 ns logic, 3.65 ns routing). Seed 2 has a 6.87 ns\\npath from ID/EX PC through target-alignment/legal-control qualification to\\na predictor target write enable (2.77 ns logic, 4.10 ns routing). Seed 3\\nhas a 6.55 ns path from ID/EX branch control through resolved training\\nqualification to a predictor target write enable (2.57 ns logic, 3.98 ns\\nrouting). None contains a tag-table read followed by tag comparison and\\ntable write enable. Training qualification and enable fanout remain timing\\nlimitations after removing the original feedback path; the mapped-cone\\naudit above confirms there is no hidden resident-state training dependency.\\n\\nThe official checked-in fixture/evaluator use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3 and\\nITERATIONS=10. They were copied unchanged for evaluation. The recorded\\ncurrent winner is 372.62 iterations/s, 167.36 MHz, 4,491,485 bracketed cycles,\\n5,438 LUT4 and 2,094 FF. These are the comparison values from the existing\\nexperiment log, not new measurements of the baseline.\\n\\nSeparately, an unmodified scratch copy built with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 and -O3\\ncompleted 10 iterations in 30,823,120 marker-bracketed cycles, versus the\\nrecorded winner's supplemental 32,836,671 cycles (6.132% fewer). It reported\\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949,\\nthe literal Correct operation validated., both markers, EBREAK and no OOB\\naccess. At the candidate's 145.48 MHz median this is 47.20 iterations/s.\\nThis is supplemental 6 KB evidence, separate from official fitness.\\nThe UART compiler-flags string is hardcoded; the build command used -O3.\\n\\nEvidence directories, all under the authorized TMPDIR:\\n\\n- predictor-formal-mcapvt3h: run.log and formal/last_run-*.log, all per-check logs.\\n- predictor-tests-qlavcwvr: seven runner XML files and Verilator builds.\\n- predictor-fpga-667ih_e0: gate-*.log, training-cone-check.json, synth.json and\\n  cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log plus routed netlists.\\n- predictor-supplemental-6k-gvvrkbrq: build.log, sim.log and result.json.\\n\\nTMPDIR is /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp.\\nOnly cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/test/test_*.py and this explicitly\\nrequested note are changed in the submitted worktree. The orchestrator's\\nacceptance remains independent of these local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T08:12:02.854732Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.39%): Registering BTB hit/counter metadata removed tag-read training feedback, but EX legality/alignment qualification and predictor payload write-enable fanout still cut median Fmax 13.07%, outweighing 6.14% fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core fetches sequentially and discards one younger fetch/decode\\nslot for every normally advancing taken control transfer. LESSONS.md and\\nthe predictor records in experiments/log.jsonl show that the tagged BTBs\\nsaved about 6.1% of timed cycles, while the stateless backward-taken/JAL\\npredictor saved 5.16%; their timing costs prevented beating the winner.\\nIn hyp-20260906-003-r3s2, median Fmax fell from 168.78 to 154.13 MHz, with\\nworst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\\nDirect JAL has a target fully determined by the accepted instruction and\\nits PC. Predicting this transfer alone can remove its bubble without\\nintroducing conditional-direction repair into the existing EX control cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core fetches sequentially and discards one younger fetch/decode\\nslot for every normally advancing taken control transfer. LESSONS.md and\\nthe predictor records in experiments/log.jsonl show that the tagged BTBs\\nsaved about 6.1% of timed cycles, while the stateless backward-taken/JAL\\npredictor saved 5.16%; their timing costs prevented beating the winner.\\nIn hyp-20260906-003-r3s2, median Fmax fell from 168.78 to 154.13 MHz, with\\nworst paths through EX selection, ID/EX reset, and divider operand enables.\\nDirect JAL has a target fully determined by the accepted instruction and\\nits PC. Predicting this transfer alone can remove its bubble without\\nintroducing conditional-direction repair into the existing EX control cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one stateless direct-JAL-only predictor in cores/bench. Predict\\nopcode 0x6f early when the fetch PC is word-aligned and the J-immediate is\\nword-aligned. Predict conditional branches, JALR, and every other opcode\\nsequentially. This is deliberately one narrow predictor, with no BTB,\\ndirection table, history, return stack, or training interface.\\n\\nPredecode raw imem_data independently of flushed instruction output and\\nof stall/redirect controls. JAL needs no funct-field qualification; check\\nPC[1:0] == 0 and instruction bit 21 == 0 before predicting, since that bit\\nis J-immediate bit 1 and bit 0 is implicit zero. Build the sign-extended\\nJ displacement and add it directly to the full 32-bit PC. Keep PC+4\\nindependent and preserve modulo-2^32 arithmetic. There is no B/J immediate\\nselector. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the existing PC register.\\n\\nApply the early target only on the edge that accepts this JAL into ID/EX.\\nPreserve the current PC priority: reset, existing EX redirect, accepted\\nfetch advance, otherwise hold. Early prediction must not flush the JAL\\nitself. Emit an early_jal bit with the IF/ID instruction so decode knows\\nwhich accepted instruction actually caused this PC advance. A held or\\nunavailable fetch must not consume a prediction; changing imem_data during\\na hold must leave the next accepted instruction and its target consistent.\\n\\nAt the ID/EX boundary, register a separate jump_needs_redirect bit equal\\nto dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal. Keep the original ctrl.is_jump and\\nctrl.is_jalr unchanged for architectural execution, link writeback, and\\ntraps. Capture the bit with the instruction and hold it through dmem and\\ndivider stalls. Clear it on reset/flush; the existing invalid-fetch NOP\\ndecodes to zero for this bit. Retain the current ID/EX payload reset and\\ninvalid-fetch policy: do not add an invalid-fetch condition to the entire\\npayload reset or add a new wide flush mux.\\n\\nIn EX, replace only the jump contribution to the existing redirect\\npredicate with the registered jump_needs_redirect bit. Thus redirect is\\nin.valid && (branch_taken || in.jump_needs_redirect) && !misalign_fault.\\nThe branch comparator, redirect target selection, and alignment logic\\notherwise keep their current behavior. An early aligned JAL cannot need\\ntarget or direction repair: the target used at its accepting edge is\\nexactly the PC-relative target EX computes from that same instruction.\\nNo saved target, predicted-next-PC comparator, outcome XOR, or fallback\\nPC+4 repair mux is required. Do not introduce a generic EX-advance or\\ndiv_wait qualification into this redirect cone; retain the winner's\\nexisting redirect/hold semantics for unpredicted branches and JALRs.\\n\\nEX must still execute every predicted JAL normally, write PC+4 to its\\nnonzero destination, and report its resolved target through RVFI pc_next.\\nMisaligned direct transfers are not predicted and retain the existing\\ntrap, suppressed link write, and PC+4 continuation. JALR keeps its current\\nforwarded target computation and bit-zero clearing. Preserve all current\\nload-use bubbles, imem invalidation, dmem holds, retained WB forwarding,\\ndivider protocol, and suppression of wrong-path side effects. A correct\\nearly JAL must also remain correct when held in EX behind an older memory\\nrequest; its already selected fetch target simply stays held.\\n\\nThe estimated net fitness gain is 2%, with low confidence because the\\ntimed direct-JAL frequency and routed timing of this narrower design have\\nnot been measured. The premise is a smaller cycle gain with much less\\ndisturbance to EX control than the previous combined branch/JAL predictor.\\nThe instruction-to-PC adder and placement changes remain timing risks.\\nCount accepted early JALs and marker-window cycles in simulation, then\\nevaluate cycles together with the median of all three FPGA Fmax seeds\\nagainst 375.78 iter/sec; fewer cycles alone do not establish improvement.\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker-window,\\nand FPGA gates. Preserve the nret=1 IO contract, all hard invariants, the\\nwinning divider/result banks, and the direct registered dmem address path.\\nAll evaluator files and other core directories remain outside this change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw-instruction direct-JAL predecode, low-bit alignment checks,\\none full-width J-target adder, and early_jal metadata. Select the\\nearly target only on an accepted fetch, preserving the existing\\nreset/redirect/stall priority and directly registered imem address.\\nLeave every conditional branch and JALR prediction sequential.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add early_jal to if_id_t and jump_needs_redirect to id_ex_t. The\\nlatter is a decode-generated redirect permission, not a prediction\\nbit for a new EX comparison. Preserve all existing architectural\\ncontrol fields, resolved PC fields, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal with the instruction as\\njump_needs_redirect. Preserve the original jump controls for link\\ndata and traps, retain the new bit on holds, and clear it with the\\nexisting reset/flush behavior. Keep the winner's invalid-fetch and\\nwide-payload reset policy to avoid a new high-fanout control path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered jump_needs_redirect bit instead of ctrl.is_jump\\nonly in the redirect predicate. Keep branch resolution, target muxes,\\ntrap qualification, JAL/JALR results, and architectural pc_next as\\nthey are. Add no direction comparison, target comparison, generic\\nEX-advance dependency, or divider-dependent redirect qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused tests for positive/negative/self JALs, displacement\\nextremes, full-address carry/wrap, PC/target alignment, and sequential\\nprediction for all non-JAL opcodes. Verify prediction consumption\\nonly on accepted fetches and priority of reset and older redirects\\nover a younger early JAL, including instruction changes during holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Cover zero-bubble accepted JALs, consecutive and self JALs, x0/x1/x5\\nlink destinations, forwarded use of the link, unpredicted branches\\nand JALRs, and misaligned-JAL trap continuation. Exercise imem stalls,\\nlong older-memory holds, a preceding divider wait, load-use stalls,\\nand an older redirect killing a younger JAL/store/divide. Check the\\naccepted target and decode certificate, exact RVFI PC/order/link\\nvalues, no wrong-path side effects, and all existing assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout and constructors for the new redirect\\npermission bit, setting it to zero for non-jumps. Retain every current\\ndivider, forwarding, result-bank, and registered-address assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw-instruction direct-JAL predecode, low-bit alignment checks,\\none full-width J-target adder, and early_jal metadata. Select the\\nearly target only on an accepted fetch, preserving the existing\\nreset/redirect/stall priority and directly registered imem address.\\nLeave every conditional branch and JALR prediction sequential.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add early_jal to if_id_t and jump_needs_redirect to id_ex_t. The\\nlatter is a decode-generated redirect permission, not a prediction\\nbit for a new EX comparison. Preserve all existing architectural\\ncontrol fields, resolved PC fields, and RVFI widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal with the instruction as\\njump_needs_redirect. Preserve the original jump controls for link\\ndata and traps, retain the new bit on holds, and clear it with the\\nexisting reset/flush behavior. Keep the winner's invalid-fetch and\\nwide-payload reset policy to avoid a new high-fanout control path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered jump_needs_redirect bit instead of ctrl.is_jump\\nonly in the redirect predicate. Keep branch resolution, target muxes,\\ntrap qualification, JAL/JALR results, and architectural pc_next as\\nthey are. Add no direction comparison, target comparison, generic\\nEX-advance dependency, or divider-dependent redirect qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused tests for positive/negative/self JALs, displacement\\nextremes, full-address carry/wrap, PC/target alignment, and sequential\\nprediction for all non-JAL opcodes. Verify prediction consumption\\nonly on accepted fetches and priority of reset and older redirects\\nover a younger early JAL, including instruction changes during holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Cover zero-bubble accepted JALs, consecutive and self JALs, x0/x1/x5\\nlink destinations, forwarded use of the link, unpredicted branches\\nand JALRs, and misaligned-JAL trap continuation. Exercise imem stalls,\\nlong older-memory holds, a preceding divider wait, load-use stalls,\\nand an older redirect killing a younger JAL/store/divide. Check the\\naccepted target and decode certificate, exact RVFI PC/order/link\\nvalues, no wrong-path side effects, and all existing assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout and constructors for the new redirect\\npermission bit, setting it to zero for non-jumps. Retain every current\\ndivider, forwarding, result-bank, and registered-address assertion.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.59, \"delta_pct\": -8.03, \"vs_baseline\": 22.19, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.52, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4442217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5512, \"ff\": 2096, \"seeds\": [164.07, 153.52, 151.88], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis. Final correctness gates pass, but measured fitness\\nregresses: **345.59 iter/sec versus 375.78 (-8.03%)**. The 1.10% reduction in\\ntimed cycles does not offset the 9.04% reduction in median Fmax. This is not a\\nfitness improvement.\\n\\nOnly four RTL files changed: `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`, `id_stage.sv`, and\\n`ex_stage.sv`. IF predecodes raw `imem_data` for opcode 0x6f, aligned PC, and\\ninstruction bit 21 clear. The sign-extended J displacement feeds one full-width\\nPC-relative adder; PC+4 remains independent. Both wrap modulo 2^32.\\n`imem_addr` remains directly assigned from the PC register. Branches, JALR,\\nand all other opcodes predict sequentially.\\n\\nPC updates prioritize reset, the existing EX redirect, and then an accepted\\nfetch (`!stall && !flush`); otherwise PC holds. The target mux selects using\\nraw JAL eligibility, while `early_jal` certifies actual acceptance and is zero\\nduring reset, hold, flush, or redirect. The explicit flush condition also\\nmakes an isolated IF flush hold PC. In the integrated winner, flush already\\nimplies an EX redirect or imem stall, so this adds no new integrated behavior.\\n\\nID/EX registers `dec_is_jump && !in.early_jal` as `jump_needs_redirect`, using\\nthe existing reset/flush and hold policy. Invalid fetches retain the existing\\ndecoded-NOP payload policy; no invalid-fetch-wide reset was added. EX changes\\nonly the jump term in its redirect predicate. Architectural jump controls,\\nPC+4 link results, resolved RVFI targets, alignment traps, and JALR forwarding\\nand bit-zero clearing remain intact. The divider, result banks, registered\\ndmem address, forwarding, hazard unit, retirement logic, and nret=1 IO are\\nunchanged. There is no predictor state, training, saved target, target/direction\\ncomparison, fallback repair mux, or new EX-advance/div_wait qualification.\\n\\nTests add direct IF and ID coverage and extend the existing pipeline and EX\\nresult-bank tests without removing their assertions. Coverage includes signed\\nJAL displacements and extremes, full-address carry/wrap, every non-JAL opcode,\\nalignment, changing instructions during holds, reset/redirect priority,\\nconsecutive/self JALs, x0/x1/x5 links and forwarding, imem stalls, an 18-cycle\\nolder memory hold, preceding divider waits, load-use bubbles, misaligned-JAL\\ncontinuation, forwarded JALR, and older redirects killing younger JAL/store/divide.\\nPipeline tests compare complete RVFI traces against the unchanged ISS and\\ncheck accepted memory transactions and divider launches separately.\\n\\nAn opt-in `test_coremark_profile.py` observes accepted early JALs directly,\\nusing the unchanged ELF and the C++ harness's exact xorshift stall sequence.\\nIt adds no RTL counters and does not replace any evaluator gate. The final\\nprofile matches the unchanged C++ simulation's markers exactly:\\n\\n- Start: 15,665; stop: 4,457,882; timed cycles: **4,442,217**.\\n- Accepted early JALs: **49,629 total; 49,367 inside the marker window**.\\n- EBREAK reached; UART/CRC validation passed; no OOB access.\\n\\nFinal local validation:\\n\\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings.\\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, 10 runners; the opt-in profile is skipped\\n  by default and separately PASS when enabled. Includes real and ALTOPS\\n  arithmetic/result-bank tests and all 25 pipeline cases.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  **PASS, 53 passed, 0 failed**, using the unchanged single-channel wrapper\\n  and configuration. Both local formal invocations passed. No formal\\n  counterexample or formal fix attempt occurred. All final generated checks\\n  have DONE (PASS), and all 14 staged RTL files match the submitted RTL.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: PASS, selftest and CoreMark.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\\n  completed all three seeds, CRC/UART, OOB, and marker-window gates.\\n- `git diff --check`: PASS. No evaluator, other core, or core.yaml edits.\\n\\nFinal routed results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61)\\nand nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, the winner's recorded tools. The median includes\\nall three final seeds:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst reported path | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 164.07 | Divider divisor_q to result_q data | 6.09 | 3.72 / 2.38 |\\n| 2 | 153.52 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.51 | 2.39 / 4.12 |\\n| 3 | 151.88 | ID/EX through control to ID/EX reset | 6.58 | 2.39 / 4.19 |\\n\\nMedian Fmax is **153.52 MHz**, versus 168.78 MHz. Final resources are 5,512\\nLUT4 and 2,096 DFF, versus 5,470 LUT4 and 2,111 DFF. At the measured cycle\\ncount the candidate needs more than **166.93 MHz** to exceed 375.78 iter/sec.\\nThe reported worst paths do not identify the IF J-target adder as limiting;\\nthey are not evidence that every IF, forwarding, or memory path has margin.\\n\\nOne local implementation refinement separated target selection from the\\nacceptance-qualified metadata bit: the first version used `out.early_jal`\\ninside the PC data mux and measured 78.73 MHz median, with long paths to\\nID/EX reset. The final version uses raw predecode in the data mux and\\nqualifies acceptance in the PC update enable. All gates were rerun on that\\nfinal version; the first route's results are archived separately.\\n\\nThe checked-in evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB** CoreMark despite the\\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its unmodified CRC contract is\\nseedcrc=e9f5, crclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\\nA separate **6 KB, -O3** ELF was built using the unchanged benchmark\\nMakefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options. It is supplemental,\\nnot a replacement for official fitness. Both comparisons below use fresh\\nsimulation of starting HEAD and final RTL, identical ELFs, ten iterations,\\nand `--istall --dstall`:\\n\\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Final cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / final iter/sec |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB, -O3 | 4,491,485 | 4,442,217 | 1.10% | 375.78 / 345.59 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB, -O3 | 32,836,671 | 32,485,445 | 1.07% | 51.40 / 47.26 |\\n\\nRates use the winner's recorded 168.78 MHz and the final measured 153.52 MHz.\\nThe 6 KB rate regresses 8.06%. Its starting marker window is\\n(56,393, 32,893,064); the final window is (55,880, 32,541,325). Both 6 KB\\nUARTs have seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47,\\ncrcfinal=5949, ten iterations, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`\\nBoth runs reach EBREAK, have valid markers, and report no OOB access. The\\nUART compiler-description string says -O2; the actual build command is -O3.\\n\\nScratch copies and generated evidence are under\\n`$TMPDIR/early-jal-hove0D` (the run's configured TMPDIR, outside other cores).\\nEvidence includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `profile-tests.log`,\\n`coremark-profile.json`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`,\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`, `timing-summary.json`,\\n`workload-validation.json`, the individual benchmark logs, and\\n`sixk/build.log`. `verified-source-sha256.json` verifies 169 staged RTL,\\ntest, and unchanged contract source files against the worktree. This\\nrequested notes file is at the worktree root per instruction 5.\\nOrchestrator acceptance remains separate from these local results.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T09:29:41.572098Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 (regression, -8.03%): Using acceptance-qualified early_jal as the IF PC mux select pulled stall/flush/redirect controls into the data path; selecting with raw predecode instead recovered median Fmax from 78.73 to 153.52 MHz, but JAL-only prediction's 1.10% CoreMark cycle saving could not cover the remaining 9.04% Fmax loss.\"}\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner still fetches sequentially and loses one useful\\nfetch/decode slot on an advancing taken branch. The predictor records\\nhyp-20260906-003-r1s2 through hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 and LESSONS.md show\\nuseful cycle savings but insufficient timing preservation. The combined\\nstatic backward-branch/JAL predictor saved 5.16% of measured cycles,\\nwhereas JAL alone saved only 1.10%. These separate experiments suggest\\nconditional branches offer the larger opportunity; their difference is\\nan estimate, not a measured branch-only result. Tagged BTBs introduced\\ntraining-enable timing paths, and both static designs exposed paths\\nending at the wide ID/EX payload reset. The next predictor should move\\nprediction-dependent decision work into decode and confine recovery\\nfanout to the small pipeline control bank.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner still fetches sequentially and loses one useful\\nfetch/decode slot on an advancing taken branch. The predictor records\\nhyp-20260906-003-r1s2 through hyp-20260906-003-r4s2 and LESSONS.md show\\nuseful cycle savings but insufficient timing preservation. The combined\\nstatic backward-branch/JAL predictor saved 5.16% of measured cycles,\\nwhereas JAL alone saved only 1.10%. These separate experiments suggest\\nconditional branches offer the larger opportunity; their difference is\\nan estimate, not a measured branch-only result. Tagged BTBs introduced\\ntraining-enable timing paths, and both static designs exposed paths\\nending at the wide ID/EX payload reset. The next predictor should move\\nprediction-dependent decision work into decode and confine recovery\\nfanout to the small pipeline control bank.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one static backward-taken/forward-not-taken conditional-branch\\npredictor with decode-encoded repair polarity and control-only squashes.\\nThe architectural claim is that roughly four percent fewer cycles can\\nsurvive FPGA routing when prediction adds neither a second outcome XOR\\nin the EX repair path nor repair-driven reset of the ID/EX data payload.\\nThis is a refinement of the failed r3s2 control path, not a claim that\\nits already-tested predictor policy alone fixes timing. Keep JAL and\\nJALR sequentially predicted. Add no target table, direction table,\\ntraining mechanism, return stack, or pipeline stage.\\n\\nIn IF, predecode raw imem_data for opcode 0x63 and legal funct3 values\\n0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7. Predict taken only for instruction bit 31 set, aligned\\nfetch PC, and instruction bit 8 clear, which ensures an aligned negative\\nB displacement. Construct only the sign-extended B immediate and one\\nfull-width PC-relative target adder; compute PC+4 independently. Select\\nthe target with raw eligibility, never an acceptance-qualified signal.\\nApply that selection only when the fetch advances into ID/EX. Preserve\\nreset, existing EX redirect, accepted fetch advance, then hold priority.\\nKeep imem_addr directly connected to the PC register and preserve\\nmodulo-2^32 arithmetic. Carry raw predicted_taken alongside the fetch;\\nit has meaning only for the same valid instruction accepted on that edge.\\nA prediction must not flush the branch that produced it.\\n\\nIn ID, retain the architectural decoded branch_op and separately register\\nrepair_invert = dec_branch_op[0] XOR in.predicted_taken. Also register\\nbranch_repair_enable = dec_is_branch AND target_word_aligned, computing\\nalignment from the two-bit sum of in.pc[1:0] and imm[1:0], independent of\\nthe full target adder. Capture predicted_taken, repair_invert, and this\\nenable with the instruction and hold them during pipeline stalls.\\n\\nEX shares the existing forwarded equality, signed-less-than, and\\nunsigned-less-than comparisons. Select base_cond using branch_op[2:1]:\\nequality for 00, signed less-than for 10, unsigned less-than for 11;\\nreserved class 01 is disabled. For legal branches, architectural\\nbranch_cond is base_cond XOR the original branch_op[0], whereas the\\ndirection-mismatch condition is base_cond XOR registered repair_invert.\\nThus predicted direction is folded into the polarity before ID/EX;\\ndo not form architectural branch_cond XOR predicted_taken in the global\\nrepair cone. Keep the original outcome for traps and resolved RVFI PC.\\n\\nThe branch contribution to redirect is branch_repair_enable AND the\\ndirection-mismatch condition. Combine it with the existing legal,\\naligned JAL/JALR redirect contribution and qualify with in.valid. A\\npredicted backward-branch exit repairs to PC+4; an unpredicted taken\\naligned branch repairs to its branch target. Select repair target using\\nregistered predicted_taken and instruction class, independently of the\\nlive comparison result. Correct predictions cause no redirect or bubble.\\nThe prequalified branch enable suppresses redirect for an unpredicted\\nmisaligned target, preserving the current taken-only trap and PC+4\\ncontinuation. JAL/JALR links, JALR bit-zero clearing, jump alignment\\nchecks, and actual pc_next remain architectural, never predicted values.\\nPreserve the winner's existing redirect priority during imem and dmem\\nstalls. A held EX branch may keep asserting the same repair until it\\nadvances; add no div_wait or generic EX-accept dependency to this cone.\\n\\nMake narrow squash distribution an integral part of this predictor's\\nrecovery mechanism. IF should emit raw instruction bits with validity\\nindicating flush/unavailability, instead of placing a redirect-driven\\nNOP mux across the instruction word. In ID/EX, reset still initializes\\nthe complete register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete\\nctrl_t bank, and the three predictor control bits. An invalid advancing\\nfetch likewise produces zero controls and valid=0, regardless of its raw\\nopcode. All PC, operand, immediate, register-address, and instruction\\npayload flops use the ordinary !stall capture enable independently of\\ndynamic flush; they may capture discarded data when a repair advances.\\nDo not reintroduce repair as a wide payload clear, enable, or output mask.\\nDuring holds retain the complete accepted instruction and its metadata;\\npreserve the existing flush-versus-hold priority from hazard_unit.\\nZero bubble controls are essential because hazards and forwarding inspect\\nsome controls without a valid gate. Check that invalid ID/EX bubbles,\\neven with retained load/store/jump/divide payloads, never act as producers,\\nredirect, write, or issue requests.\\nThe existing EX/MEM bubble behavior and retained MEM/WB forwarding data\\nremain intact. This is localized predictor recovery, not an overhaul of\\nthe downstream pipeline or ALU.\\n\\nThe estimated net gain is 3%, with low confidence. A four-percent cycle\\nreduction and one-percent Fmax loss would give approximately 3.1% higher\\nfitness. The current comparison is 382.48 iter/sec, 171.79 MHz, and\\n4,491,485 marker-window cycles; at exactly four-percent fewer cycles the\\ncandidate needs approximately 164.92 MHz just to break even. Static\\ndirection errors, the remaining fetch adder, operand enables, and FPGA\\nplacement could still erase the gain. Measure the actual branch-only\\ncycle reduction and all three routed Fmax seeds; inspect whether the\\nrepair net still reaches wide ID/EX payload reset, CE, or data muxes.\\nTreat the unchanged evaluator as authoritative and keep any supplemental\\n6 KB measurement distinct, as the prior logs document a fixture-size\\ndiscrepancy. Run existing lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB,\\nmarker-window, resource, and three-seed FPGA gates without modification.\\nPreserve nret=1, all hard invariants, divider/result-bank ownership, and\\nthe directly registered dmem address path. Scope is cores/bench only.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw legal backward-branch predecode, a B-immediate-only target\\nadder, raw predicted_taken metadata, and accepted-fetch PC selection.\\nEmit raw instruction payload with validity for flush and imem bubbles\\nso predictor repairs do not mask the instruction word. Keep direct\\nPC-to-imem addressing and existing redirect/stall priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to if_id_t, and predicted_taken, repair_invert,\\nand branch_repair_enable to id_ex_t. Preserve architectural branch\\ncontrols, all resolved PC fields, and every RVFI interface width.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch repair polarity and low-bit target alignment at\\ndecode. Separate ID/EX payload capture from control/valid squashing:\\nreset initializes everything, flush or an advancing invalid fetch\\nzeros controls and predictor bits, and data capture depends only on\\nthe ordinary stall enable. Keep the complete instruction on holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Share forwarded comparison primitives between architectural branch\\nresolution and the independently registered repair polarity. Use\\ndecode-certified branch alignment in the redirect contribution and\\npredicted_taken for fallthrough-versus-target recovery. Preserve\\nactual branch outcomes, trap/link/RVFI behavior, existing jump\\nredirects, divider protocol, result banks, and registered mem_addr.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused IF tests for every legal/reserved branch funct3, backward\\nand forward offsets, target alignment, full-width carry/wrap, and\\nsequential prediction of jumps and other opcodes. Check raw payload\\nvalidity, accepted prediction correspondence, reset/repair priority,\\nchanging instructions during holds, and unavailable instruction data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline tests for all six branch conditions and both outcomes,\\nloop exits, zero-bubble correct predictions, forwarded/load-dependent\\noperands, misaligned traps, and unchanged JAL/JALR behavior. Exercise\\nrepair during long dmem holds, imem stalls, and divider waits; killed\\nraw loads/stores/divides; consecutive repairs; and reset. Preserve\\narchitectural assertions and check control-zero bubbles, held metadata,\\nexact RVFI order/PC/traps, accepted stores, and divider launches against\\nthe unchanged ISS. Add directed ID checks that squash does not govern\\npayload capture while zero controls prevent all invalid side effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout for the three added predictor bits,\\ndefaulting them to zero in ordinary ALU/divider/jump constructors.\\nRetain every result-bank, divider, forwarding, reset, and directly\\nregistered memory-address assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw legal backward-branch predecode, a B-immediate-only target\\nadder, raw predicted_taken metadata, and accepted-fetch PC selection.\\nEmit raw instruction payload with validity for flush and imem bubbles\\nso predictor repairs do not mask the instruction word. Keep direct\\nPC-to-imem addressing and existing redirect/stall priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to if_id_t, and predicted_taken, repair_invert,\\nand branch_repair_enable to id_ex_t. Preserve architectural branch\\ncontrols, all resolved PC fields, and every RVFI interface width.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch repair polarity and low-bit target alignment at\\ndecode. Separate ID/EX payload capture from control/valid squashing:\\nreset initializes everything, flush or an advancing invalid fetch\\nzeros controls and predictor bits, and data capture depends only on\\nthe ordinary stall enable. Keep the complete instruction on holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Share forwarded comparison primitives between architectural branch\\nresolution and the independently registered repair polarity. Use\\ndecode-certified branch alignment in the redirect contribution and\\npredicted_taken for fallthrough-versus-target recovery. Preserve\\nactual branch outcomes, trap/link/RVFI behavior, existing jump\\nredirects, divider protocol, result banks, and registered mem_addr.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused IF tests for every legal/reserved branch funct3, backward\\nand forward offsets, target alignment, full-width carry/wrap, and\\nsequential prediction of jumps and other opcodes. Check raw payload\\nvalidity, accepted prediction correspondence, reset/repair priority,\\nchanging instructions during holds, and unavailable instruction data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend pipeline tests for all six branch conditions and both outcomes,\\nloop exits, zero-bubble correct predictions, forwarded/load-dependent\\noperands, misaligned traps, and unchanged JAL/JALR behavior. Exercise\\nrepair during long dmem holds, imem stalls, and divider waits; killed\\nraw loads/stores/divides; consecutive repairs; and reset. Preserve\\narchitectural assertions and check control-zero bubbles, held metadata,\\nexact RVFI order/PC/traps, accepted stores, and divider launches against\\nthe unchanged ISS. Add directed ID checks that squash does not govern\\npayload capture while zero controls prevent all invalid side effects.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update the packed ID/EX layout for the three added predictor bits,\\ndefaulting them to zero in ordinary ALU/divider/jump constructors.\\nRetain every result-bank, divider, forwarding, reset, and directly\\nregistered memory-address assertion.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 338.91, \"delta_pct\": -11.39, \"vs_baseline\": 19.83, \"fmax_mhz\": 146.05, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5602, \"ff\": 2106, \"seeds\": [153.21, 138.68, 146.05], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis. All local correctness gates pass. The branch-only\\npredictor reduces the unchanged evaluator's marker-window cycles by **4.05%**,\\nbut median routed Fmax falls from 171.79 to **146.05 MHz**. Fitness is\\n**338.91 iter/sec**, versus 382.48 for the starting winner (**-11.39%**).\\nThe proposed timing-preservation claim did not survive routing.\\n\\nIF predecodes raw opcode 0x63 and funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7. Negative displacement,\\ninstruction bit 8 clear, and aligned PC qualify static taken prediction.\\nOne sign-extended B immediate and full-width target adder are independent of\\nPC+4; the target mux uses raw eligibility. PC priority is reset, existing EX\\nredirect, accepted fetch (`!stall && !flush`), then hold. All arithmetic wraps\\nmodulo 2^32 and `imem_addr` remains directly connected to the PC register.\\nJAL/JALR and all other opcodes predict sequentially. Raw `predicted_taken`\\ntravels with raw instruction bits, with validity identifying discarded or\\nunavailable fetches. Predictions do not flush their producing branch.\\n\\nID retains architectural `branch_op` and registers the prediction, its\\ndecode-folded repair polarity, and legal-branch target alignment. Alignment\\nuses only the two-bit sum of PC and immediate low bits. Reset clears the\\ncomplete ID/EX register. Dynamic flush clears only valid, the complete control\\nbank, and all three predictor bits. An advancing invalid fetch also clears\\nthose controls. Payload capture depends only on ordinary `!stall`, including\\non repair edges; a load-use flush during a hold retains payload and clears\\ncontrols. The existing hazard priority preserves the complete accepted\\ninstruction during dmem/divider holds.\\n\\nEX shares forwarded equality/signed-less/unsigned-less comparisons and selects\\ntheir base condition with architectural `branch_op[2:1]`. The architectural\\noutcome uses original polarity; repair uses the registered polarity directly,\\nwithout a second architectural-outcome XOR with prediction. Decode-qualified\\nbranch alignment gates the repair contribution. Predicted branch exits repair\\nto PC+4; unpredicted taken branches repair to the branch target. Recovery target\\nselection is independent of the live comparison. Jump redirects, taken-only\\nalignment traps, JAL/JALR link values, JALR bit-zero clearing, and resolved RVFI\\nPC values remain architectural. No EX-accept or div_wait qualifier was added\\nto redirect; held branches continue asserting their repair until advancement.\\n\\nThe functional changes are confined to `core_pkg.sv`, `if_stage.sv`,\\n`id_stage.sv`, and `ex_stage.sv`. `core.sv` and `hazard_unit.sv` have comment-only\\nupdates to describe raw invalid payloads and control squashes. The divider,\\nALU, result banks, downstream bubbles, retained MEM/WB forwarding data,\\ndirectly registered memory-address path, nret=1 interface, decoder, and all\\nevaluator contracts are unchanged. No predictor table, training, return stack,\\nor additional pipeline stage was added.\\n\\nTests update the EX constructor's packed layout, defaulting the three added\\nbits to zero. New IF tests cover all eight funct3 values, displacement bits\\nand extremes, alignment, full-width carry/wrap, every non-branch opcode,\\nchanging held/unavailable instruction words, raw metadata, reset, and redirect\\npriority. New ID tests directly check the two-bit alignment sum, both repair\\npolarities, full metadata holds, reset, and independent payload capture during\\nflush and invalid advances. Pipeline tests preserve every original assertion\\nand compare complete retirement streams against the unchanged Python ISS.\\nThey cover all six branch conditions in both directions and outcomes, exact\\nzero-bubble prediction and one-slot repair timing, loop exits, forwarding and\\nload dependencies, branches held behind divides, 18-cycle memory holds,\\nimem stalls with changing raw data, consecutive repairs, reset during repair,\\nkilled loads/stores/divides/jumps/branches, reserved encodings, misalignment\\ntraps, and unchanged jump links. Per-cycle assertions check zero bubble\\ncontrols, metadata/payload capture, fetch priority, accepted memory requests,\\ndivider launches, result-bank ownership, and RVFI PC/order continuity.\\n\\nLocal validation used scratch copies under the configured TMPDIR, avoiding\\ngenerated writes to protected evaluator directories in the submitted tree.\\nThe available Conda Python environment supplied cocotb, pytest, YAML, and\\nclick; the default Python lacked cocotb/click. No evaluator or dependency\\nsource was modified.\\n\\n- Requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: **PASS**, no warnings.\\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: **PASS**, all 10 runners, including real and\\n  ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank checks. The final expanded pipeline runner\\n  was rerun after adding fetch-priority and additional hold coverage:\\n  **26 pipeline cases passed**. Final XML totals are 145 cocotb cases,\\n  zero failures across the 10 runners.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=4 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  **PASS: 53 passed, 0 failed**. The unchanged script auto-selected nret=1\\n  wrappers/checks. One local formal invocation; zero formal fix attempts,\\n  no counterexample. Every staged RTL source matches the final worktree.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: **PASS**, selftest and CoreMark.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`:\\n  **PASS** for all three placements and the CRC/UART, OOB, marker-window\\n  and resource evaluation; fitness regresses as reported above.\\n- `git diff --check`: **PASS**. SHA-256 evidence verifies 328 staged source,\\n  test, and contract files against the worktree. No other core or core.yaml\\n  was edited. The explicitly requested notes file is at the worktree root.\\n\\nRouted results use Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61).\\nEvery reported frequency below is the final post-route value from the\\nunchanged nextpnr seed script, with no excluded seed.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Worst endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 153.21 | ID/EX ctrl.is_branch RESET | 6.53 | 2.84 / 3.69 |\\n| 2 | 138.68 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[2] RESET | 7.21 | 2.67 / 4.54 |\\n| 3 | 146.05 | ID/EX valid RESET | 6.85 | 2.84 / 4.01 |\\n\\nThe worst paths start at the FPGA wrapper's LFSR instruction source and pass\\nthrough the raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold control logic before the\\nnarrow ID/EX reset bank. The mapped netlist has 160 physical ID/EX payload\\nflops reset only by the top-level reset and 24 physical control/valid/predictor\\nflops using the dynamic reset. A combinational fanout traversal from registered\\n`repair_invert` and `branch_repair_enable` reaches those 24 RESET pins and the\\nvalid flop's D input, **no payload RESET, CE, or D pin**. All 184 physical\\nID/EX flops share the ordinary stall-derived CE, which is outside that repair\\nfanout. Synthesis can merge duplicated bundle bits, hence physical counts are\\nsmaller than the 201-bit logical bundle. This verifies narrow recovery\\ndistribution, but does not establish timing margin for other paths.\\n\\nResources are **5,602 LUT4 and 2,106 DFF**, versus the winner's 5,513 LUT4 and\\n2,087 DFF. Four DPX9B block RAMs and one MULT36X36 remain; the memory-address\\nbank has not been replaced by the divider-result mux. At 4,309,463 cycles,\\nbreak-even Fmax against the winner is **164.83 MHz**, well above 146.05 MHz.\\n\\nThe unchanged evaluator builds aggregate **2 KB, -O3** CoreMark despite the\\nprompt's aggregate 6 KB description. Its CRC contract is seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf. This evaluator\\nresult is authoritative. A separately built aggregate **6 KB, -O3** ELF\\nuses the unchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options\\nand is supplemental only. Both comparisons use fresh baseline-HEAD and\\ncandidate simulations, identical ELFs, ten iterations, and `--istall --dstall`.\\n\\n| Workload | Starting cycles | Candidate cycles | Cycle reduction | Starting / candidate iter/sec |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Evaluator 2 KB | 4,491,485 | 4,309,463 | 4.05% | 382.48 / 338.91 |\\n| Supplemental 6 KB | 32,836,671 | 31,436,060 | 4.27% | 52.32 / 46.46 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB start/stop markers are baseline (15,801, 4,507,286) and candidate\\n(15,406, 4,324,869). The 6 KB markers are baseline (56,393, 32,893,064) and\\ncandidate (54,555, 31,490,615). Both 6 KB UARTs report seedcrc=8a02,\\ncrclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, ten iterations,\\nand `Correct operation validated.` All four fresh runs reach EBREAK with\\nvalid markers and no OOB access. UART's compiler-description string says\\n-O2; the actual recorded build commands use -O3. Rates use the winner's\\nrecorded 171.79 MHz and the candidate's measured median 146.05 MHz.\\n\\nEvidence is under:\\n`$TMPDIR/branch-repair-r5s2-93q3k2v2/`.\\nIt includes `formal-output.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `pipeline-final.log`,\\n`cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed*/nextpnr.log`,\\n`repair-fanout.json`, `workload-validation.json`, individual baseline/candidate\\nbenchmark logs, `sixk/build.log`, and `verified-source-sha256.json`.\\nLocal correctness success is separate from orchestrator acceptance.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T10:05:37.270079Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r5s2 (regression, -11.39%): Control-only ID/EX squashes and decode-folded repair polarity do not guarantee predictor timing: raw-instruction load-use/divider-hold paths to control-bank RESET remained critical, lowering median Fmax from 171.79 to 146.05 MHz and outweighing branch-only prediction's 4.05% CoreMark cycle saving.\"}\n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Backward prediction with asymmetric registered exit recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior backward-branch predictors consistently saved about 4.05% of the\\nhistorical evaluator's cycles, but their direction-mismatch recovery\\npaths reduced routed Fmax. In r9s2 and r10s2, forwarding and branch\\ncomparison fed recovery and ID-control RESET; replacing carry chains\\nwith a Boolean comparison tree made timing worse. The validity-only\\ndesign in r8s2 also failed to preserve frequency. Moving every repair\\nto MEM in r6s2 failed the unchanged liveness check. The useful next\\nexperiment is to register only the new predicted-taken exit recovery,\\nretaining the winner's immediate recovery for unpredicted transfers.\\nThis limits the additional miss cycle to backward branches that exit.\\nHistorical logs disclose a 2 KB evaluator fixture, so their cycle\\npercentages are supporting evidence, not measured 6 KB predictions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior backward-branch predictors consistently saved about 4.05% of the\\nhistorical evaluator's cycles, but their direction-mismatch recovery\\npaths reduced routed Fmax. In r9s2 and r10s2, forwarding and branch\\ncomparison fed recovery and ID-control RESET; replacing carry chains\\nwith a Boolean comparison tree made timing worse. The validity-only\\ndesign in r8s2 also failed to preserve frequency. Moving every repair\\nto MEM in r6s2 failed the unchanged liveness check. The useful next\\nexperiment is to register only the new predicted-taken exit recovery,\\nretaining the winner's immediate recovery for unpredicted transfers.\\nThis limits the additional miss cycle to backward branches that exit.\\nHistorical logs disclose a 2 KB evaluator fixture, so their cycle\\npercentages are supporting evidence, not measured 6 KB predictions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one static backward-taken conditional-branch predictor with\\nasymmetric recovery. Correct predictions remain bubble-free. An\\nunpredicted taken branch or an ordinary JAL/JALR retains the existing\\none-slot EX redirect. Only a predicted-taken branch that resolves not\\ntaken uses a registered recovery pulse and discards two younger slots.\\nKeep JAL and JALR sequentially predicted. Add no target table, history\\ntable, custom branch comparator, or arithmetic optimization.\\n\\nIF predecodes raw imem_data for BRANCH opcode 0x63 and legal funct3\\nvalues 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, and 7. Predict taken only when instruction bit 31\\nis set, the fetch PC is word aligned, and instruction bit 8 is clear.\\nForm the exact sign-extended B immediate and add it to the full 32-bit\\nPC modulo 2^32. Compute PC+4 independently. The raw eligibility bit\\nselects these two addresses without stall, flush, or acceptance in\\nthat data select. Only an accepted fetch advances this prediction;\\nreset and recovery retain priority. Keep imem_addr directly driven\\nby the PC register. A prediction never squashes its own instruction.\\n\\nCarry predicted_taken with the actual accepted instruction into ID/EX.\\nID also registers immediate_branch = decoded_is_branch AND NOT\\npredicted_taken. Keep architectural ctrl.is_branch and branch_op\\nunchanged. Both new metadata bits belong to the ordinary payload\\ncapture/hold domain; invalid metadata is inert. Preserve the winner's\\ncontrol-zero bubbles and separation of payload capture from flush.\\n\\nPreserve the existing forwarded branch_cond computation and all six\\narchitectural branch predicates. The immediate EX redirect uses\\nimmediate_branch AND branch_cond in the existing taken-branch term,\\nwith the existing jump term, validity and alignment/trap rules.\\nPrediction suppression is already encoded in the registered branch\\nenable: do not put an outcome XOR prediction, inverted repair polarity,\\nfull next-PC comparison, or late-repair acceptance logic into this\\nimmediate recovery cone. Existing held-owner and imem-stall redirect\\nbehavior remains valid for this immediate path.\\n\\nIndependently register exit_pending when a valid, legal predicted\\nbranch advances from EX with branch_cond false. Use the existing\\nEX/MEM resolved pc_next as its registered recovery address, which is\\nPC+4 for this outcome. Capture pc_next with the normal payload enable;\\nthe late condition must not enable a wide target bank. exit_pending\\nis a one-cycle event, cleared on reset and on every non-event clock,\\nincluding memory holds and an older exit recovery. Generate it only\\nwhen EX really advances, never repeatedly from a held owner. A held\\npredicted branch keeps its instruction, operands and metadata until\\nadvancement. A correctly taken prediction generates neither event.\\nEligible predictions are certified aligned direct branches, so this\\ndelayed path cannot belong to a load, store, divide, jump or trap.\\n\\nOn the following cycle exit_pending refers to the older branch now\\nin EX/MEM. Its registered target takes priority over every younger\\nEX redirect and fetch prediction, even if imem is unavailable. Kill\\nthe younger ID/EX owner before it can enter EX/MEM, and flush the\\nyounger combinational fetch slot. Force the killed EX/MEM controls\\nand validity to zero. Suppress a killed instruction's divider request,\\nresponse acceptance, effective div_wait and new exit event. The older\\nbranch advances into MEM/WB normally except for the actual commit\\ntiming described below. Do not reset or overwrite the divider result\\nbank. A younger divide must be killed before its first request edge;\\nit cannot already be active when this branch's exit event first appears.\\nMake the registered exit kill override younger load-use/divider holds.\\nKeep dmem-stall generation independent of recovery. The recovering\\nbranch itself has no memory operation, so it cannot require a dmem hold.\\n\\nMake the delayed recovery's commit schedule part of the architecture.\\nA two-slot repair followed immediately by the unchanged eight-compute-\\nclock divider can otherwise produce an eleven-clock retirement gap.\\nOn the registered-exit recovery edge, MEM/WB captures the real branch\\nrecord with valid cleared and sets a one-bit commit_pending flag.\\nOn the next edge, retain that complete record, set its valid bit once,\\nand clear commit_pending. This is an actual one-clock postponement of\\nwriteback/retirement, using the refill bubble already present in MEM;\\nit must not delay the corrected fetch or target instruction execution.\\nThe intervening EX/MEM entry is necessarily the bubble injected by\\nexit recovery, so holding MEM/WB discards no legitimate instruction.\\nReturn to ordinary MEM/WB capture on the next edge. Clear the pending\\nflag on reset and preserve the existing dmem-hold forwarding behavior.\\n\\nApply that commit schedule only to the delayed-exit owner, never to\\nan ordinary EX redirect, a correctly predicted branch, a load/store,\\nor DIV/REM. Regfile write enable and every RVFI field must continue\\nto describe the same actual MEM/WB owner and valid cycle; there is no\\nfabricated retirement or RVFI-only timing change. With zero wait,\\nthe delayed branch commits during refill and its immediately following\\nDIV/REM should retire within ten clocks of it. This must pass the\\nexisting formal liveness check unchanged. Check both the predecessor\\nto branch and branch to successor gaps, not just the latter. Do not\\nshorten the divider, change ALTOPS timing, or relax the formal bound\\nto rescue this predictor if the proposed schedule proves incorrect.\\n\\nThe physical hypothesis is that the new inverted branch-outcome path\\nterminates at one exit-event flop. It no longer directly drives IF\\nmasking, ID-control RESET, or PC recovery. The ordinary taken-redirect\\ncone retains its existing predicate polarity and registered enable;\\nthe added late recovery arrives from a register. Inspect all three\\nrouted seeds and mapped connectivity to verify this distinction,\\nincluding exit-event D, immediate redirect, kill/commit controls and\\nthe IF target adder. Retiming the exit path can still shift the worst\\npath elsewhere, so this is a low-confidence net-fitness experiment.\\n\\nForecast a 3% fitness gain. The historical single-entry r7s2 recorded\\n12,211 certified exits, suggesting that exit-only extra bubbles can\\nbe much fewer than all unpredicted-transfer bubbles; that count is\\nnot an exact prediction for this stateless implementation. Measure\\naccepted correct predictions, registered exits, immediate repairs,\\nmarker-window cycles and three-seed median Fmax on the identical\\nworkload. A measured 3.5% cycle reduction, for example, only breaks\\neven at about 177.62 MHz against the current 184.06 MHz winner.\\nReport fitness from unrounded iterations/cycle, not rounded metadata.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1, every external port, strict decoding, taken-only\\nalignment traps, suppressed trapped jump links, actual RVFI pc_next,\\nmonotonic single retirement, all arithmetic and result-bank ownership,\\nretained WB forwarding, and the directly registered memory address.\\nRun unchanged lint, unit, formal including liveness, ISS cosim,\\nCRC/UART, OOB, marker-window, resource and three-seed FPGA gates.\\nIdentify the actual evaluated working set if it differs from the\\nprompt without modifying the evaluation contract. Only bench RTL\\nand its permitted cocotb tests are implementation targets.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to IF/ID and ID/EX, and immediate_branch to\\nID/EX. Document accepted-instruction ownership and inert metadata\\non bubbles. Reuse existing EX/MEM pc_next for registered exit\\nrecovery; preserve architectural controls and downstream RVFI data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw aligned backward-branch prediction and full-width target\\ncalculation with independent PC+4. Export consumed prediction\\nmetadata and retain accepted-fetch PC enables, direct imem address,\\nreset priority and discarded-fetch invalidation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register prediction and the decode-folded immediate branch enable\\nwith payload capture. Preserve architectural branch controls,\\nzero-control bubbles, independent payload enables and full owner\\nretention across memory/divider holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep the existing comparator and immediate redirect topology,\\nsubstituting the registered immediate_branch enable for its branch\\nterm. Register the once-per-advance predicted-exit event separately\\nand expose registered pc_next as its target. Add older-exit kill\\nthat bubbles the younger EX/MEM owner and suppresses all younger\\ndivider actions and exit events without resetting result banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire separate immediate EX and registered exit recovery signals.\\nGive the older exit target priority, deliver its kill to EX, and\\nidentify the delayed branch's MEM/WB capture for commit scheduling.\\nPreserve all top-level IO and existing RVFI/writeback ownership.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate registered exit recovery so it squashes younger decode\\nand execute work despite a younger load-use/divider hold. Preserve\\nordinary EX redirect behavior, independent dmem holds and retained\\nWB forwarding; avoid any recovery-to-dmem-stall combinational loop.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one-clock real commit deferral only for the branch producing a\\nregistered exit recovery. Capture its complete MEM/WB payload with\\nvalid zero, retain it through the guaranteed following MEM bubble,\\nthen present it valid exactly once. Reset cancels the pending owner.\\nPreserve ordinary capture, dmem hold, load/store and forwarding rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Test branch eligibility, all legal/reserved funct3 encodings,\\ninstruction bit-eight alignment, full-address carry/wrap, raw\\nselection independence, exact accepted metadata, stalled instruction\\nchanges, reset and priority of recovery over a younger prediction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed bundles and verify the immediate-branch enable,\\narchitectural branch controls, prediction association, independent\\npayload capture, control-zero flushes and retained held owners.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update ID/EX packing and initialize the added kill input in existing\\ntests. Preserve every real/ALTOPS arithmetic, forwarding and direct\\naddress assertion. Check immediate versus registered recovery,\\nsingle exit events across holds, killed exit generation, and killed\\ndivider requests without completion-bank corruption.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify all six branch predicates with ALU/load-forwarded operands,\\nwarm backedges, exits, forward taken branches, JAL/JALR, illegal and\\nmisaligned transfers against the unchanged ISS. Check zero-bubble\\ncorrect prediction, one-slot immediate repair and two-slot exit\\nrepair. Observe accepted memory requests and divider launches to\\nreject wrong-path stores, loads, divisions and duplicated owners.\\nCover exit recovery over younger jumps, load-use and DIV/REM; long\\nolder dmem holds; unavailable imem; consecutive exits; and reset\\nduring exit or pending commit. Check the real branch commit is\\ndeferred exactly once into a MEM bubble with matching WB/RVFI data,\\nno lost successor, PC continuity and strict order. For every exit\\nbranch condition followed by each DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, assert the\\nunchanged zero-wait ten-clock liveness limit on both neighboring\\nretirement gaps. Retain and run every existing assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted_taken to IF/ID and ID/EX, and immediate_branch to\\nID/EX. Document accepted-instruction ownership and inert metadata\\non bubbles. Reuse existing EX/MEM pc_next for registered exit\\nrecovery; preserve architectural controls and downstream RVFI data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add raw aligned backward-branch prediction and full-width target\\ncalculation with independent PC+4. Export consumed prediction\\nmetadata and retain accepted-fetch PC enables, direct imem address,\\nreset priority and discarded-fetch invalidation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register prediction and the decode-folded immediate branch enable\\nwith payload capture. Preserve architectural branch controls,\\nzero-control bubbles, independent payload enables and full owner\\nretention across memory/divider holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep the existing comparator and immediate redirect topology,\\nsubstituting the registered immediate_branch enable for its branch\\nterm. Register the once-per-advance predicted-exit event separately\\nand expose registered pc_next as its target. Add older-exit kill\\nthat bubbles the younger EX/MEM owner and suppresses all younger\\ndivider actions and exit events without resetting result banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire separate immediate EX and registered exit recovery signals.\\nGive the older exit target priority, deliver its kill to EX, and\\nidentify the delayed branch's MEM/WB capture for commit scheduling.\\nPreserve all top-level IO and existing RVFI/writeback ownership.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate registered exit recovery so it squashes younger decode\\nand execute work despite a younger load-use/divider hold. Preserve\\nordinary EX redirect behavior, independent dmem holds and retained\\nWB forwarding; avoid any recovery-to-dmem-stall combinational loop.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one-clock real commit deferral only for the branch producing a\\nregistered exit recovery. Capture its complete MEM/WB payload with\\nvalid zero, retain it through the guaranteed following MEM bubble,\\nthen present it valid exactly once. Reset cancels the pending owner.\\nPreserve ordinary capture, dmem hold, load/store and forwarding rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Test branch eligibility, all legal/reserved funct3 encodings,\\ninstruction bit-eight alignment, full-address carry/wrap, raw\\nselection independence, exact accepted metadata, stalled instruction\\nchanges, reset and priority of recovery over a younger prediction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed bundles and verify the immediate-branch enable,\\narchitectural branch controls, prediction association, independent\\npayload capture, control-zero flushes and retained held owners.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update ID/EX packing and initialize the added kill input in existing\\ntests. Preserve every real/ALTOPS arithmetic, forwarding and direct\\naddress assertion. Check immediate versus registered recovery,\\nsingle exit events across holds, killed exit generation, and killed\\ndivider requests without completion-bank corruption.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify all six branch predicates with ALU/load-forwarded operands,\\nwarm backedges, exits, forward taken branches, JAL/JALR, illegal and\\nmisaligned transfers against the unchanged ISS. Check zero-bubble\\ncorrect prediction, one-slot immediate repair and two-slot exit\\nrepair. Observe accepted memory requests and divider launches to\\nreject wrong-path stores, loads, divisions and duplicated owners.\\nCover exit recovery over younger jumps, load-use and DIV/REM; long\\nolder dmem holds; unavailable imem; consecutive exits; and reset\\nduring exit or pending commit. Check the real branch commit is\\ndeferred exactly once into a MEM bubble with matching WB/RVFI data,\\nno lost successor, PC continuity and strict order. For every exit\\nbranch condition followed by each DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, assert the\\nunchanged zero-wait ten-clock liveness limit on both neighboring\\nretirement gaps. Retain and run every existing assertion.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 389.25, \"delta_pct\": -5.01, \"vs_baseline\": 37.63, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.75, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4360895, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5675, \"ff\": 2099, \"seeds\": [174.98, 169.75, 167.11], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Backward prediction with asymmetric registered exit recovery\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis in bench RTL and the permitted bench tests. Correctness gates pass; measured fitness regresses by **5.0128868324%**, so the forecast of a 3% gain is not supported by this experiment.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- IF recognizes only opcode 0x63, funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7, negative B immediates, an aligned PC, and instruction bit 8 clear. A raw eligibility bit selects the full-width PC+B-immediate sum or an independently computed PC+4. Acceptance only controls the PC enable; reset and recovery have priority. The memory address remains the PC register itself.\\n- Accepted prediction metadata and the decode-folded `immediate_branch` enable use ID/EX's ordinary payload capture/hold domain. Architectural branch controls, control-zero bubbles, and the separate payload/flush domains remain intact.\\n- EX retains the original forwarded comparator and all six predicates. Its immediate redirect uses the positive `immediate_branch && branch_cond` term and the existing jump/alignment rules. JAL/JALR remain sequentially predicted. Ordinary held-owner and imem-stall recovery behavior is preserved.\\n- The separate `exit_pending` flop captures a legal predicted branch's false outcome only when EX advances. Every other clock clears the event, including a memory hold and an older exit kill. Its address is the existing EX/MEM `pc_next` payload bank, captured with the normal payload enable.\\n- Registered exit recovery wins over younger redirects and predictions, flushes fetch/ID, and clears the killed EX/MEM controls/validity. It overrides younger load-use/divider holds and suppresses divider request, response acceptance, effective wait, and another exit event. Divider arithmetic and completion-bank contents are not reset by recovery.\\n- MEM/WB captures the real exit branch with valid zero and `commit_pending` set on the recovery edge. The next edge retains its complete record and presents it valid once, consuming the guaranteed MEM bubble. Corrected fetch and target execution continue normally. Ordinary MEM/WB capture then resumes; retained forwarding during dmem holds is unchanged.\\n\\nRelative to the edge that registers the exit event: recovery is edge +1, the real branch is valid in MEM/WB at +2, an immediately following divide launches at +3, accepts its response at +11, and becomes valid in MEM/WB at +12. The branch-to-divider retirement gap is ten clocks. Directed tests measure a predecessor-to-branch gap of two clocks, or three with the preceding load-use interlock.\\n\\nNo changes were made to the comparator, decoder, arithmetic, divider latency/ALTOPS timing, external ports, `nret: 1`, or evaluation contracts. The implementation uses the existing module boundaries. The additional CoreMark measurement test generates a read-only model observer in its build directory and includes the original `test/cosim/main.cpp`; it asserts byte-for-byte stdout equality against the unchanged evaluator executable.\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\nAll generated builds and evaluation copies are under `$TMPDIR/r11s2-validation`. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the formal runner writes its staging directories beneath its own source tree. The evaluated RTL was byte-compared with the delivered RTL.\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: pass, zero warnings/errors. Channel-0 RVFI contract lint: pass.\\n- `COREMARK_PROFILE=1 make test TARGET=bench`: **11 pytest runners passed**, including real/ALTOPS ALU and EX-bank suites, decoder, IF, ID, immediates, regfile, the CoreMark observer, and all **27 pipeline tests**. Existing assertions remain in use.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including the unchanged liveness, unique, PC-forward/backward, register, and instruction checks. Local formal status: **pass on first attempt; zero RTL fixes/retries; no counterexample**.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: pass, **2 ELFs** (selftest ISS comparison and CoreMark CRC validation). Directed pipeline programs also compare architectural retirements against the unchanged Python ISS and separately compare accepted memory requests and divider launches.\\n- Coverage includes 48 combinations of all six exit predicates, all four DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU successors, and ALU/load operand forwarding. Every zero-wait adjacent retirement gap is at most ten clocks; each exit-to-divider gap is exactly ten. Correct predictions have zero EX bubbles, ordinary repairs one, and exits two.\\n- Additional coverage includes wrong-path loads/stores/divides, younger JAL/JALR priority, younger load-use dependencies, long older memory holds, unavailable imem during recovery, consecutive exits, illegal/misaligned transfers, and reset during either the exit event or pending commit. The complete deferred WB record, actual RVFI owner, strict order, PC continuity, and retained result banks are checked.\\n- CoreMark reaches EBREAK, reports `oob:false`, emits both markers, passes every unchanged CRC check, and prints `Correct operation validated.`\\n- Unchanged synthesis and all three nextpnr seeds complete normally. Resources: **5,675 LUT4 / 20,736**, **2,099 DFF / 15,552**. Synthesis emits only the eight existing divider-array lowering notices; Verilator lint is clean.\\n\\nAn added event-count assertion initially included a combinational offer on the terminal EBREAK observation cycle, where the test performs no further clock edge. The test now excludes that unaccepted offer. This required no RTL or ISS change.\\n\\n## Actual workload and measurements\\n\\nThis checkout evaluates **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000**, **ITERATIONS=10**, compiled with **-O3**, with the unchanged default xorshift stall sequence (`--bench --istall --dstall`, seed 0xDEADBEEF). It is not the prompt's 6 KB workload. UART reports a 2K run and per-algorithm size 666. Its compiler-flags banner is stale (`-O2`); the actual unchanged Makefile invocation uses `-O3`. The fixture and validators were not altered.\\n\\nCoreMark ELF SHA-256: `574418d8f0933e2dc4adfe05aba090176d3af951ba897dc5d03cd34718af4fd4`.\\n\\nValidated CRCs: seed `e9f5`, list `e714`, matrix `1fd7`, state `8e3a`, final `fcaf`, matching this checkout's 2 KB evaluator.\\n\\n| Event | Whole program | Marker window [start, stop) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Accepted predicted fetches | 288,234 | 287,132 |\\n| Accepted correct predictions (valid EX advancement) | 236,174 | 235,191 |\\n| Registered exit recoveries | 52,060 | 51,941 |\\n| Immediate repairs (advancing, surviving owner) | 123,153 | 122,336 |\\n\\nResolution counts exclude repeated offers during holds and younger killed owners; the fetch count records every accepted eligible fetch. The instrumented run's complete evaluator stdout, including marker cycles and UART, is identical to the ordinary run.\\n\\n| Measurement | HEAD predecessor | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Start marker cycle | 15,801 | 15,490 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,507,286 | 4,376,385 |\\n| Counted cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,360,895 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.06 (declared winner) | 169.75 (measured) |\\n| Fitness, iterations/second | 409.79765044300495 | 389.25495798454216 |\\n\\nThe predecessor RTL was rebuilt from this worktree's HEAD into scratch and run with the identical ELF, harness, and stall flags. Its frequency is the existing winner's declared 184.06 MHz; the candidate's routed seeds are **174.98, 169.75, 167.11 MHz**.\\n\\nCycle reduction: **2.9075016392%**. Candidate unrounded iterations/cycle: **10 / 4,360,895 = 0.0000022931072635319124**. Fitness uses that fraction directly: **169.75e6 * 10 / 4,360,895 = 389.25495798454216**. The evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark: 0.000002` was not used. Break-even frequency for the measured cycle saving is **178.7084524829 MHz**; the candidate does not reach it.\\n\\n## Mapped and routed connectivity\\n\\nInspected `synth.json` and every routed seed's `pnr.json`, in addition to all three critical-path reports.\\n\\n- `cpu.u_ex.exit_pending_DFFR_Q` is a DFFR. Its D net has exactly one sink: that flop's D. Traversal from the registered `predicted_taken` bit reaches only this first register endpoint in both the mapped design and **each routed seed**. It has no direct path to PC recovery, IF masking, ID-control reset, or commit enables without crossing the event register.\\n- `immediate_branch` is a separate ordinary DFFRE (`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_33`), sharing the payload CE and reset-only payload reset with predicted metadata. Its Q feeds the ordinary immediate term and does not reach the exit-event D. The mapper shares a complemented comparator result; the immediate LUT (`INIT=A0E0`) still implements `valid && (jump || immediate_branch && branch_cond)`. There is no prediction/outcome XOR or late repair qualification in that term.\\n- The 32 exit-target bits are precisely EX/MEM `pc_next[31:0]`. In the zero-wait FPGA wrapper they map to 32 ordinary DFFRs with no event-controlled CE. Registered exit Q supplies kill/recovery and the pending-commit flop. `commit_pending` drives the retained MEM/WB bank's capture enables (321 mapped CE sinks); the existing WB-valid flop implements the real deferred record schedule. That broad registered control fanout remains a physical cost.\\n- The IF target has a separate 31-cell carry chain for bits 1..31, with immediate bit 0 hardwired zero, starting from raw instruction bit 8 and PC bit 1. Independent PC+4 has a separate 30-cell carry chain starting at PC bit 2. This is the normal constant-bit simplification of the two full-width RTL sums, not a truncated PC or an operand-select adder. IF tests cover high-address carry, wraparound, and all eligibility bits.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax, MHz | Routed worst path | Logic / route, ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\\n| 1 | 174.98 | FPGA stimulus `lfsr[18]` to ID/EX `rs1_val[23]` D | 2.39 / 3.32 |\\n| 2 | 169.75 | ID/EX `rd[3]` (also instruction bit 10) through load-use control to ID/EX `alu_op[2]` RESET | 2.51 / 3.38 |\\n| 3 | 167.11 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` through normal ALU selection to EX/MEM normal result bit 6 D | 2.99 / 2.99 |\\n\\nThe predictor-specific exit qualification is registered as intended, but the frequency loss elsewhere overwhelms the cycle saving. No arithmetic change, reduced divider latency, fabricated retirement, or relaxed liveness bound was used to improve the result.\\n\\nEvidence beneath the scratch root: `formal-gate.log`, `final-unit-gate.log`, `cosim-gate.json`, `fpga-gate.json`, `predecessor-markers.json`, `mapped-connectivity.json`, `routed-connectivity.json`, `cores/bench/sim_build/prediction_profile/prediction-counters.json`, and `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` plus `pnr.json`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T14:31:20.364111Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r11s2 (regression, -5.01%): Registering only predicted-taken exit recovery isolated the inverted branch-outcome path, but two-slot exit repairs left only 2.91% fewer 2 KB CoreMark cycles while load-use control to ID/EX RESET and ordinary data paths limited median Fmax to 169.75 MHz, below the 178.71 MHz break-even point.\"}\n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Bimodal direct-branch prediction with fully registered recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments repeatedly saved instructions' recovery slots but\\nlost more fitness through routed timing. The r11s2 asymmetric predictor\\nregistered exits and passed liveness, yet retained immediate EX recovery;\\nr12s2's surviving immediate-recovery path limited all three routed seeds to\\nroughly 91 MHz. The r6s2 all-MEM-recovery BTB failed the unchanged liveness\\ncheck before obtaining physical results. This proposal combines a small\\ndirection-only predictor with a complete register boundary on recovery and\\nan explicit architectural commit schedule for recovering control transfers.\\nIt tests whether eliminating the remaining live EX-to-squash path can make\\nuseful prediction survive the frequency-times-throughput fitness metric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments repeatedly saved instructions' recovery slots but\\nlost more fitness through routed timing. The r11s2 asymmetric predictor\\nregistered exits and passed liveness, yet retained immediate EX recovery;\\nr12s2's surviving immediate-recovery path limited all three routed seeds to\\nroughly 91 MHz. The r6s2 all-MEM-recovery BTB failed the unchanged liveness\\ncheck before obtaining physical results. This proposal combines a small\\ndirection-only predictor with a complete register boundary on recovery and\\nan explicit architectural commit schedule for recovering control transfers.\\nIt tests whether eliminating the remaining live EX-to-squash path can make\\nuseful prediction survive the frequency-times-throughput fitness metric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one 64-entry bimodal conditional-direction predictor with fully\\nregistered control-transfer recovery. Compute direct targets from the\\ncurrent fetched instruction. Early direct-JAL steering belongs to this\\nsame front end; JALR uses sequential prediction and registered recovery.\\nKeep ordinary pipeline depth and the existing arithmetic resources.\\n\\nIndex 64 two-bit saturating counters with the full fetch PC's bits [7:2].\\nReset counters to weakly not taken (01); their MSB predicts direction.\\nThe table is deliberately untagged: aliasing changes prediction accuracy,\\nnever architectural targets or legality. A raw fetch predecoder admits\\nonly BRANCH opcode 0x63 with funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7, or JAL opcode 0x6f.\\nRequire a word-aligned source and target for taken prediction. For these\\naligned source PCs, B-immediate alignment is instruction bit 8 clear and\\nJ-immediate alignment is instruction bit 21 clear. Predict an eligible\\nconditional branch taken when its counter MSB is set, and an eligible JAL\\ntaken unconditionally. Compute the exact B/J sign extension and full\\n32-bit PC-relative target modulo 2^32. Compute PC+4 independently.\\n\\nSelect the target using raw predecode/table outputs independently of\\nstall, flush, redirect, and acceptance. These controls govern PC capture,\\nnot the prediction data select. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC\\nregister. Only actual fetch acceptance may apply a prediction. Carry its\\nconsumed predicted_taken bit and two-bit counter snapshot with that same\\ninstruction into ID/EX; holds retain the complete metadata. A prediction\\nmust not squash its own instruction. Raw predecode uses the current\\nimem_data, so stale direction state cannot steer ordinary, illegal, or\\nmisaligned instructions to a cached address. There is no stored target,\\ntarget tag comparison, return stack, or speculative history rollback.\\n\\nEX preserves the existing forwarded predicates, exact branch/jump targets,\\nJAL/JALR link results, taken-only alignment faults, and actual pc_next.\\nFor a direct branch or JAL, its consumed prediction is wrong when the\\npredicted_taken bit differs from the actual legal, aligned transfer\\ndecision. A direction difference may conservatively repair even if the\\ndirect displacement happens to equal +4. Every legal aligned JALR requests\\nrecovery to its existing resolved target. Illegal encodings and invalid\\nentries cannot recover; a misaligned transfer keeps the existing trap,\\nsuppressed jump link, and sequential continuation. A predicted direct\\ntarget is exact by construction, so no generic full-next-PC comparison\\nis needed and a load, store, or DIV/REM can never own prediction recovery.\\n\\nRegister recovery_pending only when the resolving control instruction\\nactually advances from EX, with reset, memory hold, and older recovery\\ncancellation handled explicitly. Clear the event on every non-event\\nclock. Reuse EX/MEM's architectural pc_next as the recovery address,\\ncaptured with the ordinary payload enable independently of the event.\\nRemove ALL immediate EX redirects, including unpredicted conditional\\nbranches, JAL, and JALR. The live branch comparison, forwarded JALR target,\\nlegality, alignment, and direction-mismatch cones must terminate in\\nregisters before affecting PC recovery, fetch masking, ID squash, or\\nyounger-EX cancellation. This complete cut is the distinguishing timing\\nexperiment; retaining an ordinary immediate-repair fallback defeats it.\\n\\nIn the next cycle recovery_pending belongs to the older EX/MEM control\\ninstruction. Its registered target overrides younger predictions and\\nredirects fetch even when imem is unavailable. Kill the younger ID/EX\\nowner before EX/MEM capture, clear its complete controls and validity,\\nand flush the younger combinational fetch slot. Suppress its divider\\nrequest, response acceptance, effective div_wait, training, and recovery\\nevent. A killed younger divide cannot already have launched: it entered\\nEX on the edge that registered this older recovery event. Do not reset\\nthe divider or disturb its registered completion bank. The recovering\\ncontrol instruction has no memory request, so dmem readiness cannot hold\\nit. Keep dmem-stall generation independent, and give registered recovery\\npriority over younger load-use/divider holds. Correct predictions incur\\nno squash; a recovery discards two younger slots under zero wait.\\n\\nMake a real one-clock postponement of the recovering instruction's\\nwriteback/retirement part of this recovery protocol. On the recovery\\nedge, MEM/WB captures its complete architectural record with valid clear\\nand sets commit_pending. On the next edge retain that record, assert\\nvalid once, and clear commit_pending; normal capture resumes afterward.\\nThe overwritten incoming MEM record is necessarily the invalid EX/MEM\\nbubble injected by recovery. Corrected fetch and execution continue\\nduring this commit delay. For a JAL/JALR owner, its link write occurs\\non this same actual WB-valid schedule, with the ordinary forwarding\\npaths exposing the correct link to its target. All RVFI fields and\\nregfile write enable must identify this real owner and its valid cycle.\\n\\nApply deferred commit only to the recovering branch/jump, never an\\narithmetic or memory owner or a correctly predicted transfer. With the\\nevent captured at edge E0, recovery occurs at E1 and the real owner is\\nvalid in MEM/WB at E2. A corrected DIV/REM successor launches at E3,\\naccepts at E11, and becomes valid in MEM/WB at E12: a ten-clock adjacent\\nretirement gap. Check both neighboring gaps, including a predecessor\\nDIV/REM and a recovering JALR with forwarding. Preserve the existing\\nformal liveness bound, ALTOPS timing, and eight-compute-clock divider;\\nfabricated retirements or RVFI-only delay are forbidden. Reset cancels\\npending recovery and commit ownership. Existing memory-hold WB operand\\nretention remains intact.\\n\\nTrain each surviving, legal conditional branch once on EX advancement,\\nusing the actual outcome to saturate its saved counter snapshot. Capture\\ntrain_pending and the resulting two-bit value into registers, reusing\\nEX/MEM source PC for the index. The next clock writes the table using\\nonly this registered event, index, and value. A recovering branch may\\ntrain; its killed successor may not. Do not train repeatedly during\\nholds or add a combinational update-to-lookup bypass. Lookup observes\\npre-edge state. An aliased or old counter snapshot is an accuracy loss,\\nnot a correctness hazard. Table payload/reset cost must be measured.\\n\\nForecast a 4 percent net fitness gain with low confidence. For example,\\nfive percent fewer cycles with one percent lower Fmax yields about\\n4.21 percent improvement; at that cycle saving the 184.84 MHz winner's\\nbreak-even frequency is about 175.60 MHz. These are conditional targets,\\nnot measurements. Two-slot misses, small-table aliasing, the IF target\\nadder, and deferred-commit fanout can erase the gain. Historical cycle\\ncounts include a documented 2 KB evaluator fixture and must not be\\nrepresented as measured 6 KB results. Record the actual evaluated ELF,\\nworking set, marker-window cycles, accepted predictions, both direction\\nerrors, JALR recoveries, and table writes. Preserve the required 6 KB\\nCRC/UART/OOB/marker checks and report any existing fixture discrepancy\\nexplicitly without changing benchmark sources or evaluator contracts.\\n\\nInspect mapped dependencies and every routed seed: no live EX predicate\\nor target-alignment cone may reach fetch/ID squash without crossing\\nrecovery_pending. Also inspect recovery-event D, table lookup-to-PC,\\ncounter-write controls, recovery fanout, and commit enables; moving the\\nlimiting path is not evidence of improved timing. Use unrounded\\niterations/cycle times three-seed median Fmax for the final fitness.\\nDuring implementation run unchanged lint, unit, formal/liveness, ISS\\ncosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker, resource, and FPGA gates. Preserve nret=1,\\nall external ports, strict decoder defaults, monotonic RVFI order and\\nresolved PC continuity, result-bank ownership, and the directly\\nregistered normal dmem-address bank. Scope is cores/bench RTL and tests.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 64-entry two-bit direction table indexed by PC[7:2], weakly\\nnot-taken reset, combinational lookup, and registered once-per-owner\\ntraining interface with pre-edge lookup semantics and no bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode current legal aligned conditional branches and direct JALs,\\ncompute full-width immediate targets and independent PC+4, and steer\\nonly accepted fetches. Emit consumed prediction and counter metadata.\\nGive registered recovery priority and retain direct PC-to-imem wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX with predicted_taken and counter snapshot\\nmetadata. Reuse existing EX/MEM PC and architectural pc_next for\\nregistered training and repair; preserve all retirement bundles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture and hold prediction metadata with the accepted instruction\\nin the ordinary payload domain, preserving independent payload\\nenables, complete holds, and zero-control invalid entries.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace all immediate control-transfer redirects with registered\\nrecovery events and existing EX/MEM pc_next targets. Register small\\nbranch-training events and updated counters. Add older-recovery kill\\npriority over younger execution, divider launch, and predictor events.\\nPreserve arithmetic, traps, links, forwarding, and direct address banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the direction table, fetch metadata, registered recovery and\\ntraining, younger-EX kill, and recovering-owner deferred commit. Keep\\ntop-level memory and channel-0-only RVFI ports unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distribute only registered recovery to PC/ID squash and younger-EX\\ncancellation. Give it priority over younger load-use and divide\\nholds while retaining independently computed memory backpressure.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the recovering-control-owner commit_pending schedule: capture\\nthe real record invalid, retain and present it valid once next cycle,\\nthen resume normal capture. Preserve actual jump-link WB ownership,\\nall RVFI fields, reset behavior, and held-WB forwarding semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test every counter transition, index, reset state, alias collision,\\nand pre-edge read/write collision. Check raw branch/JAL legality and\\nalignment, full-width target carry/wrap, both direction errors,\\nchanged instructions, held prediction metadata, and once-only training.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Compare complete architectural retirements and accepted memory\\nrequests against the unchanged ISS. Exercise all branch predicates,\\ndirect jumps, changing JALR targets, both direction errors, link users,\\nmisaligned and illegal transfers, forwarding, long dmem/imem stalls,\\nreset during recovery/commit, consecutive repairs, and killed stores\\nand divides. Verify zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, actual deferred\\nWB ownership, and both adjacent retirement gaps for every DIV/REM\\npredecessor/successor around recovering branches, JALs, and JALRs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed bundle construction and check accepted prediction and\\ncounter snapshots, complete hold retention, ordinary payload capture,\\nand inert metadata beneath zero-control bubbles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update bundle layouts and default recovery kill inactive in existing\\ncases. Cover killed younger DIV/REM requests and preserve registered\\ncompletion-bank ownership and the independent normal memory address.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 64-entry two-bit direction table indexed by PC[7:2], weakly\\nnot-taken reset, combinational lookup, and registered once-per-owner\\ntraining interface with pre-edge lookup semantics and no bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode current legal aligned conditional branches and direct JALs,\\ncompute full-width immediate targets and independent PC+4, and steer\\nonly accepted fetches. Emit consumed prediction and counter metadata.\\nGive registered recovery priority and retain direct PC-to-imem wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX with predicted_taken and counter snapshot\\nmetadata. Reuse existing EX/MEM PC and architectural pc_next for\\nregistered training and repair; preserve all retirement bundles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture and hold prediction metadata with the accepted instruction\\nin the ordinary payload domain, preserving independent payload\\nenables, complete holds, and zero-control invalid entries.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace all immediate control-transfer redirects with registered\\nrecovery events and existing EX/MEM pc_next targets. Register small\\nbranch-training events and updated counters. Add older-recovery kill\\npriority over younger execution, divider launch, and predictor events.\\nPreserve arithmetic, traps, links, forwarding, and direct address banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the direction table, fetch metadata, registered recovery and\\ntraining, younger-EX kill, and recovering-owner deferred commit. Keep\\ntop-level memory and channel-0-only RVFI ports unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distribute only registered recovery to PC/ID squash and younger-EX\\ncancellation. Give it priority over younger load-use and divide\\nholds while retaining independently computed memory backpressure.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the recovering-control-owner commit_pending schedule: capture\\nthe real record invalid, retain and present it valid once next cycle,\\nthen resume normal capture. Preserve actual jump-link WB ownership,\\nall RVFI fields, reset behavior, and held-WB forwarding semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test every counter transition, index, reset state, alias collision,\\nand pre-edge read/write collision. Check raw branch/JAL legality and\\nalignment, full-width target carry/wrap, both direction errors,\\nchanged instructions, held prediction metadata, and once-only training.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Compare complete architectural retirements and accepted memory\\nrequests against the unchanged ISS. Exercise all branch predicates,\\ndirect jumps, changing JALR targets, both direction errors, link users,\\nmisaligned and illegal transfers, forwarding, long dmem/imem stalls,\\nreset during recovery/commit, consecutive repairs, and killed stores\\nand divides. Verify zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, actual deferred\\nWB ownership, and both adjacent retirement gaps for every DIV/REM\\npredecessor/successor around recovering branches, JALs, and JALRs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed bundle construction and check accepted prediction and\\ncounter snapshots, complete hold retention, ordinary payload capture,\\nand inert metadata beneath zero-control bubbles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update bundle layouts and default recovery kill inactive in existing\\ncases. Cover killed younger DIV/REM requests and preserve registered\\ncompletion-bank ownership and the independent normal memory address.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 329.62, \"delta_pct\": -19.9, \"vs_baseline\": 16.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 140.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4265800, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6018, \"ff\": 2249, \"seeds\": [136.76, 141.58, 140.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the 64-entry bimodal direct-branch predictor and fully registered recovery. Correctness gates pass. The forecast fitness gain was not achieved: the measured three-seed median is 140.61 MHz, and the 2 KB evaluator fitness is 329.6216418960102 iterations/s. The separately measured required 6 KB workload yields 45.03360898496885 iterations/s. Neither number uses the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark` field.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- Added 64 untagged two-bit counters, indexed by fetch PC[7:2], reset to 01. The table has no target storage, tags, bypass, or speculative history.\\n- IF validates current raw BRANCH funct3/JAL encodings and source/displacement alignment. A shared full-width B/J target adder uses the raw opcode-selected signed immediate; PC+4 has an independent adder. Prediction data selection is independent of stalls, acceptance, flush, and recovery. Only fetch acceptance advances by prediction, and imem_addr remains the PC register itself.\\n- IF/ID and ID/EX carry consumed predicted_taken and the two-bit snapshot. ID payload capture remains independent of validity/control flush; holds preserve every payload field and all prediction metadata.\\n- EX computes actual predicates, targets, traps, links, and architectural pc_next as before. Direct direction mismatch and every legal aligned JALR generate recovery only when the control owner advances. All immediate EX redirects were removed. recovery_pending clears on every non-event edge, including holds and older cancellation.\\n- The registered event kills the younger EX owner, suppresses divider launch/accept/wait and predictor events, clears EX/MEM controls/validity, flushes ID and the combinational fetch slot, and redirects using EX/MEM.pc_next even when imem is unavailable. Memory backpressure remains independently computed. The divider resource, completion bank, eight-compute-clock protocol, arithmetic, and direct normal memory-address bank are unchanged.\\n- On recovery E1, MEM/WB captures the entire recovering record invalid and sets commit_pending. At E2 that same record is retained and becomes valid once. Regfile write enable and RVFI use that real MEM/WB-valid schedule; existing forwarding exposes jump links to corrected successors. The overwritten E2 incoming record is the invalid recovery bubble. Correct fetch proceeds during the delay. Reset cancels both event and commit ownership.\\n- Conditional training is registered once per advancing surviving nontrapping branch, using its saved snapshot and actual outcome. The next edge writes using registered train_pending/train_value and EX/MEM source PC. Killed successors and held owners cannot train. Untaken branches with unaligned displacements remain legal and can train not taken.\\n- Hazard outputs use independent continuous assignments. The load-use inputs use exactly the same masked raw instruction bits as IF, avoiding a packed-metadata dependency cycle in Verilator's public-signal simulation mode without changing architectural behavior.\\n\\nTests and local gates:\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings. Public-flat-rw lint also passes without warnings. git diff --check and the unchanged channel-0 contract scanner pass.\\n- Final unchanged pytest entry point over cores/bench/test: 11 runners passed, 156 cocotb cases passed, including both real and ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank suites. Pipeline cases include many directed subcases, including all 96 combinations of six taken predicates and four DIV/REM predecessor/successor operations, plus 16 JALR and 16 direct-JAL combinations.\\n- New table/fetch tests cover all entries/states, reset/write precedence, no pre-edge bypass, aliasing, all branch funct3 values, changed instructions, JAL/JALR distinctions, source/target alignment, signed full-PC carries/wrap, and prediction selection while acceptance is blocked.\\n- Pipeline tests compare complete architectural retirements and accepted memory requests against the unchanged ISS, check monotonic order and resolved PC continuity, and monitor table state, once-only training, real deferred WB ownership, and complete metadata holds. They cover both direction errors, zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, killed stores/loads/divides, forwarded links and changing JALR targets, long imem/dmem holds, consecutive repairs, and reset in both recovery and commit phases.\\n- Recovering branch/JALR following DIV/REM: adjacent retirement gap 2. Corrected DIV/REM after recovery: gap exactly 10. With E0 event registration, recovery E1, owner valid E2, corrected divide launches E3, accepts E11 and is valid E12. Existing maximum-ten-clock and eight-compute-clock assertions remain enforced. Correctly predicted JAL has predecessor gap 1 and divide-successor gap 9.\\n- An eligible aligned JAL cannot naturally mispredict with unconditional early JAL steering. Its defensive unpredicted-recovery case is exercised directly at EX, including displacement +4; full-pipeline JAL tests check the naturally reachable correct-prediction case. No fabricated retirement or prediction fault injection was used.\\n- Local formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness, reg, PC forward/backward, unique, illegal and all RV32IM instruction checks. Two complete runs passed; the second checked final logical RTL after splitting the simulation dependency cycle. No formal counterexample and no correctness-fix retries. An initial setup invocation stopped before generating checks because default Python lacked dependencies; the installed conda Python resolved this. The unchanged run_all.sh, checks_si.cfg, and wrapper_si.sv were staged under TMPDIR, with nret=1 auto-detection and JOBS=6. No check/depth/ALTOPS changes.\\n- Unchanged ISS/cosim evaluator: PASS, both selftest.elf and CoreMark CRC validation. Unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench: PASS, all three routes completed. NRET=1 and BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv select the required existing wrappers.\\n- Required 6 KB checks also PASS separately: seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949; ITERATIONS=10; literal \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\"; ebreak=true, oob=false, both MMIO markers present, positive bracket duration.\\n\\nFixture discrepancy, preserved explicitly:\\n\\nThe current benchmark Makefile defaults to TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 and -O3, and tools/eval/fpga.py validates the 2 KB CRC set e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf. Therefore the unchanged evaluator's reported result is a 2 KB result, not a measured 6 KB result. No benchmark sources, evaluator constants, or contracts were changed. A separate scratch build used the unchanged Makefile's supported COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 parameters and the unchanged simulator, with explicit required 6 KB checks. The standard validator would reject this 6 KB UART because its constants currently describe 2 KB.\\n\\nBoth builds use -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -O3, ITERATIONS=10, and the unchanged deterministic --bench --istall --dstall bus model. UART's hardcoded \\\"Compiler flags: -O2\\\" is stale; build logs show -O3. \\\"CoreMark Size\\\" prints per-algorithm size after division by three: 666 for TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 and 2000 for TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000.\\n\\nScratch evidence root: $TMPDIR/r13s2-verify, resolving in this session to /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/r13s2-verify.\\n\\n| Measurement | Evaluator fixture, 2 KB | Required workload, 6 KB |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Actual ELF, relative to evidence root | bench/programs/coremark.elf | six-kb/bench/programs/coremark.elf |\\n| ELF SHA-256 | 865294625fc3d7a1a97ec51364363403f427688c7fae81615d188842c053df73 | d67eb75d61d307dce8fe578faba54b8abd48b6bb6359f732143dfa33bbdf07cd |\\n| Start marker cycle | 15,304 | 53,904 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,281,104 | 31,277,251 |\\n| Marker-window cycles | 4,265,800 | 31,223,347 |\\n| Exact iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,265,800 | 10 / 31,223,347 |\\n| Window accepted taken predictions | 356,784 | 2,530,324 |\\n| Window predicted NT, actually taken errors | 19,960 | 154,642 |\\n| Window predicted taken, actually NT errors | 31,897 | 218,783 |\\n| Window JALR recoveries | 13,735 | 104,164 |\\n| Window table writes | 529,588 | 3,747,912 |\\n| Window registered recoveries | 65,592 | 477,589 |\\n| Whole-run accepted taken predictions | 358,549 | 2,535,419 |\\n| Whole-run predicted NT, actually taken errors | 20,145 | 155,110 |\\n| Whole-run predicted taken, actually NT errors | 32,093 | 219,285 |\\n| Whole-run JALR recoveries | 13,794 | 104,223 |\\n| Whole-run table writes | 532,346 | 3,755,488 |\\n| Fitness, median MHz * 1e6 * exact iterations/cycle | 329.6216418960102 | 45.03360898496885 |\\n\\nCounters were observed by measure_elf() in test_branch_predictor.py using a generated observation-only simulator copy under cores/bench/sim_build/observe. Its entire stdout, including architectural final retirement, markers, cycle counts, UART and OOB status, matched the independently run unchanged simulator byte for byte for each ELF. Window counters cover [start marker, stop marker); whole-run counters include setup/printing and speculative accepted predictions. Synthesizable RTL contains no measurement counters. Full records are predictor-2k.json and predictor-6k.json.\\n\\nPhysical results and dependency inspection:\\n\\n| Seed | Routed Fmax MHz | Critical source | Critical destination | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 136.76 | EX/MEM rd[4] | recovery_pending.D | 3.97 / 3.34 |\\n| 2 | 141.58 | EX/MEM rd[0] | recovery_pending.D | 4.09 / 2.98 |\\n| 3 | 140.61 | EX/MEM rd[4] | recovery_pending.D | 3.97 / 3.14 |\\n\\nEach critical path runs through forwarding selection and branch comparison into the recovery-event register. Intermediate names containing train_value refer to shared mapped comparison logic, not an unregistered table write. Median Fmax is 140.61 MHz. Resource report: 6018 LUT4, 2249 FF; four DPX9B memory blocks and one MULT36X36 remain. Relative to the recorded parent metadata (5485 LUT4 / 2119 FF), this is +533 LUT4 / +130 FF. Synthesis's eight warnings are the unchanged divider's array-to-register lowering notices, not lint violations.\\n\\ninspect_recovery_netlist() passed on synth.json and every routed pnr_seed{1,2,3}/pnr.json. It traverses actual cell connections and reconstructs routed vector aliases through preserved cell output pins. The PC D/enable inputs, ID control/valid capture, and younger-EX valid capture have no combinational roots in EX operand values, forwarded result data, EX PC, or EX immediate; recovery_pending.Q is present in every cancellation cone. The recovery event is exactly one real DFFR. imemAddr equals the PC Q bits. No immediate EX predicate/target-alignment fallback remains.\\n\\nThe event D cone contains 361 mapped / 367 routed combinational cells. Recovery Q directly drives 123 input pins. The table is exactly 128 DFFE payload bits; the union of write/reset/decode cones contains 210 LUT3, and all write roots are exclusively reset/constants and registered training event/value/source-PC index. No update-to-lookup bypass exists. The table-dependent PC/ID consumer cone contains 93 LUT4, 16 LUT3, 1 LUT2, and 38 MUX2_LUT5 (includes downstream selection/metadata logic). Commit-pending D has two combinational cells. Commit Q has three direct sinks; its single inverted payload-enable net drives 319 MEM/WB enable pins and contains no live EX operand/target dependency. Detailed evidence is dependencies.json and each unchanged nextpnr.log.\\n\\nFor comparison, the exact starting bench RTL from HEAD 12ca6b0c4228fd491171ddb619667799976b4eae was separately compiled in scratch and run on these same ELFs and bus schedule. Its measured marker windows were 4,491,485 cycles (2 KB) and 32,836,671 (6 KB), with matching CRC/UART/OOB checks. Thus cycles improved 5.0247301282% and 4.9131777092%, respectively. Using the parent's recorded, not rerouted here, 184.84 MHz from core.yaml, candidate relative fitness is -19.9042057860% (2 KB) and -19.9981712734% (6 KB). Break-even frequencies at these measured cycle savings would be 175.552288831 and 175.758482322 MHz. Moving the limiting path to recovery-event D did not improve fitness; the low-confidence +4% forecast is rejected by these measurements.\\n\\nAll edited RTL/tests are within cores/bench. The only separate document is this explicitly requested implementation_notes.md. Original contracts, other cores, arithmetic resources, formal depths, and evaluator sources remain unchanged. Temporary copies, generated binaries, logs, and route evidence are build artifacts under the authorized bench simulation directory or TMPDIR.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T17:08:36.469819Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r13s2 (regression, -19.90%): Fully registered recovery removed live EX-to-squash paths but left EX/MEM rd-driven forwarding and branch comparison critical at recovery_pending.D in all three routed seeds, so the 64-entry bimodal predictor's 5.02% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles could not offset median Fmax falling from 184.84 to 140.61 MHz.\"}\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Bimodal prediction with branch comparison split across EX and MEM\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch path predicts sequentially and discards a useful slot\\non each taken transfer. Predictor r13s2 reduced measured cycles by 5.02%\\non the evaluator's 2 KB fixture and 4.91% on its separate 6 KB run, but\\nits 140.61 MHz median lost to the current 184.84 MHz implementation.\\nEvery routed seed was limited by EX/MEM destination-tag forwarding\\nthrough branch comparison into recovery_pending.D. Registering the\\ncompleted recovery decision left the expensive forwarding-plus-full-\\ncomparison chain intact. Earlier r10s2 also showed that merely balancing\\na wholly combinational comparator was insufficient. The promising next\\npredictor experiment is to place a real register boundary INSIDE branch\\nvalidation, while preserving the two-slot repair schedule established\\nby r13s2 and its actual deferred writeback protocol.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch path predicts sequentially and discards a useful slot\\non each taken transfer. Predictor r13s2 reduced measured cycles by 5.02%\\non the evaluator's 2 KB fixture and 4.91% on its separate 6 KB run, but\\nits 140.61 MHz median lost to the current 184.84 MHz implementation.\\nEvery routed seed was limited by EX/MEM destination-tag forwarding\\nthrough branch comparison into recovery_pending.D. Registering the\\ncompleted recovery decision left the expensive forwarding-plus-full-\\ncomparison chain intact. Earlier r10s2 also showed that merely balancing\\na wholly combinational comparator was insufficient. The promising next\\npredictor experiment is to place a real register boundary INSIDE branch\\nvalidation, while preserving the two-slot repair schedule established\\nby r13s2 and its actual deferred writeback protocol.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement one 64-entry bimodal direct-control predictor whose branch\\nvalidation is partitioned between EX and MEM. Keep the same prediction\\npolicy as r13s2 so the principal experimental variable is the validation\\nboundary: EX registers independent byte-comparison summaries; MEM\\ncombines those summaries and performs recovery. Do not register a full\\nbranch predicate or full misprediction decision after forwarding in EX.\\nOrdinary instructions retain their existing execution/retirement stages.\\n\\nIndex 64 two-bit saturating counters with fetch PC[7:2], reset to 01.\\nRaw imem_data predecode admits only legal BRANCH funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7 and\\ndirect JAL. A conditional predicts taken from the counter MSB; an\\neligible direct JAL predicts taken unconditionally. Require aligned\\nsource and target: with aligned PC, B immediate bit 1 is instr[8] and\\nJ immediate bit 1 is instr[21]. Form exact sign-extended B/J immediates,\\nfull-width modulo-2^32 targets, and an independent PC+4. Select prediction\\ndata using raw predecode/counter state, independently of acceptance,\\nstalls, flush, and repair. Apply it only on actual fetch acceptance.\\nKeep imem_addr directly driven by the PC register. JALR predicts PC+4;\\nthere is no BTB, target truncation, RAS, or speculative history.\\n\\nCarry the consumed predicted_taken and two-bit counter snapshot with\\nthe accepted instruction through ID/EX and EX/MEM. ID also registers\\nwhether the branch is signed relational. Metadata uses the ordinary\\npayload capture/hold domain; controls and valid alone determine whether\\nit has an owner. Preserve control-zero bubbles, full held metadata, the\\nexisting fetch NOP mask, and separation of payload enables from flush.\\n\\nIn EX use the existing forwarded rs1 and rs2. For each of four independent\\neight-bit slices j, compute E[j] = (rs1_byte == rs2_byte) and\\nL[j] = (rs1_byte < rs2_byte). Slices 0 through 2 compare unsigned. Slice 3\\ncompares signed bytes for BLT/BGE and unsigned bytes otherwise; this\\nmakes lexicographic combination produce the correct signed or unsigned\\nwhole-word ordering. Equal summaries are identical for both modes.\\nRegister these eight summary bits at the EX/MEM boundary with ordinary\\npayload capture. All four comparisons operate in parallel. No cross-\\nslice merge, branch-op selection, direction XOR, or repair qualification\\nmay precede this register boundary. Do not replicate comparators over\\nevery forwarding-source pair or alter ALU SLT/SLTU and M arithmetic.\\n\\nEX independently registers full control-target and fall-through addresses\\nwithout consulting the branch outcome. Reuse existing EX/MEM fields:\\nraw branch_target can hold PC+B/J immediate or the bit-zero-cleared\\nforwarded JALR sum; raw pc_next can hold PC+4. Their payload enables must\\nnot depend on outcome or recovery. Carry decoder controls and actual\\npost-forward operand values as before. EX must no longer compute a\\nseparate full-width branch comparator for pc_next, alignment traps,\\ntraining, or redirect: those consumers all use the registered summaries.\\n\\nAdd a combinational MEM control resolver between the raw EX/MEM record\\nand its MEM/forwarding consumers. Merge summaries in two balanced levels:\\nmerge (3,2) and (1,0), then merge those pairs. For high/low children use\\nE = E_high AND E_low and L = L_high OR (E_high AND L_low).\\nSelect E for BEQ/BNE or L for relational branches and XOR branch_op[0]\\nto obtain the architectural predicate. Use that same predicate for the\\nactual branch_taken, taken-only target-alignment trap, resolved pc_next,\\nand predictor outcome. Resolve JAL/JALR from registered target/control\\nfields. Preserve sequential continuation and suppressed jump-link write\\non target misalignment, and preserve decoder illegality.\\n\\nThe resolver emits a normalized architectural EX/MEM record; raw pc_next\\nis only its registered fall-through candidate, never RVFI's unresolved\\nanswer. Feed normalized controls to forwarding as well as MEM. Qualify\\njump reg_write using registered jump alignment alone: a branch already\\nhas reg_write=0, so its comparison must not enter another instruction's\\nforwarding-write-enable cone. Memory controls remain unchanged and\\nindependent of branch summaries. Keep the directly registered normal\\nEX/MEM memory-address bank separate from this resolver and result muxes.\\n\\nCompute recovery in MEM from valid, legal registered control ownership\\nand the resolved decision. A direct transfer repairs when consumed\\npredicted_taken differs from its actual legal aligned transfer; every\\nlegal aligned JALR repairs to its target. Conservatively repairing a\\ndirection mismatch with displacement +4 is acceptable. Misaligned\\ntransfers that were necessarily predicted sequentially trap and keep\\nPC+4. Illegal or ordinary instructions never own recovery. There is no\\nimmediate EX redirect fallback and no additional recovery-event register\\nafter the MEM merge. With summaries captured at E0, MEM repairs at E1:\\ncorrect predictions discard zero slots and errors discard two slots.\\n\\nMEM recovery overrides imem backpressure and younger fetch prediction,\\nflushes ID/fetch, and kills the younger EX owner before EX/MEM capture.\\nSuppress its divider request, acceptance, effective div_wait, and all\\npredictor actions; emit zero EX/MEM controls and valid. A killed younger\\ndivide cannot already have launched because it entered EX at E0. Do not\\nreset the divider or completion bank. Give older recovery precedence\\nover younger load-use/divider holds. Compute dmem stalls independently;\\na recovering control owner has no memory request. When an older memory\\noperation stalls a branch in EX, retain its complete operands/metadata\\nand capture summaries only on actual advancement. Preserve held-WB\\nforwarding and once-only retirement under arbitrary bus stalls.\\n\\nRetain r13s2's real deferred commit for a recovering branch or jump. At\\nrepair edge E1, MEM/WB captures the entire resolved owner with valid clear\\nand sets commit_pending. At E2, retain that record, assert valid once,\\nand clear commit_pending; the discarded incoming MEM record is the\\nrecovery bubble. Corrected fetching and execution continue. Regfile\\nwrites, jump-link forwarding, and all RVFI fields must describe this\\nactual WB owner and valid schedule. Correct predictions and ordinary\\ninstructions use normal commit. Reset cancels pending ownership.\\nA corrected divide launches E3, accepts E11, and reaches valid MEM/WB\\nat E12, ten clocks after its recovering predecessor. Check both adjacent\\nretirement gaps, including a DIV/REM predecessor. Preserve the existing\\nformal bound and eight-compute-clock divider; do not use RVFI-only delays\\nor fabricate retirement to work around the r6s2 liveness failure.\\n\\nTrain a surviving nontrapping conditional branch once on MEM advancement,\\nincluding an owner causing recovery. Saturate its saved counter snapshot\\nusing the MEM predicate, then register train_pending, index, and value.\\nThe following edge writes the table from those registered fields alone.\\nLookup observes pre-edge contents, with no update-to-lookup bypass.\\nHeld, invalid, and killed owners cannot train. The extra training delay\\nand aliased stale snapshots may reduce accuracy but cannot change the\\narchitecturally certified target or bypass validation.\\n\\nForecast a 4 percent net fitness improvement with low confidence. Five\\npercent fewer cycles with one percent lower Fmax would yield about\\n4.21 percent higher fitness; the corresponding break-even frequency is\\nabout 175.60 MHz against 184.84 MHz. These are conditional estimates.\\nByte-comparator mapping, MEM-merge-to-squash depth, target addition, IF\\ntarget selection, training staleness, or deferred-commit fanout can erase\\nthe benefit. Inspect every routed seed and mapped dependencies: EX\\nforwarding paths must terminate at local summary flops before a full\\npredicate is formed, while every repair input must originate in the\\nregistered MEM owner. Merely moving the worst endpoint is not success.\\n\\nDuring implementation run unchanged lint, unit, formal including liveness,\\nISS cosim, CRC/UART/OOB, marker-window, resource and three-seed FPGA gates.\\nMeasure exact iterations/cycle, correct predictions, both direction\\nerrors, JALR repairs, table updates, and median routed Fmax on identical\\nELFs for parent and candidate. Identify the actual working set: prior\\nevaluator logs describe a 2 KB fixture and separate 6 KB measurements.\\nPreserve the required 6 KB validation and report any fixture discrepancy\\nwithout changing benchmark or evaluator files. Keep nret=1, all ports,\\nstrict decoding, true resolved RVFI PCs, monotonic order, arithmetic and\\nresult-bank ownership, and all hard invariants. This proposal targets\\nonly cores/bench RTL and its permitted cocotb tests.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add 64 two-bit direction counters indexed by PC[7:2], reset to 01,\\nwith combinational pre-edge lookup and a registered training interface.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predict legal aligned conditional branches and direct JALs from raw\\nfetch data; form exact full-width targets and consumed metadata.\\nKeep prediction data selection independent of handshake controls,\\nthe existing NOP mask, and direct registered PC-to-imem addressing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add consumed prediction/counter metadata, registered signed-branch\\nmode, and four E/L comparison pairs to the appropriate bundles.\\nDocument raw EX/MEM target/fall-through candidates versus the MEM\\nresolver's actual architectural pc_next and controls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction metadata and signed-relational mode with the\\ninstruction in the ordinary payload domain. Preserve all holds,\\ncontrol-zero invalid entries, and independent payload capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register four parallel byte E/L summaries, independent control target\\nand PC+4 candidates, and prediction metadata. Remove full branch\\ncomparison and immediate redirects from EX. Add older-MEM repair\\nkill priority over younger execution and divider actions; preserve\\narithmetic, forwarding operands, completion and direct address banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/control_resolve.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the balanced MEM summary merge, architectural branch/jump and\\ntrap resolution, normalized EX/MEM record, two-slot repair strobe,\\nand registered once-per-owner training. Never allow current EX\\noperands into recovery or branch outcomes into jump write enables.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire raw EX/MEM through the control resolver for MEM and forwarding,\\nconnect predictor metadata/training, and distribute MEM repair and\\ncommit ownership. Preserve the nret=1 external interface exactly.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Give older MEM repair priority over younger holds and squash both\\nyounger slots. Retain independent memory backpressure, load-use\\ninterlocking, and existing held-WB forwarding behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the resolved architectural record and implement recovering-\\nowner commit_pending with real one-clock deferred WB ownership.\\nPreserve all RVFI data, jump-link writeback, reset cancellation,\\nnormal memory timing, and retained operands during dmem stalls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Verify counter/reset/collision behavior, raw fetch legality and\\nalignment, full-address carries/wrap, accepted metadata retention,\\ntraining delay, and once-only updates through holds and squashes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_control_resolve.py\", \"description\": \"Check each byte comparison and both merge levels against full RV32\\nsigned/unsigned predicates, including every first-differing byte,\\nequality, opposite signs, and boundary values. Exercise all branch\\noperations, both direction errors, JAL/JALR targets, and taken-only\\nalignment traps with normalized controls and actual resolved PCs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Compare complete retirements and accepted memory requests with the\\nunchanged ISS. Exercise both forwarding paths, load-use and long\\nbus stalls, consecutive controls, prediction errors, illegal/misaligned\\ntransfers, changing JALR targets and link users, killed stores/divides,\\nreset during deferred commit, and both neighboring retirement gaps\\nfor every DIV/REM operation around recovering branches and jumps.\\nVerify zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, and real once-only WB.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed-bundle construction and verify accepted metadata,\\nsigned-mode capture, complete hold retention, and inert bubbles\\nwithout weakening existing control/payload checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update bundle layouts and default older repair inactive. Verify byte\\nsummaries use exact forwarded operands and freeze with their owner;\\nadd killed-divider cases while preserving every arithmetic,\\ncompletion-bank, response-timing, and direct-address assertion.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add 64 two-bit direction counters indexed by PC[7:2], reset to 01,\\nwith combinational pre-edge lookup and a registered training interface.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predict legal aligned conditional branches and direct JALs from raw\\nfetch data; form exact full-width targets and consumed metadata.\\nKeep prediction data selection independent of handshake controls,\\nthe existing NOP mask, and direct registered PC-to-imem addressing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add consumed prediction/counter metadata, registered signed-branch\\nmode, and four E/L comparison pairs to the appropriate bundles.\\nDocument raw EX/MEM target/fall-through candidates versus the MEM\\nresolver's actual architectural pc_next and controls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture prediction metadata and signed-relational mode with the\\ninstruction in the ordinary payload domain. Preserve all holds,\\ncontrol-zero invalid entries, and independent payload capture.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register four parallel byte E/L summaries, independent control target\\nand PC+4 candidates, and prediction metadata. Remove full branch\\ncomparison and immediate redirects from EX. Add older-MEM repair\\nkill priority over younger execution and divider actions; preserve\\narithmetic, forwarding operands, completion and direct address banks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/control_resolve.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the balanced MEM summary merge, architectural branch/jump and\\ntrap resolution, normalized EX/MEM record, two-slot repair strobe,\\nand registered once-per-owner training. Never allow current EX\\noperands into recovery or branch outcomes into jump write enables.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire raw EX/MEM through the control resolver for MEM and forwarding,\\nconnect predictor metadata/training, and distribute MEM repair and\\ncommit ownership. Preserve the nret=1 external interface exactly.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Give older MEM repair priority over younger holds and squash both\\nyounger slots. Retain independent memory backpressure, load-use\\ninterlocking, and existing held-WB forwarding behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the resolved architectural record and implement recovering-\\nowner commit_pending with real one-clock deferred WB ownership.\\nPreserve all RVFI data, jump-link writeback, reset cancellation,\\nnormal memory timing, and retained operands during dmem stalls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Verify counter/reset/collision behavior, raw fetch legality and\\nalignment, full-address carries/wrap, accepted metadata retention,\\ntraining delay, and once-only updates through holds and squashes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_control_resolve.py\", \"description\": \"Check each byte comparison and both merge levels against full RV32\\nsigned/unsigned predicates, including every first-differing byte,\\nequality, opposite signs, and boundary values. Exercise all branch\\noperations, both direction errors, JAL/JALR targets, and taken-only\\nalignment traps with normalized controls and actual resolved PCs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Compare complete retirements and accepted memory requests with the\\nunchanged ISS. Exercise both forwarding paths, load-use and long\\nbus stalls, consecutive controls, prediction errors, illegal/misaligned\\ntransfers, changing JALR targets and link users, killed stores/divides,\\nreset during deferred commit, and both neighboring retirement gaps\\nfor every DIV/REM operation around recovering branches and jumps.\\nVerify zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, and real once-only WB.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update packed-bundle construction and verify accepted metadata,\\nsigned-mode capture, complete hold retention, and inert bubbles\\nwithout weakening existing control/payload checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_ex_result_banks.py\", \"description\": \"Update bundle layouts and default older repair inactive. Verify byte\\nsummaries use exact forwarded operands and freeze with their owner;\\nadd killed-divider cases while preserving every arithmetic,\\ncompletion-bank, response-timing, and direct-address assertion.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 370.86, \"delta_pct\": -9.88, \"vs_baseline\": 31.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 158.2, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4265800, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5972, \"ff\": 2301, \"seeds\": [158.2, 164.96, 154.82], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the bimodal predictor with the branch comparison split inside EX/MEM. Correctness gates pass, but the forecast fitness improvement was not achieved: median routed Fmax is 158.20 MHz versus the reproduced parent's 184.84 MHz. The 2 KB fixture loses 9.8844% fitness despite 5.0247% fewer cycles; the separate required 6 KB run loses 9.9901% fitness despite 4.9132% fewer cycles.\\n\\nThe implementation follows the proposed ownership and timing protocol:\\n\\n- `branch_predictor.sv` provides 64 two-bit counters indexed by fetch PC[7:2], reset to 01. `control_resolve.sv` registers the advancing branch's saved-snapshot update; the table writes those registered fields on the next edge. There is no update-to-lookup bypass.\\n- IF uses raw instruction bits for legal BRANCH/JAL eligibility, aligned source/target checks, exact sign-extended immediates, full-width target addition, and independent PC+4. Only actual acceptance applies prediction. JALR remains sequentially predicted. The PC register directly drives imem address, and the NOP mask remains intact.\\n- ID carries consumed direction/counter metadata and registers signed-relational mode in the ordinary payload domain. EX captures four independent byte equal/less pairs from its existing forwarded operands. For signed comparison, the top byte's sign bits are inverted before one unsigned eight-bit comparison; this is equivalent to signed byte ordering and avoids duplicate signed/unsigned comparators. There is no EX full-word branch predicate, direction inversion, alignment decision, or redirect.\\n- Raw EX/MEM `pc_next` contains PC+4 and `branch_target` contains the independently computed direct/JALR target. Raw `branch_taken` is zero. MEM merges (3,2) and (1,0), then merges the two pairs; it selects equality/ordering and applies branch-op inversion. Its normalized record supplies the actual predicate, taken-only alignment trap, architectural next PC, and forwarding/MEM controls. Jump write-enable qualification depends only on registered jump control and target alignment.\\n- MEM recovery overrides younger stalls/prediction, flushes ID/fetch, and kills EX controls/valid. Divider request, acceptance and effective wait are suppressed without resetting the divider or its completion bank. Payload capture remains independent of recovery, and the directly registered normal memory-address bank remains separate from result selection and resolution.\\n- A recovering owner is captured in the real MEM/WB bank with valid clear at E1. At E2 that entire bank is retained, valid asserts once, and `commit_pending` clears while the incoming recovery bubble is discarded. RVFI and register writes use this actual owner. Normal instructions and correct predictions retain ordinary commit timing. Reset cancels pending ownership.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed. The top-byte sign-bit transform is an equivalent implementation choice. Fetch masking and bus-stall equations were separated into explicit independent assignments to remove a false combinational-loop warning in cocotb's visibility-enabled Verilator build. A new optional profiling test binds a simulation-only observer to either revision; it does not change production RTL, ports, the cosim harness, or benchmark/evaluator files. `nret: 1`, strict decoding, ALU SLT/SLTU, multiplier/divider arithmetic, and the existing completion bank are unchanged.\\n\\nLocal validation is complete:\\n\\n| Gate | Result |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Requested literal Verilator lint command, `-Wall -Wno-MULTITOP` | PASS, no warnings/errors |\\n| Unit suite | 12 pytest runners PASS; 160 cocotb tests PASS, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic |\\n| Optional CoreMark observer | Executed separately for both revisions and both ELFs; its default pytest invocation is skipped |\\n| Unchanged `formal/run_all.sh`, automatic nret=1 wrapper/config | 53 PASS, 0 FAIL, including liveness, unique, PC forwarding, register and illegal-instruction checks |\\n| Formal attempts | First run PASS; final run after fetch-mask cleanup PASS; zero formal failures/fix attempts |\\n| Unchanged channel-0 contract check | PASS |\\n| Unchanged ISS selftest | PASS, 115 retirements compared |\\n| Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim.run_cosim` | PASS, selftest and the evaluator's CoreMark ELF |\\n| Unchanged marker/IPC gate | PASS; positive start/stop interval, 10 iterations, no elapsed-cycle substitution |\\n| CRC/UART/OOB, 2 KB and 6 KB | PASS for parent and candidate; EBREAK reached, no OOB, validation literal present |\\n| Unchanged Gowin synthesis and nextpnr scripts, seeds 1/2/3 | All six parent/candidate routes completed successfully |\\n| Diff whitespace and editing scope | PASS; only permitted RTL/tests plus this requested note |\\n\\nThe final formal run used an unchanged copy of the formal scripts, configuration, wrappers and upstream framework staged under `$TMPDIR`, because this worktree's formal framework is a symlink outside the writable worktree. The standard 20-step bounds and eight-compute-clock divider were retained. Formal is the unchanged ALTOPS gate; real arithmetic is covered by the real-operation unit suite and ISS comparisons. `formal-deep` was not run; the arithmetic implementation was not changed. Synthesis reports the same eight existing divider-array-to-register notices as the parent, with no new synthesis warnings.\\n\\nTests cover byte-ordering combinations and both merge levels, opposite signs and boundaries, all forwarding choices, full-address carry/wrap, legality/alignment, complete metadata holds, same-edge lookup/write collisions, and stale aliased snapshots. Pipeline tests compare complete retirements and accepted memory requests against the unchanged ISS under random and long directed bus stalls. They check both direction errors, correct prediction without discarded slots, two discarded slots on repair, changing JALR targets/link users, killed branches/jumps/stores/divides, misaligned/illegal controls, reset during deferred commit, and once-only training/retirement.\\n\\nAll 16 DIV/REM predecessor/successor operation pairs are exercised around recovering branches and JALR, plus all 16 pairs around predicted-taken/actually-not-taken errors. Without bus stalls, the DIV/REM-predecessor-to-recovering-control retirement gap is exactly two clocks, and the recovering-control-to-corrected-DIV/REM gap is exactly ten. Corrected divides launch E3, accept E11, and reach valid MEM/WB E12; their ordinary following consumer retires one clock later. A newly added test fixture was corrected to jump around its wrong-path sentinel before EBREAK, since EBREAK is a legal harness marker and does not halt hardware execution; the existing launch/retirement assertions were retained.\\n\\nThe actual evaluator fixture differs from the supplied 6 KB contract. The unchanged `bench/programs/Makefile` defaults to `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`, and `tools/eval/fpga.py` expects the canonical 2 KB CRCs. I preserved that fixture and separately compiled `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`. Both use the same unchanged sources, RV32IM/ILP32, `-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and identical ELFs for parent/candidate with `--bench --istall --dstall` and the harness's default deterministic stall seed. UART's compiler-flags text is hardcoded to `-O2`; the actual build command used `-O3`. Printed per-algorithm sizes are 666 and 2000 respectively, corresponding to total configurations of 2000 and 6000 bytes.\\n\\n| Working set | Parent marker cycles | Candidate marker cycles | Parent iterations/cycle | Candidate iterations/cycle | Cycle reduction |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB fixture | 4,491,485 | 4,265,800 | 2.2264351322558128e-6 | 2.3442261709409725e-6 | 5.024730% |\\n| 6 KB required validation | 32,836,671 | 31,223,347 | 3.0453757020618807e-7 | 3.202731596968128e-7 | 4.913178% |\\n\\nThese are exact ten-iteration marker-window ratios, before the evaluator's six-decimal IPC rounding. Both revisions report identical architectural control counts in each window. Correct/error direction counts below cover BRANCH and direct JAL; JALR is counted separately. For the parent, the sequential policy makes every taken direct transfer a predicted-not-taken error.\\n\\n| Size / revision | Correct direct predictions | Pred N / actual T | Pred T / actual N | JALR repairs | Total repairs | Table updates | Conditional branches | Jumps |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB parent | 235,163 | 343,792 | 0 | 13,735 | 357,527 | 0 | 529,588 | 63,102 |\\n| 2 KB candidate | 527,098 | 19,960 | 31,897 | 13,735 | 65,592 | 529,588 | 529,588 | 63,102 |\\n| 6 KB parent | 1,637,728 | 2,461,824 | 0 | 104,164 | 2,565,988 | 0 | 3,747,912 | 455,804 |\\n| 6 KB candidate | 3,726,127 | 154,642 | 218,783 | 104,164 | 477,589 | 3,747,912 | 3,747,912 | 455,804 |\\n\\n2 KB CRCs: seed `e9f5`, list `e714`, matrix `1fd7`, state `8e3a`, final `fcaf`. Required 6 KB CRCs: seed `8a02`, list `d4b0`, matrix `be52`, state `5e47`, final `5949`. Both runs contain `Correct operation validated.` and retire EBREAK with `oob:false`.\\n\\n| Routed seed | Parent Fmax MHz | Candidate Fmax MHz | Candidate limiting path |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 190.33 | 158.20 | ID/EX ALU op[3] to normal EX/MEM ALU result[23]; 2.87 ns logic + 3.45 ns routing |\\n| 2 | 184.84 | 164.96 | EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding to byte_equal[0] flop; 2.70 ns logic + 3.36 ns routing |\\n| 3 | 168.69 | 154.82 | ID/EX ALU op[3] to normal EX/MEM ALU result[23]; 2.87 ns logic + 3.59 ns routing |\\n| Median | 184.84 | 158.20 | 14.412465% lower Fmax |\\n\\nResources increased from 5,485 LUT4 / 2,119 DFF to 5,972 LUT4 / 2,301 DFF. All seeds fit and route. Final resynthesis after the fetch-mask cleanup has identical mapped cell names, types, parameters, port directions and connections to the routed netlist; only source-location attributes differ, so the reported routes apply to the final implementation.\\n\\nMapped dependency inspection found eight distinct summary flops: equality byte 0..3 maps to `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_160`..`_157`, and less-than byte 0..3 maps to `_164`..`_161`. Each D cone contains only its local operand byte and forwarding/hold controls; the top less-than cone additionally contains the registered signed mode. No summary D cone contains another summary flop, a cross-byte merge, or a completed branch predicate. The recovery-decision cone feeding deferred-commit ownership contains registered EX/MEM validity, decoder control, consumed direction, target alignment and all eight summary outputs, plus reset; it has no current EX operand/forwarding source. Jump alignment, rather than branch comparison, qualifies forwarding writes. All normal memory-address bits remain directly flop-driven. The seed-2 forwarding path terminates at a local summary as intended, but the other two seeds expose a slower ordinary ALU path. Moving the comparison boundary therefore does not establish an overall timing win.\\n\\nUsing median routed Fmax times the exact measured ratios, 2 KB fitness changes from 411.534270 to 370.856580 iterations/s; 6 KB changes from 56.290724 to 50.667214 iterations/s. Break-even Fmax for these measured cycle counts is 175.552289 MHz for 2 KB and 175.758482 MHz for 6 KB. The implementation remains below both thresholds. Residual ordinary ALU delay, local forwarding-to-byte comparison delay and routing overhead erase the cycle benefit; this candidate should be classified as a measured fitness regression, not an accepted improvement.\\n\\nEvidence is under `$TMPDIR/hyp-r14s2-validation/`: `formal-first.log`, `formal-final.log`, `unit-complete.log`, `unchanged-gates.json`, `mapped-audit.json`, each revision's `profile/results.json` and UART/observer output, and `parent/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` / `candidate/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`. The retained optional profiler is `cores/bench/test/test_predictor_profile.py` and can be invoked with RTL directory, scratch output directory, and the two ELF paths.\\n\\nELF SHA-256 identifiers:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `d389d4b612312ec5d48e8363eacfcf70e8ca7ef7b3766b8b3022e09a7370c8bf`\\n- 6 KB: `cf829b973dbe23254c6ff87f261f5927cf90eec69d6ed7e8124aee76785c9dd2`\\n- Selftest: `8f0f9078cdf34349a74d2b5d8d43f229be666dd9982145aff067685cddc100c7`\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T18:08:58.394926Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-003-r14s2 (regression, -9.88%): EX/MEM byte-summary flops cut the forwarding-to-full-branch-comparison path, but residual forwarding-to-byte-comparison and ordinary ALU-result selection limited median routed Fmax to 158.20 MHz, below the 175.55 MHz needed for the bimodal predictor's 5.02% reduction in 2 KB CoreMark cycles to break even.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path.cwd()\nfor d in [*reversed(p.parents),p]:\n f=d/'AGENTS.md'\n if f.exists():\n  print('\\\\nFILE',f)\n  print(f.read_text())\nfor f in sorted((p/'cores'/'bench').rglob('AGENTS.md')):\n print('\\\\nFILE',f)\n print(f.read_text())\nprint('\\\\nSTATUS')\nPY\n git status --short; sed -n '160,190p' /Users/bonetto/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path.cwd()\nfor d in [*reversed(p.parents),p]:\n f=d/'AGENTS.md'\n if f.exists():\n  print('\\\\nFILE',f)\n  print(f.read_text())\nfor f in sorted((p/'cores'/'bench').rglob('AGENTS.md')):\n print('\\\\nFILE',f)\n print(f.read_text())\nprint('\\\\nSTATUS')\nPY\n git status --short; sed -n '160,190p' /Users/bonetto/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nSTATUS\n\n### keywords\n\n- HWE Bench, ICLR, model capability, fixed benchmark contract, 15 rounds, 3 slots, CoreMark, V0 282.82, paper/iclr2027/main.tex\n\n## Task 2: Define three-repetition cohort and cost policy, success\n\n### rollout_summary_files\n\n- rollout_summaries/2026-09-05T17-38-53-5hEW-hwe_bench_model_capability_study_plan.md (cwd=/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament, rollout_path=/Users/bonetto/.codex/sessions/2026/09/05/rollout-2026-09-05T19-38-53-01a072a6-df12-7e61-a653-f14336ae4be6.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-05T19:00:14+00:00, thread_id=01a072a6-df12-7e61-a653-f14336ae4be6)\n\n### keywords\n\n- GPT-5.4, GPT-5.5, GPT-5.6 Sol, GPT-6 Astra, Kimi K3, Codex, OpenCode, sample standard deviation, models.json, STUDY_PLAN.md\n\n## User preferences\n\n- The user clarified this is “a benchmark” asking “How good can the models get” -> prioritize fair model comparisons under a fixed contract, not optimization-harness attribution. [Task 1]\n- Benchmark overfitting is acceptable; memory/search ablations are unnecessary, and a human baseline already exists -> do not add held-out workloads, harness ablations, or a new human study unless claims broaden. [Task 1]\n- Five runs are outside budget -> use three repetitions, show all endpoints, mean, and sample standard deviation; avoid fine-grained rankings. [Task 2]\n- Use Codex for new GPT runs and OpenCode for open-source runs; historical cost is unreliable -> retain runtime labels/confounding and report only verifiable new-run cost. [Task 2]\n\n## Reusable knowledge\n\n- Central question: CPU performance produced under fixed architectural contract/evaluation budget, and its relationship to verified model release date. Say “open-ended,” not literally unsaturable. Keep fixture, constraints, evaluator, candidate limits, and 15 rounds × 3 slots fixed. [Task 1][Task 2]\n- Planned matrix: existing GPT-5.4/5.5 OpenCode cohorts; new GPT-5.6 Sol/GPT-6 Astra via Codex at highest verified effort; Kimi K3 via OpenCode; three repetitions each. Equal proposal budgets do not prove equal inference compute. [Task 2]\n- Plot verified release date, treat reasoning variants as configurations rather than releases, show every endpoint plus mean/sample SD, and preserve raw usage. OAuth/subscription parsers returning `$0` are unavailable billing, not measured spend. [Task 2]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- Earlier planning assumed held-out transfer, second-core optimization, or harness ablations were required -> first establish whether the contribution is benchmark measurement or method superiority. [Task 1]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor row in rows:\n if row.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(row['id'], row['title'], 'fitness=',row.get('fitness'),'fmax=',row.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles=',row.get('cycles'))\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-003-r11s2','hyp-20260906-003-r13s2','hyp-20260906-003-r14s2']:\n row=next(r for r in rows if r.get('id')==rid)\n print('\\\\nDETAIL',rid)\n print('HYPOTHESIS:',row.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis'))\n notes=row.get('implementation_notes','')\n if rid.endswith('r13s2'):\n  print('NOTES:', notes)\n elif rid.endswith('r11s2'):\n  print('NOTES:', notes[-11000:])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor row in rows:\n if row.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(row['id'], row['title'], 'fitness=',row.get('fitness'),'fmax=',row.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles=',row.get('cycles'))\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-003-r11s2','hyp-20260906-003-r13s2','hyp-20260906-003-r14s2']:\n row=next(r for r in rows if r.get('id')==rid)\n print('\\\\nDETAIL',rid)\n print('HYPOTHESIS:',row.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis'))\n notes=row.get('implementation_notes','')\n if rid.endswith('r13s2'):\n  print('NOTES:', notes)\n elif rid.endswith('r11s2'):\n  print('NOTES:', notes[-11000:])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260906-003-r1s2 Small tagged bimodal predictor for repeated control transfers fitness= 291.2 fmax= 122.77 cycles= 4215937\nhyp-20260906-003-r2s2 Tagged branch prediction with registered training metadata fitness= 345.07 fmax= 145.48 cycles= 4215937\nhyp-20260906-003-r3s2 Stateless backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction fitness= 361.83 fmax= 154.13 cycles= 4259699\nhyp-20260906-003-r4s2 Early direct-JAL prediction with decode-certified redirect suppression fitness= 345.59 fmax= 153.52 cycles= 4442217\nhyp-20260906-003-r5s2 Backward-branch prediction with decode-encoded repair polarity fitness= 338.91 fmax= 146.05 cycles= 4309463\nhyp-20260906-003-r6s2 Tagged target prediction with MEM-stage validation and staged training fitness= None fmax= None cycles= None\nhyp-20260906-003-r7s2 Single-entry backedge target prediction with decode validation fitness= 268.66 fmax= 115.85 cycles= 4312067\nhyp-20260906-003-r8s2 Backward-branch prediction with validity-only EX annul fitness= 206.45 fmax= 88.97 cycles= 4309463\nhyp-20260906-003-r9s2 Backward prediction with alignment-independent EX recovery control fitness= 220.35 fmax= 94.96 cycles= 4309463\nhyp-20260906-003-r10s2 Backward prediction with balanced EX comparison fitness= 181.44 fmax= 78.19 cycles= 4309463\nhyp-20260906-003-r11s2 Backward prediction with asymmetric registered exit recovery fitness= 389.25 fmax= 169.75 cycles= 4360895\nhyp-20260906-003-r12s2 Fetch-certified single-entry backedge prediction with registered exit recovery fitness= 210.45 fmax= 91.0 cycles= 4324045\nhyp-20260906-003-r13s2 Bimodal direct-branch prediction with fully registered recovery fitness= 329.62 fmax= 140.61 cycles= 4265800\nhyp-20260906-003-r14s2 Bimodal prediction with branch comparison split across EX and MEM fitness= 370.86 fmax= 158.2 cycles= 4265800\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-003-r11s2\nHYPOTHESIS: Implement one static backward-taken conditional-branch predictor with\nasymmetric recovery. Correct predictions remain bubble-free. An\nunpredicted taken branch or an ordinary JAL/JALR retains the existing\none-slot EX redirect. Only a predicted-taken branch that resolves not\ntaken uses a registered recovery pulse and discards two younger slots.\nKeep JAL and JALR sequentially predicted. Add no target table, history\ntable, custom branch comparator, or arithmetic optimization.\n\nIF predecodes raw imem_data for BRANCH opcode 0x63 and legal funct3\nvalues 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, and 7. Predict taken only when instruction bit 31\nis set, the fetch PC is word aligned, and instruction bit 8 is clear.\nForm the exact sign-extended B immediate and add it to the full 32-bit\nPC modulo 2^32. Compute PC+4 independently. The raw eligibility bit\nselects these two addresses without stall, flush, or acceptance in\nthat data select. Only an accepted fetch advances this prediction;\nreset and recovery retain priority. Keep imem_addr directly driven\nby the PC register. A prediction never squashes its own instruction.\n\nCarry predicted_taken with the actual accepted instruction into ID/EX.\nID also registers immediate_branch = decoded_is_branch AND NOT\npredicted_taken. Keep architectural ctrl.is_branch and branch_op\nunchanged. Both new metadata bits belong to the ordinary payload\ncapture/hold domain; invalid metadata is inert. Preserve the winner's\ncontrol-zero bubbles and separation of payload capture from flush.\n\nPreserve the existing forwarded branch_cond computation and all six\narchitectural branch predicates. The immediate EX redirect uses\nimmediate_branch AND branch_cond in the existing taken-branch term,\nwith the existing jump term, validity and alignment/trap rules.\nPrediction suppression is already encoded in the registered branch\nenable: do not put an outcome XOR prediction, inverted repair polarity,\nfull next-PC comparison, or late-repair acceptance logic into this\nimmediate recovery cone. Existing held-owner and imem-stall redirect\nbehavior remains valid for this immediate path.\n\nIndependently register exit_pending when a valid, legal predicted\nbranch advances from EX with branch_cond false. Use the existing\nEX/MEM resolved pc_next as its registered recovery address, which is\nPC+4 for this outcome. Capture pc_next with the normal payload enable;\nthe late condition must not enable a wide target bank. exit_pending\nis a one-cycle event, cleared on reset and on every non-event clock,\nincluding memory holds and an older exit recovery. Generate it only\nwhen EX really advances, never repeatedly from a held owner. A held\npredicted branch keeps its instruction, operands and metadata until\nadvancement. A correctly taken prediction generates neither event.\nEligible predictions are certified aligned direct branches, so this\ndelayed path cannot belong to a load, store, divide, jump or trap.\n\nOn the following cycle exit_pending refers to the older branch now\nin EX/MEM. Its registered target takes priority over every younger\nEX redirect and fetch prediction, even if imem is unavailable. Kill\nthe younger ID/EX owner before it can enter EX/MEM, and flush the\nyounger combinational fetch slot. Force the killed EX/MEM controls\nand validity to zero. Suppress a killed instruction's divider request,\nresponse acceptance, effective div_wait and new exit event. The older\nbranch advances into MEM/WB normally except for the actual commit\ntiming described below. Do not reset or overwrite the divider result\nbank. A younger divide must be killed before its first request edge;\nit cannot already be active when this branch's exit event first appears.\nMake the registered exit kill override younger load-use/divider holds.\nKeep dmem-stall generation independent of recovery. The recovering\nbranch itself has no memory operation, so it cannot require a dmem hold.\n\nMake the delayed recovery's commit schedule part of the architecture.\nA two-slot repair followed immediately by the unchanged eight-compute-\nclock divider can otherwise produce an eleven-clock retirement gap.\nOn the registered-exit recovery edge, MEM/WB captures the real branch\nrecord with valid cleared and sets a one-bit commit_pending flag.\nOn the next edge, retain that complete record, set its valid bit once,\nand clear commit_pending. This is an actual one-clock postponement of\nwriteback/retirement, using the refill bubble already present in MEM;\nit must not delay the corrected fetch or target instruction execution.\nThe intervening EX/MEM entry is necessarily the bubble injected by\nexit recovery, so holding MEM/WB discards no legitimate instruction.\nReturn to ordinary MEM/WB capture on the next edge. Clear the pending\nflag on reset and preserve the existing dmem-hold forwarding behavior.\n\nApply that commit schedule only to the delayed-exit owner, never to\nan ordinary EX redirect, a correctly predicted branch, a load/store,\nor DIV/REM. Regfile write enable and every RVFI field must continue\nto describe the same actual MEM/WB owner and valid cycle; there is no\nfabricated retirement or RVFI-only timing change. With zero wait,\nthe delayed branch commits during refill and its immediately following\nDIV/REM should retire within ten clocks of it. This must pass the\nexisting formal liveness check unchanged. Check both the predecessor\nto branch and branch to successor gaps, not just the latter. Do not\nshorten the divider, change ALTOPS timing, or relax the formal bound\nto rescue this predictor if the proposed schedule proves incorrect.\n\nThe physical hypothesis is that the new inverted branch-outcome path\nterminates at one exit-event flop. It no longer directly drives IF\nmasking, ID-control RESET, or PC recovery. The ordinary taken-redirect\ncone retains its existing predicate polarity and registered enable;\nthe added late recovery arrives from a register. Inspect all three\nrouted seeds and mapped connectivity to verify this distinction,\nincluding exit-event D, immediate redirect, kill/commit controls and\nthe IF target adder. Retiming the exit path can still shift the worst\npath elsewhere, so this is a low-confidence net-fitness experiment.\n\nForecast a 3% fitness gain. The historical single-entry r7s2 recorded\n12,211 certified exits, suggesting that exit-only extra bubbles can\nbe much fewer than all unpredicted-transfer bubbles; that count is\nnot an exact prediction for this stateless implementation. Measure\naccepted correct predictions, registered exits, immediate repairs,\nmarker-window cycles and three-seed median Fmax on the identical\nworkload. A measured 3.5% cycle reduction, for example, only breaks\neven at about 177.62 MHz against the current 184.06 MHz winner.\nReport fitness from unrounded iterations/cycle, not rounded metadata.\n\nPreserve nret=1, every external port, strict decoding, taken-only\nalignment traps, suppressed trapped jump links, actual RVFI pc_next,\nmonotonic single retirement, all arithmetic and result-bank ownership,\nretained WB forwarding, and the directly registered memory address.\nRun unchanged lint, unit, formal including liveness, ISS cosim,\nCRC/UART, OOB, marker-window, resource and three-seed FPGA gates.\nIdentify the actual evaluated working set if it differs from the\nprompt without modifying the evaluation contract. Only bench RTL\nand its permitted cocotb tests are implementation targets.\n\nNOTES: # Backward prediction with asymmetric registered exit recovery\n\nImplemented the hypothesis in bench RTL and the permitted bench tests. Correctness gates pass; measured fitness regresses by **5.0128868324%**, so the forecast of a 3% gain is not supported by this experiment.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- IF recognizes only opcode 0x63, funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7, negative B immediates, an aligned PC, and instruction bit 8 clear. A raw eligibility bit selects the full-width PC+B-immediate sum or an independently computed PC+4. Acceptance only controls the PC enable; reset and recovery have priority. The memory address remains the PC register itself.\n- Accepted prediction metadata and the decode-folded `immediate_branch` enable use ID/EX's ordinary payload capture/hold domain. Architectural branch controls, control-zero bubbles, and the separate payload/flush domains remain intact.\n- EX retains the original forwarded comparator and all six predicates. Its immediate redirect uses the positive `immediate_branch && branch_cond` term and the existing jump/alignment rules. JAL/JALR remain sequentially predicted. Ordinary held-owner and imem-stall recovery behavior is preserved.\n- The separate `exit_pending` flop captures a legal predicted branch's false outcome only when EX advances. Every other clock clears the event, including a memory hold and an older exit kill. Its address is the existing EX/MEM `pc_next` payload bank, captured with the normal payload enable.\n- Registered exit recovery wins over younger redirects and predictions, flushes fetch/ID, and clears the killed EX/MEM controls/validity. It overrides younger load-use/divider holds and suppresses divider request, response acceptance, effective wait, and another exit event. Divider arithmetic and completion-bank contents are not reset by recovery.\n- MEM/WB captures the real exit branch with valid zero and `commit_pending` set on the recovery edge. The next edge retains its complete record and presents it valid once, consuming the guaranteed MEM bubble. Corrected fetch and target execution continue normally. Ordinary MEM/WB capture then resumes; retained forwarding during dmem holds is unchanged.\n\nRelative to the edge that registers the exit event: recovery is edge +1, the real branch is valid in MEM/WB at +2, an immediately following divide launches at +3, accepts its response at +11, and becomes valid in MEM/WB at +12. The branch-to-divider retirement gap is ten clocks. Directed tests measure a predecessor-to-branch gap of two clocks, or three with the preceding load-use interlock.\n\nNo changes were made to the comparator, decoder, arithmetic, divider latency/ALTOPS timing, external ports, `nret: 1`, or evaluation contracts. The implementation uses the existing module boundaries. The additional CoreMark measurement test generates a read-only model observer in its build directory and includes the original `test/cosim/main.cpp`; it asserts byte-for-byte stdout equality against the unchanged evaluator executable.\n\n## Validation\n\nAll generated builds and evaluation copies are under `$TMPDIR/r11s2-validation`. Contract files were copied unchanged there because the formal runner writes its staging directories beneath its own source tree. The evaluated RTL was byte-compared with the delivered RTL.\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: pass, zero warnings/errors. Channel-0 RVFI contract lint: pass.\n- `COREMARK_PROFILE=1 make test TARGET=bench`: **11 pytest runners passed**, including real/ALTOPS ALU and EX-bank suites, decoder, IF, ID, immediates, regfile, the CoreMark observer, and all **27 pipeline tests**. Existing assertions remain in use.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`: **53 passed, 0 failed**, including the unchanged liveness, unique, PC-forward/backward, register, and instruction checks. Local formal status: **pass on first attempt; zero RTL fixes/retries; no counterexample**.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: pass, **2 ELFs** (selftest ISS comparison and CoreMark CRC validation). Directed pipeline programs also compare architectural retirements against the unchanged Python ISS and separately compare accepted memory requests and divider launches.\n- Coverage includes 48 combinations of all six exit predicates, all four DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU successors, and ALU/load operand forwarding. Every zero-wait adjacent retirement gap is at most ten clocks; each exit-to-divider gap is exactly ten. Correct predictions have zero EX bubbles, ordinary repairs one, and exits two.\n- Additional coverage includes wrong-path loads/stores/divides, younger JAL/JALR priority, younger load-use dependencies, long older memory holds, unavailable imem during recovery, consecutive exits, illegal/misaligned transfers, and reset during either the exit event or pending commit. The complete deferred WB record, actual RVFI owner, strict order, PC continuity, and retained result banks are checked.\n- CoreMark reaches EBREAK, reports `oob:false`, emits both markers, passes every unchanged CRC check, and prints `Correct operation validated.`\n- Unchanged synthesis and all three nextpnr seeds complete normally. Resources: **5,675 LUT4 / 20,736**, **2,099 DFF / 15,552**. Synthesis emits only the eight existing divider-array lowering notices; Verilator lint is clean.\n\nAn added event-count assertion initially included a combinational offer on the terminal EBREAK observation cycle, where the test performs no further clock edge. The test now excludes that unaccepted offer. This required no RTL or ISS change.\n\n## Actual workload and measurements\n\nThis checkout evaluates **TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000**, **ITERATIONS=10**, compiled with **-O3**, with the unchanged default xorshift stall sequence (`--bench --istall --dstall`, seed 0xDEADBEEF). It is not the prompt's 6 KB workload. UART reports a 2K run and per-algorithm size 666. Its compiler-flags banner is stale (`-O2`); the actual unchanged Makefile invocation uses `-O3`. The fixture and validators were not altered.\n\nCoreMark ELF SHA-256: `574418d8f0933e2dc4adfe05aba090176d3af951ba897dc5d03cd34718af4fd4`.\n\nValidated CRCs: seed `e9f5`, list `e714`, matrix `1fd7`, state `8e3a`, final `fcaf`, matching this checkout's 2 KB evaluator.\n\n| Event | Whole program | Marker window [start, stop) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Accepted predicted fetches | 288,234 | 287,132 |\n| Accepted correct predictions (valid EX advancement) | 236,174 | 235,191 |\n| Registered exit recoveries | 52,060 | 51,941 |\n| Immediate repairs (advancing, surviving owner) | 123,153 | 122,336 |\n\nResolution counts exclude repeated offers during holds and younger killed owners; the fetch count records every accepted eligible fetch. The instrumented run's complete evaluator stdout, including marker cycles and UART, is identical to the ordinary run.\n\n| Measurement | HEAD predecessor | Candidate |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Start marker cycle | 15,801 | 15,490 |\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,507,286 | 4,376,385 |\n| Counted cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,360,895 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 184.06 (declared winner) | 169.75 (measured) |\n| Fitness, iterations/second | 409.79765044300495 | 389.25495798454216 |\n\nThe predecessor RTL was rebuilt from this worktree's HEAD into scratch and run with the identical ELF, harness, and stall flags. Its frequency is the existing winner's declared 184.06 MHz; the candidate's routed seeds are **174.98, 169.75, 167.11 MHz**.\n\nCycle reduction: **2.9075016392%**. Candidate unrounded iterations/cycle: **10 / 4,360,895 = 0.0000022931072635319124**. Fitness uses that fraction directly: **169.75e6 * 10 / 4,360,895 = 389.25495798454216**. The evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark: 0.000002` was not used. Break-even frequency for the measured cycle saving is **178.7084524829 MHz**; the candidate does not reach it.\n\n## Mapped and routed connectivity\n\nInspected `synth.json` and every routed seed's `pnr.json`, in addition to all three critical-path reports.\n\n- `cpu.u_ex.exit_pending_DFFR_Q` is a DFFR. Its D net has exactly one sink: that flop's D. Traversal from the registered `predicted_taken` bit reaches only this first register endpoint in both the mapped design and **each routed seed**. It has no direct path to PC recovery, IF masking, ID-control reset, or commit enables without crossing the event register.\n- `immediate_branch` is a separate ordinary DFFRE (`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_33`), sharing the payload CE and reset-only payload reset with predicted metadata. Its Q feeds the ordinary immediate term and does not reach the exit-event D. The mapper shares a complemented comparator result; the immediate LUT (`INIT=A0E0`) still implements `valid && (jump || immediate_branch && branch_cond)`. There is no prediction/outcome XOR or late repair qualification in that term.\n- The 32 exit-target bits are precisely EX/MEM `pc_next[31:0]`. In the zero-wait FPGA wrapper they map to 32 ordinary DFFRs with no event-controlled CE. Registered exit Q supplies kill/recovery and the pending-commit flop. `commit_pending` drives the retained MEM/WB bank's capture enables (321 mapped CE sinks); the existing WB-valid flop implements the real deferred record schedule. That broad registered control fanout remains a physical cost.\n- The IF target has a separate 31-cell carry chain for bits 1..31, with immediate bit 0 hardwired zero, starting from raw instruction bit 8 and PC bit 1. Independent PC+4 has a separate 30-cell carry chain starting at PC bit 2. This is the normal constant-bit simplification of the two full-width RTL sums, not a truncated PC or an operand-select adder. IF tests cover high-address carry, wraparound, and all eligibility bits.\n\n| Seed | Fmax, MHz | Routed worst path | Logic / route, ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\n| 1 | 174.98 | FPGA stimulus `lfsr[18]` to ID/EX `rs1_val[23]` D | 2.39 / 3.32 |\n| 2 | 169.75 | ID/EX `rd[3]` (also instruction bit 10) through load-use control to ID/EX `alu_op[2]` RESET | 2.51 / 3.38 |\n| 3 | 167.11 | ID/EX `alu_op[3]` through normal ALU selection to EX/MEM normal result bit 6 D | 2.99 / 2.99 |\n\nThe predictor-specific exit qualification is registered as intended, but the frequency loss elsewhere overwhelms the cycle saving. No arithmetic change, reduced divider latency, fabricated retirement, or relaxed liveness bound was used to improve the result.\n\nEvidence beneath the scratch root: `formal-gate.log`, `final-unit-gate.log`, `cosim-gate.json`, `fpga-gate.json`, `predecessor-markers.json`, `mapped-connectivity.json`, `routed-connectivity.json`, `cores/bench/sim_build/prediction_profile/prediction-counters.json`, and `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` plus `pnr.json`.\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-003-r13s2\nHYPOTHESIS: Implement one 64-entry bimodal conditional-direction predictor with fully\nregistered control-transfer recovery. Compute direct targets from the\ncurrent fetched instruction. Early direct-JAL steering belongs to this\nsame front end; JALR uses sequential prediction and registered recovery.\nKeep ordinary pipeline depth and the existing arithmetic resources.\n\nIndex 64 two-bit saturating counters with the full fetch PC's bits [7:2].\nReset counters to weakly not taken (01); their MSB predicts direction.\nThe table is deliberately untagged: aliasing changes prediction accuracy,\nnever architectural targets or legality. A raw fetch predecoder admits\nonly BRANCH opcode 0x63 with funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7, or JAL opcode 0x6f.\nRequire a word-aligned source and target for taken prediction. For these\naligned source PCs, B-immediate alignment is instruction bit 8 clear and\nJ-immediate alignment is instruction bit 21 clear. Predict an eligible\nconditional branch taken when its counter MSB is set, and an eligible JAL\ntaken unconditionally. Compute the exact B/J sign extension and full\n32-bit PC-relative target modulo 2^32. Compute PC+4 independently.\n\nSelect the target using raw predecode/table outputs independently of\nstall, flush, redirect, and acceptance. These controls govern PC capture,\nnot the prediction data select. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC\nregister. Only actual fetch acceptance may apply a prediction. Carry its\nconsumed predicted_taken bit and two-bit counter snapshot with that same\ninstruction into ID/EX; holds retain the complete metadata. A prediction\nmust not squash its own instruction. Raw predecode uses the current\nimem_data, so stale direction state cannot steer ordinary, illegal, or\nmisaligned instructions to a cached address. There is no stored target,\ntarget tag comparison, return stack, or speculative history rollback.\n\nEX preserves the existing forwarded predicates, exact branch/jump targets,\nJAL/JALR link results, taken-only alignment faults, and actual pc_next.\nFor a direct branch or JAL, its consumed prediction is wrong when the\npredicted_taken bit differs from the actual legal, aligned transfer\ndecision. A direction difference may conservatively repair even if the\ndirect displacement happens to equal +4. Every legal aligned JALR requests\nrecovery to its existing resolved target. Illegal encodings and invalid\nentries cannot recover; a misaligned transfer keeps the existing trap,\nsuppressed jump link, and sequential continuation. A predicted direct\ntarget is exact by construction, so no generic full-next-PC comparison\nis needed and a load, store, or DIV/REM can never own prediction recovery.\n\nRegister recovery_pending only when the resolving control instruction\nactually advances from EX, with reset, memory hold, and older recovery\ncancellation handled explicitly. Clear the event on every non-event\nclock. Reuse EX/MEM's architectural pc_next as the recovery address,\ncaptured with the ordinary payload enable independently of the event.\nRemove ALL immediate EX redirects, including unpredicted conditional\nbranches, JAL, and JALR. The live branch comparison, forwarded JALR target,\nlegality, alignment, and direction-mismatch cones must terminate in\nregisters before affecting PC recovery, fetch masking, ID squash, or\nyounger-EX cancellation. This complete cut is the distinguishing timing\nexperiment; retaining an ordinary immediate-repair fallback defeats it.\n\nIn the next cycle recovery_pending belongs to the older EX/MEM control\ninstruction. Its registered target overrides younger predictions and\nredirects fetch even when imem is unavailable. Kill the younger ID/EX\nowner before EX/MEM capture, clear its complete controls and validity,\nand flush the younger combinational fetch slot. Suppress its divider\nrequest, response acceptance, effective div_wait, training, and recovery\nevent. A killed younger divide cannot already have launched: it entered\nEX on the edge that registered this older recovery event. Do not reset\nthe divider or disturb its registered completion bank. The recovering\ncontrol instruction has no memory request, so dmem readiness cannot hold\nit. Keep dmem-stall generation independent, and give registered recovery\npriority over younger load-use/divider holds. Correct predictions incur\nno squash; a recovery discards two younger slots under zero wait.\n\nMake a real one-clock postponement of the recovering instruction's\nwriteback/retirement part of this recovery protocol. On the recovery\nedge, MEM/WB captures its complete architectural record with valid clear\nand sets commit_pending. On the next edge retain that record, assert\nvalid once, and clear commit_pending; normal capture resumes afterward.\nThe overwritten incoming MEM record is necessarily the invalid EX/MEM\nbubble injected by recovery. Corrected fetch and execution continue\nduring this commit delay. For a JAL/JALR owner, its link write occurs\non this same actual WB-valid schedule, with the ordinary forwarding\npaths exposing the correct link to its target. All RVFI fields and\nregfile write enable must identify this real owner and its valid cycle.\n\nApply deferred commit only to the recovering branch/jump, never an\narithmetic or memory owner or a correctly predicted transfer. With the\nevent captured at edge E0, recovery occurs at E1 and the real owner is\nvalid in MEM/WB at E2. A corrected DIV/REM successor launches at E3,\naccepts at E11, and becomes valid in MEM/WB at E12: a ten-clock adjacent\nretirement gap. Check both neighboring gaps, including a predecessor\nDIV/REM and a recovering JALR with forwarding. Preserve the existing\nformal liveness bound, ALTOPS timing, and eight-compute-clock divider;\nfabricated retirements or RVFI-only delay are forbidden. Reset cancels\npending recovery and commit ownership. Existing memory-hold WB operand\nretention remains intact.\n\nTrain each surviving, legal conditional branch once on EX advancement,\nusing the actual outcome to saturate its saved counter snapshot. Capture\ntrain_pending and the resulting two-bit value into registers, reusing\nEX/MEM source PC for the index. The next clock writes the table using\nonly this registered event, index, and value. A recovering branch may\ntrain; its killed successor may not. Do not train repeatedly during\nholds or add a combinational update-to-lookup bypass. Lookup observes\npre-edge state. An aliased or old counter snapshot is an accuracy loss,\nnot a correctness hazard. Table payload/reset cost must be measured.\n\nForecast a 4 percent net fitness gain with low confidence. For example,\nfive percent fewer cycles with one percent lower Fmax yields about\n4.21 percent improvement; at that cycle saving the 184.84 MHz winner's\nbreak-even frequency is about 175.60 MHz. These are conditional targets,\nnot measurements. Two-slot misses, small-table aliasing, the IF target\nadder, and deferred-commit fanout can erase the gain. Historical cycle\ncounts include a documented 2 KB evaluator fixture and must not be\nrepresented as measured 6 KB results. Record the actual evaluated ELF,\nworking set, marker-window cycles, accepted predictions, both direction\nerrors, JALR recoveries, and table writes. Preserve the required 6 KB\nCRC/UART/OOB/marker checks and report any existing fixture discrepancy\nexplicitly without changing benchmark sources or evaluator contracts.\n\nInspect mapped dependencies and every routed seed: no live EX predicate\nor target-alignment cone may reach fetch/ID squash without crossing\nrecovery_pending. Also inspect recovery-event D, table lookup-to-PC,\ncounter-write controls, recovery fanout, and commit enables; moving the\nlimiting path is not evidence of improved timing. Use unrounded\niterations/cycle times three-seed median Fmax for the final fitness.\nDuring implementation run unchanged lint, unit, formal/liveness, ISS\ncosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker, resource, and FPGA gates. Preserve nret=1,\nall external ports, strict decoder defaults, monotonic RVFI order and\nresolved PC continuity, result-bank ownership, and the directly\nregistered normal dmem-address bank. Scope is cores/bench RTL and tests.\n\nNOTES: Implemented the 64-entry bimodal direct-branch predictor and fully registered recovery. Correctness gates pass. The forecast fitness gain was not achieved: the measured three-seed median is 140.61 MHz, and the 2 KB evaluator fitness is 329.6216418960102 iterations/s. The separately measured required 6 KB workload yields 45.03360898496885 iterations/s. Neither number uses the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark` field.\n\nChanges:\n\n- Added 64 untagged two-bit counters, indexed by fetch PC[7:2], reset to 01. The table has no target storage, tags, bypass, or speculative history.\n- IF validates current raw BRANCH funct3/JAL encodings and source/displacement alignment. A shared full-width B/J target adder uses the raw opcode-selected signed immediate; PC+4 has an independent adder. Prediction data selection is independent of stalls, acceptance, flush, and recovery. Only fetch acceptance advances by prediction, and imem_addr remains the PC register itself.\n- IF/ID and ID/EX carry consumed predicted_taken and the two-bit snapshot. ID payload capture remains independent of validity/control flush; holds preserve every payload field and all prediction metadata.\n- EX computes actual predicates, targets, traps, links, and architectural pc_next as before. Direct direction mismatch and every legal aligned JALR generate recovery only when the control owner advances. All immediate EX redirects were removed. recovery_pending clears on every non-event edge, including holds and older cancellation.\n- The registered event kills the younger EX owner, suppresses divider launch/accept/wait and predictor events, clears EX/MEM controls/validity, flushes ID and the combinational fetch slot, and redirects using EX/MEM.pc_next even when imem is unavailable. Memory backpressure remains independently computed. The divider resource, completion bank, eight-compute-clock protocol, arithmetic, and direct normal memory-address bank are unchanged.\n- On recovery E1, MEM/WB captures the entire recovering record invalid and sets commit_pending. At E2 that same record is retained and becomes valid once. Regfile write enable and RVFI use that real MEM/WB-valid schedule; existing forwarding exposes jump links to corrected successors. The overwritten E2 incoming record is the invalid recovery bubble. Correct fetch proceeds during the delay. Reset cancels both event and commit ownership.\n- Conditional training is registered once per advancing surviving nontrapping branch, using its saved snapshot and actual outcome. The next edge writes using registered train_pending/train_value and EX/MEM source PC. Killed successors and held owners cannot train. Untaken branches with unaligned displacements remain legal and can train not taken.\n- Hazard outputs use independent continuous assignments. The load-use inputs use exactly the same masked raw instruction bits as IF, avoiding a packed-metadata dependency cycle in Verilator's public-signal simulation mode without changing architectural behavior.\n\nTests and local gates:\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings. Public-flat-rw lint also passes without warnings. git diff --check and the unchanged channel-0 contract scanner pass.\n- Final unchanged pytest entry point over cores/bench/test: 11 runners passed, 156 cocotb cases passed, including both real and ALTOPS arithmetic/result-bank suites. Pipeline cases include many directed subcases, including all 96 combinations of six taken predicates and four DIV/REM predecessor/successor operations, plus 16 JALR and 16 direct-JAL combinations.\n- New table/fetch tests cover all entries/states, reset/write precedence, no pre-edge bypass, aliasing, all branch funct3 values, changed instructions, JAL/JALR distinctions, source/target alignment, signed full-PC carries/wrap, and prediction selection while acceptance is blocked.\n- Pipeline tests compare complete architectural retirements and accepted memory requests against the unchanged ISS, check monotonic order and resolved PC continuity, and monitor table state, once-only training, real deferred WB ownership, and complete metadata holds. They cover both direction errors, zero-slot hits, two-slot repairs, killed stores/loads/divides, forwarded links and changing JALR targets, long imem/dmem holds, consecutive repairs, and reset in both recovery and commit phases.\n- Recovering branch/JALR following DIV/REM: adjacent retirement gap 2. Corrected DIV/REM after recovery: gap exactly 10. With E0 event registration, recovery E1, owner valid E2, corrected divide launches E3, accepts E11 and is valid E12. Existing maximum-ten-clock and eight-compute-clock assertions remain enforced. Correctly predicted JAL has predecessor gap 1 and divide-successor gap 9.\n- An eligible aligned JAL cannot naturally mispredict with unconditional early JAL steering. Its defensive unpredicted-recovery case is exercised directly at EX, including displacement +4; full-pipeline JAL tests check the naturally reachable correct-prediction case. No fabricated retirement or prediction fault injection was used.\n- Local formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness, reg, PC forward/backward, unique, illegal and all RV32IM instruction checks. Two complete runs passed; the second checked final logical RTL after splitting the simulation dependency cycle. No formal counterexample and no correctness-fix retries. An initial setup invocation stopped before generating checks because default Python lacked dependencies; the installed conda Python resolved this. The unchanged run_all.sh, checks_si.cfg, and wrapper_si.sv were staged under TMPDIR, with nret=1 auto-detection and JOBS=6. No check/depth/ALTOPS changes.\n- Unchanged ISS/cosim evaluator: PASS, both selftest.elf and CoreMark CRC validation. Unchanged make fpga TARGET=bench: PASS, all three routes completed. NRET=1 and BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv select the required existing wrappers.\n- Required 6 KB checks also PASS separately: seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949; ITERATIONS=10; literal \"Correct operation validated.\"; ebreak=true, oob=false, both MMIO markers present, positive bracket duration.\n\nFixture discrepancy, preserved explicitly:\n\nThe current benchmark Makefile defaults to TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 and -O3, and tools/eval/fpga.py validates the 2 KB CRC set e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf. Therefore the unchanged evaluator's reported result is a 2 KB result, not a measured 6 KB result. No benchmark sources, evaluator constants, or contracts were changed. A separate scratch build used the unchanged Makefile's supported COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3 parameters and the unchanged simulator, with explicit required 6 KB checks. The standard validator would reject this 6 KB UART because its constants currently describe 2 KB.\n\nBoth builds use -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -O3, ITERATIONS=10, and the unchanged deterministic --bench --istall --dstall bus model. UART's hardcoded \"Compiler flags: -O2\" is stale; build logs show -O3. \"CoreMark Size\" prints per-algorithm size after division by three: 666 for TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000 and 2000 for TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000.\n\nScratch evidence root: $TMPDIR/r13s2-verify, resolving in this session to /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep2/.tmp/r13s2-verify.\n\n| Measurement | Evaluator fixture, 2 KB | Required workload, 6 KB |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Actual ELF, relative to evidence root | bench/programs/coremark.elf | six-kb/bench/programs/coremark.elf |\n| ELF SHA-256 | 865294625fc3d7a1a97ec51364363403f427688c7fae81615d188842c053df73 | d67eb75d61d307dce8fe578faba54b8abd48b6bb6359f732143dfa33bbdf07cd |\n| Start marker cycle | 15,304 | 53,904 |\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,281,104 | 31,277,251 |\n| Marker-window cycles | 4,265,800 | 31,223,347 |\n| Exact iterations/cycle | 10 / 4,265,800 | 10 / 31,223,347 |\n| Window accepted taken predictions | 356,784 | 2,530,324 |\n| Window predicted NT, actually taken errors | 19,960 | 154,642 |\n| Window predicted taken, actually NT errors | 31,897 | 218,783 |\n| Window JALR recoveries | 13,735 | 104,164 |\n| Window table writes | 529,588 | 3,747,912 |\n| Window registered recoveries | 65,592 | 477,589 |\n| Whole-run accepted taken predictions | 358,549 | 2,535,419 |\n| Whole-run predicted NT, actually taken errors | 20,145 | 155,110 |\n| Whole-run predicted taken, actually NT errors | 32,093 | 219,285 |\n| Whole-run JALR recoveries | 13,794 | 104,223 |\n| Whole-run table writes | 532,346 | 3,755,488 |\n| Fitness, median MHz * 1e6 * exact iterations/cycle | 329.6216418960102 | 45.03360898496885 |\n\nCounters were observed by measure_elf() in test_branch_predictor.py using a generated observation-only simulator copy under cores/bench/sim_build/observe. Its entire stdout, including architectural final retirement, markers, cycle counts, UART and OOB status, matched the independently run unchanged simulator byte for byte for each ELF. Window counters cover [start marker, stop marker); whole-run counters include setup/printing and speculative accepted predictions. Synthesizable RTL contains no measurement counters. Full records are predictor-2k.json and predictor-6k.json.\n\nPhysical results and dependency inspection:\n\n| Seed | Routed Fmax MHz | Critical source | Critical destination | Logic / routing ns |\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 136.76 | EX/MEM rd[4] | recovery_pending.D | 3.97 / 3.34 |\n| 2 | 141.58 | EX/MEM rd[0] | recovery_pending.D | 4.09 / 2.98 |\n| 3 | 140.61 | EX/MEM rd[4] | recovery_pending.D | 3.97 / 3.14 |\n\nEach critical path runs through forwarding selection and branch comparison into the recovery-event register. Intermediate names containing train_value refer to shared mapped comparison logic, not an unregistered table write. Median Fmax is 140.61 MHz. Resource report: 6018 LUT4, 2249 FF; four DPX9B memory blocks and one MULT36X36 remain. Relative to the recorded parent metadata (5485 LUT4 / 2119 FF), this is +533 LUT4 / +130 FF. Synthesis's eight warnings are the unchanged divider's array-to-register lowering notices, not lint violations.\n\ninspect_recovery_netlist() passed on synth.json and every routed pnr_seed{1,2,3}/pnr.json. It traverses actual cell connections and reconstructs routed vector aliases through preserved cell output pins. The PC D/enable inputs, ID control/valid capture, and younger-EX valid capture have no combinational roots in EX operand values, forwarded result data, EX PC, or EX immediate; recovery_pending.Q is present in every cancellation cone. The recovery event is exactly one real DFFR. imemAddr equals the PC Q bits. No immediate EX predicate/target-alignment fallback remains.\n\nThe event D cone contains 361 mapped / 367 routed combinational cells. Recovery Q directly drives 123 input pins. The table is exactly 128 DFFE payload bits; the union of write/reset/decode cones contains 210 LUT3, and all write roots are exclusively reset/constants and registered training event/value/source-PC index. No update-to-lookup bypass exists. The table-dependent PC/ID consumer cone contains 93 LUT4, 16 LUT3, 1 LUT2, and 38 MUX2_LUT5 (includes downstream selection/metadata logic). Commit-pending D has two combinational cells. Commit Q has three direct sinks; its single inverted payload-enable net drives 319 MEM/WB enable pins and contains no live EX operand/target dependency. Detailed evidence is dependencies.json and each unchanged nextpnr.log.\n\nFor comparison, the exact starting bench RTL from HEAD 12ca6b0c4228fd491171ddb619667799976b4eae was separately compiled in scratch and run on these same ELFs and bus schedule. Its measured marker windows were 4,491,485 cycles (2 KB) and 32,836,671 (6 KB), with matching CRC/UART/OOB checks. Thus cycles improved 5.0247301282% and 4.9131777092%, respectively. Using the parent's recorded, not rerouted here, 184.84 MHz from core.yaml, candidate relative fitness is -19.9042057860% (2 KB) and -19.9981712734% (6 KB). Break-even frequencies at these measured cycle savings would be 175.552288831 and 175.758482322 MHz. Moving the limiting path to recovery-event D did not improve fitness; the low-confidence +4% forecast is rejected by these measurements.\n\nAll edited RTL/tests are within cores/bench. The only separate document is this explicitly requested implementation_notes.md. Original contracts, other cores, arithmetic resources, formal depths, and evaluator sources remain unchanged. Temporary copies, generated binaries, logs, and route evidence are build artifacts under the authorized bench simulation directory or TMPDIR.\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-003-r14s2\nHYPOTHESIS: Implement one 64-entry bimodal direct-control predictor whose branch\nvalidation is partitioned between EX and MEM. Keep the same prediction\npolicy as r13s2 so the principal experimental variable is the validation\nboundary: EX registers independent byte-comparison summaries; MEM\ncombines those summaries and performs recovery. Do not register a full\nbranch predicate or full misprediction decision after forwarding in EX.\nOrdinary instructions retain their existing execution/retirement stages.\n\nIndex 64 two-bit saturating counters with fetch PC[7:2], reset to 01.\nRaw imem_data predecode admits only legal BRANCH funct3 0/1/4/5/6/7 and\ndirect JAL. A conditional predicts taken from the counter MSB; an\neligible direct JAL predicts taken unconditionally. Require aligned\nsource and target: with aligned PC, B immediate bit 1 is instr[8] and\nJ immediate bit 1 is instr[21]. Form exact sign-extended B/J immediates,\nfull-width modulo-2^32 targets, and an independent PC+4. Select prediction\ndata using raw predecode/counter state, independently of acceptance,\nstalls, flush, and repair. Apply it only on actual fetch acceptance.\nKeep imem_addr directly driven by the PC register. JALR predicts PC+4;\nthere is no BTB, target truncation, RAS, or speculative history.\n\nCarry the consumed predicted_taken and two-bit counter snapshot with\nthe accepted instruction through ID/EX and EX/MEM. ID also registers\nwhether the branch is signed relational. Metadata uses the ordinary\npayload capture/hold domain; controls and valid alone determine whether\nit has an owner. Preserve control-zero bubbles, full held metadata, the\nexisting fetch NOP mask, and separation of payload enables from flush.\n\nIn EX use the existing forwarded rs1 and rs2. For each of four independent\neight-bit slices j, compute E[j] = (rs1_byte == rs2_byte) and\nL[j] = (rs1_byte < rs2_byte). Slices 0 through 2 compare unsigned. Slice 3\ncompares signed bytes for BLT/BGE and unsigned bytes otherwise; this\nmakes lexicographic combination produce the correct signed or unsigned\nwhole-word ordering. Equal summaries are identical for both modes.\nRegister these eight summary bits at the EX/MEM boundary with ordinary\npayload capture. All four comparisons operate in parallel. No cross-\nslice merge, branch-op selection, direction XOR, or repair qualification\nmay precede this register boundary. Do not replicate comparators over\nevery forwarding-source pair or alter ALU SLT/SLTU and M arithmetic.\n\nEX independently registers full control-target and fall-through addresses\nwithout consulting the branch outcome. Reuse existing EX/MEM fields:\nraw branch_target can hold PC+B/J immediate or the bit-zero-cleared\nforwarded JALR sum; raw pc_next can hold PC+4. Their payload enables must\nnot depend on outcome or recovery. Carry decoder controls and actual\npost-forward operand values as before. EX must no longer compute a\nseparate full-width branch comparator for pc_next, alignment traps,\ntraining, or redirect: those consumers all use the registered summaries.\n\nAdd a combinational MEM control resolver between the raw EX/MEM record\nand its MEM/forwarding consumers. Merge summaries in two balanced levels:\nmerge (3,2) and (1,0), then merge those pairs. For high/low children use\nE = E_high AND E_low and L = L_high OR (E_high AND L_low).\nSelect E for BEQ/BNE or L for relational branches and XOR branch_op[0]\nto obtain the architectural predicate. Use that same predicate for the\nactual branch_taken, taken-only target-alignment trap, resolved pc_next,\nand predictor outcome. Resolve JAL/JALR from registered target/control\nfields. Preserve sequential continuation and suppressed jump-link write\non target misalignment, and preserve decoder illegality.\n\nThe resolver emits a normalized architectural EX/MEM record; raw pc_next\nis only its registered fall-through candidate, never RVFI's unresolved\nanswer. Feed normalized controls to forwarding as well as MEM. Qualify\njump reg_write using registered jump alignment alone: a branch already\nhas reg_write=0, so its comparison must not enter another instruction's\nforwarding-write-enable cone. Memory controls remain unchanged and\nindependent of branch summaries. Keep the directly registered normal\nEX/MEM memory-address bank separate from this resolver and result muxes.\n\nCompute recovery in MEM from valid, legal registered control ownership\nand the resolved decision. A direct transfer repairs when consumed\npredicted_taken differs from its actual legal aligned transfer; every\nlegal aligned JALR repairs to its target. Conservatively repairing a\ndirection mismatch with displacement +4 is acceptable. Misaligned\ntransfers that were necessarily predicted sequentially trap and keep\nPC+4. Illegal or ordinary instructions never own recovery. There is no\nimmediate EX redirect fallback and no additional recovery-event register\nafter the MEM merge. With summaries captured at E0, MEM repairs at E1:\ncorrect predictions discard zero slots and errors discard two slots.\n\nMEM recovery overrides imem backpressure and younger fetch prediction,\nflushes ID/fetch, and kills the younger EX owner before EX/MEM capture.\nSuppress its divider request, acceptance, effective div_wait, and all\npredictor actions; emit zero EX/MEM controls and valid. A killed younger\ndivide cannot already have launched because it entered EX at E0. Do not\nreset the divider or completion bank. Give older recovery precedence\nover younger load-use/divider holds. Compute dmem stalls independently;\na recovering control owner has no memory request. When an older memory\noperation stalls a branch in EX, retain its complete operands/metadata\nand capture summaries only on actual advancement. Preserve held-WB\nforwarding and once-only retirement under arbitrary bus stalls.\n\nRetain r13s2's real deferred commit for a recovering branch or jump. At\nrepair edge E1, MEM/WB captures the entire resolved owner with valid clear\nand sets commit_pending. At E2, retain that record, assert valid once,\nand clear commit_pending; the discarded incoming MEM record is the\nrecovery bubble. Corrected fetching and execution continue. Regfile\nwrites, jump-link forwarding, and all RVFI fields must describe this\nactual WB owner and valid schedule. Correct predictions and ordinary\ninstructions use normal commit. Reset cancels pending ownership.\nA corrected divide launches E3, accepts E11, and reaches valid MEM/WB\nat E12, ten clocks after its recovering predecessor. Check both adjacent\nretirement gaps, including a DIV/REM predecessor. Preserve the existing\nformal bound and eight-compute-clock divider; do not use RVFI-only delays\nor fabricate retirement to work around the r6s2 liveness failure.\n\nTrain a surviving nontrapping conditional branch once on MEM advancement,\nincluding an owner causing recovery. Saturate its saved counter snapshot\nusing the MEM predicate, then register train_pending, index, and value.\nThe following edge writes the table from those registered fields alone.\nLookup observes pre-edge contents, with no update-to-lookup bypass.\nHeld, invalid, and killed owners cannot train. The extra training delay\nand aliased stale snapshots may reduce accuracy but cannot change the\narchitecturally certified target or bypass validation.\n\nForecast a 4 percent net fitness improvement with low confidence. Five\npercent fewer cycles with one percent lower Fmax would yield about\n4.21 percent higher fitness; the corresponding break-even frequency is\nabout 175.60 MHz against 184.84 MHz. These are conditional estimates.\nByte-comparator mapping, MEM-merge-to-squash depth, target addition, IF\ntarget selection, training staleness, or deferred-commit fanout can erase\nthe benefit. Inspect every routed seed and mapped dependencies: EX\nforwarding paths must terminate at local summary flops before a full\npredicate is formed, while every repair input must originate in the\nregistered MEM owner. Merely moving the worst endpoint is not success.\n\nDuring implementation run unchanged lint, unit, formal including liveness,\nISS cosim, CRC/UART/OOB, marker-window, resource and three-seed FPGA gates.\nMeasure exact iterations/cycle, correct predictions, both direction\nerrors, JALR repairs, table updates, and median routed Fmax on identical\nELFs for parent and candidate. Identify the actual working set: prior\nevaluator logs describe a 2 KB fixture and separate 6 KB measurements.\nPreserve the required 6 KB validation and report any fixture discrepancy\nwithout changing benchmark or evaluator files. Keep nret=1, all ports,\nstrict decoding, true resolved RVFI PCs, monotonic order, arithmetic and\nresult-bank ownership, and all hard invariants. This proposal targets\nonly cores/bench RTL and its permitted cocotb tests.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The recent predictors saved about 5% of cycles, but lost more in clock frequency. I’m checking the remaining forwarding-to-comparison path for a validation design that could preserve that cycle saving."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-003-r6s2','hyp-20260906-003-r13s2']:\n row=next(r for r in rows if r.get('id')==rid)\n print('\\\\nDETAIL',rid)\n print('HYPOTHESIS:',row['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n if rid.endswith('r6s2'):\n  print('OUTCOME:',row.get('outcome'),'ERROR:',row.get('error'))\n  print('NOTES:',row.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor rid in ['hyp-20260906-003-r6s2','hyp-20260906-003-r13s2']:\n row=next(r for r in rows if r.get('id')==rid)\n print('\\\\nDETAIL',rid)\n print('HYPOTHESIS:',row['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n if rid.endswith('r6s2'):\n  print('OUTCOME:',row.get('outcome'),'ERROR:',row.get('error'))\n  print('NOTES:',row.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-003-r6s2\nHYPOTHESIS: Add one 16-entry direct-mapped bimodal BTB, and resolve prediction errors\nfrom registered EX/MEM state in MEM. This moves recovery one cycle later\nwithout adding a stage to ordinary instruction execution or retirement.\nCorrect predictions still have no control-flow bubble; a misprediction\ndiscards two younger slots instead of the existing one. The hypothesis\nis that shorter recovery and training paths preserve enough Fmax to\noutweigh that extra miss penalty.\n\nIndex the BTB with fetch PC[5:2], compare the full PC[31:6] tag, and store\naligned target[31:2], a valid bit, and a two-bit saturating counter.\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set selects the stored target; otherwise\nselect full-width PC+4. Legal conditional branches train direction;\nJAL and JALR learn their latest aligned target and use counter 11.\nReset clears validity; uninitialized payload must never affect a miss.\nLookup depends only on PC and table state, without instruction predecode,\nan instruction-immediate fetch adder, or stall/flush qualification in\nthe target-select data cone. Keep imem_addr directly driven by the PC\nregister. Prediction is applied only on actual fetch acceptance.\n\nCarry the exact consumed predicted_next_pc, lookup hit, and counter\nsnapshot into ID/EX. Metadata follows the accepted instruction and\nholds through load/decode holds, memory backpressure, and divider waits.\nPreserve the winner's separation of payload capture from control/valid\nclearing: dynamic squashes and invalid bubbles clear controls, while\npayload flops retain their ordinary capture/hold enables. Metadata on\ninvalid entries is inert. Lookup and a simultaneous table update use\npre-edge table contents, with no combinational update bypass.\n\nEX continues computing architectural pc_next, including forwarded JALR,\nPC+4 on the existing misalignment-trap continuation, and unchanged link\nresults. Register the consumed predicted_next_pc alongside that actual\npc_next in EX/MEM. Remove the immediate EX redirect. In MEM, compare\nthese two registered full-width values for EVERY valid instruction,\nincluding ordinary instructions, loads/stores, trapped instructions,\nand completed DIV/REM with a stale BTB prediction. Assert repair only\nwhen this EX/MEM owner can advance: valid, not reset, and no older dmem\nhold. Drive repair_target directly from registered architectural\npc_next. Do not put forwarded operands, live branch condition, target\naddition, instruction legality, or div_wait in this comparison cone.\nPreserve actual pc_next as the RVFI source. A held memory owner keeps\nboth PCs until its first accepting edge; it must not consume a repair\nor retire twice while blocked.\n\nA MEM repair wins over every younger fetch prediction and EX operation.\nOn its accepting edge, redirect fetch even if imem is unavailable,\ninvalidate the younger IF/ID slot and ID/EX entry, and inject a control-\nzero invalid EX/MEM entry instead of accepting the younger EX result.\nThe current MEM owner still advances normally into MEM/WB, including\nits legitimate memory transaction and retirement. Add a younger-EX kill\ninput that suppresses divider request/accept and effective div_wait on\nthat repair cycle, and gives squash priority over the younger divide\nhold. Do not reset the divider or its completion bank on repair: the\nrepairing MEM owner can itself be a completed DIV/REM using that bank.\nAn older memory hold already prevents a younger divide from launching;\nonce the hold ends, pending MEM repair must kill it before launch.\nCompute dmem hold independently of repair to avoid combinational loops.\nWrong-path loads/stores must be killed before becoming memory owners.\n\nRetiming training is part of this same predictor. EX computes a small\nupdate action and final counter from its accepted instruction and saved\nlookup snapshot, then registers them with EX/MEM. Register the source\nPC and target payload with ordinary payload enables, reusing existing\nEX/MEM fields where possible. Only the registered action, registered\nindex, and MEM-owner advancement may drive table write enables; no\nsame-cycle EX outcome, target-alignment test, or generic EX-accept net\nmay directly enable the wide BTB payload bank. An aligned conditional\nhit saturates its saved counter and refreshes its direct target. An\naligned taken conditional miss allocates at 10; a not-taken miss does\nnothing. Legal aligned jumps write 11. Invalidate a saved hit for a\nnon-control, illegal, or misaligned-target instruction, including an\nuntaken conditional whose target is misaligned; never allocate it.\nInvalid and killed owners have action NONE. Apply each registered action\nonce on that owner's MEM advance, including the owner causing repair.\nA stale snapshot may replace intervening state, but every allocation\nwrites a coherent complete tag/target/counter tuple. Prediction errors\nare always recoverable by the full-PC comparison.\n\nEstimate a 3% fitness improvement with low confidence. The earlier\n6.135% cycle saving is evidence for early validation only; the extra\nrecovery cycle must be measured, and may erase the benefit. A candidate\nwith 3% fewer cycles breaks even at approximately 178.54 MHz against\nthe current winner, and gains about 3.09% if Fmax stays at 184.06 MHz.\nInspect all three routed seeds for MEM comparison-to-squash timing,\nregistered-action-to-BTB enables, and unchanged raw load-use paths.\nReport actual marker-window cycles, miss/recovery observations, median\nFmax, LUT4/FF use, and net fitness. Preserve nret=1, all invariants,\narithmetic/result-bank ownership, and the directly registered dmem\naddress. Run unchanged lint, unit, formal, cosim, CRC/UART, OOB, marker,\nresource, and three-seed FPGA gates. Prior logs identify a 2 KB versus\n6 KB fixture discrepancy; keep evaluator results authoritative and any\nseparate 6 KB measurement clearly supplemental without modifying either\ncontract. This proposal changes only the bench predictor and its recovery.\n\nOUTCOME: broken ERROR: formal_failed: liveness_ch0\nsummary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:04 (4)\nSBY 12:44:25 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY 12:44:25 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY 12:44:25 [insn_sub_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 12:45:01 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:01  waiting for solver (1 minute)\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:36  Status: passed\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:01:37 (97)\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:01:16 (76)\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY 12:45:36 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\nFailed: liveness_ch0\n\n--- liveness_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\nSBY 12:44:00 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 8..\nSBY 12:44:00 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\nSBY 12:44:00 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\nSBY 12:44:00 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00: